window.COURSE={"modules":[{"id":"foundations","title":"Foundations","lessons":[{"slug":"request-lifecycle","title":"How a Request Travels","subtitle":"Follow one page load from the Enter key to the last byte, and see which hops eat the time.","minutes":10,"level":"Beginner","learn":["What a DNS lookup does and when it is skipped","Why TCP and TLS setup cost round trips before any data moves","The path browser → load balancer → app server → database and back","Why distance and a slow dependency can dominate page load time"],"nodes":[{"id":"browser","label":"Browser","kind":"client","x":10,"y":40,"url":"https://shop.example","gx":0,"gy":-12},{"id":"dns","label":"DNS resolver","kind":"dns","x":30,"y":16,"gx":0,"gy":1},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":40,"y":72,"gx":10,"gy":-1},{"id":"app","label":"App server","kind":"server","x":64,"y":50,"gx":10,"gy":12},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":88,"y":24,"gx":21,"gy":12},{"id":"cdn","label":"CDN edge","kind":"cdn","x":12,"y":92,"gx":-13,"gy":-12}],"edges":[{"from":"browser","to":"dns"},{"from":"browser","to":"lb"},{"from":"lb","to":"app"},{"from":"app","to":"db"},{"from":"browser","to":"cdn"}],"metrics":[{"id":"dns","label":"DNS","unit":"ms"},{"id":"connect","label":"Connect","unit":"ms"},{"id":"server","label":"Server","unit":"ms"},{"id":"total","label":"Total","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"You press Enter","body":"You type shop.example and press Enter. Half a second later, a page appears. That short wait is really a chain of small conversations between machines. Each one must finish before the next can start. This lesson follows the chain in order and times every link.","idea":"A page load is a chain of waits. The total time is their sum.","caption":"The browser knows a name, but not yet an address.","focus":["browser"],"flows":[],"chips":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"DNS","title":"Find the address","body":"The animation shows the browser asking a DNS resolver and getting an IP address back. Computers find each other by IP address (like a phone number), not by name. DNS, the Domain Name System, is the phone book that turns shop.example into 203.0.113.7. If the answer was saved recently (cached), the lookup is almost free. The saved answer lives for a time set by its TTL (time to live). A first lookup may ask several servers and take tens of milliseconds. Here we use 20 ms.","idea":"DNS turns a name into an IP address. A cached answer makes this step nearly free.","caption":"The resolver replies with an IP address (20 ms).","focus":["browser","dns"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"dns","kind":"request","label":"A shop.example?","token":"shop.example?"},{"from":"dns","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"203.0.113.7"}],"chips":[{"node":"dns","text":"203.0.113.7"},{"node":"dns","text":"20 ms"}],"metrics":{"dns":"20","total":"20"}},{"tag":"CONNECT","title":"Open a secure connection","body":"Next the browser opens a secure connection to the load balancer, the front door of the site. First comes TCP, a handshake that makes sure both sides are ready (1 round trip). Then comes TLS, which sets up encryption (1 more round trip with TLS 1.3). A round trip, or RTT, is a message going out and its reply coming back. We assume 50 ms per round trip, so setup costs about 100 ms before any page data moves. The DNS answer is now cached, so later requests skip that lookup.","idea":"Every handshake costs a full round trip, so distance multiplies directly into delay.","caption":"Four handshake messages make 2 round trips: about 100 ms.","focus":["browser","lb"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"handshake","label":"TCP SYN"},{"from":"lb","to":"browser","kind":"handshake","label":"TCP SYN-ACK"},{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"handshake","label":"TLS hello"},{"from":"lb","to":"browser","kind":"handshake","label":"TLS keys"}],"chips":[{"node":"dns","text":"cached · 5 min","tone":"green"},{"node":"lb","text":"RTT 50 ms"},{"node":"lb","text":"TCP + TLS ≈ 100 ms","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"connect":"100","total":"120"}},{"tag":"REQUEST","title":"Send the request","body":"Now the browser sends GET /, which means 'give me the home page', along with headers and cookies. The load balancer receives it and passes it to one healthy app server. Think of it as a receptionist who sends each visitor to a free desk. This lets any single server fail or be replaced without users noticing. The request takes about 25 ms to travel one way, so the clock reads 145 ms.","idea":"The load balancer hides how many servers exist and which one answers.","caption":"The request travels 25 ms and is forwarded to an app server.","focus":["lb","app"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /"},{"from":"lb","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"GET /"}],"chips":[{"node":"lb","text":"picks 1 of 3 servers","at":"bottom"},{"node":"lb","text":"+25 ms"}],"metrics":{"total":"145"}},{"tag":"SERVER","title":"Fetch the data","body":"The app server needs data to build the page: the product list, the cart, the prices. So it sends a query to the database. A query that uses an index (like a book's index, it lets the database jump straight to the rows) comes back in about 4 ms. The server then builds the HTML from those rows. All server work here totals 40 ms, bringing the clock to 185 ms.","idea":"Server time is the app's own work plus every dependency it waits on.","caption":"The app asks the database and gets rows back.","focus":["app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT ..."},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"rows"}],"chips":[{"node":"app","text":"builds the page"},{"node":"db","text":"indexed · 4 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"app","text":"40 ms","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"server":"40","total":"185"}},{"tag":"RESPONSE","title":"Bytes come back","body":"The finished HTML travels back through the load balancer to the browser. The first byte arrives at 210 ms: 185 ms plus 25 ms of travel. That moment is called TTFB (time to first byte), a common speed measure. The browser can start reading right away. The last of the HTML finishes downloading 25 ms later, at 235 ms.","idea":"TTFB includes DNS, connect, request and server time, but not the full download.","caption":"HTML flows back: first byte at 210 ms, finished at 235 ms.","focus":["browser","lb","app"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"},{"from":"lb","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"HTML"}],"chips":[{"node":"app","text":"200 OK","tone":"green"},{"node":"browser","text":"TTFB 210 ms"}],"metrics":{"total":"235"}},{"tag":"ASSETS","title":"More files","body":"The HTML lists more files: CSS (styling), JavaScript (behavior), fonts and images. The browser fetches them from a CDN (content delivery network), servers placed near users that keep copies of static files. A separate CDN hostname needs its own lookup and handshake, but the edge is close, so that stays cheap. Several files download in parallel and together add about 75 ms, so the page is ready at 310 ms. On a repeat visit most of these come from the browser's own cache.","idea":"Serving static files from a nearby edge removes distance from most of the bytes.","caption":"Static files arrive from a nearby CDN edge (+75 ms).","focus":["browser","cdn"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"cdn","kind":"request","label":"app.css, app.js"},{"from":"cdn","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"cached files"}],"chips":[{"node":"cdn","text":"cache hit","tone":"green"},{"node":"cdn","text":"+75 ms"}],"metrics":{"total":"310"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A slow query","body":"What if the query has no index and must read every row (a full scan)? Suppose it takes 900 ms instead of 4 ms. Server time grows from 40 ms to 900 ms and the total becomes 1,170 ms. Nothing else changed. The critical path is the chain of steps that must happen one after another. One slow step on it sets the speed of the whole page. That is why you measure each hop instead of guessing.","idea":"The slowest hop on the critical path usually dominates the total, so fix that one first.","caption":"One slow query now dominates the whole page load.","focus":["app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"full scan, 900ms","token":"full scan","pkt":"slow"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"rows, late"}],"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"full scan","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"900 ms","tone":"red"},{"node":"app","text":"waiting on DB","tone":"red"}],"metrics":{"server":"900","total":"1170"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A faraway user","body":"Go back to the healthy 40 ms query, but put the user on another continent, 150 ms away by round trip. Setup is 2 round trips, so 2 × 150 = 300 ms. The request and its reply add another 150 ms. In total: DNS 20 + setup 300 + request and reply 150 + server 40 = 510 ms to the first byte. A nearby user gets it at about 210 ms. Faster code cannot beat the speed of light. Moving servers or caches closer to users can.","idea":"Latency from distance is paid per round trip, so fewer round trips and closer servers both help.","caption":"Each round trip now costs 150 ms; first byte at about 510 ms.","focus":["browser","lb"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"handshake","label":"150 ms RTT"}],"chips":[{"node":"lb","text":"RTT 150 ms"},{"node":"browser","text":"+300 ms"}],"metrics":{"dns":"20","connect":"300","server":"40","total":"510"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Where the time went","body":"A page load is DNS, connect, request, server work, response, then assets, in that order. Caching cuts DNS and asset time. Reusing connections cuts handshakes. Indexes cut server time. CDNs and nearby servers cut distance. To speed up a page, first find which hop is slow. This lesson times only the network and server hops. Parsing, drawing the page and running JavaScript add more time after the last byte.","idea":"Measure each hop, then fix the biggest one.","caption":"Every hop added to the total: 310 ms for a nearby user.","focus":["browser","dns","lb","app","db","cdn"],"flows":[],"chips":[{"node":"browser","text":"310 ms total"},{"node":"dns","text":"DNS 20 ms"},{"node":"lb","text":"connect 100 ms"},{"node":"app","text":"server 40 ms","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"dns":"20","connect":"100","server":"40","total":"310"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A page's DNS lookup takes 0 ms on a repeat visit. What is the most likely reason?","options":["DNS is skipped for HTTPS sites","The answer was cached and its TTL had not expired","The CDN resolved it","The load balancer stores IP addresses"],"answer":1,"why":"Resolvers, the OS, and browsers cache DNS answers until their TTL expires, so repeat lookups are near-instant."},{"q":"The server is 80 ms away (round trip). Roughly how long do a fresh TCP + TLS 1.3 handshake take before the request can be sent?","options":["About 40 ms","About 80 ms","About 160 ms","About 320 ms"],"answer":2,"why":"TCP needs one round trip and TLS 1.3 needs one more, so about two round trips: 2 × 80 ms."},{"q":"TTFB jumps from 200 ms to 1.2 s after a deploy, but DNS and connect times are unchanged. Where should you look first?","options":["Server-side work such as a slow database query","DNS TTLs","Image sizes on the page","The user's Wi-Fi"],"answer":0,"why":"If DNS and connection setup are unchanged, the added time is inside the server's handling, typically a slow dependency like a query."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"RTT","def":"Round-trip time: how long a message takes to reach a server and come back. Handshakes cost whole RTTs."},{"term":"TTFB","def":"Time to first byte: from the start of the navigation until the first response byte arrives, so it includes redirects, DNS, TCP/TLS setup, request travel and server time. Some tools (e.g. the DevTools 'waiting' bar) count only from request-sent."},{"term":"TLS 1.3 handshake","def":"One round trip to agree on keys before encrypted data can flow (TLS 1.2 needed two). Optional 0-RTT resumption can send data in the first flight, but has replay risks and is used sparingly."},{"term":"Critical path","def":"The longest chain of dependent steps. Its total length is the fastest the request can finish, so speeding up anything off this chain does not help."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"latency-throughput","title":"Latency & Throughput","subtitle":"Watch one service answer requests, then see why averages lie, tails hurt, and Little's Law predicts overload.","minutes":12,"level":"Beginner","learn":["The difference between latency, throughput, and bandwidth","Why p50 and p99 tell different stories and the average hides both","How fan-out turns a rare slow call into a common slow page","How to use Little's Law (L = λ × W) to size concurrency and spot overload"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Clients","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":30,"y":50},{"id":"app","label":"App server","kind":"server","x":53,"y":50},{"id":"cache","label":"Cache","kind":"cache","x":80,"y":20},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":80,"y":50},{"id":"search","label":"Search service","kind":"service","x":80,"y":80}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"lb"},{"from":"lb","to":"app"},{"from":"app","to":"cache"},{"from":"app","to":"db"},{"from":"app","to":"search"}],"metrics":[{"id":"p50","label":"p50 latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"p99","label":"p99 latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"rps","label":"Throughput","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"inflight","label":"In flight","unit":"reqs"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Fast, or fast enough?","body":"Your service feels quick when you test it alone. Then launch day comes, thousands of users arrive, and some of them wait several seconds. Nothing is broken. You were measuring only one kind of 'fast'. This lesson separates the different meanings: how long one request takes, how many you can handle at once, and what happens to the slowest ones.","idea":"Fast has several meanings. How long one request takes and how many you can serve are different questions.","caption":"Many clients send requests to one service and its backends.","focus":["client","app"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Latency: one request's wait","body":"Latency is how long one request waits, from the moment it is sent until the answer arrives. In the animation, a client calls the load balancer, the app checks the cache (a fast store of recent answers), and the reply travels back. We assume the whole trip takes 40 ms. Users feel latency directly, so lower is better.","idea":"Latency is one request's wait time. Users feel it directly.","caption":"One request, start to finish: 40 ms.","focus":["client","lb","app","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /item"},{"from":"lb","to":"app","kind":"request"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"get item"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"hit"},{"from":"app","to":"lb","kind":"response"},{"from":"lb","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"p50":"40"},"chips":[{"node":"client","text":"40 ms"},{"node":"cache","text":"hit","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Throughput: requests per second","body":"Throughput is how many requests the system finishes each second. If each request takes 40 ms and the app works on 20 at the same time, it finishes 20 ÷ 0.04 = 500 requests per second. Think of a shop: more cashiers serve more people per hour, but each customer still takes the same time. Doing work in parallel raises throughput without making any single request faster.","idea":"Throughput is completed requests per second. Parallelism raises it without cutting latency.","caption":"20 requests in progress at once give 500 per second.","focus":["client","lb","app"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"500 req/s"},{"from":"lb","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"20 at a time"},{"from":"app","to":"lb","kind":"response"},{"from":"lb","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"500 req/s"}],"metrics":{"rps":"500","inflight":"20"},"chips":[{"node":"lb","text":"500 req/s"},{"node":"app","text":"20 in flight"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Bandwidth is the pipe, not the speed","body":"Bandwidth is the most data a link can carry per second, like the width of a pipe. A wider pipe moves more bytes at once, but it does not shorten the trip. Take a 1 KB reply. On a 10 Gbit/s link and on a 100 Mbit/s link it still takes about 40 ms, because almost all of that is delay (network round trip plus server work). The transfer itself takes about 0.08 ms even on the slower link. Bandwidth matters for big files. Latency matters for small messages.","idea":"Bandwidth is capacity and latency is delay. A wider pipe barely helps a small message arrive sooner.","caption":"A wider pipe does not remove the round-trip delay.","focus":["client","lb"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"1 KB"},{"from":"lb","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"1 KB reply"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"client","text":"1 KB reply"},{"node":"lb","text":"~40 ms either way"},{"node":"lb","text":"0.08 ms transfer","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Percentiles beat averages","body":"Latency differs from request to request, so one number cannot describe it. The p50 (median) is the time that half of requests beat. Here it is 40 ms. The p99 is the time that 99 of every 100 requests beat, so the slowest 1% are worse. Here it is 400 ms. If 99% take 40 ms and 1% take 400 ms, the average is about 44 ms, which hides the slow ones completely. Report percentiles, not only the mean.","idea":"p50 shows the typical request and p99 shows the slow end. The average hides both.","caption":"Out of 100 requests, 99 take 40 ms and 1 takes 400 ms.","focus":["app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"100 requests"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"99 take 40 ms"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"1 takes 400 ms"}],"metrics":{"p50":"40","p99":"400"},"chips":[{"node":"app","text":"p50 40 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"db","text":"p99 400 ms","tone":"red"},{"node":"app","text":"average ≈ 44 ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Why the tail exists","body":"The slowest requests are called the tail. Their causes are ordinary. A garbage collection (GC) pause is when a program freezes briefly to clean up memory. A noisy neighbour is another tenant hogging the shared host. A cache miss (the data was not in the cache) forces a trip to the slow database, as shown. A lost network packet causes a retry. A lock held by another query makes yours wait. Each is rare, but together they never fully go away.","idea":"Tail latency comes from many small random stalls. You manage it; you cannot remove it.","caption":"A cache miss sends this request down the slow path to the database.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"get item"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"miss"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT ..."},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"400 ms"}],"metrics":{"p99":"400"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"miss","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"400 ms","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Fan-out multiplies the tail","body":"A single page often calls many backends at once. Suppose each call has a 1% chance of being slow, and the calls are independent. The page needs all 20 answers, so it waits for the slowest. The chance that at least one is slow is 1 − 0.99^20, about 18%. So one backend's p99 turns into roughly your page's p82. The more calls per request, the more users feel the tail.","idea":"With N parallel calls the page is only as fast as its slowest one, so rare slowness becomes common.","caption":"3 of the 20 calls shown: one slow reply delays the whole page.","focus":["app","cache","db","search"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"call 1"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"call 2"},{"from":"app","to":"search","kind":"request","label":"call 3"},{"from":"search","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"slow reply"}],"metrics":{"p99":"400"},"chips":[{"node":"app","text":"waits on 20 calls"},{"node":"search","text":"slow reply","tone":"red"},{"node":"app","text":"18% hit the tail","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Little's Law","body":"Little's Law links three numbers: L = λ × W. L is the average number of requests inside the system. λ (lambda) is the arrival rate. W is the average time one request spends there. At 500 req/s and 0.04 s each, L = 500 × 0.04 = 20 requests in flight. W is the average, not the median (about 44 ms by the earlier numbers, so L would be about 22; we use 40 ms to keep the math round). It holds for any stable system. Use it to size thread pools, connection pools and queues, with headroom above L.","idea":"Requests in flight = arrival rate × time in system, so latency and concurrency are linked.","caption":"500 requests/s × 0.04 s = 20 requests in flight.","focus":["lb","app"],"flows":[{"from":"lb","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"λ = 500/s"},{"from":"app","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"W = 0.04 s"}],"metrics":{"rps":"500","inflight":"20"},"chips":[{"node":"lb","text":"λ = 500/s"},{"node":"app","text":"W = 0.04 s"},{"node":"app","text":"L = 20 in flight","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Latency rises, the pool runs out","body":"Now the database slows down and every request takes 400 ms. Arrivals are still 500 per second, so you would need L = 500 × 0.4 = 200 requests in flight. But the app has only 50 worker threads, and 50 ÷ 0.4 = 125 requests finish per second. The other 375 per second wait in a queue. We assume clients give up after 2 s, so most requests wait until then. Clients that time out often retry, which adds even more load. One slow dependency can topple the whole service.","idea":"When time per request grows at a fixed arrival rate, needed concurrency explodes past capacity and queues feed on themselves.","caption":"Need 200 slots, have 50: 375 req/s pile up in the queue.","focus":["lb","app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"lb","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"500 req/s"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"400 ms each"},{"from":"app","to":"lb","kind":"fail","label":"queue full"}],"metrics":{"p50":"2000","p99":"2000","rps":"125","inflight":"50"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"400 ms each","tone":"red"},{"node":"app","text":"pool 100% busy","tone":"red","kind":"progress","value":100},{"node":"app","text":"125 req/s max","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"What to remember","body":"Latency is one request's wait. Throughput is requests finished per second. Bandwidth is the pipe's capacity. Track p50 and p99, never only the average, and expect fan-out to make tails matter more. Use L = λ × W to check that your pools cover your latency. To protect the tail, set timeouts, cache hot data, bound your queues, and shed load, which means rejecting extra requests early so the rest stay fast.","idea":"Measure percentiles, watch concurrency with Little's Law, and design for the tail.","caption":"A healthy service: fast median, bounded tail, enough slots.","focus":["client","lb","app","cache","db","search"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"p50":"40","p99":"400","rps":"500","inflight":"20"},"chips":[{"node":"app","text":"p50 40 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"app","text":"p99 400 ms","at":"bottom"},{"node":"lb","text":"500 req/s"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A link is upgraded from 100 Mbit/s to 10 Gbit/s. Users loading a tiny 2 KB JSON response from a server 80 ms away barely notice. Why?","options":["Bandwidth only matters for uploads","The 80 ms round trip dominates; the payload was already tiny, so more capacity saves almost nothing","Faster links increase latency for small messages","JSON is compressed automatically so link speed is irrelevant"],"answer":1,"why":"Bandwidth is capacity. For a 2 KB reply the transfer time is a fraction of a millisecond; the round-trip delay is what the user waits on."},{"q":"Your dashboard shows average latency of 60 ms, but users complain about occasional multi-second hangs. What should you add first?","options":["p95/p99 latency per endpoint","A bigger average window","More bandwidth","A second load balancer"],"answer":0,"why":"The mean is dominated by the many fast requests and hides rare slow ones. Percentiles like p99 expose the tail users are complaining about."},{"q":"A page makes 50 independent parallel backend calls, each with a 99th-percentile latency of 300 ms and a typical latency of 30 ms. About how often will the page wait roughly 300 ms or more?","options":["About 1% of the time","About 5% of the time","About 40% of the time","Almost never"],"answer":2,"why":"The chance at least one of 50 calls is slow is 1 - 0.99^50, about 40%. Fan-out makes a backend's tail the page's common case."},{"q":"A service gets 200 requests per second and the average request spends 250 ms inside it. About how many requests are in flight on average?","options":["5","200","800","50"],"answer":3,"why":"Little's Law: L = λ × W = 200 × 0.25 = 50 requests in flight, so you need at least that many worker slots plus headroom."},{"q":"Traffic is steady at 300 req/s, but a dependency slows and your average time per request triples. With a fixed pool sized with only modest headroom over the original load, what happens?","options":["Throughput rises because workers stay busy","Nothing, because arrival rate did not change","Needed concurrency triples, so the pool saturates, queues form, and latency climbs further","Latency drops because fewer requests finish"],"answer":2,"why":"L = λ × W, so tripling W triples required concurrency. Once it exceeds the pool, requests queue, which raises W again and worsens the overload."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Latency","def":"Time for one request to complete, from send to response. Measured in milliseconds; lower is better."},{"term":"Throughput","def":"Requests (or bytes) completed per second. Raised by parallelism, caching, and efficiency, not only by faster single requests."},{"term":"Bandwidth","def":"Maximum data a link can move per second. It caps throughput for big payloads but does not reduce the round-trip delay."},{"term":"p50 / p95 / p99","def":"The value that 50% / 95% / 99% of requests beat. p50 is the typical case; p99 means 1 in 100 requests is slower."},{"term":"Tail latency","def":"The slow end of the latency distribution (p99 and beyond), caused by GC pauses, cache misses, retries, and contention."},{"term":"Fan-out amplification","def":"With N parallel calls, P(at least one slow) = 1 - (1 - p)^N. A 1% tail across 20 calls hits about 18% of pages."},{"term":"Little's Law","def":"L = λ × W: average in-flight requests = arrival rate × average time in system. Use it to size pools and queues."},{"term":"Defences for the tail","def":"Hedged request: after a short delay, send a duplicate to another replica and use the first answer (for safe/idempotent reads; costs extra load). Load shedding: reject excess requests early so the rest stay fast. Also timeouts, bounded queues."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"napkin-math","title":"Napkin Math","subtitle":"Turn 'ten million daily users' into requests per second, terabytes, and gigabits in a few lines of arithmetic.","minutes":12,"level":"Beginner","learn":["Convert daily active users into average and peak requests per second","Estimate how fast storage grows and what a year of data costs in space","Convert photo or video sizes into bandwidth, and avoid the bits-versus-bytes trap","Recall the latency numbers that separate memory, SSD, network, and distance","Use powers of two and round numbers to do all of this in your head"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"10M daily users","kind":"user","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"cdn","label":"CDN","kind":"cdn","x":35,"y":18},{"id":"api","label":"API servers","kind":"server","x":38,"y":58},{"id":"cache","label":"Cache","kind":"cache","x":65,"y":28},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":88,"y":28},{"id":"store","label":"Photo storage","kind":"storage","x":65,"y":80}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"cdn"},{"from":"users","to":"api"},{"from":"api","to":"cache"},{"from":"api","to":"db"},{"from":"api","to":"store"},{"from":"cdn","to":"store"}],"metrics":[{"id":"avg","label":"Average QPS","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"peak","label":"Peak QPS","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"storage","label":"Storage / year","unit":"TB"},{"id":"egress","label":"Egress","unit":"Gbps"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Will it fit on one machine?","body":"You are designing a photo-sharing app with 10 million daily active users (DAU, people who open it each day). Before drawing any boxes, ask: how many requests per second, how much storage, how much network traffic? Napkin math answers these in minutes with rough numbers. It tells you whether one server is enough or you need a fleet.","idea":"Estimate first. Being within 2x to 3x is good enough to choose a design.","caption":"Ten million daily users, and nothing else is known yet.","focus":["users"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Round numbers are your friend","body":"Use round numbers. A day has 86,400 seconds, which we round to 100,000 (10^5). So 1 million requests a day is about 10 per second (really 11.6). For sizes, remember that 2^10 is about a thousand (KB), 2^20 a million (MB), 2^30 a billion (GB) and 2^40 a trillion (TB). Errors of 10 to 20 percent do not matter here. We also state our assumptions: each user views 100 photos a day, and 10% of users upload one photo.","idea":"Memorise 86,400 seconds per day and the powers-of-two ladder. Most estimates then become counting zeros.","caption":"Assumptions: 100 views per user per day; 10% of users upload one photo.","focus":["users","api"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"100 views / day"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"users","text":"1 day ≈ 10^5 s"},{"node":"api","text":"1M/day ≈ 10/s"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"From DAU to QPS","body":"Reads per day: 10M users × 100 views = 1 billion. Divide by about 86,400 seconds and you get about 12,000 reads per second on average. This is called QPS (queries per second). We assume each view makes one API call for photo details, while the image bytes come from the CDN. Uploads: 10% of users post one photo, so 1M a day, about 12 writes per second. Reads outnumber writes about 1000 to 1, so this system must be built for fast reads.","idea":"QPS = (DAU × actions per user per day) ÷ 86,400. The read/write ratio shapes the design.","caption":"On average, about 12,000 reads per second hit the system.","focus":["users","api"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"12k reads/s"},{"from":"users","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"12 uploads/s"}],"metrics":{"avg":"12000"},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"12k reads/s"},{"node":"api","text":"12 writes/s","at":"bottom"},{"node":"users","text":"read:write 1000:1"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Design for the peak","body":"Traffic is not flat. People browse photos in the evening, so the busiest hour can be several times the average. A common rule of thumb is peak = 2x to 5x average. We use 3x, which gives about 36,000 reads per second. Servers must handle the peak, not the average. Otherwise the app breaks exactly when most people are watching.","idea":"Size capacity for the peak: multiply the average by a peak factor (a rule of thumb, often 2x to 5x).","caption":"At peak: about 36,000 reads per second (3 × 12,000).","focus":["users","api"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"36k reads/s peak"}],"metrics":{"peak":"36000"},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"36k reads/s peak"},{"node":"users","text":"peak = 3× avg"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Storage grows every day","body":"One million uploads a day at about 2 MB each is 2 TB per day. Over 365 days that is about 730 TB, close to three quarters of a petabyte (PB, 1,000 TB). That is before making backup copies. Databases usually keep 3 full copies for safety, so about 2.2 PB. Large object stores often use erasure coding (splitting data with extra repair pieces), which costs closer to 1.5x. Resized copies and metadata add a bit more, so we ignore them in a napkin estimate.","idea":"Storage per year = writes per day × size per item × 365, then multiply by the number of copies.","caption":"Uploads pile up in object storage: 2 TB per day, 730 TB per year.","focus":["api","store"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"2 MB photo"}],"metrics":{"storage":"730"},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"2 TB / day"},{"node":"store","text":"730 TB / yr"},{"node":"store","text":"3 copies ≈ 2.2 PB","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Bandwidth: watch bits and bytes","body":"Each view serves a resized image of about 200 KB. A billion views a day is 200 TB a day, or about 2.3 GB per second. Network links are rated in bits, not bytes, so multiply by 8: about 18 Gbps on average and 55 Gbps at peak. That is far too much for the main servers. A CDN keeps copies of popular files on servers near users, so most of these bytes never touch the origin. Egress means data leaving your system.","idea":"Bandwidth = QPS × bytes per response. Multiply bytes by 8 to compare with link speeds in bits.","caption":"About 18 Gbps leaves the system, mostly served from CDN caches.","focus":["users","cdn","store"],"flows":[{"from":"store","to":"cdn","kind":"response","label":"cache misses"},{"from":"cdn","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"200 KB images"}],"metrics":{"egress":"18"},"chips":[{"node":"cdn","text":"200 TB / day"},{"node":"cdn","text":"18 Gbps avg"},{"node":"cdn","text":"55 Gbps peak","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Latency numbers to remember","body":"Some speeds are worth remembering. Reading memory takes about 100 nanoseconds. An SSD random read takes about 100 microseconds. A round trip inside one data center takes about 0.5 ms. A spinning-disk seek takes about 10 ms. A round trip between continents takes about 150 ms. Each step down this ladder is roughly 5 to 1000 times slower, so keep slow tiers off the hot path. The animation shows why: the cache answers fast and the disk-based database answers slowly.","idea":"Memory is about 1000x faster than SSD, and a cross-continent round trip is about 300x slower than one inside a data center.","caption":"A cache lookup is much cheaper than a database read from disk.","focus":["api","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"~0.5 ms"},{"from":"cache","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"hit"},{"from":"api","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"~10 ms disk"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"~0.5 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"db","text":"~10 ms disk","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Turning numbers into decisions","body":"Now divide the estimate by what one machine can do. Say one API server handles 2,000 requests per second. Peak 36,000 needs 18 servers, so plan about 25 for headroom. One database comfortably serves about 5,000 simple reads per second. A cache hit means the answer was already in the cache. A miss falls through to the database. At most 5,000 of 36,000 lookups (about 14%) can reach the database, so the cache must hit about 86% of the time. At 85% you would send 5,400 misses, too many, so aim for 90%. Then the database sees 3,600 per second, under its 5,000 limit.","idea":"Divide the estimate by what one machine can do. The result tells you how many machines, or what cache hit rate, you need.","caption":"The cache absorbs most reads, so the database sees only misses.","focus":["api","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"36k lookups/s"},{"from":"api","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"~3.6k misses/s"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"18 → 25 servers"},{"node":"cache","text":"90% hit","tone":"green"},{"node":"db","text":"72% of DB limit","tone":"green","kind":"progress","value":72}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"What if they were videos?","body":"Change one assumption: each upload is a 50 MB video instead of a 2 MB photo. Storage becomes 1M × 50 MB = 50 TB per day, which is about 18,000 TB (18 PB) per year. That is 25 times more. Nothing else in the estimate changed, yet the cost and design of the storage layer changed completely. One input can dominate everything, so check the biggest ones first.","idea":"Find the assumption that moves the result most and sanity-check that one first.","caption":"Same upload rate, 25x the bytes written.","focus":["api","store"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"50 MB video"}],"metrics":{"storage":"18250"},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"50 TB / day","tone":"red"},{"node":"store","text":"18 PB / yr","tone":"red"},{"node":"store","text":"25× more","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"The napkin recipe","body":"State your assumptions, then convert. Users to QPS: divide by 86,400. Average to peak: multiply by about 3. Items to storage: size × count × days. Responses to bandwidth: bytes × 8 for bits. Then compare each result with what one machine can do and with the latency ladder. Answers within a factor of 2 to 3 are enough to choose a design.","idea":"Write assumptions down, round aggressively, and use the results to choose between one box and a fleet.","caption":"Our photo app: 12k QPS, 36k peak, 730 TB a year, 18 Gbps.","focus":["users","cdn","api","cache","db","store"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"avg":"12000","peak":"36000","storage":"730","egress":"18"},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"12k → 36k QPS"},{"node":"store","text":"730 TB / yr"},{"node":"cdn","text":"18 Gbps"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"An app has 5 million DAU, and each user makes 20 requests a day. What is the average QPS, roughly?","options":["About 12","About 120","About 1,200","About 12,000"],"answer":2,"why":"5M x 20 = 100M requests per day. Dividing by about 100,000 seconds per day gives about 1,000, or 1,160 with 86,400. Peak would be several times that."},{"q":"300,000 uploads a day at 5 MB each, kept for one year with no replication. About how much storage is that?","options":["About 1.5 TB","About 55 TB","About 5.5 PB","About 550 TB"],"answer":3,"why":"300,000 x 5 MB = 1.5 TB per day. Times 365 is about 550 TB. Replication would multiply that further."},{"q":"You serve 1.2 GB per second on average and your network link is 10 Gbps. What does the napkin math say?","options":["It barely fits on average and cannot absorb peak","It fits easily, since 1.2 is far smaller than 10","It fits, since 10 Gbps is 10 GB/s","It is fine as long as peak is under 2x"],"answer":0,"why":"Link speeds are in bits: 1.2 GB/s x 8 = 9.6 Gbps, already about 96 percent of the link. Any peak above average would saturate it."},{"q":"Peak reads are 30,000 per second and your database safely handles 5,000. About what cache hit rate do you need at minimum?","options":["70 percent","85 percent","50 percent","99.9 percent"],"answer":1,"why":"The database may see at most 5,000 of 30,000 requests, which is about 17 percent misses, so a hit rate of roughly 83 percent. 85 percent meets it; 70 percent leaves 9,000 misses, and 50 percent leaves 15,000."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Seconds per day","def":"86,400, rounded to about 100,000 (10^5). So 1 million events per day is about 12 per second."},{"term":"Powers of two","def":"2^10 is about 1 thousand (KB), 2^20 about 1 million (MB), 2^30 about 1 billion (GB), 2^40 about 1 trillion (TB), 2^50 about 1 quadrillion (PB)."},{"term":"QPS formula","def":"QPS = queries (requests) per second. Average QPS = DAU x actions per user per day / 86,400. Peak QPS = average x 2 to 5 (rule of thumb; 3 is a common default)."},{"term":"Storage formula","def":"Per year = items per day x bytes per item x 365. Multiply by the replication factor, usually 3 for databases, about 1.5 for erasure-coded object storage, for the real footprint."},{"term":"Bandwidth","def":"QPS x bytes per response gives bytes per second. Multiply by 8 for bits per second, which is how network links are rated."},{"term":"Latency ladder","def":"Memory about 100 ns, SSD read about 100 us, same-data-center round trip about 0.5 ms, disk seek about 10 ms, cross-continent round trip about 150 ms."},{"term":"Capacity check","def":"Divide peak load by what one machine handles to get a server count, then add headroom. Do the same for a database against cache miss rate."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"dns","title":"DNS in Depth","subtitle":"Watch a cold DNS lookup walk from resolver to root, TLD and authoritative server, then see how TTLs, GeoDNS and failover bend the answer.","minutes":12,"level":"Beginner","learn":["The difference between a recursive resolver and an authoritative server","How root, TLD and authoritative servers cooperate to answer one question","What A, AAAA, CNAME, MX and TXT records are for","How TTL and caching trade speed against how fast changes spread","How GeoDNS and DNS-based failover steer users, and where they lag"],"nodes":[{"id":"browser","label":"Browser + OS","kind":"client","x":10,"y":50,"url":"https://example.com"},{"id":"resolver","label":"Recursive resolver","kind":"dns","x":33,"y":50},{"id":"root","label":"Root servers","kind":"dns","x":58,"y":12},{"id":"tld","label":".com TLD servers","kind":"dns","x":82,"y":12},{"id":"auth","label":"Authoritative NS","kind":"dns","x":84,"y":42},{"id":"us","label":"Origin US","kind":"server","x":60,"y":78},{"id":"eu","label":"Origin EU","kind":"server","x":86,"y":78}],"edges":[{"from":"browser","to":"resolver"},{"from":"resolver","to":"root"},{"from":"resolver","to":"tld"},{"from":"resolver","to":"auth"},{"from":"browser","to":"us"},{"from":"browser","to":"eu"},{"from":"auth","to":"us"},{"from":"auth","to":"eu"}],"metrics":[{"id":"lookup","label":"Lookup time","unit":"ms"},{"id":"hops","label":"Servers asked","unit":""},{"id":"ttl","label":"TTL left","unit":"s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Names are not addresses","body":"Your browser wants example.com, but networks deliver data to IP addresses (numbers like 203.0.113.7). Something must translate the name into a number, and no single computer knows every name. DNS, the Domain Name System, solves this with a hierarchy of servers. Each knows only a small slice. Layers of caching mean most lookups never travel far.","idea":"DNS is a distributed, cached phone book arranged in layers, not one big database.","caption":"The browser has a name and needs an IP address.","focus":["browser"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Ask the recursive resolver","body":"The operating system hands the question to a recursive resolver, usually run by your ISP or a public provider. A resolver does the whole chase for you and returns one final answer. It also keeps a cache, so if anyone nearby asked recently, it answers instantly. Here its cache has nothing for this name (a cache miss), so it must ask servers higher up. The 2 ms is just the short hop from the browser to the resolver.","idea":"A recursive resolver does the legwork for clients and remembers the results.","caption":"The browser asks the resolver, and the resolver's cache has no answer.","focus":["browser","resolver"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"resolver","kind":"request","label":"A example.com?"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"2","hops":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"resolver","text":"cache miss"},{"node":"browser","text":"asks resolver"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Root: who runs .com?","body":"The animation shows the resolver asking a root server. The resolver already knows the addresses of the 13 root server names. Each name is served by many machines worldwide (anycast: machines share one address and you reach the nearest). The root does not know example.com. But it knows who runs each top-level domain such as .com, so it replies with a referral, meaning 'ask them instead'. In real life the resolver has usually cached the root and .com referrals, so most lookups skip straight to the authoritative server.","idea":"The root only points to the right TLD servers. It never stores individual sites.","caption":"The root refers the resolver to the .com servers.","focus":["resolver","root"],"flows":[{"from":"resolver","to":"root","kind":"request","label":"A example.com?"},{"from":"root","to":"resolver","kind":"response","label":"ask .com TLD"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"24","hops":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"root","text":"ask .com TLD"},{"node":"resolver","text":"+22 ms"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"TLD: who runs example.com?","body":"The resolver asks again, this time a .com TLD (top-level domain) server. TLD servers know, for every registered .com name, which name servers its owner chose (stored as NS records). The reply is another referral, to example.com's own name servers. Still no IP address yet, just a closer signpost. Each step of the chase gets nearer to the owner.","idea":"The TLD tier maps each registered domain to its authoritative name servers.","caption":"The TLD server refers the resolver to example.com's servers.","focus":["resolver","tld"],"flows":[{"from":"resolver","to":"tld","kind":"request","label":"A example.com?"},{"from":"tld","to":"resolver","kind":"response","label":"ask ns1.example"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"46","hops":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"tld","text":"ask ns1.example"},{"node":"resolver","text":"+22 ms"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Authoritative: the real answer","body":"An authoritative server holds the real records for a zone, the set of records one owner manages. It answers with the A record 203.0.113.7 and a TTL (time to live) of 300 seconds, meaning 'you may reuse this answer for 5 minutes'. The resolver saves it and passes it to the browser. Count the time: about 2 ms from browser to resolver, then about 22 ms each to the root, TLD and authoritative servers. The cold lookup totals 2 + 66 = 68 ms, about 70.","idea":"Only the authoritative server is the source of truth. Everything else is a cached copy.","caption":"The authoritative server answers. The resolver caches it and replies.","focus":["resolver","auth","browser"],"flows":[{"from":"resolver","to":"auth","kind":"request","label":"A example.com?"},{"from":"auth","to":"resolver","kind":"response","label":"203.0.113.7"},{"from":"resolver","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"203.0.113.7"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"68","hops":"4","ttl":"300"},"chips":[{"node":"auth","text":"203.0.113.7"},{"node":"auth","text":"TTL 300 s"},{"node":"resolver","text":"≈ 70 ms cold","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Record types","body":"A zone holds more than one kind of record. A maps a name to an IPv4 address and AAAA to an IPv6 address. CNAME says one name is an alias for another, so www can follow example.com. MX names the mail servers. TXT holds free text, used for domain verification and email rules such as SPF (a list of servers allowed to send your mail). Here the resolver asks for www and gets back the alias plus its target address. A CNAME cannot sit at the zone apex (the bare domain, like example.com).","idea":"Pick the record type by purpose: address (A, AAAA), alias (CNAME), mail (MX), metadata (TXT).","caption":"The resolver asks for www and follows a CNAME to an A record.","focus":["resolver","auth"],"flows":[{"from":"resolver","to":"auth","kind":"request","label":"A www?"},{"from":"auth","to":"resolver","kind":"response","label":"CNAME + A"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"auth","text":"CNAME + A"},{"node":"auth","text":"no CNAME at apex","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CACHE","title":"TTL and caching","body":"Two seconds later, another user behind the same resolver asks for example.com. The resolver answers from its cache in about 2 ms. No root, TLD or authoritative server is contacted. Each cached answer carries a TTL that counts down, here from 300 to 298. At zero the answer is thrown away and fetched again. Browsers and operating systems cache too, so the real number of lookups is even lower.","idea":"A longer TTL means faster lookups and less load, but changes take longer to spread.","caption":"Warm cache: one hop, answer in about 2 ms.","focus":["browser","resolver"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"resolver","kind":"request","label":"A example.com?"},{"from":"resolver","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"cached, 298s"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"2","hops":"1","ttl":"298"},"chips":[{"node":"resolver","text":"cached · 298 s","tone":"green"},{"node":"resolver","text":"2 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"GEODNS","title":"GeoDNS: different answers by place","body":"The owner runs one site in the US and one in Europe. A GeoDNS authoritative server looks at who is asking and returns the nearest origin. It usually sees only the resolver's IP address, or a client-subnet hint (a shortened copy of the user's network address that the resolver may pass along). Here a user in Europe uses a resolver in Frankfurt, so the answer is the EU address. The user's connections then take about 15 ms instead of about 90 ms. The 60 s TTL lets steering change quickly. It is only a guess: a user on a distant resolver can be sent to the wrong region.","idea":"GeoDNS picks the answer by the asker's location, which is only as accurate as the resolver tells it.","caption":"The authoritative server returns the EU address, and the browser connects there.","focus":["resolver","auth","eu","us","browser"],"flows":[{"from":"resolver","to":"auth","kind":"request","label":"A (from Frankfurt)"},{"from":"auth","to":"resolver","kind":"response","label":"EU IP"},{"from":"browser","to":"eu","kind":"request","label":"GET /"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"24","hops":"2","ttl":"60"},"chips":[{"node":"us","text":"~90 ms away"},{"node":"eu","text":"~15 ms away","tone":"green"},{"node":"auth","text":"→ EU IP","tone":"green"},{"node":"resolver","text":"TTL 60 s","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"DNS-based failover","body":"Now the EU origin dies. The authoritative server health-checks both origins, notices, and stops returning the EU address. Detection takes a few failed probes. Meanwhile resolvers and browsers still hold the old answer until its 60 s TTL runs out, and some clients ignore TTLs, so some users hit a dead server for a minute or more. That is why failover records use short TTLs. Once the cached answer expires, the next lookup returns the US address and the browser connects there.","idea":"DNS failover works, but it is only as fast as the TTL. Short TTLs recover faster at the cost of more lookups.","caption":"The EU origin is down, but cached answers still point to it.","focus":["auth","eu","browser"],"flows":[{"from":"auth","to":"eu","kind":"fail","label":"health check"},{"from":"browser","to":"eu","kind":"fail","label":"cached IP, timeout"},{"from":"browser","to":"us","kind":"request","label":"new lookup: US IP"}],"metrics":{"ttl":"60"},"chips":[{"node":"eu","text":"down","tone":"red"},{"node":"browser","text":"stale IP · 60 s","tone":"red"},{"node":"auth","text":"TTL 60 s","at":"bottom"},{"node":"us","text":"takes over","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"What to remember","body":"A recursive resolver chases the answer through root, TLD and authoritative servers, then caches it. Records hold different facts: addresses, aliases, mail servers, metadata. TTL is the dial between speed and agility. GeoDNS and failover both build on DNS, and both are limited by cached answers you cannot take back.","idea":"DNS is a cached hierarchy. Every clever trick on top inherits the TTL delay.","caption":"The full lookup path and the two origins it can steer to.","focus":["browser","resolver","root","tld","auth","us","eu"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"lookup":"68","hops":"4","ttl":"300"},"chips":[{"node":"resolver","text":"cold ≈ 70 ms"},{"node":"resolver","text":"cached 2 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"auth","text":"TTL 300 s"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A resolver has no cached data for example.com. What is the order of servers it contacts?","options":["Authoritative, then TLD, then root","Root, then TLD, then authoritative","TLD, then root, then authoritative","Root, then authoritative, skipping the TLD"],"answer":1,"why":"The root points to the .com TLD servers, which point to the domain's authoritative servers, which hold the actual records."},{"q":"You must move your site to a new IP in three days. Your A record has a 24-hour TTL. What should you do beforehand?","options":["Nothing; DNS updates instantly worldwide","Raise the TTL so resolvers cache the new value longer","Lower the TTL to a few minutes well in advance, then change the IP","Switch the record to a TXT record"],"answer":2,"why":"Caches keep the old answer until the TTL expires, so the TTL must be lowered at least one old-TTL period before the change."},{"q":"You want example.com (the zone apex) to alias to a provider hostname, and also receive mail. Which is the real problem?","options":["MX records cannot point to hostnames","TXT records block aliases","CNAME records only work for IPv6","A CNAME cannot coexist with other records such as MX and NS at the apex"],"answer":3,"why":"A CNAME means the name is entirely an alias, so no other record types may exist at that name; apex names need NS and SOA records. Many DNS providers offer ALIAS/ANAME or CNAME flattening, which resolves the target for you and answers with plain A/AAAA records at the apex."},{"q":"Your EU origin fails and DNS failover removes it, yet some users still see errors for a minute. Most likely cause?","options":["Clients and resolvers are using cached answers until the TTL expires","The authoritative server has not noticed","GeoDNS cannot return US addresses","Root servers are overloaded"],"answer":0,"why":"DNS changes only reach users as cached copies expire, so the TTL sets a floor on failover time for those clients."},{"q":"A user in Berlin uses a public resolver that sits in the US, and GeoDNS returns the US origin. Why?","options":["Root servers geolocate users","A records are always US","GeoDNS saw the resolver's location, not the user's","The TTL expired"],"answer":2,"why":"The authoritative server only sees the resolver's address (unless a client-subnet hint is passed), so a distant resolver makes GeoDNS guess the wrong region."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Recursive resolver","def":"Server that answers clients by chasing the lookup through root, TLD and authoritative servers, and caches results."},{"term":"Authoritative server","def":"Holds the real records for a zone. Its answers are the source of truth; all other copies are caches."},{"term":"Root / TLD","def":"Root refers to TLD servers (.com, .org); TLD servers refer to each domain's name servers via NS records."},{"term":"A / AAAA","def":"Map a name to an IPv4 / IPv6 address. A name can have several, which gives crude load spreading."},{"term":"CNAME","def":"Makes a name an alias of another name. Cannot share a name with other records, so not allowed at the zone apex. Providers' ALIAS/ANAME or CNAME flattening can work around this."},{"term":"MX / TXT","def":"MX lists mail servers with priorities. TXT holds text for SPF, DKIM, DMARC and domain-ownership checks."},{"term":"TTL","def":"Seconds a cached answer may be reused. Rule of thumb: about 60 s for failover records, 300-3600 s for most records, up to a day for rarely changed ones."},{"term":"GeoDNS / failover","def":"Authoritative server varies answers by asker location or origin health. Limited by resolver accuracy and TTL lag."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"http-tls","title":"HTTP, TLS & Connections","subtitle":"Load the same 31-file page over HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 and watch round trips and blocking change the clock.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why a new connection costs round trips, and what TLS 1.3 saves","How keep-alive and connection pools avoid repeating that cost","What head-of-line blocking is, at the HTTP layer and at the TCP layer","How HTTP/2 multiplexing and HTTP/3 over QUIC each remove one kind of blocking"],"nodes":[{"id":"browser","label":"Browser","kind":"client","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"edge","label":"Edge proxy (TLS)","kind":"gateway","x":40,"y":50},{"id":"app","label":"App server","kind":"server","x":74,"y":24},{"id":"api","label":"Auth service","kind":"service","x":74,"y":76}],"edges":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","label":"50 ms RTT"},{"from":"edge","to":"app"},{"from":"edge","to":"api"}],"metrics":[{"id":"setup","label":"Setup RTTs","unit":"RTT"},{"id":"conns","label":"Connections","unit":""},{"id":"load","label":"Page load","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"31 files, one slow wire","body":"A page needs one HTML file plus 30 small assets (CSS, scripts, images). The user is 50 ms away by round trip (RTT: a message out and its reply back). We ignore download time, so only round trips and waiting count. The question: how should the browser and server talk so all 31 requests finish fast? HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 each answer differently, and the numbers show why.","idea":"With many small files, the cost is mostly round trips, not bandwidth.","caption":"One browser, one edge proxy, 31 resources to fetch.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"setup":"0","conns":"0","load":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"browser","text":"31 requests"},{"node":"edge","text":"RTT 50 ms"}]},{"tag":"CONNECT","title":"A new connection is not free","body":"Before any HTTP data moves, the browser opens a TCP connection. TCP is the layer that delivers bytes reliably. Its handshake is SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK ('hello', 'hello back', 'ready') and costs one round trip. Then TLS adds encryption. With TLS 1.3 that is one more round trip, so the first request cannot leave until about 100 ms have passed. Older TLS 1.2 needed two round trips for TLS, making it 150 ms. This step animates the TCP half. TLS comes next.","idea":"TCP plus TLS 1.3 costs 2 round trips before the first request. TLS 1.2 costs 3.","caption":"TCP handshake: round trip 1 of 2 (TLS is next).","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"handshake","label":"SYN"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"handshake","label":"SYN-ACK"},{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"handshake","label":"ACK"}],"metrics":{"setup":"2","conns":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"edge","text":"TCP = 1 RTT"},{"node":"edge","text":"TCP + TLS ≈ 100 ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Inside the TLS 1.3 handshake","body":"In TLS 1.3 the client's first message already carries its half of a key exchange. The server replies with its half, its certificate and a Finished message, and both sides can now work out the same secret keys. The certificate proves the server owns the domain, so you are not talking to an impostor. The client then sends its own Finished together with the first HTTP request. On a repeat visit, a resumption ticket can allow 0-RTT (data sent in the very first message). An attacker can replay that data, so use it only for idempotent requests, ones that are harmless to repeat, like GET.","idea":"TLS 1.3 needs one round trip. Resumption can skip it, but only for replay-safe requests.","caption":"Keys agreed in a single round trip.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"handshake","label":"ClientHello+key"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"handshake","label":"ServerHello+cert"},{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"Finished + GET /"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"edge","text":"cert ok","tone":"green"},{"node":"edge","text":"TLS 1.3 = 1 RTT","at":"bottom"},{"node":"browser","text":"0-RTT: replayable","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"KEEP-ALIVE","title":"HTTP/1.1 reuses the connection","body":"HTTP/1.1 keeps the TCP connection open after a response (keep-alive), so later requests skip the handshakes. But one connection carries one request at a time: the next request waits for the previous response. With 31 requests of 50 ms each on one connection, the page needs 100 ms of setup plus 31 × 50 ms, about 1,650 ms. The animation shows requests going out one after another.","idea":"Keep-alive removes repeated handshakes, but HTTP/1.1 still handles one request per connection at a time.","caption":"31 requests queue one after another on one connection.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"GET /app.css"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"200 app.css"},{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"GET /app.js"}],"metrics":{"conns":"1","load":"1650"},"chips":[{"node":"browser","text":"1 connection"},{"node":"browser","text":"≈ 1650 ms","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"edge","text":"1 request at a time"}]},{"tag":"HOL BLOCKING","title":"Head-of-line blocking and the six-connection hack","body":"Head-of-line blocking means one slow item holds up everything queued behind it, like one slow person at the front of a single checkout line. On an HTTP/1.1 connection, a 2-second API call stalls every request after it, because responses must come back in order. Browsers work around this by opening about six connections per host. Time: 100 ms setup, 50 ms for the HTML, then 30 assets in 5 rounds of 6 (250 ms), so about 400 ms, assuming the extra connections open early. The price is six handshakes and six sets of server memory.","idea":"HTTP/1.1 fights head-of-line blocking by multiplying connections, which is costly.","caption":"Six connections share the 31 requests.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"handshake","label":"6 connections"},{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"6 requests at once"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"6 responses"}],"metrics":{"conns":"6","load":"400"},"chips":[{"node":"browser","text":"6 connections"},{"node":"browser","text":"≈ 400 ms","at":"bottom"},{"node":"edge","text":"HOL: 2 s stall","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"POOLING","title":"Pools behind the proxy","body":"The same idea applies behind the proxy. The edge proxy keeps a pool of connections to the app server and the auth service that are already open and encrypted. It borrows one for each request instead of dialing a new one. Inside a data center a round trip is only about 0.5 ms, so a new handshake costs roughly 1 ms plus CPU for the cryptography. At thousands of requests per second that still adds up and can exhaust ports. Give pools a size limit and an idle timeout shorter than the server's, or you may send requests on connections the server already closed.","idea":"A connection pool pays the setup cost once and shares it. Size it and expire it deliberately.","caption":"The proxy reuses warm connections to its backends.","focus":["edge","app","api"],"flows":[{"from":"edge","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"reused conn"},{"from":"edge","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"reused conn"},{"from":"app","to":"edge","kind":"response","label":"200"},{"from":"api","to":"edge","kind":"response","label":"200"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"app","text":"pool · reused","tone":"green"},{"node":"api","text":"pool · reused","tone":"green"},{"node":"edge","text":"~1 ms handshake","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"HTTP/2","title":"HTTP/2 multiplexes streams","body":"HTTP/2 splits traffic into small frames, each tagged with a stream ID, so many requests and responses interleave on one TCP connection. The browser sends all 30 asset requests at once instead of waiting in line. Time: 100 ms setup, 50 ms for the HTML, 50 ms for the burst of assets, about 200 ms. It uses a single connection and compresses headers. This fixes head-of-line blocking at the HTTP layer.","idea":"Multiplexing lets one connection carry many independent requests at the same time.","caption":"31 streams interleave on one connection.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"30 streams"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"interleaved frames"}],"metrics":{"conns":"1","load":"200"},"chips":[{"node":"browser","text":"30 streams"},{"node":"browser","text":"≈ 200 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"edge","text":"1 TCP connection"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"One lost packet stalls every stream","body":"HTTP/2 still rides on TCP, which delivers bytes strictly in order. If one packet is lost, TCP holds back everything after it, even frames of unaffected streams, until the retransmission (a resend) arrives. On clean wired networks this is rare. On lossy mobile links with 1 to 2 percent loss it happens often, and HTTP/2 can lose most of its advantage. Six separate HTTP/1.1 connections suffer less, because a drop stalls only the connection it hit. The 250 ms shown is a single-loss example (200 + 50 for one extra round trip). More losses add more.","idea":"HTTP/2 removes blocking in HTTP, but TCP's in-order delivery brings it back at the transport layer.","caption":"One dropped packet freezes every stream on the connection.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"fail","label":"packet lost"},{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"dup ACKs"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"replicate","label":"retransmit"}],"metrics":{"load":"250"},"chips":[{"node":"edge","text":"packet lost","tone":"red"},{"node":"browser","text":"all streams stall","tone":"red"},{"node":"browser","text":"250 ms (1 loss)","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"HTTP/3","title":"HTTP/3 and QUIC","body":"HTTP/3 runs over QUIC, which is built on UDP (a bare-bones transport with no ordering or retransmission of its own). QUIC adds reliability, encryption and streams itself. Its transport and TLS 1.3 handshakes are combined, so setup takes 1 round trip instead of 2. Each stream recovers losses on its own, so a lost packet delays only the streams whose data it carried. Time: 50 ms setup + 50 ms HTML + 50 ms assets = 150 ms. A first visit usually starts on TCP and learns about HTTP/3 from an Alt-Svc header. Browsers fall back to TCP if UDP is blocked.","idea":"QUIC cuts setup to 1 round trip and gives each stream its own loss recovery.","caption":"One-round-trip setup. Each stream recovers from loss separately.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"handshake","label":"QUIC Initial+TLS"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"handshake","label":"keys + cert"},{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"30 streams"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"independent"}],"metrics":{"setup":"1","conns":"1","load":"150"},"chips":[{"node":"browser","text":"setup 1 RTT"},{"node":"browser","text":"≈ 150 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"edge","text":"QUIC over UDP"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Fewer round trips, less blocking","body":"Every new connection costs round trips, so reuse them: keep-alive for clients, pools behind the proxy. HTTP/1.1 sends one request at a time per connection. HTTP/2 multiplexes many streams over one TCP connection. HTTP/3 uses QUIC to remove blocking at the TCP level too. TLS 1.3 is the modern default (and required by HTTP/3). The numbers are idealized, but on healthy networks the ordering usually holds.","idea":"Reuse connections, multiplex requests, and avoid one in-order pipe shared by unrelated streams.","caption":"Same page: HTTP/1.1 1650 ms (400 with 6 connections), HTTP/2 200 ms, HTTP/3 150 ms.","focus":["browser","edge","app","api"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"setup":"1","conns":"1","load":"150"},"chips":[{"node":"browser","text":"H/1.1 ≈ 1650 ms","tone":"red"},{"node":"browser","text":"H/2 ≈ 200 ms","at":"bottom"},{"node":"edge","text":"H/3 ≈ 150 ms","tone":"green"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A server is 40 ms away (round trip). How long before the first HTTP request can be sent on a brand-new TCP + TLS 1.3 connection?","options":["About 40 ms","About 80 ms","About 120 ms","About 160 ms"],"answer":1,"why":"TCP needs one round trip and TLS 1.3 needs one more, so 2 x 40 ms = 80 ms. TLS 1.2 would add a third round trip."},{"q":"Why does HTTP/1.1 keep-alive alone not make a 30-asset page fast?","options":["Keep-alive only works for HTML","Keep-alive disables encryption","The server closes the connection after each response","Each connection still handles one request at a time, so requests queue"],"answer":3,"why":"Keep-alive removes repeated handshakes, but responses on a connection come back in order, so requests wait on each other."},{"q":"On a lossy mobile network, one packet is dropped during an HTTP/2 page load. What happens?","options":["Only the stream owning that packet waits","HTTP/2 automatically opens a second connection","All streams on the TCP connection stall until the retransmission arrives","The browser switches to HTTP/1.1 immediately"],"answer":2,"why":"TCP delivers bytes in order, so a gap blocks everything behind it, including unrelated streams. QUIC avoids this with per-stream recovery."},{"q":"Your proxy sometimes fails requests with 'connection reset' right after sitting idle. The app server closes idle connections after 60 s. What is the best fix?","options":["Set the proxy's pool idle timeout below 60 s","Disable the connection pool","Switch the proxy to HTTP/1.0","Raise the app server's timeout to 24 hours"],"answer":0,"why":"If the proxy holds a pooled connection longer than the server does, it can send on a connection already closed. Expiring idle connections first avoids that race."},{"q":"Which statement about HTTP/3 is correct?","options":["It runs over TCP with a faster handshake","It runs over QUIC on UDP and recovers lost packets per stream","It removes the need for TLS","It requires one connection per request"],"answer":1,"why":"HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP, with TLS 1.3 built in and independent loss recovery per stream."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Handshake cost","def":"TCP 1 RTT + TLS 1.3 1 RTT = 2 RTTs before the first request. TLS 1.2 adds a third. QUIC combines both into 1 RTT."},{"term":"Keep-alive","def":"Leaving a connection open for more requests, so later ones skip TCP and TLS setup. Default in HTTP/1.1."},{"term":"Head-of-line blocking","def":"A slow or lost item delays everything queued behind it. It occurs at the HTTP layer (1.1) and the TCP layer (2)."},{"term":"HTTP/2 multiplexing","def":"Many request/response streams interleaved as frames on one TCP connection, with header compression (HPACK)."},{"term":"HTTP/3 / QUIC","def":"HTTP over UDP-based QUIC: built-in TLS 1.3, per-stream loss recovery, connection IDs that survive network changes."},{"term":"Connection pool","def":"A set of warm connections reused across requests. Tune max size and set idle timeout below the peer's timeout."},{"term":"0-RTT resumption","def":"Sending data in the first flight on a repeat visit. Replayable by attackers, so use only for idempotent requests."},{"term":"Alt-Svc","def":"Header (or DNS HTTPS record) telling clients that HTTP/3 is available; clients fall back to TCP if UDP fails."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"hld-lld","title":"High-Level vs Low-Level Design","subtitle":"Walk a design from vague idea to boxes, API, data model and one component's internals, using a link shortener.","minutes":12,"level":"Beginner","learn":["The order a design process follows: requirements, estimates, high-level boxes, API, data model, deep dive","How a back-of-envelope estimate shapes the architecture before you draw anything","What high-level design (HLD) decides versus what low-level design (LLD) decides","How to design one component in detail, including its failure case","Why every added box (cache, queue) is a trade-off you should be able to defend"],"nodes":[{"id":"user","label":"Users","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"gw","label":"API gateway","kind":"gateway","x":29,"y":50},{"id":"svc","label":"Link service","kind":"service","x":52,"y":50},{"id":"cache","label":"Cache","kind":"cache","x":76,"y":20},{"id":"db","label":"Link database","kind":"db","x":76,"y":50},{"id":"q","label":"Click queue","kind":"queue","x":52,"y":84},{"id":"worker","label":"Click counter","kind":"worker","x":80,"y":84}],"edges":[{"from":"user","to":"gw"},{"from":"gw","to":"svc"},{"from":"svc","to":"cache"},{"from":"svc","to":"db"},{"from":"svc","to":"q"},{"from":"q","to":"worker"},{"from":"worker","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"writes","label":"Writes","unit":"/sec"},{"id":"reads","label":"Reads","unit":"/sec"},{"id":"storage","label":"5-yr storage","unit":"TB"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"\"Design a link shortener\"","body":"You are handed one sentence: build something like a short-link service. That is not yet a design. It does not say how big it is, who uses it, or what matters most. A good process turns the vague sentence into decisions in a fixed order. It starts wide (high-level design, HLD: the big boxes) and ends narrow (low-level design, LLD: the inside of one box). We follow that order.","idea":"Design is narrowing: the broad shape first, details only after the shape is agreed.","caption":"One vague sentence and no boxes yet.","focus":["user"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[]},{"tag":"REQUIREMENTS","title":"Ask before you draw","body":"Start by asking questions. Functional requirements say what the system does: create a short link, and redirect when someone visits it. Non-functional requirements say how well it must do it: redirects should feel instant, links must not break, and one server failing must not take the service down. Also agree on what is out of scope, such as custom domains or user accounts, so you do not design forever.","idea":"Requirements decide the design. Unstated requirements become surprises later.","caption":"Scope agreed with the person asking.","focus":["user"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"user","text":"create + redirect"},{"node":"user","text":"redirect = instant"},{"node":"user","text":"out: custom domains","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"ESTIMATES","title":"Size it with napkin math","body":"Assume 100 million new links a month, and 100 redirects for every link created. That is about 40 writes per second and 4,000 reads per second on average, perhaps 12,000 at peak. Over five years that is about 6 billion links (100M × 60 months). At roughly 500 bytes each, that is about 3 TB. These are rules of thumb, but they already show that the system is read-heavy and fits on a small database cluster.","idea":"A few minutes of arithmetic tell you what to optimize: here, reads, not storage.","caption":"Read-heavy, with modest storage.","focus":["svc","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"writes":"40","reads":"4000","storage":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"svc","text":"40 writes/s"},{"node":"svc","text":"4k reads/s","at":"bottom"},{"node":"db","text":"6B links · 3 TB"}]},{"tag":"HLD","title":"Draw the boxes","body":"Now sketch the high-level design. Users talk to an API gateway, a single front door that checks and routes requests. The gateway forwards to a stateless link service (stateless means it keeps nothing between requests, so you can add or remove copies freely). The service stores links in a database. Keep it to boxes and arrows. HLD answers which major parts exist and how they talk, not how each works inside. Add more boxes only when the numbers demand it.","idea":"HLD is the map: components and connections, with no internals yet.","caption":"Minimum viable architecture: gateway, service, database.","focus":["user","gw","svc","db"],"flows":[{"from":"user","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"request"},{"from":"gw","to":"svc","kind":"request","label":"route"},{"from":"svc","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"read/write"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"svc","text":"stateless"},{"node":"db","text":"stores links"}]},{"tag":"API","title":"Define the contract","body":"Next, write the API, the contract between the boxes. POST /links takes a long URL and returns a short code. GET /{code} looks the code up and answers 301 or 302 with the long URL in the Location header, and the browser then goes there. A 302 keeps every click visible to you. A 301 lets browsers cache the redirect and skip you next time, so you would miss those clicks.","idea":"Fix the interfaces early. They are hard to change once clients depend on them.","caption":"Create a link, then follow it.","focus":["user","gw","svc"],"flows":[{"from":"user","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"POST /links"},{"from":"gw","to":"svc","kind":"request","label":"create"},{"from":"svc","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"code: aB3xK9"},{"from":"gw","to":"user","kind":"response","label":"201 Created"},{"from":"user","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /aB3xK9"},{"from":"gw","to":"svc","kind":"request","label":"lookup"},{"from":"svc","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"302 + Location"},{"from":"gw","to":"user","kind":"response","label":"302 redirect"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"svc","text":"code: aB3xK9"},{"node":"gw","text":"201 Created","tone":"green"},{"node":"gw","text":"302 + Location","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"DATA MODEL","title":"Decide what is stored","body":"The data model says what you store and how you look it up. A link has a code, a long URL, a creation time and an owner. Every read asks one question: give me the URL for this code. So the code is the primary key (the unique lookup field), and a simple key-value lookup is enough. Knowing the access pattern first is what lets you pick a sensible database.","idea":"Choose storage from the access pattern, not from habit.","caption":"Single-key lookups: code to long URL.","focus":["svc","db"],"flows":[{"from":"svc","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"put aB3xK9"},{"from":"db","to":"svc","kind":"response","label":"stored"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"PK = code"},{"node":"db","text":"key-value lookup","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"LLD","title":"Zoom into one component","body":"Low-level design opens one box. Take code generation inside the link service. Seven characters from a 62-symbol alphabet (letters and digits) give about 3.5 trillion codes, far more than 6 billion links. But if the service picks random codes, two links can still get the same code (a collision). So it inserts only if the code is absent, and retries with a new code on a collision. LLD is classes, algorithms and edge cases like this.","idea":"LLD is the inside of a box: algorithms, data structures and the awkward edge cases.","caption":"Collision detected, so the service retries with a new code.","focus":["svc","db"],"flows":[{"from":"svc","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"insert if absent"},{"from":"db","to":"svc","kind":"fail","label":"code taken"},{"from":"svc","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"retry new code"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"svc","text":"3.5T codes"},{"node":"db","text":"code taken","tone":"red"},{"node":"svc","text":"retry new code","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Add a cache, pay for it","body":"At 4,000 reads per second, most traffic hits the same popular links. A cache (fast memory that holds recent answers) in front of the database absorbs most of that and makes redirects faster. The price is complexity. Cached data can be stale (out of date), and you must decide what happens when a link is deleted. Say that out loud. A box without a stated cost is a guess, not a design.","idea":"Every added component buys something and costs something. Name both.","caption":"Hot links are served from the cache.","focus":["svc","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"svc","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET aB3xK9"},{"from":"cache","to":"svc","kind":"response","label":"hit: long URL"}],"metrics":{"reads":"4000"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"hit","tone":"green"},{"node":"cache","text":"can go stale","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"svc","text":"4,000 reads/s"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Count clicks off the hot path","body":"Owners want click counts. Writing a counter to the database on every redirect would slow the fast path and add up to 4,000 writes per second. Instead, the service puts a click event on a queue (a waiting line of messages) and returns the redirect immediately. A worker reads the queue and updates the counts in batches. The cost: counts run a few seconds behind, which our requirements allow.","idea":"Move non-urgent work to a queue so the user-facing path stays fast.","caption":"Redirect first, count later.","focus":["svc","q","worker","db"],"flows":[{"from":"svc","to":"q","kind":"async","label":"click event"},{"from":"q","to":"worker","kind":"async","label":"batch"},{"from":"worker","to":"db","kind":"async","label":"add counts"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"q","text":"click event"},{"node":"worker","text":"counts lag a few s"},{"node":"db","text":"batched, not per click","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Wide first, then deep","body":"The order was: requirements, estimates, HLD boxes, API, data model, a deep dive into one component, then trade-offs. HLD decides what the parts are and how they connect. LLD decides how one part works inside. Later lessons cover numbers, caching and queues in depth. Here the point is the sequence, and defending each choice with a reason.","idea":"Decide the shape first, the details second, and justify every box.","caption":"The finished design, built in layers.","focus":["user","gw","svc","cache","db","q","worker"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"writes":"40","reads":"4000","storage":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"svc","text":"40 w/s · 4k r/s"},{"node":"db","text":"3 TB / 5 yr"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why do you do rough estimates before drawing the architecture?","options":["Interviewers require numbers on the whiteboard","They show what the system must optimize for, such as read-heavy traffic","They let you skip asking requirements questions","They fix the exact database vendor"],"answer":1,"why":"Estimates reveal the shape of the load (read-heavy, small storage), which decides which components are worth adding. They do not replace requirements or pick vendors."},{"q":"Which question belongs to low-level design rather than high-level design?","options":["Should we put a cache between the service and the database?","Which services does the gateway route to?","How does the code generator avoid or handle two links getting the same code?","Is the system read-heavy or write-heavy?"],"answer":2,"why":"Handling collisions is an algorithm inside one component. The other options are about which components exist or the overall load shape."},{"q":"A teammate adds a cache to the design but says nothing else. What is the most important thing missing?","options":["The cache's brand name","A diagram with colors","What it costs: staleness, invalidation and extra failure modes","A second cache for backup"],"answer":2,"why":"Every component is a trade-off. A cache speeds reads but can serve stale data, so a good design states when it may be wrong and how that is handled."},{"q":"Click counts may lag by a few seconds. How does that requirement shape the design?","options":["Counting can go through a queue, keeping redirects fast","Counts must be written to the database before redirecting","Counting needs a second database region","Redirects should use 301 instead of 302"],"answer":0,"why":"Because slightly late counts are acceptable, the service can enqueue an event and respond at once. A strict real-time requirement would push you toward synchronous writes."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Functional requirement","def":"What the system does, such as create a short link or redirect to the long URL."},{"term":"Non-functional requirement","def":"How well it does it: latency, availability, durability, scale, consistency."},{"term":"HLD","def":"High-level design: the major components, how they connect, and what each is responsible for. No internals."},{"term":"LLD","def":"Low-level design: the inside of one component, its classes, algorithms, data structures and edge cases."},{"term":"Access pattern","def":"How the data is read and written. Decide this before choosing a database or schema."},{"term":"Order of attack","def":"Requirements, estimates, HLD boxes, API, data model, deep dive on one part, then trade-offs and failure cases."},{"term":"Trade-off","def":"Every component gains something and costs something. State both, plus when your choice would flip."}],"isNew":false}]},{"id":"scaling","title":"Scaling","lessons":[{"slug":"vertical-horizontal","title":"Vertical vs Horizontal Scaling","subtitle":"Watch one overloaded server get fixed two ways, a bigger box or more boxes, and see what each choice costs.","minutes":10,"level":"Beginner","learn":["The difference between scaling up (vertical) and scaling out (horizontal)","Why a single big machine has both a hard ceiling and a single point of failure","How adding servers behind a load balancer raises capacity and survives failures","Why cost per request served often rises at the top of the instance range","When a bigger box is the right and simplest answer, especially for databases"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"Users","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":32,"y":50},{"id":"s1","label":"Server 1 (4 vCPU)","kind":"server","x":57,"y":14},{"id":"s2","label":"Server 2 (4 vCPU)","kind":"server","x":57,"y":36},{"id":"s3","label":"Server 3 (4 vCPU)","kind":"server","x":57,"y":58},{"id":"big","label":"Big server (16 vCPU)","kind":"server","x":57,"y":84},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":88,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"lb"},{"from":"users","to":"big"},{"from":"users","to":"s1"},{"from":"lb","to":"s1"},{"from":"lb","to":"s2"},{"from":"lb","to":"s3"},{"from":"s1","to":"db"},{"from":"s2","to":"db"},{"from":"s3","to":"db"},{"from":"big","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"cap","label":"Capacity","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"cpu","label":"Peak CPU","unit":"%"},{"id":"cost","label":"App cost","unit":"$/mo"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One server is running hot","body":"Picture your shop on one server with 4 vCPUs (CPU cores). For this lesson we assume it can handle about 800 requests per second, and it costs about $150 a month. Traffic has doubled and now peaks at 680, so the CPU is 85 percent busy (680 out of 800). That is risky: delays start to grow well before 100 percent, and one more busy week would cause queues and timeouts. You have two basic fixes: a bigger machine, or more machines.","idea":"To fix a capacity problem you either make the machine bigger or add more machines.","caption":"680 req/s are arriving at a server that tops out at 800.","focus":["users","s1"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"s1","kind":"request","label":"680 req/s"},{"from":"s1","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"queries"}],"metrics":{"cap":"800","cpu":"85","cost":"150"},"chips":[{"node":"s1","kind":"progress","value":85,"text":"85%"},{"node":"s1","text":"800 req/s max","at":"bottom"},{"node":"users","text":"680 req/s"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Scale up: a bigger box","body":"Scale up means swapping in a bigger machine: more CPU cores, more RAM, faster disks. The animation sends the same 680 requests per second to one 16 vCPU server. Its capacity rises to about 2,800 requests per second (a bit under 4x, because busy cores get in each other's way), so CPU drops to 24 percent. At these sizes the cloud price grows roughly with size, so the bill goes from $150 to about $600 a month. Your code does not change at all.","idea":"Scaling up is the simplest fix because the application stays exactly the same.","caption":"The same traffic now lands on one much larger server.","focus":["users","big","db"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"big","kind":"request","label":"680 req/s"},{"from":"big","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"queries"}],"metrics":{"cap":"2800","cpu":"24","cost":"600"},"chips":[{"node":"big","kind":"progress","value":24,"text":"24%"},{"node":"big","text":"2,800 req/s"},{"node":"big","text":"$600 / mo","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"The limits of one machine","body":"Keep growing the single machine and you hit walls. Imagine a 64 vCPU server: about 9,000 requests per second, not the 12,800 you would expect from 16 times the cores, because cores slow each other down. It costs about $2,400 a month, so every request you serve gets more expensive. Resizing usually needs a restart. Worst of all, it is still one machine: if it dies, the whole site is down. That is called a single point of failure (SPOF).","idea":"Scaling up has a hard ceiling, a rising cost per request, and no protection if the machine fails.","caption":"Even a 64 vCPU box is one crash away from an outage.","focus":["users","big"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"big","kind":"fail","label":"if it crashes"}],"metrics":{"cap":"9000","cpu":"8","cost":"2400"},"chips":[{"node":"big","text":"64 vCPU"},{"node":"big","text":"9,000 req/s","at":"bottom"},{"node":"big","text":"$2,400 / mo","at":"right"},{"node":"big","text":"one box = SPOF","tone":"red","at":"top"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Scale out: more boxes","body":"Scale out means adding more ordinary machines and sharing the work between them. Now a load balancer (a traffic director) sits in front of three 4 vCPU servers and sends each request to one of them. Together they handle about 2,400 requests per second, for roughly $450 of servers plus $20 for the balancer. Need more later? Add a fourth server for another 800. The catch: your app must work no matter which server answers.","idea":"Scale out adds capacity in small steps by putting ordinary machines behind a load balancer.","caption":"A load balancer now spreads requests over three servers.","focus":["users","lb","s1","s2","s3"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"680 req/s"},{"from":"lb","to":"s1","kind":"request"},{"from":"lb","to":"s2","kind":"request"},{"from":"lb","to":"s3","kind":"request"}],"metrics":{"cap":"2400","cpu":"28","cost":"470"},"chips":[{"node":"lb","text":"2,400 req/s"},{"node":"s1","text":"800 req/s"},{"node":"lb","text":"$470 / mo","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Work is shared","body":"The 680 requests per second are split three ways, about 227 per server, so each server is only about 28 percent busy. All three read and write the same database, which is where shared data lives. This only works if servers keep no personal user data in their own memory. Otherwise the next request might reach a server that has never met that user. The stateless lesson covers that fix, and the load balancing lesson covers the sharing rules.","idea":"Scale out works when any server can handle any request, so user data must live outside the servers.","caption":"Each server gets about a third of the traffic; all share one database.","focus":["lb","s1","s2","s3","db"],"flows":[{"from":"lb","to":"s1","kind":"request","label":"227 req/s"},{"from":"lb","to":"s2","kind":"request","label":"227 req/s"},{"from":"lb","to":"s3","kind":"request","label":"227 req/s"},{"from":"s1","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"queries"},{"from":"s2","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"queries"},{"from":"s3","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"queries"},{"from":"db","to":"s1","kind":"response"},{"from":"db","to":"s2","kind":"response"},{"from":"db","to":"s3","kind":"response"}],"metrics":{"cap":"2400","cpu":"28"},"chips":[{"node":"s1","text":"227 req/s"},{"node":"s2","kind":"progress","value":28,"text":"28%"},{"node":"s3","text":"227 req/s"},{"node":"db","text":"shared data"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"One server dies","body":"Now server 2 crashes. The load balancer's health checks (small regular test requests) notice, and it stops sending traffic there. The two survivors can still handle about 1,600 requests per second, far above your 680, so each takes 340 and sits at about 43 percent CPU. Users barely notice, apart from a few failed requests before detection. Keeping spare capacity beyond your peak, called N+1, turns a hardware failure into a non-event. The single big server could not do this.","idea":"Many small servers survive failures, and spare capacity is what makes that survival real.","caption":"Server 2 fails; the other two split its traffic.","focus":["lb","s1","s2","s3"],"flows":[{"from":"lb","to":"s2","kind":"fail","label":"unhealthy"},{"from":"lb","to":"s1","kind":"request","label":"340 req/s"},{"from":"lb","to":"s3","kind":"request","label":"340 req/s"}],"metrics":{"cap":"1600","cpu":"43"},"chips":[{"node":"s2","text":"crashed","tone":"red"},{"node":"lb","text":"cap 1,600 req/s","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"s1","text":"340 req/s"},{"node":"s3","text":"340 req/s"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"What scale out costs you","body":"Scaling out moves the difficulty from hardware to software and operations. You now need a load balancer, health checks, deployment to many machines, and monitoring for all of them. Each added server also adds some overhead, so gains are slightly under linear. The bigger issue is the database. The app tier can now take 3 times the traffic, but all servers share one database, and each server keeps its own pool of open database connections. More servers means more connections, and a database has a connection limit. Splitting data is much harder than adding stateless servers.","idea":"Scaling out adds complexity, and the shared database becomes the next bottleneck.","caption":"Three servers all feed one database, which is nearing its connection limit.","focus":["s1","s2","s3","db"],"flows":[{"from":"s1","to":"db","kind":"request"},{"from":"s2","to":"db","kind":"request"},{"from":"s3","to":"db","kind":"request"},{"from":"db","to":"s1","kind":"fail","label":"pool limit near"}],"metrics":{"cap":"2400","cpu":"28"},"chips":[{"node":"s1","text":"own conn pool"},{"node":"s2","text":"own conn pool"},{"node":"s3","text":"own conn pool"},{"node":"db","text":"limit near","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"When the bigger box wins","body":"A relational database is the classic case for scaling up. It keeps data consistent in one place, and splitting it across machines is hard. So the usual first move is a bigger instance with more RAM, so frequently used data stays in memory, which is fast. Small products also do well with one box: at 680 requests per second, one mid-size server, maybe with a standby, is simpler to run than a fleet. Scale up first while it is cheap and easy, then scale out when you hit the ceiling or need failure tolerance.","idea":"Scale up first for databases and small systems; scale out when you need failure tolerance or outgrow one box.","caption":"Make the database bigger first; its hot data stays in RAM.","focus":["s1","db"],"flows":[{"from":"s1","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"queries"},{"from":"db","to":"s1","kind":"response","label":"rows from RAM"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"bigger instance"},{"node":"db","text":"hot data in RAM","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"s1","text":"stateless app"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick by the constraint","body":"Scaling up is simple and needs no code change, but it has a ceiling, a rising cost per request at the top, and one point of failure. Scaling out adds capacity in small steps and survives failures, but needs stateless servers, a load balancer, and a plan for shared data. Most real systems do both: they scale the database up first (adding read replicas, extra copies used for reading, later) and scale the stateless app servers out.","idea":"Scale up for simplicity, scale out for headroom and resilience, and combine them.","caption":"Both paths side by side: one big box, or a pool of servers.","focus":["users","lb","s1","s2","s3","big","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"cap":"2400","cpu":"28","cost":"470"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"scale up"},{"node":"s2","text":"scale out"},{"node":"big","text":"ceiling · SPOF","tone":"red"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which statement best describes the main weakness of scaling up a single server, even a very large one?","options":["It always requires rewriting the application","It has a hard ceiling and remains a single point of failure","It cannot use more than 4 CPU cores","It makes the database slower by definition"],"answer":1,"why":"A bigger box needs no code change, but there is a largest machine you can buy, and if that one machine fails everything it serves goes down."},{"q":"You run three servers, each at 75 percent CPU at peak. One fails. What is the most likely result?","options":["Nothing changes because load balancers add capacity","The other two run near 90 percent, which is tight but workable","The other two need about 112 percent each, so they overload and requests fail","The failed server's traffic is automatically dropped"],"answer":2,"why":"Three servers at 75 percent carry 2.25 servers' worth of work. Two servers can only supply 2.0, so they cannot keep up. This is why N+1 headroom matters: size the fleet so peak load still fits after losing one."},{"q":"Your stateless web tier is at its limit and you must keep serving if one machine fails; your single database is at 40 percent CPU with room to spare. What is the sensible first move?","options":["Add more web servers behind the load balancer","Shard the database across four servers","Replace the web tier with one very large server","Switch the database to a different vendor"],"answer":0,"why":"Several stateless servers give more capacity and survive a single failure; one big server would still be a single point of failure, and the database has headroom so sharding adds complexity for nothing."},{"q":"A team wants to scale a primary relational database for the next year with minimal risk. Why is a larger instance often a good first choice?","options":["Databases cannot run on more than one machine ever","Bigger instances are always cheaper per core","Scaling out is impossible in the cloud","It keeps data in one place with no new consistency problems and needs no application changes"],"answer":3,"why":"A single primary avoids splitting data and the coordination problems that follow. Scaling up is often the simplest path until you outgrow it, though replicas and sharding exist for later."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Vertical scaling (scale up)","def":"Make one machine bigger with more CPU, RAM, or faster disks. Simple, no code change, but has a ceiling and one point of failure."},{"term":"Horizontal scaling (scale out)","def":"Add more machines and spread work across them. Grows in small steps and tolerates failures, but needs stateless design and a load balancer."},{"term":"Single point of failure","def":"A component whose failure takes the whole system down. One big server is the typical example."},{"term":"N+1 headroom","def":"Keep enough spare capacity that losing any one server still leaves you above peak load. A rule of thumb, not a guarantee."},{"term":"Cost curve","def":"Cloud price per vCPU is roughly linear with size, but capacity gains flatten at the top, so cost per request rises. Specialty and on-prem top-end hardware can also cost more per core."},{"term":"Default order of operations","def":"Scale up first while it is cheap and simple. Scale out the stateless tier when you need headroom or resilience. Scale databases out last (read replicas first, sharding last)."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"stateless","title":"Stateless Services","subtitle":"Watch a login break when a request reaches the wrong server, then see how moving session data out of the servers fixes it.","minutes":9,"level":"Beginner","learn":["What state a server holds between requests and why that blocks scaling","Why round-robin load balancing logs users out of stateful servers","How sticky sessions work and what they cost you","Two ways to go stateless: a shared session store and signed tokens","Why stateless servers can be added, removed or killed freely"],"nodes":[{"id":"browser","label":"Browser","kind":"user","x":9,"y":50},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":32,"y":50},{"id":"appa","label":"App server A","kind":"server","x":57,"y":22},{"id":"appb","label":"App server B","kind":"server","x":57,"y":78},{"id":"sessions","label":"Session store","kind":"cache","x":84,"y":22},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":84,"y":78}],"edges":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb"},{"from":"lb","to":"appa"},{"from":"lb","to":"appb"},{"from":"appa","to":"sessions"},{"from":"appb","to":"sessions"},{"from":"appa","to":"db"},{"from":"appb","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"servers","label":"App servers","unit":"up"},{"id":"failed","label":"Users re-login","unit":"%"},{"id":"lookup","label":"Session read","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Why did my login vanish?","body":"You log in to a shop and fill your cart. A minute later you see the login page again and your cart is empty. Your password was fine. What happened is that a different server answered, or the server that knew you was replaced. This lesson is about what that server was remembering, and where it should have kept it instead.","idea":"If your login only works on the one server that handled it, scaling out and server failures both break it.","caption":"The request reaches a server that does not know you.","focus":["browser","lb","appa","appb"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart"},{"from":"lb","to":"appb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart"},{"from":"appb","to":"lb","kind":"fail","label":"302 to /login"}],"metrics":{"servers":"2","failed":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"browser","text":"logged out","tone":"red"},{"node":"browser","text":"cart gone","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"State lives in server memory","body":"HTTP forgets you between requests, so apps use a session: a small record such as \"user 42, cart with 3 items\", stored under a random ID. Here you log in and the request lands on server A. A keeps the session in its own memory and sends back the ID as a cookie (a small value your browser attaches to every later request). The browser holds only the ID, not the data. A now holds data that the next request depends on, so A is called stateful.","idea":"A stateful server remembers something between requests that no other server knows.","caption":"Login lands on A, so the session exists only in A's memory.","focus":["browser","lb","appa"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"POST /login"},{"from":"lb","to":"appa","kind":"request","label":"POST /login"},{"from":"appa","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"Set-Cookie sid=9f2"},{"from":"lb","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"sid=9f2"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"appa","text":"sess 9f2 in RAM"},{"node":"browser","text":"cookie sid=9f2"},{"node":"appa","text":"user 42 · 3 items","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"PROBLEM","title":"Round robin hits the wrong server","body":"Your next click goes to server B, because the load balancer is taking turns (round robin). B receives your cookie sid=9f2 but has never seen that ID, so it treats you as logged out and replies 401. With two servers about half your requests miss; with ten servers about nine in ten do. Adding servers made the site worse, which is the opposite of what scaling should do.","idea":"When state is kept per server, more servers means more requests reaching a server that does not know the user.","caption":"Server B has no record of sid=9f2 and rejects the request.","focus":["lb","appb"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart sid=9f2"},{"from":"lb","to":"appb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart"},{"from":"appb","to":"lb","kind":"fail","label":"401 login again"}],"metrics":{"failed":"50"},"chips":[{"node":"appb","text":"sid unknown","tone":"red"},{"node":"lb","text":"50% miss","tone":"red"},{"node":"lb","text":"round robin","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Sticky sessions: a patch","body":"A common patch is sticky sessions: the load balancer remembers that sid=9f2 belongs to A (using a cookie or a hash of the client IP) and always sends you there. That works, but it has costs. Load can become uneven when heavy users pile onto one server. Deploys must drain each server slowly. And if A crashes, as shown, every user pinned to A loses their session, because A held the only copy.","idea":"Sticky sessions hide the problem but keep it: the server still holds the only copy, so if it dies the data is lost.","caption":"Pinned to A, requests work, until A crashes and its sessions disappear.","focus":["lb","appa"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart sid=9f2"},{"from":"lb","to":"appa","kind":"request","label":"pinned to A"},{"from":"lb","to":"appa","kind":"fail","label":"A crashed"}],"metrics":{"servers":"1","failed":"50"},"chips":[{"node":"lb","text":"sid 9f2 → A"},{"node":"appa","text":"crashed","tone":"red"},{"node":"appa","text":"pinned users lost","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FIX","title":"Move sessions to a shared store","body":"The real fix is to take the session out of the server. Any app server writes it to a fast shared store such as Redis or Memcached, filed under the session ID. In the animation A (running again after its crash) saves the session there with an expiry of 30 minutes since last use. Because every server talks to the same store, no server holds anything the others lack.","idea":"Move the state out: servers keep the logic, and a shared store keeps the memory.","caption":"A writes the session to the shared store at login.","focus":["appa","sessions"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"sessions","kind":"request","label":"SET sess:9f2 30m"},{"from":"sessions","to":"appa","kind":"response","label":"OK"}],"metrics":{"servers":"2","failed":"0","lookup":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"sessions","text":"sess:9f2 saved","tone":"green"},{"node":"sessions","text":"expires 30 min","at":"bottom"},{"node":"appa","text":"holds no state","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Any server can answer","body":"Now your next click can land on B. B reads the cookie, fetches sess:9f2 from the store (about 1 ms on the same network), learns it is user 42, then asks the database for the cart and returns it. The load balancer can use plain round robin with no pinning. The price is one extra network hop per request, plus a new component to keep highly available, usually with a replica and automatic failover.","idea":"Stateless servers pay one cheap lookup per request, and in return the balancer can pick any server.","caption":"B looks up the session in the store, then serves the cart.","focus":["lb","appb","sessions","db"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart sid=9f2"},{"from":"lb","to":"appb","kind":"request","label":"any server"},{"from":"appb","to":"sessions","kind":"request","label":"GET sess:9f2"},{"from":"sessions","to":"appb","kind":"response","label":"user 42"},{"from":"appb","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT cart"},{"from":"db","to":"appb","kind":"response","label":"3 items"},{"from":"appb","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"200 cart"},{"from":"lb","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"200 cart"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"appb","text":"any server","tone":"green"},{"node":"sessions","text":"user 42"},{"node":"sessions","text":"~1 ms","at":"bottom"},{"node":"db","text":"3 items"}]},{"tag":"ALTERNATIVE","title":"Signed tokens skip the lookup","body":"Another route is to put the session data in the client. After login the server issues a signed token, often a JWT. It holds your user ID and an expiry time, plus a signature made with a key. Any server can check that signature in microseconds, so no store is needed at all. The costs: you cannot easily cancel one token before it expires, so keep tokens short-lived (say 15 minutes) with a refresh mechanism, and never put secrets inside, because anyone can read the contents.","idea":"Tokens trade easy revocation for needing no session storage on the server.","caption":"B checks the token's signature itself; no session store is read.","focus":["browser","lb","appb","db"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"Bearer eyJ..."},{"from":"lb","to":"appb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart + token"},{"from":"appb","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT cart"},{"from":"db","to":"appb","kind":"response","label":"3 items"},{"from":"appb","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"200 cart"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"browser","text":"JWT · 15 min"},{"node":"appb","text":"verify: µs"},{"node":"appb","text":"no store lookup","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"PAYOFF","title":"Kill a server, nobody notices","body":"With no state on the servers, they are interchangeable. In the animation A fails its health check (the balancer's regular test request), so the load balancer sends everything to B. B finds your session in the store, so you stay logged in. The same trick lets you deploy by replacing servers one at a time, and autoscale by adding or removing them freely. This is what makes servers disposable, and the next lessons build on it.","idea":"Stateless servers can be added, removed or killed at any time with no user impact.","caption":"A fails its health check; B serves the cart and the session survives.","focus":["lb","appa","appb","sessions"],"flows":[{"from":"lb","to":"appa","kind":"fail","label":"health check fail"},{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart sid=9f2"},{"from":"lb","to":"appb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart"},{"from":"appb","to":"sessions","kind":"request","label":"GET sess:9f2"},{"from":"sessions","to":"appb","kind":"response","label":"user 42"},{"from":"appb","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"200 cart"}],"metrics":{"servers":"1","failed":"0","lookup":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"appa","text":"health check fail","tone":"red"},{"node":"appb","text":"serves all","tone":"green"},{"node":"browser","text":"still logged in","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Keep state out of the servers","body":"State kept in a server's memory ties users to that server and is lost when it dies. Sticky sessions fix the routing but not the loss. A shared session store or signed tokens move the state out, so any server can answer any request. The state did not vanish: it now lives in a store you must keep fast and durable. Stateless means the app servers are disposable, not that the system has no data.","idea":"Stateless app servers scale and fail over easily; the state moves to a few well-protected places.","caption":"Servers hold logic only; sessions and data live in shared stores.","focus":["browser","lb","appa","appb","sessions","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"servers":"2","failed":"0","lookup":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"sessions","text":"durable + fast"},{"node":"appa","text":"disposable","tone":"green"},{"node":"appb","text":"disposable","tone":"green"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"You run 5 stateful servers with in-memory sessions and plain round robin. Roughly what share of a logged-in user's requests land on a server that does not know them?","options":["About 0%, round robin remembers users","About 20%","About 80%","About 100%"],"answer":2,"why":"Only the server that handled the login has the session. Each later request picks one of 5 servers evenly, so 4 in 5 (80%) miss."},{"q":"A team adds sticky sessions and calls the problem solved. Which failure still loses users' logins?","options":["A user opens the site in a second tab","The load balancer adds a sixth server","The server holding a user's session crashes","The user refreshes the page"],"answer":2,"why":"Stickiness only controls routing. The session still exists in one server's memory, so if that server dies or is replaced the session is gone."},{"q":"Which statement about signed tokens (JWTs) versus a shared session store is correct?","options":["Tokens avoid a per-request lookup but are harder to revoke before expiry","Tokens are always more secure because the client holds them","A session store requires sticky sessions to work","Tokens let the server change a user's data without the client noticing"],"answer":0,"why":"A signed token is verified locally with no store read, but the server holds no record to delete, so early revocation needs extra machinery such as short lifetimes or a deny list."},{"q":"After moving sessions to Redis, the app servers are stateless. What new risk have you taken on?","options":["Servers can no longer be restarted","The session store is now a shared dependency that must be fast and highly available","Load balancing must become sticky","Cookies no longer work"],"answer":1,"why":"State did not disappear, it moved. If the session store is down or slow, every server is affected, so it needs replication, failover, and monitoring."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Stateful server","def":"Keeps data between requests (such as a session in RAM) that other servers lack, so requests must reach that same server."},{"term":"Stateless server","def":"Holds nothing a later request depends on; everything needed arrives with the request or comes from a shared store."},{"term":"Sticky session","def":"Load balancer pins a user to one server via cookie or IP hash. Easy, but skews load, slows deploys, and loses sessions on a crash."},{"term":"Shared session store","def":"Redis or Memcached keyed by session ID with a TTL. Costs about 1 ms per request and must be replicated for availability."},{"term":"Signed token (JWT)","def":"Client-held claims signed with a key. No lookup needed, but hard to revoke, so use short expiry plus refresh tokens."},{"term":"Disposable servers","def":"The payoff of statelessness: autoscale, rolling deploys, and failover all work by freely adding or removing app servers."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"load-balancing","title":"Load Balancing","subtitle":"Watch a load balancer spread traffic across servers, pick the right one, drop a dead one, and survive its own failure.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["The difference between Layer 4 and Layer 7 load balancing, and when each fits","How round robin, least connections, and consistent hashing choose a server, and where each one goes wrong","How health checks detect a dead server and how long detection really takes","Why the load balancer itself needs a standby, and how failover works"],"nodes":[{"id":"clients","label":"Clients","kind":"client","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"lb1","label":"LB (active)","kind":"lb","x":40,"y":30},{"id":"lb2","label":"LB (standby)","kind":"lb","x":40,"y":76},{"id":"a","label":"Server A","kind":"server","x":74,"y":14},{"id":"b","label":"Server B","kind":"server","x":74,"y":50},{"id":"c","label":"Server C","kind":"server","x":74,"y":86}],"edges":[{"from":"clients","to":"lb1"},{"from":"clients","to":"lb2"},{"from":"lb1","to":"lb2","label":"heartbeat"},{"from":"lb1","to":"a"},{"from":"lb1","to":"b"},{"from":"lb1","to":"c"},{"from":"lb2","to":"a"},{"from":"lb2","to":"b"},{"from":"lb2","to":"c"}],"metrics":[{"id":"ca","label":"Conns A","unit":""},{"id":"cb","label":"Conns B","unit":""},{"id":"cc","label":"Conns C","unit":""},{"id":"healthy","label":"Healthy","unit":"of 3"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One server is not enough","body":"Your API gets about 6,000 requests per second at peak, but one server tops out near 2,500. So you run three servers. The problem: clients only know one address. Something has to sit in front, accept every connection, and decide which server handles it. That something is a load balancer. It also decides what happens when a server dies.","idea":"A load balancer gives many servers one front door and chooses who answers each request.","caption":"Three servers, one entry point, and a choice to make.","focus":["clients","lb1","a","b","c"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"ca":"0","cb":"0","cc":"0","healthy":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"clients","text":"6,000 req/s"},{"node":"a","text":"max 2,500 req/s"},{"node":"lb1","text":"3 of 3 healthy","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"LAYER 4","title":"L4: route by connection","body":"A Layer 4 balancer works at the TCP/UDP level, the plain network connection. It sees only addresses and ports. When a connection opens it picks a server, then passes the bytes through without reading them. It is very fast and works with any protocol, so it suits databases, message brokers and raw TLS passthrough. The catch: it cannot see URLs, headers or cookies. With long-lived connections that carry many requests, such as gRPC over HTTP/2, every request on that connection sticks to one server, so those need Layer 7.","idea":"L4 picks a server once per connection using only IP addresses and ports, so it is fast but blind to content.","caption":"One TCP connection is pinned to one server (A).","focus":["clients","lb1","a"],"flows":[{"from":"clients","to":"lb1","kind":"handshake","label":"TCP SYN :443"},{"from":"lb1","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"same TCP stream"}],"metrics":{"ca":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"lb1","text":"L4 · TCP/UDP"},{"node":"lb1","text":"sees ip:port only","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"LAYER 7","title":"L7: route by request","body":"A Layer 7 balancer ends the connection itself, reads the HTTP request, and routes on what it finds: the path, host name, header or cookie. So /api can go to one pool of servers and /images to another. It can also safely retry idempotent requests (ones that are safe to repeat, like GET) on a different server. The cost is more CPU per request, because it parses HTTP and often decrypts TLS.","idea":"L7 understands requests, which allows smart routing and retries, at the price of more work per request.","caption":"The path /api/orders sends this request to the API pool (server B).","focus":["clients","lb1","b"],"flows":[{"from":"clients","to":"lb1","kind":"request","label":"GET /api/orders"},{"from":"lb1","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"to API pool"},{"from":"b","to":"lb1","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"},{"from":"lb1","to":"clients","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"ca":"0","cb":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"lb1","text":"L7 · reads HTTP"},{"node":"b","text":"API pool"},{"node":"b","text":"200 OK","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"ALGORITHM","title":"Round robin: take turns","body":"The simplest policy is taking turns: request 1 to A, request 2 to B, request 3 to C, then start again. It needs nothing but a counter and works well when requests cost about the same and servers are equal. It struggles when a few requests are very heavy, because the rotation keeps handing new work to a server that is already swamped.","idea":"Round robin is fair in the number of requests, not in the amount of work, so it assumes similar requests and similar servers.","caption":"Requests rotate A, B, C evenly.","focus":["lb1","a","b","c"],"flows":[{"from":"lb1","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"req 1"},{"from":"lb1","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"req 2"},{"from":"lb1","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"req 3"}],"metrics":{"ca":"10","cb":"10","cc":"10"},"chips":[{"node":"lb1","text":"round robin","at":"bottom"},{"node":"a","text":"req 1"},{"node":"b","text":"req 2"},{"node":"c","text":"req 3"}]},{"tag":"ALGORITHM","title":"Least connections: pick the idlest","body":"Now Server B is stuck on slow report exports and holds 48 open connections, while A has 12 and C has 10. Least connections sends the next request to the server with the fewest active ones, here C. Because it reacts to real load, it copes with uneven request cost far better than round robin. The catch: connection count is only a rough measure of load, and a brand-new server with zero connections can get flooded. Weights can account for servers of different sizes.","idea":"Least connections follows actual load, so slow requests naturally steer new work elsewhere.","caption":"The new request goes to C, the least busy server.","focus":["lb1","a","b","c"],"flows":[{"from":"lb1","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"fewest conns"}],"metrics":{"ca":"12","cb":"48","cc":"10"},"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"12 conns"},{"node":"b","text":"48 conns","tone":"red"},{"node":"c","text":"10 conns","tone":"green"},{"node":"lb1","text":"next → C","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"ALGORITHM","title":"Consistent hash: same key, same server","body":"Sometimes you want the same user or cache key to reach the same server every time, for example because that server keeps a warm in-memory cache. The balancer hashes the key onto a ring of servers. When a server is added or removed, only about 1/N of the keys move, instead of nearly all of them with plain hash-modulo-N. The catch: it ignores load, so a hot key can overload one server, and with few servers the split is uneven unless each gets many virtual points on the ring.","idea":"Consistent hashing gives key-based stickiness while keeping reshuffling small when the pool changes.","caption":"User 42 always maps to B, even though B is already busy.","focus":["lb1","b"],"flows":[{"from":"lb1","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"user 42"},{"from":"lb1","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"user 42 again"}],"metrics":{"ca":"12","cb":"50","cc":"11"},"chips":[{"node":"lb1","text":"consistent hash"},{"node":"b","text":"user 42 → B"},{"node":"b","text":"warm cache","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"HEALTH","title":"Health checks find the dead","body":"Server C crashes. The balancer checks every server on a schedule, for example GET /healthz every 5 seconds, and marks a server out after 3 failures in a row. That means up to about 15 seconds of bad traffic before C is removed. Passive checks help too: real requests that time out also count. Keep the check cheap and honest: test that the app can do its own work, but do not fail on a shared dependency, or one blip could pull every server out at once.","idea":"Detection takes time: the check interval times the failure threshold is how long users may hit a dead server.","caption":"Checks to C time out, so C is pulled out of rotation.","focus":["lb1","c"],"flows":[{"from":"lb1","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"GET /healthz"},{"from":"c","to":"lb1","kind":"fail","label":"timeout x3"}],"metrics":{"cc":"0","healthy":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"c","text":"timeout x3","tone":"red"},{"node":"lb1","text":"probe every 5 s"},{"node":"lb1","text":"2 of 3 healthy","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"lb1","text":"out after ~15 s","at":"top"}]},{"tag":"FAILOVER","title":"Traffic reroutes around C","body":"With C out, A and B must each carry about 3,000 requests per second at peak (6,000 divided by 2). That is above the 2,500 each can sustain, so they overload. This is why spare capacity matters. N+1 means one more server than you need: with four servers, losing one still leaves three, and 3 x 2,500 = 7,500 covers the 6,000 peak. The balancer keeps checking C and lets it back in once it passes again.","idea":"When a server is removed its load shifts to the rest, so plan capacity to survive a failure.","caption":"Only A and B receive requests now, and each is over its limit.","focus":["lb1","a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"lb1","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"3,000 req/s"},{"from":"lb1","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"3,000 req/s"}],"metrics":{"ca":"58","cb":"64","cc":"0","healthy":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"3,000 req/s","tone":"red"},{"node":"b","text":"3,000 req/s","tone":"red"},{"node":"lb1","text":"N+1 = 4 servers","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"c","text":"still probed"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The load balancer dies","body":"The balancer is itself a single point of failure: if the only one dies, everything is down. So you run two. The active one sends heartbeats (regular \"I am alive\" signals) to a standby. When they stop for a few seconds, the standby takes over the shared virtual IP address (for example using VRRP), so clients keep using the same address. Connections in flight usually drop and clients retry. Larger setups run several balancers at once behind DNS or anycast, and managed cloud balancers do this across zones for you.","idea":"Run the load balancer as a redundant pair or a managed multi-zone service so it is not the weak link.","caption":"Heartbeats stop, so the standby takes over the traffic.","focus":["lb1","lb2","clients"],"flows":[{"from":"lb1","to":"lb2","kind":"fail","label":"heartbeat lost"},{"from":"clients","to":"lb2","kind":"request","label":"same virtual IP"},{"from":"lb2","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"req"}],"metrics":{"ca":"21","cb":"30"},"chips":[{"node":"lb1","text":"heartbeat lost","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"lb1","text":"down","tone":"red"},{"node":"lb2","text":"takes over VIP","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"What to remember","body":"L4 balances connections quickly; L7 balances individual requests intelligently. Round robin suits uniform work, least connections suits uneven work, and consistent hashing suits key-based affinity. Health checks remove dead servers after a delay you can tune, and the balancer needs its own redundancy. Keep spare capacity so a lost server does not overload the survivors.","idea":"Pick the layer and the algorithm for your traffic, check health, and make the balancer redundant.","caption":"Pool, policy, health checks and redundancy working together.","focus":["clients","lb1","lb2","a","b","c"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"ca":"21","cb":"30","cc":"0","healthy":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"c","text":"out of rotation","tone":"red"},{"node":"lb2","text":"standby","tone":"green"},{"node":"a","text":"N+1 spare capacity","at":"bottom"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"You must route /api/* to one server pool and /static/* to another, and retry failed GETs on a different server. Which balancer type do you need?","options":["Layer 4, because it is faster","Layer 7, because it can read the HTTP path and retry requests","Layer 4, because paths are in the TCP header","Either one, routing by path works the same at both layers"],"answer":1,"why":"Path-based routing and per-request retries need the balancer to parse HTTP, which is Layer 7. Layer 4 only sees IPs and ports."},{"q":"Requests vary wildly: most take 10 ms but some take 20 seconds. Which policy handles this best?","options":["Round robin","Consistent hashing on user ID","Always send to the first server","Least connections"],"answer":3,"why":"Least connections reacts to servers that are tied up with slow requests and routes new work away from them. Round robin ignores load."},{"q":"A balancer probes every 10 s and removes a server after 3 failed probes. Roughly how long can users hit a server that died just after a successful probe?","options":["Up to about 30 s","About 10 s at most","Exactly 3 s","No time, removal is instant"],"answer":0,"why":"Three consecutive failures at 10 s intervals take about 30 s to confirm. Lowering the interval or threshold shortens this but risks false alarms."},{"q":"Why is consistent hashing preferred over hash(key) mod N when servers are added or removed?","options":["It makes every request faster","It removes the need for health checks","Only about 1/N of keys move to a new server, so caches stay mostly warm","It balances load perfectly with no skew"],"answer":2,"why":"With mod N, changing N remaps almost every key. A hash ring moves only the keys adjacent to the changed server."},{"q":"Your three servers each handle 2,500 req/s, and peak traffic is 6,000 req/s. One server fails. What happens?","options":["Nothing, the two survivors easily cope","The survivors need 3,000 req/s each, above their limit, so they overload","The standby load balancer absorbs the extra load","Traffic drops to 5,000 req/s automatically"],"answer":1,"why":"6,000 / 2 = 3,000 per server, above the 2,500 limit. You need N+1 headroom so a single failure stays within capacity."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"L4 balancer","def":"Routes TCP/UDP connections by IP and port without reading content. Fast and protocol-agnostic, but cannot route by URL or header."},{"term":"L7 balancer","def":"Terminates the connection and routes each HTTP request by path, host, header, or cookie. Enables retries and gradual rollouts (send 5% of traffic to a new version); costs more CPU."},{"term":"Round robin","def":"Rotate through servers in order. Simple and stateless; best when requests and servers are uniform."},{"term":"Least connections","def":"Send to the server with the fewest active connections. Handles uneven request cost; weights cover unequal servers."},{"term":"Consistent hashing","def":"Map a key onto a hash ring of servers so the same key hits the same server. Adding or removing a server moves only about 1/N of keys."},{"term":"Health check","def":"Active probe (such as GET /healthz) or passive error counting. Detection time is roughly interval times failure threshold."},{"term":"N+1 capacity","def":"Size the pool so the loss of any one server leaves enough capacity for peak load. Otherwise failover can overload the survivors and cascade."},{"term":"Active/standby LB","def":"Two balancers share a virtual IP and exchange heartbeats. If the active one goes silent, the standby takes over within seconds."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"autoscaling","title":"Autoscaling","subtitle":"Watch a lunchtime traffic spike hit a fleet, and see why reacting late hurts and how schedules and forecasts help.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How reactive, scheduled, and predictive scaling differ and when each fits","Which metrics make good scaling signals (and which mislead)","Why scaling lag exists: metric delay + boot time + warm-up","How cooldowns stop the fleet from flapping up and down","The cost of over-provisioning versus the risk of under-provisioning"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"Users","kind":"user","x":10,"y":55},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":30,"y":55},{"id":"fleet","label":"App instances","kind":"server","x":55,"y":55},{"id":"metrics","label":"Metrics store","kind":"service","x":30,"y":18},{"id":"scaler","label":"Autoscaler","kind":"service","x":55,"y":18},{"id":"sched","label":"Schedule + forecast","kind":"worker","x":82,"y":18},{"id":"cloud","label":"Cloud API","kind":"external","x":82,"y":55}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"lb"},{"from":"lb","to":"fleet"},{"from":"fleet","to":"metrics"},{"from":"metrics","to":"scaler"},{"from":"sched","to":"scaler"},{"from":"scaler","to":"cloud"},{"from":"cloud","to":"fleet"}],"metrics":[{"id":"traffic","label":"Traffic","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"instances","label":"Ready instances","unit":"count"},{"id":"cpu","label":"Avg CPU","unit":"%"},{"id":"p95","label":"p95 latency","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"How many servers do you need?","body":"An online shop gets 600 requests per second most of the day. Assume one instance (one server) comfortably handles 100 requests per second at 60 percent CPU, so 6 instances is the right size. But the number you need changes through the day, and nobody wants to watch a dashboard and add servers by hand. Autoscaling is software that changes the instance count for you.","idea":"The right fleet size moves with traffic; autoscaling is the control loop that follows it.","caption":"Steady state: 6 instances at 60% CPU.","focus":["users","lb","fleet"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"600 req/s"},{"from":"lb","to":"fleet","kind":"request","label":"100 each"}],"metrics":{"traffic":"600","instances":"6","cpu":"60","p95":"120"},"chips":[{"node":"users","text":"600 req/s"},{"node":"fleet","kind":"progress","value":60,"text":"60%"},{"node":"fleet","text":"6 instances","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"PROBLEM","title":"The lunch spike arrives","body":"At 11:45 a promotion email goes out and traffic doubles to 1,200 requests per second within a minute. Six instances are now facing twice the load. At 60 percent CPU they handled 600, so at 100 percent their ceiling is only about 1,000. CPU maxes out and requests queue up. p95 latency (the time within which 95 percent of requests finish) jumps from 120 ms to about 1.8 seconds, and some requests time out.","idea":"Too little capacity does not degrade gently: queues build up and latency explodes.","caption":"Demand doubles, but capacity does not.","focus":["users","lb","fleet"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"1,200 req/s"},{"from":"lb","to":"fleet","kind":"fail","label":"overloaded"}],"metrics":{"traffic":"1200","cpu":"100","p95":"1800"},"chips":[{"node":"users","text":"1,200 req/s","tone":"red"},{"node":"fleet","kind":"progress","value":100,"text":"100%","tone":"red"},{"node":"fleet","text":"p95 120 → 1,800 ms","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"METRICS","title":"Pick a signal to scale on","body":"Every instance reports numbers to a metrics store, usually averaged per minute. Here they report CPU at 100 percent. Good signals rise with load and move before users suffer: average CPU, requests per instance, or for queue workers the queue length or the age of the oldest message. Memory is often a poor signal, because many runtimes keep their memory after a spike. Latency is a useful alarm, but it rises only after users are already hurting.","idea":"Scale on a metric that rises with load and falls when capacity is added, ideally one that moves early.","caption":"Instances publish their CPU to the metrics store.","focus":["fleet","metrics"],"flows":[{"from":"fleet","to":"metrics","kind":"async","label":"CPU 100%"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"metrics","text":"avg CPU 100%"},{"node":"metrics","text":"1-min averages","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"REACTIVE","title":"Reactive: react to what happened","body":"A reactive policy compares a metric with a target. With target tracking you say \"keep average CPU near 60 percent\". The autoscaler computes desired = current x (measured / target): 6 x 100 / 60 = 10, so it asks the cloud API for 4 more instances. The catch: CPU cannot go above 100 percent, so a saturated fleet hides how overloaded it really is. Unsaturated, the load would read 120 percent, and 6 x 120 / 60 = 12. The real need was 12.","idea":"Reactive scaling is simple and handles surprises, but it only acts after the load has already arrived.","caption":"The autoscaler decides on +4 and calls the cloud API.","focus":["metrics","scaler","cloud"],"flows":[{"from":"metrics","to":"scaler","kind":"request","label":"avg CPU 100%"},{"from":"scaler","to":"cloud","kind":"request","label":"launch +4"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"scaler","text":"target 60%"},{"node":"scaler","text":"desired = 10","at":"bottom"},{"node":"cloud","text":"+4 instances"},{"node":"metrics","text":"CPU capped at 100%","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"LAG","title":"Scaling lag","body":"Help is not instant. Metrics are averaged per minute, so the spike is noticed about a minute late. The scaler may wait for a couple of readings to avoid false alarms. Then the cloud must start a virtual machine and boot your app, often 60 to 120 seconds. All that time users still see 1.8 s latency. Typically 3 to 5 minutes pass between the spike and relief.","idea":"Scaling lag is metric delay plus decision delay plus boot time plus warm-up, and reactive scaling always pays it.","caption":"New instances are booting; none are serving traffic yet.","focus":["cloud","fleet"],"flows":[{"from":"cloud","to":"fleet","kind":"handshake","label":"boot ~90 s"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"metrics","text":"noticed ~1 min late"},{"node":"cloud","text":"boot 60-120 s"},{"node":"cloud","text":"total 3-5 min","at":"bottom"},{"node":"fleet","text":"still 1.8 s","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"WARM-UP","title":"Warm-up and the second round","body":"The 4 new instances join, and the load balancer sends them traffic. They start cold: empty caches, no open database connections yet, code not yet optimized by the runtime. So for a minute or so they run below full speed. With 10 instances sharing 1,200 req/s (120 each), CPU settles around 72 percent, still above the 60 percent target, so the scaler adds 2 more (10 x 72 / 60 = 12). Once warm, 12 instances run at 60 percent and p95 returns to about 130 ms.","idea":"A new instance is not at full capacity the moment it boots, so give it a warm-up period before trusting its metrics.","caption":"The fleet grows from 6 to 10 to 12 and latency recovers.","focus":["scaler","cloud","fleet"],"flows":[{"from":"cloud","to":"fleet","kind":"handshake","label":"4 join, warm up"},{"from":"scaler","to":"cloud","kind":"request","label":"launch +2"},{"from":"cloud","to":"fleet","kind":"handshake","label":"2 join"}],"metrics":{"instances":"12","cpu":"60","p95":"130"},"chips":[{"node":"fleet","text":"4 join · cold"},{"node":"scaler","text":"CPU 72% → +2"},{"node":"fleet","text":"12 inst · p95 130 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"COOLDOWN","title":"Scaling in, slowly","body":"At 12:30 the spike ends and traffic falls back to 600 req/s, so CPU drops to 30 percent on 12 instances. The scaler does not remove half the fleet at once. A cooldown (say 5 to 10 minutes) and removing only a few instances at a time make scale-in slower than scale-out. Without that, a short dip would remove servers just before the next wave, and the fleet would flap up and down, paying boot lag every time.","idea":"Scale out fast and scale in slowly: removing capacity too early costs more than keeping it a bit longer.","caption":"Traffic falls, so instances are removed a few at a time, back toward 6.","focus":["metrics","scaler","cloud","fleet"],"flows":[{"from":"metrics","to":"scaler","kind":"request","label":"avg CPU 30%"},{"from":"scaler","to":"cloud","kind":"request","label":"terminate 1"},{"from":"cloud","to":"fleet","kind":"async","label":"drain, stop"}],"metrics":{"traffic":"600","instances":"11","cpu":"30","p95":"120"},"chips":[{"node":"metrics","text":"CPU 30%"},{"node":"scaler","text":"cooldown 5-10 min"},{"node":"fleet","kind":"progress","value":30,"text":"30%"},{"node":"fleet","text":"12 → 11","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"SCHEDULED","title":"Scheduled: you know the pattern","body":"The spike happens every weekday at 11:45, so waiting for CPU to rise is wasteful. A scheduled action raises the minimum to 12 instances at 11:35, leaving time to boot and warm up, and a second action lowers it back to 6 after the peak. The spike now meets full capacity with no lag. The cost is a few extra instance-hours, and the schedule goes stale if the pattern changes, such as on a holiday or when marketing changes the send time.","idea":"Scheduled scaling removes lag for predictable load, but it is only as good as your calendar.","caption":"Capacity is added before the spike arrives.","focus":["sched","scaler","cloud","fleet"],"flows":[{"from":"sched","to":"scaler","kind":"request","label":"min=12 at 11:35"},{"from":"scaler","to":"cloud","kind":"request","label":"launch +6"},{"from":"cloud","to":"fleet","kind":"handshake","label":"ready by 11:45"}],"metrics":{"traffic":"1200","instances":"12","cpu":"60","p95":"120"},"chips":[{"node":"sched","text":"min 12 @ 11:35"},{"node":"cloud","text":"+6 instances"},{"node":"fleet","text":"ready by 11:45","tone":"green"},{"node":"fleet","kind":"progress","value":60,"text":"60%"}]},{"tag":"PREDICTIVE","title":"Predictive: let history forecast","body":"Predictive scaling learns from past load, typically days to a couple of weeks of history, to forecast the next day or so and launch capacity early. It suits regular daily and weekly patterns without hand-written schedules. It cannot see a surprise, like a viral post. So teams combine all three: a forecast or schedule for the known shape, reactive scaling as a safety net, and sensible minimum and maximum limits.","idea":"Forecasts handle regular rhythms; reactive scaling covers what the forecast cannot know.","caption":"A forecast feeds the autoscaler ahead of time, while live CPU keeps watch.","focus":["metrics","sched","scaler"],"flows":[{"from":"sched","to":"scaler","kind":"async","label":"forecast: 12"},{"from":"metrics","to":"scaler","kind":"async","label":"live CPU"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"sched","text":"forecast: 12"},{"node":"scaler","text":"+ reactive backup"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Choosing a policy","body":"Reactive scaling follows live metrics and handles surprises but lags 3 to 5 minutes. Scheduled scaling removes lag for known events. Predictive scaling learns the rhythm. Set a minimum so you survive the first seconds of a spike, a maximum so a bug cannot buy 500 servers, and headroom (a 60 percent target, not 90) because capacity arrives late. Idle headroom is cheap: 6 extra instances for 1 hour is 6 instance-hours, tiny next to minutes of 1.8 s latency. You can also shorten boot lag with lean images, pre-baked machine images, or a warm pool of stopped instances.","idea":"Autoscaling trades a little idle cost for resilience; tune the target, the minimum and the lag to your traffic.","caption":"Three policies, one fleet.","focus":["metrics","scaler","sched","cloud","fleet"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"traffic":"1200","instances":"12","cpu":"60","p95":"120"},"chips":[{"node":"fleet","text":"12 inst · 120 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"fleet","kind":"progress","value":60,"text":"60%"},{"node":"scaler","text":"min / max set"},{"node":"cloud","text":"lag 3-5 min"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Six instances are overloaded and CPU reads 100% against a 60% target. Target tracking asks for 10 instances, but 12 are needed. Why is the estimate low?","options":["The cloud API caps launches at 4 instances","Memory usage was ignored","CPU saturates at 100%, so it hides how far demand exceeds capacity","The load balancer double counts traffic"],"answer":2,"why":"A saturated metric cannot show the true overload. The scaler under-adds, measures again, and adds more in a second round."},{"q":"Traffic jumps every weekday at 11:45 and new instances need about 4 minutes to boot and warm up. What fixes the latency spike best?","options":["Lower the CPU target from 60% to 50%","A scheduled action that raises the minimum before 11:45","A longer scale-in cooldown","Scaling on memory instead of CPU"],"answer":1,"why":"Reactive scaling always arrives after the spike. A predictable event is best handled by adding capacity ahead of time."},{"q":"Why do most autoscalers scale in more slowly and cautiously than they scale out?","options":["Terminated instances are billed at a premium, so removing them quickly is expensive","Cloud providers rate-limit termination calls much more strictly than launches","Idle instances cost almost nothing, so there is little reason to hurry removal","Removing capacity too early risks overload and flapping, and re-adding it pays boot lag"],"answer":3,"why":"Being a little over-provisioned costs money; being under-provisioned costs availability. The asymmetry justifies a cooldown on scale-in."},{"q":"A worker fleet processes jobs from a queue. Which scaling signal is most direct?","options":["Queue depth per worker (backlog divided by worker count)","Average CPU across workers","Total queue depth, ignoring worker count","p95 latency of the web tier"],"answer":0,"why":"Backlog per worker measures unfinished work directly and responds to added workers. CPU can look fine while the queue grows if workers wait on I/O."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Reactive scaling","def":"Adds or removes instances when a live metric crosses a threshold or drifts from a target. Handles surprises but always lags."},{"term":"Scheduled scaling","def":"Changes capacity at set times for known patterns, such as a daily peak. No lag, but goes stale when the pattern shifts."},{"term":"Predictive scaling","def":"Forecasts load from history and launches capacity ahead of demand. Good for recurring rhythms, blind to surprises."},{"term":"Target tracking","def":"Keep a metric near a value (say CPU 60%). Desired count is roughly current count × measured ÷ target, rounded up."},{"term":"Scaling lag","def":"Delay from load change to usable capacity: metric aggregation, decision time, instance boot, and warm-up. Often 3 to 5 minutes."},{"term":"Warm-up","def":"Period when a new instance is not yet at full speed (cold caches, fresh connections). Ignore its metrics until it is warm, and ramp traffic to it gradually."},{"term":"Cooldown","def":"Minimum wait after a scaling action before another. Longer for scale-in to prevent flapping."},{"term":"Min / max / headroom","def":"Minimum survives the first seconds of a spike, maximum caps cost and runaway bugs, headroom (a target well below 100%) buys time while scaling."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"cdn","title":"CDNs & Edge","subtitle":"Watch a cache miss, a hit, a shield, a purge and an edge function, and see how each one cuts distance and origin load.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why a CDN cuts latency (shorter round trips) and origin load (cache hits)","How a cache key decides whether two requests share one cached copy","What an origin shield does and why it protects the origin from many edges","How to get fresh content out: TTLs, purge, and versioned file names","When push beats pull, and what edge compute is good (and bad) at"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"Users","kind":"user","x":9,"y":50},{"id":"edge","label":"Edge PoP Mumbai","kind":"cdn","x":34,"y":22},{"id":"edge2","label":"Edge PoP Tokyo","kind":"cdn","x":34,"y":78},{"id":"shield","label":"Origin shield","kind":"cdn","x":60,"y":50},{"id":"origin","label":"Origin server","kind":"server","x":87,"y":68},{"id":"fn","label":"Edge function","kind":"worker","x":62,"y":88}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"edge"},{"from":"users","to":"edge2"},{"from":"users","to":"origin","label":"direct, 200 ms"},{"from":"edge","to":"shield"},{"from":"edge2","to":"shield"},{"from":"shield","to":"origin"},{"from":"edge2","to":"fn"}],"metrics":[{"id":"latency","label":"Latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"hit","label":"Hit ratio","unit":"%"},{"id":"origin","label":"Origin load","unit":"req/s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"The origin is on the other side of the planet","body":"Your only server (the origin) sits in Virginia. A user in Mumbai is about 200 ms away for each round trip (a message there and back). A new connection needs one round trip for TCP, one for TLS 1.3 (the encryption setup) and one for the request itself: about 600 ms of travel. Add 60 ms for the server to build the page and the user waits about 660 ms. (HTTP/3 merges the first two trips, which shrinks the gap but not the idea.) Assume the origin gets 1,000 cacheable requests per second.","idea":"Distance costs whole round trips, and the origin pays for every request that reaches it.","caption":"Every request crosses the planet to reach the origin.","focus":["users","origin"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"origin","kind":"request","label":"GET /product/42"},{"from":"origin","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"latency":"660","hit":"0","origin":"1000"},"chips":[{"node":"users","text":"RTT 200 ms"},{"node":"users","text":"≈ 660 ms","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"origin","text":"1,000 req/s"},{"node":"origin","text":"render 60 ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Put a server near the user","body":"A CDN (content delivery network) runs hundreds of small data centers called points of presence (PoPs). DNS or anycast routing sends each user to a nearby one, here Mumbai, about 20 ms away. The TCP and TLS setup now finishes at this nearby edge in about 40 ms (two 20 ms round trips) instead of 400 ms, even before any caching happens.","idea":"Ending the connection near the user is a win by itself, because the handshakes are the expensive part.","caption":"Handshake only: 40 ms with the nearby edge.","focus":["users","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"edge","kind":"handshake","label":"TCP + TLS"},{"from":"edge","to":"users","kind":"handshake","label":"keys agreed"}],"metrics":{"latency":"40"},"chips":[{"node":"edge","text":"RTT 20 ms"},{"node":"edge","text":"TCP + TLS ≈ 40 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"MISS","title":"First request: a cache miss","body":"The edge has no copy of /product/42 yet, so it forwards the request toward the origin through a shield (a middle cache, covered soon). This is a cache miss. The user spends 60 ms with the edge (40 for the handshake, 20 for the request). We assume the edge already keeps an open connection to the origin side, so the miss adds one 200 ms round trip plus 60 ms of page building: about 320 ms in total. The response says Cache-Control: max-age=300, so the edge stores a copy for 300 seconds.","idea":"Even a miss beats going direct, because the handshakes finish near the user; only a cache hit removes the long trip.","caption":"Miss: the request goes all the way to the origin.","focus":["users","edge","shield","origin"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"GET /product/42"},{"from":"edge","to":"shield","kind":"request","label":"miss"},{"from":"shield","to":"origin","kind":"request","label":"miss"},{"from":"origin","to":"shield","kind":"response","label":"200 + max-age"},{"from":"shield","to":"edge","kind":"response","label":"store copy"},{"from":"edge","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"latency":"320","hit":"0","origin":"1000"},"chips":[{"node":"edge","text":"cache miss","tone":"red"},{"node":"edge","text":"max-age 300 s","at":"bottom"},{"node":"users","text":"≈ 320 ms"}]},{"tag":"HIT","title":"Next request: a cache hit","body":"The next user in Mumbai asks for the same page. The edge finds a fresh copy and answers by itself, so the origin never hears about it. Latency is about 60 ms (40 for a fresh handshake, 20 for the request), and less on a reused connection. If 95 percent of requests are hits like this, the origin only sees 50 of the 1,000 requests per second.","idea":"Hit ratio is the CDN's main dial: each point you gain removes load from the origin and delay from users.","caption":"Hit: the edge answers without touching the origin.","focus":["users","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"GET /product/42"},{"from":"edge","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"200 (cached)"}],"metrics":{"latency":"60","hit":"95","origin":"50"},"chips":[{"node":"edge","text":"cache hit","tone":"green"},{"node":"edge","kind":"progress","value":95,"text":"95% hits"},{"node":"origin","text":"50 req/s"},{"node":"users","text":"≈ 60 ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CACHE KEY","title":"What counts as the same request?","body":"The edge files each object under a cache key, typically host plus path plus query string (defaults vary by CDN). Requests with the same key share one cached copy. Here the CDN is set to ignore utm_* tracking parameters, so both requests map to one copy. A session cookie in the key would make every user unique, and adding User-Agent would split the cache into hundreds of variants. Either way the hit ratio crashes. Static files get long TTLs (how long a copy stays valid), pages like /product/42 cache for seconds or minutes, and personalized responses must be Cache-Control: private or no-store.","idea":"Keep the cache key as small as correctness allows, and strip anything that does not change the response.","caption":"Both tracking variants map to one cached copy.","focus":["users","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"?utm_source=a"},{"from":"users","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"?utm_source=b"},{"from":"edge","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"same cached copy"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"edge","text":"key = host+path+query"},{"node":"edge","text":"utm_* ignored","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"SHIELD","title":"Origin shield: one miss, not hundreds","body":"A user in Tokyo asks for the same page. The Tokyo PoP has an empty cache, so it misses. Without a shield, each of up to ~200 PoPs could fetch the object from the origin separately. With a shield, PoPs ask one designated cache first, and only that cache talks to the origin. We assume this lifts the hit ratio from 95 to 98 percent, so the origin sees about 20 of 1,000 requests per second. The shield sits in Virginia beside the origin, about 150 ms from Tokyo, so a shield hit costs 60 + 150 = 210 ms: slower than an edge hit, far cheaper than a full origin trip.","idea":"A shield is a cache in front of the origin that turns many edge misses into one origin fetch.","caption":"Tokyo misses, but the shield has the page.","focus":["users","edge2","shield"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"edge2","kind":"request","label":"GET /product/42"},{"from":"edge2","to":"shield","kind":"request","label":"miss"},{"from":"shield","to":"edge2","kind":"response","label":"shield hit"},{"from":"edge2","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"latency":"210","hit":"98","origin":"20"},"chips":[{"node":"edge2","text":"cold · miss","tone":"red"},{"node":"shield","text":"shield hit","tone":"green"},{"node":"users","text":"≈ 210 ms"},{"node":"origin","text":"20 req/s"}]},{"tag":"PURGE","title":"Changing content that is already cached","body":"The price of product 42 changes, but cached copies live for up to 300 seconds. You can wait, or call the CDN's purge API to evict a URL or a group of URLs (many CDNs let you tag objects and purge by tag). That takes from under a second to a couple of minutes. The purge must clear the shield too, or edges would refill from its stale copy. In the diagram the call reaches the shield, which tells every edge to evict. For static files, use versioned names like app.3f9a1c.js so a new build means a new URL. A purge cannot clear copies already in browsers, so use a shorter browser max-age with a longer s-maxage for the CDN.","idea":"Short TTLs trade hit ratio for freshness; purge gives freshness on demand but sends a burst of misses to the origin; versioned file names avoid the problem for static files.","caption":"The purge clears the shield and every edge.","focus":["origin","shield","edge","edge2"],"flows":[{"from":"origin","to":"shield","kind":"async","label":"purge via API"},{"from":"shield","to":"edge","kind":"replicate","label":"evict"},{"from":"shield","to":"edge2","kind":"replicate","label":"evict"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"origin","text":"wait ≤ 300 s"},{"node":"shield","text":"purge API"},{"node":"edge","text":"evicted"},{"node":"edge2","text":"evicted"}]},{"tag":"PUSH VS PULL","title":"Who fills the cache?","body":"Everything so far was a pull CDN, the default for most sites: the edge fetches from the origin on the first miss and stores only what people ask for. In a push CDN you upload files ahead of time. That suits big files with a known release moment, like a 2 GB game patch. In practice this is often a pre-warm call, or an upload to CDN storage that edges pull from (the shield node here stands for that storage tier). The cost is managing uploads and deletions yourself.","idea":"Pull is the sensible default; push fits big, predictable releases where a cold miss would hurt.","caption":"Push: the content is placed at the edges before anyone asks.","focus":["origin","shield","edge","edge2"],"flows":[{"from":"origin","to":"shield","kind":"async","label":"upload to CDN"},{"from":"shield","to":"edge","kind":"replicate","label":"pre-warm"},{"from":"shield","to":"edge2","kind":"replicate","label":"pre-warm"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"origin","text":"push upload"},{"node":"edge","text":"pre-warmed","tone":"green"},{"node":"edge2","text":"pre-warmed","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"EDGE COMPUTE","title":"Run small code at the edge","body":"Some responses cannot be cached as they are: an A/B test variant, a redirect by country, a token check. An edge function is a short script that runs inside the PoP, with no trip to the origin. Here it reads the user's cookie and turns it into a small variant key, A or B, so the edge keeps just two cached copies instead of one per user. Total: a fresh handshake (40 ms), the request (20 ms) and about 5 ms in the function, roughly 65 ms. Keep edge code tiny and stateless; consistent writes belong near the origin.","idea":"Edge compute handles cheap per-request decisions near the user; heavy or stateful logic still belongs at the origin.","caption":"A function picks the variant without calling the origin.","focus":["users","edge2","fn"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"edge2","kind":"request","label":"GET /home"},{"from":"edge2","to":"fn","kind":"request","label":"pick variant"},{"from":"fn","to":"edge2","kind":"response","label":"variant B"},{"from":"edge2","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"cached B page"}],"metrics":{"latency":"65"},"chips":[{"node":"fn","text":"variant B"},{"node":"fn","text":"+5 ms","at":"bottom"},{"node":"edge2","text":"2 copies: A, B"},{"node":"users","text":"≈ 65 ms"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"What the edge bought us","body":"The CDN moved handshakes next to the user, answered repeat requests from cache, and let a shield protect the origin, at the cost of an extra hop on a miss. Cache keys decide what is shared; TTLs, purge and versioned names decide freshness; edge functions handle small per-request logic. Result here: about 60 ms instead of 660 ms, and 20 origin requests per second instead of 1,000.","idea":"A CDN trades some control over freshness for large gains in latency and origin capacity.","caption":"Fast for users, quiet for the origin.","focus":["users","edge","edge2","shield","origin","fn"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"latency":"60","hit":"98","origin":"20"},"chips":[{"node":"users","text":"≈ 60 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"origin","text":"20 req/s","tone":"green"},{"node":"shield","kind":"progress","value":98,"text":"98% hits"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"After a release adds a per-user session cookie to the CDN's cache key, the hit ratio drops from 95% to about 2%. What happened?","options":["The TTL became shorter for everyone","The shield stopped forwarding misses","Every user now produces a unique key, so almost no two requests share a cached copy","The edge ran out of disk space"],"answer":2,"why":"A cache key that includes a per-user value makes each request unique, so copies cannot be shared and nearly every request misses."},{"q":"A new 5 MB video is requested by users near 150 different PoPs within a minute. What does enabling an origin shield change?","options":["Users get a faster first byte at every PoP","Roughly one fetch reaches the origin, and the other PoPs fill from the shield","The video is split into 150 smaller files","PoPs no longer need to cache the video"],"answer":1,"why":"Without a shield each PoP misses and goes to the origin (up to 150 fetches). With a shield, PoPs ask it first and only it contacts the origin."},{"q":"You deploy a new JavaScript bundle several times a day and want users to get it immediately while keeping a year-long cache lifetime. Which approach fits best?","options":["Put a content hash in the file name, such as app.3f9a1c.js, and reference the new name from the HTML","Keep one file name and purge it after each deploy","Set max-age to 0 so it is never cached","Add the User-Agent to the cache key"],"answer":0,"why":"A new hash means a new URL, so there is nothing stale to purge, and each versioned file can be cached for a very long time. The short-lived HTML points to the right one."},{"q":"Which task is the best fit for an edge function rather than the origin?","options":["Charging a card and writing the order to the primary database","Running a nightly report across all orders","Resizing a library of 4 TB of archived videos","Choosing an A/B test variant from a cookie and rewriting the request"],"answer":3,"why":"Choosing a variant is small, stateless, and benefits from running next to the user. Transactions, big batch jobs, and heavy processing need the origin's resources and consistent data."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"PoP (point of presence)","def":"A CDN data center near users, holding caches and often running edge code. Big CDNs run hundreds."},{"term":"Cache key","def":"The identity under which an object is stored, usually host, path and query. Anything extra in the key splits the cache and lowers hit ratio."},{"term":"Static vs dynamic caching","def":"Static assets get long TTLs and versioned names; shared dynamic pages get short TTLs (plus stale-while-revalidate, which serves the old copy while refetching in the background); per-user responses are marked private or no-store and never shared."},{"term":"Hit ratio","def":"Share of requests served from cache. Origin load is roughly (1 minus hit ratio) times total traffic, so 95% to 99% cuts origin traffic by 5x."},{"term":"Origin shield","def":"A designated mid-tier cache that edges consult before the origin, so many edge misses become one origin fetch."},{"term":"Purge / invalidation","def":"Explicitly evicting objects by URL, prefix or tag. Fast but must be wired into your publish flow; versioned file names avoid the need."},{"term":"Pull vs push CDN","def":"Pull fills the cache on first miss from the origin (default). Push means you upload content ahead of time, good for large scheduled releases."},{"term":"Edge compute","def":"Short, stateless code run in the PoP for redirects, auth checks, variants or header changes. Not a place for heavy or strongly consistent work."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"proxies-gateways","title":"Proxies & API Gateways","subtitle":"Watch one front door take over auth, routing, rate limits and fan-out for a fleet of backend services.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["The difference between a forward proxy and a reverse proxy","What an API gateway does: routing, authentication, rate limiting, aggregation","Why a Backend-for-Frontend (BFF) exists and who should own it","Why the gateway becomes a single point of failure and how to contain that risk"],"nodes":[{"id":"web","label":"Web app","kind":"client","x":8,"y":30},{"id":"mobile","label":"Mobile app","kind":"client","x":8,"y":72},{"id":"fproxy","label":"Corporate proxy","kind":"server","x":30,"y":30},{"id":"bff","label":"Mobile BFF","kind":"server","x":30,"y":72},{"id":"gw","label":"API gateway","kind":"gateway","x":52,"y":50},{"id":"auth","label":"Auth service","kind":"service","x":52,"y":12},{"id":"orders","label":"Orders service","kind":"service","x":84,"y":20},{"id":"users","label":"Users service","kind":"service","x":84,"y":50},{"id":"catalog","label":"Catalog service","kind":"service","x":84,"y":80}],"edges":[{"from":"web","to":"fproxy"},{"from":"fproxy","to":"gw"},{"from":"mobile","to":"bff"},{"from":"bff","to":"gw"},{"from":"gw","to":"auth"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders"},{"from":"gw","to":"users"},{"from":"gw","to":"catalog"}],"metrics":[{"id":"calls","label":"Client calls","unit":"calls"},{"id":"latency","label":"Server time","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Too many doors","body":"A shop is split into small services: orders, users, catalog and auth. If every app talked to each service directly, a home screen needing the profile, orders and recommendations would make three separate calls, each with its own address, login check and retry logic. Changing one service could break apps already in users' hands. Is there one place to put all this shared plumbing?","idea":"Without a front door, every client must know every backend and repeat the same rules.","caption":"With no front door, every client must reach every service.","focus":["web","mobile","orders","users","catalog","auth"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"calls":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"mobile","text":"3 separate calls","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Forward vs reverse proxy","body":"A proxy is a middleman that passes requests along. A forward proxy sits on the client side: a company sends its employees' traffic through it to filter sites, cache downloads or hide internal addresses, and the website sees the proxy, not the employee. (For HTTPS it normally just tunnels the encrypted traffic unless the company inspects it.) A reverse proxy sits on the server side: clients talk to it as if it were the site, and it forwards to hidden backends. The gateway in this lesson is a reverse proxy.","idea":"A forward proxy acts for clients; a reverse proxy acts for servers. The difference is which side it protects.","caption":"An employee's request leaves through the company's forward proxy, then reaches the gateway.","focus":["web","fproxy","gw"],"flows":[{"from":"web","to":"fproxy","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders/42"},{"from":"fproxy","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders/42"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"fproxy","text":"client side"},{"node":"gw","text":"server side"},{"node":"fproxy","text":"dest sees the proxy","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"One entry, many routes","body":"The gateway terminates TLS (it decrypts the HTTPS connection) and then routes by path or host name: /orders goes to Orders, /users to Users, /catalog to Catalog. Clients only know one hostname, so services can be moved, split or renamed behind it without breaking anyone. We assume the gateway hop costs about 2 ms and the service answers in about 20 ms, so the request takes about 22 ms.","idea":"Routing hides the internal layout, so backends can change without clients noticing.","caption":"The path /orders/42 is routed to the Orders service.","focus":["web","fproxy","gw","orders"],"flows":[{"from":"web","to":"fproxy","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders/42"},{"from":"fproxy","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders/42"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"route /orders"},{"from":"orders","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"200 order 42"},{"from":"gw","to":"fproxy","kind":"response","label":"200 order 42"},{"from":"fproxy","to":"web","kind":"response","label":"200 order 42"}],"metrics":{"latency":"22"},"chips":[{"node":"gw","text":"TLS ends here"},{"node":"gw","text":"+2 ms","at":"bottom"},{"node":"orders","text":"20 ms"}]},{"tag":"AUTH","title":"Check who is asking","body":"Instead of every service checking logins itself, the gateway does it once at the door. With signed tokens such as JWTs it can check the signature locally in microseconds (the catch: such a token stays valid until it expires, unless you add a revocation list). With opaque tokens (random strings with no readable data) it asks the auth service, here about 5 ms. Either way, unauthenticated calls stop at the door and services receive a trusted user ID. So far: 2 + 5 + 20 = 27 ms. Services still need their own checks for sensitive actions.","idea":"Authenticate once at the edge, but do not make the gateway your only line of defence.","caption":"The gateway validates the token before it routes the request.","focus":["gw","auth"],"flows":[{"from":"gw","to":"auth","kind":"request","label":"verify token"},{"from":"auth","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"ok, user 7"}],"metrics":{"latency":"27"},"chips":[{"node":"auth","text":"verify ~5 ms"},{"node":"auth","text":"ok · user 7","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"gw","text":"27 ms total"}]},{"tag":"RATE LIMIT","title":"Protect the backends","body":"A buggy client loops and sends 5,000 requests a second. The gateway counts requests per API key, for example with a token bucket: each key has a bucket that refills at 100 tokens a minute, and every request spends one. When the bucket is empty, the gateway answers 429 Too Many Requests. This costs about 1 ms, and the backends never see the extra traffic. Limits per key are fairer than per IP, because many users can share one IP behind a proxy. With several gateway instances, the counters live in a shared fast store such as Redis, or each instance enforces its share (cheaper but approximate).","idea":"Shedding abusive traffic at the edge is cheaper than letting it reach services that cannot say no.","caption":"Excess requests are rejected with 429 at the gateway.","focus":["fproxy","gw"],"flows":[{"from":"fproxy","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"burst of calls"},{"from":"gw","to":"fproxy","kind":"fail","label":"429 slow down"}],"metrics":{"latency":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"fproxy","text":"5,000 req/s","tone":"red"},{"node":"gw","text":"100 / min per key"},{"node":"gw","text":"429 rejected","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"gw","text":"≈ 1 ms","at":"top"}]},{"tag":"AGGREGATE","title":"One call, three services","body":"A home screen needs the user profile, recent orders and recommended products. Instead of making the app send three requests over a slow network (a phone, say), the gateway fans out to all three services at the same time and merges the answers into one response. Server time is the gateway hop (2 ms) plus the token check (5 ms) plus the slowest of the three parallel calls (20 ms): 27 ms. Calling them one after another would take about 67 ms (2 + 5 + 20 + 20 + 20).","idea":"Aggregation turns one big client call into parallel internal calls on a fast network.","caption":"The gateway fans out to three services and merges the replies.","focus":["fproxy","auth","gw","orders","users","catalog"],"flows":[{"from":"fproxy","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /home"},{"from":"gw","to":"auth","kind":"request","label":"verify token"},{"from":"auth","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"ok"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"recent orders"},{"from":"gw","to":"users","kind":"request","label":"profile"},{"from":"gw","to":"catalog","kind":"request","label":"recommended"},{"from":"orders","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"orders"},{"from":"users","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"profile"},{"from":"catalog","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"products"},{"from":"gw","to":"fproxy","kind":"response","label":"merged JSON"}],"metrics":{"calls":"1","latency":"27"},"chips":[{"node":"fproxy","text":"1 call"},{"node":"gw","text":"fan-out × 3"},{"node":"gw","text":"27 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"gw","text":"chained: 67 ms","at":"top"}]},{"tag":"PATTERN","title":"A BFF per client type","body":"Web and mobile want different things: the phone wants small payloads and few calls, the web app wants rich data. Simple fan-out can stay in the gateway, but shaping data for one client (dropping fields, merging screens, renaming) is what bloats a shared gateway. That work goes into a Backend-for-Frontend (BFF): a thin server per client type, ideally owned by the team that builds that client. It adds one hop of about 2 ms, so 29 ms in total. Here the BFF sits in front of the gateway (many teams put it behind). Either way the user's token is forwarded, so the call is still authenticated and rate-limited.","idea":"A BFF lets each client team shape its own API without bloating the shared gateway.","caption":"The mobile BFF trims the home-screen payload for the phone.","focus":["mobile","bff","gw"],"flows":[{"from":"mobile","to":"bff","kind":"request","label":"GET /home"},{"from":"bff","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /home-data"},{"from":"gw","to":"bff","kind":"response","label":"full data"},{"from":"bff","to":"mobile","kind":"response","label":"lean JSON"}],"metrics":{"calls":"1","latency":"29"},"chips":[{"node":"bff","text":"+2 ms"},{"node":"bff","text":"forwards token","at":"bottom"},{"node":"mobile","text":"lean JSON"},{"node":"mobile","text":"29 ms total","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The front door fails","body":"Every request passes through the gateway, so if it goes down, everything goes down even though all the services are healthy. That is a single point of failure (SPOF). The cure: run several gateway instances in different availability zones (separate data-center buildings), behind a redundant load balancer. Keep the gateway thin, and keep shared state such as rate-limit counters in a replicated store. Add timeouts and circuit breakers (stop calling a failing service for a while and fail fast) so one slow service cannot tie up the gateway. In the animation both clients wait out a 1 s timeout.","idea":"A gateway concentrates risk; redundancy and keeping it thin are what make that acceptable.","caption":"With one gateway instance, both client paths fail together.","focus":["gw","fproxy","bff"],"flows":[{"from":"fproxy","to":"gw","kind":"fail","label":"timeout"},{"from":"bff","to":"gw","kind":"fail","label":"timeout"}],"metrics":{"latency":"1000"},"chips":[{"node":"gw","text":"gateway down","tone":"red"},{"node":"fproxy","text":"timeout · 1 s","tone":"red"},{"node":"bff","text":"timeout · 1 s","tone":"red"},{"node":"orders","text":"services healthy","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"One door, with care","body":"A forward proxy fronts clients; a reverse proxy fronts servers. An API gateway is a reverse proxy that routes, authenticates, rate limits and can aggregate, so clients stay simple and services stay hidden. Add a BFF when client types need different shapes. Run the gateway redundantly and keep business logic out of it, so the front door does not become the single point of failure.","idea":"Centralise shared edge concerns, but keep the gateway thin and redundant.","caption":"Clients make one call; the gateway handles the rest.","focus":["web","mobile","fproxy","bff","gw","auth","orders","users","catalog"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"calls":"1","latency":"29"},"chips":[{"node":"gw","text":"route · auth · limit"},{"node":"bff","text":"one per client type"},{"node":"fproxy","text":"forward = client side"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a forward proxy from a reverse proxy?","options":["A forward proxy caches responses, but a reverse proxy never does","A forward proxy represents clients to the internet; a reverse proxy represents servers to clients","A reverse proxy only works over HTTPS connections","A forward proxy sits inside the data center in front of the database"],"answer":1,"why":"The difference is which side the proxy acts for. Clients (or the network they sit on) are set up to send traffic through a forward proxy; with a reverse proxy, clients are unaware and think it is the site."},{"q":"A service behind your gateway accepts any request that reaches it, because the gateway checks tokens. What is the main risk?","options":["Tokens become too long to send in headers","The gateway cannot route requests without services","Rate limits stop working for authenticated users","Anything that bypasses the gateway, such as another internal service or a misconfigured route, is unauthenticated"],"answer":3,"why":"Edge authentication is not a full defence. Services should still verify identity and authorize sensitive actions, since the network inside is not automatically trustworthy."},{"q":"Who should own a mobile BFF?","options":["The team that builds the mobile app, so it can change the API shape without waiting on a central team","A central platform team, so every client gets identical APIs","The database team, since the BFF reads shared data","Nobody; a BFF is generated automatically by the gateway"],"answer":0,"why":"A BFF exists to let one client team shape its own API quickly. If a central team owns it, every change queues behind them and the BFF becomes another shared bottleneck."},{"q":"Your team adds order-pricing rules and discount logic into the gateway because it is convenient. Why is this a poor idea?","options":["Gateways cannot run any business code at all","It makes rate limiting impossible to configure","It couples every team's release to one shared component and enlarges the single point of failure","It forces every client to use HTTP/1.1 only"],"answer":2,"why":"Business logic belongs in services. A fat gateway becomes a deployment bottleneck and a larger blast radius, so keep it to cross-cutting concerns."},{"q":"What is the best way to reduce the gateway as a single point of failure?","options":["Make the one instance a much bigger machine","Run several stateless instances across zones behind a load balancer, with timeouts and breakers","Let clients bypass it whenever it is slow","Move authentication into every single client"],"answer":1,"why":"Redundancy removes the single instance as a failure point, and timeouts plus circuit breakers stop one slow service from exhausting the gateway's capacity."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Forward proxy","def":"Sits on the client side and makes requests on behalf of clients. Used for filtering, caching, anonymity and controlled egress."},{"term":"Reverse proxy","def":"Sits on the server side and receives requests meant for the site, then forwards them to backends. Hides topology, terminates TLS, can cache and balance load."},{"term":"API gateway","def":"A reverse proxy specialised for APIs: routing, authentication, rate limiting, request/response shaping, and sometimes aggregation, metrics and logging."},{"term":"Rate limiting","def":"Capping requests per key or IP over time, often with a token bucket. Over-limit requests get HTTP 429 and never reach backends."},{"term":"Aggregation (fan-out)","def":"The gateway or BFF calls several services in parallel and merges the results, so clients make one call instead of many."},{"term":"BFF","def":"Backend-for-Frontend: a thin server per client type (web, mobile) that shapes data for that client, ideally owned by that client's team."},{"term":"Gateway SPOF","def":"All traffic flows through the gateway, so one instance is a single point of failure. Run several stateless instances across zones and keep logic out of it."},{"term":"Circuit breaker","def":"Stops calling a failing service for a short period and fails fast, so a slow dependency cannot exhaust the gateway's workers."}],"isNew":true}]},{"id":"caching","title":"Caching","lessons":[{"slug":"caching","title":"Caching Basics","subtitle":"Watch one hot record get read and written through a cache, and see how each policy trades speed, freshness, and safety.","minutes":12,"level":"Beginner","learn":["How cache-aside, read-through, write-through, write-back, and write-around differ","How hit ratio turns into average latency and database load","How LRU and LFU choose what to throw out when the cache is full","Where caches live between the browser and the database"],"nodes":[{"id":"browser","label":"Browser","kind":"client","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"cdn","label":"CDN edge","kind":"cdn","x":30,"y":14},{"id":"app","label":"App server","kind":"server","x":38,"y":50},{"id":"cache","label":"Shared cache","kind":"cache","x":64,"y":20},{"id":"worker","label":"Flush worker","kind":"worker","x":64,"y":84},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":90,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"browser","to":"cdn"},{"from":"browser","to":"app"},{"from":"cdn","to":"app"},{"from":"app","to":"cache"},{"from":"app","to":"db"},{"from":"cache","to":"db"},{"from":"cache","to":"worker"},{"from":"worker","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"hit","label":"Hit ratio","unit":"%"},{"id":"latency","label":"Avg read","unit":"ms"},{"id":"dbload","label":"DB reads","unit":"/s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"The same row, ten thousand times","body":"Imagine a product page that keeps asking for the same few popular rows. Say 10,000 reads arrive every second, and each database read takes about 10 ms. With no cache, the database answers all 10,000 every second, even though most answers are identical repeats. A cache is a small, fast store (usually in memory) that remembers answers so the database does not repeat the work. We assume a cache read takes about 1 ms.","idea":"A cache spends a little memory, and risks slightly old data, to avoid many slow repeated reads.","caption":"No cache yet: every read goes to the database.","focus":["app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT * ..."},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"row (10 ms)"}],"metrics":{"hit":"0","latency":"10","dbload":"10000"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"10,000 reads/s","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"10 ms per read"}]},{"tag":"CACHE-ASIDE","title":"Cache-aside: the app does the work","body":"Cache-aside (also called lazy loading) means the app does the work itself. It asks the cache first. On a miss, meaning the cache does not have the answer, the app reads the database and then saves the result in the cache with a time limit (TTL, time to live) of 300 seconds. This first read costs about 11 ms, because it pays for both the cache check and the database read. Later reads of the same key can use the saved copy.","idea":"In cache-aside the app fills the cache on demand, so only data that is really read gets cached.","caption":"Miss: the app asks the cache, falls back to the database, then fills the cache.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET user:42"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"miss"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT user 42"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"row"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"SET ttl=300s"}],"metrics":{"latency":"11"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"miss","tone":"red"},{"node":"cache","text":"ttl 300 s"},{"node":"app","text":"11 ms"}]},{"tag":"HIT RATIO","title":"A hit, and why the ratio matters","body":"Now the same key is requested again. The cache has it, so this is a hit: the answer returns in about 1 ms and the database does no work. Hit ratio is hits divided by total reads. At 90%, with 1 ms hits and 11 ms misses, average latency is 0.9 x 1 + 0.1 x 11 = 2 ms. Only the 10% of reads that miss reach the database, so it sees 1,000 reads per second instead of 10,000. Going from 90% to 99% cuts that by another 10 times, to 100 per second.","idea":"Misses are what you pay for, so even a small hit-ratio gain near the top removes a lot of database load.","caption":"Hit: answered from the cache, the database is not touched.","focus":["app","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET user:42"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"hit (1 ms)"}],"metrics":{"hit":"90","latency":"2","dbload":"1000"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"hit · 1 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"cache","text":"90%","kind":"progress","value":90,"tone":"green"},{"node":"db","text":"1,000 reads/s","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"READ-THROUGH","title":"Read-through: the cache loads itself","body":"Read-through hides the miss logic from the app. The app only ever talks to the cache. If the key is missing, the cache itself fetches it from the database, saves it, and returns it. The result is the same as cache-aside, but the loading code lives in one place (the cache layer or a library) instead of in every caller. Plain Redis does not do this by itself; a library or caching layer in front of it does the loading.","idea":"Read-through and cache-aside differ only in who loads on a miss: the cache or the application.","caption":"On a miss, the cache (not the app) fetches from the database.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"get(user:7)"},{"from":"cache","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"load user 7"},{"from":"db","to":"cache","kind":"response","label":"row"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"value"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"loads itself"},{"node":"db","text":"load user 7"}]},{"tag":"WRITE-THROUGH","title":"Write-through: update both, then confirm","body":"Now writes. In write-through, the app sends the update to the cache, which writes it to the database too, and the app hears 'ok' only after both succeed. So cached data always matches saved data, and a cache crash loses nothing. If the database write fails, the cache update must fail or be undone as well. The price: every write waits for the slower database, and you may cache values nobody reads again.","idea":"Write-through keeps cache and database in step, but each write is only as fast as the database.","caption":"The database confirms the write before the app hears 'ok'.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"PUT user:42"},{"from":"cache","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"UPDATE user 42"},{"from":"db","to":"cache","kind":"response","label":"committed"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"ok"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"updated first"},{"node":"db","text":"committed","tone":"green"},{"node":"app","text":"ack after both"}]},{"tag":"WRITE-BACK","title":"Write-back: acknowledge now, save later","body":"Write-back (also called write-behind) is the opposite bet. The cache saves the write, answers 'ok' right away, and copies it to the database later in batches. Entries changed in the cache but not yet saved are called dirty. Writes become very fast, and many updates to one key can collapse into a single database write. The danger: if the cache dies before a flush, those acknowledged writes are lost, unless the cache persists or replicates them.","idea":"Write-back is the fastest write path, and the only one that can lose data you already acknowledged.","caption":"The cache says 'ok' fast; the worker saves to the database later.","focus":["app","cache","worker","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"PUT counter"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"ok (1 ms)"},{"from":"cache","to":"worker","kind":"async","label":"dirty batch"},{"from":"worker","to":"db","kind":"async","label":"bulk UPDATE"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"ack · 1 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"cache","text":"dirty: not saved","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"bulk UPDATE"}]},{"tag":"WRITE-AROUND","title":"Write-around: skip the cache on writes","body":"Write-around sends writes straight to the database and skips the cache. It suits data that is written once and rarely read soon after, such as uploaded logs or a bulk import, because it keeps that data from filling the cache. The catch: the first read after a write is a miss, and any older cached copy of that key stays stale until it expires or is removed.","idea":"Write-around protects the cache from write-heavy, rarely read data, at the cost of a cold first read.","caption":"The write goes to the database only; the cache is untouched.","focus":["app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT log row"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"ok"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"written directly"},{"node":"cache","text":"untouched"}]},{"tag":"EVICTION","title":"The cache is full: who goes?","body":"Memory is limited, so a full cache must evict (throw out) something to make room for a new key. LRU (least recently used) removes the key untouched for the longest time, betting that recent use predicts future use. LFU (least frequently used) removes the key with the fewest accesses, which protects long-term favorites. LRU is simple and common (Redis offers it as allkeys-lru, but you must turn it on), yet a one-time bulk scan can push hot keys out. LFU resists that, but once-popular keys can linger unless counts decay.","idea":"Eviction is a bet about the future: LRU bets on recency, LFU bets on popularity.","caption":"The cache is full, so storing a new key throws out an old one.","focus":["app","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"SET new key"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"ok, evicted 1"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"full","tone":"red"},{"node":"cache","text":"evicted 1"}]},{"tag":"WHERE","title":"Caches live in layers","body":"A request can be answered at several layers. The browser caches files and responses. A CDN (content delivery network) edge serves popular content from a server near the user, and on a miss forwards to the app (its origin). The app may keep a small in-process cache, a shared cache such as Redis (an in-memory key-value store) serves all app servers, and the database keeps hot pages in its own memory. Here the logo is answered by the CDN and the user data by the shared cache, so the database is never reached. Closer to the user is faster, but harder to keep fresh.","idea":"Put each cache as close to the user as the data's freshness rules allow.","caption":"Logo: CDN hit. User data: shared-cache hit. The database is never reached.","focus":["browser","cdn","app","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"cdn","kind":"request","label":"GET /logo.png"},{"from":"cdn","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"edge hit"},{"from":"browser","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"GET /api/user"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET user:42"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"hit"},{"from":"app","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"JSON"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"cdn","text":"edge hit","tone":"green"},{"node":"cache","text":"hit","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick the policy by the data","body":"Reads: cache-aside keeps the loading logic in the app; read-through moves it into the cache. Writes: write-through is safe but slower, write-back is fast but risky, and write-around keeps write-only data out of the cache. Watch hit ratio, because it sets both latency and database load, and choose an eviction policy that fits how your data is accessed. Common pairings: read-through with write-through, or cache-aside where writes go to the database and then delete the cached key. Keeping cached copies correct over time is the next lesson, on invalidation.","idea":"Choose read and write policies per data type, then watch the hit ratio.","caption":"Five policies, one goal: fewer slow reads.","focus":["browser","cdn","app","cache","worker","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"hit":"90","latency":"2","dbload":"1000"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"90%","kind":"progress","value":90,"tone":"green"},{"node":"db","text":"1,000 reads/s","tone":"green"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Cache hits take 1 ms and misses take 11 ms (cache check plus database read). With an 80% hit ratio, what is the average read latency?","options":["About 1.2 ms","About 3 ms","About 5.5 ms","About 8 ms"],"answer":1,"why":"Average = 0.8 x 1 + 0.2 x 11 = 0.8 + 2.2 = 3 ms. Only 20% of reads are misses, but they cost 11x a hit, so they supply 2.2 of the 3 ms."},{"q":"A counter service acknowledges each increment from the cache and flushes to the database every few seconds. The cache server crashes. What is the realistic loss?","options":["Nothing, the database always has every write first","Only reads are affected","The increments not yet flushed, even though they were acknowledged","The whole database is corrupted"],"answer":2,"why":"This is write-back. Unflushed dirty entries live only in the cache, so a crash loses writes the client was told succeeded."},{"q":"A nightly report reads 5 million rows exactly once, alongside normal traffic. Which cache behavior is most at risk?","options":["LRU, because the scan makes one-time keys look recently used and pushes hot keys out","LFU, because scan keys get high counts and push out hot keys","Cache-aside TTL, because scans shorten the TTL","None, since eviction ignores access order"],"answer":0,"why":"LRU treats every just-read key as valuable, so a big scan evicts the genuinely popular keys. LFU keeps frequently used keys and resists this."},{"q":"Which data is the best fit for write-around?","options":["A user's shopping cart read on every page","A feature flag read by every request","A session token checked on each call","Audit log rows that are written constantly and rarely read back"],"answer":3,"why":"Write-around avoids filling the cache with data unlikely to be read soon. The cart, flag, and session are read-heavy and belong in the cache."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Cache-aside","def":"App checks the cache, on a miss reads the database and fills the cache itself. Most common pattern; only requested data gets cached."},{"term":"Read-through","def":"App talks only to the cache; the cache loads missing keys from the database. Same effect as cache-aside, loading logic lives in the cache layer."},{"term":"Write-through","def":"Write goes to cache and database together before the ack. Consistent and durable, but writes are as slow as the database."},{"term":"Write-back","def":"Write updates only the cache and flushes to the database later. Fastest writes, but unflushed data is lost if the cache fails."},{"term":"Write-around","def":"Write goes straight to the database, skipping the cache. Good for write-once, rarely read data; first read afterward is a miss."},{"term":"Hit ratio","def":"Hits divided by total reads. Average latency = hit% x hit time + miss% x miss time; database load = misses per second."},{"term":"LRU vs LFU","def":"LRU evicts the least recently used key; LFU evicts the least frequently used. LRU is simple but vulnerable to scans; LFU protects steady favorites."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"cache-invalidation","title":"Cache Invalidation & Stampedes","subtitle":"Watch a hot cache entry expire under load, see the database get flattened, then apply the fixes one by one.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How TTL expiry and explicit invalidation each trade freshness against complexity","Why a delete-then-refill race can leave stale data in the cache","What a cache stampede (thundering herd) is and why it hurts the database most","How request coalescing and probabilistic early expiry prevent stampedes","Why a single hot key can overload one cache node, and how to spread it"],"nodes":[{"id":"clients","label":"Clients","kind":"user","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"app","label":"App servers","kind":"server","x":36,"y":50},{"id":"cache","label":"Cache","kind":"cache","x":62,"y":20},{"id":"writer","label":"Writer / admin","kind":"worker","x":62,"y":84},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":88,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"clients","to":"app"},{"from":"app","to":"cache"},{"from":"app","to":"db"},{"from":"writer","to":"db"},{"from":"writer","to":"cache"}],"metrics":[{"id":"rps","label":"Requests/s","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"dbq","label":"DB queries/s","unit":"qps"},{"id":"hit","label":"Hit rate","unit":"%"},{"id":"lat","label":"p99 latency","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"A cache that works too well","body":"A shop's home page shows a product feed that takes the database about 200 ms to build. The site gets 5,000 reads per second, and 99.9% of them are cache hits, so the database sees only about 5 queries per second. The feed is one hot key (a cache entry everybody asks for), rebuilt about once a minute. Everything looks calm. The questions: how does old data ever get replaced, and what happens when this one entry suddenly disappears?","idea":"A cache hides load from the database, so anything that empties it can expose that load all at once.","caption":"Healthy: nearly every read is answered by the cache.","focus":["clients","app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"clients","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"GET /home"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET feed"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"hit"},{"from":"app","to":"clients","kind":"response","label":"page"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5","hit":"99.9","lat":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"99.9%","kind":"progress","value":99.9,"tone":"green"},{"node":"db","text":"5 q/s","tone":"green"},{"node":"db","text":"200 ms feed"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"TTL: let it expire","body":"The simplest way to refresh a cache is a TTL (time to live): a countdown stored with the entry. Here the app saves the feed with a 60 second TTL, and the cache silently drops it when time is up. The next reader gets a miss, rebuilds the feed from the database (about 200 ms for that one request), and saves it again. No services need to coordinate, which is why TTL is the default. The cost: data can be up to 60 seconds old.","idea":"TTL is the cheapest invalidation: no coordination, but a built-in staleness window.","caption":"A miss rebuilds the entry from the database, about 200 ms for that one request.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET feed"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"miss"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT feed"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"rows"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"SET feed, ttl 60s"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5","hit":"99.9","lat":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"miss","tone":"red"},{"node":"cache","text":"ttl 60 s"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Stale reads","body":"Now an admin changes a price in the database. The cache does not know, so it keeps serving the old feed until the TTL runs out. For up to that long, every shopper sees the old price. For a news feed that is fine. For a price, stock count, or permission it can mean selling at the wrong price or showing access a user should no longer have. A shorter TTL reduces staleness, but entries are rebuilt more often, so the database does more work.","idea":"Staleness is bounded by the TTL; a shorter TTL buys freshness with more database work.","caption":"The database has the new price, but the cache still returns the old one.","focus":["writer","db","cache","app"],"flows":[{"from":"writer","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"UPDATE price"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET feed"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"fail","label":"old price"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5","hit":"99.9","lat":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"price updated","tone":"green"},{"node":"cache","text":"old price","tone":"red"},{"node":"cache","text":"stale until ttl","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Explicit invalidation","body":"To avoid waiting, the writer deletes the cache key right after the database change commits (is saved for good). The next read misses and loads the new value. Deleting is usually better than overwriting the cache, because a delete is idempotent (safe to repeat) and never writes a value that could arrive out of order. It is not airtight: a reader that loaded the old row just before the commit can write it into the cache just after the delete. So teams keep a TTL as a safety net even when they also invalidate.","idea":"Invalidate by deleting after the write commits, and keep a TTL to repair the races you missed.","caption":"The writer saves to the database first, then deletes the cache key.","focus":["writer","db","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"writer","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"UPDATE price"},{"from":"db","to":"writer","kind":"response","label":"committed"},{"from":"writer","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"DEL feed"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5","hit":"99.9","lat":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"committed","tone":"green"},{"node":"cache","text":"key deleted"},{"node":"cache","text":"ttl kept as backstop"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The stampede","body":"Now the key disappears, by TTL or by a delete, while 5,000 requests per second arrive. Every request misses and starts its own 200 ms query. During the first rebuild, about 5,000 x 0.2 = 1,000 identical queries are sent. If the database comfortably handles about 500 queries per second, it queues, slows down, and requests time out. Failed rebuilds leave the key empty, so the pile-on continues. This is a cache stampede, also called a thundering herd.","idea":"A stampede turns one expired key into thousands of identical database queries, and the overload can prevent the refill that would end it.","caption":"Every request misses and sends its own query to the database.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET feed"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"miss x 1000"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"SELECT x 1000"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5000","hit":"0","lat":"2000"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"miss x 1000","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"5,000 q/s","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"handles ~500 q/s"}]},{"tag":"FIX","title":"Request coalescing","body":"The fix: let only one request rebuild. The first request to miss takes a short lock (in one process, a single-flight map; across servers, a cache entry created only if absent, with a short expiry). Only that request queries the database. Others wait for its result, or briefly serve the previous value if you kept one. The database sees one query instead of a thousand, and waiters pay at most one rebuild time (about 200 ms). Give the lock its own timeout so a crashed leader cannot block the key forever.","idea":"Coalescing collapses many identical misses into one rebuild; always give the lock an expiry.","caption":"One request rebuilds the entry; the others wait up to about 200 ms.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"lock + GET"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"1 x SELECT"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"rows"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"SET feed"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5","hit":"99.9","lat":"200"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"lock held"},{"node":"db","text":"1 query","tone":"green"},{"node":"app","text":"waiters wait 200 ms"}]},{"tag":"FIX","title":"Refresh before it expires","body":"Coalescing still makes some people wait. Probabilistic early expiry avoids even that. Each request that reads the entry may randomly decide to rebuild it early. The chance rises as expiry gets closer, and rises with how long a rebuild takes. One lucky request refreshes the entry while everyone else keeps getting the still-valid value. The entry is usually replaced before it expires, so a mass miss becomes very unlikely. Stale-while-revalidate, which serves the old value during a refresh, is a close cousin.","idea":"Rebuilding slightly early, at random, means the key is never empty when the crowd arrives.","caption":"One request refreshes early in the background; the rest still hit.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET feed"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"hit"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"async","label":"early refresh"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"async","label":"rows"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"async","label":"SET feed"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5","hit":"99.9","lat":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"hit","tone":"green"},{"node":"db","text":"early refresh"},{"node":"cache","text":"serves valid value","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Many keys, one deadline","body":"A related trap: a warm-up job or bulk load writes 100,000 keys, all with a 60 second TTL. They all expire together, so the database faces a stampede across many different keys at once. Suppose 40 percent of requests (2,000 of 5,000 per second) land on keys that just expired. Coalescing works per key, so it cannot shrink 100,000 distinct queries. The cheap fix is jitter: add a random amount, say plus or minus 10 percent, to each TTL so expiries spread out. Make the window wide enough that rebuilds stay under what the database can handle.","idea":"Add random jitter to TTLs so keys written together do not expire together.","caption":"Keys that expire in the same second overwhelm the database.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET many keys"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"2,000 misses/s"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"2,000 queries/s"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"2000","hit":"60","lat":"800"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"100,000 keys · 60 s"},{"node":"cache","text":"60%","kind":"progress","value":60,"tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"2,000 q/s","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Hot keys","body":"Even with no misses, one key can be the problem. Suppose a celebrity's profile is read 150,000 times per second. That key lives on one cache node, and a single node serves roughly 100,000 simple operations per second (a rule of thumb; large values hit network limits sooner). That node is overloaded while other nodes sit idle. Fixes: a tiny in-process cache on each app server with a 1 to 5 second TTL, or several copies under keys like profile#1 to profile#8, read at random so reads spread out. The cost: a write must delete every copy, and a local cache can be stale for its TTL.","idea":"Hot keys are a load-spreading problem: copy the value so reads fan out across nodes.","caption":"Reads are spread over copies of the hot key (profile#1 to #8).","focus":["app","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET profile#3"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"hit"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET profile#7"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"hit"}],"metrics":{"rps":"150000","dbq":"15","hit":"99.99","lat":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"150,000 reads/s","tone":"red"},{"node":"cache","text":"~100,000 ops/s cap"},{"node":"app","text":"local ttl 1-5 s"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Keep the herd away","body":"TTL is simple but leaves a window of stale data. Deleting the key on write shrinks that window, with a TTL kept as a backstop. An empty hot key sends every request to the database at once, so coalesce rebuilds, refresh early at random, and add jitter to TTLs. For hot keys, copy the value across nodes or cache it locally for a second or two. Measure miss bursts, not just the average hit rate.","idea":"Plan for the moment the cache is empty, not only for the moment it is full.","caption":"Healthy again: few DB queries, high hit rate.","focus":["clients","app","cache","writer","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5","hit":"99.9","lat":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"ttl + delete"},{"node":"db","text":"5 q/s","tone":"green"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A key with a 60 s TTL is read 2,000 times per second and takes 500 ms to rebuild. It expires with no protection. Roughly how many database queries fire for it before the first rebuild finishes?","options":["1","About 100","About 1,000","About 120,000"],"answer":2,"why":"Every request that arrives during the 500 ms rebuild also misses: 2,000 req/s x 0.5 s = about 1,000 duplicate queries."},{"q":"Why do many systems delete the cache key after a write instead of writing the new value into the cache?","options":["Deleting is faster than any write","A delete is idempotent and never writes a possibly out-of-order value; the next read loads fresh data","Caches cannot store updated values","It removes the need for a TTL"],"answer":1,"why":"Setting a value from the writer can race with other writers and store an out-of-order result. A delete is safe to repeat, and the next reader rebuilds from the source of truth. It does not remove every race, which is why a TTL stays as a backstop."},{"q":"Which change best protects against 100,000 different keys all expiring in the same second after a bulk load?","options":["Coalescing requests per key","Making the TTL shorter","Adding a larger cache","Adding random jitter to each TTL"],"answer":3,"why":"Per-key coalescing cannot merge queries for different keys. Jitter spreads the expiry times so the rebuild work is spread out over time."},{"q":"One key receives 150,000 reads per second, more than one cache node can serve, and the node is saturated, though the hit rate is 99.99%. What is the right fix?","options":["Spread reads over copies of the key or add a short-lived in-process cache","Raise the TTL so it expires less often","Add request coalescing","Switch to explicit invalidation"],"answer":0,"why":"This is a hot key problem, not a miss problem: 150,000 reads per second exceeds the roughly 100,000 operations per second one node can handle. The load is concentrated on one node, so you copy the value to spread reads across nodes or keep it in each app server's memory for a second or two."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"TTL","def":"Time to live: how long a cached entry may be served before it is dropped. Bounds staleness without any coordination."},{"term":"Explicit invalidation","def":"Writers delete (or update) the cache key when the source data changes. Fresher than TTL but needs every write path to remember to do it."},{"term":"Cache stampede","def":"Many requests miss the same key at once and all hit the database to rebuild it. Also called a thundering herd or dogpile."},{"term":"Request coalescing","def":"Only one request rebuilds a missing key; others wait or use the old value. Also called single-flight. Give the lock a timeout."},{"term":"Probabilistic early expiry","def":"Each reader may refresh early with a rising probability near expiry, so the key is replaced before it expires."},{"term":"Stale-while-revalidate","def":"Serve the old value while one background request fetches a new one. Trades a little staleness for no waiting."},{"term":"TTL jitter","def":"Add a random offset, such as plus or minus 10 percent, to TTLs so keys written together do not expire together."},{"term":"Hot key","def":"A single key with far more traffic than others. It can saturate one cache node. Fix by replicating it or caching locally with a short TTL."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"bloom-filters","title":"Bloom Filters & Sketches","subtitle":"Watch a tiny bit array skip useless disk reads, then see how two more sketches count events and unique users in kilobytes.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How a Bloom filter answers 'definitely not here' or 'maybe here' using bits and hashes","Why false positives happen, why false negatives do not, and how bits per key set the error rate","How a filter in front of a database or cache avoids reads for keys that do not exist","How a Count-Min sketch estimates how often something happened, and which way it errs","How HyperLogLog counts unique items with about 12 KB, and why sketches merge across servers"],"nodes":[{"id":"app","label":"App server","kind":"server","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"bloom","label":"Bloom filter","kind":"cache","x":33,"y":18},{"id":"db","label":"Database (disk)","kind":"db","x":62,"y":18},{"id":"events","label":"Event stream","kind":"queue","x":33,"y":82},{"id":"cms","label":"Count-Min sketch","kind":"service","x":62,"y":58},{"id":"hll","label":"HyperLogLog","kind":"service","x":88,"y":84}],"edges":[{"from":"app","to":"bloom"},{"from":"bloom","to":"db"},{"from":"app","to":"db"},{"from":"app","to":"events"},{"from":"app","to":"cms"},{"from":"events","to":"cms"},{"from":"events","to":"hll"}],"metrics":[{"id":"reads","label":"Disk reads","unit":"per 1000"},{"id":"mem","label":"Memory","unit":"KB"},{"id":"err","label":"Typical error","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Asking for things that are not there","body":"Picture a lookup service that gets 1,000 requests for user IDs that do not exist: typos, scrapers guessing IDs, stale links. Without help, each request misses the cache (or there is none), then reads the disk only to learn there is nothing there. Each of those reads is wasted work. Can we answer 'not here' cheaply, without keeping every key in memory?","idea":"Lookups for absent keys are pure waste: they pay the full read path and return nothing.","caption":"Every request for a missing key still costs a disk read.","focus":["app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"get user:9"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"not found"}],"metrics":{"reads":"1000","mem":"0","err":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"1,000 wasted reads","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"not found"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"A Bloom filter: bits and hashes","body":"A Bloom filter is a long row of bits, all 0 at the start. To add a key, run it through k different hash functions (each turns the key into a bit position) and set those k bits to 1. For 1 million keys we use about 10 million bits, roughly 1.25 MB, with k = 7. The keys are never stored, only their bit pattern. The filter sits in memory next to the app or inside the database, so a check takes nanoseconds, not a network call. It must hold every existing key and be updated on every insert, or a real key will be wrongly rejected.","idea":"Adding a key flips k bits to 1; the filter stores no keys, only bit patterns.","caption":"Each new key sets 7 bits in the filter.","focus":["app","bloom"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"bloom","kind":"async","label":"add user:42"}],"metrics":{"mem":"1250"},"chips":[{"node":"bloom","text":"10M bits · 1.25 MB"},{"node":"bloom","text":"k = 7"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"A 'no' is always true","body":"To check a key, hash it the same k ways and look at those bits. If even one bit is 0, the key was never added, because adding it would have set that bit. The filter answers 'definitely not here' and the app skips the disk read entirely. Bits are never cleared, so this 'no' can never be wrong.","idea":"One zero bit proves absence: Bloom filters have no false negatives.","caption":"A zero bit means: skip the disk read.","focus":["app","bloom"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"bloom","kind":"request","label":"has user:9?"},{"from":"bloom","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"no, skip disk"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"bloom","text":"definitely not","tone":"green"},{"node":"bloom","text":"skip disk","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"A 'yes' only means maybe","body":"If all k bits are 1, the key may have been added, or other keys may have set those same bits by coincidence. The filter can only say 'maybe here', so the app still checks the real store. If that check finds nothing, the filter gave a false positive. It costs one wasted read, which is the price of the whole scheme: a few extra reads in exchange for skipping thousands.","idea":"All-ones means 'maybe': the filter can wrongly say yes, never wrongly say no.","caption":"The filter said maybe, the disk had nothing: one wasted read.","focus":["app","bloom","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"bloom","kind":"request","label":"has user:77?"},{"from":"bloom","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"maybe"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"get user:77"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"fail","label":"not found"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"bloom","text":"maybe"},{"node":"bloom","text":"false positive","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"wasted read"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Sizing the filter","body":"How often does the filter wrongly say 'maybe'? That depends on bits per key. As a rule of thumb, with the best k: about 5 bits per key gives roughly 10% false positives, 10 bits gives about 1%, and 14 to 15 bits gives about 0.1%. At 10 bits per key, our 1,000 absent lookups drop from 1,000 disk reads to about 10. You pay the memory once, then every absent-key lookup skips the disk. If most lookups find their key, the filter helps little. In front of a cache, it also stops absent-key lookups from falling through to the database every time (cache penetration).","idea":"Each extra 5 bits per key cuts false positives roughly ten-fold, at linear memory cost.","caption":"10 bits per key: about 1 in 100 slips through.","focus":["bloom"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"reads":"10","mem":"1250","err":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"bloom","text":"10 bits/key"},{"node":"bloom","text":"~1% false pos"},{"node":"db","text":"1,000 → ~10 reads","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"LIMIT","title":"What a Bloom filter cannot do","body":"A Bloom filter has limits. You cannot delete a key, because clearing its bits could erase bits shared with other keys and create false negatives. You cannot list the keys or read a value back. And if you add far more keys than you sized for, more bits fill up and false positives climb. Variants such as counting Bloom filters or cuckoo filters add deletion, and many systems simply rebuild the filter from time to time.","idea":"Size for the expected key count; a filter only grows more wrong as it fills, and it cannot forget.","caption":"A delete is refused: its bits may be shared with other keys.","focus":["app","bloom"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"bloom","kind":"fail","label":"delete user:42?"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"bloom","text":"no delete","tone":"red"},{"node":"bloom","text":"more keys, more errors","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Count-Min sketch: how often?","body":"A different question: how many times was each video viewed? A Count-Min sketch is a small grid of counters, say 5 rows by 2,719 columns. Each view goes onto an event stream (a queue of events). For every event, the sketch hashes the video ID once per row and adds 1 to one counter in each row. Memory is about 54 KB (5 x 2,719 four-byte counters), no matter how many different videos exist.","idea":"A Count-Min sketch keeps approximate counts for unlimited items in fixed memory.","caption":"Each event adds 1 to one counter in every row.","focus":["app","events","cms"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"events","kind":"async","label":"view video 7"},{"from":"events","to":"cms","kind":"async","label":"+1 in 5 rows"}],"metrics":{"mem":"54","err":"0.1"},"chips":[{"node":"cms","text":"5 × 2,719 counters"},{"node":"cms","text":"54 KB","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Reading a count: take the minimum","body":"To estimate a count, hash the item in each row and take the smallest of the 5 counters it points to. Other items collide into the same counters and only ever add to them, so every counter is at least the true count, and the smallest is the least inflated. The estimate can be too high but never too low. With this grid, the overshoot is at most about 0.1% of all events counted, with 99% confidence. So rare items can look far more popular than they are; Count-Min is best for finding frequent items.","idea":"Count-Min overestimates, never underestimates; the minimum across rows trims the damage.","caption":"Estimate = the smallest counter across the rows.","focus":["app","cms"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cms","kind":"request","label":"count(video 7)?"},{"from":"cms","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"about 4,210"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"cms","text":"about 4,210"},{"node":"cms","text":"never too low","tone":"green"},{"node":"cms","text":"over ≤ 0.1% of total"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"HyperLogLog: how many different?","body":"A third question: how many different users? Counting exactly needs a set of every ID: a billion 8-byte IDs is about 8 GB before overhead. HyperLogLog hashes each ID and watches for rare patterns, like a hash starting with many 0 bits. Seeing 20 leading zeros suggests about a million different items were hashed, like a streak of 20 heads in coin flips. It splits items into 16,384 buckets and combines them with a harmonic mean (which resists outliers better than a plain average). Result: about 0.8% error in 12 KB. Two HyperLogLogs from different servers merge by taking the max of each bucket, if they use the same hash and bucket count.","idea":"HyperLogLog estimates unique counts in about 12 KB and merges cleanly across machines.","caption":"Unique users counted in 12 KB.","focus":["events","hll"],"flows":[{"from":"events","to":"hll","kind":"async","label":"hash(user id)"}],"metrics":{"mem":"12","err":"0.81"},"chips":[{"node":"hll","text":"12 KB","tone":"green"},{"node":"hll","text":"0.8% error"},{"node":"hll","text":"16,384 buckets"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick the sketch by the question","body":"Is this key in the set? Use a Bloom filter: 'no' is certain, 'yes' is only maybe. How many times did this item occur? Use Count-Min: counts can be too high, never too low. How many different items were there? Use HyperLogLog: a small error that can go either way. All three trade a little accuracy for far less memory than storing every key or count. Use them where an approximate answer is fine, never where you need exactness.","idea":"Sketches trade a small, bounded error for far less memory than exact structures: Count-Min and HyperLogLog stay fixed in size, and a Bloom filter needs only about 10 bits per key.","caption":"Three questions, three small structures.","focus":["bloom","cms","hll"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"bloom","text":"no = certain","tone":"green"},{"node":"cms","text":"never too low"},{"node":"hll","text":"~0.8% error"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A Bloom filter in front of a database answers 'no' for a key. What should the app do?","options":["Query the database anyway to confirm","Retry the filter with different hash functions","Skip the database: the key is definitely absent","Add the key to the filter and carry on"],"answer":2,"why":"A 'no' means at least one required bit is 0. Since bits are never cleared, the key was never added. Bloom filters have no false negatives."},{"q":"A filter with a 1% false-positive rate guards a store. 1,000,000 lookups arrive, all for keys that do not exist. About how many disk reads happen?","options":["About 10,000","About 1,000,000","About 100","Zero, since the filter is exact"],"answer":0,"why":"Only false positives pass through the filter, so about 1% of 1,000,000 = 10,000 reads, instead of 1,000,000 without the filter."},{"q":"Your Count-Min sketch says an item occurred 5,300 times. Which statement is correct?","options":["The true count is exactly 5,300","The true count is at most 5,300, possibly less","The true count may be above or below 5,300 equally","The true count is at least 5,300, possibly more"],"answer":1,"why":"Collisions only add to counters, so estimates are never below the true count. The true count is therefore at most 5,300."},{"q":"Fifty servers each track unique daily visitors. You need one global unique count without shipping all IDs. Which fits best?","options":["A Bloom filter per server, summing the 'yes' answers","A Count-Min sketch per server, summing the counters","A plain counter per server, added together","A HyperLogLog per server, merged by taking the max of each bucket"],"answer":3,"why":"Visitors seen on several servers would be double-counted by a plain sum. HyperLogLogs merge by per-bucket max, which removes duplicates, in about 12 KB each."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Bloom filter","def":"Bit array plus k hashes. Answers 'definitely not in set' or 'maybe in set'. No false negatives, some false positives, no deletes."},{"term":"False positive rate","def":"Rule of thumb with best k: about 10% at 5 bits per key, 1% at 10, 0.1% at about 14-15. Optimal k is about 0.7 times bits per key."},{"term":"Where it is used","def":"In front of disk reads (e.g. LSM-tree database files), caches, and databases, so lookups for absent keys skip the expensive read."},{"term":"Count-Min sketch","def":"Grid of counters with one hash per row. Add: +1 in each row. Estimate: min across rows. Overestimates only."},{"term":"Heavy hitters","def":"The most frequent items. Count-Min plus a small top-N list finds trending items without a counter per item."},{"term":"HyperLogLog","def":"Estimates distinct count from leading-zero patterns of hashes. About 12 KB gives roughly 0.8% error, for billions of items."},{"term":"Mergeable","def":"Bloom filters merge by OR, Count-Min by adding counters, HyperLogLog by max per bucket, so shards can be combined (Bloom and Count-Min need same size and hashes)."},{"term":"When not to use","def":"When you need exact answers, deletion, or the actual keys: money, inventory, billing, access control."}],"isNew":true}]},{"id":"data","title":"Data & Storage","lessons":[{"slug":"sql-nosql","title":"SQL vs NoSQL","subtitle":"Watch one online shop send each kind of data to the store whose shape fits its access pattern.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How relational tables, schemas and joins let you ask questions you did not plan for","What key-value, document, wide-column and graph stores each optimize","Why NoSQL stores usually require you to design around known queries","How to choose a store by access pattern instead of by hype","Why most systems start with one relational database and add others only when forced"],"nodes":[{"id":"app","label":"Shop app server","kind":"server","x":12,"y":50},{"id":"sql","label":"Relational (SQL)","kind":"db","x":60,"y":10},{"id":"kv","label":"Key-value store","kind":"cache","x":60,"y":30},{"id":"doc","label":"Document store","kind":"db","x":60,"y":50},{"id":"wide","label":"Wide-column store","kind":"db","x":60,"y":70},{"id":"graph","label":"Graph database","kind":"db","x":60,"y":90}],"edges":[{"from":"app","to":"sql"},{"from":"app","to":"kv"},{"from":"app","to":"doc"},{"from":"app","to":"wide"},{"from":"app","to":"graph"}],"metrics":[{"id":"latency","label":"Typical op","unit":"ms"},{"id":"ops","label":"Ops per node","unit":"ops/s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One shop, five kinds of data","body":"An online shop stores five kinds of data: orders, login sessions, a product catalog, click events, and a 'people also bought' network. Each kind is written and read in a different way. Should they all share one database, or should each get its own? The numbers in this lesson are rough single-server figures that show scale, not benchmarks.","idea":"Pick a database by how the data is read and written, not by whether it is called SQL or NoSQL.","caption":"The shop app has five kinds of data and five possible stores.","focus":["app"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[]},{"tag":"RELATIONAL","title":"Orders in tables with a schema","body":"Watch checkout. The app sends one transaction (a group of writes that all succeed or all fail) to a relational (SQL) database. It keeps data in tables of rows and columns, like linked spreadsheets: customers, orders, order_items. The schema (the declared columns and types) is a contract the database enforces, including foreign keys (a column that must point at a real row in another table). Three tables are written, then COMMIT confirms all three at once. A well-tuned PostgreSQL server handles thousands of such writes per second (8,000 here).","idea":"A relational schema trades flexibility for guarantees: the database itself rejects bad or half-written data.","caption":"Checkout: one transaction writes three tables, then the database replies COMMIT ok.","focus":["app","sql"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"sql","kind":"request","label":"BEGIN; INSERT x3"},{"from":"sql","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"COMMIT ok"}],"metrics":{"latency":"3","ops":"8000"},"chips":[{"node":"sql","text":"schema enforced","tone":"green"},{"node":"sql","text":"8,000 ops/s"},{"node":"sql","text":"3 ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"JOINS","title":"Ask a question nobody planned for","body":"Next quarter someone asks: what is the revenue per product category for customers who signed up in Berlin? Relational data is normalized, meaning each fact is stored once in its own table. So one SQL query can join (link up rows from) customers, orders, items and products to answer it. Nobody had to predict this question when the tables were designed. With indexes, a shop-sized dataset answers in about 800 ms. Heavy reports like this usually move to a replica or warehouse, covered later.","idea":"Normalized tables plus joins let you answer new questions without changing how the data is stored.","caption":"One query joins four tables on demand and returns the totals.","focus":["app","sql"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"sql","kind":"request","label":"JOIN 4 tables"},{"from":"sql","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"aggregated rows"}],"metrics":{"latency":"800","ops":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"sql","text":"ad hoc join"},{"node":"sql","text":"~800 ms","at":"bottom"},{"node":"sql","text":"no redesign","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"KEY-VALUE","title":"Sessions: give me the value for this key","body":"A login session is read on every request, and only ever by its session id. A key-value store such as Redis maps a key to a value and is built for exactly that lookup. There are no joins or ad hoc queries, and Redis keeps data in memory. So the app's GET returns in about 0.5 ms, and one node serves 100,000 or more operations per second. Disk-based stores like DynamoDB take a few milliseconds. An in-memory store needs persistence or replication if sessions must survive a restart.","idea":"A key-value store is the fastest design when the only question is 'what is stored under this key?'","caption":"The app asks for one session id and gets the session back in 0.5 ms.","focus":["app","kv"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"kv","kind":"request","label":"GET sess:9f2c"},{"from":"kv","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"session blob"}],"metrics":{"latency":"0.5","ops":"100000"},"chips":[{"node":"kv","text":"0.5 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"kv","text":"100k ops/s","at":"bottom"},{"node":"kv","text":"key lookup"}]},{"tag":"DOCUMENT","title":"Catalog: one product, one document","body":"A laptop has RAM and ports; a T-shirt has size and fabric. Forcing both into one table leaves many empty columns or needs many joined tables. A document store such as MongoDB keeps each product as one JSON-like document with whatever fields it needs, so one read returns the whole product page (2 ms here). The cost: by default your code, not the database, enforces the shape (some stores offer optional validation), and joins across documents are limited and often slower at scale.","idea":"Documents fit data that is read and written as one self-contained unit with varying fields.","caption":"One lookup by SKU returns the whole product document.","focus":["app","doc"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"doc","kind":"request","label":"find sku=L-204"},{"from":"doc","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"product JSON"}],"metrics":{"latency":"2","ops":"20000"},"chips":[{"node":"doc","text":"1 read = 1 doc"},{"node":"doc","text":"2 ms","at":"bottom"},{"node":"doc","text":"schema in code"}]},{"tag":"WIDE-COLUMN","title":"Click events: huge write volume","body":"During a sale the shop logs 200,000 click events per second. Spread over a 5-node cluster, each node takes about 40,000 writes per second. A wide-column store such as Cassandra or ScyllaDB splits data by a partition key (say user id) and sorts rows inside each partition by a clustering key such as time. Writes are cheap appends. 'Latest 50 events for user 42' reads one sorted slice from one partition. The catch: you design each table around a known query, and other queries are slow. These stores often favor availability, so reads can be slightly stale (see Replication).","idea":"Wide-column stores scale writes by making every query hit one partition, so you design the table around the query.","caption":"The app appends clicks, then reads one sorted slice for user 42.","focus":["app","wide"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"wide","kind":"async","label":"append click"},{"from":"app","to":"wide","kind":"request","label":"last 50, user 42"},{"from":"wide","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"sorted slice"}],"metrics":{"latency":"2","ops":"40000"},"chips":[{"node":"wide","text":"200k writes/s"},{"node":"wide","text":"40k/node","at":"bottom"},{"node":"wide","text":"5 nodes"}]},{"tag":"GRAPH","title":"Recommendations: follow the relationships","body":"'Customers who bought this also bought...' means walking from a product to its buyers, then to their other purchases: two or three hops. In SQL each hop is another join (here a self-join, a table joined to itself). With indexes, two or three hops are fine, but results multiply and deep or variable-length paths get slow and awkward. Many graph databases store each node's neighbours directly, so a walk costs about the number of links visited (5 ms here), not the table sizes. Real recommenders often precompute results, so graphs earn their place when live path queries are the product.","idea":"Pick a graph store when queries are about paths and neighbours several hops deep.","caption":"The app asks for items 2-3 hops away and the graph follows stored links.","focus":["app","graph"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"graph","kind":"request","label":"2-3 hops from item"},{"from":"graph","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"related items"}],"metrics":{"latency":"5","ops":"8000"},"chips":[{"node":"graph","text":"2-3 hops"},{"node":"graph","text":"5 ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Run the finance report on the document store","body":"Suppose orders had gone into the document store because it was fashionable. The revenue-by-category question now needs data from three collections. A document store can join (MongoDB has $lookup), but its joins are limited and are not what the model is built for. So you either combine orders, products and customers in application code, or you copy product and customer fields into every order. Here the app fetches a quarter of orders and joins them in code, which takes about 3 seconds. Copied fields must then be kept in sync.","idea":"NoSQL stores make known queries fast by pre-shaping data; unplanned queries then cost you code or copies.","caption":"The app pulls three collections and joins them itself, which takes seconds.","focus":["app","doc"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"doc","kind":"request","label":"read 3 collections"},{"from":"doc","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"3 result sets"}],"metrics":{"latency":"3000","ops":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"doc","text":"$lookup limited","tone":"red"},{"node":"app","text":"join in code"},{"node":"doc","text":"seconds","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Start boring, add stores on evidence","body":"A common mistake is adopting five databases on day one. As a rule of thumb, one relational database with good indexes and read replicas handles most products well past a million users, and it gives you transactions, constraints and joins. Add a key-value cache when reads hurt: the app checks the cache first, reads SQL only on a miss, then fills the cache so later reads take 0.5 ms. Add wide-column or time-series storage when writes outgrow one node, and a graph store when traversals dominate. Each extra store is more to run, and cached copies can go stale, so decide when they expire.","idea":"Every extra database is one more thing to run, back up and keep in sync, so add one only for a measured need.","caption":"Cache miss: the app reads SQL, then fills the cache. Later reads hit it at 0.5 ms.","focus":["app","sql","kv"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"kv","kind":"request","label":"GET first"},{"from":"kv","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"miss"},{"from":"app","to":"sql","kind":"request","label":"on miss, read"},{"from":"sql","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"row"},{"from":"app","to":"kv","kind":"async","label":"fill cache"}],"metrics":{"latency":"0.5","ops":"100000"},"chips":[{"node":"sql","text":"default store"},{"node":"kv","text":"checked first"},{"node":"kv","text":"0.5 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Choose by access pattern","body":"Relational suits structured data, joins and transactions, and can answer questions nobody planned. Key-value is fastest for lookups by key. Documents suit self-contained records with varying fields. Wide-column scales heavy writes when queries are known and fit one partition. Graphs suit deep relationship queries. Ask how the data is read and written, how consistent it must be, and how big it gets. The lines blur: Postgres stores JSON in JSONB columns, and MongoDB offers multi-document transactions. So choose by access pattern, not by label.","idea":"List your queries first; the store that serves them directly is usually the right one.","caption":"Each store matches one access pattern.","focus":["app","sql","kv","doc","wide","graph"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"sql","text":"joins + txns"},{"node":"kv","text":"key lookup"},{"node":"wide","text":"heavy writes"},{"node":"graph","text":"deep paths"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your team must answer a brand-new analytics question tomorrow that combines customers, orders and products, and nobody planned for it. Which design makes that easiest?","options":["A key-value store keyed by order id","Normalized relational tables queried with joins","A wide-column table designed for the click-event query","Denormalized documents with product data copied into each order"],"answer":1,"why":"Normalized tables store each fact once, so joins can combine them in ways nobody anticipated. The other designs were shaped around specific known queries."},{"q":"A service only ever fetches a user's session blob by session id, millions of times per second. What is the best fit?","options":["A graph database","A relational database with many joins","A document store with secondary indexes on every field","A key-value store"],"answer":3,"why":"The only access pattern is lookup by key, which is exactly what key-value stores optimize, with no query planner or joins to pay for."},{"q":"In a wide-column store you need to list a user's latest events, and later also list all events from one country. Why is the second query a problem?","options":["The table is partitioned by user, so a country query would scan every partition unless you add a second table laid out by country","Wide-column stores cannot store country values at all, because columns must be declared up front for every row","Country lookups require a graph traversal, because a wide-column store cannot follow relationships otherwise","Wide-column stores only support writes, so every read has to go through a separate search engine"],"answer":0,"why":"Data is placed by partition key and efficient queries must supply it. A different query needs a differently keyed copy of the data."},{"q":"Which statement about 'NoSQL scales, SQL does not' is most accurate?","options":["It is true for writes only, because SQL databases serialize all writes on one machine","It is false: NoSQL stores are slower than SQL on every workload, so they never scale better","It oversimplifies: NoSQL stores often scale out by limiting query types, while a single SQL node already handles very large workloads","It is true because NoSQL stores never need indexes and so avoid the overhead that limits SQL"],"answer":2,"why":"Scale-out comes mostly from restricting queries to one partition, not from the data model's label. One relational node often serves many products, and databases that spread SQL across many nodes also exist."},{"q":"You store each order as a document that embeds a copy of the product name and price. A product is renamed. What is the main consequence?","options":["Old orders automatically update, because the store tracks references to the product","Order reads become slower, because each document must be rewritten on every read","The document store rejects the rename until every order embedding the old name is migrated","Existing orders keep the old copy, so you decide whether to leave or update them"],"answer":3,"why":"Embedding duplicates data for fast single-read access. Sometimes keeping the price paid at purchase time is desired, but other copies need explicit synchronization."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Schema","def":"The declared structure of data. SQL enforces it on write; many NoSQL stores leave it to application code by default (schema-on-read, meaning the shape is interpreted when data is read), though some offer optional validation."},{"term":"Normalization","def":"Storing each fact once across related tables to avoid duplication. Joins reassemble the data at query time."},{"term":"Denormalization","def":"Copying data into the place it is read to avoid joins. Reads get faster; writes must keep copies in sync."},{"term":"Key-value store","def":"Maps a key to a value and is optimized for lookup by key; no joins or ad hoc queries (some stores add data structures or sort keys). Examples: Redis, DynamoDB."},{"term":"Document store","def":"Stores self-contained JSON-like records with flexible fields. Good for read-as-a-unit data such as catalogs. Joins across collections exist (MongoDB $lookup) but are limited compared with a relational engine's joins. Example: MongoDB."},{"term":"Wide-column store","def":"Partitions rows by a key and sorts them within the partition. Built for heavy writes and known queries. Examples: Cassandra, ScyllaDB."},{"term":"Graph database","def":"Stores nodes and edges and traverses relationships directly. Best for multi-hop questions like recommendations or fraud rings."},{"term":"Choosing rule","def":"Write down your top queries and consistency needs first. Default to relational; add a specialized store only for a measured mismatch."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"indexes","title":"Indexes","subtitle":"Watch one query drop from reading 125,000 pages to a few dozen, then to three, and see what that speed costs on every write.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How a B-tree index finds a row in a handful of page reads","Why the column order of a composite index decides which queries it can serve","How a covering index skips the table lookup entirely","Why every extra index slows down inserts, updates, and deletes","When the database correctly chooses a full scan over an index"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Client","kind":"client","x":9,"y":50},{"id":"app","label":"Database engine","kind":"service","x":31,"y":50},{"id":"index","label":"B-tree index","kind":"db","x":58,"y":20},{"id":"table","label":"Table (data pages)","kind":"storage","x":58,"y":80},{"id":"pool","label":"Buffer pool (RAM)","kind":"cache","x":87,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"app"},{"from":"app","to":"index"},{"from":"app","to":"table"},{"from":"index","to":"table"},{"from":"index","to":"pool"},{"from":"table","to":"pool"},{"from":"app","to":"pool"}],"metrics":[{"id":"pages","label":"Pages touched","unit":"pages"},{"id":"rows","label":"Rows examined","unit":"rows"},{"id":"ms","label":"Latency","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One query, ten million rows","body":"An orders table holds 10 million rows of about 100 bytes each. The database stores them in 8 KB chunks called pages, about 125,000 pages or 1 GB in total. A user opens their order history, which runs SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42. That customer has about 50 orders. How can the database find those 50 rows among 10 million?","idea":"Finding a few rows among millions is the problem an index exists to solve.","caption":"A query arrives asking for one customer's orders.","focus":["client","app"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"customer_id = 42"}],"metrics":{"pages":"0","rows":"0","ms":"0"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"FULL SCAN","title":"Without an index: read everything","body":"Watch the animation: with no index on customer_id, the engine has no shortcut. It reads every page of the table and checks every row. Pages pass through the buffer pool, the database's RAM cache of recently used pages. That is 125,000 pages and 10 million row checks to return just 50 rows. Sequential reads are fast, but 1 GB of work still takes about 800 ms here, and it grows in step with the table.","idea":"A full scan costs time proportional to table size, no matter how few rows match.","caption":"Every table page is read and every row checked to find 50 rows.","focus":["app","table","pool"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"table","kind":"request","label":"scan all pages"},{"from":"table","to":"pool","kind":"response","label":"125,000 pages"},{"from":"pool","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"10M rows checked"}],"metrics":{"pages":"125000","rows":"10000000","ms":"800"},"chips":[{"node":"table","text":"125,000 pages","tone":"red"},{"node":"table","text":"10M row checks","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"app","text":"~800 ms","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"A B-tree is a sorted map of pages","body":"An index is a separate structure that keeps one column's values in sorted order, each with a pointer to its table row. Think of the index at the back of a book. Most databases use a B-tree (strictly a B+tree, where row pointers live only in the bottom 'leaf' pages). It is a shallow tree whose pages each hold hundreds of keys. Upper pages work like signposts and leaf pages hold the sorted entries. With about 300 keys per page, three levels cover roughly 27 million entries. Leaves are linked in order, so a range or ORDER BY just walks along them.","idea":"A wide, shallow tree means any key is only a few page reads away, even in a huge table.","caption":"The index is a small sorted structure beside the table, pointing at rows.","focus":["index","table"],"flows":[{"from":"index","to":"table","kind":"async","label":"key -> row pointer"}],"metrics":{"pages":"0","rows":"0","ms":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"index","text":"B+tree"},{"node":"index","text":"~300 keys/page","at":"bottom"},{"node":"index","text":"3 levels = 27M"}]},{"tag":"SEEK","title":"With an index: seek, then fetch","body":"Now the engine walks the tree: the root page, one middle page, then a leaf page. The leaf lists the 50 entries for customer 42 side by side. For each entry the engine follows the pointer to the table page holding the full row. The top levels are used constantly, so they stay in RAM. The total is about 3 index pages plus up to one table page per row: roughly 53 pages instead of 125,000, and about 3 ms.","idea":"An index seek reads a handful of pages instead of the whole table; cost grows with tree depth, not table size.","caption":"The engine walks the tree to the leaf, then fetches the 50 matching rows.","focus":["client","app","index","table","pool"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"index","kind":"request","label":"seek 42"},{"from":"pool","to":"index","kind":"response","label":"top levels cached"},{"from":"index","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"50 pointers"},{"from":"app","to":"table","kind":"request","label":"fetch rows"},{"from":"table","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"50 rows"},{"from":"app","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"result"}],"metrics":{"pages":"53","rows":"50","ms":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"index","text":"3 index pages"},{"node":"table","text":"+50 row fetches","tone":"green"},{"node":"app","text":"3 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"COMPOSITE","title":"Column order in a composite index","body":"The page really needs only the latest 20 orders: WHERE customer_id = 42 ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20. So you build one index on two columns, (customer_id, created_at). Entries are sorted by customer first, then by date inside each customer. The engine jumps to customer 42's block, already in date order, and reads the last 20 entries with no separate sorting. That is 23 pages (3 index + 20 rows) in about 1 ms. A query filtering only on created_at cannot seek here, because dates are scattered across all customers. This is the leftmost-prefix rule.","idea":"A composite index serves queries that use its leading columns; put equality columns first, then the range or sort column.","caption":"Entries sorted by customer, then date: the engine reads just the newest 20.","focus":["client","app","index","table"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"index","kind":"request","label":"42, newest 20"},{"from":"index","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"20 pointers"},{"from":"app","to":"table","kind":"request","label":"fetch 20 rows"},{"from":"table","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"20 rows"},{"from":"app","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"latest 20"}],"metrics":{"pages":"23","rows":"20","ms":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"index","text":"leftmost prefix"},{"node":"index","text":"no sort step","tone":"green"},{"node":"app","text":"1 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"COVERING","title":"Covering index: skip the table","body":"Suppose the page shows only the order date and total. If the index also stores total, as in (customer_id, created_at) INCLUDE (total), each index entry already holds every column the query needs. The engine answers from the index alone and skips the row fetches, which are often the costliest part of a seek. Pages drop from 23 to about 3. (In PostgreSQL this relies on VACUUM keeping the visibility map current; otherwise some table visits remain.)","idea":"A covering index answers a query entirely from the index, trading a larger index for fewer reads.","caption":"The index alone answers the query, so the table is never touched.","focus":["app","index","pool"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"index","kind":"request","label":"42, date, total"},{"from":"pool","to":"index","kind":"response","label":"leaf pages in RAM"},{"from":"index","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"20 rows"}],"metrics":{"pages":"3","rows":"20","ms":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"index","text":"no table fetch","tone":"green"},{"node":"index","text":"3 pages","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"index","text":"INCLUDE (total)"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Every index taxes every write","body":"Indexes are not free. Watch an order being inserted: the row goes into a table page, and a matching entry must go into every index, at its sorted position. With five indexes that is six pages written. A full leaf page may also split in two, which touches its parent too. The 6 counts pages written, not the tree walks (reads) needed to find each position. Updates to indexed columns move entries, and deletes remove them. Indexes buy read speed with write cost.","idea":"Write cost grows with the number of indexes, so add them for real query patterns, not by habit.","caption":"One insert writes the table row plus an entry in each of five indexes.","focus":["client","app","index","table"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"INSERT order"},{"from":"app","to":"table","kind":"request","label":"write row"},{"from":"app","to":"index","kind":"request","label":"5 index entries"}],"metrics":{"pages":"6","rows":"0","ms":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"table","text":"6 pages written"},{"node":"index","text":"5 indexes"},{"node":"index","text":"leaf split","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"When a scan is the right answer","body":"Suppose a query asks for orders with status = 'shipped', and 90 percent of rows match. Following the index would mean about 9 million random row fetches, far worse than reading the table once in order. The query planner (the part that picks a strategy) estimates this from statistics and chooses the full scan. A scan can also happen when the index cannot be used. Wrapping a column in a function, like WHERE lower(email) = ..., usually bypasses a plain index on email. An expression index on lower(email) fixes that.","idea":"Indexes help selective queries; when most rows match, a sequential scan is cheaper and the planner knows it.","caption":"The planner skips the index and scans the table because 90 percent of rows match.","focus":["app","table"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"table","kind":"request","label":"scan instead"}],"metrics":{"pages":"125000","rows":"10000000","ms":"800"},"chips":[{"node":"table","text":"90% match"},{"node":"table","text":"full scan wins","at":"bottom"},{"node":"table","text":"9M fetches","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Index for the queries you run","body":"A B-tree turns a search over millions of rows into a few page reads. Composite indexes follow the leftmost-prefix rule, so column order matters. Covering indexes skip the table. Every index slows writes and uses space, and queries that match most of the table are better served by scans. So look at your real slow queries and their query plans, then add the smallest index that fixes them.","idea":"Design indexes from your actual queries, check the query plan, and keep only the ones that earn their write cost.","caption":"Fast reads, paid for on every write.","focus":["client","app","index","table","pool"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"pages":"3","rows":"20","ms":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"index","text":"3 page reads","tone":"green"},{"node":"app","text":"1 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"table","text":"scan if 90%"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A table has an index on (customer_id, created_at). Which query can use it most effectively?","options":["WHERE created_at > '2025-01-01'","WHERE customer_id = 42 AND created_at > '2025-01-01'","WHERE created_at = '2025-01-01' ORDER BY customer_id","WHERE total > 100"],"answer":1,"why":"The index is sorted by customer_id first, so a query with an equality on customer_id lets the engine jump to one block, then range over created_at inside it. A filter on created_at alone skips the leading column."},{"q":"Your table grows from 10 million to 100 million rows. How does a B-tree seek on a single key change?","options":["It becomes about 10 times slower","It becomes 10 times faster","It stays almost the same, maybe one extra level","It doubles in cost"],"answer":2,"why":"B-trees are wide and shallow, so a tenfold growth adds at most about one level. A full scan, by contrast, would take roughly ten times longer."},{"q":"A write-heavy table has 12 indexes, and inserts have become slow. What is the most sensible first step?","options":["Find indexes no query plan uses and drop them","Add more RAM to the server","Convert every index into a covering index","Make the existing indexes covering by adding every column"],"answer":0,"why":"Each insert updates all 12 indexes. Removing unused or redundant ones cuts write cost without hurting reads. Covering indexes would make each index larger and slower to maintain."},{"q":"A query matches 80 percent of a table and the planner ignores the index. What is the likely explanation?","options":["The index is corrupt","Indexes only work on unique columns","The buffer pool is empty","A sequential scan is cheaper than millions of random row fetches"],"answer":3,"why":"Following an index means one random table lookup per match. When most rows qualify, reading the table once in order is cheaper, and the planner picks that using its statistics."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"B-tree index","def":"Sorted, shallow tree of pages mapping key values to row locations. Good for equality, ranges, and ordering. Seek cost grows with depth, not table size."},{"term":"Full scan vs seek","def":"A scan reads every page and costs O(table size). A seek walks the tree to matching entries and costs a few page reads plus any row fetches."},{"term":"Leftmost-prefix rule","def":"A composite index on (a, b, c) serves filters on a, on a and b, or on a, b and c, but a filter on b or c alone cannot seek into it (some engines can skip-scan, which only pays off when the leading column has few distinct values)."},{"term":"Column order rule of thumb","def":"Put equality-filtered columns first, then the range or sort column. Selectivity and your real queries break ties."},{"term":"Covering index","def":"An index that contains every column a query needs, so the table is never read. Costs a bigger index and more write work."},{"term":"Write cost","def":"Every insert adds an entry to every index. Updates to indexed columns and deletes also maintain the affected indexes. Page splits add more work, and more indexes means slower writes and more storage."},{"term":"Selectivity","def":"How few rows a filter keeps. A filter that keeps 50 of 10 million rows is highly selective and benefits from an index; one that keeps 90 percent is not selective, and a scan wins."},{"term":"Query plan","def":"The engine's chosen strategy for a query. Check it (EXPLAIN) to confirm an index is used instead of guessing."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"storage-engines","title":"Storage Engines: B-Trees vs LSM","subtitle":"Watch the same write travel through a B-tree and an LSM tree, and see where each one pays its cost.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How a B-tree updates a page in place, and why a tiny change rewrites a whole page","How an LSM tree turns random writes into sequential ones using a memtable, SSTables, and compaction","What write amplification and read amplification mean, with realistic numbers","Why both designs need a write-ahead log (WAL) to survive crashes","How to pick an engine from the workload: read-heavy vs write-heavy"],"nodes":[{"id":"app","label":"Application","kind":"client","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"wal","label":"Write-ahead log","kind":"storage","x":34,"y":18},{"id":"mem","label":"Memtable (RAM)","kind":"cache","x":34,"y":50},{"id":"btree","label":"B-tree pages","kind":"db","x":34,"y":84},{"id":"sst","label":"SSTables (L0)","kind":"storage","x":62,"y":26},{"id":"comp","label":"Compaction","kind":"worker","x":62,"y":72},{"id":"deep","label":"Deeper levels L1+","kind":"storage","x":88,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"app","to":"wal"},{"from":"app","to":"mem"},{"from":"app","to":"btree"},{"from":"mem","to":"sst"},{"from":"app","to":"sst"},{"from":"sst","to":"deep"},{"from":"sst","to":"comp"},{"from":"comp","to":"deep"}],"metrics":[{"id":"wamp","label":"Write amp","unit":"x"},{"id":"reads","label":"Reads/lookup","unit":"disk"},{"id":"files","label":"L0 files","unit":"files"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One write, two philosophies","body":"Your app saves one 100-byte row. Disks, both spinning and SSD, like big sequential writes (data laid down in order) and dislike many small scattered ones. A storage engine is the part of a database that puts data on disk. It must keep data sorted so reads are fast, yet still absorb writes cheaply. There are two main answers: the B-tree, which updates data in place, and the LSM tree, which never updates in place and merges later. Watch where each one pays.","idea":"A storage engine balances three costs: write cost, read cost, and space.","caption":"The app has one row to save and two possible engines.","focus":["app"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[]},{"tag":"B-TREE","title":"B-tree: edit the page in place","body":"A B-tree keeps sorted rows in fixed-size pages (8 KiB in Postgres, 16 KiB in InnoDB). To change one row, the engine finds its page, edits it, and later writes the whole page back, even for a 100-byte change. With 16 KiB pages that is up to about 160x write amplification (about 80x for 8 KiB). Write amplification means bytes physically written divided by bytes the app asked to write. Several edits to one page often share one flush, so real numbers are lower. Pages sit at scattered disk locations, so these are random writes. With the upper tree levels cached in RAM, a lookup costs about 1 disk read.","idea":"B-trees make reads cheap and predictable but pay for random, page-sized writes.","caption":"One small row change forces a whole 16 KiB page to be rewritten.","focus":["app","btree"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"btree","kind":"request","label":"update row"}],"metrics":{"wamp":"160","reads":"1","files":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"btree","text":"16 KiB page"},{"node":"btree","text":"~160x write amp","tone":"red"},{"node":"btree","text":"1 disk read","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WAL","title":"Write-ahead log first","body":"Edited pages sit in RAM for a while, so a crash could lose them. To prevent that, in a durable setup both engine types first append a small record to a write-ahead log (WAL) and fsync it, which forces it onto disk. (Some engines let you relax this for speed.) The append is sequential, so it is fast. Only then does the engine acknowledge the write to the app. After a crash, the log is replayed to rebuild the lost changes. Extra mechanisms, such as full-page writes, also guard against half-written pages.","idea":"The WAL buys durability cheaply: one sequential append before anything else.","caption":"The log record is made durable before the app gets its ack.","focus":["app","wal"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"wal","kind":"request","label":"append + fsync"},{"from":"wal","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"durable"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"wal","text":"fsync"},{"node":"wal","text":"sequential append","tone":"green"},{"node":"wal","text":"replay on crash","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"LSM WRITE","title":"LSM: write to memory","body":"An LSM tree (log-structured merge tree) takes a different route. After the WAL append, the row goes into the memtable, a sorted structure in RAM (often a skip list). No data page is read or modified on disk. The write costs one sequential log append plus one in-memory insert, typically a millisecond or less on a local SSD depending on the fsync policy. So far each byte is written about once (write amplification 1x). Unlike a B-tree update, there is no page to find first and none to flush later. The sorting and merging work is postponed.","idea":"LSM writes touch only the log and RAM, so they are fast and sequential.","caption":"The row goes to the log, then into the sorted in-memory memtable.","focus":["app","wal","mem"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"wal","kind":"request","label":"append + fsync"},{"from":"app","to":"mem","kind":"request","label":"put(key, val)"}],"metrics":{"wamp":"1","reads":"1","files":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"mem","text":"memtable"},{"node":"mem","text":"in RAM","at":"bottom"},{"node":"mem","text":"~1 ms or less","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"FLUSH","title":"Flush to an SSTable","body":"When the memtable fills up (RocksDB defaults to 64 MiB), it is frozen and written to disk in one sequential pass as an SSTable. That is a sorted, immutable file (never edited again, so it is easy to cache and copy), usually with an index and a Bloom filter, a small structure that quickly says 'not in this file'. Each byte has now been written twice, to the log and to the SSTable (2x). The matching WAL segment can be deleted, because its data is safe on disk. New writes go to a fresh memtable.","idea":"Flushing turns many random updates into one big sorted sequential file.","caption":"The full memtable is written out as an immutable level-0 SSTable.","focus":["mem","sst","wal"],"flows":[{"from":"mem","to":"sst","kind":"async","label":"flush 64 MiB"}],"metrics":{"wamp":"2","files":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"mem","text":"64 MiB limit"},{"node":"sst","text":"immutable"},{"node":"wal","text":"segment deleted","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"READ","title":"Reads must look in many places","body":"A key may live in the memtable or in any SSTable, and newer data hides older data. So a lookup checks the memtable first, then the level-0 (L0) files, which are the freshly flushed ones and can overlap (4 here), then one file per deeper level. Read amplification means how many files or pages one lookup must check. Each SSTable's Bloom filter says 'definitely not here' quickly, so most files are skipped. Without filters a lookup could touch 8 or more files; with them it is about 1 to 2 disk reads.","idea":"LSM reads pay read amplification; Bloom filters keep it to about 1 to 2 disk reads.","caption":"The lookup checks newest data first and skips most files using Bloom filters.","focus":["app","mem","sst","deep"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"mem","kind":"request","label":"check memtable"},{"from":"mem","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"miss"},{"from":"app","to":"sst","kind":"request","label":"bloom? index"},{"from":"sst","to":"deep","kind":"request","label":"maybe L1..L4"},{"from":"deep","to":"sst","kind":"response","label":"found / skip"},{"from":"sst","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"value or miss"}],"metrics":{"reads":"2","files":"4"},"chips":[{"node":"sst","text":"Bloom filter","tone":"green"},{"node":"sst","text":"L0 files overlap","at":"bottom"},{"node":"sst","text":"8+ without filter","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"COMPACT","title":"Compaction cleans up","body":"In the background, compaction merges overlapping SSTables into larger sorted ones at the next level. The 4 L0 files become 1. It drops overwritten values and deleted keys (tombstones, which are delete markers). This keeps reads fast and frees space, but every byte is rewritten several times as it moves down. With a size ratio of 10 (each level 10x bigger than the one above), leveled compaction typically lands around 10 to 30x write amplification, a rule of thumb that varies. Size-tiered compaction writes less but leaves more files and more duplicate data (space amplification).","idea":"Compaction is the bill for fast writes: the same data is rewritten again and again.","caption":"Compaction merges files and rewrites them one level lower.","focus":["sst","comp","deep"],"flows":[{"from":"sst","to":"comp","kind":"request","label":"merge sorted runs"},{"from":"comp","to":"deep","kind":"async","label":"write L1 file"}],"metrics":{"wamp":"20","reads":"2","files":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"comp","text":"10-30x write amp"},{"node":"comp","text":"size ratio 10","at":"bottom"},{"node":"sst","text":"tombstones dropped"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A write burst outruns compaction","body":"Imagine a bulk import writes faster than compaction can merge. Level-0 files pile up (24 here), and each lookup must check more of them, so reads slow down. To protect itself, the engine eventually slows or stops accepting writes (a write stall) until compaction catches up. Tuning compaction speed and file limits is a core LSM operations job. Bloom filters hide most of the cost for point lookups, but range scans and cache misses get noticeably slower.","idea":"LSM trades smooth latency for throughput; a lagging compaction shows up as slow reads and write stalls.","caption":"Compaction falls behind: L0 files pile up and writers get stalled.","focus":["app","mem","sst","comp"],"flows":[{"from":"sst","to":"comp","kind":"fail","label":"backlog"},{"from":"app","to":"mem","kind":"fail","label":"write stall"}],"metrics":{"reads":"3","files":"24"},"chips":[{"node":"sst","text":"24 L0 files","tone":"red"},{"node":"app","text":"write stall","tone":"red"},{"node":"comp","text":"falling behind","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick by workload","body":"B-trees update pages in place: strong, predictable reads and range scans, but pricier random writes. LSM trees buffer writes in memory and merge sorted files later: very high write throughput and compact storage, but read amplification and compaction work. Both use a WAL for crash safety. Write-heavy ingest favors LSM; read-heavy transactional work usually favors B-trees. The write-amplification figures (80-160x for tiny B-tree updates, 10-30x for leveled LSM) are rules of thumb. Postgres and InnoDB use B-trees; RocksDB, Cassandra and HBase use LSM trees.","idea":"No free lunch: you choose where to pay, at write time (B-tree) or in the background (LSM).","caption":"Two engines, same goal, different places to pay.","focus":["app","wal","mem","btree","sst","comp","deep"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"wamp":"20","reads":"2","files":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"btree","text":"80-160x write amp"},{"node":"sst","text":"10-30x write amp"},{"node":"wal","text":"WAL on both","at":"bottom"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A B-tree engine uses 16 KiB pages. An update changes a 100-byte row and the page is written back alone. Roughly how large is the page-level write amplification?","options":["About 1x, only the changed bytes are written","About 16x","About 160x","About 1,600x"],"answer":2,"why":"16,384 bytes written for 100 bytes changed is about 160x. In practice several changes to the same page may share one flush, so real numbers are lower."},{"q":"What does an LSM write avoid that a B-tree update eventually has to do?","options":["It writes only to the log and RAM, with no data-page read or rewrite on the write path","It skips the write-ahead log and relies on RAM alone","It writes each row straight to its final sorted position on disk","It waits for compaction to finish before it accepts the write"],"answer":0,"why":"Both engines log to the WAL first. The B-tree must also find, modify and eventually flush a whole page at a random location, which may need a random read first. The LSM defers ordering work to flush and compaction."},{"q":"An LSM database was fast, then after a huge bulk load its point lookups slow down and L0 file count keeps rising. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The Bloom filters were deleted","The WAL is too large","The memtable is too small to hold any rows","Compaction is falling behind the write rate"],"answer":3,"why":"Many overlapping L0 files mean each lookup checks more files. That points to compaction not keeping up, and writers may soon be stalled."},{"q":"The LSM engine holds recent writes in RAM. Why does it still write every change to a WAL?","options":["To make reads faster","RAM contents vanish on a crash, so the log lets the engine replay unflushed writes","To avoid ever needing compaction","To keep SSTables sorted"],"answer":1,"why":"The memtable is volatile. The WAL is a durable, sequential record of writes not yet flushed to an SSTable, replayed after a crash."},{"q":"A service writes 50k small events per second and rarely reads them back by key. Which engine fits best?","options":["Either, they behave the same","B-tree","LSM tree","Neither"],"answer":2,"why":"Write-heavy ingest with few reads suits an LSM tree: writes are cheap and sequential, and the read amplification rarely matters."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"B-tree","def":"Sorted tree of fixed-size pages updated in place. Predictable reads and range scans; random, page-sized writes."},{"term":"LSM tree","def":"Writes go to a memtable, then flush to immutable sorted files merged in the background. Great write throughput."},{"term":"WAL","def":"Write-ahead log: sequential, fsynced record of each change before it is applied, replayed after a crash."},{"term":"Memtable / SSTable","def":"Memtable is the sorted in-RAM buffer; an SSTable is an immutable sorted file on disk, typically with an index and Bloom filter."},{"term":"Write amplification","def":"Bytes physically written divided by bytes the user wrote. Leveled LSM often runs 10 to 30x; B-trees pay it per page."},{"term":"Read amplification","def":"Number of places (files or pages) checked per lookup. Low for B-trees; LSM reduces it with Bloom filters and compaction."},{"term":"Compaction","def":"Background merge of SSTables that drops overwritten and deleted data (tombstones). Keeps reads fast and limits space amplification (stale data lingering on disk), costs CPU, I/O, and rewrites."},{"term":"Bloom filter","def":"Small probabilistic structure that says a key is definitely not in a file, so lookups skip it. Can give false positives, never false negatives."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"transactions","title":"Transactions & Isolation","subtitle":"Watch two concurrent transactions corrupt a bank balance, then see how logs, row versions, and locks stop it.","minutes":14,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["What each letter of ACID promises, and which part of the engine delivers it","How lost updates, dirty reads, and write skew actually happen","How MVCC lets readers and writers work without blocking each other","When to reach for row locks, SELECT FOR UPDATE, or serializable isolation","Why stronger isolation costs waiting or retries"],"nodes":[{"id":"txa","label":"Transaction A","kind":"client","x":9,"y":24},{"id":"txb","label":"Transaction B","kind":"client","x":9,"y":78},{"id":"engine","label":"Transaction manager","kind":"service","x":37,"y":50},{"id":"lock","label":"Lock manager","kind":"service","x":62,"y":18},{"id":"versions","label":"Row versions","kind":"storage","x":62,"y":82},{"id":"wal","label":"Write-ahead log","kind":"storage","x":89,"y":24},{"id":"db","label":"accounts table","kind":"db","x":89,"y":70}],"edges":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine"},{"from":"engine","to":"lock"},{"from":"engine","to":"versions"},{"from":"engine","to":"wal"},{"from":"versions","to":"db"},{"from":"wal","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"total","label":"Balance","unit":"USD"},{"id":"versions","label":"Row versions","unit":""},{"id":"wait","label":"B waits","unit":"ms"},{"id":"bad","label":"Wrong results","unit":""}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Two people, one balance","body":"An account holds $100. Transaction A and Transaction B each try to withdraw $30 at the same moment, perhaps from two phones. The right final balance is $40. A database serving thousands of requests per second must get this right every time, even when requests overlap or the machine dies halfway. The tool for this is the transaction: a group of reads and writes treated as one unit.","idea":"A transaction groups operations into one all-or-nothing unit, and the database must stop overlapping units from corrupting each other.","caption":"Two transactions are about to touch the same $100 account row.","focus":["txa","txb","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"total":"100","versions":"1","wait":"0","bad":"0"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"ATOMICITY","title":"All or nothing","body":"A withdrawal debits the account and also writes an audit row. If the server crashes between the two writes, money is gone with no record. Atomicity means both writes happen or neither does. In the animation, A's two writes go to the transaction manager, which first appends them to a write-ahead log (WAL), a sequential file tagged with the transaction id. After a crash the engine replays or undoes work from that log, so a half-finished transaction never survives a restart.","idea":"Atomicity comes from logging changes first, so an unfinished transaction can always be rolled back.","caption":"A's two writes are logged before they count.","focus":["txa","engine","wal"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"BEGIN; 2 writes"},{"from":"engine","to":"wal","kind":"request","label":"log both writes"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"wal","text":"log first"},{"node":"wal","text":"replay on crash","at":"bottom"},{"node":"engine","text":"all or nothing"}]},{"tag":"DURABILITY","title":"Commit means it stays","body":"A withdraws $30, so the balance becomes 70. When A sends COMMIT, the engine forces the log onto disk (an fsync) and only then answers 'committed'. On datacenter NVMe with power-loss protection an fsync is often 0.1-1 ms; on a network cloud disk, roughly 1-5 ms or more (rule of thumb). That is durability: a committed transaction survives a power cut. Consistency, the C in ACID, means your rules (no negative balance, foreign keys) hold before and after. The database enforces declared constraints; your code defines the rest.","idea":"Durability means the commit record is on disk before the client hears 'committed'.","caption":"The commit waits for the log to reach disk, then A is told it succeeded.","focus":["txa","engine","wal"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"COMMIT"},{"from":"engine","to":"wal","kind":"handshake","label":"fsync"},{"from":"engine","to":"txa","kind":"response","label":"committed"}],"metrics":{"total":"70","versions":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"wal","text":"fsync 0.1-1 ms"},{"node":"engine","text":"balance 70"},{"node":"txa","text":"COMMIT ok","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"ANOMALY","title":"Lost update","body":"The balance is reset to $100. Each anomaly in this lesson (a wrong result caused by overlapping transactions) is its own scenario. Here A and B overlap. Both read the balance (100) into the application, subtract 30 in code, and write back 70. A writes 70, then B writes 70. Each looked fine alone, but one withdrawal vanished: the balance should be 40. This is a lost update, the classic bug of doing read, change, write in application code without protection.","idea":"A lost update happens when two transactions read the same old value and the later write overwrites the earlier one.","caption":"Both read 100 and both write 70, so one withdrawal is lost.","focus":["txa","txb","engine","db"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"read balance"},{"from":"engine","to":"txa","kind":"response","label":"100"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"read balance"},{"from":"engine","to":"txb","kind":"response","label":"100"},{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"write 70"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine","kind":"fail","label":"write 70 (wrong)"}],"metrics":{"total":"70","bad":"1","versions":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"balance 70","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"should be 40","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"engine","text":"lost update","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"ANOMALY","title":"Dirty read","body":"New scenario, balance back at $100. A writes 70 but has not committed yet. Imagine a database that lets B read that uncommitted 70 and act on it. If A then rolls back, B used a number that never officially existed. That is a dirty read. The Read Committed isolation level (the default in PostgreSQL and many others) forbids it: you only see data from transactions that have committed. Isolation levels are settings that say which anomalies you may see.","idea":"Isolation levels promise which anomalies you will never see; Read Committed rules out dirty reads.","caption":"B reads A's uncommitted write, then A rolls back. Read Committed prevents this.","focus":["txa","txb","engine"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"write 70 (open)"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"read balance"},{"from":"engine","to":"txb","kind":"fail","label":"dirty: 70"},{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"fail","label":"ROLLBACK"}],"metrics":{"total":"100","bad":"1","versions":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"txa","text":"uncommitted 70"},{"node":"txb","text":"dirty read","tone":"red"},{"node":"engine","text":"Read Committed","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"MVCC","title":"Keep old versions around","body":"Instead of blocking readers while A writes, most modern engines (PostgreSQL, MySQL InnoDB, Oracle) use multi-version concurrency control, or MVCC. A write creates a new version of the row and keeps the old one, so the table now has 2 versions. Each transaction reads from a snapshot, a frozen view of the data, so B keeps seeing 100 until A commits. Readers never block writers and writers never block readers. The price is storage for old versions and cleanup later (VACUUM in PostgreSQL).","idea":"MVCC gives each transaction a consistent snapshot by keeping old row versions, so reads do not wait for writes.","caption":"A creates version v2 (70). B still reads the old version v1 (100).","focus":["txa","txb","engine","versions"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"write 70"},{"from":"engine","to":"versions","kind":"async","label":"new version v2"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"versions","to":"engine","kind":"response","label":"v1 = 100"},{"from":"engine","to":"txb","kind":"response","label":"100"}],"metrics":{"total":"100","versions":"2","bad":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"versions","text":"2 versions"},{"node":"txb","text":"sees 100","at":"bottom"},{"node":"engine","text":"no read blocking","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"LOCKING","title":"Make B wait its turn","body":"Snapshots stop dirty reads but not lost updates in app code. The fix is to lock the row you plan to change: SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1 FOR UPDATE. A gets the row lock first. B asks for the same lock and must wait. After A commits, B's read returns 70 and B writes 40. B waited about 12 ms, but the answer is correct. If A and B each hold one lock and wait for the other's, that is a deadlock; the database aborts one and the app must retry. Often one statement is enough: UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 30 WHERE id = 1 AND balance >= 30.","idea":"Row locks make writers on the same row take turns: you buy correctness with waiting.","caption":"B waits on A's row lock, then sees 70 and writes 40.","focus":["txa","txb","engine","lock"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"SELECT FOR UPDATE"},{"from":"engine","to":"lock","kind":"request","label":"lock row 1 (A)"},{"from":"lock","to":"engine","kind":"response","label":"granted"},{"from":"engine","to":"txa","kind":"response","label":"balance = 100"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"SELECT FOR UPDATE"},{"from":"engine","to":"lock","kind":"request","label":"lock row 1 (B)"},{"from":"lock","to":"engine","kind":"fail","label":"held: wait"},{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"UPDATE 70; COMMIT"},{"from":"lock","to":"engine","kind":"response","label":"A committed"},{"from":"engine","to":"txb","kind":"response","label":"balance = 70"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"UPDATE 40; COMMIT"}],"metrics":{"total":"40","versions":"3","wait":"12","bad":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"lock","text":"A holds row lock"},{"node":"txb","text":"waits 12 ms","at":"bottom"},{"node":"db","text":"balance 40","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"ANOMALY","title":"Write skew","body":"Locking only the row you write does not help when two transactions write different rows based on a shared rule. Reset: checking and savings hold $60 each, and the rule is that the total must stay at or above 0. A withdraws $100 from checking, B withdraws $100 from savings. Each checks the total (120), sees room, and writes a different row. Snapshot isolation (one snapshot for the whole transaction, called Repeatable Read in PostgreSQL) sees no write conflict, so both commit and the total becomes -80. Fix: use Serializable, or lock every row the check reads.","idea":"Write skew breaks a rule that spans several rows even though no single row was written twice.","caption":"Different rows, one shared rule: both commit and the total drops to -80.","focus":["txa","txb","engine","versions"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"sum=120, debit chk"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"sum=120, debit sav"},{"from":"engine","to":"versions","kind":"async","label":"chk v2, sav v2"},{"from":"versions","to":"engine","kind":"fail","label":"no conflict seen"}],"metrics":{"total":"-80","versions":"4","wait":"0","bad":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"txa","text":"sees total 120"},{"node":"versions","text":"write skew","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"total -80","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Serializable and its price","body":"Serializable isolation promises results as if transactions ran one at a time. PostgreSQL tracks what each transaction read and wrote. On a dangerous pattern it aborts one with a serialization error, and the app must retry. It may abort some transactions that would have been fine. The ladder: Read Committed blocks dirty reads. Repeatable Read (snapshot) adds a stable view but still allows write skew. Serializable also blocks write skew. Safety and cost rise together, so protect only risky paths.","idea":"Stronger isolation trades throughput for safety: more waiting, or more aborted transactions to retry.","caption":"A commits. B is aborted, retries, sees total 20, and refuses the $100 withdrawal.","focus":["txa","txb","engine","lock"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"COMMIT"},{"from":"engine","to":"lock","kind":"request","label":"check conflicts"},{"from":"engine","to":"txa","kind":"response","label":"committed"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"COMMIT"},{"from":"engine","to":"txb","kind":"fail","label":"serial. error"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"retry: sum=20"},{"from":"engine","to":"txb","kind":"response","label":"refused: total 20"}],"metrics":{"total":"20","wait":"0","bad":"0","versions":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"engine","text":"tracks reads/writes"},{"node":"engine","text":"aborts one","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"txb","text":"retry"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick the weakest safe tool","body":"ACID in one line each: atomic via the log, consistent via your rules, isolated via MVCC and locks, durable via fsync on commit. For lost updates use an atomic UPDATE or FOR UPDATE. Dirty reads are gone at Read Committed. Write skew needs Serializable, or locking every row the check reads. Keep transactions short, retry on serialization or deadlock errors, and never hold one open while waiting for a user click. Transactions across several databases need more machinery, covered later.","idea":"Use the cheapest mechanism that rules out the anomaly you actually risk, and always be ready to retry.","caption":"The log, row versions, and locks each cover a different failure.","focus":["txa","txb","engine","lock","versions","wal","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"engine","text":"MVCC + locks"},{"node":"wal","text":"fsync on commit","at":"bottom"},{"node":"lock","text":"FOR UPDATE"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two requests each run SELECT balance, compute balance - 30 in app code, then UPDATE with the computed value. Both read 100. What final balance results at Read Committed with no locks?","options":["100, because both transactions roll back","40, because the database serializes the updates","The second transaction always gets an error","70, because the second write overwrites the first"],"answer":3,"why":"Read Committed does not stop two transactions reading the same old value. The later write of 70 overwrites the earlier one, a lost update."},{"q":"Why does MVCC let a long report query run while writers keep updating the same table?","options":["Writers are paused until the report ends","The report reads a snapshot made of older row versions, so it does not need locks held by writers","The report reads uncommitted data","The report runs on the write-ahead log"],"answer":1,"why":"MVCC keeps old versions, so the reader sees a consistent snapshot while writers create new versions. Neither blocks the other."},{"q":"On-call rule: at least one doctor must stay on call. Two doctors, both on call, each check the count (2) and then set only their own row to off. Both commit under snapshot isolation. What happened?","options":["A dirty read","A lost update","Write skew","A deadlock"],"answer":2,"why":"They wrote different rows, so there was no write-write conflict, yet the shared rule broke. That is write skew, prevented by Serializable or a lock on a shared row."},{"q":"Your service runs at Serializable isolation and sometimes gets a serialization error on commit. What is the correct response?","options":["Rerun the whole transaction from the start, usually with a small retry limit","Treat it as a bug and lower the isolation level silently","Ignore it, the data is already saved","Retry only the last statement"],"answer":0,"why":"A serialization failure means the transaction was aborted to keep results correct. The whole transaction must be retried, since its earlier reads may now be stale."},{"q":"What does the write-ahead log mainly provide?","options":["Faster reads through caching","Atomic rollback and durable commits after a crash","Protection against write skew","Automatic sharding"],"answer":1,"why":"Logging changes before applying them lets the engine undo unfinished transactions and replay committed ones after a crash."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"ACID","def":"Atomicity (all or nothing), Consistency (rules hold), Isolation (concurrent transactions do not interfere), Durability (committed data survives crashes)."},{"term":"Dirty read","def":"Reading another transaction's uncommitted data, which may later be rolled back. Prevented from Read Committed upward."},{"term":"Lost update","def":"Two read-modify-write cycles overlap and one overwrites the other. Fix with atomic UPDATE, SELECT FOR UPDATE, or optimistic version checks (UPDATE ... WHERE version = ?)."},{"term":"Write skew","def":"Two transactions read overlapping data, write different rows, and together break a rule. Needs Serializable or an explicit shared lock."},{"term":"MVCC","def":"Multi-version concurrency control: writes add row versions and reads use snapshots, so readers and writers rarely block each other."},{"term":"Isolation levels","def":"Read Committed (PostgreSQL default): no dirty reads. Repeatable Read/snapshot (MySQL default): stable view, still allows write skew. Serializable: blocks all three. Engines vary."},{"term":"SELECT ... FOR UPDATE","def":"Reads rows and locks them until commit, so concurrent writers wait. Keep the transaction short to limit waiting and deadlocks."},{"term":"Retry loop","def":"Serialization failures and deadlocks are normal under contention. Rerun the whole transaction, with a cap and a little jitter."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"replication","title":"Replication","subtitle":"Watch one write fan out to copies of a database, see where stale reads come from, and survive the leader dying.","minutes":14,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How leader-follower replication ships a write log to copies of the data","What synchronous vs asynchronous replication trades: latency against durability","Why replication lag causes stale reads, and how read-your-writes fixes the worst case","How multi-leader and leaderless (quorum) designs differ, and what conflicts they create","What happens during failover, including lost writes and split brain"],"nodes":[{"id":"leader2","label":"Leader (region 2)","kind":"db","x":38,"y":14},{"id":"app","label":"App server","kind":"server","x":9,"y":50},{"id":"leader","label":"Leader (primary)","kind":"db","x":38,"y":44},{"id":"fa","label":"Follower A (sync)","kind":"db","x":74,"y":14},{"id":"fb","label":"Follower B (async)","kind":"db","x":74,"y":62},{"id":"fc","label":"Follower C (remote)","kind":"db","x":74,"y":86},{"id":"mon","label":"Failover monitor","kind":"service","x":38,"y":86}],"edges":[{"from":"app","to":"leader"},{"from":"leader","to":"fa"},{"from":"leader","to":"fb"},{"from":"leader","to":"fc"},{"from":"app","to":"fb"},{"from":"app","to":"fa"},{"from":"app","to":"fc"},{"from":"leader","to":"leader2"},{"from":"mon","to":"leader"},{"from":"mon","to":"fa"}],"metrics":[{"id":"wlat","label":"Write ack","unit":"ms"},{"id":"lag","label":"Replica lag","unit":"ms"},{"id":"copies","label":"Durable copies","unit":"copies"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One copy is a single point of failure","body":"Your database lives on one machine. If its disk dies, the data is gone. If the machine is busy, every read waits in the same queue as every write. Replication means keeping copies of the same data on several machines, which fixes both problems. The hard part is keeping the copies in agreement while data keeps changing. Assumptions here: copies in the same region are about 1 ms apart, and one copy in another region is about 70 ms away.","idea":"Replication buys durability, read capacity and availability; the price is keeping copies consistent.","caption":"One leader holds all the data, and the app has no backup.","focus":["app","leader"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Leader-follower: one place takes writes","body":"In the most common design, every write goes to one node, the leader. It applies the change and appends it to an ordered replication log. It then ships that log to followers, which replay the entries in the same order. One writer and one order means the followers end up identical. PostgreSQL and MySQL work this way. In the animation the leader says 'OK' as soon as its own disk has the write, and ships the log afterwards (asynchronous). So only 1 durable copy exists at ack time. Nearby followers lag about 10 ms; the remote one lags 70 ms or more.","idea":"A single leader gives every follower the same ordered log, so copies converge without conflicts.","caption":"The write hits the leader, which replies OK, then streams the log to all followers.","focus":["app","leader","fa","fb","fc"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"leader","kind":"request","label":"INSERT order 41"},{"from":"leader","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"OK"},{"from":"leader","to":"fa","kind":"replicate","label":"log entry 41"},{"from":"leader","to":"fb","kind":"replicate","label":"log entry 41"},{"from":"leader","to":"fc","kind":"replicate","label":"log entry 41"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"2","lag":"10","copies":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"leader","text":"leader"},{"node":"leader","text":"1 copy at ack","at":"bottom"},{"node":"fa","text":"lag 10 ms"},{"node":"fc","text":"lag 70 ms+"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Synchronous vs asynchronous","body":"With asynchronous replication the leader answers as soon as its own disk has the write. The ack is fast, but only one copy is certain. If the leader dies before shipping the entry, that write is lost. With a synchronous follower, the leader waits for that follower's ack first. This costs one extra round trip (about 5 ms total here) but guarantees 2 copies. Waiting on every follower is fragile, because one slow node would stall all writes. So real systems keep one sync follower and the rest async. If the sync follower dies, another is promoted to sync, or the system falls back to async.","idea":"Synchronous replication trades write latency for durability; most systems make just one follower synchronous.","caption":"The leader waits for Follower A's ack before telling the app 'committed'.","focus":["app","leader","fa"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"leader","kind":"request","label":"INSERT order 42"},{"from":"leader","to":"fa","kind":"replicate","label":"log entry 42"},{"from":"fa","to":"leader","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"leader","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"committed"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"5","copies":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"leader","text":"ack ~5 ms"},{"node":"fa","text":"sync follower","tone":"green"},{"node":"leader","text":"2 copies","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Followers absorb the reads","body":"Most apps read far more than they write, often 10 to 1 or more. Followers can serve reads, so adding followers adds read capacity while the leader keeps taking the writes. In the animation the app sends a SELECT to Follower B instead of the leader. This scales reads well, but does nothing for write capacity, because every write still goes through one leader. Sharding, covered in its own lesson, addresses that limit.","idea":"Followers scale reads, not writes; every write still passes through the leader.","caption":"A read is served by Follower B instead of the leader.","focus":["app","fb"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"fb","kind":"request","label":"SELECT orders"},{"from":"fb","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"rows"}],"metrics":{"lag":"10"},"chips":[{"node":"fb","text":"serves reads","tone":"green"},{"node":"fb","text":"lag 10 ms","at":"bottom"},{"node":"leader","text":"writes only"}]},{"tag":"PROBLEM","title":"Replication lag shows you the past","body":"Async followers trail the leader. Usually that is milliseconds, but a busy follower, a big batch job or a network hiccup can push lag to seconds (800 ms here). Picture a user saving a new display name. The write goes to the leader, the page reloads, and the read lands on a follower that has not received the entry yet. The user sees the old name and thinks the save failed. Nothing is lost; the read just happened too early.","idea":"With async replication, a read from a follower can return data older than a write you just made.","caption":"The follower has not yet applied the new name, so the read returns the old one.","focus":["app","leader","fb"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"leader","kind":"request","label":"UPDATE name"},{"from":"leader","to":"fb","kind":"async","label":"still in flight"},{"from":"app","to":"fb","kind":"request","label":"SELECT name"},{"from":"fb","to":"app","kind":"fail","label":"old name"}],"metrics":{"lag":"800"},"chips":[{"node":"fb","text":"old name shown","tone":"red"},{"node":"fb","text":"lag 800 ms","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"leader","text":"new name saved","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"FIX","title":"Read-your-writes","body":"Users expect read-your-writes: after I save something, I always see it. A simple fix is to send a user's reads to the leader for a short window, say 10 seconds, after they write. That is what the animation shows. A more precise fix records the log position of their last write and reads only from a follower that has replayed past it. A related guarantee is monotonic reads, meaning a user never sees data go back in time. You get it by always sending one user to the same follower.","idea":"Right after a user writes, route their reads to the leader or to a caught-up follower.","caption":"Right after the write, the read goes to the leader and returns the new name.","focus":["app","leader"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"leader","kind":"request","label":"SELECT name"},{"from":"leader","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"new name"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"leader","text":"reads for 10 s"},{"node":"app","text":"read-your-writes","tone":"green"},{"node":"fb","text":"pin per user","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"VARIANT","title":"Multi-leader: a leader per region","body":"With one leader, users far away pay a cross-region round trip (about 70 ms) on every write. Multi-leader runs a leader in each region. Each accepts local writes at around 3 ms and ships them to the other leaders asynchronously. The cost is conflicts. If two regions edit the same row at the same time, both succeed locally and the clash shows up later. You must resolve it, for example with last-write-wins (which silently drops one edit), merge rules, or CRDTs (data types designed to merge). Use it only for multi-region writes or offline editing.","idea":"Multi-leader makes writes local and fast, but concurrent writes to the same data become conflicts you must resolve.","caption":"Two leaders accept different edits to the same title, and a conflict appears.","focus":["leader","leader2"],"flows":[{"from":"leader","to":"leader2","kind":"replicate","label":"set title = A"},{"from":"leader2","to":"leader","kind":"fail","label":"set title = B"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"3","lag":"70","copies":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"leader2","text":"local ack 3 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"leader","text":"remote 70 ms","at":"bottom"},{"node":"leader2","text":"conflicts","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"VARIANT","title":"Leaderless: quorum reads and writes","body":"Dynamo-style systems such as Cassandra have no leader. Here n = 3 replicas (the number of copies), and the 'follower' boxes in the diagram are all equal. A write goes to all three and counts as done when w = 2 reply. A read asks r = 2 replicas and takes the newest version. Because w + r > n, every read set overlaps the write set in at least one node, so it normally sees the latest write. A replica that missed the write is fixed later by read repair or background anti-entropy. Quorums are not magic: concurrent writes and sloppy quorums can still return stale data.","idea":"With w + r > n, reads and writes overlap on at least one replica, so you usually read the latest value.","caption":"The write needs 2 acks. A read of 2 replicas always includes one holding v2.","focus":["app","fa","fb","fc"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"fa","kind":"request","label":"write v2"},{"from":"app","to":"fb","kind":"request","label":"write v2"},{"from":"app","to":"fc","kind":"request","label":"write v2 (slow)"},{"from":"fa","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"fb","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"app","to":"fb","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"app","to":"fc","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"fb","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"v2"},{"from":"fc","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"v1 (stale)"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"4","lag":"70","copies":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"fa","text":"w = 2 of 3"},{"node":"fb","text":"r = 2"},{"node":"app","text":"w + r > n","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FAILURE","title":"Failover when the leader dies","body":"Now the leader crashes. A monitor notices missed heartbeats, typically after a 10 to 30 second timeout. It picks the most up-to-date follower (A, the synchronous one, which has every write that was acknowledged synchronously), promotes it, and points the app at it. Two traps follow. Writes the old leader acked only to async followers, or while the sync follower was degraded, can still be lost. And if the old leader returns believing it is still in charge, two nodes accept writes (split brain). The old leader must be fenced off or demoted using an increasing epoch number.","idea":"Failover restores service but can lose unreplicated writes and risks split brain without fencing.","caption":"The monitor sees no heartbeat, promotes Follower A, and redirects the app.","focus":["mon","leader","fa","app"],"flows":[{"from":"mon","to":"leader","kind":"fail","label":"no heartbeat"},{"from":"mon","to":"fa","kind":"request","label":"promote, epoch 8"},{"from":"fa","to":"app","kind":"handshake","label":"I am leader"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"2","copies":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"leader","text":"DOWN","tone":"red"},{"node":"mon","text":"timeout 10-30 s"},{"node":"fa","text":"promoted","tone":"green"},{"node":"leader","text":"split brain risk","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick the weakest guarantee you can live with","body":"Leader-follower is the default: one ordered log, easy to reason about, with followers for reads and standby. Choose sync or async by how much data loss you can tolerate versus how much latency you can afford. Expect lag and handle it with read-your-writes. Reach for multi-leader or leaderless only when you need multi-region writes or very high availability, and then plan for conflicts. Rehearse failover before you need it.","idea":"Every replication choice trades latency, consistency and availability; decide which one you can give up.","caption":"One leader, a sync follower, async followers, and a failover plan.","focus":["app","leader","fa","fb","fc","mon"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"wlat":"5","lag":"10","copies":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"leader","text":"1 ordered log"},{"node":"fa","text":"sync follower","tone":"green"},{"node":"fb","text":"async reads","at":"bottom"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A leader uses fully asynchronous replication and its disk is destroyed a few milliseconds after it acknowledged a write. What is the realistic outcome?","options":["Nothing is lost, because followers always have every acknowledged write","The write is retried by the app and always recovered","That write may be gone if it had not reached any follower yet","All data on all followers is corrupted"],"answer":2,"why":"Async mode acknowledges after the leader's own write. Entries not yet shipped exist only on the dead leader, so acknowledged writes can be lost."},{"q":"A user posts a comment and immediately reloads, but the comment is missing. A few seconds later it appears. Which cause and fix fit best?","options":["Replication lag on the follower that served the read; read from the leader briefly after a write","The leader dropped the write; add more followers","A cache bug; restart the followers","A split brain; promote the follower to leader"],"answer":0,"why":"This is the classic read-your-writes violation from async lag. Reading from the leader (or a caught-up follower) after a write fixes it without slowing every write."},{"q":"A leaderless store has n = 3. Which setting guarantees read and write sets overlap by at least one replica?","options":["w = 1, r = 1","w = 2, r = 1","w = 1, r = 2","w = 2, r = 2"],"answer":3,"why":"The overlap rule is w + r > n. With n = 3, w = 2 and r = 2 gives 4 > 3. Both w = 2, r = 1 and w = 1, r = 2 give 3, which is not greater than 3, so neither guarantees overlap, and w = 1, r = 1 clearly fails."},{"q":"After failover, the old leader restarts and still accepts client writes alongside the new leader. What is this called, and what prevents it?","options":["Replication lag; add a synchronous follower","Read repair; run anti-entropy","Quorum overlap; raise r","Split brain; fence the old leader with an epoch number"],"answer":3,"why":"Two nodes both believing they are leader is split brain. Epoch numbers or fencing tokens make storage and followers reject the stale leader's writes."},{"q":"Why is making every follower synchronous usually a bad idea?","options":["Synchronous followers cannot serve reads","It forces all reads to go through the leader","Any single slow or down follower would block or fail every write","It forces the leader to use a stronger isolation level"],"answer":2,"why":"Writes wait for all acks, so availability and latency become that of the worst replica. Systems typically use one sync follower and keep the rest async."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Leader / follower","def":"The leader accepts all writes and ships an ordered log; followers replay it and can serve reads or take over if the leader fails."},{"term":"Sync vs async","def":"Sync waits for a follower's ack before confirming a write (more durable, slower). Async confirms immediately (fast, can lose recent writes on leader loss)."},{"term":"Semi-synchronous","def":"Wait for one follower's ack (ideally any one of several) and replicate to the rest async. You keep at least two copies, and a single slow async follower cannot stall writes, but a lost sync follower must be replaced or the system must fall back to async."},{"term":"Replication lag","def":"How far a follower trails the leader. Usually milliseconds, but it can grow to seconds under load and makes follower reads stale."},{"term":"Read-your-writes","def":"Guarantee that a user sees their own earlier writes. Implement by reading from the leader or a caught-up follower after a write."},{"term":"Multi-leader","def":"Several nodes accept writes, usually one per region. Local writes are fast, but concurrent edits create conflicts needing resolution."},{"term":"Quorum (w + r > n)","def":"In leaderless stores, require w write acks and r read replies so the sets overlap. Helps freshness but is not strict consistency."},{"term":"Failover and split brain","def":"Promote a follower when the leader dies. Risks: losing unreplicated writes, and two leaders if the old one returns, so fence with epochs."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"sharding","title":"Sharding","subtitle":"Split one huge table across machines, then watch hot keys, scatter-gather queries and resharding test the design.","minutes":13,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How range and hash partitioning place rows, and what each makes easy or hard","What makes a good shard key, and why it must appear in your hot queries","Why hot partitions happen even with hashing, and how to relieve them","What a cross-shard query costs and why tail latency grows with fan-out","How resharding moves data, and why fixed buckets beat hash mod N"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"App clients","kind":"client","x":8,"y":40},{"id":"router","label":"Shard router","kind":"gateway","x":32,"y":40},{"id":"map","label":"Shard map","kind":"service","x":32,"y":84},{"id":"a","label":"Shard A","kind":"db","x":64,"y":14},{"id":"b","label":"Shard B","kind":"db","x":64,"y":40},{"id":"c","label":"Shard C","kind":"db","x":64,"y":66},{"id":"d","label":"Shard D (new)","kind":"db","x":89,"y":40}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"router"},{"from":"router","to":"map"},{"from":"router","to":"a"},{"from":"router","to":"b"},{"from":"router","to":"c"},{"from":"a","to":"d"},{"from":"b","to":"d"},{"from":"c","to":"d"}],"metrics":[{"id":"shards","label":"Shards","unit":""},{"id":"hot","label":"Hottest shard","unit":"% load"},{"id":"fan","label":"Shards per query","unit":""},{"id":"moved","label":"Data moved","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One database is not enough","body":"Picture an orders table with 3 billion rows, about 6 TB, taking 20,000 writes per second at peak. One machine runs out of disk, memory and write speed. Read replicas help with reads, but each copy still holds all 6 TB and every write still hits one leader. Sharding fixes this by splitting the rows themselves across machines. Here three shards each hold about 2 TB and take about 6,700 writes per second (a third of the load each).","idea":"Replication copies the whole dataset; sharding divides it so each machine owns only a slice.","caption":"Three shards, each owning about a third of the rows.","focus":["a","b","c"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"shards":"3","hot":"33","fan":"1","moved":"0"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Route by shard key","body":"Every row carries a shard key, here user_id. A router (a small service in front) looks the key up in the shard map, a table of who owns what, and sends the query to the one shard that owns it. In the animation, user 8841 goes to Shard B and nowhere else. The app never needs to know how many shards exist. The map is tiny, so routers cache it and refresh it only when it changes.","idea":"The shard key decides where a row lives, so every query is routed by it.","caption":"User 8841 is routed to Shard B and nowhere else.","focus":["client","router","map","b"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"router","kind":"request","label":"user 8841"},{"from":"router","to":"map","kind":"request","label":"who owns it?"},{"from":"map","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"Shard B"},{"from":"router","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"SELECT orders"},{"from":"b","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"rows"},{"from":"router","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"fan":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"router","text":"key -> shard b"},{"node":"map","text":"tiny, cached","at":"bottom"},{"node":"client","text":"1 shard hit","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Hash partitioning spreads load","body":"With hash partitioning, the shard is chosen from hash(user_id), a scrambled number made from the key. Neighbouring ids land on unrelated shards, so inserts and reads spread almost evenly across all three (about 34 percent on the busiest). The price is order. A range scan, such as user ids 1000 to 2000, now touches every shard, because adjacent keys are deliberately scattered.","idea":"Hashing buys an even spread by giving up range scans on the key.","caption":"Writes fan out evenly across Shards A, B and C.","focus":["router","a","b","c"],"flows":[{"from":"router","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"INSERT"},{"from":"router","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"INSERT"},{"from":"router","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"INSERT"}],"metrics":{"hot":"34"},"chips":[{"node":"router","text":"hash(user_id)"},{"node":"b","text":"even spread","tone":"green"},{"node":"router","text":"range scan: all","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Range partitioning keeps order","body":"Range partitioning gives each shard a contiguous slice of keys, for example order dates by month. Scanning one month, or the newest 100 orders, hits one shard and is cheap. The catch: if the key is a timestamp, every new insert lands in the newest range. That one shard takes nearly all the writes (about 90 percent here) while older shards sit almost idle.","idea":"Ranges make scans cheap but concentrate writes whenever the key grows over time.","caption":"Today's writes all pile onto the newest range, Shard C.","focus":["router","c","a"],"flows":[{"from":"router","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"new orders"},{"from":"router","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"new orders"},{"from":"router","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"old reads"}],"metrics":{"hot":"90"},"chips":[{"node":"c","text":"~90% of writes","tone":"red"},{"node":"a","text":"nearly idle","at":"bottom"},{"node":"router","text":"range by month"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Choosing the shard key","body":"A good shard key has many distinct values, spreads traffic evenly, and appears in your most common queries. Sharding orders by user_id means 'show me my orders' hits exactly one shard (the animation shows this). Sharding by country would give only about 200 values, wildly uneven sizes, and a few giant shards. Pick the key from your access pattern (how the app actually reads data), not from the schema.","idea":"Pick a key with many distinct values that your hottest queries already filter on.","caption":"A query that carries the shard key touches just one shard.","focus":["client","router","b"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"router","kind":"request","label":"my orders"},{"from":"router","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"user_id = 8841"},{"from":"b","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"20 rows"}],"metrics":{"hot":"34","fan":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"client","text":"my orders"},{"node":"b","text":"1 shard hit","tone":"green"},{"node":"router","text":"key: user_id","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A hot partition","body":"Now one tenant, say a viral store, generates 40 percent of all traffic. Hashing cannot help, because every request carries the same key and so lands on the same shard. That shard takes the tenant's 40 percent plus its normal third of the remaining 60 percent (20 percent), about 60 percent of all load, while the other two coast at about 20 percent each. Fixes include a cache in front, a dedicated shard for the big tenant, or adding a small suffix to its key so its rows spread over several shards.","idea":"Hashing spreads different keys evenly, but it cannot spread one key that is itself hot.","caption":"One hot key overloads Shard A while B and C coast.","focus":["router","a"],"flows":[{"from":"router","to":"a","kind":"fail","label":"hot tenant"},{"from":"router","to":"a","kind":"fail","label":"hot tenant"}],"metrics":{"hot":"60"},"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"hot partition","tone":"red"},{"node":"a","text":"one tenant 40%","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"a","kind":"progress","value":60,"text":"60%"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A query without the key","body":"Someone asks for all orders from merchant 77. The merchant is not the shard key, so the router cannot tell which shard holds them. It sends the query to every shard and merges the answers. This is called scatter-gather. The result arrives only when the slowest shard replies. Cross-shard joins and transactions are harder still, so design schemas to avoid them.","idea":"A query missing the shard key fans out to every shard, and the slowest shard sets the latency.","caption":"The router asks all three shards and waits for the last reply.","focus":["router","a","b","c"],"flows":[{"from":"router","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"merchant 77"},{"from":"router","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"merchant 77"},{"from":"router","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"merchant 77"},{"from":"a","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"partial"},{"from":"b","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"partial"},{"from":"c","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"partial"}],"metrics":{"hot":"34","fan":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"router","text":"scatter-gather"},{"node":"router","text":"fan-out 3","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"c","text":"slowest sets time"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Resharding: add a fourth shard","body":"Growth means adding Shard D. With plain hash mod N (the remainder after dividing by the shard count), going from 3 to 4 shards changes the owner of about 75 percent of keys. That is a huge copy. The standard fix is many fixed buckets, say 256, mapped to shards by the shard map. Adding D moves only whole buckets, roughly one quarter of the data, taken evenly from A, B and C, as the animation shows.","idea":"Hash into fixed buckets and move buckets, so a new shard takes only its fair share of the data.","caption":"A, B and C each hand some buckets to the new Shard D.","focus":["a","b","c","d"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"d","kind":"replicate","label":"buckets"},{"from":"b","to":"d","kind":"replicate","label":"buckets"},{"from":"c","to":"d","kind":"replicate","label":"buckets"}],"metrics":{"shards":"4","moved":"25"},"chips":[{"node":"router","text":"mod N: 75% move","tone":"red"},{"node":"d","text":"new shard"},{"node":"d","text":"~25% moved","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Switching over safely","body":"Live traffic keeps writing while buckets copy. The usual pattern: bulk copy the buckets, replay the changes made during the copy until D is almost caught up, briefly pause writes to those buckets, then update the shard map so the router points them at D. Old copies are deleted later. Clients see at most a short blip, never a stop. With four shards, the busiest now carries about 25 percent.","idea":"Copy, catch up, flip the map, then clean up; the map change is the moment ownership moves.","caption":"The router fetches the new shard map and starts routing those buckets to D.","focus":["router","map","b","d"],"flows":[{"from":"b","to":"d","kind":"replicate","label":"catch-up log"},{"from":"router","to":"map","kind":"request","label":"map version?"},{"from":"map","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"v8: buckets to D"}],"metrics":{"hot":"25","moved":"25"},"chips":[{"node":"d","text":"bulk copy"},{"node":"map","text":"map updated","at":"bottom"},{"node":"router","text":"brief write pause"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"What to remember","body":"Sharding splits rows across machines by a shard key. Hash partitioning spreads load but loses range scans. Range partitioning keeps order but can create a hot newest shard. Pick a key with many distinct values that your main queries use. Expect hot keys and scatter-gather as costs, and reshard by moving fixed buckets. Shard last: indexes, caching and replicas are cheaper to try first.","idea":"Shard only when one machine truly cannot cope, and choose the key for your queries.","caption":"Four shards, one router, one shard map.","focus":["client","router","map","a","b","c","d"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"shards":"4","hot":"25","fan":"1","moved":"25"},"chips":[{"node":"router","text":"shard key: user_id"},{"node":"d","text":"4 shards"},{"node":"map","text":"fixed buckets","at":"bottom"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A cluster uses hash(key) mod N to pick a shard and grows from 3 to 4 shards. About what share of keys change owner?","options":["About 25 percent","About 50 percent","About 75 percent","None, hashing is stable"],"answer":2,"why":"Only keys whose value mod 3 equals mod 4 stay put, about one in four. The rest move, which is why fixed buckets in a shard map are preferred."},{"q":"Orders are range-partitioned by created_at. Write latency spikes on exactly one shard at peak while the others are idle. What is the cause?","options":["Every new order falls in the newest range, so that shard takes all inserts","Range partitioning cannot handle writes","The shard map is out of date","The other shards are replicas"],"answer":0,"why":"A monotonically increasing key sends all fresh writes to the last range. A hashed key, or a compound key that leads with something well spread, avoids it."},{"q":"A multi-tenant app shards by tenant_id with hashing, but one tenant sends 40 percent of traffic and its shard is overloaded. What best addresses it?","options":["Add more shards to the cluster","Switch from hash to range partitioning","Hash the tenant_id with a stronger function","Give that tenant its own shard, or split its key with a suffix across several shards"],"answer":3,"why":"All of one tenant's requests share one key, so any hash maps them to the same shard. More shards or a better hash cannot split a single key; you must change how that key is placed."},{"q":"In an 8-shard cluster you query by a column that is not the shard key. What happens, and what limits the latency?","options":["The router guesses one shard and may miss rows","It scatters to all 8 shards, and the slowest shard sets the latency","It scans only the shard that was most recently used","It fails, because non-key queries are not allowed"],"answer":1,"why":"Without the key the router cannot narrow the target, so it queries every shard and merges. The query waits for the slowest reply, so the chance of hitting one slow shard grows with fan-out."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Shard key","def":"The column(s) whose value decides which shard owns a row. Choose high cardinality, even spread, and presence in your hot queries."},{"term":"Hash partitioning","def":"Shard = hash(key) mapped to a bucket. Spreads load evenly, but adjacent keys are scattered, so range scans hit every shard."},{"term":"Range partitioning","def":"Each shard owns a contiguous key range. Great for range scans; risks hot shards when keys grow over time, like timestamps."},{"term":"Hot partition","def":"One shard receiving far more traffic than its peers, often due to a single popular key. Fix with caching, key splitting or a dedicated shard."},{"term":"Scatter-gather","def":"Sending a query to all shards and merging results. Latency is the slowest shard's, and cost grows with shard count."},{"term":"Fixed buckets","def":"Hash into many logical buckets (for example 256) and map buckets to shards. Resharding moves whole buckets instead of rehashing every key."},{"term":"Shard map","def":"Small lookup table of which shard owns which bucket or range. Routers cache it; updating it is the cutover moment in resharding."},{"term":"Rule of thumb","def":"Shard last. Try indexes, caching, read replicas and a bigger machine first, since sharding permanently complicates joins and transactions."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"consistent-hashing","title":"Consistent Hashing","subtitle":"Watch a cache cluster gain and lose nodes, and see why a hash ring moves a quarter of the keys instead of three quarters.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why hash(key) mod N reshuffles almost every key when N changes","How a hash ring maps keys and nodes onto one circle and walks clockwise","Why adding or removing a node moves only about one node's share (1/N) of the keys","How virtual nodes even out load and handle uneven machines","Where rings show up: cache clients and Dynamo-style stores"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"App / cache client","kind":"client","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"ring","label":"Ring lookup (client)","kind":"service","x":32,"y":50},{"id":"a","label":"Cache A","kind":"cache","x":68,"y":14},{"id":"b","label":"Cache B","kind":"cache","x":68,"y":36},{"id":"c","label":"Cache C","kind":"cache","x":68,"y":58},{"id":"d","label":"Cache D (new)","kind":"cache","x":68,"y":80},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":92,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"ring"},{"from":"ring","to":"a"},{"from":"ring","to":"b"},{"from":"ring","to":"c"},{"from":"ring","to":"d"},{"from":"a","to":"b"},{"from":"b","to":"c"},{"from":"c","to":"d"},{"from":"a","to":"db"},{"from":"b","to":"db"},{"from":"c","to":"db"},{"from":"d","to":"db"},{"from":"a","to":"d"},{"from":"a","to":"c"}],"metrics":[{"id":"moved","label":"Keys moved","unit":"%"},{"id":"hit","label":"Hit rate","unit":"%"},{"id":"hot","label":"Busiest node","unit":"% of avg"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Which cache holds this key?","body":"Your site keeps 30 million cached items on three cache servers, A, B and C, because no single machine has enough memory. Every client must agree on which server owns which key, without talking to each other. If two clients disagree, one writes a value the other can never find. How do you split keys so everyone agrees, and so that adding a server later does not hurt? Server D is waiting to be added.","idea":"Partitioning needs a rule that every client computes identically, and that survives the cluster changing size.","caption":"Three caches are in use; Cache D is waiting to be added.","focus":["client","a","b","c"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"moved":"0","hit":"95","hot":"100"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"The simple rule: hash mod N","body":"The obvious rule is server = hash(key) mod 3. A hash function turns any key into a number, and mod 3 takes the remainder after dividing by 3. A good hash spreads keys evenly, so each server gets about a third, and every client computes the same answer. Example: user:42 hashes to 7, and 7 mod 3 is 1, so it lives on B (A is 0, B is 1, C is 2). The animation shows a hit on B. This works well as long as the number of servers never changes.","idea":"hash(key) mod N is stateless and even, but it bakes the server count into every key's address.","caption":"user:42 hashes to Cache B, and the hit rate is a healthy 95%.","focus":["client","ring","b"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"ring","kind":"request","label":"GET user:42"},{"from":"ring","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"7 mod 3 = B"},{"from":"b","to":"ring","kind":"response","label":"hit"},{"from":"ring","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"value"}],"metrics":{"moved":"0","hit":"95","hot":"100"},"chips":[{"node":"ring","text":"hash(key) mod 3"},{"node":"b","text":"7 mod 3 = 1 -> B","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"PROBLEM","title":"Add one server, lose most of the cache","body":"Traffic grows, so you add Cache D and change the rule to mod 4. Now 7 mod 4 is 3, which is D, not B, and D is empty, so the lookup misses and goes to the database. A key stays put only if its hash gives the same answer under both rules, about one key in four. So roughly 75% of keys now point at a server that does not hold them. They all miss at once and stampede the database. (Assume a 95% baseline hit rate and that moved keys miss until refilled, so the hit rate falls to about 24%.)","idea":"With mod N, going from N to N+1 servers remaps about N/(N+1) of all keys, so scaling out wipes most of the cache.","caption":"75% of lookups now miss the cache and fall through to the database.","focus":["ring","d","db"],"flows":[{"from":"ring","to":"d","kind":"request","label":"7 mod 4 = D"},{"from":"d","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"miss, query"}],"metrics":{"moved":"75","hit":"24","hot":"100"},"chips":[{"node":"ring","text":"mod 4: 75% remapped","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"hit 95% -> 24%","tone":"red"},{"node":"d","text":"new node","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Put servers and keys on a ring","body":"Start over with servers A, B and C. Consistent hashing hashes both servers and keys onto the same number range, say 0 to 2^32, then bends that range into a circle called the ring. Each server sits where its name hashes. To find a key's owner, hash the key, then walk clockwise until you reach the first server. Each client does this lookup locally, and clients with the same server list build the same ring, so no per-key coordination is needed.","idea":"A key belongs to the first node clockwise from its hash, so ownership depends on neighbours, not on the total count.","caption":"user:42 lands between A and B and walks clockwise to B.","focus":["client","ring","b"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"ring","kind":"request","label":"hash(user:42)"},{"from":"ring","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"next clockwise"},{"from":"b","to":"ring","kind":"response","label":"hit"},{"from":"ring","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"value"}],"metrics":{"moved":"0","hit":"95","hot":"100"},"chips":[{"node":"ring","text":"range 0 to 2^32"},{"node":"b","text":"first clockwise"},{"node":"client","text":"local lookup","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"ADD NODE","title":"A new node steals one arc","body":"Cache D joins and hashes to a point on the ring, between C and A. Only the keys on the arc just before D now stop at D. Those keys used to walk past D to A. Every other key still reaches the same server as before. With four nodes that arc covers on average about a quarter of the keys (arcs are random; step 7 returns to this), so 25% move and the hit rate dips to about 71% until they refill. D can fetch those keys from A or just let them refill from the database.","idea":"Adding a node moves only the keys it takes over, about 1/N of the total, and they all come from one neighbour.","caption":"D takes over the arc just before it; those keys came from A.","focus":["ring","c","d","a"],"flows":[{"from":"ring","to":"d","kind":"request","label":"D joins ring"},{"from":"a","to":"d","kind":"async","label":"arc keys (~25%)"}],"metrics":{"moved":"25","hit":"71","hot":"100"},"chips":[{"node":"d","text":"takes ~25% of keys"},{"node":"a","text":"loses an arc","at":"bottom"},{"node":"ring","text":"only one arc","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"REMOVE NODE","title":"A failed node hands off to its successor","body":"Now Cache B crashes. Its keys do not scatter everywhere. Walking clockwise from B's old arc, the next node, C, becomes the owner. Keys owned by A, C and D are untouched, so their cached data stays warm. Only B's share, about a quarter of all keys, misses once and then refills from the database. The hit rate dips to about 71% meanwhile.","idea":"Removing a node affects only the keys it owned; every other node keeps its data.","caption":"B's keys fall to C; A and D are unaffected.","focus":["ring","b","c","db"],"flows":[{"from":"ring","to":"b","kind":"fail","label":"B is down"},{"from":"ring","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"B's keys"},{"from":"c","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"refill"}],"metrics":{"moved":"25","hit":"71","hot":"100"},"chips":[{"node":"b","text":"DOWN","tone":"red"},{"node":"c","text":"new owner"},{"node":"ring","text":"~25% miss once","at":"bottom"},{"node":"db","text":"refill","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"PROBLEM","title":"Random arcs are uneven","body":"Hashing four server names onto a circle gives arcs of random length. One node can easily own 40% of the ring while another owns 10%. Worse, when a node dies its whole arc lands on one successor, which may double that node's load and make it fail too. In this unlucky example C ends up at 190% of the average load, close to twice the average.","idea":"With one point per server, ring positions are luck, and a dead node's load goes to a single neighbour.","caption":"C absorbs all of B's load and runs far above average.","focus":["ring","c"],"flows":[{"from":"ring","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"2x traffic"}],"metrics":{"moved":"25","hit":"71","hot":"190"},"chips":[{"node":"c","text":"arc up to 40%","tone":"red"},{"node":"c","text":"up to 2x avg load","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"ring","text":"random arcs"}]},{"tag":"FIX","title":"Virtual nodes smooth the load","body":"Instead of one point, each physical server gets many virtual nodes (vnodes), for example 128 points, each hashed from a name like C#17. The server owns many small arcs scattered around the ring. As a rule of thumb, load then lands within roughly 10% of equal (the busiest node drops to 110% of average), and when a server dies its arcs pass to many different neighbours. A bigger machine can simply be given more vnodes. The trade-off: more vnodes means a bigger ring to store and more peers involved in rebuilds.","idea":"Many small arcs per server turn random imbalance into a near-even split, and spread a failure across all survivors.","caption":"Each server owns many scattered arcs, so load is nearly even.","focus":["ring","a","b","c","d"],"flows":[{"from":"ring","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"vnodes A#1..128"},{"from":"ring","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"vnodes B#1..128"},{"from":"ring","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"vnodes C#1..128"},{"from":"ring","to":"d","kind":"request","label":"vnodes D#1..128"}],"metrics":{"moved":"25","hit":"71","hot":"110"},"chips":[{"node":"ring","text":"128 vnodes/server"},{"node":"a","text":"load within ~10%","tone":"green"},{"node":"d","text":"dies: arcs spread","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"IN PRACTICE","title":"Caches and Dynamo-style stores","body":"Cache clients such as memcached libraries use a ring so adding a server does not flush everything. Dynamo-style databases such as Cassandra and Riak follow the design of Amazon's original Dynamo paper (the managed DynamoDB service works differently). There, a key is stored on the next several distinct physical servers clockwise, typically 3. That adds replication on top of partitioning. With vnodes the next points may belong to the same machine, so the walk skips them until it has 3 different servers. Nodes share ring membership by gossip, so any node can route a request.","idea":"The same ring that places keys also picks replicas: the next few distinct nodes clockwise.","caption":"A key is copied to the next distinct nodes clockwise.","focus":["a","b","c"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"replica 2"},{"from":"a","to":"c","kind":"replicate","label":"replica 3"}],"metrics":{"moved":"25","hit":"71","hot":"110"},"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"3 replicas"},{"node":"b","text":"next 3 distinct"},{"node":"ring","text":"gossip membership","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Stable ownership under change","body":"Mod N is simple but remaps about N/(N+1) of keys when the server count changes. A hash ring puts keys and nodes on one circle, so a key belongs to the next node clockwise, and a change moves only about one node's share (1/N) of the keys. Virtual nodes fix uneven arcs and let big machines carry more. Rebalancing is limited, not free: moved keys still need to be copied or refilled.","idea":"Consistent hashing makes cluster membership changes local instead of global.","caption":"Ring plus vnodes: small, even, local moves.","focus":["client","ring","a","b","c","d","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"moved":"25","hit":"71","hot":"110"},"chips":[{"node":"ring","text":"moves ~1/N"},{"node":"ring","text":"mod N: N/(N+1)","at":"bottom"},{"node":"d","text":"25% moved"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A cluster using hash(key) mod N grows from 4 to 5 servers. About what fraction of keys now map to a different server?","options":["About 20%","About 50%","About 80%","About 100% exactly"],"answer":2,"why":"A key stays only if its hash gives the same remainder mod 4 and mod 5, roughly 1 in 5. So about 4/5, or 80%, move. It is not exactly 100% because some hashes coincide."},{"q":"On a hash ring with 5 equal nodes (one ring point each), a sixth is added. Roughly what share of keys move, and where do they come from?","options":["About 1/6 of keys, all taken from the new node's clockwise neighbour","About 5/6 of keys, from every node equally","About 1/6 of keys, taken evenly from all five existing nodes","About 1/6 of keys, from the new node's own old data"],"answer":0,"why":"With one point per node, the new node claims the arc just before it, roughly 1/6 of the ring, and every key in that arc previously belonged to the next node clockwise. All other keys keep their owner. Virtual nodes spread this donation across many nodes instead of one."},{"q":"Why do virtual nodes help when a physical server fails?","options":["They make the hash function faster","Every vnode is a full replica, so there are more copies","They reduce the number of replicas needed","Its many small arcs are inherited by many different survivors, not one neighbour"],"answer":3,"why":"With one point per server, a dead node's whole arc goes to one successor, which can overload. With many vnodes the arcs are scattered, so the extra load is spread across most surviving nodes."},{"q":"A store uses a ring and replicates each key to the next 3 vnodes clockwise. A subtle bug: all 3 turn out to be vnodes of the same physical machine. What went wrong?","options":["The hash function is not uniform enough","Replica choice must skip vnodes of an already chosen physical node","Replication should always use mod N instead","The cluster needs a central router to assign replicas"],"answer":1,"why":"Replicas on one machine die together, so replication is useless. Dynamo-style systems walk the ring and take the next distinct physical nodes, skipping extra vnodes of the same machine."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"hash mod N","def":"Simple partitioning, server = hash(key) mod N. Even, but changing N remaps about N/(N+1) of keys."},{"term":"Hash ring","def":"A circular hash space. Nodes and keys both hash onto it; a key belongs to the first node clockwise."},{"term":"Keys moved on change","def":"Adding or removing one node moves about 1/N of the keys, where N is the larger of the before and after node counts, and only to or from its ring neighbours."},{"term":"Virtual node (vnode)","def":"One of many ring points owned by a physical server, usually tens to a few hundred per node (memcached ketama uses 160; Cassandra 4+ defaults to 16 with token allocation). Evens out load and spreads failure."},{"term":"Weighted vnodes","def":"Give larger machines more vnodes so their share of the ring matches their capacity."},{"term":"Preference list","def":"In Dynamo-style stores, the next N distinct physical nodes clockwise from a key's position, used as its replicas."},{"term":"Still your job","def":"Moved keys must be copied or refilled, hot keys stay hot (a ring spreads keys, not traffic per key), and clients need a consistent node list."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"files","title":"Files & Object Storage","subtitle":"Follow a 2 GB video upload straight into an object store, and see why your API server never touches the bytes.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How block, file, and object storage differ and which fits which job","Why big files go in an object store and their metadata in a database","How a presigned URL lets a client upload without passing through your API","How multipart upload makes huge uploads resumable and parallel","How a CDN serves reads and how an upload event triggers background work"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Client app","kind":"client","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"api","label":"API server","kind":"server","x":34,"y":24},{"id":"meta","label":"Metadata DB","kind":"db","x":58,"y":10},{"id":"store","label":"Object store","kind":"storage","x":82,"y":50},{"id":"cdn","label":"CDN edge","kind":"cdn","x":58,"y":84},{"id":"worker","label":"Post-upload worker","kind":"worker","x":88,"y":84}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"api"},{"from":"api","to":"meta"},{"from":"client","to":"store"},{"from":"api","to":"store"},{"from":"client","to":"cdn"},{"from":"cdn","to":"store"},{"from":"store","to":"worker"},{"from":"worker","to":"meta"}],"metrics":[{"id":"apibytes","label":"API bytes","unit":"MB"},{"id":"parts","label":"Parts","unit":"count"},{"id":"urlttl","label":"URL lifetime","unit":"min"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Where do the bytes go?","body":"A user taps Upload on a 2 GB video. Your app also needs to remember who owns it, its title, and whether it has been processed yet. Do you push 2 GB through your API servers into a database column? Almost nobody does. This lesson shows where the bytes live, where the facts about them live, and how the two get connected.","idea":"Big binary data and small structured facts want different homes.","caption":"A client holds a 2 GB file, and the system must store it safely.","focus":["client"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Block, file, object","body":"There are three kinds of storage. Block storage is a raw disk: fixed-size blocks you read and write at an offset, used for database files and VM disks. File storage adds folders and paths that many machines can share (NFS-style). Object storage, like S3, is a flat bucket of whole objects found by key. You write a whole object with PUT and read it with GET, optionally just a byte range. You cannot edit bytes in the middle; you replace the whole object. In return you get huge scale, low cost per GB, and very high durability (S3 advertises 11 nines).","idea":"Block is for databases and VMs, file is for shared folders, object is for blobs at scale.","caption":"The object store holds whole blobs addressed by key.","focus":["store"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"11 nines","tone":"green"},{"node":"store","text":"PUT / GET whole","at":"bottom"},{"node":"store","text":"no in-place edit"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"The naive path: proxy it","body":"The simplest design is to proxy: the client sends the file to the API, and the API forwards it to the object store. It works, but every upload ties up an API connection for the whole transfer, and each byte crosses the API's network twice (in from the client, out to the store). The metric shows 2,048 MB through the API for one file. With 2 GB per upload and ten thousand users, your API fleet moves 20 TB. A slow phone on bad Wi-Fi can hold a worker for minutes.","idea":"Proxying uploads turns your API into a bandwidth pipe, which is the wrong thing to scale.","caption":"Every byte passes through the API server on its way to the store.","focus":["client","api","store"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"2 GB upload"},{"from":"api","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"2 GB forwarded"}],"metrics":{"apibytes":"2048"},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"2 GB via API","tone":"red"},{"node":"api","text":"20 TB at 10k users","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"store","text":"2 GB arrives"}]},{"tag":"PATTERN","title":"Metadata DB + blob","body":"Split the job in two. The blob (the raw file bytes) lives in the object store under a key such as videos/u42/9f3a.mp4. A row in the metadata database records the facts: owner, title, size, content type, the object key, and a status like pending. In the animation the client asks to upload and the API inserts that pending row. The database can answer 'list my videos by date', which an object store cannot do well, since it only lists keys by prefix. The row is the index; the blob is the payload.","idea":"Store the key and facts in a database; store the bytes in the object store.","caption":"The client asks to upload, and the API records a pending row.","focus":["client","api","meta"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"start upload"},{"from":"api","to":"meta","kind":"request","label":"INSERT pending"},{"from":"meta","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"id + key"}],"metrics":{"apibytes":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"meta","text":"status: pending"},{"node":"meta","text":"owner, key, size","at":"bottom"},{"node":"store","text":"videos/u42/9f3a.mp4"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Presigned URL","body":"The API asks its storage library to sign a URL for exactly one operation: PUT this key, valid for 15 minutes. Signing is local math using a secret the client never sees, so it takes microseconds and needs no storage call. The client gets the URL and uploads straight to the store, so 0 MB pass through the API. Anyone holding the URL can use it until it expires, so keep lifetimes short and scope it to one key. A signed PUT does not limit size, so validate after upload or use a presigned POST policy with a size limit.","idea":"A presigned URL is a short-lived permission slip: the API grants it, the store honors it.","caption":"The API hands the client a signed, expiring upload URL.","focus":["client","api"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"may I upload?"},{"from":"api","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"presigned PUT"}],"metrics":{"apibytes":"0","urlttl":"15"},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"signed PUT"},{"node":"api","text":"valid 15 min","at":"bottom"},{"node":"client","text":"uploads direct","tone":"green"},{"node":"api","text":"0 MB via API","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"MULTIPART","title":"Upload in parts","body":"One 2 GB PUT is fragile: if the connection drops at 95 percent, you start over. So for big files the API starts a multipart upload and signs one URL per part (here 32 parts of 64 MB; S3 needs at least 5 MiB per part except the last). The client PUTs the parts straight to the store in parallel and collects an ETag (a receipt) for each. It sends the ETag list to the API, which calls complete, and the store stitches the parts into one object. A failed part is retried alone. Save the upload ID in the row so a restarted client can resume.","idea":"Multipart makes big uploads parallel and resumable: retry a part, not the file.","caption":"The API signs 32 part URLs, and the client uploads the parts straight to the store.","focus":["client","api","store"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"start multipart"},{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"part URLs"},{"from":"api","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"32 signed URLs"},{"from":"client","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"parts 1..32"},{"from":"store","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"ETag per part"},{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"ETag list"},{"from":"api","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"complete"}],"metrics":{"parts":"32"},"chips":[{"node":"client","text":"32 parts x 64 MB"},{"node":"store","text":"parts in parallel","at":"bottom"},{"node":"api","text":"ETag list"},{"node":"client","text":"retry 1 part","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"ASYNC","title":"React to the upload","body":"When the object appears, the store emits an event (for example ObjectCreated) to a queue or function trigger, and a worker picks it up. The worker reads the object and treats it as untrusted: it checks type and size, makes thumbnails or transcodes, then flips the row from pending to ready. If the worker crashes, the message is redelivered. Delivery is at-least-once, so processing must be safe to run twice. New S3 objects are strongly consistent, so the worker sees the file right away.","idea":"Use storage events to trigger background work, then flip the row to ready.","caption":"The object arrives, an event fires, and a worker marks the row ready.","focus":["store","worker","meta"],"flows":[{"from":"store","to":"worker","kind":"async","label":"ObjectCreated"},{"from":"worker","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"read object"},{"from":"store","to":"worker","kind":"response","label":"bytes"},{"from":"worker","to":"meta","kind":"request","label":"status=ready"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"ObjectCreated"},{"node":"worker","text":"at-least-once","at":"bottom"},{"node":"meta","text":"pending -> ready","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"READ","title":"Serve it from the edge","body":"To watch the video, the client asks the API where it is. The API looks up the row in the metadata DB and returns a CDN URL. A CDN (content delivery network) keeps cached copies at edge locations near viewers. The first viewer in a region causes a cache miss, and the CDN pulls the file from the object store. Later viewers get the cached copy from nearby. Private files use a presigned GET URL straight from the store, or CDN-signed URLs if you still want edge caching.","idea":"Read through a CDN for speed and cost; use a presigned GET for private files.","caption":"The CDN fills from the store on a miss, then serves nearby viewers.","focus":["client","api","meta","cdn","store"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"where is it?"},{"from":"api","to":"meta","kind":"request","label":"lookup row"},{"from":"meta","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"object key"},{"from":"api","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"CDN URL"},{"from":"client","to":"cdn","kind":"request","label":"GET video"},{"from":"cdn","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"cache miss"},{"from":"store","to":"cdn","kind":"response","label":"object bytes"},{"from":"cdn","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"video"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"cdn","text":"1st viewer: miss"},{"node":"cdn","text":"later: cache hit","tone":"green"},{"node":"meta","text":"DB lookup","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The upload is abandoned","body":"The user closes the app after 20 of 32 parts. Those parts sit in the store, invisible but billed, and the row is stuck on pending. Two cleanups fix this. A job deletes pending rows older than a day and aborts their multipart upload. A lifecycle rule (a storage setting that acts on objects by age) aborts incomplete multipart uploads after, say, 7 days, as a backstop for anything the job misses. The mirror problem is deleting a row but forgetting the blob, so deletes should remove both.","idea":"Two systems mean two cleanups: expire stale multipart parts and stale pending rows.","caption":"A dropped upload leaves orphaned parts behind and a row stuck on pending.","focus":["client","store","meta"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"store","kind":"fail","label":"connection lost"}],"metrics":{"parts":"20"},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"20 of 32 parts","tone":"red"},{"node":"meta","text":"stuck: pending","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"store","text":"abort after 7 d"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"The whole pattern","body":"Bytes go in an object store, facts go in a metadata database, and the API only brokers permission. A presigned URL lets the client upload directly, multipart makes huge files resumable, events trigger processing, and a CDN serves reads. Block storage stays for databases and VM disks. Object stores cannot edit in place, so changing a file means writing a new object.","idea":"Keep your API out of the data path: sign a URL and let the client talk to the store.","caption":"The API handles tiny messages while the store handles gigabytes.","focus":["client","api","meta","store","cdn","worker"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"apibytes":"0","parts":"32","urlttl":"15"},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"bytes"},{"node":"meta","text":"facts","at":"bottom"},{"node":"api","text":"0 MB via API","tone":"green"},{"node":"cdn","text":"edge reads"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why do most designs hand the client a presigned URL instead of proxying the upload through the API?","options":["Object stores refuse requests that come from API servers","Presigned URLs encrypt the file in transit, which an API server cannot do","The bytes skip your API, so it only handles tiny requests and scales independently of upload volume","Presigned URLs remove the need for a metadata database"],"answer":2,"why":"The signature is created locally and the client sends bytes directly to the store, so API servers do not carry upload bandwidth. You still need a database for metadata."},{"q":"A 2 GB multipart upload of 32 parts fails on part 27 because of a network blip. What should the client do?","options":["Retry only part 27, then complete the upload","Restart all 32 parts","Switch to block storage","Delete the metadata row and ask the user to try again later"],"answer":0,"why":"Each part is uploaded and acknowledged independently with its own ETag, so only the failed part needs to be resent before the complete call."},{"q":"Why store the object key and attributes in a database row rather than relying on the bucket alone?","options":["Object stores cannot attach any metadata to an object at all","Object keys are not allowed to contain a user's name or ID","Objects are encrypted at rest so they cannot be read back by key","A database can query by owner, date or status; a bucket mainly lists keys by prefix"],"answer":3,"why":"Object stores are good at get-by-key and prefix listing. Questions like all videos owned by user 42 this week belong in a database that can index those fields."},{"q":"You need to update a single 4 KB page in the middle of a large data file many times per second. Which storage fits best?","options":["Object storage, rewriting the object each time","Block storage, which supports reads and writes at an offset","File storage, because it stores folders of objects","A presigned URL with a long lifetime"],"answer":1,"why":"Block storage allows small in-place writes at an offset, which is why databases use it. Objects are replaced whole, so a 4 KB edit would mean rewriting the entire object."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Block storage","def":"Raw fixed-size blocks read or written at an offset. Used for database files and VM disks. Low latency, in-place updates."},{"term":"File storage","def":"Hierarchical folders and paths, often shared by many machines over a network. Good for shared home directories and legacy apps."},{"term":"Object storage","def":"Flat bucket of whole objects found by key. Cheap, huge, durable, but no in-place partial edits: replace the whole object."},{"term":"Metadata DB + blob","def":"Keep owner, size, status and the object key in a database row; keep the bytes in the object store. The row is how you search and list."},{"term":"Presigned URL","def":"A time-limited URL signed by your server for one operation on one key. Lets a client talk directly to the store. Keep expiry short."},{"term":"Multipart upload","def":"Split a large file into parts (S3: 5 MiB to 5 GiB each, max 10,000). The API starts the upload and signs one URL per part; the client uploads in parallel, retries failed parts, then the API completes. Abort stale ones with a lifecycle rule."},{"term":"Storage events","def":"The store emits a notification when an object is created, so a worker can scan, resize or transcode it. Handlers must be idempotent."},{"term":"CDN in front","def":"Cache popular objects at edge locations. Cuts latency and egress cost; for private content use a presigned GET from the store, or CDN-signed URLs to keep edge caching."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"search-index","title":"Search & Inverted Indexes","subtitle":"Watch a text query hit a sharded search cluster, and see how data from the main database gets into it.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How text is tokenized and normalized so queries match documents","What an inverted index is and why it beats scanning every document","How TF-IDF and BM25 turn matches into a ranked list","How a query fans out to shards and the results are merged","How the search index stays in sync with the primary database, and what happens when it lags"],"nodes":[{"id":"user","label":"Search box","kind":"user","x":8,"y":40},{"id":"api","label":"Search service","kind":"service","x":31,"y":40},{"id":"s1","label":"Shard 1","kind":"storage","x":60,"y":14},{"id":"s2","label":"Shard 2","kind":"storage","x":60,"y":40},{"id":"s3","label":"Shard 3","kind":"storage","x":60,"y":66},{"id":"db","label":"Primary database","kind":"db","x":8,"y":88},{"id":"feed","label":"Change stream","kind":"queue","x":31,"y":88},{"id":"indexer","label":"Indexer","kind":"worker","x":60,"y":88}],"edges":[{"from":"user","to":"api"},{"from":"api","to":"s1"},{"from":"api","to":"s2"},{"from":"api","to":"s3"},{"from":"db","to":"feed"},{"from":"feed","to":"indexer"},{"from":"indexer","to":"s3"}],"metrics":[{"id":"query","label":"Query time","unit":"ms"},{"id":"shards","label":"Shards hit","unit":""},{"id":"lag","label":"Index lag","unit":"s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Find the words, fast","body":"A shop has 300 million product listings, about 1 KB of text each, so roughly 300 GB. A user types 'wireless headphones'. Reading every listing and checking for those words means scanning 300 GB. At 1 GB per second that takes about five minutes (300,000 ms). A normal B-tree index on one column does not help, because the user's words can be anywhere in the text. Search needs a different structure.","idea":"Scanning text for every query is hopeless; search engines precompute where every word lives.","caption":"A query arrives, and it must not touch 300 million documents.","focus":["user","api"],"flows":[{"from":"user","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"wireless headph."}],"metrics":{"query":"300000","shards":"0","lag":"0"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Tokenize and normalize","body":"Before text is stored, an analyzer cleans it up. It splits 'Wireless Headphones, 40% off!' into tokens (words), lowercases them, drops punctuation, and often removes filler words like 'the'. Many analyzers also stem words, meaning they cut them to a root, so 'headphones' and 'headphone' become one term. The same analyzer must run on the query, or 'Headphones' would never match 'headphone'. In the animation the service cleans the query and passes it on. Shard 2 stands in for any shard.","idea":"Index time and query time must use the same analysis, or terms will not line up.","caption":"The analyzer turns the raw query into the same terms the index stores.","focus":["user","api","s2"],"flows":[{"from":"user","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"Wireless Headph."},{"from":"api","to":"s2","kind":"request","label":"wireless headphone"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"lowercase + stem"},{"node":"s2","text":"headphones -> stem","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"The inverted index","body":"An inverted index maps each term to a posting list: the sorted IDs of the documents that contain it. Like the index at the back of a book, it goes from word to pages. Say 'wireless' appears in docs 3, 8, 21 and 'headphone' in docs 8, 21, 40. For a query needing both words, the engine walks the two sorted lists and keeps the IDs found in both: docs 8 and 21. It reads two short lists instead of the whole shard, in about 6 ms.","idea":"An inverted index flips documents-to-words into words-to-documents, so a lookup touches only relevant IDs.","caption":"One shard intersects two posting lists and finds docs 8 and 21.","focus":["api","s2"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"s2","kind":"request","label":"wireless+headphone"},{"from":"s2","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"docs 8, 21"}],"metrics":{"query":"6","shards":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"s2","text":"wireless: 3, 8, 21"},{"node":"s2","text":"headphone: 8, 21, 40","at":"bottom"},{"node":"s2","text":"match: 8, 21","tone":"green"},{"node":"api","text":"6 ms","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Ranking the matches","body":"Matching is not enough. Thousands of listings may contain both words, and the user wants the best first. TF-IDF scores a document higher when the term appears often in it (term frequency, TF), and lower when the term is common everywhere (inverse document frequency, IDF), since 'the' tells you nothing. BM25, the default in Lucene-based engines, refines this: repeated terms give diminishing returns, and long documents are penalized. In the animation the shard returns doc 21 with a score of 7.3. Ranking adds a little time: about 9 ms.","idea":"Relevance rewards terms that are frequent in this document and rare overall; BM25 adds diminishing returns and length adjustment.","caption":"The shard returns its matches with relevance scores attached.","focus":["api","s2"],"flows":[{"from":"s2","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"doc 21: 7.3"}],"metrics":{"query":"9"},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"BM25 ranking"},{"node":"s2","text":"TF-IDF basis","at":"bottom"},{"node":"api","text":"9 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Shard the index","body":"300 million documents will not fit well on one machine, so the index is split into shards. Each document goes to one shard, chosen by hashing its ID. The search service cannot know which shard holds the best answers, so it sends the query to all of them in parallel. This is the scatter step. Each shard searches only its own third of the data, about 100 million documents.","idea":"Sharding splits the data; every query fans out to every shard because any shard may hold matches.","caption":"The query is scattered to all three shards at once.","focus":["api","s1","s2","s3"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"s1","kind":"request","label":"query"},{"from":"api","to":"s2","kind":"request","label":"query"},{"from":"api","to":"s3","kind":"request","label":"query"}],"metrics":{"shards":"3","query":"12"},"chips":[{"node":"s1","text":"~100M docs"},{"node":"s2","text":"~100M docs"},{"node":"s3","text":"~100M docs"},{"node":"api","text":"12 ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Gather and merge","body":"Each shard replies with only its own top 10 hits and their scores. The search service merges the three short lists and keeps the global top 10. This is the gather step. Total time (35 ms here, including network and merging) is set by the slowest shard, so one sluggish shard slows every query. Deep pages are costly: asking for results 1000 to 1010 forces each shard to return its top 1010.","idea":"Latency is the slowest shard plus merge cost, and deep pagination multiplies the work.","caption":"The shards reply, the service merges the top hits, and the user gets 10 results.","focus":["user","api","s1","s2","s3"],"flows":[{"from":"s1","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"top 10"},{"from":"s2","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"top 10"},{"from":"s3","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"top 10"},{"from":"api","to":"user","kind":"response","label":"10 results"}],"metrics":{"query":"35"},"chips":[{"node":"s1","text":"top 10 hits"},{"node":"api","text":"merge 3 lists","at":"bottom"},{"node":"api","text":"35 ms"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Syncing from the database","body":"The primary database is the source of truth. The search index is a derived copy. When a seller edits a listing, the change is read from the database's change log and put on a stream (a feed of events). An indexer consumes it, re-analyzes the text, and writes it to the right shard, about 2 seconds later. Writing to both the database and the index from the app is risky: if one write fails, the two silently disagree.","idea":"Treat the index as a rebuildable copy fed by a change stream, not as a second place to write.","caption":"A database change flows through the stream into a shard.","focus":["db","feed","indexer","s3"],"flows":[{"from":"db","to":"feed","kind":"async","label":"row changed"},{"from":"feed","to":"indexer","kind":"async","label":"listing 8841"},{"from":"indexer","to":"s3","kind":"async","label":"index doc"}],"metrics":{"lag":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"source of truth","tone":"green"},{"node":"feed","text":"change stream"},{"node":"indexer","text":"re-analyze","at":"bottom"},{"node":"s3","text":"lag 2 s"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The indexer falls behind","body":"Suppose the indexer crashes, or a bulk price update floods the stream. Searches still work, but they show stale data: a sold-out item still appears, and a new listing is missing. Index lag climbs from seconds to minutes (3 minutes here). Because the stream keeps the events, a restarted indexer replays them. If the index is badly wrong, rebuild it from the database into a fresh index and swap it in.","idea":"Search is eventually consistent with the database; monitor lag and keep a way to replay or rebuild.","caption":"The indexer is down, so changes pile up in the stream and results go stale.","focus":["feed","indexer"],"flows":[{"from":"feed","to":"indexer","kind":"fail","label":"consumer down"}],"metrics":{"lag":"180"},"chips":[{"node":"indexer","text":"crashed","tone":"red"},{"node":"feed","text":"events kept","tone":"green"},{"node":"s3","text":"stale results","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"indexer","text":"lag 3 min","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"From words to ranked results","body":"Analyzers turn text into normalized terms. An inverted index maps each term to the documents containing it, so a query reads short posting lists instead of everything. BM25-style scoring ranks the matches. Sharding spreads the data, queries scatter to all shards and merge the top hits, and a change stream keeps the index fed from the database, accepting some lag.","idea":"Search = analyze, invert, score, shard, and feed the index from the source of truth.","caption":"The full path: sync in the background, search in milliseconds.","focus":["user","api","s1","s2","s3","db","feed","indexer"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"query":"35","shards":"3","lag":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"35 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"feed","text":"lag 2 s"},{"node":"s2","text":"inverted index","at":"bottom"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your index stores the term \"headphone\", but a user searches \"Headphones\" and gets zero results. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The posting list for headphone is too long","The query was not run through the same analyzer (lowercasing and stemming) as the documents","There are too few shards","BM25 scored the document too low to show"],"answer":1,"why":"Matching happens on normalized terms. If the query skips the analysis used at index time, its terms never line up with the stored ones."},{"q":"A query for \"the red shoes\" returns plenty of documents. Why does the word \"the\" barely affect their ranking?","options":["Its inverse document frequency is very low because almost every document contains it","Its term frequency is always zero","Stemming removes it from the query","Posting lists cannot store common terms"],"answer":0,"why":"IDF measures rarity across the corpus. A term in nearly every document has a tiny IDF, so it adds almost nothing to the score."},{"q":"A 3-shard cluster has p50 shard latency of 10 ms, but one shard often takes 120 ms. What happens to search latency?","options":["It stays near 10 ms because results are averaged","It becomes about 40 ms, the mean of the shards","Many queries take about 120 ms, since the merge waits for the slowest shard","Only 1/3 of queries are affected"],"answer":2,"why":"A scattered query is not complete until every shard replies, so the slowest shard sets latency for every query it participates in, and that is all of them."},{"q":"Your app writes a product to the database, then to the search index. The index write fails after the DB write succeeds. Which design best prevents silent drift?","options":["Retry the index write forever inside the web request","Write to the index first, then the database","Skip the database and store products only in the search index","Feed the index from the database's change stream so missed updates can be replayed"],"answer":3,"why":"A change stream makes the database the single source of truth and lets an indexer retry or replay changes. Dual writes from the app have no such recovery path."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Tokenization / analysis","def":"Splitting text into terms and normalizing them (lowercase, strip punctuation, drop stop words, stem). Must be identical at index and query time."},{"term":"Inverted index","def":"Map from each term to a sorted posting list of document IDs that contain it. Multi-word queries intersect or union the lists."},{"term":"TF-IDF","def":"Score = how often a term appears in a document, weighted by how rare it is across all documents. Common words count for little."},{"term":"BM25","def":"Modern TF-IDF refinement: repeated terms saturate (parameter k1, often 1.2) and long documents are normalized (parameter b, often 0.75)."},{"term":"Scatter-gather","def":"Send the query to every shard, take each shard's top N, merge into the global top N. Latency follows the slowest shard."},{"term":"Deep pagination","def":"Asking for page 100 makes every shard return 100 pages of hits. Prefer search-after cursors over large offsets."},{"term":"Change stream / CDC","def":"A feed of row changes read from the database log, consumed by an indexer. Keeps the index a rebuildable copy of the source of truth."},{"term":"Index lag","def":"Delay between a database change and its visibility in search. Typically seconds; track it and alert when it grows."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"geospatial","title":"Geospatial Indexing","subtitle":"Watch a ride app find the nearest drivers by turning 2D space into cells it can look up directly.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why latitude and longitude do not work well with ordinary indexes","How geohash turns a location into a string whose prefix means 'same box'","How a quadtree adapts to crowded and empty areas","What S2 and H3 cells add, and why you search a cell plus its neighbours","How a live driver index is kept fresh and queried in a few milliseconds"],"nodes":[{"id":"rider","label":"Rider app","kind":"user","x":10,"y":22},{"id":"driver","label":"Driver app","kind":"client","x":10,"y":78},{"id":"search","label":"Nearby service","kind":"service","x":36,"y":22},{"id":"queue","label":"Location stream","kind":"queue","x":36,"y":78},{"id":"worker","label":"Index updater","kind":"worker","x":62,"y":78},{"id":"cache","label":"Cell index","kind":"cache","x":62,"y":26},{"id":"db","label":"Driver store","kind":"db","x":88,"y":52}],"edges":[{"from":"rider","to":"search"},{"from":"search","to":"cache"},{"from":"search","to":"db"},{"from":"driver","to":"queue"},{"from":"queue","to":"worker"},{"from":"worker","to":"cache"},{"from":"worker","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"candidates","label":"Candidates","unit":"drivers"},{"id":"cells","label":"Cells read","unit":"cells"},{"id":"latency","label":"Query time","unit":"ms"},{"id":"pings","label":"Location pings","unit":"/sec"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Who is closest to me?","body":"A rider taps 'request ride' at one point in a city. About 200,000 drivers are online in the metro area, each one a latitude and longitude that changes every few seconds. The app must return the 5 closest available drivers in well under a second. How do you search positions without checking every driver?","idea":"Proximity search asks for everything near a point, and a point has two coordinates, not one.","caption":"A rider asks for nearby drivers.","focus":["rider","search"],"flows":[{"from":"rider","to":"search","kind":"request","label":"near 12.97, 77.59"}],"metrics":{"candidates":"200000"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"PROBLEM","title":"The naive scan","body":"The simplest answer computes the distance to every driver and sorts. That is 200,000 distance calculations per request. An ordinary B-tree index on latitude does not rescue you: it narrows only one axis, to a long stripe across the whole city, and you still filter the rest. Even a composite (lat, lon) index narrows well only on the first column. Real databases ship spatial indexes (PostGIS uses an R-tree, a tree of nested bounding rectangles; Redis GEO uses geohash). The next steps show the cell-based idea. Assume the full scan costs roughly 400 ms, which is too slow at thousands of requests per second.","idea":"A one-dimensional index cannot answer a two-dimensional 'nearby' question efficiently.","caption":"Scanning every driver takes about 400 ms.","focus":["search","db"],"flows":[{"from":"search","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"scan 200k rows"}],"metrics":{"candidates":"200000","latency":"400"},"chips":[{"node":"search","text":"200k distance calcs","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"~400 ms","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"db","text":"full scan","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"GEOHASH","title":"Geohash: space as a string","body":"Geohash repeatedly halves the map, alternating longitude and latitude, and writes the result as a short string such as 'tdr1v9'. Points in the same box share the same prefix. At 6 characters a cell is roughly 1.2 km by 0.6 km; at 7 it is about 150 m square. Now 'nearby' becomes an ordinary lookup: fetch the drivers stored under the rider's cell key. Here that returns 14 drivers instead of 200,000, and an in-memory index answers in about 4 ms.","idea":"Geohash turns a 2D position into a string key, so a plain key lookup or prefix scan finds a neighbourhood.","caption":"Look up one cell key, not every driver.","focus":["search","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"search","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"cell tdr1v9"},{"from":"cache","to":"search","kind":"response","label":"14 drivers"}],"metrics":{"cells":"1","candidates":"14","latency":"4"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"geohash, 6 chars"},{"node":"cache","text":"~1.2 km x 0.6 km","at":"bottom"},{"node":"cache","text":"14 candidates"},{"node":"search","text":"4 ms","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"The boundary problem","body":"A rider standing at the edge of a cell may have the closest driver just across the line, in the next cell with a completely different prefix. A shared prefix guarantees nearness, but a different prefix does not mean far away. The standard fix is to query the rider's cell plus its 8 neighbours (9 cells, about 60 candidates here), then compute exact distances on those few results. Pick a cell at least as wide as the distance you care about. If you get fewer than 5 candidates, widen to the next ring of cells.","idea":"Cells are a coarse filter: search the cell and its neighbours, then rank by exact distance.","caption":"Query 9 cells, get a short candidate list.","focus":["search","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"search","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"own + 8 neighbours"},{"from":"cache","to":"search","kind":"response","label":"~60 candidates"}],"metrics":{"cells":"9","candidates":"60","latency":"8"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"cell + 8 neighbours"},{"node":"cache","text":"60 candidates","at":"bottom"},{"node":"search","text":"8 ms"}]},{"tag":"QUADTREE","title":"Quadtree: cells that adapt","body":"A quadtree starts with the whole map as one box. Whenever a box holds more than a set number of points, say 100, it splits into four children, and so on. Dense downtown ends up in tiny boxes while an empty suburb stays one big box. Lookups descend the tree. The catch: it usually lives in memory on one node, and moving drivers mean constant inserts and removals. It has the same edge problem too, so you also read neighbouring leaves. Here we count a leaf plus 4 adjacent leaves (5 cells).","idea":"A quadtree splits only where data is dense, so every leaf holds a similar number of points.","caption":"The tree descends to a small leaf box.","focus":["search","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"search","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"descend tree"},{"from":"cache","to":"search","kind":"response","label":"leaf + neighbours"}],"metrics":{"cells":"5","candidates":"60","latency":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"splits at 100 pts"},{"node":"cache","text":"leaf + 4 leaves","at":"bottom"},{"node":"search","text":"5 ms","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"S2 / H3","title":"S2 and H3: cells on a sphere","body":"Flat grids distort near the poles. Google's S2 maps the globe onto a cube and orders cells along a space-filling curve (a path that visits every cell while keeping neighbours close in order), so each cell is a single 64-bit id and nearby cells get nearby ids. Uber's H3 uses hexagons, whose 6 neighbours are all the same distance away, which makes 'rings' of cells simple. H3 resolution runs 0 to 15, higher meaning smaller hexagons; at resolution 8 a hexagon is about 0.7 square km. A hexagon plus one ring is 7 cells.","idea":"S2 and H3 are geohash's idea done on a sphere: fixed ids per cell, with cheap neighbour and ring lookups.","caption":"Search the rider's hexagon plus one ring of neighbours.","focus":["search","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"search","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"H3 res 8, ring 1"},{"from":"cache","to":"search","kind":"response","label":"ids in 7 cells"}],"metrics":{"cells":"7","candidates":"55","latency":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"S2: 64-bit cell id"},{"node":"cache","text":"H3 hexagons","at":"bottom"},{"node":"cache","text":"res 8 ~ 0.7 km2"}]},{"tag":"UPDATES","title":"Keeping drivers fresh","body":"Each driver app sends a GPS ping every 4 seconds, so 200,000 drivers produce about 50,000 writes per second. Pings go onto a stream, and an updater reads them. On every ping it refreshes the driver's last-seen time, a cheap in-memory write. It moves the id between cell sets only when the driver crosses into a new cell. A periodic sweep (or a per-driver key with a TTL, time to live) drops anything not refreshed within about 30 seconds, so offline drivers disappear on their own. The latest coordinates also go to the driver store (a key-value store handles this rate, or you can batch writes).","idea":"Refresh last-seen on every ping, move cell membership only on a cell change, and let stale entries expire.","caption":"Pings refresh the driver; a cell change moves the id.","focus":["driver","queue","worker","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"driver","to":"queue","kind":"async","label":"GPS ping"},{"from":"queue","to":"worker","kind":"async","label":"batch"},{"from":"worker","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"move cell A to B"},{"from":"worker","to":"db","kind":"async","label":"latest position"}],"metrics":{"pings":"50000"},"chips":[{"node":"driver","text":"ping every 4 s"},{"node":"queue","text":"50k writes/s"},{"node":"worker","text":"moves on cell change","at":"bottom"},{"node":"cache","text":"TTL ~30 s"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Rank and answer","body":"With about 60 candidate ids in hand, the service loads each driver's current coordinates, rating and availability from the driver store. It computes the true distance, drops anyone who is busy or too far, sorts, and returns the top 5. The cell lookup shrank 200,000 drivers to a few dozen, so the expensive exact work runs on almost nothing. The 12 ms here is the cell lookup plus loading 60 rows.","idea":"Two phases win: a cheap cell filter to shortlist, then exact math on the shortlist.","caption":"Shortlist, exact distance, top 5 back.","focus":["rider","search","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"search","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"cells"},{"from":"cache","to":"search","kind":"response","label":"60 ids"},{"from":"search","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"load 60"},{"from":"db","to":"search","kind":"response","label":"rows"},{"from":"search","to":"rider","kind":"response","label":"top 5"}],"metrics":{"cells":"9","candidates":"60","latency":"12"},"chips":[{"node":"search","text":"60 candidates"},{"node":"search","text":"top 5","at":"bottom"},{"node":"search","text":"12 ms","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A stadium empties","body":"Fixed-size cells assume roughly even density. After a concert, 5,000 drivers pile into a single cell, so one lookup returns a huge list while the quiet cell next door is empty. Assume a lookup of that size takes about 60 ms. Fixes: use a finer resolution where cells are busy, let a quadtree split that area, or cap the shortlist per ring and expand outward in distance order only if you need more.","idea":"Uneven density breaks fixed cells; go finer or adaptive in hot spots.","caption":"One hot cell returns thousands of ids.","focus":["search","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"cache","to":"search","kind":"response","label":"5,000 ids"}],"metrics":{"cells":"9","candidates":"5000","latency":"60"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"5,000 in one cell","tone":"red"},{"node":"cache","text":"60 ms","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"From 200,000 to a few dozen","body":"Proximity search is hard because 'near' is two-dimensional. Geohash, quadtrees, S2 and H3 all map space to cells so that a lookup replaces a scan. Usually you search the cell plus its neighbours, then rank by exact distance. Keep the index in memory, update it only when a driver changes cell, and choose the cell size to match how crowded the area is.","idea":"Index space as cells, shortlist by cell and neighbours, then rank exactly.","caption":"Cells turn a scan into a lookup.","focus":["rider","driver","search","queue","worker","cache","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"cells":"9","candidates":"60","latency":"12","pings":"50000"},"chips":[{"node":"search","text":"200k -> ~60","tone":"green"},{"node":"cache","text":"in memory","at":"bottom"},{"node":"search","text":"12 ms","tone":"green"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can't a plain B-tree index on latitude alone make 'drivers within 2 km' fast?","options":["It indexes the longitude axis only","It narrows only one axis, leaving a long stripe of rows to filter by longitude","It would need one index per driver","It only works for exact matches"],"answer":1,"why":"A latitude range selects a band across the whole city. The longitude condition still has to be checked row by row inside it, so the index helps far less than for a one-dimensional query."},{"q":"A rider sits right on the edge of a geohash cell. What query avoids missing the closest driver?","options":["Search only the rider's own cell with a longer prefix","Retry with a shorter hash only if the first result is empty","Search the rider's cell and its 8 neighbours, then sort by exact distance","Sort all drivers by their geohash string"],"answer":2,"why":"Nearby points can fall in different cells. Reading the neighbours covers drivers within about one cell width, and exact distance on the small shortlist picks the closest of them."},{"q":"Which situation most favours a quadtree over fixed-size cells?","options":["A dense downtown next to large empty areas","Drivers spread evenly across a flat plain","Needing a 64-bit id per location","Storing the index only on disk"],"answer":0,"why":"A quadtree splits only where points are dense. Fixed cells are either too coarse downtown or needlessly many in sparse areas; a quadtree gives each leaf a similar number of points."},{"q":"Drivers ping every 4 seconds, yet a driver moves between cell sets far less often. Why?","options":["Pings are dropped at random","Cells are recomputed once per hour","Riders update the index instead","Most pings keep the driver in the same cell, so only a last-seen refresh is needed"],"answer":3,"why":"A cell is roughly 150 m to a kilometre wide, so most pings leave the driver in the same cell. The updater just refreshes last-seen and moves set membership only on a cell change, which keeps index churn low."},{"q":"After a concert, one cell returns 5,000 candidates and queries slow down. What is the most sensible response?","options":["Use a coarser cell size everywhere","Use finer cells or an adaptive split in that hot area","Remove the neighbour lookup","Move the index to disk"],"answer":1,"why":"The cell is too coarse for that density. A finer resolution or a quadtree split keeps the shortlist small where the crowd is, without changing quiet areas."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Geohash","def":"String encoding of a location from interleaved lat/lon bits. Longer string means smaller box; a shared prefix means the same enclosing box."},{"term":"Boundary problem","def":"Close points can sit in different cells. Query the cell plus its neighbours, then rank by exact distance."},{"term":"Quadtree","def":"Recursive 4-way split of the map, splitting a box only when it holds too many points. Adapts to uneven density."},{"term":"S2","def":"Google's library: sphere mapped to a cube, cells ordered along a Hilbert curve (a space-filling curve that keeps neighbours close in order), each cell a 64-bit id with 31 levels."},{"term":"H3","def":"Uber's hexagonal grid with 16 resolutions (0 to 15; higher means smaller hexagons). Every neighbour is equidistant, so ring and distance queries are simple."},{"term":"Filter then rank","def":"Use cells as a cheap coarse filter to shortlist tens of candidates, then compute exact distance on just those."},{"term":"Cell size choice","def":"Rule of thumb: pick a size that holds tens of results, not thousands. Go finer in dense areas, widen rings in sparse ones."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"time-series","title":"Time-Series Data","subtitle":"Watch a metrics system absorb 100,000 points per second, roll old data up, and drop it on schedule.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why time-series workloads are append-heavy and mostly read as recent ranges","How time-partitioned chunks make writes, queries and deletes cheap","How rollups (downsampling) trade detail for speed and storage","Why retention is a policy of dropping whole chunks, not deleting rows","How high-cardinality labels blow up the number of series"],"nodes":[{"id":"agents","label":"App hosts","kind":"client","x":9,"y":50},{"id":"queue","label":"Ingest queue","kind":"queue","x":29,"y":50},{"id":"writer","label":"Ingest writer","kind":"worker","x":50,"y":50},{"id":"hot","label":"Raw chunks (hot)","kind":"db","x":72,"y":22},{"id":"rollup","label":"Rollup job","kind":"worker","x":72,"y":78},{"id":"cold","label":"Rollup store","kind":"storage","x":92,"y":78},{"id":"dash","label":"Dashboard","kind":"user","x":92,"y":22}],"edges":[{"from":"agents","to":"queue"},{"from":"queue","to":"writer"},{"from":"writer","to":"hot"},{"from":"hot","to":"rollup"},{"from":"rollup","to":"cold"},{"from":"dash","to":"hot"},{"from":"dash","to":"cold"}],"metrics":[{"id":"writes","label":"Writes","unit":"k pts/s"},{"id":"rawday","label":"Raw per day","unit":"GB"},{"id":"keep","label":"Raw kept","unit":"days"},{"id":"query","label":"Query time","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"A firehose of numbers","body":"Picture 10,000 servers, each reporting 100 metrics (CPU, latency, queue depth) every 10 seconds. That is 1 million separate series (one stream of values per metric per server) and 100,000 new points per second, forever. Nobody edits old points, and people mostly ask about the last hour. For now raw data is kept for 90 days. How do you store this so it stays fast and cheap? These numbers are an assumed scenario.","idea":"Time-series data is a never-ending stream of timestamped appends, read mostly by recent time range.","caption":"Many hosts, constant small writes.","focus":["agents"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"writes":"100","rawday":"17","keep":"90","query":"0"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Buffer the stream","body":"Each host sends points as (series name, timestamp, value). They land in an ingest queue first, so a slow database or a traffic spike delays points instead of losing them. The writer then pulls batches (5,000 points here) instead of handling 100,000 tiny writes per second one by one. Appending in bulk is cheap; many tiny writes are not.","idea":"Buffer and batch: appends are cheap in bulk and painful one at a time.","caption":"Points flow through a buffer to the writer.","focus":["agents","queue","writer"],"flows":[{"from":"agents","to":"queue","kind":"async","label":"name,ts,value"},{"from":"queue","to":"writer","kind":"async","label":"batch of 5,000"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"queue","text":"buffers spikes","tone":"green"},{"node":"writer","text":"batch writes","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Append into the newest chunk","body":"The writer groups points by series and writes them into the chunk for the current time window, say one day. Within a chunk, data compresses well: consecutive timestamps differ by a steady 10 seconds, and values change slowly. At about 2 bytes per point (good schemes reach 1 to 2), a day of 8.6 billion points is about 17 GB here. Slightly late points are still accepted into recent chunks.","idea":"Writes only touch the newest chunk, and regular timestamps compress extremely well.","caption":"Today's chunk takes all the writes.","focus":["writer","hot"],"flows":[{"from":"writer","to":"hot","kind":"request","label":"append to today"},{"from":"hot","to":"writer","kind":"response","label":"ack"}],"metrics":{"rawday":"17"},"chips":[{"node":"hot","text":"1-2 bytes/point"},{"node":"hot","text":"~17 GB/day","at":"bottom"},{"node":"hot","text":"1-day chunk"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Query a time range","body":"A dashboard asks for one service's p95 latency (the value 95 percent of requests beat) over the last hour. Because data is partitioned by time, the database opens only today's chunk and skips every older one. This is partition pruning, and it is the main reason time-partitioned storage stays fast as history grows. This narrow query reads 360 points (one hour at 10 s) and returns in about 40 ms.","idea":"Time partitioning lets a range query ignore almost all the data.","caption":"One chunk is read; the rest are skipped.","focus":["dash","hot"],"flows":[{"from":"dash","to":"hot","kind":"request","label":"last 1h, svc=api"},{"from":"hot","to":"dash","kind":"response","label":"360 points"}],"metrics":{"query":"40"},"chips":[{"node":"dash","text":"p95, last hour"},{"node":"hot","text":"newest chunk only","tone":"green"},{"node":"hot","text":"~40 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Long ranges hurt","body":"Now someone charts one metric across 90 days from raw data. The database must open 90 chunks and read every point for the matching series (over 700,000), yet the screen can only draw a couple thousand points anyway. Scanning far more detail than the chart can show wastes time and money. In a big fleet this kind of query can take many seconds (about 9 s here).","idea":"Raw points answer long-range questions slowly, and the chart cannot show that detail anyway.","caption":"A 90-day raw scan is slow.","focus":["dash","hot"],"flows":[{"from":"dash","to":"hot","kind":"fail","label":"90 days raw"}],"metrics":{"query":"9000"},"chips":[{"node":"dash","text":"90 days of raw","tone":"red"},{"node":"hot","text":"90 chunks","tone":"red"},{"node":"dash","text":"~9 s","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Roll up into coarser points","body":"A rollup job reads finished raw chunks and writes summaries: one point per series per minute, then one per hour. Each summary keeps count, sum, min and max, not just the average, so later math stays correct. A 1-minute rollup has 6 times fewer points than 10-second data, and an hourly one has 360 times fewer. Percentiles cannot be averaged, so they need stored histograms or sketches.","idea":"Downsampling trades detail for a huge cut in points; keep count, sum, min and max so it stays useful.","caption":"Raw data is summarized into minute and hour points.","focus":["hot","rollup","cold"],"flows":[{"from":"hot","to":"rollup","kind":"async","label":"read old chunk"},{"from":"rollup","to":"cold","kind":"async","label":"1m + 1h points"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"rollup","text":"1 min: 6x fewer"},{"node":"rollup","text":"1 hour: 360x fewer","at":"bottom"},{"node":"cold","text":"count, sum, min, max"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Serve long ranges from rollups","body":"The 90-day chart now reads hourly rollups: about 2,160 points per series instead of over 700,000 raw ones. The query layer picks the coarsest resolution that still gives enough points for the screen. The chart that took 9 seconds returns in about 30 ms, with the trade-off that you cannot zoom back into 10-second detail.","idea":"Pick the resolution to match the question: coarse for long ranges, raw for short ones.","caption":"Long queries are answered from rollups.","focus":["dash","cold"],"flows":[{"from":"dash","to":"cold","kind":"request","label":"90 days, 1h step"},{"from":"cold","to":"dash","kind":"response","label":"2,160 points"}],"metrics":{"query":"30"},"chips":[{"node":"dash","text":"2,160 points","tone":"green"},{"node":"cold","text":"hourly rollups","at":"bottom"},{"node":"dash","text":"~30 ms","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Retention: drop, do not delete","body":"Nobody needs 10-second data from a year ago, so a retention policy (a rule for how long to keep data) might keep raw data for 15 days, 1-minute rollups for 13 months, and hourly ones for years. Because each chunk covers a fixed time window, expiry means dropping a whole old chunk, which is close to deleting a file. Running DELETE on billions of rows would be slow and leave the table bloated. These durations are examples; pick yours from alerting and audit needs.","idea":"Retention works best as dropping whole time chunks, not deleting individual rows.","caption":"Expired raw chunks are removed whole.","focus":["hot","rollup"],"flows":[{"from":"rollup","to":"hot","kind":"async","label":"safe to drop"}],"metrics":{"keep":"15"},"chips":[{"node":"hot","text":"raw: 15 days"},{"node":"rollup","text":"1-min: 13 months"},{"node":"hot","text":"drop whole chunks","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A label explodes the series count","body":"A developer adds user_id as a label on a request-count metric. Every distinct label combination is its own series, so 1 million series can become 100 million. Indexes and memory grow with series count, not with points per second, so the system falls over even though traffic is unchanged. Keep labels low-cardinality (few distinct values, like region or status code) and put per-user detail in logs or traces.","idea":"Cost grows with the number of distinct series, so unbounded labels are the classic way to break a metrics store.","caption":"Series count explodes and ingest stalls.","focus":["agents","queue","writer","hot"],"flows":[{"from":"agents","to":"queue","kind":"async","label":"user_id labels"},{"from":"queue","to":"writer","kind":"async","label":"100M series"},{"from":"writer","to":"hot","kind":"fail","label":"index/memory full"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"agents","text":"+ user_id label","tone":"red"},{"node":"hot","text":"1M -> 100M series","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"hot","text":"memory grows","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Design for time","body":"Buffer and batch the appends, store them in time-partitioned compressed chunks, and answer recent queries from the newest chunks. Roll old data up so long ranges read few points, and enforce retention by dropping whole chunks. Watch series cardinality, because it is the cost that grows quietly.","idea":"Time is the partition key, the query filter, and the retention unit all at once.","caption":"Ingest, chunk, roll up, expire.","focus":["agents","queue","writer","hot","rollup","cold","dash"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"writes":"100","rawday":"17","keep":"15","query":"30"},"chips":[{"node":"hot","text":"chunks + compress"},{"node":"cold","text":"rollups","at":"bottom"},{"node":"dash","text":"~30 ms","tone":"green"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does partitioning data into daily chunks make 'last hour' queries fast?","options":["Each chunk is stored on a faster disk","The database can skip every chunk outside the time range","Daily chunks are never compressed","It removes the need for timestamps"],"answer":1,"why":"Partition pruning means only the chunk covering the queried window is read, so cost does not grow as history accumulates."},{"q":"You downsample raw data to 1-minute points storing only the average. Which question can you no longer answer correctly?","options":["What was the typical load in that minute?","What was the peak value in that minute?","How many hours of data exist?","Which host sent the data?"],"answer":1,"why":"An average hides spikes. Keeping min, max, count and sum in each rollup preserves peaks and lets you recompute averages correctly."},{"q":"You need to expire data older than 15 days from a 10 TB table. What is usually the best approach in a time-series store?","options":["Run DELETE with a timestamp filter every night","Rewrite the table without the old rows","Compress old rows more aggressively","Drop the whole chunk partitions outside the window"],"answer":3,"why":"Chunks align with time, so dropping one is a cheap metadata and file operation. Row-by-row deletes are slow and leave dead space."},{"q":"A metrics store runs out of memory after a release, though points per second is unchanged. A new metric now carries a request_id label. What is the cause?","options":["Too many readers on the dashboard","The rollup job runs too often","Series count exploded because each request_id is a new series","Timestamps are arriving out of order"],"answer":2,"why":"Memory and index size scale with distinct series. An unbounded label creates a new series per value, which is high cardinality."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Time series","def":"A sequence of values for one named thing, each with a timestamp, such as CPU% of host A every 10 seconds."},{"term":"Series cardinality","def":"The number of distinct series (metric name plus label values). Memory and index cost scale with it."},{"term":"Time-partitioned chunk","def":"A storage unit covering a fixed time window, so queries can skip chunks and retention can drop them whole."},{"term":"Downsampling / rollup","def":"Replacing many fine points with fewer coarse ones (for example 10s to 1m). Store count, sum, min and max."},{"term":"Retention policy","def":"Rules for how long each resolution is kept, for example raw 15 days, 1-minute 13 months."},{"term":"Delta-of-delta encoding","def":"Store the change in the gap between timestamps; steady intervals become near-zero values that compress to a bit or two."},{"term":"Out-of-order write","def":"A point arriving after newer ones for the same series. Stores usually accept it only within a recent window."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"oltp-olap","title":"OLTP vs OLAP & Warehouses","subtitle":"Watch one revenue report choke a checkout database, then see how a warehouse and a pipeline fix it.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How OLTP workloads (many tiny reads and writes) differ from OLAP workloads (few huge scans)","Why row storage suits transactions and column storage suits analytics","How ETL and ELT move data from the live database into a warehouse or lake","Why running analytics on the production database hurts customers","When to pick a warehouse, a data lake, or both"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"Shoppers","kind":"user","x":8,"y":22},{"id":"oltp","label":"OLTP DB (row store)","kind":"db","x":30,"y":22},{"id":"etl","label":"ETL / ELT pipeline","kind":"worker","x":52,"y":50},{"id":"lake","label":"Data lake (files)","kind":"storage","x":52,"y":84},{"id":"wh","label":"Warehouse (columns)","kind":"db","x":74,"y":50},{"id":"analyst","label":"Analyst dashboard","kind":"client","x":88,"y":22}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"oltp"},{"from":"oltp","to":"etl"},{"from":"etl","to":"wh"},{"from":"etl","to":"lake"},{"from":"lake","to":"wh"},{"from":"wh","to":"analyst"},{"from":"analyst","to":"oltp"}],"metrics":[{"id":"p99","label":"Checkout p99","unit":"ms"},{"id":"scanned","label":"Data scanned","unit":"GB"},{"id":"query","label":"Report time","unit":"s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Why does checkout slow down at 9am?","body":"A shop keeps 500 million orders in one Postgres-style database. Checkout is fast: p99 latency (the time that 99 percent of requests beat) is about 8 ms. Every morning an analyst opens a dashboard titled revenue by day, and suddenly shoppers see slow or timing-out checkouts. Same database, same data. What changed? The kind of question being asked, not the amount of traffic.","idea":"Two workloads can be harmless alone and destructive together on the same database.","caption":"Healthy checkout, one analyst about to ask a big question.","focus":["users","oltp","analyst"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"p99":"8","scanned":"0","query":"0"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"OLTP","title":"Transactions: many tiny operations","body":"OLTP (online transaction processing) means thousands of small operations per second, each touching one or a few rows: insert this order, read this user's cart, update this stock count. Each must be fast and correct. Databases built for this store each row's fields together on disk, so one row is one cheap read or write, usually found through an index. In the animation a shopper inserts an order and gets 'committed' back in about 8 ms.","idea":"OLTP is point reads and writes by key, optimized for latency and correctness.","caption":"Shoppers write and read single rows in milliseconds.","focus":["users","oltp"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"oltp","kind":"request","label":"INSERT order"},{"from":"oltp","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"committed"}],"metrics":{"p99":"8"},"chips":[{"node":"oltp","text":"1 row = 1 read"},{"node":"oltp","text":"p99 8 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"users","text":"1000s of ops/s"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Analytics hits the live database","body":"Now the analyst runs SELECT day, SUM(amount) FROM orders GROUP BY day. It must read every one of the 500 million rows. At roughly 200 bytes per row that is about 100 GB. Even at an optimistic 2 GB/s that is around 50 seconds of heavy disk and CPU use. It can also push the pages checkout keeps in memory out of the buffer cache (the RAM that holds recently used disk pages). Checkout queries now wait behind it, and p99 jumps from 8 ms to 900 ms.","idea":"A full-table scan competes for the same disk, cache and CPU that transactions need.","caption":"The big scan starves checkout of cache and I/O.","focus":["analyst","oltp","users"],"flows":[{"from":"analyst","to":"oltp","kind":"request","label":"SUM ... GROUP BY"},{"from":"users","to":"oltp","kind":"fail","label":"checkout slow"}],"metrics":{"p99":"900","scanned":"100","query":"50"},"chips":[{"node":"analyst","text":"reads 500M rows"},{"node":"oltp","text":"~100 GB scan","tone":"red"},{"node":"oltp","text":"p99 900 ms","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"oltp","text":"~50 s","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Row store vs column store","body":"A row store keeps all fields of one order side by side, which is perfect for fetching a whole order. A column store keeps all values of one column together, so the revenue query reads only the date and amount columns: about 16 bytes per row instead of 200, or 8 GB instead of 100 GB. Similar values sit together too, so they compress very well, often 3 to 10 times. That shrinks 8 GB to roughly 1 to 3 GB (2 GB here). This is an illustration of what the same report would cost on a column layout.","idea":"Columns let analytics read only what it needs, and compress better; rows make single-record access cheap.","caption":"The same data laid out by column: far fewer bytes to scan.","focus":["oltp","wh"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"p99":"8","scanned":"2","query":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"wh","text":"16 vs 200 B/row"},{"node":"wh","text":"3-10x compression","at":"bottom"},{"node":"wh","text":"1-3 GB","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"ETL","title":"Copy data out: ETL","body":"We cannot just move everything to a column store, because checkout still needs fast row writes. So we keep both and copy. In classic ETL (extract, transform, load) a pipeline pulls changes from the OLTP database, cleans and reshapes them on its own workers, then loads finished tables into the warehouse. Reading changes from the database log (change data capture) puts far less load on the database than rescanning tables.","idea":"The OLTP database stays the source of truth; analytics works on a copy.","caption":"The pipeline extracts changes and loads the warehouse.","focus":["oltp","etl","wh"],"flows":[{"from":"oltp","to":"etl","kind":"async","label":"change feed"},{"from":"etl","to":"wh","kind":"async","label":"cleaned tables"}],"metrics":{"p99":"8"},"chips":[{"node":"etl","text":"change data capture"},{"node":"wh","text":"finished tables","at":"bottom"},{"node":"oltp","text":"light load","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"ELT + LAKE","title":"Dump first, transform later: ELT","body":"Modern warehouses scale compute elastically, so many teams flip the order: ELT (extract, load, transform). Raw data lands first, either straight in the warehouse or in a data lake, which is cheap object storage holding files such as Parquet or JSON in any shape. Transformations then run as SQL inside the warehouse. The lake also feeds machine learning and data nobody has modeled yet. Table formats like Iceberg or Delta on top of lake files let one copy be queried like a warehouse (a lakehouse).","idea":"A lake stores raw data cheaply and flexibly; a warehouse stores modeled data for fast SQL.","caption":"Raw files land in the lake, then the warehouse transforms them.","focus":["etl","lake","wh"],"flows":[{"from":"etl","to":"lake","kind":"async","label":"raw files"},{"from":"lake","to":"wh","kind":"async","label":"load + SQL"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"lake","text":"Parquet / JSON"},{"node":"lake","text":"cheap object storage","at":"bottom"},{"node":"wh","text":"SQL transforms"}]},{"tag":"OLAP","title":"The same report, off the hot path","body":"OLAP (online analytical processing) means few queries that each scan huge amounts of data and aggregate it. The analyst now runs the revenue report on the warehouse. It reads two compressed columns (2 GB), spread across many nodes in parallel, and answers in about a second. Checkout never notices, and p99 stays at 8 ms, because the OLTP database is not queried for the report (only the light change feed still reads from it).","idea":"OLAP is built for big scans and aggregates; isolating it protects transactions.","caption":"The warehouse answers the report; checkout is untouched.","focus":["analyst","wh"],"flows":[{"from":"analyst","to":"wh","kind":"request","label":"SUM ... GROUP BY"},{"from":"wh","to":"analyst","kind":"response","label":"daily totals"}],"metrics":{"p99":"8","scanned":"2","query":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"wh","text":"~1 s","tone":"green"},{"node":"wh","text":"2 columns read","at":"bottom"},{"node":"oltp","text":"p99 8 ms","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"The price: freshness and complexity","body":"The copy is never instant. With change capture the warehouse may lag by seconds to minutes; with a nightly batch job, up to a day. You also now run a pipeline that can break, duplicate rows, or drift from the source. Warehouses are poor at single-row updates, so do not point your app at one. Pick the lag the business actually needs. A read replica shields checkout but is still a row store, so the report stays slow (see the Replication lesson).","idea":"You trade data freshness and operational effort for isolation and fast analytics.","caption":"The warehouse is a slightly stale copy fed by a pipeline.","focus":["oltp","etl","wh"],"flows":[{"from":"oltp","to":"etl","kind":"async","label":"lag: ~1-5 min"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"wh","text":"lag: secs to a day"},{"node":"etl","text":"can duplicate rows","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Two workloads, two systems","body":"OLTP serves many small, correct, low-latency row operations, so it uses row storage and indexes. OLAP serves few huge scans and aggregates, so it uses column storage and parallel scans. Do not run the second on the first. Move data with ETL or ELT, keep raw history in a lake if you want flexibility, and serve reports from a warehouse. Start with a warehouse; add a lake when you have raw, unstructured or ML data.","idea":"Match the storage layout and system to the access pattern, and keep analytics off the transaction path.","caption":"Transactions on the left, analytics on the right.","focus":["users","oltp","etl","lake","wh","analyst"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"p99":"8","scanned":"2","query":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"oltp","text":"row store"},{"node":"wh","text":"column store"},{"node":"lake","text":"raw history","at":"bottom"},{"node":"etl","text":"ETL / ELT"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A report needs SUM(amount) over 500 million rows of a 40-column table. Why is a column store usually much faster than a row store here?","options":["It stores fewer rows overall","It reads only the amount column, and that column compresses well","It skips the need for aggregation","It keeps all data in memory by default"],"answer":1,"why":"A column store reads just the columns the query needs, and similar values in one column compress strongly, so far fewer bytes come off disk."},{"q":"Why can one heavy analytics query make checkout slow even though it only reads data?","options":["Reads always lock every table for writing","Read queries are replicated to shoppers","It increases the number of rows in the table","It competes for disk, CPU and buffer cache with transactions"],"answer":3,"why":"The scan saturates I/O and CPU and can push hot pages out of cache, so small transactional queries wait. Reads do not need to block writes for this to hurt."},{"q":"A warehouse is fed by a nightly batch job. What is the main cost?","options":["Checkout gets slower while the job runs on the OLTP database","Reports can be up to a day stale and the pipeline is another system to run","The warehouse can no longer use column storage","Analysts must query the OLTP database directly"],"answer":1,"why":"A copy is never instant: a nightly batch means reports lag by up to a day, and the pipeline itself can break or drift. That freshness and operational effort is the price of isolation."},{"q":"A team lands raw JSON and CSV exports in cheap object storage first and models them into tables later, only when someone needs them. Which approach is this?","options":["A data lake, often feeding ELT","ETL into an OLTP database","Row-based sharding","A write-through cache"],"answer":0,"why":"Storing raw files first and transforming afterwards is the data lake plus ELT pattern; classic ETL transforms before loading."},{"q":"Your app needs to update one customer's address and show it back instantly. Where should that live?","options":["Only in the warehouse, since it holds all data","In the data lake as a new file each time","In the OLTP database, with the warehouse updated later by the pipeline","In the dashboard tool"],"answer":2,"why":"Warehouses are tuned for big scans, not frequent single-row updates. The OLTP database is the source of truth and the warehouse catches up afterwards."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"OLTP","def":"Online transaction processing: many small, fast reads and writes of individual rows, such as orders and carts. Needs low latency and strong correctness."},{"term":"OLAP","def":"Online analytical processing: few queries that scan and aggregate huge volumes of history, such as revenue by region. Needs throughput, not single-row speed."},{"term":"Row vs column store","def":"Row stores keep a record's fields together (good for point access). Column stores keep each column together (good for scans, compression)."},{"term":"ETL vs ELT","def":"ETL transforms data before loading it into the target. ELT loads raw data first and transforms it inside the warehouse using SQL."},{"term":"Data warehouse","def":"A column-oriented analytical database holding cleaned, modeled data, queried with SQL by analysts and dashboards."},{"term":"Data lake","def":"Cheap object storage of raw data in open file formats (for example Parquet). Flexible, but needs structure added before it is easy to query."},{"term":"Change data capture","def":"Reading a database's change log to stream inserts, updates and deletes downstream, with less load than repeated full table queries."},{"term":"Rule of thumb","def":"Never point heavy analytics at the production OLTP database. Use a copy, with a freshness lag the business can accept."}],"isNew":true}]},{"id":"distributed","title":"Distributed Systems","lessons":[{"slug":"cap","title":"CAP & PACELC","subtitle":"Cut the link between two replicas and watch the system be forced to choose between answering and being right.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["What CAP really says: during a network partition you give up consistency or availability","Why partitions are a fact of life, not an option you can decline","How PACELC adds the everyday trade-off: latency versus consistency when nothing is broken","The difference between strong, causal, and eventual consistency, with examples","How to pick a model per feature instead of per whole system"],"nodes":[{"id":"alice","label":"Alice (US user)","kind":"user","x":10,"y":22},{"id":"a","label":"Replica A (US)","kind":"db","x":34,"y":55},{"id":"b","label":"Replica B (EU)","kind":"db","x":66,"y":55},{"id":"bob","label":"Bob (EU user)","kind":"user","x":90,"y":22}],"edges":[{"from":"alice","to":"a"},{"from":"a","to":"b","label":"replication link"},{"from":"b","to":"bob"}],"metrics":[{"id":"wlat","label":"Write latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"rlat","label":"Read latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"wok","label":"Writes accepted","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Two copies of one value","body":"Imagine a setting x stored on two servers, called replicas: A in the US and B in Europe, so each user reads from a nearby copy. Both hold x = 4. Alice (US) sets x = 5 on A. A moment later, Bob (Europe) reads x from B. What should Bob see? And what if the link between A and B is cut right now?","idea":"Copying data to two places raises a question: which copy is allowed to be behind?","caption":"Alice's write reaches A; B still holds x = 4.","focus":["alice","a","b","bob"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"write x = 5"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"x = 5","tone":"green"},{"node":"b","text":"x = 4"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"5","rlat":"5","wok":"100"}},{"tag":"BASELINE","title":"Healthy network: easy","body":"The link is healthy, so A copies x = 5 to B and waits for B's \"got it\" (an acknowledgement, or ack) before telling Alice it worked. Now a read from either replica returns 5. This is strong consistency: the system acts as if there were only one copy. The cost is time. Alice waits for a round trip across the Atlantic, about 80 ms, on top of 5 ms for the local write: 85 ms in total.","idea":"With a healthy network you can have both consistency and availability. You just pay for it in latency.","caption":"A copies to B and waits for the ack before replying to Alice.","focus":["alice","a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"write x = 5"},{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"x = 5"},{"from":"b","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"a","to":"alice","kind":"response","label":"ok"}],"chips":[{"node":"b","text":"x = 5","tone":"green"},{"node":"alice","text":"write 85 ms"},{"node":"a","text":"round trip ≈ 80 ms","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"85","rlat":"5","wok":"100"}},{"tag":"PARTITION","title":"The link breaks","body":"Now a cut cable or a bad router setting breaks the path between the regions. Both replicas are still running and both users can reach their nearest one, but A and B cannot talk to each other. This is a network partition. Neither side can tell whether the other has crashed or is just unreachable. Any system spread across a network will eventually see one, so you cannot design partitions away.","idea":"You do not get to choose whether partitions happen. CAP is about what you do once one does.","caption":"A and B are both up, but the link between them is down.","focus":["a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"fail","label":"link down"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"link cut","tone":"red","at":"edge:a-b"},{"node":"a","text":"healthy","tone":"green"},{"node":"b","text":"healthy","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CHOOSE C","title":"Option 1: stay correct, refuse","body":"Option 1 puts correctness first. Such a system is called CP (consistent, partition-tolerant). A cannot be sure B will ever see the write, so it rejects Alice's request with an error (a 503, as the animation shows). B likewise refuses reads it cannot prove are current. Nobody sees stale or conflicting data, but users get errors while the partition lasts. Real CP systems with three or more replicas keep serving on the side that still has a majority. Only the minority side stops.","idea":"Choosing consistency means some requests fail for as long as the partition lasts.","caption":"A returns an error instead of accepting a write that B cannot see.","focus":["alice","a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"write x = 5"},{"from":"a","to":"alice","kind":"fail","label":"503 unavailable"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"503 unavailable","tone":"red"},{"node":"b","text":"reads refused","tone":"red"},{"node":"alice","text":"0% writes ok","tone":"red"}],"metrics":{"wok":"0"}},{"tag":"CHOOSE A","title":"Option 2: stay up, diverge","body":"Option 2 keeps the service up. Such a system is called AP (available, partition-tolerant). A accepts x = 5 and replies at once, because it does not wait for B. But Bob's read from B returns the old value 4. That answer is stale (out of date) but still valid, because B did respond. If Bob had also written x on B, the two copies would now disagree and must be reconciled later.","idea":"Choosing availability means the replicas can disagree while the partition lasts.","caption":"A accepts the write; B answers with the old value x = 4.","focus":["alice","a","b","bob"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"write x = 5"},{"from":"a","to":"alice","kind":"response","label":"ok"},{"from":"bob","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"read x"},{"from":"b","to":"bob","kind":"response","label":"x = 4 (stale)"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"x = 5","tone":"green"},{"node":"a","text":"ok · 5 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"b","text":"x = 4 · stale","tone":"red"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"5","rlat":"5","wok":"100"}},{"tag":"HEALING","title":"The link comes back","body":"When the link comes back, an AP system has to catch up. A sends the missed update to B, and B applies it, so both hold 5 again. This is eventual consistency: if writes stop, all replicas end up equal. It promises the copies will agree, but not when. The hard case is two conflicting writes made on both sides. Deciding which one wins, or merging them, is covered in the clocks and CRDT lessons.","idea":"Eventual consistency promises the copies will converge, but not when, and not which conflicting write survives.","caption":"A pushes the missed write to B, and the copies match again.","focus":["a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"x = 5"},{"from":"b","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"ack"}],"chips":[{"node":"b","text":"x = 5","tone":"green"},{"node":"a","text":"x = 5"},{"node":"a","text":"converged","tone":"green","at":"edge:a-b"}],"metrics":{"wok":"100"}},{"tag":"PACELC","title":"Even with no partition","body":"CAP only describes failures, but partitions are rare while the trade-off is constant. PACELC (say \"pass-elk\") extends it: if there is a Partition, choose Availability or Consistency; Else, choose Latency or Consistency. That gives combinations such as PA/EL (favour uptime and speed) and PC/EC (favour correctness), plus the mixed PA/EC and PC/EL. The \"Else\" half matters most day to day, because every request pays it. Here, Alice waits for B's ack on every write.","idea":"PACELC: the everyday price of consistency is latency, paid even when nothing is broken.","caption":"Synchronous replication: Alice waits for B's ack.","focus":["alice","a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"write x = 5"},{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"x = 5"},{"from":"b","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"a","to":"alice","kind":"response","label":"ok"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"round trip ≈ 80 ms","at":"edge:a-b"},{"node":"alice","text":"write 85 ms"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"85","rlat":"5","wok":"100"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Else: pick latency","body":"The alternative is asynchronous replication. A confirms to Alice after its own 5 ms write, then sends x = 5 to B in the background. Writes become 17 times faster (5 ms instead of 85 ms). The price is that for roughly the next 40 ms (one way across the Atlantic), or longer under load, Bob can still read 4. Dynamo-style stores such as Cassandra and Riak lean PA/EL by default, though quorum settings can shift that (see the Quorums lesson). Google Spanner is usually classed as PC/EC.","idea":"Async replication buys speed by letting replicas lag briefly. Sync replication buys correctness with delay.","caption":"A replies at once and copies to B in the background.","focus":["alice","a","b","bob"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"write x = 5"},{"from":"a","to":"alice","kind":"response","label":"ok (5 ms)"},{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"async","label":"x = 5, later"},{"from":"bob","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"read x"},{"from":"b","to":"bob","kind":"response","label":"x = 4 (stale)"}],"chips":[{"node":"alice","text":"write 5 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"a","text":"17x faster","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"b","text":"stale ≈ 40 ms","tone":"red"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"5","rlat":"5","wok":"100"}},{"tag":"MODELS","title":"A ladder of consistency","body":"Consistency comes in levels. Strong (linearizable): every read sees the latest completed write, as if there were one copy. Causal: if one event could have influenced another, everyone sees them in that order. Example: Alice posts a question and Bob replies, so nobody may see the reply before the question. Eventual: copies only converge over time, with no ordering promise. Weaker levels are cheaper and faster. Causal can even stay available during a partition, since it needs ordering information, not agreement on every write.","idea":"Pick the weakest level your feature can tolerate. Causal adds ordering bookkeeping; strong adds cross-replica coordination.","caption":"Causal order: B must hold the post before showing the reply.","focus":["alice","a","b","bob"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"post"},{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"post"},{"from":"bob","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"reply to post"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"post first"},{"node":"bob","text":"reply after post"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"How to think about it","body":"CAP: when a partition splits the replicas, choose consistency (reject some requests) or availability (accept requests and let copies diverge). It is not \"pick two of three\", because partitions will happen. PACELC: even with no partition, choose latency or consistency. Decide per feature. A bank balance and a unique username need strong consistency. A like counter or a feed can be eventual.","idea":"Ask two questions: what happens during a partition, and what latency do I pay when things are healthy?","caption":"All four parts: clients, two replicas, and the link between.","focus":["alice","a","b","bob"],"flows":[],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"sync: 85 ms write"},{"node":"a","text":"async: 5 ms write","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"5","rlat":"5","wok":"100"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A three-replica database splits into a group of 2 and a lone replica. The lone replica rejects all writes. In CAP terms, what did the system choose?","options":["Availability on every node","Consistency, giving up availability on the minority side","It gave up partition tolerance","Neither; this is not a CAP situation"],"answer":1,"why":"Refusing writes that cannot be safely replicated preserves a single history (consistency) at the cost of the isolated node being unavailable. This is the CP choice."},{"q":"A teammate says: 'We chose CA, so we never have to worry about partitions.' What is the flaw?","options":["CA systems cannot have more than one replica","Availability is impossible without partition tolerance","CAP only applies to NoSQL databases","Network partitions happen regardless; you only decide how the system behaves when one occurs"],"answer":3,"why":"On any real network, partitions happen whether you plan for them or not. A 'CA' design just has undefined or accidental behavior when one occurs."},{"q":"Cross-region round trip is 80 ms. A service with no failures at all still sees 80 ms added to every write. Which idea explains this?","options":["PACELC's 'else' branch: consistency costs latency even when healthy","The C in CAP, applied during partitions","Eventual consistency","Causal ordering"],"answer":0,"why":"Waiting for a distant replica to acknowledge is the latency price of strong consistency, paid on every write even without a partition."},{"q":"In a social app, Bob sees a reply to Alice's comment but not the comment itself. Which guarantee would prevent this?","options":["Eventual consistency","Read-your-writes only for the commenter","Causal consistency","A longer replication timeout"],"answer":2,"why":"Causal consistency ensures that if one event could have caused another, every reader sees them in that order. Plain eventual consistency allows the reply to arrive first."},{"q":"Which feature most clearly needs strong consistency rather than eventual?","options":["View count on a video","Claiming a unique username at signup","Number of followers shown on a profile","Recently viewed items list"],"answer":1,"why":"Two replicas accepting the same username concurrently would break uniqueness. Counters and recency lists tolerate being briefly stale."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"CAP","def":"During a network partition, a replicated system can keep consistency or availability, not both. Partitions are not optional."},{"term":"Consistency (CAP sense)","def":"Linearizability: every read returns the latest completed write, as if there were a single copy. Not the same as ACID consistency."},{"term":"Availability (CAP sense)","def":"Every non-failing node answers every request with a non-error response, even if the data is stale."},{"term":"PACELC","def":"If Partition: Availability or Consistency; Else: Latency or Consistency. Captures the everyday cost of strong guarantees."},{"term":"Strong consistency","def":"Reads see the latest write. Needs coordination between replicas, so it costs round trips and can reject requests in a partition."},{"term":"Causal consistency","def":"Related events (a question and its reply) are seen in order by everyone; unrelated events may appear in any order."},{"term":"Eventual consistency","def":"If writes stop, replicas converge. No promise about when, or about reads in the meantime. Fast and highly available."},{"term":"Rule of thumb","def":"Choose consistency per feature: money, uniqueness, and inventory lean strong; counters, feeds, and caches lean eventual."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"quorums","title":"Quorums (N, R, W)","subtitle":"Watch a leaderless database write to three copies, survive a dead node, and heal itself afterwards.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["What N, R and W mean and why R + W > N makes reads overlap with the latest write","Why a leaderless system can keep accepting writes while a replica is down","How a sloppy quorum and hinted handoff trade guarantees for availability","How read repair and anti-entropy bring stale replicas back in sync","What quorums still do not give you, such as ordering of concurrent writes"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Client","kind":"client","x":9,"y":50,"url":"https://shop.example"},{"id":"coord","label":"Coordinator","kind":"service","x":34,"y":50},{"id":"ra","label":"Replica A","kind":"db","x":64,"y":14},{"id":"rb","label":"Replica B","kind":"db","x":64,"y":50},{"id":"rc","label":"Replica C","kind":"db","x":64,"y":86},{"id":"stand","label":"Node D (stand-in)","kind":"db","x":89,"y":68}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"coord"},{"from":"coord","to":"ra"},{"from":"coord","to":"rb"},{"from":"coord","to":"rc"},{"from":"coord","to":"stand"},{"from":"stand","to":"rc"},{"from":"ra","to":"rb"}],"metrics":[{"id":"n","label":"Copies (N)","unit":"copies"},{"id":"need","label":"Quorum size","unit":"nodes"},{"id":"fresh","label":"Fresh replicas","unit":"replicas"},{"id":"ver","label":"Version seen","unit":"ver"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"No leader, so who is right?","body":"In a leaderless database (the original Amazon Dynamo design, Cassandra, Riak), any node can accept a write. Each key is stored on N replicas, meaning N copies on different machines. Here N = 3: replicas A, B and C. Replicas fail and come back at different times, so their copies drift apart. If a client reads while they disagree, how many replicas must it ask to be sure it sees the latest write?","idea":"With no single leader, correctness comes from how many replicas you wait for, not from asking one special node.","caption":"One key lives on three replicas. Any of them may be behind.","focus":["ra","rb","rc"],"flows":[],"chips":[{"node":"rb","text":"N = 3 replicas"}],"metrics":{"n":"3","fresh":"3","ver":"1"}},{"tag":"WRITE","title":"Write with W = 2","body":"The client sends cart:42 = v2 (version 2 of a shopping cart) to a coordinator, the node that runs the request. Any node can play this role; it is drawn separately here. The coordinator forwards the write to all three replicas but waits for only W = 2 acknowledgements (acks, meaning \"got it\" replies). A and B answer in about 2 ms. C is down and never answers. Two acks meet W, so the write succeeds and C is simply behind for now.","idea":"A write succeeds once W replicas have it. The other N - W copies catch up later.","caption":"A and B ack; C is down. Two acks meet W = 2.","focus":["coord","ra","rb","rc"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"coord","kind":"request","label":"PUT cart v2"},{"from":"coord","to":"ra","kind":"request","label":"v2"},{"from":"coord","to":"rb","kind":"request","label":"v2"},{"from":"coord","to":"rc","kind":"fail","label":"no reply"},{"from":"ra","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"rb","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"coord","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"OK"}],"chips":[{"node":"coord","text":"2/3 acks","tone":"green"},{"node":"ra","text":"v2 · 2 ms"},{"node":"rb","text":"v2 · 2 ms"},{"node":"rc","text":"down","tone":"red"}],"metrics":{"need":"2","fresh":"2","ver":"2"}},{"tag":"READ","title":"Read with R = 2","body":"C has restarted but still holds the old v1. Now the client reads. The coordinator asks R = 2 replicas for their copy, say B and C. B returns v2 and C returns v1, so the coordinator picks the newest, v2, and replies. (It compares version numbers or timestamps; the clocks lesson covers why that is harder than it sounds.) Why is the answer always fresh? Any 2 replicas must include at least one that took the write, because 2 + 2 = 4 is greater than N = 3. Read repair, which would also fix C, comes later.","idea":"If R + W > N, every read set shares at least one replica with every write set, so a read sees the latest write that finished before it began. This is not full linearizability (see the CAP and consensus lessons).","caption":"Read set {B, C} overlaps write set {A, B} at B.","focus":["coord","rb","rc"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"coord","kind":"request","label":"GET cart"},{"from":"coord","to":"rb","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"coord","to":"rc","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"rb","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v2"},{"from":"rc","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v1 (stale)"},{"from":"coord","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"v2"}],"chips":[{"node":"rb","text":"v2","tone":"green"},{"node":"rc","text":"v1 · stale","tone":"red"},{"node":"coord","text":"R=2: picks v2","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{"need":"2","fresh":"2","ver":"2"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Turning the knobs","body":"In Cassandra and Riak you can choose R and W per request. W = 1 with R = 1 is the fastest, but 1 + 1 is not greater than 3, so a read can hit a stale replica. The animation shows this: after the W = 2 write, R = 1 happens to ask C and returns v1, because 1 + 2 is not greater than 3. W = 3 with R = 1 gives fresh reads, but one dead replica then blocks every write. The common middle for N = 3 is W = 2, R = 2, which survives one failure on both paths. Bigger quorums also wait on slower replicas, which raises tail latency (the speed of the slowest few percent of requests).","idea":"Pick R and W to trade freshness, write availability and latency. R + W > N buys overlap, at the cost of waiting for more nodes.","caption":"R = 1 asked only the stale replica C and got v1.","focus":["coord","rc"],"flows":[{"from":"coord","to":"rc","kind":"request","label":"read (R=1)"},{"from":"rc","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v1 (stale)"},{"from":"coord","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"v1"}],"chips":[{"node":"rc","text":"returns v1","tone":"red"},{"node":"coord","text":"R + W = 3, not > 3","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"need":"1","fresh":"2","ver":"1"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Too many replicas down","body":"Now a network problem cuts the coordinator off from A and C. Only B is reachable, and W is still 2. A strict quorum would reject the write, since only 1 of the 3 designated replicas can ack. A sloppy quorum is more flexible. The coordinator writes to a healthy node outside the set, D, tagged with a hint: \"this data belongs to C\". B plus D give two acks, so the write is accepted. Dynamo and Riak count stand-ins toward the quorum. Cassandra stores hints but does not count them toward the consistency level (except level ANY).","idea":"A sloppy quorum keeps writes available during failures by counting stand-in nodes, at the cost of the overlap guarantee.","caption":"D holds C's copy as a hint so the write can succeed.","focus":["coord","rb","stand","ra","rc"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"coord","kind":"request","label":"PUT cart v3"},{"from":"coord","to":"ra","kind":"fail","label":"unreachable"},{"from":"coord","to":"rc","kind":"fail","label":"unreachable"},{"from":"coord","to":"rb","kind":"request","label":"v3"},{"from":"coord","to":"stand","kind":"request","label":"v3 + hint for C"},{"from":"rb","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"stand","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"coord","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"OK"}],"chips":[{"node":"ra","text":"unreachable","tone":"red"},{"node":"rc","text":"unreachable","tone":"red"},{"node":"stand","text":"v3 + hint for C"},{"node":"coord","text":"2 acks: B + D","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"need":"2","fresh":"1","ver":"3"}},{"tag":"CAVEAT","title":"The catch with sloppy quorums","body":"Here is the catch. D is not one of the three replicas that reads consult. While the partition lasts, another client reaches a second coordinator on A and C's side (drawn as the same Coordinator box to keep the diagram small). It reads A and C, and both missed v3: A returns v2 and C returns v1. It answers v2, even though v3 was acknowledged and R + W is still greater than N. Only 1 of the 3 designated replicas (B) holds v3. The overlap promise only works when the quorums are drawn from the designated replicas. Sloppy quorums give eventual consistency and high write availability, not a read-your-writes guarantee.","idea":"With stand-ins, R + W > N no longer guarantees a fresh read until the hint is delivered.","caption":"Reading A and C returns old data; v3 sits on B and D.","focus":["coord","ra","rc","stand"],"flows":[{"from":"coord","to":"ra","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"coord","to":"rc","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"ra","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v2"},{"from":"rc","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v1"}],"chips":[{"node":"ra","text":"v2","tone":"red"},{"node":"rc","text":"v1","tone":"red"},{"node":"coord","text":"returns v2 (stale)","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"stand","text":"v3 · not asked"}],"metrics":{"need":"2","fresh":"1","ver":"2"}},{"tag":"HANDOFF","title":"Hinted handoff","body":"The partition heals and C is reachable again. D notices it holds a hint for C, forwards v3 to it, then deletes its temporary copy. This is hinted handoff: a stand-in holds a write for someone who is away and hands it over on return. Systems usually cap how long they keep hints, often a few hours, so a node that stays down longer must be repaired another way. Handoff is a fast path, not a guarantee.","idea":"Hinted handoff replays missed writes to a recovered replica, but hints are best-effort and time-limited.","caption":"D hands v3 to C once C is back.","focus":["stand","rc"],"flows":[{"from":"stand","to":"rc","kind":"replicate","label":"hint: v3"},{"from":"rc","to":"stand","kind":"response","label":"ack, drop hint"}],"chips":[{"node":"rc","text":"v3","tone":"green"},{"node":"stand","text":"hint dropped"}],"metrics":{"n":"3","fresh":"2","ver":"3"}},{"tag":"REPAIR","title":"Read repair","body":"A is still on v2, because it was cut off during the v3 write. In this example no hint was stored for A (D was the only stand-in and it holds C's hint; hints for A could also have been lost or expired). A client now reads from A and B. B returns v3 and A returns v2. The coordinator answers the client with v3 and, at the same moment, writes v3 back to A. The read itself found the gap and fixed it, at the cost of one extra write. This is read repair.","idea":"Read repair heals stale replicas as a side effect of reads, so popular keys converge quickly.","caption":"The coordinator sees that A is behind and pushes v3 to it.","focus":["coord","ra","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"coord","to":"ra","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"coord","to":"rb","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"ra","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v2"},{"from":"rb","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v3"},{"from":"coord","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"v3"},{"from":"coord","to":"ra","kind":"replicate","label":"repair: v3"}],"chips":[{"node":"rb","text":"v3"},{"node":"ra","text":"v2 → v3 (repair)","tone":"green"},{"node":"coord","text":"answers v3","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"fresh":"3","ver":"3"}},{"tag":"ANTI-ENTROPY","title":"Background anti-entropy","body":"Read repair only fixes keys that get read, and hints can expire or be lost. So replicas also compare their data in the background (in some systems this is a scheduled repair job). Each builds a Merkle tree, a tree of hashes over key ranges, and sends its root hash to a peer. If the roots match, nothing is copied. If not, they walk down to the ranges that differ and exchange only those keys. Example: a rarely read key in range 7 was missed by both hints and reads, so A is behind on range 7 and B sends A those keys. Cassandra's nodetool repair builds Merkle trees on demand; Dynamo and Riak run anti-entropy automatically.","idea":"Anti-entropy uses hash trees to find and fix differences cheaply, covering keys that read repair and hints miss.","caption":"A and B compare hash roots, then sync only the differing range.","focus":["ra","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"handshake","label":"Merkle root"},{"from":"rb","to":"ra","kind":"handshake","label":"mismatch: range 7"},{"from":"rb","to":"ra","kind":"replicate","label":"keys in range 7"}],"chips":[{"node":"ra","text":"Merkle root sent"},{"node":"rb","text":"mismatch: range 7","tone":"red"},{"node":"ra","text":"gets range 7 keys","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"fresh":"3","ver":"3"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"What quorums give you","body":"Store N copies. Wait for W acks on writes and R replies on reads. If R + W > N and you use the real replica set, every read overlaps the latest write. Sloppy quorums and hinted handoff favour availability. Read repair and anti-entropy pull stale copies back in line. Quorums do not order concurrent writes to the same key: that needs version tracking, covered in the clocks lesson.","idea":"Quorums tune freshness versus availability. Healing mechanisms bring the copies together eventually.","caption":"Write to W, read from R, repair in the background.","focus":["client","coord","ra","rb","rc","stand"],"flows":[],"chips":[{"node":"coord","text":"2 + 2 > 3","tone":"green"},{"node":"ra","text":"v3"},{"node":"rb","text":"v3"},{"node":"rc","text":"v3"}],"metrics":{"n":"3","need":"2","fresh":"3","ver":"3"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which setting does NOT guarantee that a read overlaps with the most recent acknowledged write (assume strict quorums and no failures during the read)?","options":["N = 3, W = 2, R = 2","N = 5, W = 3, R = 3","N = 5, W = 2, R = 3","N = 3, W = 3, R = 1"],"answer":2,"why":"Overlap needs R + W > N. For N = 5, W = 2 and R = 3 give 5, which is not greater than 5, so a read set of 3 can miss both replicas that took the write."},{"q":"A sloppy quorum accepts a write using a stand-in node outside the key's usual N replicas. Why can a later read with R + W > N still return old data?","options":["Stand-in nodes always reject reads","The read set may contain only designated replicas that never received the write, since the stand-in is not among them","R + W > N only applies to leader-based systems","Hinted handoff deletes the data after a write"],"answer":1,"why":"The overlap argument depends on both sets being drawn from the same N replicas. The acknowledged copy lives on a node reads do not consult until the hint is handed off."},{"q":"A rarely accessed key is stale on one replica, the hint meant for it expired, and nobody reads the key. What eventually repairs it?","options":["Read repair, because every key is read eventually","Raising R above W","Hinted handoff, which never expires","Anti-entropy, which compares replicas in the background using hash trees"],"answer":3,"why":"Read repair needs a read and the hint is gone, so a periodic background comparison such as Merkle-tree anti-entropy is what finds and copies the missing data."},{"q":"You set N = 3 and W = 3 to make writes as durable as possible. What is the main cost?","options":["If any one replica is down or slow, writes fail or stall","Reads can no longer see the latest write","The coordinator must be a leader","Data is stored only twice"],"answer":0,"why":"W = N means every replica must acknowledge, so a single failure blocks writes and the slowest replica sets write latency. Durability goes up but availability goes down."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"N, R, W","def":"N = replicas per key, W = acks required before a write succeeds, R = replies required before a read returns. In systems like Cassandra and Riak, R and W can be chosen per request."},{"term":"R + W > N","def":"Read and write sets share a replica, so a read sees the latest completed write. A write that fails to reach W acks is not rolled back, so a later read may still see it. Not linearizability. Common for N = 3: R = W = 2."},{"term":"Majority quorum","def":"floor(N/2) + 1 replicas, for example 2 of 3 or 3 of 5. Using it for both R and W tolerates the loss of a minority."},{"term":"Sloppy quorum","def":"Accepts writes on stand-in nodes outside the key's replica set when designated ones are unreachable. More available, but loses the overlap guarantee."},{"term":"Hinted handoff","def":"A stand-in stores the write with a hint naming the intended replica and forwards it when that node returns. Hints are time-limited and best-effort."},{"term":"Read repair","def":"When a read sees different versions, the coordinator returns the newest and writes it back to stale replicas. Only fixes keys that are read."},{"term":"Anti-entropy","def":"Background sync where replicas compare Merkle trees of their data and copy only the differing ranges. Catches cold keys and expired hints."},{"term":"What quorums do not do","def":"They do not order concurrent writes to the same key. You still need version metadata such as vector clocks or a merge rule to avoid silently losing updates."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"consensus","title":"Consensus (Raft)","subtitle":"Watch five servers agree on one write, lose their leader, elect a new one, and never lose a committed entry.","minutes":14,"level":"Advanced","learn":["Why a group of machines needs consensus instead of one trusted copy","How Raft elects a leader using terms, randomized timeouts and majority votes","How log replication and majority commit make a write durable","Why a minority partition can never commit and a stale leader is forced to step down","What etcd and ZooKeeper use consensus for, and what they are not for"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Client","kind":"client","x":9,"y":50},{"id":"s1","label":"S1","kind":"server","x":38,"y":50},{"id":"s2","label":"S2","kind":"server","x":74,"y":12},{"id":"s3","label":"S3","kind":"server","x":74,"y":34},{"id":"s4","label":"S4","kind":"server","x":74,"y":66},{"id":"s5","label":"S5","kind":"server","x":74,"y":88}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"s1"},{"from":"client","to":"s3"},{"from":"s1","to":"s2"},{"from":"s1","to":"s3"},{"from":"s1","to":"s4"},{"from":"s1","to":"s5"},{"from":"s3","to":"s2"},{"from":"s3","to":"s4"},{"from":"s4","to":"s5"}],"metrics":[{"id":"term","label":"Term","unit":""},{"id":"acks","label":"Copies stored","unit":"of 5"},{"id":"need","label":"Majority needs","unit":"copies"},{"id":"commit","label":"Commit time","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Who decides what is true?","body":"Say you keep a cluster's critical settings, such as which node owns which job, on one server. That server dies and everything stops. So you keep copies on five servers. Now a harder problem appears. If two clients write different values at the same moment, or a server crashes mid-write, how do all five end up agreeing on one history? Making machines agree despite crashes and lost messages is called consensus.","idea":"Copies give you availability. Consensus keeps those copies from disagreeing.","caption":"Five servers must act like one reliable machine.","focus":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"flows":[],"chips":[{"node":"s3","text":"majority = 3 of 5"}],"metrics":{"term":"1","acks":"0","need":"3"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"One leader, many followers","body":"Raft is a popular consensus algorithm. It makes the problem simple by electing one leader. Only the leader accepts writes. Followers copy what the leader sends. Time is cut into numbered terms, like presidential terms: each term has at most one leader. Here S1 leads term 1. Every 100 ms or so it sends tiny heartbeat messages (\"I'm still alive\") to all followers, as the animation shows.","idea":"One leader per term means all writes are ordered in one place, so there is nothing to argue about.","caption":"S1 leads term 1 and sends heartbeats to every follower.","focus":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"flows":[{"from":"s1","to":"s2","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat T1"},{"from":"s1","to":"s3","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat T1"},{"from":"s1","to":"s4","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat T1"},{"from":"s1","to":"s5","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat T1"}],"chips":[{"node":"s1","text":"leader · term 1","tone":"green"},{"node":"s2","text":"follower"},{"node":"s1","text":"heartbeat 100 ms","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"term":"1"}},{"tag":"REPLICATE","title":"A write enters the log","body":"A client sends set x=5. The leader adds it to its own log, which is an ordered list of commands. It then sends an AppendEntries message to every follower, carrying the entry and its position in the log. Each follower writes the entry to disk before replying. Right now the entry exists but is not committed (not yet safe), so the leader has told the client nothing. Only the leader's own copy exists so far: 1 of the 3 needed.","idea":"A log entry is only a proposal until enough servers have stored it.","caption":"The leader appends x=5 and sends it to all followers.","focus":["client","s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"s1","kind":"request","label":"set x=5"},{"from":"s1","to":"s2","kind":"replicate","label":"append x=5"},{"from":"s1","to":"s3","kind":"replicate","label":"append x=5"},{"from":"s1","to":"s4","kind":"replicate","label":"append x=5"},{"from":"s1","to":"s5","kind":"replicate","label":"append x=5"}],"chips":[{"node":"s1","text":"x=5 uncommitted"},{"node":"s1","text":"1 of 3 acks","at":"bottom"},{"node":"s4","text":"writes to disk"}],"metrics":{"acks":"1","need":"3"}},{"tag":"COMMIT","title":"Majority means committed","body":"Followers reply as they finish their disk writes. The moment the leader plus two followers hold the entry, that is 3 of 5, a majority has it. The entry is now committed: it will survive any failure the cluster is built to survive. The leader applies it, tells the client \"ok\", and lets followers know on its next message. In one data center this takes a few milliseconds, mostly the disk flush.","idea":"Committed means a majority has it on disk. Any future leader needs a majority too, so the two groups must overlap and include it.","caption":"Three of five have it, so x=5 is committed.","focus":["s1","s2","s3","client"],"flows":[{"from":"s2","to":"s1","kind":"response","label":"stored"},{"from":"s3","to":"s1","kind":"response","label":"stored"},{"from":"s1","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"ok, committed"}],"chips":[{"node":"s1","text":"committed","tone":"green"},{"node":"s1","text":"3/5 acks","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"client","text":"ok · 5 ms","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{"acks":"3","need":"3","commit":"5"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The leader dies","body":"Now S1 goes silent, from a crash or a long pause. Followers expect a heartbeat every 100 ms or so. If none arrives within the election timeout, a follower assumes the leader is gone and runs for leader, becoming a candidate. The timeout is random for each server, so they do not all fire at the same instant. (etcd defaults: 100 ms heartbeat, 1000 ms election timeout.) Until a new leader exists the cluster accepts no writes. A write sent now waits about a second with etcd defaults.","idea":"Followers detect a dead leader by silence. Random timeouts make simultaneous candidates unlikely.","caption":"Heartbeats stop. S3's timer runs out first.","focus":["s1","s3","client"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"s1","kind":"fail","label":"no response"},{"from":"s1","to":"s3","kind":"fail","label":"no heartbeat"}],"chips":[{"node":"s1","text":"silent","tone":"red"},{"node":"s3","text":"no heartbeat","tone":"red"},{"node":"client","text":"write waits ≈ 1 s","tone":"red"}],"metrics":{"commit":"1000"}},{"tag":"ELECTION","title":"S3 asks for votes","body":"S3's timer fires first. It raises the term to 2, votes for itself, and sends RequestVote messages to the others. Each server gives at most one vote per term, and only to a candidate whose log is at least as up to date as its own. S2 and S4 say yes. With S3's own vote that is 3 of 5, a majority, so S3 becomes leader of term 2 and starts sending heartbeats at once.","idea":"One vote per server per term means there can be at most one leader per term.","caption":"S3 wins term 2 with votes from S2 and S4.","focus":["s3","s2","s4"],"flows":[{"from":"s3","to":"s2","kind":"request","label":"vote? term 2"},{"from":"s3","to":"s4","kind":"request","label":"vote? term 2"},{"from":"s2","to":"s3","kind":"response","label":"granted"},{"from":"s4","to":"s3","kind":"response","label":"granted"}],"chips":[{"node":"s3","text":"candidate · term 2"},{"node":"s2","text":"vote granted","tone":"green"},{"node":"s4","text":"vote granted","tone":"green"},{"node":"s3","text":"3/5 votes","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"term":"2","acks":"3","need":"3","commit":"1000"}},{"tag":"SAFETY","title":"Committed entries survive","body":"Why is x=5 not lost? It sits on a majority, and a winning candidate also needs a majority of votes. Any two majorities of five share at least one server, and that server refuses to vote for a candidate missing x=5. So the new leader always holds every committed entry. What if S1 was only frozen or cut off, not crashed? It still thinks it leads term 1. Its first message from S3 carries term 2, so S1 steps down and becomes a follower; any uncommitted entries it had are overwritten.","idea":"Overlapping majorities plus the \"log must be up to date\" vote rule mean a committed entry can never be voted away.","caption":"Stale S1 sees term 2 and becomes a follower.","focus":["s3","s1"],"flows":[{"from":"s3","to":"s1","kind":"replicate","label":"append, term 2"},{"from":"s1","to":"s3","kind":"response","label":"I step down"}],"chips":[{"node":"s3","text":"leader · term 2","tone":"green"},{"node":"s1","text":"steps down"}],"metrics":{"term":"2"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A network partition","body":"Now suppose a network split cuts S4 and S5 off from the other three. S1 has rejoined as a follower, so S1, S2 and S3 are a majority of 3 and keep working with S3 as leader. S4 and S5 can gather only 2 votes (their own two) but need 3, so they cannot elect a leader or commit anything. S4 may bump its term to 3, but it can never win with it. Real systems add a pre-vote check so an isolated server does not inflate its term and disturb the leader when the network heals. The minority refuses to answer rather than risk two conflicting histories. That is the consistency choice from the CAP lesson.","idea":"Only the side with a majority can make progress, so a partition can never produce two committed histories.","caption":"S4 and S5 are isolated and cannot commit.","focus":["s3","s4","s5"],"flows":[{"from":"s4","to":"s3","kind":"fail","label":"unreachable"},{"from":"s4","to":"s5","kind":"request","label":"vote? term 3"},{"from":"s5","to":"s4","kind":"response","label":"granted"}],"chips":[{"node":"s3","text":"leader · 3 of 5","tone":"green"},{"node":"s4","text":"2 votes, needs 3","tone":"red"},{"node":"s5","text":"can't commit","tone":"red"}],"metrics":{"term":"2"}},{"tag":"IN PRACTICE","title":"What etcd and ZooKeeper do","body":"You rarely write Raft yourself. etcd (which uses Raft) and ZooKeeper (which uses a similar protocol called Zab) run a small cluster of 3 or 5 servers holding a small amount of critical data: configuration, who is the leader of a service, cluster membership, locks. Kubernetes keeps all of its state in etcd. These stores are slow for bulk data, because every write pays a round trip to a majority. Use them for coordination, not for your application's rows.","idea":"Use consensus stores for small, high-value coordination data, never as your main database.","caption":"A client reads and writes small coordination data.","focus":["client","s2","s3"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"s3","kind":"request","label":"put /leader=A"},{"from":"s3","to":"s2","kind":"replicate","label":"append"},{"from":"s2","to":"s3","kind":"response","label":"stored"}],"chips":[{"node":"s3","text":"3 of 5 acks","tone":"green"},{"node":"client","text":"put /leader=A"}],"metrics":{"term":"2","acks":"3","need":"3","commit":"5"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Agreement by majority","body":"Raft elects one leader per numbered term. The leader copies each log entry to followers and commits it once a majority has stored it. A new leader needs a majority of votes, and those voters must hold every committed entry, so committed data survives leader crashes. A minority side stalls instead of diverging. The price is a majority round trip on every write, so use it for small coordination data.","idea":"Majority plus terms gives one history. The costs are a round trip per write and no progress without a majority.","caption":"Leader, terms, majority commit, safe failover.","focus":["client","s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"flows":[],"chips":[{"node":"s3","text":"leader · term 2","tone":"green"},{"node":"s3","text":"3/5 acks","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"client","text":"commit ≈ 5 ms"}],"metrics":{"term":"2","acks":"3","need":"3","commit":"5"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A Raft cluster has 5 servers. The leader has stored an entry itself, and one follower has also stored it. Can the leader tell the client the write is committed?","options":["Yes, two copies is enough to survive one crash","No, it needs at least 3 of 5 servers to hold the entry","Yes, because the leader's own copy is authoritative","No, it needs all 5 servers to hold the entry"],"answer":1,"why":"Commit requires a majority, 3 of 5. With only 2 copies, the other three servers could form a majority, elect a leader without the entry, and the write would be lost."},{"q":"Why must a server refuse to vote for a candidate whose log is behind its own?","options":["To keep elections short","To stop the old leader from restarting","To prevent followers from voting twice in a term","To make sure the winner already holds every committed entry"],"answer":3,"why":"Any committed entry sits on a majority, and any winning candidate also needs a majority of votes. The overlapping server blocks candidates that lack the entry."},{"q":"A 6-server cluster is split into groups of 3 and 3 by a network failure. What happens?","options":["Both groups elect leaders and keep accepting writes","The group containing the old leader keeps working","Neither group can reach a majority of 4, so neither can commit","The group with the lowest server IDs wins automatically"],"answer":2,"why":"A majority of 6 is 4. With 3 and 3, neither side has it, so writes stall. This is also why odd cluster sizes are preferred."},{"q":"The leader of term 1 was frozen by a long pause. Meanwhile S3 won term 2. When the old leader resumes, it still thinks it leads and tries to send an entry. What happens?","options":["Followers reject it because its term is lower, and it steps down","Followers accept it if it has more entries than S3","Followers accept it because it arrived last","Followers start a new election in term 1"],"answer":0,"why":"A paused or partitioned leader can still believe it leads. Servers reject messages from a lower term, and a leader that sees a higher term immediately becomes a follower. Terms keep stale leaders harmless."},{"q":"Which workload is a poor fit for storing directly in etcd or ZooKeeper?","options":["Service leader and membership info","A cluster's feature-flag configuration","Millions of per-user order rows with heavy write traffic","A distributed lock's ownership record"],"answer":2,"why":"Every write goes through a single leader and waits for a quorum, and the whole dataset is replicated to each server. Coordination state is small, but bulk application data should live in a scalable database."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Consensus","def":"Getting a group of machines to agree on one value or one ordered history even when some crash or messages are lost."},{"term":"Term","def":"Numbered election period in Raft. At most one leader per term; a higher term always overrides a lower one."},{"term":"Quorum / majority","def":"More than half the servers: floor(N/2)+1. A cluster of 2f+1 servers survives f failures."},{"term":"Commit rule","def":"A leader may report an entry committed once a majority has stored it durably. Never earlier."},{"term":"Election timeout","def":"How long a follower waits without a heartbeat before running for leader. Randomized per server to avoid ties; must exceed the heartbeat interval by a wide margin."},{"term":"Vote restriction","def":"A server only votes for a candidate whose log is at least as up to date as its own, so the winner has every committed entry."},{"term":"etcd / ZooKeeper","def":"Small, strongly consistent stores built on Raft (etcd) or Zab (ZooKeeper). Used for config, leader election, membership and locks, not bulk data."},{"term":"Cluster size","def":"Use 3 or 5 servers. Even sizes add cost without adding failure tolerance, and more servers make every write slower."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"leader-election-locks","title":"Leader Election & Distributed Locks","subtitle":"Watch two workers fight over one lock, see a paused process corrupt data, and see a fencing token stop it.","minutes":12,"level":"Advanced","learn":["How a lock service picks one leader and why the lock is really a lease with an expiry time","Why a lock that only has a TTL is unsafe when a process pauses (GC, VM stall, slow network)","How a fencing token lets the protected resource reject a stale lock holder","Where the lock service itself gets its safety (consensus) and what that costs you"],"nodes":[{"id":"a","label":"Worker A","kind":"worker","x":10,"y":30},{"id":"b","label":"Worker B","kind":"worker","x":10,"y":80},{"id":"lock","label":"Lock service","kind":"service","x":47,"y":18},{"id":"store","label":"Shared storage","kind":"storage","x":86,"y":60}],"edges":[{"from":"a","to":"lock"},{"from":"b","to":"lock"},{"from":"a","to":"store"},{"from":"b","to":"store"}],"metrics":[{"id":"ttl","label":"Lease TTL","unit":"s"},{"id":"token","label":"Newest token","unit":""},{"id":"pause","label":"A frozen for","unit":"s"},{"id":"stale","label":"Stale accepted","unit":"writes"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Only one may write","body":"You run two copies of a worker so the job survives a crash. But a nightly job must be run by only one copy at a time, or the same file is written twice and gets corrupted. So one worker must be chosen as the leader, and if it dies, the other must take over quickly. The workers cannot see each other's memory. How do they agree on who is in charge?","idea":"Leader election is mutual exclusion across machines: at most one holder, plus a way to recover when the holder dies.","caption":"Two workers, one shared resource, only one allowed to write.","focus":["a","b","store"],"flows":[],"chips":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"ELECTION","title":"Race to create one key","body":"Both workers ask a lock service (etcd, ZooKeeper, or a database row with a unique key) to create the key /leader. The service handles requests one at a time, so exactly one create succeeds. Here A wins and becomes leader. B gets an \"already exists\" error. B then either retries later or watches the key to be told when it disappears.","idea":"A central service that handles requests one at a time turns a race between machines into a single winner.","caption":"A wins the create; B is told the key already exists.","focus":["a","b","lock"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"lock","kind":"request","label":"create /leader"},{"from":"b","to":"lock","kind":"request","label":"create /leader"},{"from":"lock","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"OK"},{"from":"lock","to":"b","kind":"fail","label":"already exists"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"OK · leader","tone":"green"},{"node":"b","text":"already exists","tone":"red"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"LEASE","title":"A lock must expire","body":"If A held the key forever and then crashed, nobody could ever take over. So the lock is a lease: it is valid for a TTL (time to live), here 10 seconds, and A must keep renewing it before it runs out. The service also hands out a fencing token, a number that goes up with every grant. A gets token 33. A short TTL fails over fast but may expire a healthy, slow leader. A long TTL slows recovery. Renewing at about a third of the TTL is a common rule of thumb.","idea":"A lease turns \"held until released\" into \"held until it times out\", so a dead holder cannot block everyone forever.","caption":"A holds a 10 s lease and token 33, and keeps renewing.","focus":["a","lock"],"flows":[{"from":"lock","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"10s lease, tok 33"},{"from":"a","to":"lock","kind":"async","label":"renew"}],"chips":[{"node":"lock","text":"fencing token 33"},{"node":"a","text":"lease 10 s","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{"ttl":"10","token":"33","stale":"0"}},{"tag":"WORK","title":"The healthy case","body":"While A keeps renewing, B stays out and A writes to storage as the leader. Each write carries A's token, 33. Nothing is wrong here, and for many jobs a plain TTL lock would look fine in testing. The danger is not a crash, which the lease handles well. The danger is a holder that is slow but still alive.","idea":"TTL locks work in the happy path, which is why their flaw survives testing.","caption":"A renews its lease and writes with token 33.","focus":["a","lock","store"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"lock","kind":"async","label":"renew"},{"from":"a","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"write (token 33)"},{"from":"store","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"ok"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"leader","tone":"green"},{"node":"a","text":"renewing","at":"bottom"},{"node":"store","text":"write ok · tok 33","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A freezes for 15 seconds","body":"Just after checking that it still holds the lock, A hits a long pause: a garbage-collection (memory cleanup) stall, a VM migration, or a stuck disk. The whole process freezes, so it cannot renew its lease, and no code in it runs while time passes. A check made before the pause may be false by the time it resumes, so checking the lease first cannot protect A. In the animation, A's renewal never arrives.","idea":"A process cannot reliably know it was paused. A check like \"do I still hold the lock?\" can be out of date by the time the write happens.","caption":"A is frozen, so its renewal never reaches the lock service.","focus":["a","lock"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"lock","kind":"fail","label":"no renewal"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"paused (GC)","tone":"red"},{"node":"lock","text":"no renewal","tone":"red"}],"metrics":{"pause":"0"}},{"tag":"EXPIRE","title":"The lease runs out","body":"After 10 seconds with no renewal, the lock service expires A's lease and deletes the key. B, which was watching, is told the key is gone, creates it at once, and becomes the new leader with token 34. From the service's point of view this is correct: A looked dead, so B took over. The problem is that A does not know yet.","idea":"The lock service did the right thing. The problem is that A does not know its lease is gone.","caption":"The lease expires and B takes over with token 34.","focus":["b","lock"],"flows":[{"from":"lock","to":"b","kind":"async","label":"watch: key gone"},{"from":"b","to":"lock","kind":"request","label":"create /leader"},{"from":"lock","to":"b","kind":"response","label":"10s lease, tok 34"}],"chips":[{"node":"lock","text":"A lease expired","tone":"red"},{"node":"b","text":"leader · token 34","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{"token":"34","pause":"10"}},{"tag":"FAILURE","title":"Two writers, one file","body":"At second 15, A wakes up. Its last check said it was leader, so it finishes its write. B is also writing. If storage accepts anything from anyone, A's old write lands after B's newer one and overwrites it. The lock service had no bug: the lock was granted correctly. The unsafe part is trusting the holder. No TTL is long enough to rule out a pause, so a longer TTL only makes this bug rarer.","idea":"A lock plus TTL cannot guarantee exclusion. A stale holder can still act after its lease is gone.","caption":"A's stale write lands next to B's write.","focus":["a","b","store"],"flows":[{"from":"b","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"write (token 34)"},{"from":"store","to":"b","kind":"response","label":"ok"},{"from":"a","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"write (stale)"},{"from":"store","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"ok (bad!)"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"wakes at 15 s"},{"node":"store","text":"stale write accepted","tone":"red"},{"node":"store","text":"2 writers","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"pause":"15","stale":"1"}},{"tag":"FENCING","title":"Let the resource say no","body":"Now replay the same pause, this time with fencing. Every write carries its fencing token, and storage remembers the highest token it has seen. B wrote with 34, so A's later write with 33 is rejected as stale. The check happens in storage, so it works however long A was frozen and whatever A believes. It also catches a write that was sent in time but delayed in the network.","idea":"A fencing token makes the protected resource, not the lock holder, the final judge of who may write.","caption":"Replay: storage rejects token 33 because it has seen 34.","focus":["a","b","store"],"flows":[{"from":"b","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"write (token 34)"},{"from":"store","to":"b","kind":"response","label":"ok"},{"from":"a","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"write (token 33)"},{"from":"store","to":"a","kind":"fail","label":"rejected: 33 < 34"}],"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"highest seen: 34"},{"node":"store","text":"33 rejected","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"b","text":"token 34 ok","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{"stale":"0"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"What this costs","body":"The lock service must itself survive failures, so real ones (etcd, ZooKeeper) replicate through a consensus protocol and need a majority of servers up (see the Raft lesson). Every grant needs a round trip to a majority, so lock operations take milliseconds, and with no majority the service cannot grant or renew anything. Fencing also needs storage that can compare tokens, and the check and the write must be one atomic step, for example a conditional write. A lock that only saves effort, like avoiding duplicate emails, can skip fencing. A lock that protects correctness cannot.","idea":"Use fencing when correctness depends on the lock. Accept a plain TTL lock only when a rare double run is harmless.","caption":"The lock service itself needs a replicated majority (not drawn).","focus":["lock","store"],"flows":[],"chips":[{"node":"lock","text":"majority needed"},{"node":"store","text":"atomic token check"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Lease, token, check","body":"Election picks one winner by letting only one create succeed. A lease with a TTL lets the system recover from a dead leader. But a paused leader can wake up after its lease ended, so every grant carries an increasing fencing token and storage rejects any older token. The TTL gives liveness (the system keeps moving). Fencing gives safety (no two writers).","idea":"Never trust \"I hold the lock\" on its own. Make the resource verify the token.","caption":"Election, lease, and fencing together.","focus":["a","b","lock","store"],"flows":[],"chips":[{"node":"lock","text":"lease 10 s"},{"node":"store","text":"rejects < 34","tone":"green"},{"node":"b","text":"leader · tok 34","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{"ttl":"10","token":"34","pause":"15","stale":"0"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a lock that is just a key with a 10 s TTL unsafe for protecting a shared file?","options":["The lock service cannot create keys atomically","A holder may be paused past its TTL and still write, unaware that a new holder exists","TTLs make the lock service slower than 10 s","Two workers can never be granted the same key"],"answer":1,"why":"The service correctly expires the lease and grants it to someone else, but the old holder cannot tell that time passed. Its write can arrive after the new holder's."},{"q":"Worker A has token 33 and was frozen. B now has token 34 and has written. What does storage do with A's write if it implements fencing?","options":["Accepts it, since it arrived last","Accepts it if A's clock says its lease is still valid","Rejects it because 33 is lower than the highest token seen, 34","Asks the lock service whether A is leader before deciding"],"answer":2,"why":"Storage keeps the highest token it has seen and refuses any lower one. This does not depend on clocks or on A's beliefs."},{"q":"Why do etcd and ZooKeeper replicate lock state with a consensus protocol?","options":["Consensus makes lock operations faster","Consensus removes the need for TTLs","Consensus lets storage skip token checks","A single lock-service node crashing would lose the lock state or block everyone"],"answer":3,"why":"The lock service is itself a single point of failure unless its state is replicated. Consensus keeps one agreed copy as long as a majority is up. It does not replace TTLs or fencing."},{"q":"You use a lock only to avoid sending the same daily email twice, and an occasional duplicate is acceptable. What is the reasonable choice?","options":["A plain TTL lock is enough, since a rare double run is harmless","You must add fencing tokens to the email provider","Use no lock, because locks cannot work across machines","Make the TTL 1 ms so it is always fresh"],"answer":0,"why":"When the lock is only an efficiency optimisation, an occasional violation costs little. Fencing matters when correctness depends on exclusion."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Lease","def":"A lock that is valid only for a fixed time and must be renewed. If the holder stops renewing, the service frees it so others can take over."},{"term":"Fencing token","def":"A number that grows with every lock grant. The protected resource rejects any request carrying a lower token than one it has already seen."},{"term":"Lock service","def":"A small replicated store (etcd, ZooKeeper, Consul) that serialises create/renew requests and uses consensus to stay correct if some nodes fail."},{"term":"Leader election","def":"Choosing one node to do a job. Common pattern: all nodes try to create one key, the winner is leader, the rest watch the key."},{"term":"Pause hazard","def":"GC pauses, VM stalls and slow I/O can freeze a process longer than the TTL. After waking it may still act as leader."},{"term":"Efficiency vs correctness","def":"A lock used to save work can be a simple TTL lock. A lock that protects data integrity needs fencing at the resource."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"clocks-ordering","title":"Clocks & Ordering","subtitle":"Watch two replicas disagree about which write came last, then fix it with logical clocks.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why wall clocks on different machines never agree exactly, and what NTP can and cannot fix","How last-write-wins can silently throw away the newer write","What happens-before means and how to tell ordered events from concurrent ones","How Lamport timestamps and vector clocks capture causality","Why hybrid logical clocks pair physical time with a counter"],"nodes":[{"id":"c1","label":"Client 1","kind":"client","x":10,"y":25},{"id":"c2","label":"Client 2","kind":"client","x":10,"y":75},{"id":"a","label":"Replica A","kind":"server","x":40,"y":25},{"id":"b","label":"Replica B","kind":"server","x":40,"y":75},{"id":"ntp","label":"NTP time server","kind":"external","x":72,"y":10},{"id":"store","label":"Merged record","kind":"db","x":80,"y":55}],"edges":[{"from":"c1","to":"a"},{"from":"c2","to":"b"},{"from":"a","to":"b"},{"from":"a","to":"store"},{"from":"b","to":"store"},{"from":"ntp","to":"a"},{"from":"ntp","to":"b"}],"metrics":[{"id":"skew","label":"Clock skew","unit":"ms"},{"id":"lamport","label":"Lamport clock","unit":""},{"id":"kept","label":"Writes kept","unit":"of 2"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Which write came last?","body":"A shopping-cart colour is stored on two replicas (copies on two servers). Client 1 sets it to \"red\" through Replica A. Fifty milliseconds later, Client 2 reads \"red\", changes it to \"blue\", and writes through Replica B. Blue is clearly the newer intent. Can the system work that out just by comparing timestamps?","idea":"Deciding which event came after which is the core problem. Timestamps are only one way to answer it.","caption":"Two clients, two replicas, one value. Which write is last?","focus":["c1","c2","a","b"],"flows":[],"chips":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Clocks drift and disagree","body":"Each computer counts time with a small quartz crystal, which is never perfect. A typical crystal is off by 10 to 100 parts per million, which adds up to as much as 8 seconds a day if left alone. So two servers always show slightly different times. Here Replica B's clock runs 120 ms behind Replica A's. That gap is called clock skew.","idea":"There is no single \"now\" shared by all machines. Every clock is a slightly wrong local guess.","caption":"Replica B's clock is 120 ms behind A's.","focus":["a","b"],"flows":[],"chips":[{"node":"b","text":"skew 120 ms","tone":"red"},{"node":"a","text":"up to 8 s/day drift"}],"metrics":{"skew":"120"}},{"tag":"NTP","title":"NTP shrinks skew, never removes it","body":"NTP (Network Time Protocol) lets a machine ask a time server for the time, estimate how long the answer took to arrive, and nudge its own clock. Over the public internet it typically gets within a few to a few tens of milliseconds. In the animation both replicas sync and the skew drops from 120 ms to about 5 ms. Carefully tuned datacenters (chrony or PTP) can get well under a millisecond. But errors come back: a missed sync, a VM pause, or a leap second can throw a clock off.","idea":"Synchronized clocks are close, not equal. A timestamp comparison smaller than the error margin means nothing.","caption":"Both replicas sync with NTP, and skew drops to about 5 ms.","focus":["ntp","a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"ntp","kind":"request","label":"what time?"},{"from":"ntp","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"time + delay"},{"from":"b","to":"ntp","kind":"request","label":"what time?"},{"from":"ntp","to":"b","kind":"response","label":"time + delay"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"clock nudged"},{"node":"b","text":"skew ≈ 5 ms","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{"skew":"5"}},{"tag":"FAILURE","title":"Last-write-wins loses data","body":"Suppose the sync lapsed and B is 120 ms behind again. Replica A stamps red at 10:00:00.100. Fifty ms later, B stamps blue with its slow clock: 10:00:00.030, a smaller number than red's. The merge rule last-write-wins (LWW) keeps the biggest timestamp, so red wins and the newer blue write is silently thrown away. No error, no log, just a lost update.","idea":"LWW trusts wall clocks to order events. When skew is bigger than the gap between writes, it can drop the real latest write.","caption":"Blue is dropped because B's clock is behind.","focus":["c1","c2","a","b","store"],"flows":[{"from":"c1","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"set red"},{"from":"a","to":"store","kind":"replicate","label":"red @ .100"},{"from":"c2","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"set blue"},{"from":"b","to":"store","kind":"fail","label":"blue @ .030"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"red @ .100"},{"node":"b","text":"blue @ .030"},{"node":"store","text":"keeps red","tone":"red"},{"node":"store","text":"blue lost","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"skew":"120","kept":"1"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Happens-before","body":"Instead of asking \"what time was it?\", ask \"what could have influenced what?\". Event X happens before Y if: they run on the same machine and X is first, or X sends a message that Y receives, or a chain of such links connects them. If neither happens before the other, they are concurrent: neither could have known about the other. Here red then blue is ordered, because Client 2 read red before writing blue.","idea":"Causality, not wall-clock time, defines order. X happens before Y only if information could have flowed from X to Y.","caption":"Client 2's write depends on red, so it is causally later.","focus":["c1","a","c2","b"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"red"},{"from":"c2","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"b","to":"c2","kind":"response","label":"read: red"},{"from":"c2","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"write blue"}],"chips":[{"node":"c2","text":"read red first"},{"node":"b","text":"red → blue (ordered)"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"LAMPORT","title":"Lamport timestamps","body":"Leslie Lamport's trick needs no clocks at all. Each node keeps a counter. It adds 1 for every local event and attaches the counter to every message it sends. On receiving a message, the node sets its counter to max(its own, the message's) + 1. Here A sends red with L=4. B receives it, jumps past 4, then writes blue and reaches L=6. So if X happens before Y, X's number is smaller. The reverse is not true: a smaller number does not prove causality.","idea":"A Lamport clock gives an order that respects causality, but it cannot tell concurrent events from ordered ones.","caption":"Counters ride on messages, so B jumps past A's value.","focus":["a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"red, L=4"},{"from":"b","to":"a","kind":"replicate","label":"blue, L=6"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"sent red, L = 4"},{"node":"b","text":"blue, L = 6"}],"metrics":{"lamport":"6"}},{"tag":"VECTOR","title":"Vector clocks spot concurrency","body":"A vector clock keeps one counter per node. Red is written at A as [A1, B0]. B receives it and writes blue as [A1, B1]. Every entry is at least as large, so blue safely replaces red. But suppose A also takes a different write, [A2, B0], without seeing blue. Compared with [A1, B1], A is higher in one and B is higher in the other, so they are incomparable (concurrent). The system keeps both as siblings for the app or a merge rule to resolve. Dynamo-style stores such as Riak use this.","idea":"Comparing vectors tells you one of three things: older, newer, or truly concurrent. The cost is a vector that grows with the number of writers.","caption":"Ordered writes replace each other. Concurrent ones are both kept.","focus":["a","b","store"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"red [A1,B0]"},{"from":"b","to":"a","kind":"replicate","label":"blue [A1,B1]"},{"from":"a","to":"store","kind":"replicate","label":"[A2,B0]"},{"from":"b","to":"store","kind":"replicate","label":"[A1,B1]"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"[A2,B0] write"},{"node":"b","text":"blue [A1,B1]"},{"node":"store","text":"2 siblings kept"}],"metrics":{"kept":"2"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Hybrid logical clocks","body":"Lamport and vector values mean nothing to a human and cannot answer \"show me data from 9am\". A hybrid logical clock (HLC) stores the physical time plus a small counter. It follows the wall clock closely, but if a message arrives stamped with a time ahead of the local clock, it keeps that time and bumps the counter instead of going backwards. CockroachDB and YugabyteDB use HLCs. Google Spanner takes another route: its TrueTime API reports an uncertainty interval, and commits wait it out, typically a few milliseconds.","idea":"HLCs keep causal order and stay close to real time, but they still depend on clocks being reasonably close.","caption":"Timestamps carry the time plus a tiebreak counter.","focus":["a","b","ntp"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"t=.100, c=0"},{"from":"b","to":"a","kind":"replicate","label":"t=.100, c=1"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"t=.100, c=0"},{"node":"b","text":"t=.100, c=1"}],"metrics":{"skew":"5","kept":"2"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick the right clock","body":"Wall clocks drift, and NTP only narrows the error, so comparing timestamps from different machines can reorder events and let LWW lose data. Lamport clocks give an order that respects causality. Vector clocks also detect concurrency. HLCs mix causality with readable time. How to merge concurrent writes is left to the conflict-resolution lessons.","idea":"Use time for humans and logs. Use causality tracking when correctness depends on order.","caption":"Causality tracking keeps both writes visible.","focus":["c1","c2","a","b","ntp","store"],"flows":[],"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"LWW can lose data","tone":"red"},{"node":"a","text":"vector: concurrency"},{"node":"b","text":"HLC: readable time"}],"metrics":{"skew":"5","lamport":"6","kept":"2"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Replica A and B are synced by NTP to within about 20 ms. Two writes to the same key happen 5 ms apart on different replicas. What can a timestamp comparison tell you?","options":["Which write is newer, because NTP is accurate","Which write is newer, as long as the clocks are in the same timezone","Nothing reliable, since the gap is smaller than the clock error","That the writes are concurrent"],"answer":2,"why":"Skew of 20 ms is larger than the 5 ms gap, so either replica's stamp could be ahead. The comparison is within the noise and cannot be trusted."},{"q":"Last-write-wins is used to merge two replicas. Which scenario most clearly causes silent data loss?","options":["Both replicas have exactly the same clock","The replica that took the later write has a clock running behind the other","The writes are to different keys","The network is slow but all messages arrive"],"answer":1,"why":"A later write stamped by a slow clock gets a smaller timestamp, so the merge discards it even though it was the latest in real time."},{"q":"Two vector clocks are [A:3, B:1] and [A:2, B:2]. What is their relationship?","options":["The first is older","The second is older","They are identical","They are concurrent"],"answer":3,"why":"Neither vector is greater than or equal in every entry: A is higher in the first, B is higher in the second. So neither write saw the other."},{"q":"Event X has Lamport timestamp 7 and event Y has Lamport timestamp 9. What can you conclude?","options":["X happened before Y","Y cannot have happened before X","X and Y are concurrent","Y happened before X"],"answer":1,"why":"If Y happened before X, then Y's timestamp would be smaller. So Y cannot precede X, but X may still be concurrent with Y, which is why 'X happened before Y' is not guaranteed."},{"q":"Why would a database choose a hybrid logical clock over a plain Lamport clock?","options":["HLC timestamps stay close to real time, so you can query by time and still respect causality","HLCs need no counter","HLCs eliminate clock skew","HLCs detect concurrent writes like vector clocks"],"answer":0,"why":"An HLC pairs physical time with a counter: readable, near wall-clock, and causality-safe. It does not remove skew or give per-node concurrency detection."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Clock skew","def":"The difference between two machines' clocks at the same real instant. Always non-zero; NTP keeps it to roughly ms, not zero."},{"term":"Clock drift","def":"How fast a clock gains or loses time. Cheap crystals drift tens of parts per million, several seconds per day if unsynced."},{"term":"Happens-before (→)","def":"X → Y if X precedes Y on one node, or X is a send whose receive is Y, or by chaining. Otherwise the events are concurrent."},{"term":"Lamport timestamp","def":"A counter bumped per event and set to max(local, received) + 1 on receive. If X → Y then L(X) < L(Y); the reverse is not guaranteed."},{"term":"Vector clock","def":"One counter per node. Compare entry-wise to find older, newer, or concurrent. Grows with the number of writers."},{"term":"Hybrid logical clock","def":"Physical time plus a logical counter. Stays near wall-clock time and never goes backwards. Used by CockroachDB, YugabyteDB."},{"term":"Last-write-wins (LWW)","def":"Keeps the highest timestamp. Simple, but loses updates under skew or true concurrency. Fine for idempotent overwrites like caches."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"gossip-failure-detection","title":"Gossip & Failure Detection","subtitle":"Watch a cluster notice a dead node, spread the news by rumor, and then get fooled by a network split.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How heartbeats let one node judge whether another is alive","Why a fixed timeout is a blunt tool and what a phi-accrual detector does instead","How gossip spreads membership news to every node in roughly log N rounds","Why, over an unreliable network, a failure detector can only suspect and never know, and how that causes false alarms","What split brain is and why a majority rule contains it"],"nodes":[{"id":"a","label":"Node A","kind":"service","x":12,"y":50},{"id":"b","label":"Node B","kind":"service","x":38,"y":20},{"id":"c","label":"Node C","kind":"service","x":38,"y":80},{"id":"d","label":"Node D","kind":"service","x":64,"y":20},{"id":"e","label":"Node E","kind":"service","x":64,"y":80},{"id":"client","label":"Client","kind":"client","x":88,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"a","to":"b"},{"from":"a","to":"c"},{"from":"b","to":"c"},{"from":"b","to":"d"},{"from":"c","to":"e"},{"from":"c","to":"d"},{"from":"d","to":"e"},{"from":"client","to":"d"},{"from":"client","to":"e"},{"from":"client","to":"a"}],"metrics":[{"id":"gap","label":"Silence","unit":"ms"},{"id":"phi","label":"Suspicion phi","unit":""},{"id":"aware","label":"Know E is down","unit":"of 4"},{"id":"leaders","label":"Active leaders","unit":""}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Is that node dead or just quiet?","body":"A five-node cluster stores data and must route around machines that fail. One node, A, is the leader that coordinates writes. But no node can look at another's power light. All it can see is messages arriving or not arriving. Silence could mean a crash, a slow disk, a long garbage-collection pause (the program freezes while it cleans up memory), or a broken network cable. This lesson shows how a cluster turns silence into a decision.","idea":"Over an unreliable network, failure detection is a guess from missing messages, never a direct observation.","caption":"Five peers and a client. Nobody can see another node's health.","focus":["a","b","c","d","e"],"flows":[],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"leader","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{"gap":"0","phi":"0","aware":"0","leaders":"1"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Heartbeats","body":"Each node sends a tiny heartbeat message (\"I'm alive\") to its peers on a schedule, say once per second. Every receiver records when the last one arrived. As long as beats keep landing about a second apart, the sender is presumed healthy. Here E sends beats #41 and #42 to D. A heartbeat only proves the sender could send a moment ago, not that it is doing useful work. Big clusters limit the cost: each node beats to only a few peers, or the counter rides on gossip messages.","idea":"A heartbeat is a regular \"I am alive\" message. Its absence is the only warning sign.","caption":"E sends a heartbeat to D every second, and D logs each arrival.","focus":["d","e"],"flows":[{"from":"e","to":"d","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat #41"},{"from":"e","to":"d","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat #42"}],"chips":[{"node":"e","text":"heartbeat #42"},{"node":"d","text":"gap 1 s · phi 0.4","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{"gap":"1000","phi":"0.4"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"The fixed timeout","body":"Now E crashes and its heartbeats stop. The simplest rule is a fixed timeout: if no heartbeat arrives for 5 seconds, declare E dead. It is easy to understand but rigid. On a quiet local network, 5 seconds is slower than it needs to be. On a congested or cross-region link where delays vary a lot, 5 seconds will wrongly fire on healthy nodes. One number cannot suit every network or every moment.","idea":"A fixed timeout trades detection speed against false alarms, and the right value changes with conditions.","caption":"E has crashed. D waits for the 5 s timer to expire.","focus":["d","e"],"flows":[{"from":"e","to":"d","kind":"fail","label":"no heartbeat"}],"chips":[{"node":"e","text":"crashed","tone":"red"},{"node":"d","text":"5 s timeout fires","tone":"red"}],"metrics":{"gap":"5000","phi":"2.2"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Phi-accrual detection","body":"A phi-accrual detector (used by Cassandra and Akka) learns how late beats normally are. It reports a suspicion score, phi, that grows as silence continues. Phi 1 means a gap this long would happen about 10% of the time if the node were healthy. Phi 2 is about 1%, and phi 8 about one in a hundred million. With a 1 s average gap, threshold 8 needs about 18.4 s of silence. That is slower than the 5 s rule on a steady link, because 8 is deliberately cautious. The gain is that the same threshold stretches on a jittery link (4 s average gives about 74 s) and tightens on a fast one (100 ms average gives about 1.8 s). A lower threshold detects sooner but causes more false alarms.","idea":"Phi adapts to the network's observed jitter and gives a graded suspicion level, not a plain yes or no.","caption":"Silence grows, and phi climbs past the threshold of 8.","focus":["d","e"],"flows":[{"from":"e","to":"d","kind":"fail","label":"phi rising"}],"chips":[{"node":"d","text":"phi 8.1","tone":"red"},{"node":"d","text":"threshold 8","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"gap":"18600","phi":"8.1","aware":"1"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Gossip starts","body":"D now suspects E, but every node must end up with the same view of who is alive. Instead of D messaging everyone, nodes gossip: every second, each node picks a few random peers and swaps what it knows, tagging each fact with a version number. D tells B and C that E looks down. Cassandra gossips heartbeat counters and each node judges for itself. SWIM-style systems (SWIM is a gossip membership protocol) gossip the suspicion itself, as here. New nodes join through a seed node, and no coordinator is needed.","idea":"Gossip means each node regularly swaps its knowledge with random peers, with no central coordinator.","caption":"D tells two random peers that E is suspected.","focus":["d","b","c"],"flows":[{"from":"d","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"E suspect v7"},{"from":"d","to":"c","kind":"replicate","label":"E suspect v7"}],"chips":[{"node":"d","text":"suspects E"},{"node":"b","text":"E suspect v7"},{"node":"c","text":"E suspect v7"}],"metrics":{"aware":"3"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"The rumor spreads","body":"In the next round, B and C each pick their own random peers, and A hears the news. With one peer per round, the number of informed nodes roughly doubles in the early rounds. So news reaches N nodes in about log2(N) rounds, plus a few more for stragglers: roughly 15 to 17 rounds for 1,000 nodes. Contacting a few peers per round makes it faster. Each node sends the same small number of messages per round however big the cluster is.","idea":"Informed nodes roughly double each early round, so spread time grows with log N while each node's load stays flat.","caption":"A, B, C and D now all know. The live cluster agrees.","focus":["a","b","c","d"],"flows":[{"from":"b","to":"a","kind":"replicate","label":"E suspect v7"},{"from":"c","to":"a","kind":"replicate","label":"E suspect v7"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"E suspect v7"},{"node":"d","text":"4 of 4 aware","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{"aware":"4"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"False alarms","body":"Suppose in an alternate run that E is fine, but a 20 s garbage-collection pause froze it. Its heartbeats stop, phi passes 8, and the cluster marks it down, though E is healthy. When E wakes up it can refute the rumor. In SWIM, a suspect raises its incarnation number (a counter only that node increments), and the higher number overwrites the suspicion in gossip. SWIM also uses indirect probes: other nodes ping the suspect for you, which avoids false alarms from one bad link. A too-sensitive threshold causes flapping, where nodes are shunned and readmitted and data moves for nothing. A too-lax one leaves real failures undetected longer.","idea":"A detector is never certain. The threshold balances slow detection against false alarms.","caption":"In this alternate run E was only paused, yet all four peers think it is down until it refutes.","focus":["e","c"],"flows":[{"from":"c","to":"e","kind":"fail","label":"no reply (GC)"},{"from":"e","to":"c","kind":"async","label":"I am back"}],"chips":[{"node":"e","text":"paused 20 s","tone":"red"},{"node":"c","text":"phi 8.7","tone":"red"},{"node":"e","text":"refutes: alive","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"gap":"20000","phi":"8.7","aware":"4"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Split brain","body":"Now a network partition cuts the cluster in two. A, B and C can talk to each other, and so can D and E, but the two groups cannot reach each other. Each side sees the other go silent and decides it is the survivor. If both sides elect their own leader and accept writes, you get split brain: two active leaders with diverging data. Here a client writes x=1 to A and another write x=2 lands on E, so the two sides now disagree.","idea":"Split brain is when both halves of a partition believe they alone are in charge.","caption":"Both sides suspect the other and each has a leader. Clients write to both.","focus":["b","d","c","e","client","a"],"flows":[{"from":"b","to":"d","kind":"fail","label":"partitioned"},{"from":"c","to":"e","kind":"fail","label":"partitioned"},{"from":"client","to":"e","kind":"request","label":"write x=2"},{"from":"c","to":"d","kind":"fail","label":"partitioned"},{"from":"client","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"write x=1"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"x=1"},{"node":"e","text":"x=2 · conflict","tone":"red"},{"node":"c","text":"partitioned","tone":"red","at":"edge:c-d"}],"metrics":{"gap":"20000","phi":"8.7","aware":"3","leaders":"2"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Containing it with a majority","body":"The usual defence is a quorum rule: only a group holding a strict majority of the configured members (3 of 5 here) may act as leader or accept writes. The 2-node side can still tell it lost contact, so it steps down or goes read-only. Two separate groups can never both hold a majority, so at most one side can elect a leader or commit writes. A stale leader on the minority side may linger briefly, so real systems add terms, leases and fencing, covered in later lessons.","idea":"Detection only suspects. A majority rule lets at most one side make progress, because two majorities always overlap.","caption":"A, B and C keep the majority. D and E refuse writes.","focus":["a","b","c","d","e"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"commit 3 of 5"},{"from":"a","to":"c","kind":"replicate","label":"commit 3 of 5"},{"from":"e","to":"client","kind":"fail","label":"no quorum"}],"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"3 of 5 = majority","tone":"green"},{"node":"e","text":"no quorum","tone":"red"}],"metrics":{"leaders":"1"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"From silence to decision","body":"Heartbeats make silence measurable. Phi-accrual turns the length of that silence into a graded suspicion that adapts to the network. Gossip spreads the verdict to every node in about log N rounds with no coordinator. Because any verdict can be wrong, safety-critical actions such as electing a leader need a majority, not just one detector's opinion.","idea":"Detect with heartbeats, spread with gossip, and act only with a majority.","caption":"Heartbeats, suspicion, gossip, then quorum.","focus":["a","b","c","d","e"],"flows":[],"chips":[{"node":"d","text":"phi 8 → suspect"},{"node":"c","text":"≈ log N rounds"}],"metrics":{"aware":"4","leaders":"1"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A fixed 5 s heartbeat timeout works in the data center but fires constantly on a cross-region link. What does a phi-accrual detector change?","options":["It sends heartbeats more often so silence is shorter","It judges silence against the observed arrival pattern, so tolerance adapts to jitter","It removes the need for heartbeats by using gossip","It guarantees a suspected node is really dead"],"answer":1,"why":"Phi compares the current silence with the learned arrival pattern, so the same threshold tolerates longer gaps on a jittery link and shorter ones on a steady link, instead of one fixed number for both."},{"q":"A 1,000-node cluster gossips once per second and each node contacts one random peer. About how long until a rumor reaches nearly everyone?","options":["A fraction of a second","About 1,000 seconds","It never fully converges","Roughly 10 to 20 seconds"],"answer":3,"why":"About log2(1000), around 10, plus several rounds for stragglers, so a bit under 20 rounds."},{"q":"A node freezes for 20 s in a GC pause, is marked down, then resumes. What does this show about failure detectors?","options":["The detector had a bug","Heartbeats should be sent over TCP","Slow and dead are indistinguishable from outside, so verdicts are suspicions that can be wrong","Gossip should have blocked the verdict"],"answer":2,"why":"From a peer's view a paused node and a crashed node look identical, so any detector can only suspect and must tolerate being wrong."},{"q":"A 5-node cluster partitions into groups of 3 and 2. Which rule prevents two leaders?","options":["Only the group with at least 3 of the 5 configured members may lead","Both groups elect a leader, then merge by timestamp","The group whose heartbeats are fastest leads","The group containing the original leader always leads"],"answer":0,"why":"Requiring a strict majority of the full membership means two disjoint groups can never both qualify, so at most one side can lead or commit."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Heartbeat","def":"A small periodic message meaning I am alive. Receivers track the time of the last one; missing beats raise suspicion."},{"term":"Fixed timeout","def":"Declare a node dead after N seconds of silence. Simple, but one value cannot suit both fast LANs and jittery WANs."},{"term":"Phi-accrual","def":"A detector outputting a continuous suspicion score from learned heartbeat timing. Phi 8 is about a one-in-100-million chance of a false alarm under the model."},{"term":"Gossip protocol","def":"Each round, every node swaps state with a few random peers. Spreads news in about log N rounds with flat per-node load."},{"term":"Membership list","def":"Each node's view of who is in the cluster and whether they look up or down, versioned so newer facts win in gossip. New nodes join via a seed node and spread by gossip."},{"term":"False positive","def":"A healthy node wrongly marked down, often after a GC pause or network blip. Aggressive thresholds cause flapping."},{"term":"Split brain","def":"Two partitions both act as the authority and accept conflicting writes. Prevent with majority quorums."},{"term":"Majority quorum","def":"More than half of the configured members, e.g. 3 of 5. Any two majorities overlap, so only one side can proceed."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"crdts","title":"Conflict Resolution & CRDTs","subtitle":"Watch two replicas accept writes while cut off from each other, then see why last-write-wins loses data and merge rules do not.","minutes":14,"level":"Advanced","learn":["Why multi-leader and offline-first systems must handle concurrent writes","How last-write-wins silently drops updates, and when that is acceptable","How a G-counter merges by taking the per-replica maximum","How an OR-set lets an add survive a concurrent remove","The idea behind collaborative text editing, and where CRDTs stop working"],"nodes":[{"id":"alice","label":"Alice's phone","kind":"client","x":15,"y":25},{"id":"ra","label":"Replica A","kind":"region","x":55,"y":25},{"id":"bob","label":"Bob's laptop","kind":"client","x":15,"y":75},{"id":"rb","label":"Replica B","kind":"region","x":55,"y":75}],"edges":[{"from":"alice","to":"ra"},{"from":"bob","to":"rb"},{"from":"ra","to":"rb","label":"sync link"}],"metrics":[{"id":"a","label":"Replica A","unit":"likes"},{"id":"b","label":"Replica B","unit":"likes"},{"id":"truth","label":"True total","unit":"likes"},{"id":"lost","label":"Lost updates","unit":"writes"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Two copies, one cable cut","body":"A post has 10 likes, stored on Replica A and Replica B so each user writes to a nearby copy. Now the link between them drops, or one user goes offline in a tunnel. Both replicas must keep accepting writes, because refusing them would make the app unusable. When the link returns, the copies will have drifted apart. What should the merged value be?","idea":"If replicas accept writes on their own, concurrent writes to the same data will happen. The only question is how you resolve them.","caption":"Both replicas start in sync at 10 likes.","focus":["ra","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"replicate","label":"10 likes"}],"chips":[{"node":"ra","text":"10 likes"},{"node":"rb","text":"10 likes"}],"metrics":{"a":"10","b":"10","truth":"10","lost":"0"}},{"tag":"CONFLICT","title":"Concurrent writes","body":"While cut off, Alice taps like once on Replica A, so A stores 11. Bob taps like twice on Replica B, so B stores 12. Neither write knew about the other. That is what concurrent means: neither happened before the other. The honest total is 13 (10 + 1 + 2), but each replica holds a different number and the sync link is down.","idea":"Concurrent means neither write had seen the other. It does not mean they happened at the same instant.","caption":"A shows 11, B shows 12, but the true total is 13.","focus":["alice","ra","bob","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"ra","kind":"request","label":"like +1"},{"from":"bob","to":"rb","kind":"request","label":"like +2"},{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"fail","label":"partitioned"}],"chips":[{"node":"ra","text":"11 likes"},{"node":"rb","text":"12 likes"},{"node":"ra","text":"partitioned","tone":"red","at":"edge:ra-rb"}],"metrics":{"a":"11","b":"12","truth":"13"}},{"tag":"LWW","title":"Last write wins","body":"The simplest rule is last-write-wins (LWW). Every write carries a timestamp, and on sync the newest timestamp overwrites the other copy. Here B wrote last, so both settle on 12. The copies agree, but Alice's like is gone and nobody is told. LWW is fine when only the latest value matters, such as a profile photo, a last-seen time or a cache entry. It also trusts wall clocks, which can disagree by tens of milliseconds or more, so the winner may not even be the truly later write (see Clocks & Ordering).","idea":"LWW always converges, but it throws away one side of every conflict. Use it only when losing a write is fine.","caption":"Sync keeps B's value, and Alice's like is lost.","focus":["ra","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"rb","to":"ra","kind":"replicate","label":"12 @ t=10:04"}],"chips":[{"node":"ra","text":"12 likes"},{"node":"rb","text":"12 likes"},{"node":"ra","text":"1 like lost","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"a":"12","b":"12","lost":"1"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Merge instead of overwrite","body":"A CRDT (conflict-free replicated data type) is a data structure built so any two copies can be merged without a coordinator. Its merge function needs three properties. Commutative: the order of the two inputs does not matter. Associative: how you group several merges does not matter. Idempotent: merging the same state twice changes nothing. (Taking the maximum of two numbers has all three.) Then replicas that receive the same updates end up identical, whatever the order and even with duplicates.","idea":"A CRDT turns conflict resolution into a math property of the merge function, so no write is arbitrarily discarded.","caption":"The replicas now swap their state and merge it.","focus":["ra","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"replicate","label":"state"},{"from":"rb","to":"ra","kind":"replicate","label":"state"}],"chips":[{"node":"ra","text":"commutative: any order"},{"node":"rb","text":"associative: grouping"},{"node":"ra","text":"idempotent: repeats ok","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"G-COUNTER","title":"A counter that cannot lose","body":"Instead of one number, a G-counter (grow-only counter) keeps one slot per replica. Start at {A:5, B:5}, total 10. Alice's like bumps A's slot to 6. Bob's two likes bump B's slot to 7. Each replica only ever writes its own slot. On sync, merge takes the maximum of each slot: {A:6, B:7}. The value is the sum of the slots, 13, which is the true total.","idea":"Give each replica its own slot and merge with max. The counter's value is the sum of the slots.","caption":"The slots merge to {A:6, B:7}, total 13.","focus":["ra","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"replicate","label":"{A:6,B:5}"},{"from":"rb","to":"ra","kind":"replicate","label":"{A:5,B:7}"}],"chips":[{"node":"ra","text":"{A:6, B:7}","tone":"green"},{"node":"rb","text":"{A:6, B:7}","tone":"green"},{"node":"ra","text":"sum = 13","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"a":"13","b":"13","truth":"13","lost":"0"}},{"tag":"IDEMPOTENT","title":"Duplicates and reordering are safe","body":"Networks retry, duplicate and reorder messages. Here Replica B receives A's state a second time, late, after it already merged it. Taking the max of values it already has changes nothing, so the total stays 13. This is why state-based CRDT sync can use simple at-least-once delivery (send until acknowledged, duplicates allowed) with no deduplication. Op-based CRDTs need more care about delivery order. A G-counter cannot go down. A PN-counter uses two G-counters, one for increments and one for decrements, and subtracts.","idea":"Idempotent merge means replaying old state is harmless, so state-based sync tolerates duplicates and reordering.","caption":"A duplicate state message changes nothing.","focus":["ra","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"replicate","label":"{A:6,B:5} again"}],"chips":[{"node":"rb","text":"no change · still 13","tone":"green"},{"node":"ra","text":"13 likes","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{"a":"13","b":"13","truth":"13"}},{"tag":"OR-SET","title":"A set with add and remove","body":"New example: a shared cart already holds milk, added earlier with tag #t1 and synced to both replicas. While cut off, Alice removes milk on A, which deletes tag #t1. Bob, unaware, adds milk again on B with a brand new tag #t9. An observed-remove set (OR-set) tags every add with a unique id, and a remove deletes only the tags the remover has seen. After the merge #t1 is gone but #t9 remains, so milk stays in the cart. This is called add-wins. (The likes tiles belong to the earlier example.)","idea":"Unique tags let a remove cancel only the adds it knew about, so a concurrent add survives.","caption":"The remove cancels the old tag, and the new add stays.","focus":["alice","ra","bob","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"ra","kind":"request","label":"remove #t1"},{"from":"bob","to":"rb","kind":"request","label":"add milk #t9"},{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"replicate","label":"remove #t1"},{"from":"rb","to":"ra","kind":"replicate","label":"milk {#t1,#t9}"}],"chips":[{"node":"ra","text":"removes #t1"},{"node":"rb","text":"adds #t9"},{"node":"rb","text":"add wins","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"COLLAB","title":"Collaborative text editing","body":"A shared document needs two people typing in the same sentence without overwriting each other. Sequence CRDTs, such as those in Yjs and Automerge, give every character a unique id and a position defined relative to its neighbours, not a plain index like \"character 5\". Concurrent inserts land in the same order on every replica, and deletes leave a tombstone (a marker where the character used to be). The cost is metadata that grows with edit history. An older approach, operational transformation, rewrites operations through a central server. Sequence CRDTs avoid that need.","idea":"Identify each character by a stable id instead of a shifting index, and concurrent edits can merge the same way everywhere.","caption":"Alice's and Bob's concurrent inserts merge into one document.","focus":["alice","ra","bob","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"ra","kind":"request","label":"insert 'big '"},{"from":"bob","to":"rb","kind":"request","label":"insert 'red '"},{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"replicate","label":"ops"},{"from":"rb","to":"ra","kind":"replicate","label":"ops"}],"chips":[{"node":"ra","text":"unique id per char"},{"node":"rb","text":"same order on all","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"What CRDTs cannot do","body":"Merge rules work when combining is meaningful: counters, sets, text, flags. They cannot enforce a global rule such as \"a balance must stay at or above zero\" or \"a username must be unique\". Two replicas can each approve a write that looks valid alone but is invalid together. Example: a balance of 100 allows an 80 withdrawal on A and another 80 withdrawal on B, but together they leave -60. Those cases need coordination, such as consensus or a single owner, and you pay latency for it. Bounded-counter designs hand each replica a budget in advance, which is coordination done ahead of time.","idea":"CRDTs give availability and convergence. Rules that span replicas still need coordination.","caption":"Two conflicting approvals cannot be merged away (tiles are from the likes example).","focus":["alice","ra","bob","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"ra","kind":"request","label":"withdraw 80"},{"from":"bob","to":"rb","kind":"request","label":"withdraw 80"},{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"fail","label":"balance -60"}],"chips":[{"node":"ra","text":"withdraw 80 ok"},{"node":"rb","text":"withdraw 80 ok"},{"node":"ra","text":"balance -60","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick the merge rule on purpose","body":"Concurrent writes are inevitable once replicas accept writes independently. LWW is simple but drops data and trusts clocks. CRDTs use a merge that is commutative, associative and idempotent, so every replica converges. G-counters take the per-slot max, OR-sets let adds beat concurrent removes, and sequence CRDTs merge text. Use them when losing writes is unacceptable and coordination costs too much.","idea":"Choose the conflict rule on purpose. The default of overwriting loses data.","caption":"Both replicas converge on 13 with nothing lost.","focus":["alice","ra","bob","rb"],"flows":[],"chips":[{"node":"ra","text":"13 likes","tone":"green"},{"node":"rb","text":"13 likes","tone":"green"},{"node":"rb","text":"0 lost writes","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"a":"13","b":"13","truth":"13","lost":"0"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two replicas of a like counter each receive a different update while partitioned. After sync using last-write-wins, what happens?","options":["Both updates are combined into the sum","One replica's update is discarded and the copies agree","The replicas stay different until an admin picks one","The older timestamp wins because it was first"],"answer":1,"why":"LWW keeps the value with the newest timestamp and overwrites the other, so the copies converge but one write is silently lost."},{"q":"A G-counter is at {A:6, B:5} on one replica and {A:5, B:7} on another. What does a merge produce?","options":["{A:5, B:5}, total 10","{A:11, B:12}, total 23","{A:6, B:5}, because it arrived first","{A:6, B:7}, total 13"],"answer":3,"why":"Merge takes the maximum of each replica's own slot, giving {A:6, B:7}. Adding slots would double count updates that both sides already saw."},{"q":"Why is it safe for CRDT sync to deliver the same state message twice?","options":["The network guarantees exactly-once delivery","Each message is encrypted with a nonce","The merge is idempotent, so reapplying known state changes nothing","Replicas ignore messages older than one minute"],"answer":2,"why":"Idempotence is part of the merge contract. Taking max or union with state you already have yields the same state."},{"q":"In an OR-set, one user removes an item while another concurrently adds the same item. Why does the item survive?","options":["The remove only deletes the unique tags it had observed, and the new add has a tag it never saw","Adds always have a later wall-clock time","The set locks the item during removal","Removes are ignored if the network is partitioned"],"answer":0,"why":"Each add gets a unique tag. A remove cancels only the tags it knew about, so a concurrent add with a fresh tag is preserved."},{"q":"You must guarantee a bank balance never drops below zero across replicas. Which statement is true?","options":["A PN-counter enforces the limit automatically","A CRDT alone cannot enforce it; you need coordination for that invariant","LWW with synchronized clocks enforces it","Use a G-counter for withdrawals"],"answer":1,"why":"Two replicas can each approve a withdrawal that is valid alone but overdraws together. Cross-replica invariants need coordination such as consensus or a single owner."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Concurrent writes","def":"Writes where neither had seen the other when it was made. They are not about exact simultaneity, only missing knowledge."},{"term":"Last-write-wins (LWW)","def":"Keep the write with the newest timestamp and drop the rest. Simple and convergent, but loses data and depends on clocks."},{"term":"CRDT","def":"A data type whose state merge is commutative, associative and idempotent (op-based variants have equivalent delivery rules), so replicas converge without a coordinator."},{"term":"G-counter","def":"Grow-only counter with one slot per replica. Merge is per-slot max; the value is the sum. Add a second one to make a PN-counter."},{"term":"OR-set","def":"Observed-remove set. Each add has a unique tag and a remove deletes only the tags it saw, so concurrent adds survive."},{"term":"State-based vs op-based","def":"State-based CRDTs ship full or delta state and merge it. Op-based ones ship operations and need reliable, causally ordered delivery."},{"term":"Tombstone","def":"A marker left in place of a deleted item so concurrent operations still resolve. It makes metadata grow until garbage collected."},{"term":"Where CRDTs fail","def":"Global invariants like non-negative balances or unique usernames still need coordination such as consensus."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"unique-ids","title":"Unique ID Generation","subtitle":"Watch several servers mint IDs at the same time, and see why a plain counter stops working and what replaces it.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why a database auto-increment counter becomes a bottleneck and breaks when you add shards","How UUIDv4 and UUIDv7 differ, and why random keys hurt database indexes","How a Snowflake ID packs time, machine and sequence into 64 bits","How a ticket server hands out ID ranges, and what happens when a clock jumps backward","What sortable IDs buy you, and what they leak"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Clients","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50,"url":"https://shop.example"},{"id":"appa","label":"App server A","kind":"service","x":36,"y":22},{"id":"appb","label":"App server B","kind":"service","x":36,"y":78},{"id":"ticket","label":"Ticket server","kind":"server","x":64,"y":12},{"id":"db","label":"Orders database","kind":"db","x":64,"y":50},{"id":"idgen","label":"Snowflake (per server)","kind":"worker","x":64,"y":88},{"id":"idx","label":"Primary-key index","kind":"storage","x":90,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"appa"},{"from":"client","to":"appb"},{"from":"appa","to":"db"},{"from":"appb","to":"db"},{"from":"appa","to":"ticket"},{"from":"appb","to":"ticket"},{"from":"appa","to":"idgen"},{"from":"appb","to":"idgen"},{"from":"db","to":"idx"}],"metrics":[{"id":"hops","label":"ID-source hops","unit":"per ID"},{"id":"size","label":"ID size","unit":"bytes"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Every row needs a name","body":"Every new order, message or user needs an ID that no other row will ever have, like a serial number. With one server, a simple counter is enough. But when many servers create rows at the same moment, who decides the next number? A good answer is unique, cheap to produce, and not a bottleneck or a single point of failure. The animation shows two servers that both need IDs right now.","idea":"An ID scheme is judged on uniqueness, speed, size, sortability, and whether it needs coordination.","caption":"Two servers both need IDs right now.","focus":["client","appa","appb"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"appa","kind":"request","label":"create order"},{"from":"client","to":"appb","kind":"request","label":"create order"}],"chips":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"AUTO-INC","title":"Let the database count","body":"The simplest scheme is a database auto-increment column (a sequence). The app inserts a row and the database hands back 1001, then 1002, and so on. IDs are small (8 bytes), keep increasing (with occasional gaps), and never collide. Use a 64-bit column, because a 32-bit INT overflows at about 2.1 billion rows. The catch: every insert goes through one database, which is one hop for every ID. A single primary can only take so many writes (rule of thumb: about 10,000 inserts per second).","idea":"Auto-increment is correct and compact, but one counter means one choke point.","caption":"The single database hands out the next number.","focus":["appa","db"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT order"},{"from":"db","to":"appa","kind":"response","label":"id = 1001"}],"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"id = 1001"},{"node":"appa","text":"8 bytes · 1 hop"},{"node":"db","text":"cap ≈ 10k inserts/s","at":"bottom"}],"metrics":{"hops":"1","size":"8"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Add a second writer","body":"Traffic grows, so you split the data across two databases (shards), and each keeps its own counter. Now both hand out 1001. The moment you merge, export, or reference rows across shards, the IDs collide. You can patch it by giving shard 1 the odd numbers and shard 2 the even ones, but a third shard means changing the scheme. (The database box is drawn once but stands for two shards.)","idea":"A counter only works while exactly one thing owns it. Sharding breaks that ownership.","caption":"Two shards, two counters: both issue 1001, so merged rows clash.","focus":["appa","appb","db"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"id 1001 (shard 1)"},{"from":"appb","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"id 1001 (shard 2)"}],"chips":[{"node":"appa","text":"id 1001 (shard 1)"},{"node":"appb","text":"id 1001 (shard 2)"},{"node":"db","text":"duplicate ID","tone":"red"}],"metrics":{"hops":"1","size":"8"}},{"tag":"UUID V4","title":"Roll the dice locally","body":"A UUIDv4 is a 128-bit ID in which 122 bits are random. Each server makes one on its own, with no coordination, millions per second. A collision is possible in theory but absurdly unlikely: after a billion IDs, the chance of any duplicate is around 1 in 10 quintillion. The price is size (16 bytes) and randomness. Random keys land all over the primary-key index (the sorted lookup structure for the table) instead of at one spot, so writes are scattered and slow.","idea":"UUIDv4 trades coordination for bigger size and index-unfriendly randomness.","caption":"Each server mints its own ID with no network call.","focus":["appa","appb","db"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT uuid4"},{"from":"appb","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT uuid4"}],"chips":[{"node":"appa","text":"16 bytes · 0 hops"},{"node":"db","text":"scattered writes","tone":"red"}],"metrics":{"hops":"0","size":"16"}},{"tag":"UUID V7","title":"Put time at the front","body":"UUIDv7 (standardized in RFC 9562, 2024) keeps the 128-bit shape but starts with a 48-bit Unix timestamp in milliseconds, followed by about 74 bits of random and counter data. There is still no coordination, and IDs created later sort later. In a B-tree index (the usual sorted index), new keys land at the right edge, so the busy pages stay in memory and page splits are rare. Ordering is only millisecond-accurate, and anyone who sees the ID can read the timestamp.","idea":"UUIDv7 keeps UUID's no-coordination property and adds time ordering, which indexes love.","caption":"Time-prefixed keys append at the edge of the index.","focus":["appa","db","idx"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT uuid7"},{"from":"db","to":"idx","kind":"async","label":"append at end"}],"chips":[{"node":"appa","text":"48-bit time prefix"},{"node":"idx","text":"append at end","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{"hops":"0","size":"16"}},{"tag":"SNOWFLAKE","title":"Time + machine + sequence","body":"A Snowflake-style ID fits in 64 bits: 1 unused sign bit, 41 bits of milliseconds since a custom start date (about 69 years), 10 bits for the worker number (1,024 workers), and a 12-bit sequence. Within one millisecond a worker counts 0 to 4095, so one worker can issue 4,096,000 IDs per second. As long as each worker has a unique number, no two machines can make the same ID, and no network call is needed. In the diagram the generator is one box, but in practice it is a small library inside each app server with its own worker number. (Run as a separate service, it would add one hop.)","idea":"Snowflake splits the ID into fields, so uniqueness comes from who and when, not from a shared counter.","caption":"Each app's local generator (drawn once) builds a sortable 64-bit ID.","focus":["appa","idgen"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"idgen","kind":"request","label":"next id"},{"from":"idgen","to":"appa","kind":"response","label":"64-bit id"}],"chips":[{"node":"idgen","text":"41+10+12 bits"},{"node":"idgen","text":"4,096,000 IDs/s","at":"bottom"},{"node":"appa","text":"0 hops · 8 bytes"}],"metrics":{"hops":"0","size":"8"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The clock goes backward","body":"Snowflake depends on the wall clock. If a clock correction (an NTP step, a leap second or a VM resume) moves time backward, even by 20 ms, the generator may repeat a timestamp it already used and issue a duplicate ID. A safe generator remembers the last timestamp it used, and after a restart must not start before it, so persist it or wait out the difference. If the clock is behind, it waits or refuses instead of guessing. Worker numbers must also be unique: assign them by a coordinator or config, never randomly.","idea":"Time-based IDs are only as safe as the clock and the uniqueness of worker numbers.","caption":"The generator refuses to issue IDs until the clock recovers.","focus":["appa","idgen"],"flows":[{"from":"idgen","to":"appa","kind":"fail","label":"clock moved back"}],"chips":[{"node":"idgen","text":"clock moved back","tone":"red"},{"node":"appa","text":"duplicate risk","tone":"red"}],"metrics":{"hops":"0","size":"8"}},{"tag":"TICKET SERVER","title":"Lease ranges of IDs","body":"A ticket server is a small dedicated service, historically a database table with auto-increment, that hands out numbers. Asking it for every ID would bring back the bottleneck. So each app asks for a block, say 1,000 IDs, and spends them locally. That is one hop per 1,000 IDs (0.001 per ID). Run two ticket servers, one issuing odd blocks and one even, so one can fail. IDs are small and mostly increasing. A crashed app loses its unused block, leaving harmless gaps.","idea":"Handing out ranges turns one coordination call into thousands of free local IDs.","caption":"App A leases a block of 1,000 IDs.","focus":["appa","ticket"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"ticket","kind":"request","label":"need 1000 ids"},{"from":"ticket","to":"appa","kind":"response","label":"10000-10999"}],"chips":[{"node":"ticket","text":"10000-10999 lease"},{"node":"appa","text":"1 hop per 1,000 IDs"}],"metrics":{"hops":"0.001","size":"8"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Why sortable matters","body":"Sortable IDs (UUIDv7, Snowflake, and to a lesser extent ticket blocks) allow cursor paging, such as WHERE id < last_seen ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 50, and keep inserts append-like. But order across machines is only approximate (same-millisecond ties, clock skew), so never treat it as true event order. IDs also leak creation time and volume. Counters and time-based IDs are guessable, so do not use them as secrets. Check access on every request, or expose a random public ID.","idea":"Sortable IDs make queries and indexes faster, but their order is approximate and creation time becomes visible.","caption":"Both servers append to the same edge of the index.","focus":["appa","appb","db","idx"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT id ...4101"},{"from":"appb","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT id ...4102"},{"from":"db","to":"idx","kind":"async","label":"append at end"}],"chips":[{"node":"appa","text":"id …4101"},{"node":"appb","text":"id …4102"},{"node":"idx","text":"append at end","tone":"green"}],"metrics":{"hops":"0","size":"8"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick by constraint","body":"Use auto-increment for a single database where simplicity wins. Use UUIDv7 when you want no coordination and standard tooling. Use Snowflake-style 64-bit IDs when size and throughput matter and you can manage worker numbers and clocks. Use ticket servers (ID blocks) when you want small numeric IDs with rare coordination. On large indexed tables, prefer UUIDv7 over UUIDv4 as a primary key, unless you need order-free values.","idea":"Every scheme trades coordination, size, ordering and clock trust. Choose the one whose weakness you can live with.","caption":"Five schemes, one trade-off each.","focus":["client","appa","appb","ticket","db","idgen","idx"],"flows":[],"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"UUIDv7: 16 bytes"},{"node":"idgen","text":"Snowflake: 8 bytes"},{"node":"ticket","text":"block: 1 hop per 1k"}],"metrics":{}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two database shards each auto-increment from 1, and you later merge their orders into one table. What goes wrong?","options":["Nothing, since each row has its own timestamp","The IDs become random and unsortable","Both shards produced the same IDs, so rows collide","Auto-increment IDs cannot exceed 32 bits"],"answer":2,"why":"Each counter is independent, so both shards issue 1001, 1002 and so on. Merged rows have duplicate keys, which is why a shared counter does not survive sharding."},{"q":"A Snowflake worker has a 12-bit sequence field and 1 ms timestamp resolution. What is the most IDs one worker can issue per second?","options":["1,024","40,960","65,536","4,096,000"],"answer":3,"why":"12 bits gives 4,096 values per millisecond, and there are 1,000 ms in a second, so 4,096 × 1,000 = 4,096,000. The 10-bit worker field (1,024) only sets how many workers exist."},{"q":"Why does inserting UUIDv7 keys usually perform better than UUIDv4 keys on a large B-tree primary-key index?","options":["New v7 keys land near the end of the index, so writes stay on a few hot pages","UUIDv7 is shorter than UUIDv4","UUIDv7 needs no unique check","UUIDv7 is generated by the database"],"answer":0,"why":"The leading timestamp makes keys increase over time, so inserts append at the right edge. Random v4 keys land on arbitrary pages, causing cache misses and page splits. Both are 16 bytes."},{"q":"A Snowflake generator notices the system clock is 20 ms behind the timestamp of the last ID it issued. What is the safest behavior?","options":["Use the current clock anyway and hope the sequence differs","Wait or refuse until the clock passes the last timestamp used","Pick a new random worker number","Reset the sequence to zero and continue"],"answer":1,"why":"Reusing an earlier millisecond risks repeating a timestamp, worker and sequence combination already issued, which would create a duplicate. Waiting for time to catch up preserves uniqueness."},{"q":"A ticket server hands each app a block of 1,000 IDs. What is the main benefit?","options":["One network call per 1,000 IDs","IDs are globally time-ordered","IDs are unguessable","No gaps even if an app crashes"],"answer":0,"why":"Apps spend a leased block locally, so coordination happens once per block. A crashed app loses its unused block, leaving gaps, and block order is not true time order."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Auto-increment","def":"A database counter. Small and ordered, but one writer owns it, so it caps throughput and breaks when you shard."},{"term":"UUID V4","def":"128 bits, 122 random. No coordination, collision odds negligible, but 16 bytes and random order that scatters index writes."},{"term":"UUID V7","def":"128 bits led by a 48-bit millisecond Unix timestamp plus random bits (RFC 9562). Time-sortable, index-friendly, no coordination."},{"term":"Snowflake ID","def":"64 bits: 41 time (ms), 10 worker, 12 sequence. About 4.1 million IDs per second per worker; needs unique worker numbers and sane clocks."},{"term":"Ticket server","def":"A small service that leases blocks of numbers to apps. Coordination happens once per block; run two with odd/even offsets for availability."},{"term":"K-sorted","def":"Roughly time-ordered: IDs sort by creation to within the clock and millisecond precision, not by exact event order across machines."},{"term":"Clock rollback","def":"The system clock moving backward after a sync. Generators must remember the last timestamp and wait or refuse rather than reuse it."},{"term":"Keyset pagination","def":"Paging with WHERE id < cursor ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT n. Fast with sortable IDs, no OFFSET scans."}],"isNew":false}]},{"id":"communication","title":"Communication","lessons":[{"slug":"apis","title":"API Styles: REST, RPC, GraphQL","subtitle":"Load one mobile order screen three different ways and see what each API style costs in round trips and bytes.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How REST models resources and why it leans on HTTP verbs and caching","Why gRPC and protobuf suit fast service-to-service calls","How GraphQL lets the client ask for exactly the fields it needs, and what that costs the server","Why cursor pagination beats offset pagination on large, changing data","How to evolve an API without breaking existing clients","A simple rule for choosing a style for a given caller"],"nodes":[{"id":"web","label":"Web client","kind":"client","x":10,"y":28},{"id":"mobile","label":"Mobile app","kind":"client","x":10,"y":75},{"id":"gw","label":"API gateway","kind":"gateway","x":34,"y":50},{"id":"orders","label":"Orders (REST)","kind":"service","x":62,"y":16},{"id":"inv","label":"Inventory (gRPC)","kind":"service","x":62,"y":84},{"id":"gql","label":"GraphQL server","kind":"server","x":62,"y":50},{"id":"db","label":"Data stores","kind":"db","x":88,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"web","to":"gw"},{"from":"mobile","to":"gw"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders"},{"from":"gw","to":"inv"},{"from":"gw","to":"gql"},{"from":"gql","to":"orders"},{"from":"gql","to":"inv"},{"from":"orders","to":"db"},{"from":"inv","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"trips","label":"Round trips","unit":"calls"},{"id":"payload","label":"Payload","unit":"KB"},{"id":"latency","label":"Screen load","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One screen, many needs","body":"A phone app needs to draw one order screen. It shows the order, the customer's name, and each item with its stock status. That data lives in two backend services: Orders and Inventory. How should the app ask for it? The best answer depends on who is calling and how far away they are. We assume the phone has a 60 ms round trip (RTT: the time for a request to go out and the answer to come back).","idea":"An API style is the agreed way a caller asks for data. The best style depends on who is calling.","caption":"The phone needs data held by two backend services.","focus":["mobile","gw","orders","inv"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[]},{"tag":"REST","title":"REST: resources and verbs","body":"REST treats each thing as a resource with its own URL. Verbs say what to do: GET reads, POST creates, PUT or PATCH changes, DELETE removes. Status codes report the result (200 OK, 404 not found, 409 conflict). In the animation the phone sends one GET /orders/812 and gets the order back: one round trip. Assume an 18 KB JSON reply and 10 ms of server time per call, so 60 + 10 = 70 ms. GET is safe to repeat, so browsers and CDNs can cache it. GET, PUT and DELETE are idempotent (repeating them changes nothing more). POST is not, so payment APIs add an idempotency key.","idea":"REST maps things to URLs and actions to HTTP verbs. That makes it easy to cache and easy to test with curl.","caption":"One GET fetches one order: 1 trip, 70 ms.","focus":["mobile","gw","orders","db"],"flows":[{"from":"mobile","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders/812"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders/812"},{"from":"orders","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"200 order JSON"},{"from":"gw","to":"mobile","kind":"response","label":"200 order JSON"}],"metrics":{"trips":"1","payload":"18","latency":"70"},"chips":[{"node":"mobile","text":"RTT 60 ms"},{"node":"orders","text":"~10 ms / call"},{"node":"mobile","text":"18 KB","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"REST's chattiness problem","body":"The order reply holds only ids for the customer and items. So the phone needs more calls: the order, then the customer, then the stock. Each waits for the last, costing 60 ms of network plus 10 ms of server time. That is 3 x 70 = 210 ms. Running the last two in parallel still takes 2 round trips. Replies also carry fields the screen never shows (over-fetching), so the total reaches about 40 KB (assumed). Needing extra calls is under-fetching. Here the Orders service also serves customer data.","idea":"A fixed-shape resource forces extra calls (under-fetching) or unused bytes (over-fetching) when a screen needs a custom view.","caption":"Three calls, one after another, to build one screen.","focus":["mobile","gw","orders","inv"],"flows":[{"from":"mobile","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders/812"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"order"},{"from":"orders","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"order JSON"},{"from":"gw","to":"mobile","kind":"response","label":"order JSON"},{"from":"mobile","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /customers/9"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"customer"},{"from":"orders","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"customer JSON"},{"from":"gw","to":"mobile","kind":"response","label":"customer JSON"},{"from":"mobile","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /stock?ids"},{"from":"gw","to":"inv","kind":"request","label":"stock"},{"from":"inv","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"stock JSON"},{"from":"gw","to":"mobile","kind":"response","label":"stock JSON"}],"metrics":{"trips":"3","payload":"40","latency":"210"},"chips":[{"node":"mobile","text":"3 round trips","tone":"red"},{"node":"mobile","text":"≈ 210 ms","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"mobile","text":"40 KB","tone":"red","at":"top"}]},{"tag":"RPC","title":"gRPC: fast calls between services","body":"Inside the data center, the gateway talks to Inventory with gRPC, a remote procedure call (RPC) framework. RPC means calling a function on another machine as if it were local. A .proto file defines the methods and typed messages. Protobuf packs them into compact binary, often 3-10x smaller than the same JSON (a rule of thumb). Many calls share one HTTP/2 connection. The animation shows one fast hop: 12 ms and a 5 KB reply. Two catches: browsers cannot call gRPC without a proxy, and the shared long-lived connection needs L7 or client-side load balancing.","idea":"gRPC gives up readable text and direct browser access. In return it is fast and strictly typed, which suits internal calls.","caption":"The gateway asks Inventory for stock over binary gRPC.","focus":["gw","inv","db"],"flows":[{"from":"gw","to":"inv","kind":"request","label":"GetStock(ids)"},{"from":"inv","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT stock"},{"from":"db","to":"inv","kind":"response","label":"rows"},{"from":"inv","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"StockReply"}],"metrics":{"trips":"1","payload":"5","latency":"12"},"chips":[{"node":"gw","text":"gRPC · HTTP/2"},{"node":"inv","text":"12 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"inv","text":"3-10x smaller","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"GRAPHQL","title":"GraphQL: ask for exactly what you need","body":"With GraphQL the phone sends one query to a single endpoint. The query names the exact fields it wants: order id, customer name, and each item's name and in-stock flag. The GraphQL server collects them from Orders and Inventory (a fan-out: one request splits into several), then returns only what was asked for. One round trip carries about 6 KB. Assume the fan-out takes 30 ms, so the screen loads in 60 + 30 = 90 ms instead of 210 ms.","idea":"GraphQL lets the client choose the response shape, so many calls collapse into one precise reply.","caption":"One query in; the server fans out to Orders and Inventory.","focus":["mobile","gw","gql","orders","inv"],"flows":[{"from":"mobile","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"POST /graphql"},{"from":"gw","to":"gql","kind":"request","label":"query { order }"},{"from":"gql","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"order, customer"},{"from":"gql","to":"inv","kind":"request","label":"stock"},{"from":"orders","to":"gql","kind":"response","label":"order, customer"},{"from":"inv","to":"gql","kind":"response","label":"stock"},{"from":"gql","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"6 KB JSON"},{"from":"gw","to":"mobile","kind":"response","label":"6 KB JSON"}],"metrics":{"trips":"1","payload":"6","latency":"90"},"chips":[{"node":"mobile","text":"1 round trip","tone":"green"},{"node":"gql","text":"~30 ms fan-out"},{"node":"mobile","text":"6 KB","at":"bottom"},{"node":"mobile","text":"≈ 90 ms","tone":"green","at":"top"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The hidden costs of GraphQL","body":"That freedom moves work onto the server. Say a client asks for 100 orders, each with 10 items, each with stock. A naive server makes 1 call for the orders, 100 for their items and 1,000 for stock: over 1,100 backend calls from one query. This is the N+1 problem (one call for the list, then one more per item). Fix it by batching lookups. Queries are usually POSTs to one URL, so normal HTTP caching mostly does not apply. Teams cache by object id, or use persisted queries over GET. Add depth and cost limits so one query cannot overload the system.","idea":"GraphQL needs batching, query-cost limits and its own caching. The server pays for the client's freedom.","caption":"One deep query turns into over a thousand backend calls.","focus":["gql","orders","inv"],"flows":[{"from":"gql","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"items of order 1"},{"from":"gql","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"items of order 2"},{"from":"gql","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"...x100"},{"from":"gql","to":"inv","kind":"request","label":"stock of item 1"},{"from":"gql","to":"inv","kind":"request","label":"...x1000"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"gql","text":"N+1","tone":"red"},{"node":"orders","text":"100 calls","tone":"red"},{"node":"inv","text":"1,000 calls","tone":"red"},{"node":"gql","text":"POST · no HTTP cache","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"PAGINATION","title":"Listing without loading everything","body":"Any list endpoint needs pagination, or it would return every row at once. Offset style (?limit=50&offset=100000) is simple, but the database must skip 100,000 rows each time. If rows are inserted meanwhile, pages shift and items repeat or vanish. Cursor style (?limit=50&after=ord_7f3a) resumes after the last row you saw. The cursor is an opaque token holding that row's sort key (for example created_at plus id), so the database jumps straight there using an index. Cost stays flat and pages stay stable. The trade-off: you can go next or previous, but not jump to page 40.","idea":"Use cursor pagination for large or changing lists. Keep offset for small, stable data.","caption":"Orders returns 50 rows plus a cursor for the next page.","focus":["web","gw","orders","db"],"flows":[{"from":"web","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders?after"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"after=c1"},{"from":"orders","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"id > c1 LIMIT 50"},{"from":"db","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"50 rows"},{"from":"orders","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"50 + next cursor"},{"from":"gw","to":"web","kind":"response","label":"50 + next cursor"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"LIMIT 50"},{"node":"gw","text":"next cursor"},{"node":"db","text":"flat cost","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"VERSIONING","title":"Changing an API without breaking callers","body":"Old app versions stay installed for years, so you cannot just change a response. Safe changes are additive: new optional fields or new endpoints. Breaking changes (rename, remove, change a type) need a new version, such as /v2/orders or a version header. Keep the old one until few people use it. The animation shows the old phone app on v1 and the web app on v2, both served at once. Protobuf lets you add fields but never reuse a field number. GraphQL marks fields deprecated, then removes them later.","idea":"Change APIs by adding, not breaking. When a break is unavoidable, run old and new versions side by side.","caption":"Old and new clients call different versions at the same time.","focus":["web","mobile","gw","orders"],"flows":[{"from":"mobile","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /v1/orders/812"},{"from":"web","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /v2/orders/812"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"v1 and v2"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"mobile","text":"v1"},{"node":"web","text":"v2"},{"node":"orders","text":"v1 + v2 live","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Choosing a style","body":"Use REST for public, cacheable APIs that many unknown clients call. Use gRPC between your own services, where speed and strict contracts matter. Use GraphQL when several client types (phone, web) need different views of connected data. Many systems mix all three behind a gateway. Page lists with cursors and change APIs additively. A purpose-built endpoint (a backend-for-frontend) can also give a one-trip result without GraphQL.","idea":"Pick by caller: REST for the public, gRPC for internal hops, GraphQL for flexible client views.","caption":"Each style fits a different caller.","focus":["web","mobile","gw","orders","inv","gql"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"trips":"1","payload":"6","latency":"90"},"chips":[{"node":"mobile","text":"1 round trip","tone":"green"},{"node":"mobile","text":"≈ 90 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A phone on a 100 ms RTT network needs data that takes three dependent REST calls. What is the most direct fix for load time?","options":["Compress the JSON with gzip","Switch the calls from GET to POST","Return the combined data in one call, for example via a GraphQL query or a purpose-built endpoint","Add more database replicas"],"answer":2,"why":"Dependent calls cost one RTT each, about 300 ms here. Cutting the number of round trips helps far more than shrinking bytes."},{"q":"Why does gRPC suit calls between your own backend services better than a public browser-facing API?","options":["gRPC uses compact binary messages, HTTP/2 multiplexing, and typed generated clients, while browsers lack direct support","gRPC works natively in every browser","gRPC responses are cached by CDNs automatically","gRPC removes the need for a schema"],"answer":0,"why":"Binary protobuf and HTTP/2 reduce size and latency, and the .proto contract gives strict types. Browsers need a proxy, so public APIs usually stay REST or GraphQL."},{"q":"A GraphQL endpoint slows down after clients start requesting orders with nested items and reviews. Which is the likely cause and remedy?","options":["HTTP verbs are wrong; switch to PUT","The schema is too small; add more types","Protobuf encoding is too large","N+1 resolver calls; batch lookups and add query depth or cost limits"],"answer":3,"why":"Nested fields can trigger one backend call per parent item. Batching (such as a data loader) and cost limits keep fan-out under control."},{"q":"A feed is paged with ?limit=20&offset=. While a user reads page 2, ten new posts are inserted at the top. What happens when they load page 3?","options":["Nothing; offsets are stable","Some posts from page 2 appear again, because everything shifted down","Ten posts are skipped","The server returns an error"],"answer":1,"why":"Inserts at the front shift every later row, so offset-based pages repeat items. A cursor anchored to the last seen id avoids this."},{"q":"You must rename the field total to totalCents in a REST response used by apps you cannot force to update. What is the safest approach?","options":["Rename it in place and announce the change","Delete total and return an error to old clients","Add totalCents alongside total, keep both, and remove total only after old clients are gone or in a new version","Change the field's type but keep its name"],"answer":2,"why":"Additive changes do not break existing callers. Removal or rename is a breaking change that needs a transition period or a new version."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"REST","def":"Resources at URLs, acted on with HTTP verbs and status codes. Easy to cache and debug; fixed response shapes can cause over- or under-fetching."},{"term":"gRPC / protobuf","def":"RPC over HTTP/2 with a .proto contract and compact binary messages. Fast and strictly typed; best for internal service calls."},{"term":"GraphQL","def":"One endpoint where the client sends a query naming exact fields. Fewer round trips; needs batching, cost limits, and id-based caching."},{"term":"Over/under-fetching","def":"Over-fetching returns unused fields; under-fetching forces extra calls because one response lacks needed data."},{"term":"N+1 problem","def":"One query for a list plus one extra call per item. Fix by batching lookups or joining."},{"term":"Cursor pagination","def":"Resume from the last seen key (?after=id). Stable under inserts and constant cost, unlike large offsets."},{"term":"Additive change","def":"Adding optional fields or endpoints. Does not break old clients; renames, removals, and type changes do."},{"term":"Idempotent","def":"Repeating the call has the same effect as doing it once. GET, PUT, and DELETE should be; POST usually is not, so use idempotency keys for payments."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"realtime","title":"Real-Time: Polling, SSE, WebSockets","subtitle":"Watch one chat message reach a user who never asked for it, using four different ways to let a server speak first.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why plain HTTP cannot push, and how short and long polling fake it","How Server-Sent Events and WebSockets keep one connection open","When to pick polling, SSE or WebSockets","Why connection gateways are stateful and what that does to scaling","How a message crosses gateways through a pub/sub bus, and what happens when a gateway dies"],"nodes":[{"id":"alice","label":"Alice (sender)","kind":"client","x":10,"y":24},{"id":"bob","label":"Bob (receiver)","kind":"client","x":10,"y":78},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":30,"y":50},{"id":"gw1","label":"Gateway A","kind":"gateway","x":51,"y":24},{"id":"gw2","label":"Gateway B","kind":"gateway","x":51,"y":78},{"id":"bus","label":"Pub/sub bus","kind":"queue","x":72,"y":50},{"id":"chat","label":"Chat service","kind":"service","x":90,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"alice","to":"lb"},{"from":"bob","to":"lb"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw1"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw2"},{"from":"gw1","to":"bus"},{"from":"gw2","to":"bus"},{"from":"bus","to":"chat"},{"from":"gw1","to":"chat"}],"metrics":[{"id":"reqs","label":"Requests/s","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"conns","label":"Open conns","unit":"conns"},{"id":"delay","label":"Avg delay","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"The server has news, the client is silent","body":"Alice sends Bob a chat message. The server now has news for Bob, but Bob's app has not asked for anything. Plain HTTP only works one way: the client asks and the server answers. A server cannot start the conversation. So how does Bob's screen update without him refreshing? In this lesson we assume 100,000 connected users.","idea":"Plain HTTP is ask-then-answer, so real-time needs a trick or a different protocol.","caption":"Bob is idle; the news is waiting on the server.","focus":["alice","bob"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"reqs":"0","conns":"0"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"SHORT POLL","title":"Ask again every few seconds","body":"The simplest fix is short polling: Bob's app asks 'anything new?' every 5 seconds. It works with any server and cache. The cost is waste. 100,000 users polling every 5 s is 20,000 requests per second, and almost all come back empty. A new message also waits half the interval on average, about 2.5 s, before Bob sees it. Each poll is a short-lived HTTP request, not a held-open connection.","idea":"Short polling is very simple, but it wastes requests and delays messages by half the poll interval on average.","caption":"Most polls come back empty.","focus":["bob","lb","gw2"],"flows":[{"from":"bob","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"any news?"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw2","kind":"request","label":"any news?"},{"from":"gw2","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"nothing"},{"from":"lb","to":"bob","kind":"response","label":"empty 200"}],"metrics":{"reqs":"20000","delay":"2500"},"chips":[{"node":"bob","text":"poll every 5 s"},{"node":"lb","text":"20,000 req/s","tone":"red"},{"node":"bob","text":"~2.5 s delay","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"LONG POLL","title":"Ask, and the server waits","body":"Long polling cuts the waste. If there is nothing new, the server does not answer. It holds the request open, often up to 30 seconds, and replies the moment a message arrives. Then Bob's app asks again. A message could slip through the gap between requests, so each request carries the id of the last message seen and the server replays anything newer. Delivery takes about one network trip (we assume 10 ms). Idle traffic drops to about 100,000 / 30 = 3,300 requests per second, but each message still costs a fresh request.","idea":"Long polling delivers almost instantly over plain HTTP, by parking the request until there is news.","caption":"The request is held, then answered the moment news arrives.","focus":["bob","lb","gw2"],"flows":[{"from":"bob","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"wait for news"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw2","kind":"request","label":"held up to 30 s"},{"from":"gw2","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"new message"},{"from":"lb","to":"bob","kind":"response","label":"new message"}],"metrics":{"reqs":"3333","conns":"100000","delay":"10"},"chips":[{"node":"gw2","text":"held ≤ 30 s"},{"node":"lb","text":"3,300 req/s"},{"node":"bob","text":"delay ≈ 10 ms","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"SSE","title":"Server-Sent Events: one long download","body":"Server-Sent Events (SSE) make that held response permanent. Bob opens one GET with Accept: text/event-stream. The server never finishes the reply and keeps writing small text events into it. The browser's EventSource reconnects by itself and sends Last-Event-ID so the server can resume. SSE is one-way (server to client) and text-only, which suits feeds and notifications. Bob can still send data with normal POSTs. Notes: EventSource cannot set headers, so authenticate with a cookie or short-lived token. Use HTTP/2, since HTTP/1.1 browsers allow only about 6 connections per site. Make sure proxies do not buffer the stream.","idea":"SSE is a one-way push stream over ordinary HTTP, with automatic reconnect and resume built in.","caption":"One open response; events flow down it.","focus":["bob","lb","gw2"],"flows":[{"from":"bob","to":"lb","kind":"handshake","label":"GET /events"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw2","kind":"handshake","label":"stream opens"},{"from":"gw2","to":"lb","kind":"async","label":"event: msg"},{"from":"lb","to":"bob","kind":"async","label":"event: msg"}],"metrics":{"reqs":"0","conns":"100000","delay":"10"},"chips":[{"node":"bob","text":"1 GET, stays open"},{"node":"gw2","text":"~50,000 conns"},{"node":"bob","text":"auto-reconnect","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WEBSOCKET","title":"WebSockets: a two-way pipe","body":"A WebSocket starts as a normal HTTP request with an Upgrade header. The server answers 101 Switching Protocols, and the same TCP connection becomes a two-way channel (full-duplex). Either side can now send small frames at any time, each with only a 2 to 14 byte header. In the animation Bob sends a 'typing' frame up and a 'new msg' frame comes down. Delivery is as fast as SSE, about one network trip. The gain is two-way traffic. Unlike EventSource, the browser WebSocket API does not reconnect or resume for you, so your code must. It suits chat, games and shared editing.","idea":"A WebSocket upgrades one HTTP connection into a two-way message channel with tiny per-message overhead.","caption":"Upgrade handshake first, then frames flow both ways.","focus":["bob","lb","gw2"],"flows":[{"from":"bob","to":"lb","kind":"handshake","label":"Upgrade: websocket"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw2","kind":"handshake","label":"Upgrade"},{"from":"gw2","to":"lb","kind":"handshake","label":"101 Switching"},{"from":"lb","to":"bob","kind":"handshake","label":"101 Switching"},{"from":"bob","to":"lb","kind":"async","label":"frame: typing"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw2","kind":"async","label":"frame: typing"},{"from":"gw2","to":"lb","kind":"async","label":"frame: new msg"},{"from":"lb","to":"bob","kind":"async","label":"frame: new msg"}],"metrics":{"reqs":"0","conns":"100000","delay":"10"},"chips":[{"node":"gw2","text":"101 Switching","tone":"green"},{"node":"bob","text":"2-14 B header"},{"node":"bob","text":"two-way","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"The gateway remembers who is connected","body":"Open connections make servers stateful. In the animation, Bob's socket lives in the memory of one process, Gateway B, and a message can reach Bob only through it. Each idle connection costs roughly 10 to 50 KB of memory (a rule of thumb), so 50,000 connections on one gateway is only 0.5 to 2.5 GB. We assume the 100,000 users are split over two gateways, about 50,000 each. To scale, add gateways. A load balancer only picks a gateway when a connection first opens, so use least-connections routing, and expect a new gateway to start empty.","idea":"A connection lives on one specific gateway, so a message cannot be sent from just any server.","caption":"Bob is attached to Gateway B only.","focus":["bob","gw2"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"gw2","text":"holds Bob's socket"},{"node":"gw2","text":"10-50 KB / conn","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FAN-OUT","title":"Delivering across gateways","body":"Alice is on Gateway A and Bob on Gateway B. A gateway is the server process that holds client connections. In the animation Alice's message reaches Gateway A, which has the chat service save it first. Then Gateway A publishes it to a pub/sub bus, a shared channel every gateway listens to. The bus gives Gateway B a copy, and B writes it down Bob's socket. Why save first? Simple buses such as Redis Pub/Sub are often at-most-once (a missed message is gone) and keep no replay. A client that reconnects catches up from the saved store.","idea":"Save first, then publish. The bus is the fast path, and the store covers anything the bus loses.","caption":"Save first, then Gateway A to bus to Gateway B to Bob.","focus":["alice","gw1","bus","gw2","bob","chat"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"lb","kind":"async","label":"send frame"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw1","kind":"async","label":"send frame"},{"from":"gw1","to":"chat","kind":"async","label":"save first"},{"from":"gw1","to":"bus","kind":"async","label":"publish room:42"},{"from":"bus","to":"gw2","kind":"async","label":"room:42"},{"from":"gw2","to":"lb","kind":"async","label":"frame"},{"from":"lb","to":"bob","kind":"async","label":"new message"}],"metrics":{"delay":"30"},"chips":[{"node":"chat","text":"saved first","tone":"green"},{"node":"bus","text":"room:42"},{"node":"gw2","text":"Bob's socket"},{"node":"bob","text":"delay 30 ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A gateway dies","body":"Gateway B crashes or is redeployed, and its 50,000 sockets drop at the same instant. If they all reconnect within about a second, that is a burst of 50,000 requests per second. This is a thundering herd: the surviving gateway queues them and delay climbs toward a second. The fix is on the client. Wait a random, growing delay before each retry (exponential backoff with jitter), then send the last message id seen so the server can resume. For planned deploys, drain first: stop accepting new connections, then close old ones gradually.","idea":"Connections are fragile. Clients must reconnect after random delays and resume from the last message id.","caption":"Gateway B fails and every client rushes to reconnect.","focus":["gw2","lb","bob"],"flows":[{"from":"lb","to":"gw2","kind":"fail","label":"crash"},{"from":"bob","to":"lb","kind":"handshake","label":"reconnect, last id"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw1","kind":"handshake","label":"resume"}],"metrics":{"reqs":"50000","conns":"50000","delay":"800"},"chips":[{"node":"gw2","text":"crashed","tone":"red"},{"node":"gw2","text":"50,000 sockets lost","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"lb","text":"50,000 req/s","tone":"red"},{"node":"bob","text":"delay ~800 ms","tone":"red","at":"top"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Choosing the tool","body":"Pick the simplest tool that fits the direction of traffic. Short polling suits rare updates where caching helps, such as a job-status check. SSE suits one-way streams: notifications, prices, live logs. WebSockets suit cases where the client sends often and speed matters: chat, games, shared cursors. Whatever you pick, send a heartbeat (a tiny 'still here' message) every 20 to 30 seconds. Proxies and load balancers cut quiet connections, often after 60 s.","idea":"Choose by direction of traffic, and always send heartbeats.","caption":"Match the transport to the direction of traffic.","focus":["bob","lb","gw1","gw2"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"reqs":"0","conns":"100000","delay":"30"},"chips":[{"node":"lb","text":"heartbeat 20-30 s"},{"node":"lb","text":"idle cut ~60 s","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"From asking to being told","body":"HTTP cannot push, so short polling asks often and long polling parks the question. SSE keeps a one-way stream open, and WebSockets give a two-way channel. Open connections pin each user to one gateway, so messages cross gateways through a pub/sub bus, and clients must reconnect with backoff and resume. Durable queues and event logs, which can back this bus, have their own lessons.","idea":"Real-time is a held-open connection plus a way to route each message to the gateway that owns it.","caption":"Connections at the edge, a bus in the middle.","focus":["alice","bob","lb","gw1","gw2","bus","chat"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"reqs":"0","conns":"100000","delay":"30"},"chips":[{"node":"bob","text":"delay 30 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"gw2","text":"~50,000 conns"},{"node":"bus","text":"pub/sub"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"100,000 users short-poll every 10 seconds. Roughly how many requests per second does the server see, and what is the average delivery delay?","options":["About 1,000 req/s and 10 s delay","About 10,000 req/s and 5 s delay","About 10,000 req/s and 10 s delay","About 100,000 req/s and 5 s delay"],"answer":1,"why":"100,000 / 10 s = 10,000 requests per second. A message arrives at a random point in the interval, so the average wait is half of it, 5 s."},{"q":"A live stock ticker only sends prices from server to browser and must reconnect automatically after Wi-Fi drops. Which is the best fit?","options":["Short polling every second","WebSockets with custom reconnect code","Server-Sent Events","Long polling with a 5 s timeout"],"answer":2,"why":"Traffic is one-way, which is exactly what SSE does. EventSource reconnects and resumes with Last-Event-ID built in, so WebSockets add complexity without a benefit."},{"q":"Alice is on Gateway A and Bob on Gateway B. Why can't Gateway A just write to Bob directly?","options":["Bob's socket exists only in Gateway B's memory","WebSocket frames cannot carry text","The load balancer blocks gateway-to-gateway traffic","Gateways may only talk to the database"],"answer":0,"why":"A connection is held by one process. Gateway A has no handle for it, so the message must reach Gateway B, usually through a pub/sub bus."},{"q":"A gateway holding 50,000 sockets restarts and all clients reconnect instantly, overloading the other gateways. What is the standard defence?","options":["Close all sockets after 5 seconds","Switch every client to short polling","Add a second load balancer","Reconnect with exponential backoff plus random jitter"],"answer":3,"why":"Jitter spreads reconnects over time and backoff slows repeated tries, so the herd arrives gradually instead of as one spike."},{"q":"A WebSocket works on your laptop but drops after exactly 60 seconds of silence in production. The most likely cause?","options":["Browsers cap WebSocket age at 60 s","An idle timeout on a proxy or load balancer closing quiet connections","The server ran out of ports","TLS certificates expire every minute"],"answer":1,"why":"Intermediaries close connections that carry no traffic. Periodic ping or heartbeat frames every 20 to 30 seconds keep them open."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Short polling","def":"Client asks on a timer. Simple and cache-friendly, but wasteful when idle, and delay averages half the interval."},{"term":"Long polling","def":"Server holds the request until data exists or a timeout (about 30 s), then client re-asks. Low delay over plain HTTP."},{"term":"SSE","def":"One long HTTP response of text events, server to client only. Auto-reconnect and Last-Event-ID resume are built into EventSource."},{"term":"WebSocket","def":"HTTP Upgrade (101) turns the connection into a full-duplex frame channel. Best when both sides send often."},{"term":"Stateful gateway","def":"A server that holds open sockets in memory. Needs a way to route messages to the gateway owning each user."},{"term":"Fan-out via pub/sub","def":"Gateways subscribe to channels for their users; a publisher reaches every subscribed gateway without knowing who is where."},{"term":"Backoff with jitter","def":"Retry after a random, growing delay so mass reconnects do not stampede. Pair with a last-seen id to resume."},{"term":"Heartbeat","def":"Small periodic ping (every 20-30 s) that stops proxy idle timeouts and reveals dead peers early."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"queues","title":"Message Queues","subtitle":"Watch a checkout hand work to a queue, survive a traffic spike and a worker crash, and park a poison message.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How a queue decouples producers from consumers and absorbs bursts","What at-least-once delivery means and why duplicates happen","How acknowledgements and the visibility timeout recover from crashed workers","Why consumers must be idempotent","What a dead-letter queue is for and when messages end up there"],"nodes":[{"id":"producer","label":"Checkout service","kind":"service","x":10,"y":52},{"id":"queue","label":"Email queue","kind":"queue","x":38,"y":52},{"id":"w1","label":"Worker A","kind":"worker","x":66,"y":20},{"id":"w2","label":"Worker B","kind":"worker","x":66,"y":52},{"id":"dlq","label":"Dead-letter queue","kind":"queue","x":66,"y":84},{"id":"mail","label":"Email provider","kind":"external","x":90,"y":36}],"edges":[{"from":"producer","to":"queue"},{"from":"queue","to":"w1"},{"from":"queue","to":"w2"},{"from":"w1","to":"mail"},{"from":"w2","to":"mail"},{"from":"queue","to":"dlq"}],"metrics":[{"id":"depth","label":"Queue depth","unit":"msgs"},{"id":"inflight","label":"In flight","unit":"msgs"},{"id":"sent","label":"Emails sent","unit":"emails"},{"id":"dead","label":"Dead letters","unit":"msgs"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Don't make checkout wait","body":"After a purchase, the checkout service must email a receipt. If checkout calls the email provider directly, the customer waits for that call (often 300 ms to 2 s). Worse, if the provider is down, the order fails just because of an email. But a receipt can arrive a few seconds later. So why should checkout wait for it?","idea":"Work that does not need to finish before you reply should not hold up the reply.","caption":"Checkout is tied to the slow email provider.","focus":["producer","mail"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"depth":"0","inflight":"0","sent":"0","dead":"0"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Hand the job to a queue","body":"Instead, checkout writes a small message such as 'send receipt for order 81' to a queue. The queue confirms it has stored the message, usually within a few milliseconds. The service that writes the message is the producer. Checkout can now reply to the customer right away, even if no email worker is running. Think of the queue as a to-do tray: the producer drops a note in, and workers pick notes up when they are free.","idea":"A queue keeps messages (durably, if set to persist) until a worker is ready to handle them.","caption":"Checkout drops a message in the queue and gets 'stored'.","focus":["producer","queue"],"flows":[{"from":"producer","to":"queue","kind":"request","label":"send receipt #81"},{"from":"queue","to":"producer","kind":"response","label":"stored"}],"metrics":{"depth":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"producer","text":"done in ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"queue","text":"depth 1"}]},{"tag":"BUFFER","title":"Absorb a spike","body":"A flash sale sends 300 orders per second for 10 seconds: 3,000 messages. Assume each worker sends about 40 emails per second, so two workers manage 80 per second. In 10 seconds they send 800, so about 2,200 messages are left waiting. At 80 per second the backlog drains in about 27 s, roughly half a minute. Without the queue, checkout would have to wait on, or fail against, an email system sized for 300 per second.","idea":"A queue turns a short burst into a longer, steady flow that the workers can handle.","caption":"Depth climbs during the burst, then drains.","focus":["producer","queue","w1","w2"],"flows":[{"from":"producer","to":"queue","kind":"request","label":"300 msgs/s x 10s"}],"metrics":{"depth":"2200","sent":"800"},"chips":[{"node":"queue","text":"2,200 waiting"},{"node":"w1","text":"40 / s"},{"node":"w2","text":"40 / s"},{"node":"queue","text":"drains in ~30 s","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"DELIVER","title":"Receive hides the message","body":"Worker A asks the queue for a message. The queue does not delete it. It hides it from other workers for a set time, called the visibility timeout (30 seconds here), and counts one delivery attempt. The message is lent to Worker A, not given away. Meanwhile Worker B receives different messages, so the work is shared. In the HUD, one message moves from waiting to in flight.","idea":"Receiving only lends a message. It stays in the queue until a worker acknowledges it.","caption":"Worker A holds a 30-second lease on one message.","focus":["queue","w1","w2"],"flows":[{"from":"w1","to":"queue","kind":"request","label":"receive"},{"from":"queue","to":"w1","kind":"response","label":"msg + 30s lease"}],"metrics":{"depth":"2199","inflight":"1","sent":"800"},"chips":[{"node":"w1","text":"lease 30 s"},{"node":"queue","text":"1 in flight"},{"node":"queue","text":"2,199 waiting","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"ACK","title":"Acknowledge on success","body":"Worker A calls the email provider and gets a success reply. Only then does it send an acknowledgement (ack) to the queue, and the ack deletes the message for good. The order matters: ack after the work is done, never before. If the worker acked first and died a moment later, the email would be lost, with no copy left to retry.","idea":"An ack means 'done, delete it'. Send it only after the work really succeeded.","caption":"Email sent first, then the message is deleted.","focus":["w1","mail","queue"],"flows":[{"from":"w1","to":"mail","kind":"request","label":"send email"},{"from":"mail","to":"w1","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"},{"from":"w1","to":"queue","kind":"response","label":"ack (delete)"}],"metrics":{"inflight":"0","sent":"801"},"chips":[{"node":"mail","text":"200 OK","tone":"green"},{"node":"queue","text":"acked · deleted","tone":"green"},{"node":"w1","text":"sent 801","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A worker crashes mid-job","body":"Now Worker A sends an email but crashes before it can ack. The queue never hears back. After the 30 second visibility timeout, the message becomes visible again and Worker B receives it as attempt 2. Nothing is lost, which is the good news. The bad news: Worker B sends the email again, so the customer gets two. The sent counter shows it: 801, then 802 (A), then 803 (B's duplicate).","idea":"At-least-once delivery means no message is lost, but a message may be handled twice.","caption":"No ack arrives, so the message is delivered again.","focus":["w1","queue","w2","mail"],"flows":[{"from":"w1","to":"mail","kind":"request","label":"send email"},{"from":"w1","to":"queue","kind":"fail","label":"crash, no ack"},{"from":"w2","to":"queue","kind":"request","label":"receive (try 2)"},{"from":"queue","to":"w2","kind":"response","label":"msg, try 2"},{"from":"w2","to":"mail","kind":"request","label":"send email"}],"metrics":{"depth":"2198","inflight":"1","sent":"803"},"chips":[{"node":"w1","text":"crashed","tone":"red"},{"node":"queue","text":"timeout 30 s"},{"node":"w2","text":"try 2"},{"node":"mail","text":"duplicate email","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Make consumers idempotent","body":"A plain queue cannot promise exactly-once handling, so make handling twice harmless. This is called idempotency. Replay the same crash with a fix: Worker B sends the email with a key such as 'receipt-order-81', and the provider ignores the repeat, so the count stays at 802. If the provider has no such key, check a table of processed keys first. That narrows the gap but cannot be perfectly atomic, so a rare duplicate remains, which is fine for a receipt. Also set the visibility timeout above your slowest normal job.","idea":"Pair at-least-once delivery with idempotent workers, and the result is effectively once.","caption":"Same crash, but the repeat is ignored.","focus":["w2","mail"],"flows":[{"from":"w2","to":"mail","kind":"request","label":"key: order-81"},{"from":"mail","to":"w2","kind":"response","label":"dup ignored (key)"},{"from":"w2","to":"queue","kind":"response","label":"ack (delete)"}],"metrics":{"inflight":"0","sent":"802"},"chips":[{"node":"w2","text":"key order-81"},{"node":"mail","text":"dup ignored","tone":"green"},{"node":"w2","text":"acked","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A poison message","body":"One message has a malformed email address, so every attempt fails. Retried forever, it would waste capacity and hide real problems. So the queue has a maximum receive count, here 5. After 5 failed deliveries (exact rules vary by broker), the queue moves the message to a dead-letter queue (DLQ), where it waits for a person or a repair job. Redrive means moving parked messages back to the main queue once the bug is fixed. A truly malformed address is a permanent error, so a real worker should fail it fast.","idea":"A dead-letter queue parks messages that keep failing, so they stop wasting retries and can be inspected.","caption":"After 5 failed tries the message is parked in the DLQ.","focus":["queue","dlq","w1"],"flows":[{"from":"w1","to":"queue","kind":"request","label":"receive (try 5)"},{"from":"w1","to":"queue","kind":"fail","label":"error, no ack"},{"from":"queue","to":"dlq","kind":"async","label":"move to DLQ"}],"metrics":{"inflight":"0","dead":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"w1","text":"try 5","tone":"red"},{"node":"queue","text":"max receives 5"},{"node":"dlq","text":"1 dead letter","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"What the queue bought us","body":"Producers write and move on. Workers pull at their own pace, and the queue buffers any gap. Receive lends a message, ack deletes it, and the visibility timeout brings back work from dead workers. That gives at-least-once delivery, so workers must be idempotent. A DLQ catches messages that never succeed, and a growing DLQ or queue depth is your alarm. The 3,000 burst messages end as 2,999 emails sent plus 1 dead letter. A queue gives each message to one worker. If several services each need every message, that is pub/sub, covered next.","idea":"Decouple with a queue, ack after success, expect duplicates, and watch the DLQ.","caption":"The full path, with a safety net.","focus":["producer","queue","w1","w2","dlq","mail"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"depth":"0","inflight":"0","sent":"2999","dead":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"queue","text":"depth 0","tone":"green"},{"node":"mail","text":"2,999 sent"},{"node":"dlq","text":"1 dead","tone":"red"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A worker receives a message, finishes the job, but crashes just before sending the ack. What happens next with a standard at-least-once queue?","options":["The message is lost because the worker died","The message becomes visible again after the visibility timeout and is delivered to a worker again","The queue detects the crash and deletes the message","The producer is told to resend it"],"answer":1,"why":"Without an ack, the lease simply expires and the message returns to the queue. That is how at-least-once delivery avoids loss, at the cost of possible duplicate processing."},{"q":"Jobs normally take 20 seconds but sometimes 90 seconds. The visibility timeout is 30 seconds. What is the most likely symptom?","options":["Workers will run out of memory","Messages will be dropped silently","Slow jobs will be redelivered to another worker while still running, causing duplicates","The queue will stop accepting new messages"],"answer":2,"why":"A job that outlasts the lease becomes visible to others while the first worker is still going. Set the timeout above your slowest normal job, or extend the lease while working."},{"q":"Why should a consumer acknowledge a message only after the work succeeds?","options":["Acking early would lose the work if the consumer crashes afterwards","Acks are slow, so they should be delayed","The queue refuses early acks","Late acks prevent duplicates"],"answer":0,"why":"Ack deletes the message. If you ack first and crash, no copy remains to retry. Acking last trades possible duplicates for no loss."},{"q":"Your DLQ depth suddenly rises from 0 to 4,000 right after a deploy. What is the best reading?","options":["The queue is healthy; the DLQ is just a cache","Producers are sending too fast","The new consumer code probably fails on messages it should handle, so investigate and then redrive the messages","Visibility timeout is too long"],"answer":2,"why":"Messages typically reach the DLQ after repeated failed attempts. A jump right after a deploy points at a consumer bug, and once it is fixed the parked messages can be moved back to the main queue."},{"q":"Orders arrive in a 10 second burst at 300/s. Two workers process 40/s each. What does the queue do for you here?","options":["It makes the workers faster","It holds the backlog (about 2,200 messages) and lets the workers drain it at their own pace","It rejects messages above 80 per second","It guarantees each email is sent once"],"answer":1,"why":"A queue buffers the gap between arrival rate and processing rate. It does not speed up consumers or guarantee exactly-once, but the producer is not slowed by the consumers' pace (up to the queue's limits)."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Producer / consumer","def":"The producer writes messages to the queue; the consumer reads and handles them. Neither needs to know the other's speed or whether it is currently running."},{"term":"Visibility timeout","def":"Time a received message stays hidden; with no ack it reappears. Set it above your slowest job. Names vary: SQS says visibility timeout, Pub/Sub says ack deadline, RabbitMQ redelivers when the consumer connection drops."},{"term":"Ack","def":"Tells the queue the work is done so the message can be deleted. Send it after success, not before."},{"term":"At-least-once delivery","def":"Every message is delivered one or more times. Nothing is lost, but duplicates can happen after crashes or timeouts."},{"term":"Idempotent consumer","def":"Handling the same message twice has the same effect as once, for example by using a dedupe key or an upsert."},{"term":"Dead-letter queue (DLQ)","def":"Holding area for messages that exceeded the maximum receive count. Alert on its depth, fix the cause, then redrive."},{"term":"Queue depth / age","def":"Messages waiting and the age of the oldest one. Rising depth means consumers are too slow or stuck; use it to autoscale workers."},{"term":"Backpressure","def":"When consumers fall behind, the queue grows. Limit its size or slow producers if the backlog could outlive its usefulness."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"pubsub","title":"Pub/Sub & Event Logs","subtitle":"Publish one event to a partitioned log and watch independent consumer groups read it at their own pace, then replay it.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How a topic lets one published event reach many independent subscribers (fan-out)","How partitions split a topic for scale and why order is only guaranteed within one partition","How consumer groups share partitions and how offsets record each group's progress","What consumer lag means and what happens when a consumer crashes mid-stream","Why a retained log can be replayed, unlike a message that is deleted once read"],"nodes":[{"id":"orders","label":"Orders service","kind":"service","x":10,"y":30},{"id":"pay","label":"Payments service","kind":"service","x":10,"y":72},{"id":"p0","label":"Partition 0","kind":"queue","x":38,"y":18},{"id":"p1","label":"Partition 1","kind":"queue","x":38,"y":50},{"id":"p2","label":"Partition 2","kind":"queue","x":38,"y":82},{"id":"b1","label":"Billing worker 1","kind":"worker","x":66,"y":25},{"id":"b2","label":"Billing worker 2","kind":"worker","x":66,"y":75},{"id":"an","label":"Analytics (group B)","kind":"worker","x":88,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"orders","to":"p0"},{"from":"orders","to":"p1"},{"from":"pay","to":"p1"},{"from":"pay","to":"p2"},{"from":"p0","to":"b1"},{"from":"p1","to":"b1"},{"from":"p2","to":"b2"},{"from":"p2","to":"b1"},{"from":"p0","to":"an"},{"from":"p1","to":"an"},{"from":"p2","to":"an"}],"metrics":[{"id":"log","label":"Msgs in log","unit":"msgs"},{"id":"lagA","label":"Group A lag","unit":"msgs"},{"id":"lagB","label":"Group B lag","unit":"msgs"},{"id":"rate","label":"Publish rate","unit":"msg/s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One event, many readers","body":"When an order is placed, billing must charge it, analytics must count it, and a search index may want it too. If the Orders service called each of them directly, it would need to know every listener and wait for the slowest one. Pub/sub (publish-subscribe) flips this. The producer announces the event once and does not care who listens.","idea":"Pub/sub separates who produces an event from who consumes it.","caption":"Orders has one event and several unknown readers.","focus":["orders"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"log":"0","lagA":"0","lagB":"0","rate":"0"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Publish to a topic","body":"A topic is a named stream of events, such as order-events. In the animation Orders publishes an OrderPlaced event to the topic and gets an acknowledgement. It never learns who reads it. The events are kept in a stored log, and every consumer group reads the full stream on its own. Adding a new reader later needs no change to the producer. Here the topic carries 1,200 messages per second.","idea":"A topic is a named channel. Fan-out means every subscriber group sees every event.","caption":"The event is appended to the order-events topic.","focus":["orders","p1"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"p1","kind":"request","label":"OrderPlaced"},{"from":"p1","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"ack"}],"metrics":{"log":"1","rate":"1200"},"chips":[{"node":"orders","text":"1,200 msg/s"},{"node":"p1","text":"acked","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Partitions split the topic","body":"One machine cannot absorb a busy topic, so it is cut into partitions (here 3). The producer sets a key, such as the customer id, and a hash of the key picks the partition. The same key always lands in the same partition, so one customer's events stay together. Different keys spread out for parallel work. Orders and Payments both publish here, and the topic holds about 13,000 records so far at 1,200 per second. Adding partitions later changes where keys land, so leave headroom. A very busy key can overload one partition (a hot partition).","idea":"The key decides the partition: same key, same partition; many keys, spread load.","caption":"Two producers write to three partitions by key hash.","focus":["orders","pay","p0","p1","p2"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"p0","kind":"async","label":"key=u17"},{"from":"orders","to":"p1","kind":"async","label":"key=u42"},{"from":"pay","to":"p1","kind":"async","label":"key=u42"},{"from":"pay","to":"p2","kind":"async","label":"key=u90"}],"metrics":{"log":"13100","rate":"1200"},"chips":[{"node":"p0","text":"key u17"},{"node":"p1","text":"key u42 ×2"},{"node":"p2","text":"key u90"},{"node":"orders","text":"3 partitions","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"A log with offsets","body":"Each partition is an append-only log: new records go on the end, and each gets the next number, called its offset (0, 1, 2, and so on). Reading does not remove a record. The broker keeps it until the retention time runs out. Order is guaranteed inside one partition only. Records on different partitions have no defined order relative to each other. That is why events that must stay in order share a key.","idea":"Ordering holds per partition, so put events that must stay ordered under one key.","caption":"Partition 1 holds customer u42's events in write order.","focus":["p1"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"log":"13100"},"chips":[{"node":"p1","text":"offsets 0, 1, 2…"},{"node":"p1","text":"ordered in partition","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Consumer groups share work","body":"Billing runs two workers in one consumer group named billing (group A in the HUD). A consumer group is a team that shares the work. The group coordinator gives each partition to exactly one worker, so two workers never read the same partition at once. Worker 1 owns partitions 0 and 1, and worker 2 owns partition 2. Each worker pulls records from its own offset, at its own speed. The group is 800 records behind the newest data (its lag).","idea":"In a group, each partition has one owner, so the number of partitions caps the group's parallelism.","caption":"Group A splits the three partitions between two workers.","focus":["p0","p1","p2","b1","b2"],"flows":[{"from":"b1","to":"p0","kind":"request","label":"poll from 4210"},{"from":"b1","to":"p1","kind":"request","label":"poll from 3977"},{"from":"b2","to":"p2","kind":"request","label":"poll from 4105"},{"from":"p2","to":"b2","kind":"response","label":"batch of 500"}],"metrics":{"lagA":"800"},"chips":[{"node":"b1","text":"owns p0, p1"},{"node":"b2","text":"owns p2"},{"node":"b1","text":"group lag 800","at":"bottom"},{"node":"p2","text":"batch of 500","at":"top"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Another group, same log","body":"Analytics reads the same topic as a different group (group B). Groups are independent: each keeps its own offsets, so analytics reads all three partitions in full without affecting billing. There is still only one stored log. This is the fan-out. Unlike a queue, where a message goes to one worker, reading here removes nothing, so a slow analytics job cannot slow billing.","idea":"Each group has its own position in the log, so groups never compete for records.","caption":"Group B reads every partition on its own.","focus":["p0","p1","p2","an"],"flows":[{"from":"an","to":"p0","kind":"request","label":"poll, group B"},{"from":"an","to":"p1","kind":"request","label":"poll, group B"},{"from":"an","to":"p2","kind":"request","label":"poll, group B"},{"from":"p0","to":"an","kind":"response","label":"records"},{"from":"p1","to":"an","kind":"response","label":"records"},{"from":"p2","to":"an","kind":"response","label":"records"}],"metrics":{"lagB":"12000"},"chips":[{"node":"an","text":"group B"},{"node":"an","text":"reads all 3"},{"node":"an","text":"group lag 12,000","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Offsets and lag","body":"After processing a batch, a worker commits its offset: a note saying 'everything before this point is done'. Lag is the log's newest offset minus the committed offset, so it shows how far behind a group is. Billing at 800 behind is normal: at 1,200 messages per second that is under a second of delay. Analytics at 12,000 behind is about 10 seconds, fine for a slower batch-style job. Lag that keeps rising means the workers cannot keep up with publishing.","idea":"Lag is the key health signal. It shows how far each group is behind the newest record.","caption":"Workers commit offsets; the gap to the log's end is lag.","focus":["b1","b2","an"],"flows":[{"from":"b1","to":"p1","kind":"async","label":"commit 4477"},{"from":"b2","to":"p2","kind":"async","label":"commit 4605"}],"metrics":{"lagA":"800","lagB":"12000"},"chips":[{"node":"b1","text":"commit 4477"},{"node":"b2","text":"commit 4605"},{"node":"b1","text":"group lag 800","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"an","text":"group lag 12,000","at":"top"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A worker crashes","body":"Worker 2 dies after processing some records but before committing. The group notices its missing heartbeats and rebalances: worker 1 takes over partition 2 and resumes from the last committed offset, 4605. Records worker 2 handled after that commit are processed again. This is at-least-once delivery, so handlers should be idempotent (a repeat does no extra harm). While partition 2 has no owner its records pile up, so group A lag climbs (to about 3,000 here, an illustrative figure) until worker 1 catches up.","idea":"A crash causes a replay from the last commit, so consumers must tolerate duplicates.","caption":"Partition 2 moves to worker 1 after worker 2 fails.","focus":["p2","b2","b1"],"flows":[{"from":"p2","to":"b2","kind":"fail","label":"worker lost"},{"from":"p2","to":"b1","kind":"handshake","label":"reassigned"},{"from":"b1","to":"p2","kind":"request","label":"resume 4605"}],"metrics":{"lagA":"3000"},"chips":[{"node":"b2","text":"crashed","tone":"red"},{"node":"b1","text":"takes p2"},{"node":"p2","text":"resume 4605","at":"bottom"},{"node":"b1","text":"lag ~3,000","tone":"red","at":"top"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Replay the past","body":"Suppose analytics shipped a bug and miscounted for three days. At 1,200 messages per second, three days is about 311 million records. The broker keeps records for a set time, say 7 days (about 726 million records). So you stop group B, fix the code and move its offsets back three days. It re-reads the old records and rebuilds its results. Plain pub/sub drops events for absent subscribers, and a queue deletes messages once handled. A log keeps them.","idea":"Retention turns the topic into a replayable history, limited by the retention window.","caption":"Group B rewinds its offsets and reads old records again.","focus":["p0","p1","p2","an"],"flows":[{"from":"an","to":"p0","kind":"request","label":"seek -3 days"},{"from":"an","to":"p1","kind":"request","label":"seek -3 days"},{"from":"an","to":"p2","kind":"request","label":"seek -3 days"},{"from":"p0","to":"an","kind":"response","label":"old records"},{"from":"p1","to":"an","kind":"response","label":"old records"},{"from":"p2","to":"an","kind":"response","label":"old records"}],"metrics":{"log":"726000000","lagB":"311000000"},"chips":[{"node":"an","text":"seek −3 days"},{"node":"an","text":"311 M records","at":"bottom"},{"node":"p0","text":"7 d retention"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"The log is the contract","body":"Producers append keyed events to a topic. Partitions give scale, and order holds only inside one partition. Each consumer group owns partitions, tracks its own offsets and moves at its own speed, so many groups can read the same data. Retention makes replay possible. Plan for duplicates, and watch lag.","idea":"Topic plus partitions plus offsets gives fan-out, scale and replay from one stored log.","caption":"One retained log feeds many independent groups.","focus":["orders","pay","p0","p1","p2","b1","b2","an"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"log":"726000000","lagA":"0","lagB":"0","rate":"1200"},"chips":[{"node":"orders","text":"1,200 msg/s"},{"node":"b1","text":"lag 0","tone":"green"},{"node":"an","text":"lag 0","tone":"green"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Events for customer 42 sometimes reach a consumer out of order, even though one service publishes them in order. Which cause fits best?","options":["The broker sorts records by size","The consumer group has too many workers","The events were published without a key, so they spread over several partitions","Offsets reset to zero after each commit"],"answer":2,"why":"Order is guaranteed only within a partition. Without a consistent key, one customer's events land on different partitions and can be read in any relative order."},{"q":"A topic has 4 partitions and a consumer group runs 6 workers. What happens?","options":["All 6 share the work equally","4 workers each own a partition and 2 sit idle","Each record is delivered to all 6 workers","The broker creates 2 more partitions"],"answer":1,"why":"Within a group each partition is owned by one consumer, so useful parallelism is capped at the partition count. The extra workers are idle standbys."},{"q":"A worker handles a record, then crashes before committing its offset. After the rebalance, what should you expect?","options":["The record is lost for good","The record is silently skipped","The group stops until an operator intervenes","The record is delivered again, so the handler should tolerate repeats"],"answer":3,"why":"The new owner resumes from the last committed offset, so work done after that commit is redone. Idempotent handlers make this harmless."},{"q":"A new team wants every order from the last two days, and retention is seven days. What makes this possible?","options":["Reading does not delete records, so a new group can start from an earlier offset","The producer must republish the events","The broker copies messages to each new subscriber when it joins","Only the original group may read old offsets"],"answer":0,"why":"The log keeps records until retention expires and each group tracks its own offsets, so a new group can seek back and read the retained history."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Topic","def":"A named stream of events. Producers append to it; any number of consumer groups can subscribe and each gets the full stream."},{"term":"Partition","def":"One ordered, append-only slice of a topic. More partitions allow more parallel writers and readers."},{"term":"Message key","def":"Value hashed to choose a partition. Same key means same partition, which preserves per-key order."},{"term":"Offset","def":"A record's position number within its partition. Consumers commit offsets to remember their progress."},{"term":"Consumer group","def":"Consumers that share a topic's partitions; each partition has one owner in the group. Different groups are independent."},{"term":"Consumer lag","def":"Log end offset minus the group's committed offset. Growing lag means consumers cannot keep up."},{"term":"Retention","def":"How long, or how much, data the broker keeps, for example 7 days. Replay is only possible inside this window."},{"term":"Rebalance","def":"Reassigning partitions when consumers join or leave. Processing on the moved partitions pauses briefly (all partitions with older eager rebalancing) and records may be redelivered."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"batch-stream","title":"Batch vs Stream Processing","subtitle":"Watch one shop's click events get turned into hourly revenue two ways, and see what each way costs you.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How a MapReduce-style batch job splits, shuffles and reduces a big dataset","How a stream processor keeps running state and emits results continuously","What tumbling, sliding and session windows are and when to use each","Why late events happen and how watermarks decide when a window closes","How lambda and kappa architectures trade complexity for freshness and simplicity"],"nodes":[{"id":"src","label":"Event sources","kind":"user","x":7,"y":50},{"id":"log","label":"Event log","kind":"queue","x":28,"y":50},{"id":"lake","label":"Data lake","kind":"storage","x":28,"y":86},{"id":"batch","label":"Batch job","kind":"worker","x":50,"y":18},{"id":"stream","label":"Stream processor","kind":"worker","x":50,"y":56},{"id":"serving","label":"Serving store","kind":"db","x":72,"y":38},{"id":"dash","label":"Dashboard","kind":"client","x":93,"y":38}],"edges":[{"from":"src","to":"log"},{"from":"log","to":"lake"},{"from":"lake","to":"batch"},{"from":"log","to":"stream"},{"from":"batch","to":"serving"},{"from":"stream","to":"serving"},{"from":"serving","to":"dash"}],"metrics":[{"id":"rate","label":"Events in","unit":"/s"},{"id":"lag","label":"Result lag","unit":"s"},{"id":"late","label":"Late events","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Same events, two ways to answer","body":"A shop's site and apps send about 5,000 events per second (clicks, carts, orders). That is roughly 430 million a day, or about 215 GB at 500 bytes each. The business wants revenue per hour on a dashboard. There are two ways to get it. You can let data pile up and crunch it in big jobs (batch). Or you can process each event the moment it arrives (stream). Either way, events first land in a durable event log.","idea":"Batch processes a finished pile of data. Stream processes data that never finishes.","caption":"Events flow from the sources into a durable log.","focus":["src","log"],"flows":[{"from":"src","to":"log","kind":"request","label":"click, order"}],"metrics":{"rate":"5000"},"chips":[{"node":"src","text":"5,000 events/s"},{"node":"log","text":"~215 GB / day"}]},{"tag":"BATCH","title":"Batch: collect, then compute","body":"Batch works on a bounded dataset: a fixed set of files with a known start and end. In the animation the log is copied into a data lake (cheap storage for raw files) as hourly files. Nothing is computed yet; the data just piles up. Because the input is complete and never changes, a failed job can simply be rerun on the same files and give the same answer.","idea":"A bounded input is finished and repeatable, so reruns are safe and simple.","caption":"Events are archived as hourly files.","focus":["log","lake"],"flows":[{"from":"log","to":"lake","kind":"async","label":"hourly files"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"lake","text":"hourly files"},{"node":"lake","text":"rerunnable","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"MAPREDUCE","title":"MapReduce: split, shuffle, reduce","body":"When an hour closes, the batch job reads that hour's files: about 9 GB (5,000 x 3,600 x 500 bytes). It cuts them into 128 MB splits, roughly 70 pieces. Map tasks run in parallel on the splits and emit (hour, amount) pairs. The shuffle sends all pairs with the same key to one reducer, and each reducer adds them up. Totals land in the serving store, the database the dashboard reads. Spark and similar engines use the same split, shuffle, aggregate idea. The job starts only after the hour ends, so the dashboard is over an hour behind (HUD: 4,200 s, about 70 min).","idea":"Batch scales by running many independent map tasks, then grouping by key. Its cost is waiting.","caption":"Parallel map and reduce tasks write hourly totals.","focus":["lake","batch","serving"],"flows":[{"from":"batch","to":"lake","kind":"request","label":"read 70 splits"},{"from":"lake","to":"batch","kind":"response","label":"9 GB of files"},{"from":"batch","to":"serving","kind":"async","label":"hourly totals"}],"metrics":{"lag":"4200"},"chips":[{"node":"batch","text":"~70 splits"},{"node":"lake","text":"9 GB / hour"},{"node":"serving","text":"1 h+ behind","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"STREAM","title":"Stream: compute as events arrive","body":"A stream processor reads the log continuously. Its input is unbounded: it never ends, so there is no final answer, only a running one. It keeps state in memory, such as a running revenue sum per hour. It updates that sum on every event and pushes fresh totals to the serving store. In the animation the dashboard asks for revenue and gets numbers about 2 seconds old, instead of over an hour old.","idea":"Streaming gives up the simplicity of finished input in return for results that are only seconds old.","caption":"Each event updates the running totals immediately.","focus":["log","stream","serving","dash"],"flows":[{"from":"log","to":"stream","kind":"async","label":"each event"},{"from":"stream","to":"serving","kind":"async","label":"running sums"},{"from":"dash","to":"serving","kind":"request","label":"revenue?"},{"from":"serving","to":"dash","kind":"response","label":"fresh totals"}],"metrics":{"lag":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"stream","text":"running sums"},{"node":"serving","text":"~2 s behind","tone":"green"},{"node":"dash","text":"fresh totals","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WINDOWS","title":"Windows: cutting an endless stream","body":"To get an hourly number from a never-ending stream, you cut it into windows. A tumbling window is fixed and does not overlap, like each clock hour. A sliding window overlaps: for example, the last hour recomputed every 5 minutes, so each event falls into 12 windows. A session window groups one user's activity and closes after a quiet gap, say 30 minutes.","idea":"Pick the window shape from the question: fixed buckets, a rolling view, or per-user activity.","caption":"The processor groups events into time windows.","focus":["log","stream"],"flows":[{"from":"log","to":"stream","kind":"request","label":"10:00-11:00 bucket"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"stream","text":"tumbling · 1 h"},{"node":"stream","text":"sliding · 12 windows","at":"bottom"},{"node":"stream","text":"session · 30 min gap","at":"top"}]},{"tag":"LATE EVENTS","title":"Late events and watermarks","body":"A phone loses signal and sends an order stamped 10:58 only at 11:03. Windows should use event time (when it happened), not processing time (when it arrived), or this order lands in the wrong hour. But the processor must decide when a window is finished. A watermark says 'I assume no event older than this is still coming'. It is often the newest event time minus a fixed wait, such as 2 minutes. This order is 5 minutes late, past that wait, so it counts as late. The processor can drop it, send it aside, or use it to correct the result. The 0.2% late rate is an assumed example.","idea":"A watermark is a guess about completeness. A shorter wait gives faster results but more late drops.","caption":"The order arrives after the 10:00-11:00 window has closed.","focus":["src","log","stream","serving"],"flows":[{"from":"src","to":"log","kind":"async","label":"t=10:58, at 11:03"},{"from":"log","to":"stream","kind":"request","label":"late event"},{"from":"stream","to":"serving","kind":"replicate","label":"correction"}],"metrics":{"late":"0.2"},"chips":[{"node":"stream","text":"watermark −2 min"},{"node":"log","text":"t=10:58, at 11:03"},{"node":"stream","text":"late 0.2%","at":"bottom"},{"node":"serving","text":"correction"}]},{"tag":"LAMBDA","title":"Lambda: run both side by side","body":"Early stream engines gave approximate results and could lose data. So teams ran two paths over the same events. A speed layer (stream) gives quick but rough numbers. A batch layer recomputes accurate numbers later and overwrites them. The serving store merges the two. This works, but you write, test and debug the same business logic twice, in two systems, and the results must agree.","idea":"Lambda buys speed plus accuracy, at the price of two codebases that must match.","caption":"Batch and stream both write into one serving store.","focus":["log","lake","batch","stream","serving"],"flows":[{"from":"log","to":"stream","kind":"async","label":"speed layer"},{"from":"lake","to":"batch","kind":"async","label":"batch layer"},{"from":"stream","to":"serving","kind":"async","label":"fast, rough"},{"from":"batch","to":"serving","kind":"async","label":"slow, exact, fixes"}],"metrics":{"lag":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"stream","text":"fast, rough"},{"node":"batch","text":"slow, exact"},{"node":"serving","text":"merges both","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"KAPPA","title":"Kappa: one stream, replay for fixes","body":"Modern stream engines handle state, checkpoints and event time well, so kappa drops the batch path. The log is the source of truth and keeps data for days or months. To fix a bug or change logic, deploy the new version and replay the log from the start (offset 0) into a new output table. Then switch readers to it. A batch is just a stream that happens to end. The catch is retention: replay only works if the log still holds the history.","idea":"Kappa keeps one codebase by treating reprocessing as a replay of the log.","caption":"New code replays the log from offset 0.","focus":["log","stream","serving"],"flows":[{"from":"log","to":"stream","kind":"replicate","label":"replay offset 0"},{"from":"stream","to":"serving","kind":"async","label":"new table"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"log","text":"replay from 0"},{"node":"serving","text":"new table"},{"node":"stream","text":"one codebase","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The stream job crashes","body":"The processor dies at 11:20 and loses 20 minutes of running totals held in memory. A batch task would simply rerun. A stream job instead takes periodic checkpoints: a snapshot of its state plus the log position (offset) it had reached, say every minute. On restart it reloads the last checkpoint and re-reads from that offset. We assume restart and catch-up take about 70 s, so the result lag spikes to 70 s. Some events are processed twice, so output writes must be idempotent (safe to repeat) or transactional, or totals would double count.","idea":"Checkpoint plus replay recovers a stream job. Idempotent output keeps the replay from double counting.","caption":"The job restores a checkpoint and re-reads the log.","focus":["log","stream","serving"],"flows":[{"from":"stream","to":"serving","kind":"fail","label":"crash 11:20"},{"from":"log","to":"stream","kind":"replicate","label":"from checkpoint"}],"metrics":{"lag":"70"},"chips":[{"node":"stream","text":"crashed 11:20","tone":"red"},{"node":"stream","text":"checkpoint ~1 min","at":"bottom"},{"node":"serving","text":"idempotent writes"},{"node":"stream","text":"lag 70 s","tone":"red","at":"top"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Choosing between them","body":"Use batch when hours of delay are fine and you need cheap, repeatable crunching of huge history, such as nightly reports or model training. Use stream when decisions need seconds, such as fraud checks or live dashboards. Streams need windows, event time and watermarks to cope with endless, out-of-order input. As a rule of thumb, prefer one codebase over lambda when your engine can replay the log or run the same code in batch mode.","idea":"Choose by how stale an answer you can accept, and pay for extra machinery only if you need it.","caption":"Batch is simple and hours old; stream is seconds old and needs more care.","focus":["src","log","lake","batch","stream","serving","dash"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"lag":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"stream","text":"~2 s lag","tone":"green"},{"node":"batch","text":"hourly lag","at":"bottom"},{"node":"log","text":"5,000 events/s"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"An order happened at 10:58 on a phone with no signal and reached the stream processor at 11:03. Why should hourly windows use event time here?","options":["Event time is always faster to compute","It removes the need for a log","The order belongs to the 10:00-11:00 hour, and arrival time would put it in the wrong one","Processing time cannot be used with windows"],"answer":2,"why":"Event time records when something actually happened, so results stay correct despite delays. Processing time would count the order in 11:00-12:00 and change results whenever the pipeline is slow."},{"q":"What is the main ongoing cost of a lambda architecture?","options":["The log cannot store more than a day of data","The same business logic must be written and kept consistent in a batch system and a stream system","Batch jobs cannot read from the data lake","Serving stores cannot accept two writers"],"answer":1,"why":"Lambda runs two code paths over the same events. Any logic change must be made twice, and the two paths can disagree, which is the main reason teams move to kappa."},{"q":"Which workload fits batch processing best?","options":["Recomputing a product-recommendation model over three years of purchase history each night","Blocking a stolen card within one second of a purchase","A live dashboard of orders in the last minute","Alerting when a sensor reads above a threshold"],"answer":0,"why":"Large, bounded history with no need for second-level freshness is where batch is cheapest and simplest. The other three need results in seconds."},{"q":"You find a bug in a kappa pipeline's revenue logic from last week. What is the sound fix?","options":["Patch the running job and accept that last week's numbers stay wrong","Delete the log and wait for new events","Convert the pipeline to lambda immediately","Deploy the fixed job, replay the log from the needed offset into a new output, then switch readers to it"],"answer":3,"why":"Kappa treats reprocessing as replay. This works only if the log's retention still covers the affected period, so retention is a design decision."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Bounded vs unbounded","def":"Bounded data has a known end (a day's files), so batch can finish. Unbounded data never ends (live events), so stream jobs run forever."},{"term":"MapReduce","def":"Map tasks process input splits in parallel and emit key/value pairs; the shuffle groups equal keys; reduce tasks combine each group's values."},{"term":"Tumbling window","def":"Fixed-size, non-overlapping buckets, such as each clock hour. Every event belongs to exactly one window."},{"term":"Sliding window","def":"Fixed size but advancing in smaller steps (1 hour every 5 minutes). Windows overlap, so each event counts in several."},{"term":"Session window","def":"Groups one key's events until a gap of inactivity (for example 30 minutes). Length varies per user."},{"term":"Event time vs processing time","def":"Event time is when it happened; processing time is when the system sees it. Use event time for correct results under delays."},{"term":"Watermark","def":"A moving estimate that no earlier events are still coming, often newest event time minus a fixed delay. It closes windows; later events are dropped or handled separately."},{"term":"Lambda vs kappa","def":"Lambda runs batch and stream paths and merges them. Kappa uses only a stream over a replayable log and reprocesses by replaying."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"event-sourcing-cqrs","title":"Event Sourcing & CQRS","subtitle":"Follow one bank deposit as it is stored as an event, projected into a read model, and queried, and see why the read side lags.","minutes":12,"level":"Advanced","learn":["How storing events instead of current state keeps a full history","How current state is rebuilt by replaying a stream of events","Why CQRS splits the write model from one or more read models","Why the read side is eventually consistent and how to live with it","How snapshots keep command replay short, and how new projections are rebuilt from history"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Client","kind":"client","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"cmd","label":"Command service","kind":"service","x":28,"y":20},{"id":"store","label":"Event store","kind":"db","x":52,"y":20},{"id":"proj","label":"Projector","kind":"worker","x":74,"y":20},{"id":"readdb","label":"Read model","kind":"db","x":88,"y":52},{"id":"snap","label":"Snapshots","kind":"storage","x":30,"y":52},{"id":"qry","label":"Query service","kind":"service","x":54,"y":82}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"cmd"},{"from":"cmd","to":"store"},{"from":"cmd","to":"snap"},{"from":"store","to":"proj"},{"from":"proj","to":"readdb"},{"from":"client","to":"qry"},{"from":"qry","to":"readdb"}],"metrics":[{"id":"balance","label":"Read balance","unit":"USD"},{"id":"stored","label":"Events stored","unit":"events"},{"id":"replayed","label":"Events replayed","unit":"events"},{"id":"lag","label":"Read lag","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"The balance says 70. Why?","body":"A normal database keeps one row per account and overwrites it: balance = 70. The row cannot tell you how it got there, what it was last Tuesday, or whether a bug changed it. Event sourcing flips this. We store what happened (account opened, deposited 100, withdrew 30) and compute the balance from those facts: 100 - 30 = 70. Here the store already holds three events for one account.","idea":"Keep the facts that happened, and derive the current state from them.","caption":"Three events are stored; together they add up to 70.","focus":["store"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"balance":"70","stored":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"3 events"},{"node":"store","text":"balance 70","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WRITE","title":"Commands append events","body":"The client sends a command (a request to do something): deposit 50. In the animation the command service first loads this account's events and replays them to get the current state (3 events, balance 70). It checks its rules, then appends a new Deposited event as event 4. The append says 'only if the stream is still at version 3'. If another writer got there first, the append is rejected and the command retries. Events are never edited in place. A fix is a new event.","idea":"A command is checked against replayed state, then appends an unchangeable event with an expected version.","caption":"The command loads the stream, then appends event number 4.","focus":["client","cmd","store"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"cmd","kind":"request","label":"Deposit 50"},{"from":"cmd","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"load stream"},{"from":"store","to":"cmd","kind":"response","label":"3 events"},{"from":"cmd","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"append @ v3"},{"from":"store","to":"cmd","kind":"response","label":"ok, v4"},{"from":"cmd","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"accepted, v4"}],"metrics":{"stored":"4","replayed":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"v3 → v4"},{"node":"cmd","text":"replayed 3"},{"node":"store","text":"4 events","at":"bottom"},{"node":"client","text":"accepted v4","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The gap before the read side","body":"The write is committed, but the screen reads from a separate store, the read model. Asked for the balance right now, the read model has not heard about event 4 yet, so it still says 70. This is eventual consistency: the read side catches up a moment later. With a push subscription the lag is often tens of milliseconds (our 40 ms is illustrative). A polling projector or a backlog can stretch it to seconds. To cope, the command can return the new version and the read waits until the read model reaches it, or the UI can show the new balance at once (optimistically).","idea":"CQRS trades instant read-after-write for a separate, scalable read side. Cope by waiting for the command's version or updating the UI optimistically.","caption":"A read sent right now returns the old balance.","focus":["client","qry","readdb"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"qry","kind":"request","label":"balance?"},{"from":"qry","to":"readdb","kind":"request","label":"get account"},{"from":"readdb","to":"qry","kind":"response","label":"70"},{"from":"qry","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"70 (stale)"}],"metrics":{"lag":"40"},"chips":[{"node":"readdb","text":"balance 70","tone":"red"},{"node":"qry","text":"stale read","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"readdb","text":"lag ≈ 40 ms"}]},{"tag":"PROJECT","title":"A projector builds the read model","body":"A projector is a small worker that subscribes to the event store and reads new events in order. It uses them to update a table shaped for the screen. In the animation, Deposited 50 turns into balance = 120 in the read model. The projector remembers the last event number it applied, so after a crash it resumes from there. It must also be safe to re-apply an event it already saw.","idea":"A projection is a function over the event stream. It must be idempotent and restartable.","caption":"Event 4 flows to the projector and updates the read model.","focus":["store","proj","readdb"],"flows":[{"from":"store","to":"proj","kind":"async","label":"Deposited 50"},{"from":"proj","to":"readdb","kind":"replicate","label":"balance = 120"}],"metrics":{"balance":"120"},"chips":[{"node":"proj","text":"applies event 4"},{"node":"readdb","text":"balance 120","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"QUERY","title":"Queries never touch the events","body":"Now the same read returns 120. The query service reads one prepared row, with no replaying and no joins. CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation) means writes and reads use separate models. The read model can sit in whatever store suits the question: a SQL table for account screens, a search index for transactions, a cache for dashboards. You can scale reads separately from writes, and add read models without touching the write side.","idea":"CQRS lets each side use the model and storage that fits its job.","caption":"The query service answers from the prepared read model.","focus":["client","qry","readdb"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"qry","kind":"request","label":"balance?"},{"from":"qry","to":"readdb","kind":"request","label":"get account"},{"from":"readdb","to":"qry","kind":"response","label":"120"},{"from":"qry","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"120"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"readdb","text":"balance 120","tone":"green"},{"node":"qry","text":"1 row read"},{"node":"client","text":"sees 120","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"REBUILD","title":"New question, new projection","body":"Product asks for monthly deposits per account over the last three years. With overwritten rows that history is gone. With events, you write a new projector and replay the whole history into a fresh read model, while the old one keeps serving. Assume the store holds 50 million events and the projector handles 20,000 per second. 50,000,000 / 20,000 = 2,500 s, about 40 minutes. Once it catches up with events that arrived meanwhile, you switch traffic to the new view.","idea":"Events are the source of truth, so any read model can be thrown away and rebuilt from scratch.","caption":"The whole history is replayed into a brand new read model.","focus":["store","proj","readdb"],"flows":[{"from":"store","to":"proj","kind":"async","label":"replay all events"},{"from":"proj","to":"readdb","kind":"replicate","label":"new view"}],"metrics":{"stored":"50000000","replayed":"50000000"},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"50 M events"},{"node":"proj","text":"20,000 / s"},{"node":"proj","text":"≈ 40 min","at":"bottom"},{"node":"readdb","text":"new view"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A very long stream","body":"Replaying everything is fine for a background projector, but it is a problem on the write path. Take a busy account, say a merchant with 12,150 events. Every command would load and replay all of them. At roughly 1 KB per event, that is about 12 MB read per deposit, which turns a few-millisecond command into a slow one. Most streams are short, but the long ones hurt.","idea":"Command time grows with stream length, unless you shorten what has to be replayed.","caption":"Each command now replays 12,150 events.","focus":["cmd","store"],"flows":[{"from":"cmd","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"12,150 events"}],"metrics":{"replayed":"12150"},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"12,150 events","tone":"red"},{"node":"cmd","text":"≈ 12 MB per command","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"SNAPSHOT","title":"Snapshots shorten the replay","body":"The fix is a snapshot: a saved copy of the account's state plus the version it reflects. Every so often (say every 500 events) the service saves one. On the next command it loads the latest snapshot, then only the events after it. With a snapshot at version 12,000, it replays 150 events instead of 12,150. A snapshot is just a cache, not the truth. If it is lost or its format changes, rebuild it from the events.","idea":"A snapshot is saved state plus a version. Load it, then replay only the newer events.","caption":"A snapshot plus 150 newer events replaces 12,150.","focus":["cmd","snap","store"],"flows":[{"from":"cmd","to":"snap","kind":"request","label":"latest snapshot"},{"from":"snap","to":"cmd","kind":"response","label":"state @ v12000"},{"from":"cmd","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"tail after v12000"},{"from":"store","to":"cmd","kind":"response","label":"150 events"}],"metrics":{"replayed":"150"},"chips":[{"node":"snap","text":"snapshot @ v12000"},{"node":"store","text":"150 events","tone":"green"},{"node":"cmd","text":"150 replayed","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"What you pay for this","body":"You gain history, audit and rebuildable views. You pay in complexity. Old events live forever, so their shape must change carefully (versioned events, plus upcasters that translate old ones). Mistakes are fixed by appending a correcting event, not by editing. Personal data in unchangeable events complicates deletion requests. A common answer is to encrypt each user's data and throw away the key. For plain CRUD screens, this is usually overkill.","idea":"Use event sourcing where history and audit matter. CQRS alone can be used without it.","caption":"The power of history against the cost of immutability.","focus":[],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Facts in, views out","body":"Commands append unchangeable events, guarded by an expected version. State is a fold over those events (a running total built by applying them one by one), shortened by snapshots. Projectors turn the log into read models shaped for queries. They are always slightly behind, so design screens and APIs for eventual consistency. Need a new view? Replay. Need more read capacity? Add read models. Keep event sourcing for domains that benefit from history.","idea":"Write the truth once as events, and derive every view from it.","caption":"The write side appends; the read side projects and answers.","focus":["client","cmd","store","proj","readdb","qry","snap"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"balance":"120","lag":"40"},"chips":[{"node":"readdb","text":"balance 120"},{"node":"readdb","text":"lag 40 ms","at":"bottom"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two commands for the same account arrive at once, both after loading the stream at version 7. Each tries to append with 'expected version 7'. What should happen?","options":["Both succeed and the events are merged","Both fail so nobody wins","One append succeeds; the other is rejected and retries against the new state","The read model decides which one wins"],"answer":2,"why":"The expected-version check is optimistic concurrency. Only one append can move the stream from 7 to 8; the loser must reload and re-validate, because its decision used stale state."},{"q":"A user deposits money, is redirected to their account page, and sees the old balance. Refreshing a second later shows the new one. What is the best explanation and fix?","options":["The projector had not applied the event yet; hand the UI the new version and let it wait or update optimistically","The event store dropped the event on write; add more replication to the store","The snapshots are stale for this account; take them more often than before","The query service is buggy; make it read straight from the event store"],"answer":0,"why":"This is read-side eventual consistency. Fix it in the experience: hand the UI the command's new version and wait for the read model to reach it, or update the UI optimistically. Reading the event store for queries would throw away the point of CQRS."},{"q":"You need a report no one planned for, covering all past activity. Why is that easy with event sourcing?","options":["Snapshots already contain every report","The write model can answer any query directly","Events are small, so scans are always fast","Replay the full event history through a new projector into a new read model"],"answer":3,"why":"Events keep every recorded fact in order within each stream, so a new projection can be computed from the start, provided the events captured the data it needs. Overwritten state could not supply facts it never kept."},{"q":"What is the correct role of a snapshot?","options":["It replaces old events, which can then be deleted","It is a cached state at a known version that shortens replay; the events remain the source of truth","It keeps the read model consistent with the write model","It lets queries skip the projector"],"answer":1,"why":"A snapshot speeds up loading an aggregate by starting from saved state. It can always be rebuilt from events, so it never replaces them."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Event","def":"An immutable fact in past tense, such as Deposited 50. It is appended to a stream and treated as immutable."},{"term":"Aggregate / stream","def":"The unit whose events are stored and replayed together, e.g. one account. Consistency rules are enforced inside one stream."},{"term":"Expected version","def":"A check on append: only write if the stream is still at the version you loaded. Gives optimistic concurrency without locks."},{"term":"CQRS","def":"Separate models for changing state (commands) and reading it (queries). Often paired with event sourcing, but usable without it."},{"term":"Projection","def":"A read model built by folding events into a shape suited to queries. Idempotent, restartable, and rebuildable by replay."},{"term":"Eventual consistency","def":"The read side trails the write side by a short, variable delay. Design reads, UIs and APIs to tolerate a stale view."},{"term":"Snapshot","def":"Saved aggregate state plus its version, taken every N events. Load it and replay only the tail. A cache, not the truth."},{"term":"Upcasting","def":"Translating old stored event versions to the current shape when reading, since events cannot be rewritten in place."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"service-discovery","title":"Service Discovery","subtitle":"Watch an Orders service find, health-check and call a Payments service whose instances keep changing addresses.","minutes":10,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why hard-coded addresses break when instances autoscale, crash and redeploy","How registration, heartbeats and TTLs keep a registry honest","The difference between client-side and server-side discovery, and what each costs","How DNS-based discovery works and why caching makes it sluggish","What happens to callers during the stale window after an instance dies"],"nodes":[{"id":"orders","label":"Orders service","kind":"service","x":10,"y":48},{"id":"dns","label":"Internal DNS","kind":"dns","x":22,"y":28},{"id":"registry","label":"Service registry","kind":"db","x":38,"y":14},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":38,"y":82},{"id":"pay1","label":"payments-1","kind":"server","x":74,"y":14},{"id":"pay2","label":"payments-2","kind":"server","x":74,"y":48},{"id":"pay3","label":"payments-3","kind":"server","x":74,"y":82}],"edges":[{"from":"orders","to":"registry"},{"from":"orders","to":"dns"},{"from":"orders","to":"lb"},{"from":"orders","to":"pay2"},{"from":"orders","to":"pay3"},{"from":"pay1","to":"registry"},{"from":"pay2","to":"registry"},{"from":"pay3","to":"registry"},{"from":"lb","to":"registry"},{"from":"lb","to":"pay1"},{"from":"lb","to":"pay3"},{"from":"dns","to":"registry"}],"metrics":[{"id":"instances","label":"Registered","unit":"inst"},{"id":"extra","label":"Extra hop","unit":"ms"},{"id":"stale","label":"Stale window","unit":"s"},{"id":"failed","label":"Failed calls","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Where is Payments right now?","body":"Orders must call Payments. In a fixed setup you would write Payments' address into a config file. In the cloud that breaks: instances are replaced on every deploy, added when traffic grows and removed when they crash, and each new one gets a new IP address. Service discovery is how callers find the current, healthy instances by name. Here Orders knows only the name 'payments' and needs a live lookup.","idea":"Instances come and go, so callers need a live lookup from a stable name to current addresses.","caption":"Orders knows a name, not any address.","focus":["orders"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"instances":"0","failed":"0"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"REGISTER","title":"Instances announce themselves","body":"When an instance finishes starting and is ready for traffic, it tells the registry its service name and address, such as payments at 10.0.1.4:8080. The registry is a live phone book of who is available. Some systems have the instance register itself, and others have a platform agent do it. Register only when ready: an instance still loading its cache should not get traffic yet. In the animation, three Payments instances register.","idea":"A registry is a live map from a service name to the addresses that are ready right now.","caption":"Three Payments instances register.","focus":["pay1","pay2","pay3","registry"],"flows":[{"from":"pay1","to":"registry","kind":"request","label":"register 10.0.1.4"},{"from":"pay2","to":"registry","kind":"request","label":"register 10.0.1.5"},{"from":"pay3","to":"registry","kind":"request","label":"register 10.0.1.6"}],"metrics":{"instances":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"registry","text":"3 registered","tone":"green"},{"node":"pay1","text":"10.0.1.4"},{"node":"pay2","text":"10.0.1.5"},{"node":"pay3","text":"10.0.1.6"}]},{"tag":"HEALTH","title":"Staying registered means proving you are alive","body":"A crashed instance cannot say goodbye, so the registry needs another way to know. Each instance sends a heartbeat (a tiny 'I am alive' message) every 5 seconds. Or the registry checks a health endpoint instead. After 3 missed beats (15 seconds here, a typical choice) the entry is removed. On a clean shutdown, instances deregister first, so heartbeats are the safety net for crashes. Short intervals detect failure faster but add load, and can wrongly drop a healthy instance after a brief network blip (flapping).","idea":"Heartbeats plus a timeout turn silence into removal, so dead instances age out on their own.","caption":"Healthy instances keep sending heartbeats.","focus":["pay1","pay2","pay3","registry"],"flows":[{"from":"pay1","to":"registry","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat"},{"from":"pay2","to":"registry","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat"},{"from":"pay3","to":"registry","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat"}],"metrics":{"stale":"15"},"chips":[{"node":"pay1","text":"heartbeat 5 s"},{"node":"registry","text":"drop after 15 s","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CLIENT-SIDE","title":"The caller picks the instance","body":"In client-side discovery, Orders asks the registry for the list of healthy Payments addresses, keeps it in memory and picks one itself, for example round-robin (taking turns). In the animation it calls payments-2 directly, with no middle hop. The cost: every client, in every language, needs this lookup, caching and balancing code, and all of it must be kept up to date.","idea":"Client-side discovery saves a network hop but spreads discovery code into every caller.","caption":"Orders looks up the list, then calls payments-2 directly.","focus":["orders","registry","pay2"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"registry","kind":"request","label":"payments?"},{"from":"registry","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"3 addresses"},{"from":"orders","to":"pay2","kind":"request","label":"POST /charge"},{"from":"pay2","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"extra":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"orders","text":"round-robin"},{"node":"registry","text":"3 addresses"},{"node":"pay2","text":"200 OK","tone":"green"},{"node":"orders","text":"no extra hop","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"SERVER-SIDE","title":"A proxy picks for the caller","body":"In server-side discovery, Orders calls one stable address: a load balancer or proxy. The proxy keeps a fresh copy of the healthy list from the registry (it syncs in the background, not on every call), picks an instance and forwards the request. Callers stay simple and need no discovery code. The trade-off is an extra hop, often around 1 ms inside one data center, and the proxy itself must stay highly available.","idea":"Server-side discovery keeps callers simple but adds a hop and a component that must not fail.","caption":"Orders calls the load balancer, which routes to a healthy instance.","focus":["orders","lb","registry","pay1"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"POST /charge"},{"from":"lb","to":"registry","kind":"request","label":"sync healthy list"},{"from":"registry","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"healthy list"},{"from":"lb","to":"pay1","kind":"request","label":"POST /charge"},{"from":"pay1","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"},{"from":"lb","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"extra":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"lb","text":"+1 ms hop"},{"node":"registry","text":"healthy list"},{"node":"pay1","text":"200 OK","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"DNS","title":"Using DNS as the registry","body":"The simplest discovery tool is DNS. Payments gets a name such as payments.internal, and the platform keeps it pointing at the healthy addresses. A records give only IPs, so you need a fixed port or SRV records to learn the port. Every language already resolves names, so no client library is needed. The weakness is caching: an answer lives for its TTL (time to live, say 30 seconds). So a dead instance can stay in use for about 15 s (registry timeout) plus 30 s (TTL), roughly 45 s in the worst case.","idea":"DNS discovery works everywhere, but caching makes it slow to notice change.","caption":"DNS returns the current addresses, valid for the TTL.","focus":["orders","dns","registry"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"dns","kind":"request","label":"A payments?"},{"from":"dns","to":"registry","kind":"request","label":"healthy?"},{"from":"registry","to":"dns","kind":"response","label":"3 addresses"},{"from":"dns","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"3 IPs, TTL 30"}],"metrics":{"stale":"45"},"chips":[{"node":"dns","text":"TTL 30 s"},{"node":"dns","text":"A records","at":"bottom"},{"node":"orders","text":"45 s stale worst case","tone":"red","at":"top"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"An instance dies","body":"Now payments-3 crashes without warning. It stops sending heartbeats, but the registry removes it only after 15 seconds, and a client with a cached list may keep it even longer. During that window about one call in three fails, because with 3 instances round-robin sends a third of the calls to the dead one. A retry on another instance hides most of this when the request never reached the dead one (a connect failure) or the call is idempotent. For a charge, send an idempotency key so a retry cannot bill twice.","idea":"Failure is never noticed instantly, so callers need timeouts and retries to cover the stale window.","caption":"Calls to the dead instance fail until it is removed.","focus":["orders","pay3","registry"],"flows":[{"from":"pay3","to":"registry","kind":"fail","label":"no heartbeat"},{"from":"orders","to":"pay3","kind":"fail","label":"connect timeout"}],"metrics":{"instances":"3","failed":"33","stale":"15"},"chips":[{"node":"pay3","text":"crashed","tone":"red"},{"node":"registry","text":"removed after 15 s"},{"node":"orders","text":"~33% fail","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The registry itself is down","body":"By now payments-3 has been removed from the list. What if the registry itself becomes unreachable? A client that cached the last good list can keep calling instances, which is why caches are a feature, not a shortcut. New instances will not be found and dead ones will not be removed until the registry returns, but existing traffic keeps flowing. So registries run as replicated clusters, and callers keep serving from their last known list (serve stale) while retrying the registry in the background.","idea":"Run the registry as a cluster, and let callers keep serving from their last known list.","caption":"Orders keeps calling payments-2 from its cached list.","focus":["orders","registry","pay2"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"registry","kind":"fail","label":"unreachable"},{"from":"orders","to":"pay2","kind":"request","label":"cached address"},{"from":"pay2","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"instances":"2","failed":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"registry","text":"unreachable","tone":"red"},{"node":"orders","text":"cached list","tone":"green"},{"node":"pay2","text":"200 OK","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Name in, healthy address out","body":"Instances register when ready and prove they are alive with heartbeats. Callers find them through the registry directly (client-side), through a proxy (server-side), or through DNS. Every approach has a delay between a failure and everyone noticing, so timeouts, retries and caches that survive a registry outage are part of the design. Kubernetes, for instance, gives each Service a stable DNS name and virtual IP, and routes only to pods that pass their readiness checks. Callers still need timeouts and retries.","idea":"Discovery maps names to healthy addresses. The real design work is handling stale answers.","caption":"Register, heartbeat, look up, call, retry.","focus":["orders","dns","registry","lb","pay1","pay2","pay3"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"instances":"2","extra":"1","stale":"15","failed":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"registry","text":"2 registered"},{"node":"lb","text":"+1 ms hop"},{"node":"orders","text":"0% failed","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A service sends heartbeats every 5 seconds and is removed after 3 missed beats. It crashes. About how long can the registry still list it?","options":["Under 1 second","About 5 seconds","Up to about 15 seconds","Until someone deregisters it by hand"],"answer":2,"why":"A crash gives no goodbye. The registry waits for 3 missed 5-second beats, so the dead entry can remain for up to roughly 15 seconds."},{"q":"Your team uses five languages and wants no discovery code in callers and needs instant failover and load-aware routing. Which fits best, and what do you pay?","options":["Client-side discovery; you pay with a stale cache","Server-side discovery; you pay an extra hop and must keep the proxy available","Hard-coded IPs; you pay nothing","Heartbeats; you pay with longer TTLs"],"answer":1,"why":"A proxy keeps logic out of every client, at the cost of one more hop and one more component that must stay highly available. DNS also avoids client code but adds TTL staleness."},{"q":"Users report errors for about a minute after each deploy removes old instances, though DNS records update within seconds. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The registry is storing too many instances","TLS handshakes are too slow","The load balancer has too many healthy backends","Clients cache DNS answers longer than the TTL, so they keep calling old addresses"],"answer":3,"why":"Some runtimes cache DNS beyond the TTL, so they keep using addresses that no longer exist. Check resolver caching and add retries."},{"q":"The registry goes down for two minutes. Orders cached the instance list 10 seconds ago. What should happen?","options":["Orders keeps calling instances from its cached list","Orders stops calling Payments until the registry is back","Payments instances shut themselves down","Orders rebuilds the list using a broadcast"],"answer":0,"why":"Existing traffic can continue from the last known list. Only changes, such as new or dead instances, go unnoticed until the registry returns."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Service registry","def":"A store mapping a service name to the addresses of its currently healthy instances. Examples: Consul, etcd, Eureka, the Kubernetes API."},{"term":"Heartbeat and TTL","def":"Instances report regularly; if reports stop for a set time, the entry is dropped. Shorter means faster detection but more load and false removals."},{"term":"Client-side discovery","def":"The caller queries the registry and picks an instance itself. No extra hop, but every client needs the logic."},{"term":"Server-side discovery","def":"The caller uses a stable proxy address, and the proxy looks up and picks an instance. Simpler clients, one extra hop."},{"term":"DNS-based discovery","def":"A service name resolves to the current addresses. Works with every client, but TTLs and client caches delay updates."},{"term":"Readiness vs liveness","def":"Liveness asks whether the process is alive; readiness asks whether it can take traffic now. Register on readiness, restart on liveness."},{"term":"Stale window","def":"The time between an instance failing and callers stopping use of it. Cover it with timeouts, retries and sensible TTLs."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"outbox-cdc","title":"Outbox Pattern & CDC","subtitle":"Watch one order go from a database write to the search index and cache without ever losing the event.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why writing to a database and a message broker separately can silently lose events","How a transactional outbox makes the data and the event commit together","How change data capture (CDC) reads the database log to publish events without polling","Why delivery is at-least-once and consumers must be idempotent","How the same event stream keeps caches and search indexes in sync"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Client","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"orders","label":"Orders service","kind":"service","x":27,"y":50},{"id":"db","label":"Orders DB + outbox","kind":"db","x":46,"y":50},{"id":"cdc","label":"CDC connector","kind":"worker","x":64,"y":20},{"id":"broker","label":"Event log","kind":"queue","x":88,"y":20},{"id":"updater","label":"Sync consumer","kind":"worker","x":76,"y":50},{"id":"cache","label":"Cache","kind":"cache","x":62,"y":82},{"id":"search","label":"Search index","kind":"storage","x":88,"y":82}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"orders"},{"from":"orders","to":"db"},{"from":"orders","to":"broker"},{"from":"db","to":"cdc"},{"from":"cdc","to":"broker"},{"from":"broker","to":"updater"},{"from":"updater","to":"cache"},{"from":"updater","to":"search"}],"metrics":[{"id":"lost","label":"Lost events/hr","unit":""},{"id":"unsent","label":"Unsent rows","unit":""},{"id":"lag","label":"End-to-end lag","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"The order exists, the search does not","body":"A customer places an order. The Orders service saves it in its database. It also needs to tell the rest of the system: the search index, the cache, the email service. Those live in other systems. How do you make sure the database row and the announcement both happen, or neither does? We assume about 1,000 orders per second in this lesson.","idea":"One business action must change the database and notify others, and those are two different systems.","caption":"An order arrives and is saved in the database.","focus":["client","orders","db"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"POST /orders"},{"from":"orders","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT order"}],"metrics":{"lost":"0","unsent":"0","lag":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"orders","text":"1,000 orders/s"}]},{"tag":"PROBLEM","title":"The dual write","body":"The naive fix: save to the database, then publish to the broker (the message system). These are two separate writes with no shared transaction. If the service crashes, or the broker is unreachable, between them, the order is saved but the event is gone, and search never learns of it. Swapping the order does not help: you may announce an order that then fails to save. If even 0.1 percent of publishes fail at 1,000 per second, that is 1 per second, about 3,600 lost events per hour.","idea":"Two separate writes cannot be made atomic just by ordering them carefully. One of them can always fail alone.","caption":"The row is saved but the publish fails, so the event is lost.","focus":["orders","db","broker"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT order"},{"from":"db","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"ok, committed"},{"from":"orders","to":"broker","kind":"fail","label":"publish fails"}],"metrics":{"lost":"3600"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"committed","tone":"green"},{"node":"broker","text":"publish fails","tone":"red"},{"node":"orders","text":"3,600 lost / hr","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Transactional outbox","body":"Instead of calling the broker, the service writes the event into an outbox table in the same database, inside the same transaction as the order row. A transaction means both rows commit together or neither does. So the event is durable the moment the order is. Publishing becomes a separate, retryable job that reads the outbox. A broker outage can delay events but never lose them. In the HUD, lost stays 0 and one unsent row waits.","idea":"Use the one thing you already trust, a single local transaction, to make the data and the event inseparable.","caption":"The order row and the outbox row commit in one transaction.","focus":["orders","db"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"BEGIN; 2 INSERTs"},{"from":"db","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"COMMIT"}],"metrics":{"lost":"0","unsent":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"2 INSERTs, 1 txn"},{"node":"db","text":"COMMIT","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"orders","text":"0 lost","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Who moves rows to the broker?","body":"Something must read the outbox and publish it. The simple way is a polling publisher. Every few hundred milliseconds it runs a SELECT for unsent rows, publishes them and marks them sent. It works, but it adds constant query load, adds delay up to the poll interval (HUD: 500 ms until the event reaches the log), and needs care to keep per-order ordering when several pollers run. The alternative is to read the database's own change log, which is next. Here the connector box plays the polling relay.","idea":"Polling the outbox is correct and simple. Its costs are query load and added delay.","caption":"A polling relay reads unsent rows, publishes them and marks them sent.","focus":["db","cdc","broker"],"flows":[{"from":"cdc","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"poll unsent rows"},{"from":"db","to":"cdc","kind":"response","label":"new rows"},{"from":"cdc","to":"broker","kind":"async","label":"publish events"},{"from":"cdc","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"mark sent"}],"metrics":{"unsent":"0","lag":"500"},"chips":[{"node":"cdc","text":"polling"},{"node":"db","text":"1 row marked sent"},{"node":"cdc","text":"lag 500 ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CDC","title":"Change data capture from the log","body":"Every database already writes committed changes to a log for crash recovery and replication: the write-ahead log (WAL) in Postgres, the binlog in MySQL. A CDC (change data capture) connector such as Debezium reads that log, turns each new outbox insert into an event and publishes it. It never queries your tables, sees changes in commit order, and typically delivers in tens to a few hundred milliseconds (HUD: 300 ms to reach the log). It remembers its log position so it can resume after a restart.","idea":"CDC turns the database's own commit log into an event stream, with no polling and no extra queries.","caption":"The connector tails the log and publishes the new event.","focus":["db","cdc","broker"],"flows":[{"from":"db","to":"cdc","kind":"replicate","label":"WAL stream"},{"from":"cdc","to":"broker","kind":"async","label":"OrderPlaced"}],"metrics":{"unsent":"0","lag":"300"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"WAL"},{"node":"cdc","text":"lag 300 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"cdc","text":"keeps log position","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Keeping cache and search in sync","body":"A sync consumer reads OrderPlaced from the event log. It adds the order to the search index and deletes or refreshes the cached entry for that customer's order list. The database stays the source of truth. The cache and index are derived copies fed by the stream. They are eventually consistent: a search may miss the new order for a moment. The HUD shows 800 ms in total: about 300 ms to reach the log plus about 500 ms for the consumer to apply it.","idea":"Derived stores follow the stream, so they run slightly behind the database but cannot drift apart forever.","caption":"The consumer updates the index and invalidates the cache.","focus":["broker","updater","cache","search"],"flows":[{"from":"broker","to":"updater","kind":"async","label":"OrderPlaced"},{"from":"updater","to":"search","kind":"request","label":"index order"},{"from":"updater","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"invalidate key"}],"metrics":{"lag":"800"},"chips":[{"node":"search","text":"order indexed"},{"node":"cache","text":"key invalidated"},{"node":"updater","text":"lag 800 ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The connector crashes mid-publish","body":"Suppose the connector publishes an event, then dies before saving its log position. On restart it resumes from the old position and publishes that event again. So delivery is at-least-once, not exactly-once. The fix lives in the consumer. Each event carries a unique id, and a repeat is treated as a no-op, for example by upserting the search document by order id. In the animation, id 7f3a arrives twice and the second copy changes nothing.","idea":"Expect duplicates and make consumers idempotent. Do not count on exactly-once.","caption":"A republished event arrives twice; the consumer ignores the repeat.","focus":["cdc","broker","updater","search"],"flows":[{"from":"cdc","to":"broker","kind":"async","label":"OrderPlaced"},{"from":"broker","to":"updater","kind":"async","label":"event id 7f3a"},{"from":"cdc","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"crash, pos. lost"},{"from":"cdc","to":"broker","kind":"async","label":"OrderPlaced again"},{"from":"broker","to":"updater","kind":"async","label":"same id 7f3a"},{"from":"updater","to":"search","kind":"request","label":"upsert (no-op)"}],"metrics":{"lag":"900"},"chips":[{"node":"cdc","text":"crashed","tone":"red"},{"node":"updater","text":"id 7f3a ×2"},{"node":"search","text":"upsert, no-op","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The broker is down for 90 seconds","body":"Orders keep committing because the service only talks to its own database. Events pile up unsent: at 1,000 per second, about 90,000 are waiting after 90 seconds, and lag grows to match (90 s). When the broker returns, the connector resumes from its saved position and drains the backlog in order. One real risk: the database must keep its log segments for the connector while it is behind, so a long outage can fill the disk. Monitor lag and cap retention.","idea":"The outbox keeps writes working through a broker outage, at the cost of delayed sync and a longer-retained log.","caption":"Events queue up in the database, then drain after recovery.","focus":["orders","db","cdc","broker"],"flows":[{"from":"cdc","to":"broker","kind":"fail","label":"broker down"},{"from":"orders","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"orders commit"}],"metrics":{"unsent":"90000","lag":"90000"},"chips":[{"node":"broker","text":"down 90 s","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"90,000 unsent","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"orders","text":"still committing","tone":"green"},{"node":"cdc","text":"lag 90 s","tone":"red","at":"top"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Atomic write, then reliable fan-out","body":"The dual write loses events because two systems cannot commit together. The outbox puts the event in the same transaction as the data. CDC (or a poller) then moves it to the event log at least once, and idempotent consumers update caches and search indexes. You trade a little freshness and some operational care for never losing a change.","idea":"Commit the event with the data, ship it from the log, and remove duplicates at the consumer.","caption":"Order, outbox, log, consumer: no event is lost.","focus":["orders","db","cdc","broker","updater","cache","search"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"lost":"0","unsent":"0","lag":"800"},"chips":[{"node":"orders","text":"0 lost","tone":"green"},{"node":"updater","text":"idempotent"},{"node":"search","text":"lag 800 ms","at":"bottom"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A service does INSERT into its database, then publishes to a broker. It crashes between the two calls. What is the result?","options":["Both are rolled back automatically","The broker retries and the event is still delivered","The row exists but the event is never published","The event is published but the row is missing"],"answer":2,"why":"The database commit already happened and the broker call never ran. With no shared transaction, nothing remembers that an event is owed."},{"q":"Why does the transactional outbox solve the dual-write problem?","options":["It makes the broker support transactions across systems","The event is written in the same local transaction as the data, so they commit or fail together","It guarantees the broker never goes down","It publishes the event twice to be safe"],"answer":1,"why":"Both the business row and the outbox row live in one database, so a single ordinary transaction makes them atomic. Publishing later is a retryable step."},{"q":"A CDC connector restarts and re-reads a few events it had already published. What should the consumer do?","options":["Crash so the operator notices","Process them again, since duplicates are harmless by default","Ask the connector to delete them from the log","Treat repeats as no-ops using the event ID or an upsert"],"answer":3,"why":"CDC and outbox relays give at-least-once delivery, so duplicates are normal. Idempotent handling makes repeated events safe."},{"q":"Compared with a polling publisher, what is a real advantage of log-based CDC?","options":["It gives exactly-once delivery with no consumer logic","It removes the need for an outbox table in every case","It adds no query load on the tables and sees changes in commit order","It makes the cache strongly consistent with the database"],"answer":2,"why":"CDC reads the commit log, so it does not run repeated SELECTs and it follows commit order. It is still at-least-once and the downstream copies stay eventually consistent."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Dual write","def":"Writing to two systems (database and broker) in separate steps. A failure between them leaves them inconsistent, and events can be lost."},{"term":"Transactional outbox","def":"An outbox table written in the same transaction as the business data. A separate relay publishes its rows, so events are durable once the data is."},{"term":"Polling publisher","def":"A job that periodically selects unsent outbox rows and publishes them. Simple, but adds query load and delay up to the poll interval."},{"term":"CDC","def":"Change data capture: reading a database's commit log (WAL or binlog) to emit row changes as events, in commit order, without querying the tables."},{"term":"Log position / offset","def":"The connector's saved place in the database log. After a restart it resumes there, which is why duplicates are possible but loss is not."},{"term":"Idempotent consumer","def":"A consumer that gives the same result when it sees an event twice, using the event ID or an upsert by key. Required with at-least-once delivery."},{"term":"Outbox vs raw table CDC","def":"Capturing business tables directly leaks your schema to consumers. An outbox row is a deliberate, versioned event contract you control."},{"term":"Operational watch-outs","def":"Monitor connector lag and retained log size. A stalled connector makes the database keep log segments and can fill disk. Clean old outbox rows."}],"isNew":true}]},{"id":"reliability","title":"Reliability","lessons":[{"slug":"redundancy","title":"Redundancy & Failover","subtitle":"Watch a single-path system break, then add spare parts until one failure no longer takes the site down.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How to spot a single point of failure (SPOF) in a request path","Why chained components multiply availability down, and parallel ones multiply failure odds down","The difference between active-active and active-passive redundancy","How health checks and failover work, and what they cost in time and data","Why redundancy fails when the copies share a fate"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"Users","kind":"user","x":9,"y":50},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":30,"y":50},{"id":"monitor","label":"Health checker","kind":"service","x":30,"y":88},{"id":"app-a","label":"App server A","kind":"server","x":56,"y":22},{"id":"app-b","label":"App server B","kind":"server","x":56,"y":62},{"id":"primary","label":"DB primary","kind":"db","x":85,"y":22},{"id":"standby","label":"DB standby","kind":"db","x":85,"y":78}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"lb"},{"from":"lb","to":"app-a"},{"from":"lb","to":"app-b"},{"from":"app-a","to":"primary"},{"from":"app-b","to":"primary"},{"from":"app-a","to":"standby"},{"from":"app-b","to":"standby"},{"from":"primary","to":"standby"},{"from":"monitor","to":"lb"},{"from":"monitor","to":"app-a"},{"from":"monitor","to":"app-b"},{"from":"monitor","to":"standby"},{"from":"monitor","to":"primary"}],"metrics":[{"id":"avail","label":"Availability","unit":"%"},{"id":"down","label":"Downtime/year","unit":"min"},{"id":"failover","label":"Failover time","unit":"s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One machine, one bad day","body":"Picture an online shop running on one app server. Availability is the share of time it works. This server is up 99.9% of the time, called \"three nines\" (each extra nine cuts allowed downtime tenfold). That sounds great, but the missing 0.1% of a year is about 526 minutes, nearly 9 hours when nobody can buy anything. Disks fill, hardware dies and bad releases ship. So how do you keep serving when one part breaks?","idea":"Every machine will fail eventually. The design question is what users see when it does.","caption":"One server, 99.9% available, about 526 minutes of downtime a year.","focus":["app-a"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"avail":"99.9","down":"526"},"chips":[{"node":"app-a","text":"99.9% up"},{"node":"app-a","text":"526 min/yr","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Find the single points of failure","body":"Follow one request through the diagram. It passes a load balancer (it spreads traffic across servers), one app server, then one database. Each is a single point of failure (SPOF): if it dies, the request fails. All three must work at the same time, called \"in series\", so you multiply their availability. 0.9999 x 0.999 x 0.999 is about 99.79%, roughly 1,100 minutes a year. A chain is always less reliable than its weakest link.","idea":"In a chain, availabilities multiply, so the whole is always worse than its worst part.","caption":"One request crosses three parts in a row. Each one is a SPOF.","focus":["lb","app-a","primary"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart"},{"from":"lb","to":"app-a","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart"},{"from":"app-a","to":"primary","kind":"request","label":"SELECT"}],"metrics":{"avail":"99.79","down":"1103"},"chips":[{"node":"lb","text":"99.99%"},{"node":"app-a","text":"99.9%"},{"node":"primary","text":"99.9%"},{"node":"app-a","text":"series ≈ 99.79%","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Add a spare in parallel","body":"We add a second app server, B, beside A, and the balancer can now send requests to either. The app layer fails only when both are down at once. If each is down 0.1% of the time and they fail independently (one failing says nothing about the other), both are down 0.001 x 0.001 = 0.000001 of the time. That layer is 99.9999% available. The whole system rises to about 99.89%, but the single database (99.9%) is now the weakest part.","idea":"Side by side, you multiply failure odds together, which shrinks them fast. This only helps the layer you duplicated.","caption":"The balancer can now pick either of two servers.","focus":["lb","app-a","app-b"],"flows":[{"from":"lb","to":"app-a","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart"},{"from":"lb","to":"app-b","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart"}],"metrics":{"avail":"99.89","down":"579"},"chips":[{"node":"app-a","text":"down 0.1%"},{"node":"app-b","text":"down 0.1%"},{"node":"app-b","text":"tier 99.9999%","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Active-active: all copies work","body":"The animation splits 100 requests a second across both servers, 50 each. This is active-active: every copy works all the time, so when one dies the balancer just stops sending it traffic. The catch is spare room. If each server runs at 70% load and one dies, the survivor must take 140% and falls over too. With two servers, keep each under 50% load. With four, up to 75% each is safe. Planning so the survivors can carry everything is called N+1.","idea":"Active-active needs spare capacity: after a failure, the remaining copies must absorb the whole load.","caption":"100 req/s is split 50/50 across both servers.","focus":["lb","app-a","app-b"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"100 req/s"},{"from":"lb","to":"app-a","kind":"request","label":"50 req/s"},{"from":"lb","to":"app-b","kind":"request","label":"50 req/s"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"app-a","text":"50 req/s"},{"node":"app-b","text":"50 req/s"},{"node":"lb","text":"keep each < 50% load","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Detect the failure and route around it","body":"App server A crashes. The health checker (a small service that pings each server) sends probes (tiny test requests) and gets no reply from A. It probes every 5 seconds and declares A unhealthy after 3 misses in a row, so about 15 seconds pass while users still hit a dead server. Then the balancer is told, stops using A and sends all traffic to B, which answers 200 OK (the standard success reply). Faster probes shorten the gap but risk false alarms from one slow reply.","idea":"Failover means detecting the failure, then rerouting. Detection time is downtime you must plan for.","caption":"Probes to A fail, the balancer is told, and traffic shifts to B.","focus":["monitor","lb","app-a","app-b"],"flows":[{"from":"monitor","to":"app-a","kind":"fail","label":"probe: no reply"},{"from":"monitor","to":"lb","kind":"async","label":"A unhealthy"},{"from":"lb","to":"app-b","kind":"request","label":"all traffic"},{"from":"app-b","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"failover":"15"},"chips":[{"node":"app-a","text":"no reply","tone":"red"},{"node":"monitor","text":"3 misses = down"},{"node":"app-b","text":"200 OK","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Active-passive for stateful parts","body":"Databases are harder to copy than app servers because they hold data, and two copies that both accept writes would disagree. The usual answer is active-passive: one primary takes all writes and streams every change (the moving \"change log\") to a standby that only follows. The copying is often asynchronous, meaning the primary does not wait for the standby, so the standby can lag by milliseconds to seconds. That lag is the data you could lose in a crash. (Server A is repaired and back in rotation.)","idea":"Active-passive keeps one writer to avoid conflicts. Asynchronous copying is fast but can lose the last few writes.","caption":"Apps write to the primary, which streams changes to an idle standby.","focus":["app-a","app-b","primary","standby"],"flows":[{"from":"app-a","to":"primary","kind":"request","label":"INSERT order"},{"from":"app-b","to":"primary","kind":"request","label":"UPDATE cart"},{"from":"primary","to":"standby","kind":"replicate","label":"change log"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"primary","text":"primary · writes"},{"node":"standby","text":"standby · follows"},{"node":"standby","text":"lag ms to s","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Failing over the database","body":"Now the primary dies. The standby sees the change stream stop, and the health checker confirms the primary is silent. Before promoting the standby, the old primary must be fenced off (cut off from network and storage). Otherwise a half-alive old primary and the new one could both accept writes, called split-brain. Then the standby is promoted (made the new primary) and the apps send writes to it. Assume about 30 seconds in total; real systems range from seconds to a couple of minutes. Writes in that window fail or queue, and changes not yet copied may be lost. The whole system now sits near 99.99%, about 55 minutes a year, mostly from the single balancer.","idea":"Stateful failover is slower and can lose recent writes, so measure it instead of assuming it is instant.","caption":"Primary fails, the standby is promoted, and apps move their writes over.","focus":["primary","standby","monitor","app-a","app-b"],"flows":[{"from":"primary","to":"standby","kind":"fail","label":"heartbeat lost"},{"from":"monitor","to":"primary","kind":"fail","label":"probe: no reply"},{"from":"monitor","to":"standby","kind":"handshake","label":"promote"},{"from":"app-a","to":"standby","kind":"request","label":"writes"},{"from":"app-b","to":"standby","kind":"request","label":"writes"}],"metrics":{"failover":"30","avail":"99.989","down":"55"},"chips":[{"node":"primary","text":"down · fenced","tone":"red"},{"node":"standby","text":"promoted","tone":"green"},{"node":"standby","text":"~30 s failover","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The spare you forgot","body":"What is still single? The load balancer and the health checker. In the animation, users reach a dead balancer and fail. The balancer alone causes about 53 of those 55 minutes a year (99.99%). Fix it with two balancers sharing a floating IP (an address that jumps to whichever balancer is alive). DNS (answers may be cached for minutes) or anycast (many sites announce one address and the network sends users to a live one) also work. Add checkers in several places too. That leaves about 1 minute a year, mostly switch-over time. Add 4 database failovers a year at 30 s, which is 2 minutes. Total: about 3 minutes.","idea":"Every layer you forget is still a SPOF. Redundancy is only as good as its least-duplicated part.","caption":"The balancer fails for users. A second balancer and checker would fix it (not drawn).","focus":["users","lb","monitor"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"lb","kind":"fail","label":"LB down"}],"metrics":{"avail":"99.9994","down":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"lb","text":"53 of 55 min/yr","tone":"red"},{"node":"lb","text":"2 LBs · floating IP","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"monitor","text":"several checkers"},{"node":"users","text":"≈ 3 min/yr","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"When copies share a fate","body":"The parallel math assumed independent failures. Here both servers sit on one rack, share one power feed and just received the same buggy release. One cause kills both at once, and the checker gets no reply from either. The pair now acts like one server. Even with a perfect balancer and database, the app layer is only 99.9%, so the system falls back to about 99.9% (roughly 530 minutes a year). Fix this by spreading copies across racks and availability zones (isolated data-centre groups within a region), staggering deploys, and trying each new version on a canary (a small first slice of traffic).","idea":"Redundancy only helps when failures are independent. Correlated failures make two copies behave like one.","caption":"One shared cause takes down both servers at once.","focus":["lb","app-a","app-b"],"flows":[{"from":"monitor","to":"app-a","kind":"fail","label":"probe: no reply"},{"from":"monitor","to":"app-b","kind":"fail","label":"probe: no reply"}],"metrics":{"avail":"99.9","down":"530"},"chips":[{"node":"app-a","text":"no reply","tone":"red"},{"node":"app-b","text":"no reply","tone":"red"},{"node":"lb","text":"shared fate","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Spare parts, honestly counted","body":"Remove SPOFs layer by layer: servers, database, balancer, even the checker. In a chain, availability multiplies down. Side by side, failure odds multiply down. Stateless tiers (no stored data) are easy to run active-active. Stateful ones usually use one writer plus a standby, unless the datastore is built for multiple writers. Count detection and promotion time as downtime, keep spare capacity, and spread copies so one cause cannot hit them all. A later lesson on multi-region extends these ideas.","idea":"Remove SPOFs in every layer, keep failures independent, and budget for the failover itself.","caption":"Redundant at every layer and spread across failure domains.","focus":["users","lb","monitor","app-a","app-b","primary","standby"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"avail":"99.9994","down":"3","failover":"30"},"chips":[{"node":"users","text":"99.9994%","tone":"green"},{"node":"users","text":"3 min/yr","at":"bottom"},{"node":"standby","text":"30 s failover"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A request needs service X and service Y in series. Each is 99% available and they fail independently. Roughly what is the combined availability?","options":["99.99%","99%","98%","100%"],"answer":2,"why":"In series you multiply: 0.99 x 0.99 = 0.9801, about 98%. The chain is worse than either part alone."},{"q":"Two identical servers, each 99% available and failing independently, sit in parallel behind a perfect balancer. What is the best estimate for the pair?","options":["99.99%","99%","98%","99.5%"],"answer":0,"why":"The pair is down only when both are down: 0.01 x 0.01 = 0.0001, so 99.99%. This holds only if failures are truly independent."},{"q":"Two active-active servers each run at 65% of capacity. One crashes. What most likely happens?","options":["Nothing, since the other server was healthy","The survivor needs about 130% of capacity and overloads","The load balancer adds a server automatically","Latency halves because there is less to coordinate"],"answer":1,"why":"The survivor inherits all the traffic, 65% + 65% = 130% of one server. Active-active needs headroom so survivors can carry the full load."},{"q":"Why can promoting a database standby lose data even though it worked as designed?","options":["Standbys cannot store writes","The load balancer drops write requests","Promotion always wipes the new primary","Asynchronous replication may not have delivered the primary's last few changes"],"answer":3,"why":"With asynchronous replication the primary acknowledges writes before the standby has them, so a crash can lose the lag window. Synchronous replication avoids that at the cost of slower writes."},{"q":"You run two app servers in the same rack, deploying the same new build to both at once. Why is your 'parallel' math misleading?","options":["Two servers never beat one","The failures are correlated, so one cause can take both down together","Racks do not support load balancing","The math only works for databases"],"answer":1,"why":"Multiplying failure probabilities requires independence. A shared rack, power feed or bad release breaks that assumption, so the pair acts like one."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"SPOF","def":"Single point of failure: any component whose failure stops the whole service because nothing can replace it."},{"term":"Series vs parallel","def":"Series (all parts must work): multiply availabilities, 0.999 x 0.999 = 0.998. Parallel (any one suffices): 1 - (1 - a)^n, so two 99% copies give 99.99% if independent."},{"term":"Split-brain","def":"Two nodes both believe they are the primary and accept conflicting writes. Prevent it with fencing of the old primary and a quorum or consensus vote before promotion."},{"term":"Nines","def":"99.9% allows about 526 min/year of downtime; 99.99% about 53 min; 99.999% about 5 min."},{"term":"Active-active","def":"All copies serve traffic at once. Failover is just removal from rotation, but spare capacity (N+1) is needed."},{"term":"Active-passive","def":"One copy serves while a standby follows. Suits stateful systems with a single writer; failover needs a promotion step."},{"term":"Health check","def":"A probe such as an HTTP /health call every few seconds. Detection time is roughly interval x failures to mark unhealthy."},{"term":"Failure domain","def":"A group that can fail together (rack, power feed, zone, release). Spread copies across domains so failures stay independent."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"timeouts-retries","title":"Timeouts & Retries","subtitle":"Watch a checkout service survive a flaky payment API, then watch careless retries knock that API over.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How to pick a timeout from observed latency instead of guessing","How exponential backoff with jitter spaces retries out","Why layered retries multiply load into a retry storm","How retry budgets and deadlines cap the damage","Which failures are safe to retry and which are not"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Mobile client","kind":"client","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"checkout","label":"Checkout service","kind":"service","x":32,"y":50},{"id":"payment","label":"Payment API","kind":"service","x":57,"y":50},{"id":"db","label":"Payments DB","kind":"db","x":84,"y":50},{"id":"others","label":"Other callers","kind":"client","x":57,"y":14}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"checkout"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payment"},{"from":"payment","to":"db"},{"from":"others","to":"payment"}],"metrics":[{"id":"timeout","label":"Timeout","unit":"ms"},{"id":"attempts","label":"Max attempts","unit":"tries"},{"id":"load","label":"Payment load","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"ok","label":"Success","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"The payment API gets flaky","body":"A checkout service calls a payment API to charge cards. Normally it answers in about 80 ms. The p99 (the slowest 1 in 100 calls) is 400 ms. Today about 30 percent of calls fail or hang, so 3 in 10 charges go wrong. Should checkout wait forever, give up at once, or try again? Each choice has a cost, and this lesson walks through them.","idea":"Any call over a network can be slow, fail, or never answer, so the caller needs a plan for each case.","caption":"Checkout depends on a payment API that now fails 3 calls in 10.","focus":["checkout","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"charge $42"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"fail","label":"502 / no reply"}],"metrics":{"timeout":"none","attempts":"1","load":"600","ok":"70"},"chips":[{"node":"payment","text":"~80 ms · p99 400"},{"node":"payment","text":"30% fail","tone":"red"},{"node":"checkout","text":"600 req/s"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Set a timeout first","body":"Without a timeout, a hung call holds a thread and a connection for as long as the OS allows, maybe minutes or forever. Enough hung calls and checkout itself runs out of threads. A timeout caps the wait: here checkout gives up at 500 ms and frees its thread. A common rule of thumb is a little above the dependency's p99 (400 ms here), then adjust from real data. Too low aborts calls that would have worked. Also set a shorter connect timeout (100-200 ms) for just opening the connection. Attempts x timeout, plus backoff, must fit the overall deadline.","idea":"A timeout turns an unbounded wait into a bounded one. Base it on measured p99, not on hope.","caption":"Checkout stops waiting at 500 ms and frees its thread.","focus":["checkout","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"charge $42"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"fail","label":"timeout @500ms"}],"metrics":{"timeout":"500","attempts":"1","ok":"70"},"chips":[{"node":"checkout","text":"timeout 500 ms"},{"node":"payment","text":"no reply","tone":"red"},{"node":"checkout","text":"connect 100-200 ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Retry the transient failure","body":"Many failures are brief: a dropped packet, a restarting server, a moment of overload. So trying again often works. (Retrying a charge safely needs idempotency, covered later.) If each attempt fails 30 percent of the time, independently, all three attempts fail only 0.3 x 0.3 x 0.3, about 3 percent. Success climbs from 70 to about 97 percent. This only works when the cause is temporary: a dead service fails every time. Retries also cost load. A call averages 1 + 0.3 + 0.09 = 1.39 attempts, so 600 req/s becomes about 834.","idea":"Retries convert short-lived faults into successes, but only if the next attempt has a real chance of working.","caption":"A second attempt reaches the Payment API and succeeds.","focus":["checkout","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"attempt 1"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"fail","label":"503"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"attempt 2"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"attempts":"3","load":"834","ok":"97"},"chips":[{"node":"payment","text":"attempt 1: 503","tone":"red"},{"node":"payment","text":"attempt 2: 200","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"checkout","text":"3 tries: 97%","tone":"green"},{"node":"payment","text":"834 req/s","at":"right"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Back off exponentially","body":"Retrying instantly hammers a service that is already struggling. Exponential backoff waits longer after each failure, doubling the gap: 100 ms, 200 ms, 400 ms, 800 ms, up to a cap such as 2 seconds. In the animation, the second try waits 100 ms and the third waits 200 ms. The growing gap gives the service room to recover. Pair backoff with a maximum number of attempts, because waiting longer alone does not stop a caller from retrying forever.","idea":"Doubling the wait after every failure gives a struggling service breathing room.","caption":"Each retry waits twice as long as the previous one.","focus":["checkout","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"try 1"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"after 100 ms"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"after 200 ms"}],"metrics":{"attempts":"3","load":"834"},"chips":[{"node":"checkout","text":"waits: 100, 200, 400"},{"node":"checkout","text":"cap 2 s","at":"bottom"},{"node":"payment","text":"room to recover","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Add jitter","body":"Suppose the payment API blips for one second and 5,000 callers all fail together. With plain backoff they all retry at exactly 100 ms, then all at 200 ms, hitting the service in synchronized waves. Jitter adds randomness. With full jitter each caller sleeps a random time between 0 and its current backoff value. In the animation, retries arrive at different moments (+37 ms, +64 ms, +91 ms), spreading into a smooth trickle instead of spikes.","idea":"Jitter breaks up synchronized retry waves; always add it to backoff.","caption":"Many callers retry at random moments instead of all at once.","focus":["others","checkout","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"others","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"retry +37 ms"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"retry +91 ms"},{"from":"others","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"retry +64 ms"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"ok":"97"},"chips":[{"node":"others","text":"5,000 callers"},{"node":"checkout","text":"retry +91 ms"},{"node":"payment","text":"smooth trickle","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The retry storm","body":"Now the payment API is truly overloaded. It normally handles about 1,000 requests per second, but its capacity has halved to about 500. Callers time out and retry. Worse, the client app and checkout each allow 3 attempts, so one user tap can become up to 9 calls to payment (3 x 3). Normal load of 600 req/s can balloon to as much as 5,400, about ten times what payment can serve. That extra load slows payment further and causes even more retries. The helper has become the attacker, called a retry storm.","idea":"Retries at every layer multiply: 3 attempts across 2 layers is up to 9 calls per user action.","caption":"Layered retries pile load onto the already struggling Payment API.","focus":["client","checkout","payment","others"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"checkout","kind":"request","label":"3 tries"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"3 x 3 tries"},{"from":"others","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"more retries"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"fail","label":"overloaded"}],"metrics":{"attempts":"9","load":"5400","ok":"10"},"chips":[{"node":"client","text":"3 attempts"},{"node":"checkout","text":"3 attempts"},{"node":"payment","text":"5,400 req/s","tone":"red"},{"node":"payment","text":"can serve ~500","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Cap it with a retry budget","body":"A retry budget caps retries as a share of total traffic, for example no more than 10 percent of recent requests may be retries. It is often built as a token bucket: each first attempt earns a fraction of a token and each retry spends one. When the bucket is empty, the call fails right away (fail fast). The price is that some calls a retry would have rescued now fail, so success only edges up from 70 to about 77 percent, not 97. But in a real outage load rises just 10 percent, so the service can recover.","idea":"A retry budget puts a hard ceiling on extra load no matter how many things fail.","caption":"Once the budget is spent, checkout fails fast instead of retrying.","focus":["client","checkout","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"first attempts"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"fail","label":"503"},{"from":"checkout","to":"client","kind":"fail","label":"fail fast"}],"metrics":{"attempts":"1 + 10%","load":"660","ok":"77"},"chips":[{"node":"checkout","text":"retries ≤ 10%"},{"node":"payment","text":"660 req/s","tone":"green"},{"node":"client","text":"fails fast"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Retry only what is safe, within a deadline","body":"Retry only errors that may pass: connection errors, timeouts, 502, 503, 504 and 429 (honour a Retry-After hint). Never retry 400, 403 or 404, because the same request fails again. A timeout on a write leaves it unknown whether the work happened, so retrying a charge can bill twice unless it carries an idempotency key (see the Idempotency lesson). Also give the whole user action a deadline, say 2 seconds, and pass the time left downstream so no layer keeps retrying after the user left. Three 500 ms attempts plus 300 ms of backoff is about 1.8 s, which nearly fills that deadline.","idea":"Retry only transient, safe errors, and stop when the overall deadline runs out.","caption":"The time left shrinks as the request moves downstream.","focus":["client","checkout","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"checkout","kind":"request","label":"deadline 2000ms"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"left: ~1400ms"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"},{"from":"checkout","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"timeout":"500","attempts":"1 + 10%","load":"660","ok":"77"},"chips":[{"node":"client","text":"deadline 2 s"},{"node":"checkout","text":"~1400 ms left"},{"node":"checkout","text":"≤ 1.8 s total","at":"bottom"},{"node":"payment","text":"200 OK","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"A safe retry recipe","body":"A safe retry recipe: set a timeout near p99. Retry only temporary errors, with exponential backoff and full jitter. Retry at one layer, not every layer. Cap retries with a budget of about 10 percent, and bound the whole thing with a deadline. Done well, retries hide small faults. Done badly, they turn a small fault into an outage.","idea":"Timeouts bound the wait, backoff and jitter space retries, and budgets and deadlines bound the damage.","caption":"All pieces together keep the system stable when payment stumbles.","focus":["client","checkout","payment"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"timeout":"500","attempts":"1 + 10%","load":"660","ok":"77"},"chips":[{"node":"checkout","text":"timeout 500 ms"},{"node":"checkout","text":"retries ≤ 10%","at":"bottom"},{"node":"payment","text":"660 req/s","tone":"green"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Backoff starts at 100 ms and doubles each time, with full jitter. Roughly how long might a caller wait before its 3rd retry?","options":["Exactly 400 ms","A random time between 0 and 400 ms","A random time between 400 and 800 ms","A random time between 0 and 100 ms"],"answer":1,"why":"The delays are 100, 200, then 400 ms before the 3rd retry. Full jitter picks a random value between 0 and that ceiling."},{"q":"A user taps Pay. The app, the checkout service and the payment API each make up to 3 attempts to the layer below it, ending at the payments database. How many calls can reach the database in the worst case?","options":["3","9","12","27"],"answer":3,"why":"Attempts multiply across layers: 3 x 3 x 3 = 27. This is why retries should usually happen at only one layer."},{"q":"Which failure is the best candidate for an automatic retry?","options":["A 503 response from an idempotent GET request","A 400 response saying the card number is malformed","A 404 for an order id that does not exist","A 403 because the API key is revoked"],"answer":0,"why":"A 503 is often temporary, so a later attempt may succeed. The other errors are caused by the request or credentials and will fail the same way every time."},{"q":"Traffic is 1,000 requests per second and the dependency is completely down. With a retry budget of 10 percent, at most about how many calls per second can it receive?","options":["3,000","2,000","1,100","4,000"],"answer":2,"why":"Retries are capped at about 10 percent of first attempts, so the total cannot exceed roughly 1,100 (1,000 first attempts plus about 100 retries). Without a budget, 3 attempts each would send about 3,000."},{"q":"Why does a timeout set far below the dependency's p99 cause trouble?","options":["It makes backoff delays too long","It disables jitter","It aborts calls that would have succeeded and triggers extra retries","It makes the connect timeout longer than the request timeout"],"answer":2,"why":"If the timeout is under p99, a large share of healthy but slow calls get cancelled and retried, which adds load and wastes the work already done."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Timeout","def":"Maximum time to wait for a reply. Set the request timeout slightly above the dependency's measured p99, use a shorter connect timeout, and set both on every network call. Attempts x timeout plus backoff must fit the deadline."},{"term":"Exponential backoff","def":"Wait base x 2^(n-1) before retry n (n = 1, 2, 3...), up to a cap. Example: 100, 200, 400, 800 ms, capped near 2 s."},{"term":"Full jitter","def":"Sleep a random time between 0 and the current backoff ceiling. Spreads out synchronized retries from many callers."},{"term":"Retry storm","def":"Retries add load to a struggling service, which fails more calls, which causes more retries. Layered retries multiply it."},{"term":"Retry budget","def":"A cap on retries as a share of traffic, often about 10 percent. Past the cap, fail fast instead of retrying."},{"term":"Deadline","def":"One time limit for the whole user action, passed downstream so each layer knows how much time is left."},{"term":"Safe to retry","def":"Retry timeouts, connection errors, 429 (honour Retry-After), 502, 503 and 504 only when the operation is idempotent or carries an idempotency key. Do not retry other 4xx errors such as 400, 403 and 404."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"idempotency","title":"Idempotency & Exactly-Once","subtitle":"Watch a payment get retried after a lost response, and see how an idempotency key stops the customer being charged twice.","minutes":11,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why a retry after a timeout can run the same operation twice","How an idempotency key plus a dedup table makes a request safe to repeat","Why exactly-once delivery is really at-least-once delivery plus idempotent processing","How to make the dedup check and the side effect commit atomically","Where keys expire, collide, or get reused with a different payload"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Mobile app","kind":"client","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"api","label":"Payments API","kind":"service","x":32,"y":50},{"id":"ledger","label":"Ledger + dedup DB","kind":"db","x":56,"y":50},{"id":"queue","label":"Event queue","kind":"queue","x":80,"y":50},{"id":"worker","label":"Receipt worker","kind":"worker","x":92,"y":18},{"id":"workerdb","label":"Processed-IDs DB","kind":"db","x":92,"y":82}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"api"},{"from":"api","to":"ledger"},{"from":"ledger","to":"queue"},{"from":"queue","to":"worker"},{"from":"worker","to":"workerdb"}],"metrics":[{"id":"charges","label":"Charges made","unit":"x"},{"id":"requests","label":"Requests sent","unit":"x"},{"id":"dupes","label":"Dupes blocked","unit":"x"},{"id":"dollars","label":"Customer billed","unit":"$"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Did my payment go through?","body":"A customer taps Pay for a $40 order. The request reaches the server and the charge goes through, but the reply gets lost on a bad mobile network. The app only sees a timeout, so it cannot tell whether the charge happened. Retrying might charge the customer twice. Giving up might lose the order. What should the app do?","idea":"After a timeout, a caller cannot tell 'never arrived' from 'done, but the reply was lost'.","caption":"One tap, one charge, one lost reply.","focus":["client","api"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"charges":"0","requests":"0","dupes":"0","dollars":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"client","text":"no reply · timeout","tone":"red"},{"node":"api","text":"did it charge?"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"The charge succeeds","body":"First, the happy path. The app sends POST /charges (a request to create a charge) for $40. The API writes the charge to the ledger (the database table of payments) and returns 200, meaning success. The ledger now holds one charge. Notice that nothing in this request says 'this is the same order as before'. That will matter in a moment.","idea":"A plain POST has no identity: the server cannot tell a repeat from a new request.","caption":"Ledger records one $40 charge.","focus":["client","api","ledger"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"POST /charges"},{"from":"api","to":"ledger","kind":"request","label":"INSERT $40"},{"from":"ledger","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"ok"},{"from":"api","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"charges":"1","requests":"1","dollars":"40"},"chips":[{"node":"ledger","text":"1 charge · $40","tone":"green"},{"node":"api","text":"200 OK","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The reply is lost","body":"Now the 200 reply never reaches the phone because the connection dropped. After a 10-second timeout, the app thinks the request failed. Retrying is the right instinct (the timeouts and retries lesson covers how to space retries out). But a retry is only safe if repeating the operation is harmless. Right now it is not.","idea":"Retries mean a request may run at least once, possibly more. So duplicates will happen, even if rarely, and you must design for them.","caption":"Response dropped; the client sees a timeout.","focus":["client","api"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"client","kind":"fail","label":"reply lost"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"reply lost","tone":"red"},{"node":"client","text":"timeout 10 s","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The naive retry double-charges","body":"The app retries the same POST. The server has no memory of the first one, so it inserts a second $40 charge. The customer is billed $80 for one order, and a support ticket and refund follow. At scale this adds up. Take 1 million payments a day where 0.1% hit 'first charge worked, reply lost, retry': that is about 1,000 duplicate charges every day.","idea":"Without a guard, every retry of a non-idempotent operation can repeat its side effect.","caption":"Second insert: customer billed $80.","focus":["client","api","ledger"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"POST /charges"},{"from":"api","to":"ledger","kind":"request","label":"INSERT $40 again"},{"from":"ledger","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"ok (2nd charge)"}],"metrics":{"charges":"2","requests":"2","dollars":"80"},"chips":[{"node":"ledger","text":"2 charges · $80","tone":"red"},{"node":"client","text":"billed $80","tone":"red"},{"node":"api","text":"~1,000 dupes/day","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Add an idempotency key","body":"Let us rewind and replay the same order, this time with a key. An operation is idempotent if doing it many times has the same effect as doing it once. Setting a name with PUT /users/42 is idempotent by design: repeating it leaves the same state. Creating a charge is not. So the client makes a unique key (a random UUID) for each user action, sends it in an Idempotency-Key header, and reuses the same key on every retry.","idea":"The client names the intent once. The server uses that name to turn repeats into one effect.","caption":"Same key on every attempt for this order.","focus":["client","api"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"key: 7f3a..."}],"metrics":{"charges":"0","requests":"1","dollars":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"client","text":"key 7f3a…"},{"node":"api","text":"Idempotency-Key hdr"},{"node":"client","text":"UUID per action","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"First attempt claims the key","body":"The API looks the key up in a dedup table (a table of keys already seen) that lives in the same database as the ledger. The key is new, so in one atomic transaction (all-or-nothing) the API stores the key, the $40 charge, and the response to send back. They commit together. The key column has a unique constraint, which means the database refuses a second row with the same key. So if two attempts arrive at once, exactly one wins.","idea":"A unique constraint plus a transaction makes 'check then act' atomic. Checking first in app code is a race.","caption":"Key and charge commit together.","focus":["api","ledger"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"ledger","kind":"request","label":"claim 7f3a"},{"from":"ledger","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"new key"},{"from":"api","to":"ledger","kind":"request","label":"INSERT $40+reply"}],"metrics":{"charges":"1","requests":"1","dollars":"40"},"chips":[{"node":"ledger","text":"7f3a · saved"},{"node":"ledger","text":"1 charge · $40","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"api","text":"unique key"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"The retry is recognised","body":"The reply is lost again, and the app retries with the same key. The API finds the key already stored, so it does not insert a second charge. It returns the saved response from the first attempt instead. The customer sees success, and the ledger still shows exactly one charge. In the animation, the retry costs one cheap lookup instead of a second payment.","idea":"A repeat returns the original result. It must not run the side effect again.","caption":"Duplicate blocked; stored reply returned.","focus":["client","api","ledger"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"retry key 7f3a"},{"from":"api","to":"ledger","kind":"request","label":"seen?"},{"from":"ledger","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"yes + reply"},{"from":"api","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"200 (saved)"}],"metrics":{"charges":"1","requests":"2","dupes":"1","dollars":"40"},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"key seen"},{"node":"api","text":"no 2nd charge","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"ledger","text":"1 charge · $40","tone":"green"},{"node":"client","text":"200 (saved)","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Exactly-once is a stack of at-least-once","body":"The ledger also announces 'charge created' on a queue, reliably (for example with a transactional outbox: the event is saved in the same transaction, then sent by a relay). Queues usually deliver at-least-once: if a worker crashes before confirming, the message comes back. True exactly-once delivery over a network is not possible in general. So the worker keeps a table of processed message IDs, saves message 91, and skips it on try 2. An outside action like an email cannot join that transaction: pass the message ID to the provider as its own key, or accept a rare duplicate.","idea":"Exactly-once effect = at-least-once delivery + idempotent processing. Save the dedup record in the same transaction as the effect.","caption":"Worker checks its processed IDs and skips the redelivery.","focus":["ledger","queue","worker","workerdb"],"flows":[{"from":"ledger","to":"queue","kind":"async","label":"charge.created"},{"from":"queue","to":"worker","kind":"async","label":"msg 91 (try 1)"},{"from":"worker","to":"workerdb","kind":"request","label":"seen 91? no: save"},{"from":"queue","to":"worker","kind":"async","label":"msg 91 (try 2)"},{"from":"worker","to":"workerdb","kind":"request","label":"seen 91?"},{"from":"workerdb","to":"worker","kind":"response","label":"yes, skip"}],"metrics":{"dupes":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"queue","text":"at-least-once"},{"node":"worker","text":"try 2 · skipped","tone":"green"},{"node":"workerdb","text":"seen 91"}]},{"tag":"PITFALL","title":"Edge cases that bite","body":"Keys need care. They are kept with a TTL (time to live, meaning they are deleted after a set time such as 24 hours). Pick a window longer than any client retry or queue redelivery, or a late retry would run again. If the same key arrives with a different amount, reject it with an error such as 422 instead of guessing, as the animation shows. If the first attempt is still running, a concurrent retry should wait or get a 409 Conflict, not start a second run. Make keys random per action, never just the user ID.","idea":"Scope the key per action, bind it to the request payload, expire it deliberately, and handle in-flight duplicates.","caption":"Key reused with a new amount is rejected.","focus":["client","api","ledger"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"7f3a, $90"},{"from":"api","to":"client","kind":"fail","label":"422 mismatch"}],"metrics":{"requests":"3","dupes":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"422 mismatch","tone":"red"},{"node":"client","text":"7f3a, $90"},{"node":"ledger","text":"key TTL 24 h","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Safe to retry","body":"Networks lose replies, so callers must retry, and retries create duplicates. The fix has two sides. The client sends one key per intent. The server claims the key atomically with the effect, returns the stored result for repeats, and expires keys after a window. Queue consumers do the same with message IDs. That is how systems get exactly-once effects from at-least-once delivery.","idea":"Assume duplicates will happen and make them harmless.","caption":"One order, one charge, however many retries.","focus":["client","api","ledger","queue","worker","workerdb"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"charges":"1","dupes":"2","dollars":"40"},"chips":[{"node":"ledger","text":"1 charge · $40","tone":"green"},{"node":"client","text":"1 key per intent"},{"node":"workerdb","text":"dupes skipped","tone":"green"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A client times out waiting for a charge response and retries with no idempotency key. What is the real risk?","options":["The server will reject the retry as malformed","The retry will always be faster","The charge may run twice, because the first may have succeeded","The database will deadlock"],"answer":2,"why":"A timeout does not tell the client whether the first request executed. Without a key the server cannot recognise the repeat, so a successful first attempt plus a retry equals two charges."},{"q":"Your handler does: SELECT key; if absent, INSERT the ledger charge, then INSERT key. Two identical requests arrive at the same moment. What goes wrong?","options":["Both see the key absent and both record a charge","Nothing, SELECT is atomic","The second request is blocked by the first automatically","The key is stored twice but only one charge occurs"],"answer":0,"why":"Check-then-act is a race. Both requests can pass the SELECT before either inserts. Claim the key with a unique-constraint INSERT in the same transaction as the ledger write so only one can win."},{"q":"Which statement about exactly-once processing is most accurate?","options":["Modern brokers guarantee each message is delivered exactly once over the network","It is achieved by disabling retries","It only matters for databases, not queues","Exactly-once effects come from at-least-once delivery combined with an idempotent consumer"],"answer":3,"why":"A sender cannot know if a lost ack means the message was processed, so it must redeliver. Exactly-once effect comes from deduplicating by message ID on the consumer side."},{"q":"A request arrives with a known idempotency key but a different amount than the stored one. What should the API do?","options":["Return the stored response for the old amount","Reject it with an error because the key was reused with a different request","Process it as a new charge","Silently overwrite the stored record"],"answer":1,"why":"The same key must mean the same intent. A different payload signals a client bug or misuse, and guessing could charge the wrong amount or hide the error. 422 (or 400) is typical for a payload mismatch; 409 is for a duplicate still in flight."},{"q":"Why is PUT /users/42 with name set to Ana naturally idempotent while POST /charges is not?","options":["PUT is faster","Repeating PUT sets the same final state, while each POST creates a new record","PUT is encrypted","POST cannot be retried"],"answer":1,"why":"Setting a resource to a fixed value gives the same state no matter how many times it runs. Creating a new record each call changes state every time, so it needs a key."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Idempotent operation","def":"Applying it N times leaves the system in the same state as applying it once. Setting a value is idempotent; incrementing a counter is not."},{"term":"Idempotency key","def":"A client-generated unique ID (such as a UUID) sent with every attempt of one logical action so the server can recognise repeats."},{"term":"Dedup table","def":"Storage of seen keys or message IDs, often with the saved response, protected by a unique constraint. Uses a TTL (e.g. 24 hours) longer than any retry or redelivery window."},{"term":"At-least-once delivery","def":"The sender retries until acknowledged, so a message may arrive more than once but is never silently lost. The common broker default."},{"term":"Exactly-once effect","def":"Not exactly-once delivery. It is at-least-once delivery plus an idempotent consumer that records its dedup ID in the same transaction as the effect, or passes the ID to an external system as its key."},{"term":"Atomic claim","def":"Insert the key under a unique constraint in the same transaction as the effect. Avoid check-then-act, which races under concurrency."},{"term":"Payload binding","def":"Store a hash of the request with the key. Same key with a different payload is an error, not a replay."},{"term":"Natural idempotency","def":"Designing operations as 'set X to V' or 'upsert by ID' so repeats are harmless without any key table."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"circuit-breakers","title":"Circuit Breakers & Bulkheads","subtitle":"Watch one slow dependency drain a whole service, then watch a breaker, a bulkhead and a fallback stop it.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why a slow dependency is more dangerous than a dead one","How a circuit breaker moves between closed, open and half-open","How failing fast frees threads and gives the sick service room to recover","How bulkheads cap the damage one dependency can do to shared pools","How to pick a fallback that degrades the page instead of breaking it"],"nodes":[{"id":"user","label":"Shoppers","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"shop","label":"Shop API","kind":"service","x":31,"y":50},{"id":"cb","label":"Circuit breaker","kind":"gateway","x":55,"y":28},{"id":"rec","label":"Recommendations","kind":"service","x":82,"y":28},{"id":"pay","label":"Payments","kind":"external","x":82,"y":70},{"id":"cache","label":"Popular items cache","kind":"cache","x":55,"y":86}],"edges":[{"from":"user","to":"shop"},{"from":"shop","to":"cb"},{"from":"cb","to":"rec"},{"from":"shop","to":"pay"},{"from":"cb","to":"cache"}],"metrics":[{"id":"threads","label":"Busy threads","unit":"of 200"},{"id":"errors","label":"Page errors","unit":"%"},{"id":"p99","label":"Page p99","unit":"ms"},{"id":"recrate","label":"Calls to recs","unit":"/s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"A nice-to-have takes down the page","body":"A shop's product page calls two services. Recommendations is the 'you may also like' row, a nice-to-have. Payments is essential. The Shop API handles 500 requests per second with 200 worker threads (a thread handles one request at a time). Recommendations normally answers in 40 ms, so about 20 threads wait on it at any moment (500 x 0.04 s), plus a couple for other work: 22 busy. A thin wrapper sits in between, a plain pass-through for now. What happens when that one optional service stops answering?","idea":"A dependency you could live without can still sink you if you keep waiting on it.","caption":"Healthy baseline: 500 req/s, about 22 busy threads.","focus":["shop","rec","pay"],"flows":[{"from":"user","to":"shop","kind":"request","label":"GET /product"},{"from":"shop","to":"cb","kind":"request","label":"get recs"},{"from":"cb","to":"rec","kind":"request"},{"from":"rec","to":"cb","kind":"response","label":"40 ms"},{"from":"cb","to":"shop","kind":"response","label":"recs"},{"from":"shop","to":"pay","kind":"request","label":"charge"}],"metrics":{"threads":"22","errors":"0","p99":"120","recrate":"500"},"chips":[{"node":"rec","text":"40 ms"},{"node":"shop","kind":"progress","value":11,"text":"11%"},{"node":"shop","text":"500 req/s","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Slow is worse than down","body":"Recommendations now hangs for 30 seconds before failing. A crashed service usually refuses connections instantly, so the caller moves on. A hung one holds a thread until a timeout fires. By Little's law (calls in progress = arrival rate x time each takes), 500 per second x 30 seconds wants 15,000 threads, but we only have 200. The pool is full in under half a second. Now even checkout, which never needed recommendations, queues for a thread and fails with 503.","idea":"Slow calls hold resources. Running out of them turns one sick dependency into a full outage, called a cascading failure.","caption":"Threads pile up waiting on Recommendations, so checkout fails too.","focus":["shop","rec"],"flows":[{"from":"shop","to":"cb","kind":"request","label":"get recs"},{"from":"cb","to":"rec","kind":"request"},{"from":"rec","to":"cb","kind":"fail","label":"no reply"},{"from":"user","to":"shop","kind":"request","label":"GET /checkout"},{"from":"shop","to":"user","kind":"fail","label":"503"}],"metrics":{"threads":"200","errors":"60","p99":"30000","recrate":"500"},"chips":[{"node":"rec","text":"no reply · 30 s","tone":"red"},{"node":"shop","kind":"progress","value":100,"text":"100%"},{"node":"shop","text":"503 on checkout","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Closed: counting quietly","body":"Let us rewind to the healthy moment to see what a circuit breaker does normally. It is a small wrapper around calls to one dependency. Like an electrical breaker, 'closed' means current flows: every call goes through. The breaker watches recent results in a rolling window (say the last 10 seconds) and counts failures and timeouts. In this normal state it costs almost nothing, just a counter.","idea":"Closed is the normal state: pass everything through and keep score.","caption":"Breaker closed: calls pass, outcomes are counted.","focus":["shop","cb","rec"],"flows":[{"from":"shop","to":"cb","kind":"request","label":"get recs"},{"from":"cb","to":"rec","kind":"request"},{"from":"rec","to":"cb","kind":"response","label":"ok"},{"from":"cb","to":"shop","kind":"response","label":"recs"}],"metrics":{"threads":"22","errors":"0","p99":"120","recrate":"500"},"chips":[{"node":"cb","text":"CLOSED","tone":"green"},{"node":"cb","text":"10 s window","at":"bottom"},{"node":"rec","text":"ok","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Trip: closed becomes open","body":"Now Recommendations hangs again. Suppose the rule is: once the window holds at least 20 calls, trip if 50% or more failed. A short per-call timeout (500 ms here) turns each hang into a counted failure. It takes a few seconds for enough failures to pile up, so the breaker trips soon, not instantly. A timeout alone cannot save us: 500 calls/s x 0.5 s is about 250 calls in flight, more than 200 threads. Once the limit is crossed, the breaker opens. These numbers are rules of thumb you tune from real traffic.","idea":"A timeout makes slowness countable. The failure-rate threshold decides when to trip.","caption":"Failures cross 50%: the breaker trips open.","focus":["cb","rec"],"flows":[{"from":"cb","to":"rec","kind":"request","label":"call"},{"from":"rec","to":"cb","kind":"fail","label":"timeout 500ms"},{"from":"cb","to":"rec","kind":"request","label":"call"},{"from":"rec","to":"cb","kind":"fail","label":"timeout 500ms"}],"metrics":{"threads":"200","errors":"35","p99":"600","recrate":"500"},"chips":[{"node":"cb","text":"OPEN","tone":"red"},{"node":"cb","text":"≥20 calls, ≥50% fail","at":"bottom"},{"node":"rec","text":"timeout 500 ms","tone":"red"},{"node":"shop","kind":"progress","value":100,"text":"100%"}]},{"tag":"FAIL FAST","title":"Open: reject in a millisecond","body":"While open, the breaker does not call Recommendations at all. It answers at once with an error the Shop API can handle. Each request now holds a thread for about a millisecond instead of 500 ms or more, so busy threads fall to just a few. Page errors drop as the last hung calls drain, and the row is simply left out until the next step adds a fallback. The sick service also stops getting 500 calls per second, which gives it quiet time to recover.","idea":"Failing fast protects the caller's threads and gives the sick service room to heal.","caption":"Calls rejected locally; Recommendations is left alone.","focus":["shop","cb"],"flows":[{"from":"shop","to":"cb","kind":"request","label":"get recs"},{"from":"cb","to":"shop","kind":"fail","label":"open: rejected"}],"metrics":{"threads":"3","errors":"5","p99":"40","recrate":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"cb","text":"OPEN · rejects","tone":"red"},{"node":"shop","text":"3 busy threads","tone":"green"},{"node":"rec","text":"0 calls/s","tone":"green"},{"node":"cb","text":"~1 ms per call","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FALLBACK","title":"Degrade instead of erroring","body":"A rejected call still needs an answer. For optional data, the breaker's fallback (a backup answer) returns a cached list of the 20 most popular items, refreshed every few minutes. Shoppers see a slightly less personal row and nothing else changes. Pick fallbacks that are cheap and do not depend on the failing service: a cache, a default value, or hiding the widget. Never fall back to calling another fragile service.","idea":"Graceful degradation: return a worse answer instead of no answer.","caption":"Breaker serves popular items from cache.","focus":["shop","cb","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"cb","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"top 20"},{"from":"cache","to":"cb","kind":"response","label":"popular items"},{"from":"cb","to":"shop","kind":"response","label":"fallback recs"},{"from":"shop","to":"user","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"threads":"4","errors":"0","p99":"45","recrate":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"cb","text":"fallback served","tone":"green"},{"node":"cache","text":"top 20 popular"},{"node":"shop","text":"200 OK","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Half-open: let a few probes through","body":"Staying open forever would be a bug. After a cool-down (say 30 seconds) the breaker goes half-open: it lets a few trial calls through, here 3, and holds back everyone else. In the animation all 3 probes succeed, so the breaker closes and normal traffic resumes. In this simple model, if any probe fails, the breaker snaps back to open and the cool-down restarts.","idea":"Half-open tests recovery with a trickle, so a healing service is not flooded by the full crowd.","caption":"Probes succeed: breaker closes, recs return.","focus":["cb","rec"],"flows":[{"from":"cb","to":"rec","kind":"request","label":"probe 1 of 3"},{"from":"rec","to":"cb","kind":"response","label":"ok 45 ms"},{"from":"cb","to":"rec","kind":"request","label":"probe 2 of 3"},{"from":"rec","to":"cb","kind":"response","label":"ok 41 ms"},{"from":"cb","to":"rec","kind":"request","label":"probe 3 of 3"},{"from":"rec","to":"cb","kind":"response","label":"ok 43 ms"},{"from":"cb","to":"shop","kind":"response","label":"closed again"}],"metrics":{"threads":"22","errors":"0","p99":"120","recrate":"500"},"chips":[{"node":"cb","text":"half-open"},{"node":"rec","text":"3 of 3 probes ok","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"BULKHEAD","title":"Bulkheads cap the blast radius","body":"Ships have watertight compartments so one leak cannot sink the hull. Do the same with thread pools. Here Recommendations gets its own limit of 40 concurrent calls out of the 200 threads (normal use is about 20, so there is room for bursts). If it hangs again, only those 40 slots fill and extra recommendation calls are rejected at once. Payments keeps its own pool, so checkout carries on untouched. The breaker will soon trip as well.","idea":"A bulkhead caps how much of a shared resource any one dependency may use.","caption":"Recs pool full and rejecting; payments unaffected.","focus":["shop","cb","pay"],"flows":[{"from":"shop","to":"cb","kind":"request","label":"get recs"},{"from":"cb","to":"shop","kind":"fail","label":"pool full: 40/40"},{"from":"user","to":"shop","kind":"request","label":"POST /pay"},{"from":"shop","to":"pay","kind":"request","label":"charge"},{"from":"pay","to":"shop","kind":"response","label":"ok"}],"metrics":{"threads":"42","errors":"0","p99":"600","recrate":"80"},"chips":[{"node":"cb","text":"pool 40/40 full","tone":"red"},{"node":"pay","text":"charge ok","tone":"green"},{"node":"pay","text":"own pool","at":"bottom"},{"node":"shop","kind":"progress","value":21,"text":"21%"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Where breakers go wrong","body":"Breakers can go wrong. They should trip on timeouts and server errors, not client mistakes like 404 or 400. Choose which statuses count (429 and 503 often should). A breaker per instance only sees its own traffic, so a low-traffic caller may never reach the 20-call minimum. A threshold too tight opens on a blip. Too loose reacts late. A silently stale fallback needs alerts so people notice the breaker is open. And never retry a rejection from an open breaker: it only adds load (see Timeouts & Retries).","idea":"Breakers need sensible failure definitions, minimum volume, a visible state and a harmless fallback.","caption":"Tuning and monitoring decide whether it helps.","focus":["cb"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"cb","text":"min 20 calls"},{"node":"cb","text":"404 / 400 not counted","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Fail fast, isolate, degrade","body":"A slow dependency exhausts threads, so a timeout turns slowness into a countable failure. The breaker moves closed, then open, then half-open: it stops calling a sick service and tests recovery gently. A bulkhead gives each dependency its own capped pool. A fallback turns a rejected call into a lesser but working answer. Together they keep one broken optional feature from becoming a total outage.","idea":"Timeouts detect, breakers stop, bulkheads contain, fallbacks soften.","caption":"Page stays up while recommendations are down.","focus":[],"flows":[],"metrics":{"threads":"4","errors":"0","p99":"45","recrate":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"cb","text":"fail fast","tone":"green"},{"node":"shop","text":"0% errors","tone":"green"},{"node":"cache","text":"popular items","at":"bottom"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Recommendations goes from 40 ms to hanging for 30 s. Why is that usually more dangerous than it simply being down?","options":["Hung calls hold threads and connections, so the caller runs out of capacity","Hung calls use more network bandwidth than refused ones","A hung service writes corrupted data into shared databases","The load balancer removes the caller from rotation immediately"],"answer":0,"why":"A dead service refuses quickly and frees the thread. A slow one keeps each thread occupied, and at 500 req/s the pool of 200 fills in well under a second."},{"q":"A breaker is open. What does it do with a new call?","options":["Queues it until the dependency recovers","Retries it against the dependency three times","Rejects it immediately, or returns a fallback, without calling the dependency","Sends it to the dependency but ignores the response"],"answer":2,"why":"Open means the dependency is not contacted. The call returns at once with an error or fallback, which frees the thread and relieves the dependency."},{"q":"Why does the half-open state allow only a few probe calls rather than all traffic?","options":["To measure latency percentiles accurately","To avoid hitting a barely recovered service with full load and knocking it over again","Because half-open only applies to read-only calls","To keep the cache warm"],"answer":1,"why":"A recovering service may be fragile. A trickle of trial calls tests health cheaply, and a single failure reopens the breaker."},{"q":"How is a bulkhead different from a circuit breaker?","options":["A bulkhead reacts to failure rates; a breaker only counts threads","They are two names for the same mechanism","A bulkhead only works for databases","A bulkhead is a fixed cap on concurrent use per dependency; a breaker reacts to observed failures"],"answer":3,"why":"The bulkhead is a static limit that works even before failures are measured. The breaker is dynamic and based on outcomes. They are used together."},{"q":"The checkout 'charge card' call fails. Which fallback is most appropriate?","options":["Tell the user clearly that payment could not be completed and let them retry","Report the payment as successful and reconcile later without telling the user","Show a cached list of popular items","Silently skip payment and ship the order"],"answer":0,"why":"Fallbacks suit optional data. Essential, money-moving operations must not pretend to succeed; the right degradation is an honest error or a retry-safe path."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Closed","def":"Normal state. Calls pass through while the breaker counts recent failures in a rolling window."},{"term":"Open","def":"Tripped state. Calls are rejected immediately or served a fallback; the dependency is left alone."},{"term":"Half-open","def":"After a cool-down, a few probe calls test recovery. Success closes the breaker; failure reopens it."},{"term":"Example settings","def":"Example only (libraries differ): trip at 50% failures once the window has 20+ calls, stay open 30 s, allow 3 probes. Tune from real traffic."},{"term":"Timeout first","def":"A breaker needs timeouts to turn hangs into failures it can count. Keep timeouts short."},{"term":"Bulkhead","def":"A per-dependency cap on concurrent calls, threads or connections, so one dependency cannot consume the shared pool."},{"term":"Fallback","def":"A cheap substitute answer: cache, default, or hiding the feature. Never use for money-moving writes."},{"term":"What to count","def":"Count timeouts and 5xx errors, often 429 and 503 too, and slow calls if your library supports it. Usually not 4xx client errors like 400 or 404."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"backpressure","title":"Backpressure & Load Shedding","subtitle":"Watch a traffic spike bury an unbounded queue, then see bounded queues, shedding and admission control keep a service alive.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why an unbounded queue turns a short spike into a long outage","How queue length converts directly into waiting time","How bounded queues and fast rejection protect latency","How to shed low-priority work first and reject at the edge","How adaptive concurrency limits track a slowing dependency"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"Interactive users","kind":"user","x":9,"y":28},{"id":"batch","label":"Batch jobs","kind":"client","x":9,"y":80},{"id":"gw","label":"Admission control","kind":"gateway","x":31,"y":54},{"id":"queue","label":"Request queue","kind":"queue","x":52,"y":30},{"id":"workers","label":"Worker pool","kind":"worker","x":72,"y":54},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":91,"y":30}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"gw"},{"from":"batch","to":"gw"},{"from":"gw","to":"queue"},{"from":"queue","to":"workers"},{"from":"workers","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"arrivals","label":"Arrivals","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"depth","label":"Queue depth","unit":"items"},{"id":"wait","label":"Queue wait","unit":"ms"},{"id":"rejected","label":"Rejected","unit":"req/s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"What happens when work arrives faster than it leaves?","body":"Picture a service where a pool of workers finishes at most 100 requests per second. Requests wait in a queue (a line) until a worker is free. Today 80 requests per second arrive, 80% of the ceiling, so the line stays short. Every real system has a ceiling like this. The question is what it does when arrivals go above it, even briefly.","idea":"Every service has a maximum throughput. The design question is how it behaves beyond it.","caption":"Arrivals sit below capacity, so the queue stays small.","focus":["users","gw","queue","workers"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"80 req/s"},{"from":"gw","to":"queue","kind":"request"},{"from":"queue","to":"workers","kind":"request"},{"from":"workers","to":"db","kind":"request"},{"from":"db","to":"workers","kind":"response"}],"metrics":{"arrivals":"80","depth":"5","wait":"50","rejected":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"gw","text":"80 req/s"},{"node":"workers","kind":"progress","value":80,"text":"80%"},{"node":"workers","text":"ceiling 100 req/s","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"A spike makes the queue grow","body":"A marketing email lands and arrivals jump to 150 requests per second. Workers still finish only 100, so 50 extra requests per second pile up in the queue. After 10 seconds the queue holds 500 requests, and a new request waits 5 seconds. A queue is a buffer that absorbs short bursts, which is useful, but only if the burst ends before the buffer fills.","idea":"Queue growth = arrivals minus capacity. A buffer hides overload only while it has room.","caption":"150 in, 100 out: the queue grows by 50 per second.","focus":["users","gw","queue","workers"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"150 req/s"},{"from":"gw","to":"queue","kind":"request","label":"150 req/s"},{"from":"queue","to":"workers","kind":"request","label":"100 req/s"}],"metrics":{"arrivals":"150","depth":"500","wait":"5000","rejected":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"gw","text":"150 req/s"},{"node":"workers","text":"100 req/s"},{"node":"queue","text":"500 queued · 5 s","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The unbounded queue","body":"Suppose the queue has no size limit. After 60 seconds of the spike it holds 3,000 requests (50 x 60). A new request waits behind all of them: 3,000 divided by 100 per second is 30 seconds. The service never returns an error. It just gets slower and slower, and memory use climbs until the process may crash.","idea":"Wait time = queue length divided by service rate. A long queue means a long wait for everyone.","caption":"3,000 items deep: every new request waits 30 seconds.","focus":["queue","workers"],"flows":[{"from":"gw","to":"queue","kind":"request","label":"keeps accepting"}],"metrics":{"arrivals":"150","depth":"3000","wait":"30000","rejected":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"queue","text":"3,000 queued","tone":"red"},{"node":"queue","text":"30 s wait","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"gw","text":"keeps accepting"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Work nobody is waiting for","body":"Say the spike ends and arrivals fall back to 80 per second, but the 3,000-item backlog remains. Clients, proxies and impatient users often give up after about 5 seconds. A request that waited 30 seconds was abandoned before a worker started it, yet the worker still does the full work and the answer is thrown away. Workers stay 100% busy but almost nothing useful comes out. Draining 3,000 items at a net 20 per second (100 out minus 80 in) takes 150 seconds. Workers can check each request's deadline and skip expired ones, one more reason to keep queues short.","idea":"Long queues make workers burn capacity on stale requests, so overload outlasts the spike that caused it.","caption":"Stale requests reach workers after clients gave up.","focus":["queue","workers","db","gw"],"flows":[{"from":"queue","to":"workers","kind":"fail","label":"30 s old"},{"from":"workers","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"wasted work"}],"metrics":{"arrivals":"80","depth":"3000","wait":"30000","rejected":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"queue","text":"30 s old","tone":"red"},{"node":"workers","text":"100% busy · 0 useful","tone":"red"},{"node":"queue","text":"drain 150 s","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Bound the queue","body":"Cap the queue at 200 items. At 100 requests per second that is at most 2 seconds of wait, inside the caller's 5-second timeout. When the queue is full, new requests get an immediate 503 or 429 error instead of a slow death. This is backpressure: the system pushes the overload signal back to the sender, which can slow down, retry later or show a message. Other forms make the sender wait instead, like a blocked write on a full buffer or a shrinking TCP window.","idea":"A bounded queue caps latency and turns overload into a fast, explicit error callers can react to.","caption":"Queue full at 200: excess requests are refused instantly.","focus":["gw","queue","users"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"150 req/s"},{"from":"gw","to":"queue","kind":"request","label":"100 accepted"},{"from":"gw","to":"users","kind":"fail","label":"503 queue full"}],"metrics":{"arrivals":"150","depth":"200","wait":"2000","rejected":"50"},"chips":[{"node":"queue","text":"200 max · 2 s","tone":"green"},{"node":"gw","text":"503 queue full","tone":"red"},{"node":"gw","text":"100 accepted","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Shed the least important work","body":"Rejecting at random hurts checkout as much as a nightly report. So tag requests with a priority. Say 50 of the 150 requests per second come from batch jobs. The gateway rejects those first and keeps interactive traffic flowing. Batch work can wait or retry in an hour, while a shopper who sees an error leaves. This is load shedding: deliberately dropping some work to protect the rest.","idea":"Shedding by priority spends the unavoidable loss on the work that matters least.","caption":"Batch requests get 503; interactive requests go through.","focus":["batch","gw","users"],"flows":[{"from":"batch","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"50 req/s"},{"from":"gw","to":"batch","kind":"fail","label":"503 shed"},{"from":"users","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"100 req/s"},{"from":"gw","to":"queue","kind":"request","label":"interactive"}],"metrics":{"arrivals":"150","depth":"60","wait":"600","rejected":"50"},"chips":[{"node":"batch","text":"50 req/s · shed","tone":"red"},{"node":"users","text":"100 req/s kept","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Reject early, reject cheaply","body":"Admission control means deciding at the front door, before any real work starts. A rejection costs almost nothing. A request that is queued and then dropped has already used memory, a connection and maybe a database call. The gate can look at queue depth, number in flight, or the age of the oldest item, and aim for a wait target such as 500 ms. A Retry-After header tells polite clients when to come back.","idea":"The cheapest place to say no is before the request enters the system.","caption":"The gateway keeps expected wait near 500 ms.","focus":["gw","queue"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"gw","kind":"request"},{"from":"gw","to":"users","kind":"fail","label":"503 + Retry-After"},{"from":"gw","to":"queue","kind":"request","label":"admitted"}],"metrics":{"arrivals":"150","depth":"50","wait":"500","rejected":"50"},"chips":[{"node":"gw","text":"reject at the door"},{"node":"gw","text":"503 + Retry-After","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"queue","text":"target wait 500 ms","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Fixed limits go stale","body":"A fixed limit is a guess about capacity, and capacity changes. Say a limit of 100 req/s was tuned when 20 workers each took 200 ms per request (20 / 0.2 = 100). Now the database slows to 400 ms, so the same workers finish only 50 req/s. A fixed limit would keep admitting 100. Adaptive concurrency fixes this by limiting requests in flight (concurrency = throughput x latency). It raises the limit slowly while latency is healthy and cuts it sharply, for example by 10% to 50%, when latency rises. Here the in-flight limit stays near 20, so at 400 ms the admitted rate falls to about 50 req/s and the excess is refused.","idea":"Adaptive limits follow real capacity by watching latency, instead of trusting a number someone picked last year.","caption":"The database slows, so the adaptive limit shrinks to match.","focus":["gw","workers","db","users"],"flows":[{"from":"workers","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"400 ms each"},{"from":"db","to":"workers","kind":"response","label":"slow"},{"from":"gw","to":"users","kind":"fail","label":"503 over limit"}],"metrics":{"arrivals":"100","depth":"25","wait":"500","rejected":"50"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"400 ms each","tone":"red"},{"node":"workers","text":"only 50 req/s","tone":"red"},{"node":"gw","text":"limit 100 → 50 /s","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Say no early, say no fast","body":"Overload is a math problem: when arrivals exceed capacity, something must give. An unbounded queue gives up latency, and eventually the whole service. Bounded queues, priority shedding, admission control and adaptive limits give up a little work on purpose so the rest finishes on time. Clients should treat 429 and 503 as a signal to back off (see the timeouts and retries lesson).","idea":"Protect latency by refusing excess work deliberately, early and in priority order.","caption":"Fast, explicit rejection keeps the system healthy.","focus":["users","batch","gw","queue","workers","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"arrivals":"100","depth":"25","wait":"500","rejected":"50"},"chips":[{"node":"queue","text":"wait 500 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"gw","text":"50 req/s shed"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Workers handle 100 requests per second and the queue holds 3,000 items. Roughly how long does a newly accepted request wait?","options":["About 3 seconds","About 30 seconds","About 300 seconds","No wait, queues are processed in parallel"],"answer":1,"why":"Wait is queue length divided by service rate: 3,000 / 100 = 30 seconds, long past most client timeouts."},{"q":"Why is a bounded queue with fast 503 errors often better than a huge queue that accepts everything?","options":["It raises the maximum throughput of the workers","It guarantees no request is ever lost","It removes the need for any timeouts","It caps latency, so accepted requests finish before callers give up and workers avoid stale work"],"answer":3,"why":"A bound does not add capacity. It keeps waits short so the work done is still useful, and it tells callers early that they should back off."},{"q":"During overload you must drop 50 req/s. Which approach best fits load shedding?","options":["Drop a random 50 req/s across all traffic","Drop the oldest requests only","Drop batch and background requests first, keep checkout and other interactive traffic","Restart the workers to clear the load"],"answer":2,"why":"Priority-based shedding spends the unavoidable loss on work that can wait or be retried, protecting the user-facing path."},{"q":"The database gets twice as slow. Why does an adaptive concurrency limit beat a fixed rate limit of 100 req/s?","options":["It lowers the limit when latency rises, matching the reduced real capacity","It always admits more traffic than a fixed limit","It stores requests on disk until the database recovers","It makes the database faster"],"answer":0,"why":"A fixed rate limit encodes the old capacity and keeps admitting 100 req/s into a system that can now finish only 50. An adaptive limit watches latency and shrinks, so the excess is refused instead of piling up."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Backpressure","def":"A signal from a slow consumer to its producers to slow down or stop, such as a full queue, a blocked write or a 429 response."},{"term":"Load shedding","def":"Deliberately dropping some requests under overload so the rest still complete quickly."},{"term":"Queue wait","def":"Roughly queue length divided by service rate. Keep it well under the caller's timeout."},{"term":"Bounded queue","def":"A queue with a fixed maximum size. When full, new work is rejected instead of buffered forever."},{"term":"Admission control","def":"A gate at the entrance that accepts or rejects work based on load signals such as queue depth, in-flight count or oldest item age."},{"term":"Priority shedding","def":"Tagging requests by importance and rejecting low-priority work first, for example batch before interactive."},{"term":"Adaptive concurrency","def":"A limit on in-flight requests that rises slowly while latency is healthy and drops multiplicatively when latency climbs (AIMD style)."},{"term":"429 vs 503","def":"429 means this caller is sending too much; 503 means the service is overloaded or unavailable. A per-class shed is sent as 429 if that class has its own quota, 503 if the whole service is overloaded. Both may include Retry-After."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"rate-limiting","title":"Rate Limiting","subtitle":"Watch a flood of requests hit a limiter, and see how buckets, windows and Redis keep one client from starving everyone else.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How a token bucket allows short bursts but caps the long-run rate","How a leaky bucket smooths traffic, and how it differs from a token bucket","Why fixed windows allow double bursts at the boundary and how sliding windows fix it","How to share counters across many servers with atomic Redis operations","Where to enforce limits and what to do when the limiter itself fails"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Normal client","kind":"client","x":10,"y":24},{"id":"abuser","label":"Noisy client","kind":"external","x":10,"y":78},{"id":"gateway","label":"Gateway + limiter","kind":"gateway","x":36,"y":52},{"id":"redis","label":"Redis counters","kind":"cache","x":62,"y":18},{"id":"service","label":"API service","kind":"service","x":64,"y":62},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":88,"y":62}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"gateway"},{"from":"abuser","to":"gateway"},{"from":"gateway","to":"redis"},{"from":"gateway","to":"service"},{"from":"service","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"tokens","label":"Tokens left","unit":"tokens"},{"id":"allowed","label":"Allowed","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"rejected","label":"Rejected","unit":"req/s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One client, all the capacity","body":"Our API can comfortably handle about 200 requests per second. Then a buggy script in one customer's code starts sending 500 requests per second with its API key (the ID that says who is calling). Nobody is attacking us, yet every other customer now sees timeouts. How do we stop one caller from using more than its fair share?","idea":"Rate limiting protects shared capacity by capping how much any one caller may use.","caption":"The noisy client floods the gateway and the service.","focus":["abuser","gateway","service"],"flows":[{"from":"abuser","to":"gateway","kind":"request","label":"500 req/s"},{"from":"gateway","to":"service","kind":"request","label":"overload"}],"metrics":{"tokens":"10","allowed":"500","rejected":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"abuser","text":"500 req/s","tone":"red"},{"node":"service","text":"safe ~200 req/s"},{"node":"service","text":"others time out","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Token bucket","body":"Give each API key a bucket that holds up to 10 tokens and refills at 2 tokens per second. Every request must take one token. If the bucket is empty, the request is refused. In the animation, a normal client sends one request and spends one token, leaving 9. A quiet client keeps a full bucket, so it can burst 10 requests at once. A steady client is held to 2 per second on average. Two numbers (capacity and refill rate) set burst size and sustained rate.","idea":"A token bucket allows bursts up to its capacity while enforcing a long-run average rate.","caption":"A normal client spends a token per request.","focus":["client","gateway","service"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"gateway","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders"},{"from":"gateway","to":"service","kind":"request","label":"token taken"},{"from":"service","to":"gateway","kind":"response","label":"200"},{"from":"gateway","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"200"}],"metrics":{"tokens":"9","allowed":"2","rejected":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"gateway","text":"cap 10 · +2/s"},{"node":"gateway","text":"9 tokens left","at":"bottom"},{"node":"client","text":"200 OK","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"REJECT","title":"Saying no cheaply","body":"The noisy client empties its 10 tokens in about 20 ms (10 requests at 500 per second) and then gets only the 2 tokens refilled each second. The gateway answers the rest with HTTP 429 Too Many Requests and a Retry-After header (how long to wait). It does this without touching the service or database. Rejecting costs microseconds, while serving costs real work. Good clients read Retry-After and back off.","idea":"Reject early and cheaply with 429 and Retry-After, so excess load never reaches the expensive parts.","caption":"The gateway returns 429 to the noisy client.","focus":["abuser","gateway"],"flows":[{"from":"abuser","to":"gateway","kind":"request","label":"500 req/s"},{"from":"gateway","to":"abuser","kind":"fail","label":"429 Retry-After"}],"metrics":{"tokens":"0","allowed":"2","rejected":"498"},"chips":[{"node":"gateway","text":"0 tokens","tone":"red"},{"node":"abuser","text":"429 Too Many","tone":"red"},{"node":"gateway","text":"2 req/s allowed","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Leaky bucket","body":"A leaky bucket is another method. Picture a queue with a hole: requests enter the bucket and drain out at a fixed rate, say 2 per second. If the bucket is full, new requests overflow and are dropped. The output to the service is perfectly smooth, which helps when the service cannot handle bursts. The price is that bursts are delayed in the queue instead of being served right away.","idea":"A token bucket lets bursts through. A leaky bucket flattens them into a constant flow.","caption":"Requests drain to the service at a steady pace.","focus":["gateway","service"],"flows":[{"from":"gateway","to":"service","kind":"async","label":"steady 2/s"}],"metrics":{"tokens":"0","allowed":"2","rejected":"498"},"chips":[{"node":"gateway","text":"leaks 2/s"},{"node":"service","text":"steady 2/s","tone":"green"},{"node":"gateway","text":"full → drop","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Fixed vs sliding windows","body":"A simpler idea is a fixed window: allow 100 requests per minute and reset a counter at each minute boundary. The flaw: a client can send 100 requests at 12:00:59 and 100 more at 12:01:00, which is 200 in two seconds, double the limit. A sliding window log stores every timestamp, so it is exact but uses more memory. A sliding window counter blends the current and previous window counts, which is cheap and close enough.","idea":"Fixed windows are cheap but allow a 2x burst at the boundary. Sliding windows close the gap at a small cost.","caption":"Two back-to-back windows let a 2x burst through.","focus":["abuser","gateway"],"flows":[{"from":"abuser","to":"gateway","kind":"fail","label":"100 + 100 in 2s"}],"metrics":{"allowed":"100","rejected":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"abuser","text":"100 + 100 in 2 s","tone":"red"},{"node":"gateway","text":"limit 100/min"},{"node":"gateway","text":"2× limit at edge","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"DISTRIBUTED","title":"Shared counters in Redis","body":"Real gateways run as many instances behind a load balancer. If each keeps its own bucket, a limit of 100 per minute across 10 instances quietly becomes up to 1,000. So the buckets live in one shared Redis (a fast in-memory store), keyed by API key. The gateway sends one Lua script (a small program that runs inside Redis) that reads the bucket, refills it from elapsed time and takes a token in one step. Doing a separate read and then a write would let two instances both see one token left and both spend it. In the animation Redis replies 'ok, 7 left' for a regular customer's key.","idea":"Keep counters in one shared store and update them atomically, or the limit multiplies by the number of instances.","caption":"Every gateway instance asks Redis, which decides.","focus":["gateway","redis"],"flows":[{"from":"gateway","to":"redis","kind":"request","label":"EVAL bucket"},{"from":"redis","to":"gateway","kind":"response","label":"ok, 7 left"}],"metrics":{"tokens":"7","allowed":"2","rejected":"498"},"chips":[{"node":"redis","text":"ok, 7 left","tone":"green"},{"node":"gateway","text":"1 Lua script"},{"node":"redis","text":"bucket per key","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Redis goes down","body":"Now the limiter's own dependency fails. If the gateway fails closed (rejects everything), a counter outage becomes a full outage. If it fails open (allows everything), we lose protection exactly when load may be high. A common compromise is a coarse local limit per instance, accepting some inaccuracy, plus a short timeout (a few milliseconds) on the Redis call so the limiter never slows requests. Here this gateway instance falls back to about 20 requests per second for this key (each extra instance adds its own 20, so the real limit gets looser).","idea":"Decide the limiter's failure mode in advance. Usually fail open or use a local limit, never block all traffic.","caption":"Redis is unreachable; the gateway uses a local fallback.","focus":["gateway","redis"],"flows":[{"from":"gateway","to":"redis","kind":"fail","label":"timeout 5 ms"},{"from":"gateway","to":"service","kind":"request","label":"local limit"}],"metrics":{"tokens":"5","allowed":"20","rejected":"480"},"chips":[{"node":"redis","text":"timeout 5 ms","tone":"red"},{"node":"gateway","text":"local cap 20 req/s"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Where to enforce, and on what key","body":"Limits can sit at a CDN or edge, at the API gateway, or inside each service. Edge limits stop floods cheaply. Gateway limits know the customer and plan. Service limits protect one costly endpoint such as search or export. Choose the key carefully: per API key or user is fair, while per IP punishes everyone behind a shared office or mobile address. Different endpoints and plans often get different limits.","idea":"Enforce as early as you know who the caller is, and pick a key that matches who you want to be fair to.","caption":"Limits apply at the gateway, with a stricter one in the service.","focus":["client","gateway","service","db"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"gateway","kind":"request","label":"key: acme"},{"from":"gateway","to":"service","kind":"request","label":"within plan"},{"from":"service","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"costly query"}],"metrics":{"tokens":"9","allowed":"2","rejected":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"gateway","text":"key: acme"},{"node":"gateway","text":"within plan","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"db","text":"costly query"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Fair shares for everyone","body":"A token bucket allows bursts at a set average rate. A leaky bucket smooths output. Fixed windows are simple but burst at the boundary, so prefer sliding windows when accuracy matters. With many servers, keep counters in Redis and update them atomically. Return 429 with Retry-After, decide how the limiter fails, and enforce at the earliest point that knows who the caller is.","idea":"Pick an algorithm for the burst behaviour you want, share state atomically, and plan for limiter failure.","caption":"The limiter shields the service from one noisy caller.","focus":["client","abuser","gateway","redis","service","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"tokens":"9","allowed":"2","rejected":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"gateway","text":"429 + Retry-After"},{"node":"redis","text":"shared counters"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"An API uses a token bucket with capacity 20 and a refill of 5 tokens per second. A client was idle for a minute, then sends 30 requests instantly. Roughly how many succeed?","options":["5","20","30","0"],"answer":1,"why":"The idle client has a full bucket of 20 tokens, so 20 succeed immediately. The other 10 are refused until tokens refill at 5 per second."},{"q":"A limit of 60 requests per minute uses a fixed window. A client sends 60 requests at 10:00:59 and 60 more at 10:01:01. What happens?","options":["All 120 are allowed within 2 seconds","The second batch is rejected","Only 30 of the second batch pass","The first batch is rejected"],"answer":0,"why":"The counter resets at 10:01:00, so both batches fit in separate windows. This boundary burst is the main weakness of fixed windows."},{"q":"Eight gateway instances each hold a local counter for a 100 per minute limit, and requests are spread evenly. What is the effective limit for one customer?","options":["About 100 per minute","About 12 per minute","Up to about 800 per minute","Unlimited"],"answer":2,"why":"Each instance allows 100 on its own, so the combined limit can reach 8 x 100. Shared counters in Redis restore the real limit."},{"q":"Why is the Redis rate-limit check done in a single Lua script rather than a GET followed by a SET?","options":["Lua is faster than any other language","It avoids needing a key expiry","Redis cannot do GET and SET separately","It makes the check and update atomic, so two instances cannot both spend the last token"],"answer":3,"why":"With separate commands, two gateways can both read 1 token left and both allow a request. A script runs without interleaving, closing that race."},{"q":"Your limiter's Redis cluster is down during peak traffic. Which response is usually the best default?","options":["Reject every request until Redis returns","Disable all limits permanently","Fall back to a coarse local limit with a short Redis timeout","Retry Redis in a loop until it answers"],"answer":2,"why":"Failing closed turns a limiter outage into an outage of the whole API, and unlimited access removes protection. A local fallback keeps service up with approximate limits."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Token bucket","def":"Bucket of tokens refilled at a fixed rate; each request spends one. Allows bursts up to capacity, with a capped average rate."},{"term":"Leaky bucket","def":"Queue that drains at a constant rate and drops overflow. Produces smooth output but delays bursts."},{"term":"Fixed window","def":"Counter per time slice (for example per minute). Cheap, but can allow up to 2x the limit across a boundary."},{"term":"Sliding window","def":"Counts requests in the last N seconds. The log version is exact; the counter version weights the previous window and is approximate."},{"term":"Atomic Redis check","def":"Use a Lua script (or INCR with EXPIRE set together) so read, refill and spend happen as one step across all instances."},{"term":"429 + Retry-After","def":"HTTP status and header telling the client it was limited and when to try again. Reject before doing real work."},{"term":"Limiter key","def":"What you count by: API key, user, IP or endpoint. IP is crude because many users can share one address."},{"term":"Fail open / closed","def":"Whether requests pass or are blocked when the limiter cannot decide. Prefer fail open or a local fallback for availability."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"multi-region","title":"Multi-Region & Disaster Recovery","subtitle":"Watch an entire region fail, and see how much data and time each recovery design costs you.","minutes":14,"level":"Advanced","learn":["What RPO and RTO mean and how they drive design and cost","Why replicas are not backups, and what each one protects against","Active-passive versus active-active regions and their trade-offs","How data residency rules limit where data may be copied","Why untested failover plans tend to fail, and how drills fix that"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"Users","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"router","label":"Global traffic router","kind":"dns","x":28,"y":50},{"id":"appa","label":"App, region A","kind":"server","x":50,"y":20},{"id":"dba","label":"DB, region A","kind":"db","x":73,"y":20},{"id":"appb","label":"App, region B","kind":"server","x":50,"y":80},{"id":"dbb","label":"DB, region B","kind":"db","x":73,"y":80},{"id":"vault","label":"Backup vault","kind":"storage","x":91,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"router"},{"from":"router","to":"appa"},{"from":"router","to":"appb"},{"from":"appa","to":"dba"},{"from":"appb","to":"dbb"},{"from":"dba","to":"dbb","label":"replication"},{"from":"dba","to":"vault"},{"from":"vault","to":"dbb"}],"metrics":[{"id":"lag","label":"Replica lag","unit":"s"},{"id":"lost","label":"Data lost","unit":"s"},{"id":"down","label":"Downtime","unit":"min"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"A whole region goes dark","body":"Your shop runs in region A, a cluster of data centers in one metro area. Today a power and network failure takes the whole region offline: every server, including the database. Multi-region design is the plan for that day, by keeping a second copy of the system in another region. Two questions decide the plan. How much recent data can you afford to lose? And how long can you afford to be down?","idea":"Disaster recovery starts with two business questions, not with technology.","caption":"All traffic flows to region A today.","focus":["users","router","appa","dba"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"router","kind":"request","label":"orders"},{"from":"router","to":"appa","kind":"request","label":"all traffic"},{"from":"appa","to":"dba","kind":"request","label":"write"}],"metrics":{"lag":"0","lost":"0","down":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"router","text":"all traffic to A"},{"node":"dba","text":"writes commit"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"RPO and RTO","body":"RPO (recovery point objective) is how much data you can lose, measured as time. An RPO of 5 minutes means the newest 5 minutes of writes may vanish. RTO (recovery time objective) is how long the service may stay down, including the time to notice and decide, not just to repair. The business picks both targets. Tighter targets cost more, so a payments ledger and a recommendations cache should not share the same numbers.","idea":"RPO is data lost, RTO is time lost. Pushing either toward zero gets steeply more expensive.","caption":"Two clocks start ticking when the region fails.","focus":[],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"dba","text":"RPO: data you lose"},{"node":"router","text":"RTO: time you are down"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"A standby copy in region B","body":"Region B keeps a replica (a live copy) of the database. Writes commit in region A, then stream to B asynchronously as the write-ahead log (WAL), the ordered list of changes. We do not wait for B to confirm each write, because that would add roughly 60 to 150 ms to every write. The cost is replication lag, the delay before B catches up. It is typically under a second, but can spike under load. Anything not yet shipped when A dies is lost, so lag is your real RPO.","idea":"Asynchronous replication is fast, but its lag is exactly the data you can lose.","caption":"Writes stream to B about one second behind.","focus":["appa","dba","dbb"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"dba","kind":"request","label":"commit"},{"from":"dba","to":"dbb","kind":"replicate","label":"WAL stream"}],"metrics":{"lag":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"dbb","text":"lag ≈ 1 s"},{"node":"dba","text":"sync = +60–150 ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"A replica is not a backup","body":"A replica copies every change, including your mistakes. If a bad deploy runs DELETE without a WHERE clause, the replica faithfully deletes the same rows a moment later. A backup is a separate point-in-time copy, kept with logs so you can restore to just before the mistake. Replicas guard against hardware loss. Backups guard against human error, corruption and ransomware. Keep at least one copy immutable or in a separate account, and remember a big restore can take hours.","idea":"Replicas give availability. Backups give history. You need both.","caption":"The bad write replicates, but the vault keeps history.","focus":["dba","dbb","vault"],"flows":[{"from":"dba","to":"dbb","kind":"fail","label":"bad DELETE copied"},{"from":"dba","to":"vault","kind":"async","label":"snapshot + logs"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"dbb","text":"bad DELETE copied","tone":"red"},{"node":"vault","text":"snapshot + logs","tone":"green"},{"node":"vault","text":"restore: hours","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Region A fails","body":"Now region A is unreachable. The router's health checks fail and requests to A time out; noticing takes about a minute here. The replica in B holds everything up to about one second ago, so roughly that much data is stranded in A. The danger: if you promote B while A is only cut off, not dead, both sides might accept writes (split-brain). Teams prevent this by fencing the old primary (blocking it from accepting writes), and often by requiring a human to confirm.","idea":"The failure is easy to see. Deciding that it is real is the hard part.","caption":"Region A stops answering; B has a copy.","focus":["router","appa","dba"],"flows":[{"from":"router","to":"appa","kind":"fail","label":"health check"},{"from":"appa","to":"dba","kind":"fail","label":"unreachable"}],"metrics":{"lost":"1","down":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"appa","text":"unreachable","tone":"red"},{"node":"dba","text":"≈ 1 s data stranded","tone":"red"},{"node":"router","text":"health check fails","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Active-passive failover","body":"In active-passive, B is a warm standby: running at reduced size and ready to scale up. An engineer promotes the replica to primary, scales up the app servers and flips the router so users go to B, as the animation shows. With a rehearsed runbook (the written step-by-step recovery plan) this commonly takes 5 to 30 minutes. A cold standby, not running until needed, takes hours. A pilot light keeps only the data tier running. The outcome here: RPO of about 1 second and RTO of about 10 minutes.","idea":"Active-passive is cheaper and simpler, but you pay in minutes of downtime and an idle region.","caption":"Traffic shifts to B after the replica is promoted.","focus":["users","router","appb","dbb"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"router","kind":"request","label":"orders"},{"from":"router","to":"appb","kind":"handshake","label":"route to B"},{"from":"appb","to":"dbb","kind":"request","label":"write (promoted)"}],"metrics":{"lost":"1","down":"10"},"chips":[{"node":"dbb","text":"promoted","tone":"green"},{"node":"appb","text":"warm → scaled up"},{"node":"router","text":"RTO ≈ 10 min"},{"node":"dbb","text":"RPO ≈ 1 s","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Active-active","body":"In active-active, both regions serve users all the time. Losing one just means the router sends everyone to the other. That one must already have spare room: each region is sized to carry the full load alone, so each runs at 50% or less normally. RTO is seconds to about a minute, depending on how fast the router notices and clients stop using cached answers. With asynchronous replication RPO is still about the lag. The price is write conflicts, where two regions change the same record at once, so you need conflict rules or a home region per user.","idea":"Active-active buys near-zero RTO at the cost of conflict handling, double capacity and complexity.","caption":"Both regions serve traffic and sync.","focus":["router","appa","appb","dba","dbb"],"flows":[{"from":"router","to":"appa","kind":"request","label":"50%"},{"from":"router","to":"appb","kind":"request","label":"50%"},{"from":"dbb","to":"dba","kind":"replicate","label":"two-way sync"}],"metrics":{"lag":"1","lost":"1","down":"0.5"},"chips":[{"node":"router","text":"50% / 50%"},{"node":"appa","text":"≤ 50% load"},{"node":"dbb","text":"two-way sync"},{"node":"router","text":"RTO ≈ 30 s","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Data residency","body":"Laws such as the GDPR limit moving personal data outside the European Economic Area unless safeguards apply, and some countries require data to stay in-country. Suppose EU data lives in region A and region B is outside the EEA, so B may not hold a copy. Recovery then uses backups in another region inside the same jurisdiction. Assume logs are archived every 5 minutes and a restore takes about 90 minutes. Both RPO (up to 5 minutes) and RTO (90 minutes) are far worse than with a live replica.","idea":"Where recovery data may live is a legal limit. Check it before choosing the failover region.","caption":"EU data may not be copied to B; recovery uses backups in another EU region.","focus":["router","appa","dba","dbb","vault"],"flows":[{"from":"router","to":"appa","kind":"request","label":"EU users pinned"},{"from":"dba","to":"dbb","kind":"fail","label":"copy not allowed"},{"from":"dba","to":"vault","kind":"async","label":"EU-only backup"}],"metrics":{"lost":"300","down":"90"},"chips":[{"node":"appa","text":"EU users pinned"},{"node":"dbb","text":"copy not allowed","tone":"red"},{"node":"vault","text":"EU-only backup","tone":"green"},{"node":"vault","text":"restore ≈ 90 min","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Failover drills","body":"A plan nobody has run is a guess. Real failures expose stale credentials, too little capacity in region B, DNS caches that ignore your short TTL (how long resolvers may keep an answer), and runbooks that depend on a tool living in the dead region. Teams run drills: first shift a small share of traffic, then a full game day that really fails over, and measure the actual RPO and RTO against the targets. Practise failing back too, and restore from the vault into region B to time that path.","idea":"Only a measured drill tells you your real RTO. Practice turns a guess into a number.","caption":"A drill shifts traffic to B and measures the clock.","focus":["router","appb","dbb","vault"],"flows":[{"from":"router","to":"appb","kind":"handshake","label":"drill: 5% to B"},{"from":"vault","to":"dbb","kind":"response","label":"restore test"}],"metrics":{"lost":"1","down":"10"},"chips":[{"node":"router","text":"5% → B"},{"node":"vault","text":"restore test"},{"node":"appb","text":"measure RPO / RTO"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick the cheapest design that meets the targets","body":"Set RPO and RTO for each system from the cost of downtime and data loss. Use replicas for fast failover and backups for history, because they solve different problems. Active-passive is simpler and slower. Active-active is faster and harder. Check data residency before copying data anywhere, and drill regularly so the numbers on paper are the numbers you really get.","idea":"Replicas for speed, backups for history, drills for truth.","caption":"Targets, copies, and drills together form the plan.","focus":["users","router","appa","dba","appb","dbb","vault"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"lag":"1","lost":"1","down":"10"},"chips":[{"node":"dbb","text":"RPO ≈ 1 s"},{"node":"router","text":"RTO ≈ 10 min"},{"node":"vault","text":"history backups"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A team's database replica lags the primary by about 2 seconds under normal load. The region fails and they promote the replica. Roughly what is their data loss?","options":["None, because the replica has a full copy","About 24 hours, the backup interval","About 2 seconds of recent writes","Only writes from the failed region's cache"],"answer":2,"why":"Asynchronous replicas trail the primary by the lag, so writes in that window never left the failed region. Lag is effectively your RPO."},{"q":"A buggy migration deletes a customer table at 14:02. Why does the cross-region replica not save you?","options":["Replication copies the delete to the replica within moments","Replicas only store indexes, not rows","Replicas are read-only so they cannot be restored","Cross-region replication is too slow to matter"],"answer":0,"why":"Replication is faithful, including mistakes. You need point-in-time backups with logs to restore to 14:01."},{"q":"The business says the service may be down at most 30 seconds in a region loss. Which design fits best?","options":["Nightly backups copied to another region","A cold standby rebuilt from infrastructure code","Active-passive with a manual runbook","Active-active with enough spare capacity in each region"],"answer":3,"why":"Only a design where the other region is already serving traffic reaches RTO in seconds. Manual promotion typically takes minutes or more."},{"q":"You add active-active writes in two regions. What new problem must you solve that active-passive avoided?","options":["Reads can no longer be served locally","Two regions may update the same record concurrently, creating conflicts","DNS can no longer route users","Data can no longer be backed up cross-region"],"answer":1,"why":"With a single writer there is one order of changes. With two writers you need conflict rules, home-region ownership, or a globally consistent store."},{"q":"A runbook has never been executed. What is the most likely outcome in a real region failure?","options":["It works, since the steps are written down","It works, but only if the replica is promoted first","Actual recovery takes longer than the stated RTO because of hidden gaps","It succeeds, since region B has been idle and is therefore up to date"],"answer":2,"why":"Untested plans hide expired credentials, capacity shortfalls, and dependencies on the failed region. Drills reveal them before they cost you."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"RPO","def":"Recovery point objective: the most data, measured in time, you accept losing. Set by lag or backup interval."},{"term":"RTO","def":"Recovery time objective: the longest acceptable outage from failure until service is restored."},{"term":"Replica vs backup","def":"Replicas copy changes live for fast failover, mistakes included. Backups are separate point-in-time history for restoring."},{"term":"Active-passive","def":"One region serves traffic; another stands by. Simpler and cheaper, RTO in minutes to hours."},{"term":"Active-active","def":"Several regions serve traffic at once. RTO in seconds to a minute, but needs spare capacity and conflict handling."},{"term":"Split-brain","def":"Two regions both believe they are primary and accept conflicting writes. Prevent with fencing (blocking the old primary from accepting writes), often combined with quorum or human confirmation."},{"term":"Data residency","def":"Legal rules limiting where data and its copies may be stored. It constrains replica and backup placement."},{"term":"Game day","def":"A planned drill that fails over for real and measures actual RPO and RTO against targets."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"chaos-engineering","title":"Chaos Engineering & SLOs","subtitle":"Define how reliable a checkout must be, measure it, spend the error budget on safe failure experiments, and learn from them.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How an SLI, an SLO and an error budget relate to each other","How to turn a 99.9% target into minutes of allowed downtime","Why teams alert on burn rate instead of raw error counts","How to run a chaos experiment with a hypothesis, a small blast radius and an abort rule","How the error budget decides whether to ship, experiment or freeze"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"Shoppers","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"checkout","label":"Checkout service","kind":"service","x":31,"y":50},{"id":"payments","label":"Payments service","kind":"service","x":57,"y":20},{"id":"inventory","label":"Inventory DB","kind":"db","x":57,"y":84},{"id":"metrics","label":"Metrics store","kind":"storage","x":84,"y":50},{"id":"alerts","label":"On-call alerts","kind":"external","x":84,"y":14},{"id":"chaos","label":"Chaos tool","kind":"worker","x":31,"y":88}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"checkout"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payments"},{"from":"checkout","to":"inventory"},{"from":"checkout","to":"metrics"},{"from":"metrics","to":"alerts"},{"from":"chaos","to":"payments","label":"inject"},{"from":"chaos","to":"metrics","label":"watch"}],"metrics":[{"id":"sli","label":"30-day SLI","unit":"%"},{"id":"budget","label":"Budget left","unit":"min"},{"id":"burn","label":"Burn rate","unit":"x"},{"id":"blast","label":"Users hit","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"How reliable is reliable enough?","body":"Your checkout service will fail sometimes, because every service it depends on fails sometimes. The useful questions are: how often is failure acceptable, how do we know, and how do we find weak spots before customers do? Asking for 100% is not an answer. Each extra nine costs far more than the last, and users cannot tell the difference past a point.","idea":"Reliability is a target you choose and measure, not something you maximise without limit.","caption":"Shoppers depend on checkout, which depends on payments and inventory.","focus":["users","checkout","payments","inventory"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"sli":"100","budget":"43","burn":"0","blast":"0"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"SLI","title":"Measure what users feel","body":"A service level indicator (SLI) is a number that says how well the service is doing, usually good events divided by valid events. For checkout, a good event is an order that returns a non-error response (not a 5xx) in under 800 ms. Valid means requests that should count, not health checks or the user's own bad input. Count it at the edge or load balancer, close to the user, and send the result to the metrics store, as the animation shows. CPU and memory are poor SLIs, because a busy server can still serve users fine.","idea":"A good SLI is the ratio of good to valid requests, measured as close to the user as practical.","caption":"Every request is counted as good or bad and sent to metrics.","focus":["users","checkout","payments","metrics"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"checkout","kind":"request","label":"place order"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payments","kind":"request","label":"charge"},{"from":"payments","to":"checkout","kind":"response","label":"ok"},{"from":"checkout","to":"metrics","kind":"async","label":"good / total"}],"metrics":{"sli":"100"},"chips":[{"node":"checkout","text":"good: <800 ms, no 5xx"},{"node":"metrics","text":"good ÷ valid"},{"node":"metrics","text":"SLI 100%","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"SLO","title":"Set the target and the budget","body":"A service level objective (SLO) is the goal for the SLI over a time window. Say checkout must be good for 99.9% of requests over 30 days. The other 0.1% is the error budget, the amount of badness you may spend. A 30-day window is 43,200 minutes, so 0.1% is about 43 minutes of full outage, or the same amount spread across partial failures. The budget is not a failure. It is room to ship changes and take risks.","idea":"SLO = target for the SLI. Error budget = 100% minus the SLO, as allowed badness.","caption":"Target 99.9% over 30 days leaves about 43 minutes of budget.","focus":["checkout","metrics"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"sli":"100","budget":"43"},"chips":[{"node":"metrics","text":"SLO 99.9% / 30 d"},{"node":"metrics","text":"budget ≈ 43 min","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"checkout","text":"0.1% may fail"}]},{"tag":"BUDGET","title":"An outage spends the budget","body":"Earlier this month a bad deploy made payments fail for 13 minutes. Every checkout in that window was a bad event. With steady traffic, about 13 of the 43 budget minutes are gone and the 30-day SLI is now about 99.97%. About 30 minutes remain. The budget turns a vague 'we had a rough month' into a number that engineers and product owners can both read.","idea":"Every bad minute is a withdrawal. The remaining budget says how much risk you can still afford.","caption":"Payments failed for 13 minutes; budget drops to 30.","focus":["checkout","payments","metrics"],"flows":[{"from":"checkout","to":"payments","kind":"request","label":"charge"},{"from":"payments","to":"checkout","kind":"fail","label":"charge error"},{"from":"checkout","to":"metrics","kind":"async","label":"bad events"}],"metrics":{"sli":"99.97","budget":"30"},"chips":[{"node":"payments","text":"13 min down","tone":"red"},{"node":"checkout","text":"charge error","tone":"red"},{"node":"metrics","text":"SLI ≈ 99.97%"},{"node":"metrics","text":"30 min left","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"BURN RATE","title":"Alert on how fast you burn","body":"Paging on every error is noisy. Paging only when the budget is gone is too late. Burn rate is the actual error rate divided by the allowed error rate. If 1.44% of requests fail against a 0.1% budget, the burn rate is 14.4. At that speed the whole 30-day budget is gone in about 50 hours, and 2% of it burns in one hour. A common pattern is to page on a fast burn (around 14x over an hour) and open a ticket on a slow burn (around 1x over a few days). Checking a long and a short window together lets alerts clear quickly after a fix.","idea":"Burn rate says how long until the budget is gone, so alerts can match urgency.","caption":"A fast burn pages the on-call engineer.","focus":["metrics","alerts"],"flows":[{"from":"metrics","to":"alerts","kind":"async","label":"burn 14.4x"}],"metrics":{"burn":"14.4"},"chips":[{"node":"metrics","text":"1.44% vs 0.1%"},{"node":"metrics","text":"burn 14.4×","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"alerts","text":"page: fast burn"}]},{"tag":"CHAOS","title":"Break it on purpose, with a hypothesis","body":"Chaos engineering means injecting real failure on purpose to test whether the system behaves as you believe. Start with a steady state, the normal you can measure: 'checkout p99 stays under 800 ms and success stays above 99.9%'. Checkout p99 is about 450 ms today. Hypothesis: 'if payments gets 300 ms slower, checkout still meets both', since 450 + 300 = 750 ms. Faults can be delay, errors, killed instances or blocked dependencies. Here the chaos tool adds the delay and watches the same SLI. It is an experiment, not vandalism.","idea":"A chaos experiment tests a written hypothesis against a measurable steady state.","caption":"The chaos tool adds 300 ms of delay to payments.","focus":["chaos","payments","metrics"],"flows":[{"from":"chaos","to":"payments","kind":"fail","label":"+300 ms delay"},{"from":"chaos","to":"metrics","kind":"request","label":"read SLI"},{"from":"metrics","to":"chaos","kind":"response","label":"steady"}],"metrics":{"burn":"0.1","blast":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"chaos","text":"+300 ms delay"},{"node":"metrics","text":"p99 450 ms"},{"node":"metrics","text":"steady state","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"BLAST RADIUS","title":"Keep the blast radius small","body":"Blast radius is how many users and systems an experiment can hurt. Begin with the smallest slice that still teaches you something: one instance, one test account or 1% of traffic, for a few minutes, off peak but with owners at their desks. In the animation only 1% of checkouts take the slowed path. Widen only after each smaller run passes. Staging is good for finding tooling bugs, but it rarely has real traffic, so many lessons only show up in production, once monitoring and an abort switch are in place.","idea":"Start tiny, widen step by step, and never expand while a run is failing.","caption":"Only 1% of checkouts go through the delayed payments path.","focus":["users","checkout","payments"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"checkout","kind":"request","label":"1% sampled"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payments","kind":"request","label":"slow charge"},{"from":"payments","to":"checkout","kind":"response","label":"ok, +300 ms"}],"metrics":{"blast":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"users","text":"1% sampled"},{"node":"payments","text":"+300 ms"}]},{"tag":"FINDING","title":"The experiment finds a weakness","body":"At 1% the hypothesis holds. At 5%, the slow charges hold on to connections and threads longer. The connection pool to payments (a fixed set of reusable connections) fills up, requests queue, and some pass 800 ms and count as bad. The cause: the call has no tight timeout or fallback, so checkout waits instead of failing fast or accepting the order and retrying the charge later. This planned test costs well under a tenth of a minute of budget, which a 2 a.m. incident would not.","idea":"A failed hypothesis is a win: you found the flaw cheaply and on your own schedule.","caption":"At 5% blast radius, slow payments push checkout past 800 ms.","focus":["chaos","checkout","payments","metrics"],"flows":[{"from":"chaos","to":"payments","kind":"fail","label":"+300 ms delay"},{"from":"payments","to":"checkout","kind":"response","label":"slow"},{"from":"checkout","to":"metrics","kind":"async","label":"some bad"}],"metrics":{"sli":"99.97","budget":"30","burn":"5","blast":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"chaos","text":"5% of traffic"},{"node":"checkout","text":"pool saturated","tone":"red"},{"node":"checkout","text":"no tight timeout","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"metrics","text":"burn 5×","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"ABORT","title":"Abort rules and game days","body":"Every experiment needs an automatic stop. For example, abort if burn exceeds 3x over a few minutes, or if the SLI drops below a floor, and the chaos tool removes the fault at once. Guardrails use a short window, unlike hour-long paging windows. Here burn reaches 5x within a couple of minutes at 5%, so the tool halts the run. A game day is a scheduled team exercise: several experiments run together with owners watching and a rollback plan ready, and notes written afterwards.","idea":"Safe chaos has a kill switch tied to the same SLI that defines normal.","caption":"Burn limit crossed, the chaos tool stops injecting.","focus":["chaos","payments","metrics","alerts"],"flows":[{"from":"metrics","to":"chaos","kind":"response","label":"limit hit"},{"from":"chaos","to":"payments","kind":"response","label":"remove fault"},{"from":"metrics","to":"alerts","kind":"async","label":"abort notice"}],"metrics":{"burn":"0.1","blast":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"metrics","text":"abort at 3× burn"},{"node":"metrics","text":"burn 5× · limit hit","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"payments","text":"fault removed","tone":"green"},{"node":"alerts","text":"abort notice"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Measure, spend, test, learn","body":"An SLI measures what users feel, an SLO sets the target, and the error budget is what remains to spend. Burn-rate alerts say how urgent a problem is. Chaos experiments test a hypothesis with a small blast radius and an abort rule, and game days practise this as a team. A simple policy: with plenty of budget, ship and experiment. When it is nearly spent, ship only low-risk changes and pause new experiments (chaos runs spend budget too). At zero, freeze feature launches until the window recovers. The thresholds are a team choice.","idea":"Reliability is managed like money: set a budget, track it, and spend it on learning.","caption":"The full loop from metric to experiment.","focus":[],"flows":[],"metrics":{"sli":"99.97","budget":"30","burn":"0.1","blast":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"metrics","text":"SLI ≈ 99.97%"},{"node":"metrics","text":"30 min left","at":"bottom"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your SLO is 99.9% good requests over 30 days. About how much full-outage time does the error budget allow?","options":["About 4 minutes","About 43 minutes","About 7 hours","About 43 hours"],"answer":1,"why":"30 days is 43,200 minutes. 0.1% of that is about 43 minutes. 99.99% would give about 4 minutes, and 99% about 7 hours."},{"q":"Which is the best SLI for a checkout API?","options":["Average CPU use across servers","Number of servers running","Share of order requests that succeed in under 800 ms","Total requests per day"],"answer":2,"why":"A good SLI is a ratio of good to valid events measured from the user's side. CPU, server count and raw volume do not say whether users were served well."},{"q":"An alert shows a burn rate of 14.4 against a 30-day SLO. What does that mean?","options":["The budget will last about 50 hours if this continues","14.4% of requests are failing","The budget lasts 14.4 days","The SLO is 14.4 nines"],"answer":0,"why":"Burn rate is the actual error rate divided by the allowed error rate. At 14.4x, 30 days divided by 14.4 is about 2 days, so roughly 50 hours."},{"q":"You want to test how checkout copes with slow payments. Which plan is safest and most useful?","options":["Slow payments for all users at peak time, to see real impact","Run it only in staging and assume production behaves the same","Slow 1% of traffic briefly, auto-stop if the SLI breaches a floor","Kill payments entirely and watch what the on-call does"],"answer":2,"why":"State a hypothesis, start with a small blast radius, watch the steady-state SLI, and stop automatically on breach. Staging alone misses real traffic, and full outages at peak are reckless."},{"q":"Mid-month the error budget is almost used up. What does a sensible error-budget policy do?","options":["Raise the SLO so the numbers look better","Pause risky launches and chaos runs, and prioritise reliability work","Ignore it until the window resets","Add alerts on every single error"],"answer":1,"why":"The budget's job is to steer trade-offs. When it is nearly spent, the team slows risk-taking and fixes reliability until the window recovers."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"SLI","def":"Service level indicator: a measured ratio of good events to valid events, such as fast successful checkouts over all checkouts."},{"term":"SLO","def":"Service level objective: the target for an SLI over a window, for example 99.9% over 30 days."},{"term":"SLA","def":"Service level agreement: a contract with consequences, often refunds, if a promised level is missed. Usually looser than the internal SLO."},{"term":"Error budget","def":"100% minus the SLO. At 99.9% over 30 days it is about 43 minutes of full outage, available to spend on risk. A policy ties it to action: ship and experiment while plenty is left, slow down when nearly spent, freeze launches at zero."},{"term":"Burn rate","def":"Actual error rate divided by allowed error rate. 1x spends the budget exactly over the window; 14.4x empties it in about 50 hours."},{"term":"Steady state","def":"The measurable normal behaviour, such as the SLI staying above target, that a chaos experiment tries to keep intact."},{"term":"Blast radius","def":"How many users or systems an experiment can affect. Start small, such as 1% of traffic, and widen only after passing."},{"term":"Game day","def":"A planned team exercise that runs failure experiments with owners watching, an abort plan and a written follow-up."}],"isNew":true}]},{"id":"architecture","title":"Architecture","lessons":[{"slug":"microservices","title":"Monolith vs Microservices","subtitle":"Watch one checkout move from a single codebase to separate services, and see what you gain and what the network costs.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["What a monolith and a modular monolith are, and why the second is often the better start","How to draw service boundaries around business capabilities, not technical layers","Why each service must own its data, and what you give up (joins, easy transactions)","The costs of distribution: latency, partial failure, availability that multiplies, harder debugging","When splitting is worth it and when it is premature"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Client","kind":"client","x":8,"y":50,"url":"https://shop.example"},{"id":"gw","label":"API gateway","kind":"gateway","x":27,"y":50},{"id":"mono","label":"Monolith app","kind":"server","x":50,"y":12},{"id":"shared","label":"Shared database","kind":"db","x":85,"y":12},{"id":"orders","label":"Orders service","kind":"service","x":50,"y":38},{"id":"ordersdb","label":"Orders DB","kind":"db","x":85,"y":38},{"id":"bus","label":"Event queue","kind":"queue","x":68,"y":25},{"id":"pay","label":"Payments service","kind":"service","x":50,"y":64},{"id":"paydb","label":"Payments DB","kind":"db","x":85,"y":64}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"gw"},{"from":"gw","to":"mono"},{"from":"mono","to":"shared"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders"},{"from":"orders","to":"ordersdb"},{"from":"orders","to":"pay"},{"from":"pay","to":"paydb"},{"from":"orders","to":"bus"},{"from":"bus","to":"pay"},{"from":"orders","to":"paydb"}],"metrics":[{"id":"hops","label":"Internal hops","unit":"hops"},{"id":"latency","label":"Checkout","unit":"ms"},{"id":"avail","label":"Availability","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One app does everything","body":"Picture a small online shop built as one program, called a monolith. Checkout, orders, payments and stock all live inside it and share one database. When one part needs another, it just calls a function, which is fast and simple. The animation shows one checkout request saved in a single transaction, a group of database changes that all succeed or all fail together. Now the team grows from 5 to 50 people. Should we break this program up, and what would it cost?","idea":"A monolith is one program deployed as a unit: its internal calls are cheap and one transaction covers everything.","caption":"The whole checkout runs inside one program and one database.","focus":["client","gw","mono","shared"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"POST /checkout"},{"from":"gw","to":"mono","kind":"request","label":"POST /checkout"},{"from":"mono","to":"shared","kind":"request","label":"1 transaction"},{"from":"shared","to":"mono","kind":"response","label":"committed"}],"metrics":{"hops":"1","latency":"30","avail":"99.8"},"chips":[{"node":"mono","text":"1 deployable"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Modular monolith first","body":"Before splitting anything over the network, split the code. A modular monolith is still one program, but it is divided into modules such as orders and payments. Each module offers a small public interface and keeps its own tables, and other modules must not read those tables directly. The animation shows both modules saving data in one transaction. Calls stay in one process, so there is no network cost, but it still deploys and scales as one unit. Enforce the rules with build checks, or the boundaries slowly fade.","idea":"Clean module boundaries inside one program give much of the design benefit of microservices without the network cost.","caption":"One program, one database, but each module uses only its own tables.","focus":["mono","shared"],"flows":[{"from":"mono","to":"shared","kind":"request","label":"orders tables"},{"from":"mono","to":"shared","kind":"request","label":"payments tables"},{"from":"shared","to":"mono","kind":"response","label":"rows"}],"metrics":{"hops":"1","latency":"30","avail":"99.8"},"chips":[{"node":"mono","text":"in-process calls","tone":"green"},{"node":"shared","text":"1 transaction"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Draw boundaries by capability","body":"When one team or module really needs to ship on its own, pull it out as a separate service. Cut along business capabilities such as orders, payments or catalog, not technical layers like 'all the controllers'. A good boundary changes for one reason and belongs to one team. The animation sends checkout through the API gateway (the single front door that routes each request to the right service) to a new Orders service, which saves order 4821 in its own database. There is still one internal hop, so checkout stays at about 30 ms.","idea":"A good service matches one business capability and one team, so most changes touch only one service.","caption":"The gateway now sends checkout to the Orders service, which saves order 4821.","focus":["client","gw","orders","ordersdb","pay"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"POST /checkout"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"POST /orders"},{"from":"orders","to":"ordersdb","kind":"request","label":"insert order"},{"from":"ordersdb","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"order 4821"}],"metrics":{"hops":"1","latency":"30","avail":"99.8"},"chips":[{"node":"gw","text":"single front door"},{"node":"ordersdb","text":"order 4821"},{"node":"client","text":"still 30 ms"}]},{"tag":"DATA","title":"Each service owns its data","body":"Each service owns its data. Payments has its own database, and only the Payments service may read or write it. Everyone else must ask through its API. In the animation, Orders asks Payments to charge order 4821 and Payments saves it in its own database, but Orders reading that database directly is blocked. This lets Payments change its tables freely. The price: you can no longer join orders to payments in one SQL query or wrap both writes in one transaction. Sharing a database would quietly rebuild the monolith. Checkout now spans three boxes, so it gets slower and less available (next step).","idea":"Owning its data is what makes a service independent; a shared database ties services back together.","caption":"Orders must ask Payments through its API; reading Payments' database directly is blocked.","focus":["orders","pay","paydb"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"pay","kind":"request","label":"charge(order 4821)"},{"from":"pay","to":"paydb","kind":"request","label":"insert payment"},{"from":"paydb","to":"pay","kind":"response","label":"ok"},{"from":"orders","to":"paydb","kind":"fail","label":"direct read: no"}],"metrics":{"hops":"2","latency":"36","avail":"99.7"},"chips":[{"node":"paydb","text":"private to Payments","tone":"green"},{"node":"orders","text":"no join, no txn","tone":"red"},{"node":"pay","text":"avail 99.7%","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"COST","title":"A function call became a network call","body":"A function call inside one program takes well under a microsecond. A network round trip in one data center takes about 0.5 to 2 ms, plus serializing (turning data into bytes), TLS encryption and routing. Checkout gains one hop, and Payments writes to its own database, so it grows from about 30 to 36 ms here. Availability falls too. Say each box, database included, is up 99.9% of the time. Gateway plus one app gives about 99.8% (17.5 hours down a year). Gateway, Orders and Payments give 0.999 x 0.999 x 0.999, about 99.7% (26 hours), if failures are independent. Pass a trace ID along every call, since one checkout now logs in three places.","idea":"Every network hop adds delay and a new way to fail, and the availability of chained services multiplies.","caption":"Checkout now crosses three boxes, so it is slower and less available.","focus":["gw","orders","pay"],"flows":[{"from":"gw","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"about 1 ms"},{"from":"orders","to":"pay","kind":"request","label":"1 ms + work"},{"from":"pay","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"paid"},{"from":"orders","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"201 Created"}],"metrics":{"hops":"2","latency":"36","avail":"99.7"},"chips":[{"node":"gw","text":"30 → 36 ms"},{"node":"orders","text":"≈ 1 ms per hop","at":"bottom"},{"node":"pay","text":"0.999³ ≈ 99.7%"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"No cross-service transaction","body":"In the monolith, the order and the payment were saved in one transaction. Now they sit in two databases, so one transaction cannot cover both. One fix: Orders saves the order, publishes an OrderPlaced event to a queue (a waiting line for messages) and replies 202 Accepted at once. Payments picks the event up later and charges. Checkout answers faster (about 32 ms) and does not fail just because Payments is down, but the queue is now a dependency, and the data is only eventually consistent: for a while the order exists but is unpaid. The Outbox and Idempotency lessons cover keeping save and publish in step, and charging once.","idea":"Splitting data trades one all-or-nothing transaction for eventual consistency, which you must design around.","caption":"Orders queues OrderPlaced and answers; Payments handles it a moment later.","focus":["gw","orders","bus","pay","paydb"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"bus","kind":"async","label":"OrderPlaced"},{"from":"orders","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"202 Accepted"},{"from":"bus","to":"pay","kind":"async","label":"OrderPlaced"},{"from":"pay","to":"paydb","kind":"request","label":"insert payment"}],"metrics":{"hops":"2","latency":"32","avail":"99.7"},"chips":[{"node":"bus","text":"OrderPlaced"},{"node":"gw","text":"202 Accepted","tone":"green"},{"node":"pay","text":"paid later"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Payments gets slow","body":"Now suppose Payments gets slow, taking 3 seconds per call because its card provider is struggling, and Orders has set no timeout (a limit on how long to wait). Orders keeps waiting. Each waiting request holds a thread, a worker that handles one job at a time, so Orders soon runs out of free threads and stops serving anyone. One slow service has dragged down its caller: a cascading failure. The defences are short timeouts, limited retries and circuit breakers (which pause calls to a failing service). The Timeouts and Retries lesson covers them.","idea":"In a distributed system a slow dependency can be as dangerous as a dead one, because callers pile up waiting.","caption":"Orders is stuck waiting on a slow Payments and runs out of threads.","focus":["orders","pay"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"pay","kind":"request","label":"charge(...)"},{"from":"pay","to":"orders","kind":"fail","label":"slow reply (3 s)"}],"metrics":{"hops":"2","latency":"3036","avail":"99.7"},"chips":[{"node":"pay","text":"3 s per call","tone":"red"},{"node":"orders","text":"no timeout","tone":"red"},{"node":"orders","text":"no free threads","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"When splitting pays off","body":"Microservices give you independent deploys, independent scaling and team autonomy. These matter when many teams keep colliding in one codebase, or when one part has very different load from the rest. The cost is harder debugging across services, versioned APIs, many build pipelines and on-call duty for more moving parts. As a rough rule of thumb, one team or a handful of engineers is usually faster with a well-structured modular monolith. The highlighted boxes show the two shapes of the same checkout.","idea":"Split because of a specific pain, such as team contention or scaling, not because microservices sound modern.","caption":"Same checkout, two shapes: one program, or separate services.","focus":["mono","orders","pay"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"hops":"2","latency":"36","avail":"99.7"},"chips":[{"node":"mono","text":"1 team → monolith","tone":"green"},{"node":"orders","text":"own deploys"},{"node":"pay","text":"own scaling"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Monolith, modular, or micro","body":"A monolith is simple and fast inside. A modular monolith keeps that speed while enforcing clean boundaries. Microservices cut along business capabilities and give each service its own data, which allows independent deploys and scaling. The price is network delay, partial failures, lower combined availability, no easy cross-service transaction and harder debugging. So start modular, and split only on purpose.","idea":"Boundaries first, network second: make the modules clean before you make them remote.","caption":"Orders and Payments run separately, each with its own database.","focus":["client","gw","orders","ordersdb","pay","paydb","bus"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"hops":"2","latency":"36","avail":"99.7"},"chips":[{"node":"gw","text":"36 ms"},{"node":"pay","text":"avail 99.7%"},{"node":"paydb","text":"own data","tone":"green"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A team wants cleaner code boundaries but has 8 engineers and one product. What is usually the best first move?","options":["Split into ten microservices immediately","Build a modular monolith with enforced module interfaces","Give every module direct access to every table","Move all data into one shared service database"],"answer":1,"why":"A modular monolith gives clear boundaries and ownership without network calls or distributed transactions, which a small team does not need yet."},{"q":"Orders and Payments are separate services, but both read and write the same database tables. What is the main problem?","options":["Queries become slower than before","They cannot be deployed at all","The database will refuse more than one client","A schema change in one can break the other, so they are not truly independent"],"answer":3,"why":"Shared tables couple the services at the data level. Independent deploys and schema changes need each service to own its data and expose it only through an API."},{"q":"A request passes through a gateway and two services in a chain, each 99.9% available with its database counted inside. Roughly what is the availability of the whole chain?","options":["99.9%","99.0%","99.7%","97.0%"],"answer":2,"why":"Availabilities in series multiply: 0.999 x 0.999 x 0.999 is about 0.997, so every extra hard dependency lowers the total."},{"q":"Which split follows a good service boundary?","options":["A Payments service owned by one team that changes for payment reasons","A controllers service, a models service, and a views service","A Catalog service per database table that every team shares","One service per developer, each owning a slice of every feature"],"answer":0,"why":"Good boundaries follow business capabilities with a single owning team. Technical layers force almost every feature to change all services at once."},{"q":"After extracting Payments, Orders calls it synchronously and Payments becomes very slow. What can happen if Orders has no timeout?","options":["Orders automatically fails fast because the platform adds timeouts","Orders threads pile up waiting, and Orders starts failing too","The queue automatically retries everything","Nothing, because slow is not a failure"],"answer":1,"why":"Waiting callers hold threads and connections. Without timeouts the slowness cascades upstream and takes the caller down as well."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Monolith","def":"One deployable unit containing all features, usually with a single database. Simple to build, test, and run; scales and deploys as a whole."},{"term":"Modular monolith","def":"A monolith divided into modules with explicit interfaces and private data. Gives clear boundaries without network calls."},{"term":"Service boundary","def":"The line around a business capability owned by one team. Good boundaries change for one reason and need few cross-service calls."},{"term":"Database per service","def":"Each service alone reads and writes its data; others use its API. Enables independent change but removes joins and shared transactions."},{"term":"Network call cost","def":"Roughly 0.5 to 2 ms inside a data center, versus nanoseconds in-process, plus serialization and new failure modes."},{"term":"Availability in series","def":"Multiply the availabilities of hard dependencies, assuming independent failures: gateway, Orders and Payments at 99.9% each give about 99.7%."},{"term":"Cascading failure","def":"A slow or failing service exhausts its callers' threads, spreading the outage upstream. Fight it with timeouts and circuit breakers."},{"term":"Eventual consistency","def":"Data in separate stores agrees after a short delay rather than instantly. The usual price of splitting data across services."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"sagas","title":"Sagas & Distributed Transactions","subtitle":"Watch one order touch three services, see why a global lock breaks, and see how a saga undoes work instead.","minutes":12,"level":"Advanced","learn":["Why two-phase commit (2PC) blocks and why most microservice teams avoid it","How a saga replaces one big transaction with a chain of small local ones","The difference between orchestration and choreography, and when each fits","How compensating actions undo earlier steps, and why they must be idempotent and retried","What a saga cannot give you: isolation between concurrent sagas"],"nodes":[{"id":"user","label":"Customer","kind":"user","x":8,"y":62},{"id":"orch","label":"Order service","kind":"service","x":28,"y":28},{"id":"payment","label":"Payment service","kind":"service","x":58,"y":16},{"id":"inventory","label":"Inventory service","kind":"service","x":58,"y":50},{"id":"shipping","label":"Shipping service","kind":"service","x":58,"y":84},{"id":"broker","label":"Event bus","kind":"queue","x":88,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"user","to":"orch"},{"from":"orch","to":"payment"},{"from":"orch","to":"inventory"},{"from":"orch","to":"shipping"},{"from":"payment","to":"broker"},{"from":"inventory","to":"broker"},{"from":"shipping","to":"broker"},{"from":"orch","to":"broker"}],"metrics":[{"id":"lock","label":"Locks held","unit":"ms"},{"id":"commits","label":"Local commits","unit":"of 3"},{"id":"undone","label":"Undone","unit":"steps"},{"id":"elapsed","label":"Elapsed","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One order, three databases","body":"A customer places one order. Behind the scenes that means three separate jobs: charge the card (Payment database), reserve the item (Inventory database) and book a courier (Shipping database). Each service owns its own database, so no single BEGIN ... COMMIT (one transaction where all changes succeed or none do) can cover all three. The animation shows the order arriving at the Order service. The worry: what if the card is charged but the courier booking fails? This lesson shows how to keep the three writes consistent without one shared transaction.","idea":"Once data is split across services, one ACID transaction (all-or-nothing, consistent, isolated, durable) can no longer cover the whole business action.","caption":"One order must change data in three services, each with its own database.","focus":["user","orch","payment","inventory","shipping"],"flows":[{"from":"user","to":"orch","kind":"request","label":"place order"}],"metrics":{"lock":"0","commits":"0","undone":"0","elapsed":"0"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"2PC","title":"The classic answer: two-phase commit","body":"Two-phase commit (2PC) is the classic fix. A coordinator, here the Order service, runs two rounds. Round one: it asks every participant to PREPARE, meaning do the work, lock the rows and promise it can commit. Round two: if all answer YES it sends COMMIT, otherwise ABORT. The animation shows three PREPAREs, three YES votes, then three COMMITs. The result is all-or-nothing, but the rows stay locked the whole time. With 5 ms round trips it takes roughly 10 to 20 ms; we use 15 ms.","idea":"2PC gives all-or-nothing results, but every participant must hold its locks until the coordinator decides.","caption":"Votes are collected, then COMMIT is sent, and locks are held the whole time.","focus":["orch","payment","inventory","shipping"],"flows":[{"from":"orch","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"PREPARE"},{"from":"orch","to":"inventory","kind":"request","label":"PREPARE"},{"from":"orch","to":"shipping","kind":"request","label":"PREPARE"},{"from":"payment","to":"orch","kind":"response","label":"YES"},{"from":"inventory","to":"orch","kind":"response","label":"YES"},{"from":"shipping","to":"orch","kind":"response","label":"YES"},{"from":"orch","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"COMMIT"},{"from":"orch","to":"inventory","kind":"request","label":"COMMIT"},{"from":"orch","to":"shipping","kind":"request","label":"COMMIT"}],"metrics":{"lock":"15","elapsed":"15"},"chips":[{"node":"orch","text":"coordinator"},{"node":"orch","text":"~15 ms total","at":"bottom"},{"node":"payment","text":"locked 15 ms","tone":"red"},{"node":"shipping","text":"locked 15 ms","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"WHY NOT","title":"Why 2PC hurts at scale","body":"What if the coordinator crashes after the YES votes arrive but before it sends its decision? Every participant is stuck. It promised it could commit, so it cannot abort alone, and it keeps its locks. An operator can force a guess, but that risks inconsistent data. Say the restart takes 30 seconds: for that long, other orders touching those rows wait in line. Even without a crash, one slow participant stalls everyone. And a card processor does not speak XA, the standard 2PC protocol for databases, so across independent services and third-party APIs 2PC is rarely an option.","idea":"2PC blocks on coordinator failure, and it is a poor fit across independently owned services and third-party APIs.","caption":"The coordinator is down and three services are stuck holding locks.","focus":["orch","payment","inventory","shipping"],"flows":[{"from":"payment","to":"orch","kind":"response","label":"YES"},{"from":"inventory","to":"orch","kind":"response","label":"YES"},{"from":"shipping","to":"orch","kind":"response","label":"YES"},{"from":"orch","to":"payment","kind":"fail","label":"coordinator down"},{"from":"orch","to":"inventory","kind":"fail","label":"coordinator down"},{"from":"orch","to":"shipping","kind":"fail","label":"coordinator down"}],"metrics":{"lock":"30000","elapsed":"30000"},"chips":[{"node":"orch","text":"down 30 s","tone":"red"},{"node":"payment","text":"locks held 30 s","tone":"red"},{"node":"inventory","text":"voted YES, stuck","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"The saga idea","body":"A saga drops the global lock. It splits the job into a chain of small local transactions, one per service. Each commits right away and releases its locks. If a later step fails, the saga runs a compensating action (a new action that reverses the business effect, like a refund) for every step already done. While it runs, the data is briefly inconsistent; it becomes consistent once the saga finishes (eventual consistency). You give up atomicity and isolation to stay available. Assume 10 ms per step and a 3 ms lock. The animation shows step one: Payment charges $40 and commits.","idea":"A saga is a series of local commits, each with a planned undo, giving eventual consistency instead of atomicity.","caption":"Payment commits on its own and frees its lock after about 3 ms.","focus":["orch","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"orch","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"charge $40"},{"from":"payment","to":"orch","kind":"response","label":"charged"}],"metrics":{"lock":"3","commits":"1","elapsed":"10"},"chips":[{"node":"payment","text":"1 of 3 committed","tone":"green"},{"node":"payment","text":"lock 3 ms","at":"bottom"},{"node":"orch","text":"10 ms / step"}]},{"tag":"ORCHESTRATION","title":"Orchestration: a conductor drives it","body":"In an orchestrated saga, one component holds the workflow. Here it is the Order service: it tells each service what to do and waits for the answer. Payment is already charged. The animation shows the orchestrator telling Inventory to reserve the item (done), then telling Shipping to book a courier, which is still in progress. The order of steps and the failure rules live in one place, often a saved state machine, so the saga can resume after a crash. Each service just does its local job and replies.","idea":"Orchestration keeps the saga's flow in one place that tells each service what to do next.","caption":"The orchestrator commands each step in turn and waits for the reply.","focus":["orch","inventory","shipping"],"flows":[{"from":"orch","to":"inventory","kind":"request","label":"reserve item"},{"from":"inventory","to":"orch","kind":"response","label":"reserved"},{"from":"orch","to":"shipping","kind":"request","label":"book courier"}],"metrics":{"commits":"2","elapsed":"20"},"chips":[{"node":"inventory","text":"reserved","tone":"green"},{"node":"shipping","text":"in progress"},{"node":"orch","text":"20 ms elapsed"}]},{"tag":"COMPENSATE","title":"A step fails: run the undo steps","body":"Shipping finds no courier and reports failure. Its own transaction rolled back, so there is nothing to undo there. The orchestrator walks backwards: it releases the reserved stock, then refunds the $40. These are new forward actions, not database rollbacks, because those steps already committed. Tip: put steps likely to fail early and hard-to-undo steps late, after the pivot (the go/no-go step). Here Shipping fails last, so a real design might book the courier first, or only authorize the card and capture it at the pivot, so an early failure is a quiet void, not a visible refund. If a reply times out, treat the result as unknown and compensate anyway.","idea":"Compensating actions are new transactions that reverse committed steps, run in reverse order.","caption":"No courier: the saga releases the stock, then refunds the payment.","focus":["orch","payment","inventory","shipping"],"flows":[{"from":"shipping","to":"orch","kind":"fail","label":"no courier"},{"from":"orch","to":"inventory","kind":"request","label":"release item"},{"from":"orch","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"refund $40"}],"metrics":{"commits":"2","undone":"2","elapsed":"50"},"chips":[{"node":"shipping","text":"no courier","tone":"red"},{"node":"inventory","text":"stock released"},{"node":"payment","text":"refund $40"},{"node":"orch","text":"2 undone · 50 ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CHOREOGRAPHY","title":"Choreography: services react to events","body":"The alternative has no conductor. The Order service publishes an OrderPlaced event on the event bus (a shared message channel). Payment hears it, charges, and publishes PaymentTaken. Inventory hears that, reserves stock and publishes StockReserved, and Shipping reacts to that. Each service only knows which events it listens to and which it emits. There is no central coordinator to fail, though the bus itself must stay highly available. Adding a listener changes nothing else, but nobody holds the whole flow. Each bus hop adds about 5 ms, so the six hops shown take about 30 ms.","idea":"Choreography encodes the saga as a chain of events, with no central coordinator and no single place that shows the whole flow.","caption":"Each event passes the baton to the next service through the bus.","focus":["orch","payment","inventory","shipping","broker"],"flows":[{"from":"orch","to":"broker","kind":"async","label":"OrderPlaced"},{"from":"broker","to":"payment","kind":"async","label":"OrderPlaced"},{"from":"payment","to":"broker","kind":"async","label":"PaymentTaken"},{"from":"broker","to":"inventory","kind":"async","label":"PaymentTaken"},{"from":"inventory","to":"broker","kind":"async","label":"StockReserved"},{"from":"broker","to":"shipping","kind":"async","label":"StockReserved"}],"metrics":{"lock":"3","commits":"2","undone":"0","elapsed":"30"},"chips":[{"node":"broker","text":"5 ms / hop"},{"node":"broker","text":"no coordinator","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"orch","text":"~30 ms total"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Choosing between the two","body":"Choreography handles failure too. Shipping emits ShipFailed, Inventory hears it and releases stock, and Payment reacts to that by refunding. (Payment could also listen to ShipFailed directly.) As a rule of thumb, choreography suits short sagas of two to four steps with few owners, because it is simple and loosely coupled. Beyond that, the event chain is hard to follow and circular dependencies creep in, so orchestration pays off: one place to read, test, time out and monitor. Its cost is one more component to run, and it can attract business logic, so keep it to sequencing.","idea":"Choreography wins on coupling for small flows; orchestration wins on visibility once flows grow or branch.","caption":"Choreographed failure: ShipFailed, then stock released, then refund.","focus":["shipping","broker","inventory","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"shipping","to":"broker","kind":"fail","label":"ShipFailed"},{"from":"broker","to":"inventory","kind":"async","label":"ShipFailed"},{"from":"inventory","to":"broker","kind":"async","label":"StockReleased"},{"from":"broker","to":"payment","kind":"async","label":"StockReleased"},{"from":"payment","to":"broker","kind":"async","label":"PaymentRefunded"}],"metrics":{"commits":"2","undone":"2","elapsed":"55"},"chips":[{"node":"shipping","text":"ShipFailed","tone":"red"},{"node":"inventory","text":"StockReleased"},{"node":"payment","text":"PaymentRefunded"}]},{"tag":"PITFALLS","title":"Retries, duplicates, and dirty reads","body":"Messages are usually delivered at least once, so duplicates happen. Every step and compensation must be idempotent (safe to run twice, with the same result). The animation sends refund #881 twice, and Payment answers 'already done' the second time because each command carries a saga and step ID. Retry compensations with backoff, and alert a human if one cannot succeed. Save the local change and the outgoing message in one local transaction (a transactional outbox) so a crash cannot lose the next step. Because each step is visible once committed, mark in-progress data with a PENDING flag (a semantic lock).","idea":"Sagas need idempotent steps and explicit handling of half-finished state, because they give no isolation.","caption":"The duplicate refund command is ignored.","focus":["orch","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"orch","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"refund #881"},{"from":"orch","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"refund #881"},{"from":"payment","to":"orch","kind":"response","label":"already done"}],"metrics":{"undone":"2","elapsed":"60"},"chips":[{"node":"orch","text":"idempotency key"},{"node":"payment","text":"already done","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Atomic, or available","body":"2PC gives atomic commits across services, but it blocks if the coordinator fails and holds locks, so it is rare between microservices. A saga gives up atomicity and isolation: each local transaction commits, and a failure triggers compensations in reverse order. Use orchestration for longer or branching flows and choreography for short ones. Make every step idempotent, and plan for the in-between states.","idea":"Replace one big lock with small commits and planned undo steps, and design for the in-between.","caption":"Local commits plus compensations when something fails.","focus":["user","orch","payment","inventory","shipping","broker"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"lock":"3","commits":"2","undone":"2","elapsed":"50"},"chips":[{"node":"orch","text":"orchestrate: long"},{"node":"broker","text":"choreograph: short"},{"node":"payment","text":"idempotent"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A 2PC coordinator crashes after all participants voted YES but before it sent COMMIT. What is the main problem?","options":["Participants abort immediately and the order is lost","Participants keep their locks and wait for a decision","Participants commit by themselves after a short timeout","The vote results are replayed from the event bus"],"answer":1,"why":"Having voted YES, a participant promised it can commit and cannot safely abort or commit without the decision, so it blocks while holding locks."},{"q":"In a saga, the payment step has committed and shipping then reports that no courier is available. What is the correct recovery?","options":["Roll back the payment database transaction","Retry shipping forever and leave payment as is","Run a compensating action such as a refund","Delete the payment row directly from the Payment DB"],"answer":2,"why":"The payment transaction already committed and released its locks, so it cannot be rolled back. A new forward action, the refund, reverses its business effect."},{"q":"Your saga has seven steps with conditional branches and the team keeps losing track of where an order is stuck. Which change helps most?","options":["Switch to choreography so each service owns less","Use 2PC for the last three steps of the flow","Remove every compensation to simplify the flow","Orchestrate it with a stored workflow state"],"answer":3,"why":"Orchestration puts the flow and its current state in one place, which makes long or branching sagas readable, testable, and monitorable."},{"q":"A refund command is delivered twice because of a retry. Why is that safe in a well-built saga?","options":["The event bus guarantees exactly-once delivery everywhere","The refund is idempotent, keyed by saga and step ID","Sagas hold a global lock during compensation","Retries are disabled during compensation"],"answer":1,"why":"At-least-once delivery means duplicates happen, so each step and compensation must detect a repeat via an idempotency key and do nothing the second time."},{"q":"While one order's saga is mid-flight, a second request reads the last item as reserved, but that saga may still fail and release it. Which tool reduces this problem?","options":["Hold row locks in all three databases until the saga ends","Retry compensations faster","A bigger event bus","Mark the item PENDING (a semantic lock) so others know it is mid-saga"],"answer":3,"why":"Sagas commit each step early and give no isolation, so other requests can see half-finished state. A semantic lock such as a PENDING flag tells them the reservation may still be undone, so they can wait, warn, or treat it as uncertain. Holding cross-database locks for the whole saga is exactly the blocking that sagas avoid."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Two-phase commit (2PC)","def":"Coordinator asks all participants to PREPARE, then COMMIT or ABORT. Atomic, but blocking and lock-heavy."},{"term":"Saga","def":"A sequence of local transactions, each with a compensating action, giving eventual consistency across services."},{"term":"Orchestration","def":"A central orchestrator sends commands to each step and owns the flow and failure rules. Better for long flows."},{"term":"Choreography","def":"Services react to each other's events with no central coordinator. Loosely coupled, but the flow is implicit."},{"term":"Compensating action","def":"A new transaction that semantically undoes a committed step, such as a refund. Run in reverse order and retried until it succeeds."},{"term":"Idempotency key","def":"An ID sent with each command so a duplicate delivery is recognised and ignored."},{"term":"Semantic lock","def":"A status flag like PENDING that marks data as mid-saga so other requests treat it carefully."},{"term":"Pivot step","def":"The go/no-go step of a saga. Steps before it are compensatable; once it commits, the saga is committed to finishing, and later steps are retried until they succeed."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"service-mesh","title":"Sidecars & Service Mesh","subtitle":"Watch a proxy next to every service add encryption, timeouts and retries to a call, and see what that costs.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["What a sidecar proxy is and how it intercepts traffic without code changes","How mTLS gives each service a verified identity and encrypts every hop","How a mesh applies per-try timeouts and budgeted retries to a flaky dependency","The split between the control plane (config) and the data plane (traffic)","What a mesh costs in latency, memory and operational complexity"],"nodes":[{"id":"orders","label":"Orders service","kind":"service","x":8,"y":42},{"id":"scorders","label":"Orders sidecar","kind":"gateway","x":31,"y":42},{"id":"scpay1","label":"Payments sidecar A","kind":"gateway","x":58,"y":18},{"id":"pay1","label":"Payments pod A","kind":"service","x":85,"y":18},{"id":"scpay2","label":"Payments sidecar B","kind":"gateway","x":58,"y":66},{"id":"pay2","label":"Payments pod B","kind":"service","x":85,"y":66},{"id":"ctl","label":"Control plane","kind":"server","x":31,"y":88}],"edges":[{"from":"orders","to":"scorders"},{"from":"scorders","to":"scpay1"},{"from":"scorders","to":"scpay2"},{"from":"scpay1","to":"pay1"},{"from":"scpay2","to":"pay2"},{"from":"ctl","to":"scorders"},{"from":"ctl","to":"scpay1"},{"from":"ctl","to":"scpay2"}],"metrics":[{"id":"success","label":"Success","unit":"%"},{"id":"latency","label":"Call latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"tries","label":"Attempts","unit":"x"},{"id":"ram","label":"Proxy RAM","unit":"MB/pod"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Every service needs the same plumbing","body":"Orders calls Payments over the network. Someone has to encrypt that call, prove who is calling, set a timeout, retry safely and record metrics. If every team writes this itself, in its own language and library, you end up with 40 slightly different versions, and fixing one bug means redeploying everything. Payments runs as two copies, pod A and pod B (a pod is one running copy of a service). Can we move all this plumbing out of the application?","idea":"Cross-cutting network behavior is repeated in every service unless you pull it into shared infrastructure.","caption":"Orders must call Payments, which runs as two pods, A and B.","focus":["orders","pay1","pay2"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"A sidecar proxy beside each pod","body":"A sidecar is a small helper proxy (Envoy is the common one) that runs in the same pod as the app and shares its network. Network rules quietly redirect the app's outgoing calls to the proxy, so the app just makes a plain call and never knows. The animation shows Orders sending GET /payment/42 and its sidecar catching it. The proxy then does the networking work on the app's behalf. The app code does not change.","idea":"A sidecar intercepts traffic at the network level, so any language gets the same behavior with no code changes.","caption":"Orders makes a plain call, and its sidecar catches it.","focus":["orders","scorders"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"scorders","kind":"request","label":"GET /payment/42"}],"metrics":{"ram":"80"},"chips":[{"node":"scorders","text":"Envoy sidecar"},{"node":"scorders","text":"~80 MB RAM","at":"bottom"},{"node":"orders","text":"app unchanged","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Control plane vs data plane","body":"The sidecars together are the data plane: they carry every real request. A separate control plane (for example Istiod) never touches requests. It watches which pods exist, holds your policies, and pushes routes, endpoint lists (the addresses of healthy pods) and certificates to every proxy. The animation shows those pushes. Think of the control plane as the planner and the proxies as the workers.","idea":"The data plane moves traffic; the control plane only tells the data plane how to behave.","caption":"The control plane pushes routes and certificates to each proxy.","focus":["ctl","scorders","scpay1","scpay2"],"flows":[{"from":"ctl","to":"scorders","kind":"async","label":"routes + cert"},{"from":"ctl","to":"scpay1","kind":"async","label":"routes + cert"},{"from":"ctl","to":"scpay2","kind":"async","label":"routes + cert"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"ctl","text":"pushes config"},{"node":"scorders","text":"data plane"},{"node":"scpay1","text":"data plane"},{"node":"scpay2","text":"data plane"}]},{"tag":"SECURITY","title":"mTLS: both sides prove who they are","body":"Normal TLS only proves the server's identity. In mutual TLS (mTLS) the client shows a certificate (a signed ID card) too. The control plane gives each workload a short-lived certificate, often valid about 24 hours, tied to its identity, such as the orders service account. The animation shows the two sidecars exchanging certificates, checking each other and agreeing on encryption keys. Now Payments can trust that the caller really is Orders, and the link is encrypted.","idea":"mTLS gives every hop encryption plus a verified service identity, and short-lived certs limit damage from a leak.","caption":"The two sidecars swap certificates and verify each other's identity.","focus":["scorders","scpay1"],"flows":[{"from":"scorders","to":"scpay1","kind":"handshake","label":"mTLS hello + cert"},{"from":"scpay1","to":"scorders","kind":"handshake","label":"cert + key share"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"scorders","text":"client cert"},{"node":"scpay1","text":"mTLS ok","tone":"green"},{"node":"scpay1","text":"cert ≈ 24 h","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"A request through two proxies","body":"With the secure link up, the call goes app, sidecar, sidecar, app. The proxies pick a healthy Payments pod from the list the control plane gave them, record a trace span (a timing record) and metrics, and forward the request. Each proxy adds about 1 to 2 ms, so the two proxies add roughly 2 to 4 ms. The whole call takes about 14 ms here, and 200 comes back.","idea":"The mesh sits on the request path, so every call crosses two extra proxies.","caption":"The request goes orders, sidecar, sidecar, payments, and the 200 reply comes back.","focus":["orders","scorders","scpay1","pay1"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"scorders","kind":"request","label":"GET /payment/42"},{"from":"scorders","to":"scpay1","kind":"request","label":"mTLS"},{"from":"scpay1","to":"pay1","kind":"request","label":"GET /payment/42"},{"from":"pay1","to":"scpay1","kind":"response","label":"200"},{"from":"scpay1","to":"scorders","kind":"response","label":"200"},{"from":"scorders","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"200"}],"metrics":{"success":"100","latency":"14","tries":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"orders","text":"14 ms call"},{"node":"scorders","text":"+1–2 ms"},{"node":"pay1","text":"200 OK","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Payments pod A stalls","body":"Now pod A hangs, perhaps stuck in a garbage-collection pause (the runtime freezing to clean up memory). Without a limit, Orders would wait forever and its threads would pile up. The mesh config sets a per-try timeout of 250 ms: a limit on each single attempt. When it expires, the Orders sidecar gives up on that attempt itself, with no code in the Orders app.","idea":"A per-try timeout in the proxy turns an open-ended hang into a bounded, known cost.","caption":"The attempt on pod A times out after 250 ms.","focus":["scorders","scpay1","pay1"],"flows":[{"from":"scorders","to":"scpay1","kind":"request","label":"GET /payment/42"},{"from":"scpay1","to":"pay1","kind":"request","label":"GET /payment/42"},{"from":"pay1","to":"scpay1","kind":"fail","label":"no reply"},{"from":"scorders","to":"scpay1","kind":"fail","label":"timeout 250ms"}],"metrics":{"latency":"250","tries":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"pay1","text":"stalled","tone":"red"},{"node":"scorders","text":"timeout 250 ms","tone":"red"},{"node":"scpay1","text":"no reply","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RETRY","title":"Retry on a different pod","body":"The mesh retries once, and it is set to avoid the pod that just failed (Istio does this by default), so the retry lands on pod B and succeeds. The user sees a slower but successful call: 250 ms of waiting plus about 14 ms, 264 ms total. This request is a read, so repeating it is safe. A charge would need an idempotency key so Payments can drop a duplicate, so you turn retries on per route. A retry budget (for example at most 20% extra requests) stops a retry storm from burying a struggling service.","idea":"Retries hide transient failures, but only if the call is safe to repeat and the volume is capped.","caption":"The second attempt goes to pod B and succeeds.","focus":["scorders","scpay2","pay2"],"flows":[{"from":"scorders","to":"scpay2","kind":"request","label":"retry #1"},{"from":"scpay2","to":"pay2","kind":"request","label":"GET /payment/42"},{"from":"pay2","to":"scpay2","kind":"response","label":"200"},{"from":"scpay2","to":"scorders","kind":"response","label":"200"}],"metrics":{"success":"100","latency":"264","tries":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"scorders","text":"264 ms total"},{"node":"scpay2","text":"retry #1"},{"node":"pay2","text":"200 OK","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"What the mesh costs","body":"Every pod now runs an extra proxy. A sidecar commonly uses tens of MB of memory (roughly 50 to 100 MB is a rule of thumb) plus some CPU, so 1,000 pods can burn 50 to 100 GB of RAM on proxies alone. Calls gain about 2 to 4 ms across the two proxies, and your team must learn, upgrade and debug a whole new layer. Newer designs such as Istio ambient mode (generally available since late 2024) move the proxy to one shared component per node to cut the per-pod cost.","idea":"A mesh buys uniform security and reliability at the price of latency, memory and operational complexity.","caption":"Each pod now carries its own proxy, plus extra hops.","focus":["orders","scorders","scpay1","pay1","scpay2","pay2"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"ram":"80","latency":"14","tries":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"scorders","text":"50-100 MB per pod"},{"node":"orders","text":"+2–4 ms"},{"node":"scpay1","text":"1000 pods: 50-100 GB","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The control plane goes down","body":"Suppose the control plane crashes. The proxies already hold their routes, endpoint lists and certificates in memory, so live traffic keeps flowing, as the animation shows. What breaks is change: new pods are not discovered, policy edits do not apply and certificates cannot renew. A short outage is fine. Certificates last about 24 hours, so an outage longer than that would eventually break mTLS.","idea":"Because the planes are separate, a control plane outage blocks changes, not existing traffic.","caption":"Config pushes fail, but requests still succeed.","focus":["ctl","orders","scorders","scpay1","pay1"],"flows":[{"from":"ctl","to":"scorders","kind":"fail","label":"unreachable"},{"from":"orders","to":"scorders","kind":"request","label":"GET /payment/42"},{"from":"scorders","to":"scpay1","kind":"request","label":"cached config"},{"from":"scpay1","to":"pay1","kind":"request","label":"GET /payment/42"},{"from":"pay1","to":"scpay1","kind":"response","label":"200"},{"from":"scpay1","to":"scorders","kind":"response","label":"200"},{"from":"scorders","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"200"}],"metrics":{"success":"100","latency":"14","tries":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"ctl","text":"down","tone":"red"},{"node":"scorders","text":"cached config","tone":"green"},{"node":"pay1","text":"200 OK","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Plumbing as infrastructure","body":"A service mesh puts a sidecar proxy beside every service. The data plane of proxies carries the traffic, applying mTLS, per-try timeouts and budgeted retries the same way everywhere. The control plane hands out config and certificates. You gain consistency and security without app changes. You pay with extra hops, memory per pod and one more system to run.","idea":"Use a mesh when many services need uniform security and resilience, and the overhead is worth that consistency.","caption":"The control plane configures; the data plane carries traffic.","focus":["orders","scorders","scpay1","pay1","scpay2","pay2","ctl"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"ctl","text":"config + certs"},{"node":"scorders","text":"mTLS + retries"},{"node":"orders","text":"no app change","tone":"green"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"The control plane in a service mesh crashes. What happens to requests already flowing between services?","options":["All requests fail immediately because proxies need live instructions","They keep working using the config and certificates the proxies already hold","They are routed through the control plane instead","They continue unencrypted until it recovers"],"answer":1,"why":"The control plane only distributes configuration. Proxies cache it and keep carrying traffic; only changes and cert renewals are blocked."},{"q":"What does mTLS add compared with ordinary TLS from a browser to a server?","options":["Stronger encryption algorithms","It removes the handshake round trips","The client also presents a certificate, so the server knows which service is calling","It lets proxies read plaintext without keys"],"answer":2,"why":"In mTLS both sides authenticate. That gives the receiving service a verified caller identity it can write policies against, not just an encrypted pipe."},{"q":"A mesh makes up to 3 attempts per call to Payments. Why might that make an overloaded Payments service worse?","options":["Retries disable mTLS","Retries always go to the same pod","Sidecars cannot time out a request they retry","Retries multiply load: if every caller retries, traffic can triple exactly when the service is struggling"],"answer":3,"why":"Unbounded retries amplify load into a retry storm. A retry budget and retrying only idempotent routes keep the amplification small."},{"q":"A team with 3 services in one language asks whether to adopt a full mesh. Which reasoning is best?","options":["Yes, a mesh is always required for microservices","Probably not yet: a shared client library may give the same timeouts and retries with far less overhead and operational burden","No, because sidecars cannot work with only 3 services","Yes, because meshes remove all network latency"],"answer":1,"why":"A mesh pays off with many services, mixed languages and a security need for uniform mTLS. For a few services the added hops, memory and complexity often outweigh the benefit."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Sidecar proxy","def":"A proxy container in the same pod as the app that intercepts its network traffic and applies mesh policy without code changes."},{"term":"Data plane","def":"The proxies that carry real requests and enforce mTLS, timeouts, retries and emit metrics."},{"term":"Control plane","def":"The component that watches the cluster, stores policy and pushes routes, endpoints and certificates to the proxies."},{"term":"mTLS","def":"Mutual TLS: both client and server present certificates, giving encryption plus verified service identity on each hop."},{"term":"Per-try timeout","def":"A limit on a single attempt, so a hung pod costs a bounded time before the proxy retries elsewhere."},{"term":"Retry budget","def":"A cap on extra traffic from retries (for example 20% of requests) to prevent retry storms. Retry only idempotent calls."},{"term":"Mesh overhead","def":"Rule of thumb: about 1 to 2 ms per proxy hop, and tens of MB of RAM per sidecar. Measure in your own cluster."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"serverless","title":"Serverless & Containers","subtitle":"Watch the same request hit an always-on container and a function that has to be created first, and see when each one wins.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["What a container is and what an orchestrator like Kubernetes decides for you","How a scheduler places pods and a control loop keeps the desired count true","How functions-as-a-service scale to zero and what a cold start costs","Why cold starts happen and the usual ways to shrink them","How to choose between containers and functions for a given workload"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Client","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"gw","label":"Gateway / router","kind":"gateway","x":29,"y":50},{"id":"sched","label":"Scheduler","kind":"service","x":54,"y":14},{"id":"pod","label":"Container pod","kind":"server","x":54,"y":50},{"id":"fn","label":"Function runtime","kind":"worker","x":54,"y":86},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":88,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"gw"},{"from":"gw","to":"pod"},{"from":"gw","to":"fn"},{"from":"sched","to":"pod"},{"from":"pod","to":"db"},{"from":"fn","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"latency","label":"Latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"pods","label":"Running pods","unit":""},{"id":"fns","label":"Warm functions","unit":""}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Who runs your code, and when?","body":"Your API handler is written. Someone must start it on a machine, give it CPU and memory, restart it if it crashes and add copies when traffic grows. You can hand that job to a container orchestrator, which keeps your code running all the time, or to a functions platform, which runs it only while a request is in flight. The same code, with very different trade-offs. The diagram shows a client and a gateway (the router that receives requests) waiting to see where the handler will run.","idea":"Containers and functions are two answers to the same question: who owns starting and stopping your process?","caption":"A client and a gateway: where should the handler run?","focus":["client","gw"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"latency":"0","pods":"0","fns":"0"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"A container is a packaged process","body":"A container bundles your app with its libraries and runs as an ordinary process. The kernel isolates it using namespaces (what the process can see) and cgroups (how much it can use). Once the image is on the machine, it starts in well under a second, and it costs megabytes, not the gigabytes of a full virtual machine. Kubernetes wraps one or more containers in a pod, the smallest unit it schedules.","idea":"A container is an isolated process plus its dependencies, not a small virtual machine.","caption":"The pod holds your container image, ready to serve.","focus":["pod"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"pod","text":"starts in < 1 s"},{"node":"pod","text":"MB, not GB"}]},{"tag":"ORCHESTRATE","title":"The scheduler places pods","body":"You tell Kubernetes the desired state: run 3 copies (replicas) of this image, each asking for 0.5 CPU and 512 MB. The scheduler looks at every node (a machine in the cluster) and its spare capacity, and picks a fit for each new pod, as the animation shows. Then controllers loop forever: compare what is running with what you asked for, and fix any difference by starting or killing pods.","idea":"You declare the desired state; control loops keep reconciling reality toward it, including replacing crashed pods.","caption":"The scheduler assigns pods to nodes that have spare capacity.","focus":["sched","pod"],"flows":[{"from":"sched","to":"pod","kind":"async","label":"assign to node"}],"metrics":{"pods":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"sched","text":"desired: 3 replicas"},{"node":"pod","text":"0.5 CPU · 512 MB"},{"node":"pod","text":"3 running","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Steady traffic on warm pods","body":"A request reaches the gateway, which routes it to one of the running pods. The process is already loaded with its database connections open, so the handler just runs. We assume a simple request takes about 25 ms. The catch: those 3 pods are paid for around the clock, busy or idle. Adding pods is not instant either. The scheduler must place each one, the node must download the image and the pod must pass health checks, so scaling up takes seconds to minutes.","idea":"Always-on containers give predictable low latency, and you pay for capacity whether or not it is used.","caption":"A request is served by one of 3 pods that are already running.","focus":["client","gw","pod","db"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders"},{"from":"gw","to":"pod","kind":"request","label":"route to 1 of 3"},{"from":"pod","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"query"},{"from":"db","to":"pod","kind":"response","label":"rows"},{"from":"pod","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"},{"from":"gw","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"latency":"25"},"chips":[{"node":"gw","text":"route to 1 of 3"},{"node":"pod","text":"warm · 25 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"pod","text":"paid even if idle","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Functions run only on demand","body":"With functions-as-a-service you upload a handler, not a server. The platform runs it per event and bills for the time your code actually runs, in milliseconds on most platforms. When nothing is happening, zero copies run and you pay nothing. On platforms like AWS Lambda each copy handles one request at a time, so 200 simultaneous requests mean up to 200 copies (some platforms let one copy serve many). Scaling is automatic, within limits such as maximum concurrency and run time.","idea":"Functions scale to zero and out per request, and you are billed for execution time rather than idle capacity.","caption":"While idle, the function runtime holds no copies at all.","focus":["fn"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"fns":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"fn","text":"0 copies when idle"},{"node":"fn","text":"billed per ms"},{"node":"fn","text":"1 request per copy","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The first request: a cold start","body":"A request arrives and no copy of the function exists. The platform must find a host, create a sandbox (an isolated space to run in), download your code, start the language runtime and run your setup code, including opening a database connection. This is a cold start. Setup typically takes 100 ms to a couple of seconds, and heavy runtimes like Java can take several seconds without snapshotting. We assume about 800 ms, so this request takes roughly 850 ms. Only that unlucky request pays it.","idea":"A cold start is the setup tax paid by a request that arrives when no warm copy is available.","caption":"The sandbox is built first, so the handler cannot run right away.","focus":["client","gw","fn","db"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders"},{"from":"gw","to":"fn","kind":"handshake","label":"sandbox + init"},{"from":"fn","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"connect + query"},{"from":"db","to":"fn","kind":"response","label":"rows"},{"from":"fn","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"},{"from":"gw","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"latency":"850","fns":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"fn","text":"cold start","tone":"red"},{"node":"fn","text":"+800 ms setup","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"client","text":"≈ 850 ms","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Warm reuse and the fan-out","body":"After a request, the platform keeps the sandbox alive for some minutes. The next request skips setup and runs in about 30 ms (a little more than the pod, because of platform routing). But a burst of 500 requests at once needs up to 500 copies, and each new one cold starts. If every copy opens its own database connection, that can pass a database limit of a few hundred, as the animation shows. Connection poolers (shared connections) and concurrency limits exist for this reason.","idea":"Warm copies are fast, but bursts create many cold starts and can overwhelm downstream dependencies like databases.","caption":"A warm copy answers in about 30 ms; a 500-copy burst overruns the database limit.","focus":["gw","fn","db"],"flows":[{"from":"gw","to":"fn","kind":"request","label":"reuse warm copy"},{"from":"fn","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"query"},{"from":"db","to":"fn","kind":"response","label":"rows"},{"from":"gw","to":"fn","kind":"request","label":"burst: 500 reqs"},{"from":"db","to":"fn","kind":"fail","label":"conn limit hit"}],"metrics":{"latency":"30","fns":"500"},"chips":[{"node":"fn","text":"warm · 30 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"fn","text":"500 copies","at":"bottom"},{"node":"db","text":"conn limit hit","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Shrinking cold starts","body":"Cold starts can be reduced, not removed. Keep the package small, avoid heavy work at startup, pick a fast-starting runtime and load rarely used libraries only when needed. You can also pay for provisioned (minimum) instances that stay warm, and some platforms snapshot a ready runtime and restore it. Each fix trades money or complexity for speed, so the more you keep warm, the closer you drift toward an always-on container. Here we keep 5 copies provisioned.","idea":"You can buy down cold starts with warm capacity, which erodes the cost advantage of scale to zero.","caption":"Keeping copies warm costs money but removes the wait.","focus":["gw","fn"],"flows":[{"from":"gw","to":"fn","kind":"request","label":"hits warm copy"}],"metrics":{"latency":"30","fns":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"fn","text":"5 provisioned","tone":"green"},{"node":"fn","text":"warm · 30 ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Choosing between them","body":"Pick containers for steady, high-volume, latency-sensitive services, long-lived connections such as websockets, and long jobs or tight control of the environment. Pick functions for spiky or rare traffic, small event jobs like resizing an image after upload, and small teams who do not want to run a cluster. Serverless container platforms blur the line by running container images that scale to zero. Many systems use both: containers for the core, functions around the edges.","idea":"Steady and latency-critical favours containers; spiky, rare or event-driven favours functions; mixing is normal.","caption":"One gateway, two backends, chosen per workload.","focus":[],"flows":[{"from":"gw","to":"pod","kind":"request","label":"steady API"},{"from":"gw","to":"fn","kind":"request","label":"spiky events"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"pod","text":"steady, high volume"},{"node":"fn","text":"spiky, rare"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"You declare 3 replicas in Kubernetes and one node dies, taking a pod with it. What happens?","options":["The service stays at 2 pods until an operator steps in to fix it","All pods restart together to keep versions consistent","A controller sees 2 running versus 3 desired and the scheduler places a replacement","The gateway queues requests until the failed node returns"],"answer":2,"why":"Orchestrators run reconciliation loops that compare actual and desired state. The missing pod is recreated on a node with free capacity without human action. Detection is not instant: by default a dead node's pods are replaced after a few minutes."},{"q":"A function handles 5 requests a day and each runs 200 ms. A container serving it runs 24/7. Which statement is most likely true?","options":["The function is cheaper because you pay only for the tiny execution time","The container is cheaper because it avoids all startup cost","They cost the same because both reserve one CPU","The function cannot run this workload at all"],"answer":0,"why":"At very low volume, idle capacity dominates. Scale to zero means almost nothing is billed, and the occasional cold start rarely matters for such traffic."},{"q":"Which request pays the cold start penalty?","options":["Every request to a function","A request that arrives when no warm copy is free, such as after idle time or a burst","Only the first request after deploying","Requests with large payloads"],"answer":1,"why":"Cold starts occur whenever a new copy must be created: after idle expiry, on scale-out for concurrent requests, or after a new deploy."},{"q":"A traffic spike takes your functions from 5 to 400 concurrent copies and the database starts refusing connections. What is the best fix?","options":["Give each function more memory so it finishes sooner","Switch the database to a different region","Increase the cold start timeout on the platform","Cap function concurrency and add a connection pooler in front of the database"],"answer":3,"why":"Each copy opens its own connections, so scale-out multiplies them. Limiting concurrency and pooling connections protects the database."},{"q":"A voice-chat service holds open connections for an hour each and needs steady low latency. What fits best?","options":["Functions, because they scale to zero when idle","Containers, because long-lived connections suit always-on processes","Functions, because a cold start only happens once ever","Neither can handle persistent connections at all"],"answer":1,"why":"Functions have run-time limits and are billed for execution time, so holding hour-long connections is awkward and expensive. Always-on containers handle them naturally."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Container","def":"Isolated process with its dependencies, sharing the host kernel. Starts in under a second once its image is local, and is far lighter than a VM."},{"term":"Pod","def":"Smallest Kubernetes unit: one or more tightly coupled containers scheduled together on one node."},{"term":"Scheduler","def":"Chooses a node for each new pod based on requested CPU and memory, spare capacity, and placement rules."},{"term":"Reconciliation loop","def":"Controllers repeatedly compare desired state with actual state and act to close the gap, such as replacing a dead pod."},{"term":"Function (FaaS)","def":"A handler run per event, billed by execution time, scaling to zero when idle. Limited run time and concurrency."},{"term":"Cold start","def":"Extra latency when a new sandbox must be created: place, fetch code, start runtime, initialise. Roughly 100 ms to seconds."},{"term":"Cold start fixes","def":"Smaller packages, light startup work, faster runtimes, provisioned warm instances, or snapshot restore."},{"term":"Rule of thumb","def":"Steady, latency-critical, long-lived connections: containers. Spiky, rare, event-driven glue: functions."}],"isNew":true}]},{"id":"security","title":"Security","lessons":[{"slug":"authentication","title":"Authentication","subtitle":"Watch a user prove who they are with a password, a session or token, an OAuth login, and a second factor.","minutes":14,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why passwords are stored as slow salted hashes, never as text or fast hashes","How server-side sessions and signed JWTs differ, and what each costs you","The OAuth 2.0 / OIDC authorization-code login flow, hop by hop","How MFA adds a second factor and why phishing-resistant factors matter","How short-lived access tokens plus refresh tokens limit the damage of theft"],"nodes":[{"id":"browser","label":"Browser","kind":"client","x":10,"y":50,"url":"https://app.example"},{"id":"app","label":"App server","kind":"server","x":38,"y":50},{"id":"sessions","label":"Session store","kind":"cache","x":68,"y":18},{"id":"users","label":"User database","kind":"db","x":68,"y":50},{"id":"idp","label":"Identity provider","kind":"external","x":38,"y":86},{"id":"phone","label":"Authenticator","kind":"user","x":10,"y":86},{"id":"attacker","label":"Attacker","kind":"external","x":92,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"browser","to":"app"},{"from":"app","to":"users"},{"from":"app","to":"sessions"},{"from":"browser","to":"idp"},{"from":"app","to":"idp"},{"from":"browser","to":"phone"},{"from":"users","to":"attacker"}],"metrics":[{"id":"hash","label":"Hash cost","unit":"ms"},{"id":"lookups","label":"Store reads","unit":"per req"},{"id":"ttl","label":"Credential life","unit":"min"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Who is this, really?","body":"HTTP has no memory: every request arrives as if it were the first. So when someone clicks Pay, how does the server know it is the same person who logged in a minute ago, and that they really are who they claim to be? Authentication means proving who you are. This lesson follows one login, from the first password to the tokens that keep you logged in.","idea":"Authentication proves identity once, then hands the client something cheaper to present on every later request.","caption":"A browser and an app server. Nobody has proved who they are yet.","focus":["browser","app"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[]},{"tag":"PASSWORD","title":"Check the password without keeping it","body":"You type your password and the browser sends it to the app over TLS (an encrypted connection). The app fetches your stored salt and hash from the user database. A salt is a random value saved next to the hash, so two users with the same password get different hashes. The app hashes what you typed with that salt and compares. The hash must be deliberately slow: Argon2id and scrypt are slow and memory-hungry, bcrypt is only slow. Aim for about 100 ms per check (a rule of thumb; never go below OWASP's minimum settings). A fast hash like SHA-256 lets attackers try billions of guesses per second. A match is not the end: MFA comes next.","idea":"Store a per-user salted slow hash. The server compares hashes and never needs to keep the password itself.","caption":"The app fetches the stored salt and hash, then compares.","focus":["browser","app","users"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"POST /login"},{"from":"app","to":"users","kind":"request","label":"get salt+hash"},{"from":"users","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"salt + hash"}],"metrics":{"hash":"100","ttl":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"app","text":"Argon2id · ~100 ms"},{"node":"users","text":"salt + hash"}]},{"tag":"SESSION","title":"Option A: a server-side session","body":"Once login has fully succeeded (password plus any second factor, see the MFA step), the app creates a random 128-bit session ID. It saves the ID in a session store such as Redis, with the user ID and a 30-minute idle timeout. Then it sends the ID to the browser in a cookie marked HttpOnly (scripts cannot read it), Secure (HTTPS only) and SameSite (not sent on most cross-site requests). Browsers attach cookies automatically, so add anti-CSRF checks too. Each later request carries the cookie and costs one store lookup. To log someone out or ban them, delete their record.","idea":"A session is an opaque ID that points at data on the server. Revoking is instant, but every request costs a lookup.","caption":"App saves the session and sets a cookie. Each request then looks it up.","focus":["app","sessions","browser"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"sessions","kind":"request","label":"SET sid -> user 42"},{"from":"sessions","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"OK, TTL 30m"},{"from":"app","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"Set-Cookie: sid"},{"from":"browser","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"Cookie: sid"},{"from":"app","to":"sessions","kind":"request","label":"lookup sid"},{"from":"sessions","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"user 42"}],"metrics":{"lookups":"1","ttl":"30","hash":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"sessions","text":"TTL 30 min"},{"node":"browser","text":"HttpOnly · Secure"},{"node":"sessions","text":"1 lookup / request","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Option B: a signed JWT","body":"Instead of keeping state on the server, the app can hand out a JSON Web Token (JWT). It holds claims (facts such as user ID and expiry) plus a signature made with a secret key. Any service can check the signature without calling a database. With public-key signing, services need only the public key, so they cannot forge tokens. The client sends the JWT as a Bearer token (whoever holds it is trusted). The catch: a JWT stays valid until it expires and is hard to revoke, so keep access tokens short, around 15 minutes.","idea":"JWTs trade revocability for stateless verification; keep them short-lived because you cannot take them back.","caption":"Signed token is checked locally: no store lookup.","focus":["app","browser"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"JWT (exp 15m)"},{"from":"browser","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"Bearer JWT"}],"metrics":{"lookups":"0","ttl":"15"},"chips":[{"node":"browser","text":"JWT · exp 15 min"},{"node":"app","text":"0 store reads","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"OAUTH","title":"Log in with someone else's identity","body":"With \"Sign in with Google\", your app never sees the password. Your app redirects the browser to the identity provider (IdP), sending a random state value and a PKCE challenge (a hash of a secret the app keeps). The user logs in at the IdP, which redirects back with a short-lived, single-use authorization code. Your server trades the code, plus the original PKCE secret (the verifier) and its client secret if it has one, for an ID token (OpenID Connect, OIDC: who the user is) and an access token. Only the code travels through the browser. Your app then starts its own session.","idea":"OAuth/OIDC delegates the password to a specialist; your app only exchanges a one-time code for tokens over a back channel.","caption":"Redirect out, code comes back, tokens swap server to server.","focus":["browser","app","idp","sessions"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"redirect to IdP"},{"from":"browser","to":"idp","kind":"request","label":"login + consent"},{"from":"idp","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"redirect + code"},{"from":"browser","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"code"},{"from":"app","to":"idp","kind":"request","label":"code + verifier"},{"from":"idp","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"ID + access token"},{"from":"app","to":"sessions","kind":"request","label":"start session"}],"metrics":{"lookups":"1","ttl":"30"},"chips":[{"node":"idp","text":"code + PKCE"},{"node":"app","text":"ID + access token"},{"node":"sessions","text":"session started","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"MFA","title":"Add a second factor","body":"A password alone can be phished, guessed, or reused from another breach. MFA (multi-factor authentication) asks for something more: a one-time code from an authenticator app, a push approval, or a passkey (WebAuthn: a key pair kept on your device or hardware key). A passkey signs a challenge tied to the real site's domain, so a lookalike site gets nothing usable. SMS codes beat nothing but can be SIM-swapped (an attacker moves your phone number to their SIM). Here the app pauses the login, the browser asks the authenticator, and only then does the app issue the session or tokens.","idea":"Require a second, independent factor, and prefer phishing-resistant ones like passkeys over typed codes.","caption":"Login pauses until the second factor checks out.","focus":["app","browser","phone"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"MFA required"},{"from":"browser","to":"phone","kind":"request","label":"prompt"},{"from":"phone","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"signed or 6 digits"},{"from":"browser","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"factor 2"},{"from":"app","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"login complete"}],"metrics":{"hash":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"phone","text":"passkey"},{"node":"browser","text":"factor 2"},{"node":"app","text":"login complete","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"REFRESH","title":"Stay logged in without long-lived access","body":"A 15-minute access token would log users out constantly, so login also returns a refresh token that lasts days or weeks. When the access token expires, the browser sends the refresh token and gets a fresh pair. Good practice is rotation: each refresh token works once, and if an old one is replayed the server revokes the whole chain, because replay suggests theft. The refresh token lives in a server-checked store, so it can be revoked (drawn here as the session store).","idea":"Short access tokens limit exposure; a revocable, rotating refresh token provides the long session.","caption":"Expired access token is swapped for a fresh pair.","focus":["browser","app","sessions"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"refresh token"},{"from":"app","to":"sessions","kind":"request","label":"valid? rotate"},{"from":"sessions","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"ok, new one"},{"from":"app","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"new access+refresh"}],"metrics":{"lookups":"1","ttl":"15"},"chips":[{"node":"browser","text":"refresh: days"},{"node":"sessions","text":"rotate on use"},{"node":"app","text":"new pair","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The user table leaks","body":"Suppose an attacker steals the whole user table. Because each password is a slow salted hash, they must guess one password at a time. At 100 ms per try that is only 10 guesses per second per CPU core. Attackers with GPUs and many cores multiply that, which is why weak passwords still fall. Salts stop one guess from checking every user at once. Plain text or unsalted MD5 would have exposed everyone immediately, and reused passwords would be tried on other sites.","idea":"Assume the database will leak one day; slow salted hashes plus MFA turn a breach into a slow, partial problem.","caption":"Attacker holds the hashes but must guess one at a time.","focus":["users","attacker"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"attacker","kind":"fail","label":"table dumped"}],"metrics":{"hash":"100"},"chips":[{"node":"users","text":"table dumped","tone":"red"},{"node":"attacker","text":"10 guesses/s/core","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"The whole login","body":"Check the password against a slow salted hash, or delegate to an IdP with the OAuth/OIDC code flow. Add MFA, ideally a passkey. Then issue either a server-side session cookie, or a short-lived signed token with a rotating refresh token. Sessions make revoking easy, JWTs make verifying stateless, and many real systems combine both. What a logged-in user may do is authorization, a separate lesson.","idea":"Prove identity once with strong factors, then carry it in something short-lived and revocable.","caption":"Password or IdP, second factor, then tokens.","focus":["browser","app","sessions","users","idp","phone"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"hash":"100","lookups":"1","ttl":"15"},"chips":[{"node":"app","text":"~100 ms hash"},{"node":"sessions","text":"1 lookup"},{"node":"browser","text":"JWT 15 min"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is bcrypt or Argon2id a better password hash than SHA-256?","options":["It produces a shorter output that is cheaper to store","It is reversible, so support staff can recover forgotten passwords","It is deliberately slow and tunable, so brute-forcing a leaked table is expensive","It removes the need for a salt"],"answer":2,"why":"Password hashes are deliberately slow (and, for Argon2id and scrypt, memory-hard) so attackers cannot test billions of guesses per second. They are not reversible, and salts are still used (usually built in)."},{"q":"A user reports their laptop was stolen and you must log them out everywhere right now. Which design makes that easiest?","options":["Server-side sessions: delete the user's session records","Self-contained JWTs valid for 24 hours with no server state","Asking the user to change their password (existing sessions stay valid)","Rotating the cookie's SameSite setting"],"answer":0,"why":"Sessions live on the server, so deleting them ends access immediately. A stateless JWT stays valid until it expires unless you add a denylist, which brings back server state."},{"q":"In the OAuth 2.0 authorization-code flow, why does the browser carry only a one-time code instead of the access token?","options":["Codes never expire, so the app can retry later","The token is exchanged server-to-server with the client secret or PKCE verifier, so a code intercepted in the browser is useless alone","Browsers cannot store tokens at all","It lets the IdP skip authenticating the user"],"answer":1,"why":"The code is short-lived and single-use, and redeeming it requires proof only your server (or the original client via PKCE) has, so the tokens never pass through the URL."},{"q":"An attacker runs a fake login page that relays whatever the user types, including a 6-digit authenticator code, to the real site in real time. Which second factor best defeats this?","options":["SMS code","A longer password","Emailed one-time code","A passkey (WebAuthn)"],"answer":3,"why":"Passkeys sign a challenge bound to the genuine site's domain, so a lookalike domain gets nothing usable. Typed codes can be relayed by the phishing page."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Salt","def":"Random per-user value mixed into the password before hashing, so identical passwords hash differently and precomputed tables fail."},{"term":"Argon2id / bcrypt","def":"Slow, tunable password hashes. A common rule of thumb is roughly 100 ms per hash on your hardware; never go below OWASP's minimum settings."},{"term":"Session cookie","def":"Random opaque ID pointing at server-side state. Set HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite (and use CSRF checks). Instantly revocable, costs a lookup per request."},{"term":"JWT","def":"Signed token holding claims and an expiry. Verified without a lookup, but valid until it expires. Keep access tokens ~5-15 minutes."},{"term":"OAuth 2.0 vs OIDC","def":"OAuth 2.0 delegates access to resources; OpenID Connect adds an ID token on top that says who the user is. Use the code flow with PKCE."},{"term":"Refresh token rotation","def":"Each refresh token is single-use and replaced on refresh; replaying an old one signals theft and revokes the chain."},{"term":"MFA / passkey","def":"A second independent factor. Passkeys (WebAuthn) are phishing-resistant because they are bound to the site's domain."},{"term":"Authentication vs authorization","def":"Authentication: who are you. Authorization: what may you do. Do the first, then the second, as separate checks."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"authorization","title":"Authorization","subtitle":"Watch one request get checked against roles, attributes, and relationships, then see how tenant isolation stops a bug from leaking data.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How RBAC, ABAC, and ReBAC answer the question 'may this user do this?' differently","Why policy decisions belong in one place while enforcement happens at every door","How least privilege and default deny shrink the damage of mistakes and stolen tokens","Why multi-tenant data needs isolation enforced below the application code"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Alice's browser","kind":"client","x":8,"y":50,"url":"https://docs.example"},{"id":"gateway","label":"API gateway","kind":"gateway","x":28,"y":50},{"id":"pdp","label":"Policy service","kind":"service","x":52,"y":18},{"id":"store","label":"Policy data","kind":"db","x":82,"y":18},{"id":"app","label":"Docs service","kind":"server","x":56,"y":66},{"id":"db","label":"Tenant database","kind":"db","x":85,"y":66}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"gateway"},{"from":"gateway","to":"pdp"},{"from":"pdp","to":"store"},{"from":"gateway","to":"app"},{"from":"app","to":"pdp"},{"from":"app","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"decision","label":"Decision","unit":"ms"},{"id":"rows","label":"Rows returned","unit":"rows"},{"id":"foreign","label":"Other-tenant","unit":"rows"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Logged in is not allowed","body":"Alice works at Acme and has just logged in. She asks for document 42. The system already knows who she is (authentication is a separate lesson). The new question is different: is she allowed to read this particular document? And could a bug show her Globex's documents by mistake?","idea":"Authentication answers 'who are you?'; authorization answers 'what may you do, to which thing?'","caption":"Alice is identified, but nothing says what she may do yet.","focus":["client"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"The request arrives","body":"Alice's browser sends GET /docs/42 with a signed token that names her user ID and her tenant (her company, here acme). The gateway checks that the token is genuine. Then it must decide whether to let the call through. It does not hold the rules itself: it will ask one dedicated policy service, the only place the rules live. One place for rules means every service gets the same answer.","idea":"The caller's identity is an input to the decision, never the decision itself.","caption":"Gateway receives the request with a verified identity.","focus":["client","gateway"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"gateway","kind":"request","label":"GET /docs/42"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"client","text":"tenant acme"},{"node":"gateway","text":"token valid","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"RBAC","title":"Roles: what job does she have?","body":"The gateway asks the policy service \"may alice read docs?\", and the policy service looks up her roles. In role-based access control (RBAC), permissions attach to roles and users are given roles. Alice is an editor, and editors may read and edit documents. RBAC is easy to explain and audit. It strains when rules depend on context, because you end up inventing roles like 'editor-of-project-7-in-EU'. Timing here is illustrative: about 1 ms per network hop, and 4 hops make about 4 ms.","idea":"RBAC is easy to audit but cannot say 'only her own team's files' without role explosion.","caption":"Policy service looks up Alice's roles.","focus":["gateway","pdp","store"],"flows":[{"from":"gateway","to":"pdp","kind":"request","label":"alice docs:read?"},{"from":"pdp","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"roles of alice"},{"from":"store","to":"pdp","kind":"response","label":"editor"}],"metrics":{"decision":"4"},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"alice: editor"},{"node":"pdp","text":"~1 ms per hop"}]},{"tag":"ABAC","title":"Attributes: what is the context?","body":"Next the policy service checks attributes: facts about the user, the document, and the situation. In attribute-based access control (ABAC), a rule might say: allow read if user.tenant equals doc.tenant, the document is not 'secret', and the request comes from a managed device. The service fetches the document's attributes (tenant=acme) and sees the tenants match. One rule covers millions of documents, but rules are harder to predict and test than a role list. Facts such as device state arrive with the call from the gateway.","idea":"ABAC expresses context-dependent rules compactly, at the price of harder-to-predict outcomes.","caption":"Rules compare facts about user, document, and request.","focus":["pdp","store"],"flows":[{"from":"pdp","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"doc:42 attrs"},{"from":"store","to":"pdp","kind":"response","label":"tenant=acme"}],"metrics":{"decision":"6"},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"tenant=acme"},{"node":"pdp","text":"same tenant · allow","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"REBAC","title":"Relationships: how is she connected?","body":"Relationship-based access control (ReBAC) stores links such as 'doc 42 is in folder Q3', 'folder Q3 is viewable by group eng', and 'Alice is in eng'. A check walks that chain of links from Alice to the document, as the animation shows. It handles nested folders and groups naturally, which is why Google Drive style sharing (popularised by Google's Zanzibar system) works this way. As a rule of thumb, the store should answer these graph queries in under about 10 ms, because every request waits on it.","idea":"ReBAC grants access by following chains of relationships, which fits sharing and hierarchies.","caption":"Check walks Alice, group eng, folder Q3, doc 42.","focus":["pdp","store"],"flows":[{"from":"pdp","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"alice -> eng -> Q3"},{"from":"store","to":"pdp","kind":"response","label":"path found"}],"metrics":{"decision":"8"},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"alice → eng → Q3"},{"node":"store","text":"target < 10 ms","at":"bottom"},{"node":"pdp","text":"path found","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CENTRAL","title":"One brain, many enforcers","body":"The policy service is the policy decision point (PDP): it decides allow or deny. The gateway and the Docs service are policy enforcement points (PEPs): they must obey the answer. The gateway asks first, about coarse rules ('may she call the docs API at all?'). Then the Docs service asks again about the specific object, because only it knows which document the call touches. Answers can be cached, for example for 30 seconds: faster, but a revoked permission lingers. If the policy service is unreachable, enforcers must fail closed (deny), so run it redundantly or as a local sidecar.","idea":"Decide centrally so rules stay consistent; enforce at every door so none can be skipped.","caption":"Gateway asks and is allowed, then the service re-checks the object.","focus":["gateway","app","pdp"],"flows":[{"from":"gateway","to":"pdp","kind":"request","label":"alice docs:read?"},{"from":"pdp","to":"gateway","kind":"response","label":"allow"},{"from":"gateway","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"GET /docs/42"},{"from":"app","to":"pdp","kind":"request","label":"read doc:42?"},{"from":"pdp","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"allow"}],"metrics":{"decision":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"pdp","text":"allow","tone":"green"},{"node":"pdp","text":"cached 30 s","at":"bottom"},{"node":"app","text":"fail closed"}]},{"tag":"TENANT","title":"Isolating tenants","body":"In a multi-tenant system, many customers share the same servers and tables, so every query must be limited to the caller's tenant. Take the tenant ID from the verified token. If a user can pick a tenant, check the pick against the token's memberships. Never trust a tenant ID the client merely claims. Make the database enforce it too, for instance with Postgres row-level security (the database itself hides other tenants' rows): the app connects as a role that cannot bypass the policy and sets the tenant for each request. Here the query returns exactly one row.","idea":"Tenant scope comes from the verified identity and is enforced by the data layer, not just by app code.","caption":"The query is restricted to tenant acme, so one row comes back.","focus":["app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"tenant=acme, id=42"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"1 row"}],"metrics":{"rows":"1","foreign":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"tenant=acme"},{"node":"db","text":"1 row","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A developer forgets the filter","body":"Now suppose a new list endpoint ships without the tenant condition, and the database has no row-level rule behind it. Say the table holds 1,200 documents and Acme owns only one of them. The query returns every row: 1,199 belong to other customers, and even the first page of results would include some. No attacker was needed, just one forgotten filter. Isolation must not depend on every developer remembering a WHERE clause.","idea":"If isolation lives only in application code, one missed filter becomes a cross-customer data breach.","caption":"Unscoped query returns other tenants' rows.","focus":["app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"no tenant filter"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"fail","label":"all rows"}],"metrics":{"rows":"1200","foreign":"1199"},"chips":[{"node":"app","text":"no tenant filter","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"1,200 rows","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Least privilege and default deny","body":"Give each user, token, and service only what its job needs (least privilege), and make 'no matching rule' mean deny. Alice's token carries a narrow scope (a permission label inside the token) such as docs:read, so a leaked token cannot delete anything. The Docs service's database login should not be able to drop tables either. Now Alice asks for a Globex document. The Docs service asks the policy service, no rule allows it, the answer is deny, and she gets a 403 Forbidden (many multi-tenant APIs return 404 instead, so outsiders cannot confirm a document exists).","idea":"Default deny plus narrow grants turns mistakes and stolen credentials into small incidents.","caption":"Object-level check finds no matching rule, so the request is refused.","focus":["client","gateway","app","pdp"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"gateway","kind":"request","label":"GET /docs/9001"},{"from":"gateway","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"GET /docs/9001"},{"from":"app","to":"pdp","kind":"request","label":"alice read 9001?"},{"from":"pdp","to":"app","kind":"fail","label":"deny"},{"from":"app","to":"gateway","kind":"fail","label":"403"},{"from":"gateway","to":"client","kind":"fail","label":"403 Forbidden"}],"metrics":{"decision":"3","rows":"0","foreign":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"client","text":"scope docs:read"},{"node":"pdp","text":"deny","tone":"red"},{"node":"client","text":"403 Forbidden","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Who may do what","body":"Authorization checks a verified identity against rules. RBAC uses roles, ABAC uses attributes, ReBAC uses relationships, and real systems often mix them. Keep decisions in one policy service, enforce at every service boundary, default to deny, grant the minimum, and enforce tenant scope in the database as well as in code.","idea":"Centralize the decision, enforce everywhere, deny by default, and isolate tenants below the app.","caption":"Every layer can say no.","focus":["client","gateway","pdp","store","app","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"decision":"3","rows":"0","foreign":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"pdp","text":"default deny","tone":"green"},{"node":"db","text":"row-level security","tone":"green"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A company's rule is 'managers may approve expenses only for people in their own reporting chain.' Which model fits most naturally?","options":["RBAC with a single 'manager' role","ReBAC, following manager-of links in the org graph","Authentication with MFA","Encrypting the expense table"],"answer":1,"why":"The permission depends on how two people are connected. A plain 'manager' role would let every manager approve for everyone; relationships express the chain directly."},{"q":"Your RBAC system now has 400 roles such as 'viewer-project-12-eu'. What is the best diagnosis?","options":["Roles are too coarse and rules depend on context, so attributes or relationships fit better","You need longer passwords","The policy service is too fast","Users should share one admin role"],"answer":0,"why":"Role explosion signals that the real rule involves resource or context facts. ABAC or ReBAC encode those directly instead of minting a role per combination."},{"q":"Why does the Docs service re-check permission even though the gateway already allowed the request?","options":["The gateway's allow decision only counts if the service re-signs it with a new token","Tokens cannot be verified twice","Re-checking is required by the TLS handshake","Only the service knows which specific object the call touches, and a bypassed gateway must not mean open access"],"answer":3,"why":"Coarse checks at the edge cannot cover object-level rules, and defense in depth means an internal caller or a gateway misconfiguration does not skip authorization."},{"q":"An endpoint reads tenant_id from a JSON field in the request body to scope its query. What is the flaw?","options":["JSON is slow to parse","The database cannot filter by tenant","A caller can set the field to another tenant's ID; tenant must come from the verified token","It breaks least privilege only for admins"],"answer":2,"why":"Anything the client sends is attacker-controlled. Tenant scope must derive from verified identity, and ideally be enforced again by row-level security."},{"q":"After Alice is removed from group eng, she can still read files for about 30 seconds. What is the most likely cause?","options":["A cached policy decision has not yet expired","RBAC forbids revocation","Row-level security is disabled","Her password is still valid"],"answer":0,"why":"Caching decisions speeds checks but delays revocation by up to the cache lifetime. Short TTLs or explicit invalidation shrink that window."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Authn vs authz","def":"Authentication proves who you are. Authorization decides what that identity may do to a specific resource."},{"term":"RBAC","def":"Permissions attach to roles, users get roles. Simple and auditable, but role counts explode when context matters."},{"term":"ABAC","def":"Rules over attributes of user, resource, and environment. Flexible and compact, but harder to test and explain."},{"term":"ReBAC","def":"Access follows relationship paths (user, group, folder, doc). Natural for sharing and hierarchies."},{"term":"PDP / PEP","def":"Policy decision point computes allow or deny; policy enforcement points (gateway, services) obey it."},{"term":"Least privilege","def":"Grant the minimum access needed, with narrow token scopes and default deny when no rule matches."},{"term":"Tenant isolation","def":"Take tenant ID from the verified identity and enforce it in the data layer (row-level security, per-tenant schemas or databases)."},{"term":"Decision caching","def":"Caching allow/deny for seconds cuts latency but delays revocation; choose the TTL by how bad stale access is."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"encryption","title":"Encryption in Transit & at Rest","subtitle":"Watch one card number get protected on the wire with TLS and on disk with envelope encryption and a KMS.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How a TLS 1.3 handshake uses asymmetric keys to agree on a fast symmetric key","Why bulk data is encrypted with symmetric keys and asymmetric crypto only sets them up","How envelope encryption keeps master keys inside a KMS and data keys next to the data","How key rotation works without re-encrypting everything at once","Why hashing is not encryption and when to use each"],"nodes":[{"id":"browser","label":"Browser","kind":"client","x":10,"y":55,"url":"https://shop.example"},{"id":"edge","label":"TLS terminator","kind":"gateway","x":31,"y":55},{"id":"app","label":"Payments service","kind":"service","x":53,"y":55},{"id":"kms","label":"KMS","kind":"external","x":76,"y":17},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":90,"y":55},{"id":"store","label":"Object storage","kind":"storage","x":76,"y":88}],"edges":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","label":"HTTPS"},{"from":"edge","to":"app","label":"mTLS"},{"from":"app","to":"kms"},{"from":"app","to":"db"},{"from":"app","to":"store"}],"metrics":[{"id":"rtt","label":"Browser TLS RTTs","unit":"RTT"},{"id":"kms","label":"KMS calls","unit":"calls"},{"id":"keys","label":"Keys in DB","unit":"keys"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Who can read this card number?","body":"A customer types a card number into the browser. It must cross the internet, pass through our servers, and then sit in a database and in nightly backups. If it is plain text, anyone who can watch the network, steal a disk, or copy a backup can read it. So we need protection in two places: while it moves (in transit) and while it sits (at rest).","idea":"Data is exposed in two states, moving and stored, and each needs its own protection.","caption":"A card number must travel and then be stored.","focus":["browser"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"rtt":"0","kms":"0","keys":"0"},"chips":[]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Two kinds of keys","body":"Encryption scrambles data with a key. Symmetric encryption (like AES-256-GCM) uses one shared secret key to lock and unlock. It is very fast: often several GB/s per core with hardware support. Asymmetric encryption uses a key pair: a public key anyone may have and a private key kept secret. It lets two strangers agree on a secret over an open network, but it is far slower (a rule of thumb: around a thousand times), so it is never used for bulk data.","idea":"Use asymmetric crypto to agree on a secret, then symmetric crypto to move the data.","caption":"Both sides need the same fast key, shared safely.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"browser","text":"AES-256-GCM"},{"node":"edge","text":"key pair"},{"node":"edge","text":"asym ~1000x slower","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"TLS 1.3 handshake","body":"TLS is the encryption behind HTTPS. In the TLS 1.3 handshake, the browser sends a hello with a fresh ephemeral public value (made for this one connection, then thrown away). The server replies with its own value and a certificate, and signs the exchange with its private key. The browser checks that the certificate chains to a trusted authority and matches the hostname. Both sides then compute the same session key without ever sending it. This takes one round trip, so on a path with a 50 ms round trip it adds about 50 ms (on top of the TCP connect) before encrypted data flows.","idea":"The handshake proves the server's identity and derives a shared key that never crosses the wire.","caption":"Handshake finishes in one round trip.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"handshake","label":"ClientHello"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"handshake","label":"cert + key share"}],"metrics":{"rtt":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"browser","text":"cert ok","tone":"green"},{"node":"edge","text":"1 RTT · +50 ms"},{"node":"browser","text":"session key","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Data moves under the session key","body":"Now the card number is encrypted with the symmetric session key, using an authenticated mode such as AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305. \"Authenticated\" means any tampering is detected, not just hidden. Someone watching the network sees only random-looking bytes (plus who is talking to whom and how much). Forward secrecy comes from the throwaway ephemeral values: if the server's long-term key is stolen later, recorded traffic still cannot be decrypted.","idea":"Encryption in transit hides data and protects integrity; ephemeral keys give forward secrecy.","caption":"Only ciphertext crosses the internet.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"ciphertext"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"edge","text":"ciphertext"},{"node":"browser","text":"AES-GCM"},{"node":"browser","text":"forward secrecy","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Where TLS ends","body":"The TLS terminator (the server that ends the TLS connection) decrypts the traffic so it can route and inspect it. If it then forwards plain HTTP, anyone inside the network can read the card number. Many teams re-encrypt that hop with mutual TLS (mTLS), where both sides present certificates. The cost is extra CPU and certificate management. TLS protects one connection, not the data after it is received.","idea":"TLS covers one hop; decide deliberately whether internal hops get encrypted too.","caption":"The internal hop is re-encrypted with mutual TLS.","focus":["edge","app"],"flows":[{"from":"edge","to":"app","kind":"handshake","label":"mTLS"},{"from":"edge","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"ciphertext"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"edge","text":"TLS ends here"},{"node":"app","text":"mTLS","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"At rest: envelope encryption","body":"Disk encryption protects a stolen drive, but not someone who steals a database login. To protect the field itself, use envelope encryption. The service asks the KMS (key management service, a hardened vault for keys) for a data key. The KMS returns it twice: plain, and wrapped (encrypted) by a master key that never leaves the KMS. The service encrypts the card number with the plain key, stores the wrapped key beside the ciphertext in the database, and discards the plain key from memory.","idea":"A master key in the KMS wraps many small data keys; only wrapped keys sit next to the data.","caption":"KMS issues a data key, plain and wrapped. Only the wrapped copy is stored.","focus":["app","kms","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"kms","kind":"request","label":"GenerateDataKey"},{"from":"kms","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"plain + wrapped"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"ciphertext+wrapped"}],"metrics":{"kms":"1","keys":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"kms","text":"master key stays","tone":"green"},{"node":"app","text":"plain key discarded"},{"node":"db","text":"ciphertext + wrapped","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Reading it back","body":"To read the record, the service loads the ciphertext and the wrapped data key, then asks the KMS to unwrap the key. The KMS checks the caller's permissions and logs the call, so every decrypt is auditable. The service then decrypts locally. The KMS only handles tiny keys, never the data, so a single master key can protect terabytes. Many services cache unwrapped keys briefly to cut KMS calls.","idea":"The KMS gates and audits access to keys; the bulk data never travels to it.","caption":"KMS unwraps the key; decryption is local.","focus":["app","kms","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"read record"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"ciphertext+wrapped"},{"from":"app","to":"kms","kind":"request","label":"Decrypt(wrapped)"},{"from":"kms","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"plain data key"}],"metrics":{"kms":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"kms","text":"decrypt logged","tone":"green"},{"node":"app","text":"decrypts locally"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Rotating the master key","body":"Policy says rotate the master key yearly, or on demand. Re-encrypting every record would be slow and risky. Instead the KMS creates a new master key version for all new wraps and keeps old versions to unwrap old keys, so old data keys keep working. Re-wrapping the small wrapped keys under the new version is optional and can run in the background. The bulk ciphertext is untouched. If a key was actually exposed, re-encrypt the data it protected, because rotation alone does not undo a copy.","idea":"Envelope encryption makes rotation cheap: re-wrap small keys, not big data.","caption":"Wrapped key in the DB re-wrapped under master key v2.","focus":["app","kms","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"load wrapped key"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"wrapped key v1"},{"from":"app","to":"kms","kind":"request","label":"ReEncrypt(key)"},{"from":"kms","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"key v2"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"async","label":"new wrapped key"}],"metrics":{"kms":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"kms","text":"master key v2"},{"node":"db","text":"wrapped key v1 → v2"},{"node":"db","text":"bulk untouched","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Hashing is not encryption","body":"Encryption can be reversed with the key. A hash function such as SHA-256 is one-way: it turns any input into a fixed 32-byte fingerprint that cannot be undone. Use hashes to detect corruption (did this backup change?). To resist tampering, keep the hash where an attacker cannot write, or use a keyed MAC or signature. Hashes also store passwords, but only with a slow salted algorithm. Never hash a card number you need back later, and a small input space can be brute-forced anyway.","idea":"Encrypt when you must read the data back; hash when you only need to compare or verify.","caption":"Encrypted backup goes to object storage; its checksum is kept elsewhere.","focus":["app","store","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"encrypted backup"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"async","label":"SHA-256 checksum"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"encrypted backup"},{"node":"db","text":"SHA-256 · 32 B"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Layers, not one lock","body":"TLS uses asymmetric crypto once to derive a symmetric session key, which protects data on each hop. At rest, envelope encryption keeps data keys beside the data and master keys in a KMS that checks and logs every use. Rotation re-wraps small keys instead of re-encrypting everything. Hashes verify, encryption conceals. Managing the keys, not choosing the algorithms, is the hard part.","idea":"Pick the right tool per state, and protect the keys more carefully than the data.","caption":"Every hop and every stored copy is covered.","focus":["browser","edge","app","kms","db","store"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"rtt":"1","kms":"3","keys":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"kms","text":"every use logged","tone":"green"},{"node":"db","text":"data key wrapped"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does TLS use asymmetric cryptography only during the handshake and not for the whole connection?","options":["Asymmetric keys are shorter and easier to store","Asymmetric operations are far slower, so they set up a symmetric key that does the bulk work","Symmetric encryption cannot detect tampering","Browsers do not support asymmetric encryption for data"],"answer":1,"why":"Asymmetric crypto solves key agreement between strangers but is orders of magnitude slower. TLS uses it once, then switches to a fast symmetric cipher."},{"q":"In envelope encryption, what is stored next to the encrypted record in the database?","options":["The plain data key","The KMS master key","The data key wrapped by the master key","A SHA-256 hash of the plain data key"],"answer":2,"why":"The wrapped data key is useless without the KMS, which holds the master key. Storing the plain key or master key beside the data would defeat the purpose."},{"q":"You rotate the master key in the KMS. What must happen to 5 TB of existing ciphertext to keep it readable?","options":["All 5 TB must be decrypted and re-encrypted immediately","Nothing; the KMS keeps old versions and can still unwrap old data keys","The browser must redo the TLS handshake","The data must be hashed with the new key"],"answer":1,"why":"Old master key versions remain available for unwrapping. Optionally re-wrap the small data keys in the background; the 5 TB of ciphertext is not touched."},{"q":"A service stores a partner API token that it must later send back to the partner. Which is correct?","options":["Hash it with SHA-256, since hashes are safer","Store it in plain text behind TLS","Encode it with Base64","Encrypt it, because it must be recovered in its original form"],"answer":3,"why":"A hash cannot be reversed, so the token could never be sent again. Encryption is reversible with the key. Base64 is only encoding and offers no secrecy."},{"q":"An attacker records TLS 1.3 traffic for a year, then steals the server's long-term private key. What can they read?","options":["Everything recorded, since the key decrypts all sessions","Only the first request of each session","Nothing from the recorded sessions, thanks to forward secrecy","Only sessions that used resumption"],"answer":2,"why":"Session keys come from ephemeral key exchange and are discarded. The long-term key only proves identity, so it cannot decrypt past traffic."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Symmetric (AES-GCM)","def":"One shared key locks and unlocks. Fast, used for all bulk data. GCM adds tamper detection."},{"term":"Asymmetric (RSA, ECDH)","def":"Public/private key pair. Slow; used for key agreement and signatures, not bulk data."},{"term":"TLS 1.3 handshake","def":"One round trip to verify the certificate and derive a session key. Ephemeral keys give forward secrecy."},{"term":"mTLS","def":"Mutual TLS: both client and server present certificates. Common for service-to-service traffic."},{"term":"Envelope encryption","def":"Data encrypted with a data key; data key wrapped by a master key held in a KMS. Wrapped key is stored with the data."},{"term":"KMS","def":"Key management service. Keeps master keys in hardened storage, never exports them, enforces access rules and logs every use."},{"term":"Key rotation","def":"Replace keys on a schedule or after exposure. With envelopes, re-wrap data keys rather than re-encrypt all data."},{"term":"Hash vs encryption","def":"Hash is one-way (verify, fingerprint, passwords with slow salted hashes). Encryption is reversible with a key."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"ddos-abuse","title":"DDoS & Abuse Protection","subtitle":"Watch a flood of fake traffic hit a site, then see each layer of defense peel it away while real users keep getting through.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How volumetric attacks (clog the pipe) differ from application-layer attacks (exhaust the app)","Why anycast spreads an attack across many sites and scrubbing removes the junk","What a WAF can and cannot stop, and why bot detection is a probability game","How per-client limits protect expensive endpoints and why the key you limit on matters","Why the origin must be hidden and why defense is layered"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"Real users","kind":"user","x":8,"y":22},{"id":"botnet","label":"Botnet","kind":"external","x":8,"y":78},{"id":"edge","label":"Anycast edge","kind":"cdn","x":30,"y":50},{"id":"scrub","label":"Scrubbing","kind":"service","x":30,"y":88},{"id":"waf","label":"WAF + bot check","kind":"gateway","x":54,"y":24},{"id":"limiter","label":"Rate limiter","kind":"gateway","x":54,"y":72},{"id":"app","label":"Origin app","kind":"server","x":77,"y":48},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":92,"y":82}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"edge"},{"from":"botnet","to":"edge"},{"from":"botnet","to":"app"},{"from":"edge","to":"scrub"},{"from":"edge","to":"waf"},{"from":"waf","to":"limiter"},{"from":"limiter","to":"app"},{"from":"edge","to":"app"},{"from":"app","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"attack","label":"Attack traffic","unit":"Gbps"},{"id":"origin","label":"Reaching origin","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"legit","label":"Real users OK","unit":"%"},{"id":"blocked","label":"Junk blocked","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"A quiet shop, then a flood","body":"A shop normally serves about 500 requests per second from real customers, on a 10 Gbps link to its origin (the real server). At 9 a.m. a botnet (200,000 hacked devices under one attacker's control) starts sending traffic at it. Some of the traffic tries to fill the network pipe. Some looks like ordinary page loads. How do you keep customers shopping while the attack runs?","idea":"An attack is not one problem: the pipe, the servers, and the business logic can each be the target.","caption":"Normal day: real users reach the origin and get 200 OK.","focus":["users","edge","app"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"GET /shop"},{"from":"edge","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"GET /shop"},{"from":"app","to":"edge","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"attack":"0","origin":"500","legit":"100","blocked":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"users","text":"500 req/s"},{"node":"app","text":"10 Gbps link"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Volumetric attack: clog the pipe","body":"A volumetric attack tries to use up bandwidth or packet capacity. It often uses spoofed UDP floods or reflection and amplification (a small query to an open DNS or NTP server returns a much bigger reply, aimed at the victim). Here the botnet pushes 400 Gbps straight at the origin's own IP address, which has only a 10 Gbps link. The servers are fine, but the road to them is full, so real users cannot get in.","idea":"Volumetric attacks win on raw size, so no single origin can absorb them alone.","caption":"400 Gbps of junk swamps a 10 Gbps link.","focus":["botnet","app"],"flows":[{"from":"botnet","to":"app","kind":"fail","label":"UDP flood"},{"from":"botnet","to":"app","kind":"fail","label":"reflected junk"}],"metrics":{"attack":"400","legit":"2","origin":"10"},"chips":[{"node":"botnet","text":"400 Gbps","tone":"red"},{"node":"app","text":"pipe full","tone":"red"},{"node":"app","text":"10 Gbps link","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Anycast: spread the blow","body":"With anycast, the same IP address is announced from dozens or hundreds of locations, and internet routing sends each sender to a nearby one. A worldwide botnet therefore lands on many edge sites (the provider's servers near users), each seeing only a slice: spread evenly over 100 sites, 400 Gbps is about 4 Gbps each. Edge networks have far more total capacity than any one customer, so they are not overwhelmed. But scrubbing has not run yet (next step), so junk still leaks through and only about 60% of real requests (300 of 500 req/s) reach the origin.","idea":"Anycast turns one big target into many small ones, so capacity adds up instead of piling onto a single link.","caption":"The attack splits across many edge sites, but some junk still reaches the origin.","focus":["botnet","users","edge","app"],"flows":[{"from":"botnet","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"~4 Gbps per site"},{"from":"users","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"GET /shop"},{"from":"edge","to":"app","kind":"fail","label":"junk still passes"}],"metrics":{"attack":"400","legit":"60","origin":"300"},"chips":[{"node":"edge","text":"~4 Gbps per site"},{"node":"users","text":"60% get through","tone":"red"},{"node":"app","text":"300 req/s","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Scrubbing: drop the junk early","body":"Scrubbing drops packets that cannot be legitimate: spoofed sources, malformed packets, floods on ports you do not use, and known attack patterns. Heavily hit traffic can be sent to a dedicated scrubbing center that forwards only clean traffic. SYN cookies let the edge survive a SYN flood (fake connection openings) without keeping state for each one. Dropping a packet this early costs almost nothing. Now about 99% of junk is gone, 95% of real users get through, and the origin sees about 500 req/s again (roughly 475 real plus a little leftover junk).","idea":"Filter as far from the origin as possible, because the cost of a dropped packet rises the deeper it travels.","caption":"Scrubbing removes most of the flood.","focus":["edge","scrub"],"flows":[{"from":"edge","to":"scrub","kind":"request","label":"suspect traffic"},{"from":"scrub","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"clean (forwarded)"}],"metrics":{"attack":"400","origin":"500","legit":"95","blocked":"99"},"chips":[{"node":"scrub","text":"99% junk dropped","tone":"green"},{"node":"edge","text":"SYN cookies"},{"node":"app","text":"500 req/s","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"The quiet attack that remains","body":"The flood is still being scrubbed, but the attacker adds a second attack. About 2,000 of the bots send perfectly valid HTTPS requests to the search page and login form, roughly 4 per second each, so 8,000 per second in total. Each looks like a normal browser. This is an application-layer (layer 7) attack. It is only a few tens of Mbps, tiny next to the flood, but search runs an expensive database query, so the origin buckles. Packet filters cannot help because every packet is legitimate. The Junk blocked tile now counts these requests, and nothing filters them yet, so it falls to 0%.","idea":"Layer 7 attacks are cheap for the attacker and expensive for you, so volume is the wrong thing to measure.","caption":"Valid-looking requests reach the origin and overload the database.","focus":["botnet","edge","app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"botnet","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"GET /search?q=..."},{"from":"edge","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"GET /search?q=..."},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"slow query x8000"}],"metrics":{"attack":"400","origin":"8500","legit":"30","blocked":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"botnet","text":"8,000 req/s","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"8,000 slow queries","tone":"red"},{"node":"app","text":"8,500 req/s","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"WAF and bot detection","body":"A web application firewall (WAF) reads HTTP and applies rules: it blocks known exploit patterns such as SQL injection strings, odd methods and bad headers. Those rules do nothing against a valid search request, so bot detection is also needed. Both work because the edge ends the TLS connection and can read the HTTP. Bot detection scores each client using IP reputation, TLS fingerprint, header order, and whether it runs JavaScript and acts like a person. Humans pass, clear bots are blocked, and uncertain ones get a challenge (JavaScript check, proof-of-work, or CAPTCHA). Scoring is statistical: some bots slip through and some humans get challenged.","idea":"A WAF recognizes known-bad patterns; bot detection estimates who is probably automated. Both are probabilistic.","caption":"The edge inspects HTTP, blocks bots, and challenges suspects.","focus":["edge","waf","limiter"],"flows":[{"from":"edge","to":"waf","kind":"request","label":"score each request"},{"from":"waf","to":"edge","kind":"fail","label":"block / challenge"},{"from":"waf","to":"limiter","kind":"request","label":"likely human"}],"metrics":{"origin":"2050","legit":"90","blocked":"80"},"chips":[{"node":"waf","text":"bot score"},{"node":"waf","text":"80% blocked","tone":"green"},{"node":"app","text":"2,050 req/s","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Per-client limits on costly routes","body":"Some bots look human enough to pass, so a rate limiter caps what any one client can do, with tighter limits on expensive routes: for example 20 searches per minute per client and 5 login attempts per minute per account. Choose the key carefully. One IP is weak because a botnet has thousands and many real users share an office or mobile-carrier address, so combine IP, session or API key, and account. Over-limit requests get HTTP 429 with a Retry-After header. Per-account limits can be abused to lock out the real owner, so prefer slowing or challenging over a hard lock.","idea":"Limit by who is asking and what it costs you; the key you choose decides who gets caught.","caption":"The limiter caps each client, sparing the origin.","focus":["waf","limiter","app"],"flows":[{"from":"waf","to":"limiter","kind":"request","label":"passed checks"},{"from":"limiter","to":"waf","kind":"fail","label":"429 too many"},{"from":"limiter","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"within quota"},{"from":"app","to":"limiter","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"origin":"620","legit":"98","blocked":"98"},"chips":[{"node":"limiter","text":"20 searches/min"},{"node":"limiter","text":"429 + Retry-After"},{"node":"app","text":"620 req/s","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"False positives and a hidden origin","body":"Every rule can hurt real users: a few real customers get challenged. A blocked shopper during a sale costs money, so new rules often start in log-only mode, and a challenge is gentler than a block. Also protect the back door. If the attacker learns the real origin IP, they can skip the edge entirely. So accept traffic at the origin only from the edge, using firewall allow-lists plus an authenticated origin pull (mutual TLS or a secret header) or a tunnel. Set autoscaling caps and spend alerts so an attack cannot run up a huge bill.","idea":"Defense is a dial between blocking attackers and annoying customers, and it fails if the origin is reachable directly.","caption":"Origin only accepts traffic from the edge.","focus":["botnet","edge","waf","app"],"flows":[{"from":"botnet","to":"app","kind":"fail","label":"direct to origin"},{"from":"edge","to":"waf","kind":"request","label":"allowed path only"}],"metrics":{"origin":"615","legit":"97","blocked":"98"},"chips":[{"node":"botnet","text":"direct to origin","tone":"red"},{"node":"edge","text":"edge-only origin","tone":"green"},{"node":"app","text":"615 req/s","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Layers, each cheaper than the last","body":"Anycast spreads volume across many sites, scrubbing drops junk packets at the edge, the WAF and bot scoring filter bad HTTP, and per-client limits cap what remains on costly routes. Each layer is cheaper to run than the one behind it. After tuning, the origin sees about 570 req/s: roughly 490 real requests plus about 80 junk. Both attacks are still running. No layer is perfect, so combine them, watch false positives, and keep the origin hidden.","idea":"Stop abuse as early and cheaply as possible, and assume each layer leaks a little.","caption":"Origin back near 570 req/s, users served.","focus":["users","botnet","edge","scrub","waf","limiter","app","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"attack":"400","origin":"570","legit":"98","blocked":"99"},"chips":[{"node":"app","text":"570 req/s","tone":"green"},{"node":"users","text":"98% real users OK","tone":"green"},{"node":"edge","text":"99% junk blocked"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A flood of 300 Gbps of spoofed UDP packets is aimed at a site with a 10 Gbps link. Which defense addresses the real problem?","options":["Anycast capacity plus packet scrubbing","A WAF rule that blocks SQL injection strings","More app servers behind the load balancer","A per-user rate limit of 60 requests per minute"],"answer":0,"why":"The link itself is full, so extra servers or HTTP rules never see the traffic. Only far larger distributed capacity and packet filtering upstream of the origin helps."},{"q":"An attacker sends only 200 valid-looking requests per second to /search, yet the database falls over. Why are packet-level scrubbers unlikely to help?","options":["Scrubbers only inspect UDP, so HTTPS requests pass through untouched","Scrubbers only act after the request has reached the database","Every packet is valid; the harm is the work each request triggers","HTTPS is encrypted, so no defense can ever inspect it at any layer"],"answer":2,"why":"Layer 7 attacks abuse expensive application work with well-formed requests. They need HTTP-aware defenses: WAF rules, bot scoring, and per-client limits on costly routes."},{"q":"You limit login to 5 attempts per minute per source IP. A botnet of 50,000 devices tries one password each against one account. What weakness does this show?","options":["The limit of 5 per minute is too generous for any single IP address","IP-only limits are evaded by distributed attackers; key on account too","Login endpoints cannot be rate limited without breaking real users","429 responses leak which account names exist to the attacker's tools"],"answer":1,"why":"Each device makes one attempt, so none trips an IP limit. A limit keyed on the target account, plus bot detection, catches distributed attacks."},{"q":"Your edge protection is solid, yet the origin still goes down under attack. The attacker found the origin's real IP from an old DNS record. What fix matters most?","options":["Accept origin traffic only from the edge; replace the leaked IP","Make the WAF rules much stricter so that more requests are blocked","Lower the DNS TTL so that cached copies of the record expire much sooner","Add a second database replica so reads are shared during an attack"],"answer":0,"why":"Traffic sent straight to the origin skips every edge layer. Restricting the origin to edge-only traffic closes that bypass; a new IP replaces the leaked one."},{"q":"A new bot rule blocks 99% of attack traffic in testing but also challenges 3% of real checkout users. What is a sensible rollout?","options":["Enable full blocking immediately; attacks are worse than lost sales","Discard it, since any false positive at all is unacceptable for checkout","Apply it only to traffic from the countries attackers usually come from","Log-only first, tune, then soft challenge before any hard block"],"answer":3,"why":"Bot scoring is probabilistic. Observing first and escalating from log to challenge to block limits harm to real customers while you tune thresholds."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Volumetric attack","def":"Tries to exhaust bandwidth or packet capacity (UDP floods, reflection and amplification). Needs huge distributed capacity to absorb."},{"term":"Application-layer (L7) attack","def":"Valid-looking HTTP requests aimed at expensive endpoints like search or login. Low volume, high cost per request."},{"term":"Anycast","def":"One IP announced from many sites; routing sends each sender to the nearest. Splits attack traffic and cuts latency for users."},{"term":"Scrubbing","def":"Filtering junk packets (spoofed, malformed, wrong ports) upstream of the origin, often at the edge or a dedicated center."},{"term":"WAF","def":"Firewall that understands HTTP and blocks known-bad patterns such as injection strings. Weak against valid-looking abuse on its own."},{"term":"Bot detection","def":"Scores clients using IP reputation, TLS and header fingerprints, and behavior; allows, challenges, or blocks. Probabilistic, so expect errors."},{"term":"Per-client rate limit","def":"Caps requests per key (IP, session, API key, account), tighter on costly routes. Return 429 with Retry-After."},{"term":"Hide the origin","def":"Accept traffic only from the edge network, authenticated (mutual TLS, secret header, or tunnel); a leaked origin IP lets attackers bypass every upstream layer."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"secrets-management","title":"Secrets Management","subtitle":"Watch a database password go from a leaked file to a short-lived credential that a vault hands out and then revokes.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why secrets in source code, images, and env files keep leaking","How a vault issues and audits secrets instead of storing copies everywhere","How short-lived, dynamic credentials shrink the damage of a leak","How rotation works without downtime, using overlapping validity","How workload identity removes the first secret needed to reach the vault"],"nodes":[{"id":"repo","label":"Git repo / image","kind":"storage","x":10,"y":22},{"id":"attacker","label":"Attacker","kind":"external","x":10,"y":86},{"id":"app","label":"Payments service","kind":"service","x":37,"y":55},{"id":"vault","label":"Secrets vault","kind":"service","x":64,"y":22},{"id":"idp","label":"Workload identity","kind":"gateway","x":64,"y":88},{"id":"db","label":"Orders database","kind":"db","x":89,"y":55}],"edges":[{"from":"repo","to":"app"},{"from":"attacker","to":"repo"},{"from":"attacker","to":"db"},{"from":"app","to":"db"},{"from":"app","to":"idp"},{"from":"app","to":"vault"},{"from":"vault","to":"db"},{"from":"idp","to":"vault"}],"metrics":[{"id":"life","label":"Secret lifetime","unit":"min"},{"id":"window","label":"Attacker window","unit":"min"},{"id":"copies","label":"Copies of secret","unit":""}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Where does the password live?","body":"The payments service must log in to the orders database, so it needs a credential. That credential is a secret: a value that grants access to whoever knows it. Whatever holds the secret becomes as sensitive as the database itself. So where should it live, so that the service can read it, nobody else can, and a leak is survivable?","idea":"A secret is only as safe as the least-protected place a copy of it sits.","caption":"The service needs a credential to reach the database.","focus":["app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"login: ???"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[]},{"tag":"PROBLEM","title":"Secrets in code get cloned","body":"The easy answer is to commit the password to the repo or bake it into the container image. Now every developer laptop, CI runner, fork, and image registry holds a copy, and git history keeps it even after you delete the line. Bots scan public repos for keys within minutes, and a private repo leak is only a stolen laptop away. The password is rarely changed, so it stays valid for a year or more (525,600 minutes). The tiles show 40 copies.","idea":"Code and images are copied freely by design, so a secret inside them is copied freely too.","caption":"The secret rides along with every copy of the repo.","focus":["repo","app","attacker"],"flows":[{"from":"repo","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"build + password"},{"from":"attacker","to":"repo","kind":"fail","label":"clones history"}],"metrics":{"life":"525600","window":"525600","copies":"40"},"chips":[{"node":"repo","text":"40 copies","tone":"red"},{"node":"attacker","text":"clones history","tone":"red"},{"node":"app","text":"valid ~1 year","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"PROBLEM","title":"Env files only move the problem","body":"Env vars and .env files keep the secret out of git, which is a real improvement. But the file still gets pasted into chat, copied to each server, printed by crash dumps and debug endpoints, and inherited by every child process. It is still one long-lived static value, with no record of who read it, and changing it means editing every place it was copied. The tiles show 12 copies, down from 40 but far from safe.","idea":"Env vars are a delivery mechanism, not a store: no access control, no audit, no expiry.","caption":"Still a static password, now scattered across hosts.","focus":["app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"env DB_PASS"}],"metrics":{"life":"525600","window":"525600","copies":"12"},"chips":[{"node":"app","text":"12 copies","tone":"red"},{"node":"app","text":"static password","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Workload identity: prove who you are","body":"A vault would hold the secret in one place, but then the service must prove who it is to the vault, which needs a first secret (the bootstrap problem). Workload identity solves this. The platform (Kubernetes, a cloud provider, SPIFFE) already knows which workload is running and vouches for it by issuing a signed token. The token lasts about ten minutes and no password is stored anywhere.","idea":"Let the platform attest who the workload is, so no bootstrap secret has to be shipped with it.","caption":"The platform issues a short-lived identity token.","focus":["app","idp","vault"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"idp","kind":"request","label":"I am payments/prod"},{"from":"idp","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"signed token 10m"}],"metrics":{"life":"10","copies":"0","window":"10"},"chips":[{"node":"idp","text":"signed token"},{"node":"app","text":"10 min token","tone":"green"},{"node":"app","text":"no stored password","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"The vault checks the token, then lends","body":"The service presents its token to the vault. The vault verifies the signature against the identity provider, then looks up a policy (payments may read only the orders-db path). Every read is written to an audit log: who, what, when. Secrets are stored encrypted, so the vault is the one place to protect, monitor, and rate-limit. The vault now holds the single copy of the secret.","idea":"A vault centralizes storage, policy, and audit, so access is a decision rather than a copy.","caption":"Identity is verified, and policy allows one path.","focus":["app","idp","vault"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"vault","kind":"request","label":"token + orders-db"},{"from":"vault","to":"idp","kind":"request","label":"verify signature"},{"from":"idp","to":"vault","kind":"response","label":"token valid"}],"metrics":{"copies":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"vault","text":"orders-db only"},{"node":"vault","text":"audit log","tone":"green"},{"node":"idp","text":"signature valid","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Dynamic credentials with a TTL","body":"Better still, the vault does not hand out a stored password. It connects to the database, creates a brand-new user with only the grants needed, and returns it with a one-hour lease (a time limit). Each service instance gets its own unique login, so logs show exactly which instance did what. Nothing static exists to steal from a repo. The tiles now show a 60-minute lifetime.","idea":"Short-lived, per-instance credentials turn a permanent secret into a disposable one.","caption":"The vault creates a one-hour database user on demand.","focus":["app","vault","db"],"flows":[{"from":"vault","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"CREATE USER, 1h"},{"from":"vault","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"user + pass, lease"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"login v-pay-8f2"}],"metrics":{"life":"60","window":"60","copies":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"vault","text":"lease 1 h","tone":"green"},{"node":"db","text":"user per instance"},{"node":"app","text":"v-pay-8f2","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Rotation without downtime","body":"Some systems cannot create users on demand, such as a third-party API key or a legacy database, so you rotate the secret on a schedule, often every 30 to 90 days (the tiles show 30 days). The safe pattern is overlap: create the new secret, keep the old one valid for a grace period, let services pick up the new one, then retire the old. Flipping the switch at one instant causes an outage.","idea":"Rotate by overlapping old and new, so readers can switch over before the old value dies.","caption":"Vault rotates the credential while both stay valid.","focus":["vault","db","app"],"flows":[{"from":"vault","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"add new password"},{"from":"app","to":"vault","kind":"request","label":"renew / re-read"},{"from":"vault","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"new version"}],"metrics":{"life":"43200","copies":"1","window":"43200"},"chips":[{"node":"vault","text":"rotate every 30-90 d"},{"node":"vault","text":"old + new overlap","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"app","text":"new version"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A leaked log line","body":"Suppose a debug log accidentally prints the live database credential and an attacker finds it. With a static password they would own the database indefinitely. Here they can use it only until the lease ends, at most an hour. Network rules and a database user limited to a few tables narrow the damage further.","idea":"Lifetime and scope bound the damage; you cannot always prevent a leak, but you can cap it.","caption":"Stolen credential works, but only inside its TTL.","focus":["attacker","db"],"flows":[{"from":"attacker","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"uses stolen cred"},{"from":"db","to":"attacker","kind":"response","label":"login OK (<1h)"}],"metrics":{"window":"60","life":"60"},"chips":[{"node":"attacker","text":"cred leaked","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"login OK (< 1 h)","tone":"red"},{"node":"attacker","text":"window <= 60 min","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Revoke on detection","body":"Alerting spots a login from an unfamiliar address. An operator revokes the lease in the vault, which drops that database user within seconds. With a static shared password you would have to rotate it and redeploy every consumer at once. Revoking by lease ID is cheap because every credential was issued individually. Assume it takes about five minutes to notice and decide, and the revoke itself takes seconds, so the attacker window is 5 minutes.","idea":"Per-lease issuance makes revocation surgical instead of a company-wide fire drill.","caption":"The vault drops the stolen user; attacker is locked out.","focus":["vault","db","attacker"],"flows":[{"from":"vault","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"DROP USER (revoke)"},{"from":"attacker","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"login denied"}],"metrics":{"window":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"vault","text":"lease revoked","tone":"green"},{"node":"attacker","text":"login denied","tone":"green"},{"node":"attacker","text":"window 5 min","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Few copies, short lives","body":"Keep secrets out of code, images, and env files. Let the platform prove who the workload is, let a vault decide and log who may read what, and prefer dynamic credentials that expire on their own. Where a secret must stay static, rotate it with an overlap. The aim is not a perfect secret but a leak that costs minutes, not months.","idea":"Identity instead of bootstrap secrets, vault instead of copies, expiry instead of hope.","caption":"Identity, vault, short leases, fast revoke.","focus":["app","vault","idp","db","attacker"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"life":"60","window":"5","copies":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"app","text":"1 copy","tone":"green"},{"node":"vault","text":"1 h lease"},{"node":"attacker","text":"window 5 min","at":"bottom"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A developer deletes a committed database password in the next commit and pushes. Is the secret safe?","options":["Yes, the file no longer contains it","Yes, if the repo is private","No, it remains in git history and any clone, so it must be rotated","Only if the commit is squashed in CI"],"answer":2,"why":"Git keeps history and clones copy it. Once a secret has been committed, treat it as exposed and rotate it; removing the line does not."},{"q":"Why is workload identity better than giving each service a vault token stored in its config?","options":["Tokens in config are slower to validate","The config token is itself a long-lived secret that must be delivered and protected","Vaults cannot validate config tokens","Workload identity encrypts the database traffic"],"answer":1,"why":"A stored vault token just recreates the original problem one level up. Platform-attested identity avoids shipping any bootstrap secret."},{"q":"An attacker steals a dynamic database credential with a 1-hour lease from a log file. Which statement is most accurate?","options":["They have access forever, same as a static password","They have access until the lease expires or is revoked, limited to that user's grants","The credential is useless the moment it is logged","They can ask the vault to renew it"],"answer":1,"why":"A bounded TTL and narrow grants cap the damage, and revoking the lease ends access early. Renewal requires the service's own identity, which the attacker lacks."},{"q":"You must rotate an API key used by 30 running instances with zero downtime. What is the safest approach?","options":["Replace the key everywhere at the same instant","Stop the service, change the key, restart","Create the new key, keep the old valid briefly, let instances switch, then retire the old key","Rotate only when an incident occurs"],"answer":2,"why":"Overlapping validity lets instances pick up the new value at different times without failed requests; an instant flip guarantees some callers hold a dead key."},{"q":"Which is the strongest argument for a vault over an encrypted .env file kept in the repo?","options":["Vaults use a stronger cipher","It avoids needing any authentication","It makes secrets smaller","It adds per-read policy, audit logs, expiry, and revocation"],"answer":3,"why":"The encrypted file still needs a decryption key, offers no per-reader audit, and cannot expire or revoke. Control and visibility, not cipher strength, are the vault's value."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Secret","def":"Any value that grants access if known: passwords, API keys, private keys, tokens, connection strings."},{"term":"Secrets vault","def":"A service that stores secrets encrypted, enforces per-identity policy, and logs every read. Examples: HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager."},{"term":"Dynamic secret","def":"A credential minted on demand for one client with a TTL, then deleted automatically. No stored password to leak."},{"term":"Lease / TTL","def":"The time a credential is valid. Short leases (minutes to hours) limit leak damage; leases can be renewed or revoked."},{"term":"Rotation","def":"Replacing a secret on a schedule or after exposure. Use overlap, with old and new valid together, to avoid downtime."},{"term":"Workload identity","def":"A platform-issued, signed identity for a running workload (Kubernetes service account token, cloud IAM role, SPIFFE ID). Removes the bootstrap secret."},{"term":"Blast radius","def":"What an attacker can reach with one stolen secret. Shrink it with least-privilege grants, per-service credentials, and expiry."},{"term":"Secret scanning","def":"Automated checks in pre-commit hooks and CI that block commits containing keys. A safety net, not a replacement for a vault."}],"isNew":true}]},{"id":"shipping","title":"Shipping & Operations","lessons":[{"slug":"testing","title":"Testing Distributed Systems","subtitle":"Watch one Orders service get tested with five kinds of test, from unit tests to load tests, and see what each layer catches.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why a test pyramid keeps most tests fast and few tests broad","How contract tests catch API drift between teams without a shared staging environment","Why integration tests should use a real database, not a mock","How to inject latency and failures to prove timeouts and fallbacks work","How a load test finds limits such as connection pool exhaustion"],"nodes":[{"id":"load","label":"Load generator","kind":"worker","x":10,"y":16},{"id":"runner","label":"Test runner","kind":"client","x":10,"y":52},{"id":"svc","label":"Orders service","kind":"service","x":36,"y":52},{"id":"broker","label":"Contract broker","kind":"storage","x":36,"y":88},{"id":"proxy","label":"Fault proxy","kind":"gateway","x":62,"y":52},{"id":"db","label":"Postgres","kind":"db","x":88,"y":22},{"id":"pay","label":"Payments (provider)","kind":"external","x":88,"y":80}],"edges":[{"from":"runner","to":"svc"},{"from":"load","to":"svc"},{"from":"svc","to":"db"},{"from":"svc","to":"proxy"},{"from":"proxy","to":"pay"},{"from":"runner","to":"broker"},{"from":"broker","to":"pay"}],"metrics":[{"id":"tests","label":"Tests run","unit":""},{"id":"suite","label":"Suite time","unit":"s"},{"id":"p99","label":"p99 latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"errors","label":"Error rate","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Green tests, broken checkout","body":"Imagine the Orders service has hundreds of passing unit tests. Yet after a release, checkout breaks because the Payments team renamed a JSON field. Later, one slow Payments reply freezes the whole service. Unit tests could not see either problem, because they fake everything outside the code. Real failures happen in the gaps between components, so we need tests for those gaps too.","idea":"Passing unit tests prove your code matches your assumptions, not that your assumptions are true.","caption":"Orders talks to a database and to Payments. Those links are where bugs hide.","focus":["svc","db","pay"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[]},{"tag":"PYRAMID","title":"Many fast unit tests","body":"The bottom layer is unit tests: tiny checks of pure logic, like adding up a price or moving an order from new to paid. They run in memory with no network. The runner sends 1,200 of them to Orders. At about 5 ms each that is only a few seconds, plus about 30 s to build and start up, so roughly 40 seconds in total. They are cheap, so run them on every commit. Above them sit fewer, slower layers, and at the very top a handful of end-to-end tests that drive the whole system through its UI or API. Those are realistic but slow and flaky (they sometimes fail for no code reason). This shape is the test pyramid, a rule of thumb: the higher the layer, the slower and costlier, so keep fewer.","idea":"Put most tests where feedback is fastest and failures are easiest to diagnose.","caption":"The runner sends 1,200 in-memory unit tests to Orders; all pass in about 40 s.","focus":["runner","svc"],"flows":[{"from":"runner","to":"svc","kind":"request","label":"1,200 unit tests"},{"from":"svc","to":"runner","kind":"response","label":"pass"}],"metrics":{"tests":"1200","suite":"40"},"chips":[{"node":"runner","text":"1,200 unit tests"},{"node":"runner","text":"≈ 40 s","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"svc","text":"5 ms each"}]},{"tag":"CONTRACT","title":"Contract tests for the API gap","body":"Orders is the consumer of the Payments API (it calls it). Payments is the provider (it serves it). Orders' tests write down what it relies on, such as 'POST /charges replies with amount and status', and publish that contract to a broker, a shared noticeboard for contracts. Payments' own build then downloads the contracts and checks its real code against them. If Payments renames status, its build fails before release, so the team that caused the break sees it first. Say 40 contract tests add about 15 seconds. No shared staging environment is needed.","idea":"A contract test catches breaking API changes at the provider's build, where they are cheapest to fix.","caption":"Orders publishes its contract; Payments fetches it, verifies, and reports back.","focus":["runner","broker","pay"],"flows":[{"from":"runner","to":"broker","kind":"async","label":"publish contract"},{"from":"pay","to":"broker","kind":"request","label":"fetch contracts"},{"from":"broker","to":"pay","kind":"response","label":"consumer contracts"},{"from":"pay","to":"broker","kind":"async","label":"publish result"}],"metrics":{"tests":"1240","suite":"55"},"chips":[{"node":"broker","text":"40 contracts"},{"node":"runner","text":"+15 s","at":"bottom"},{"node":"pay","text":"verifies real code"}]},{"tag":"INTEGRATION","title":"Use the real database","body":"Integration tests use real dependencies instead of fakes. The runner starts a throwaway Postgres in a container (tools like Testcontainers do this), applies the real migrations (the scripts that create the tables), and runs 80 tests that send real SQL. A fake database would happily accept a query that breaks a unique rule, misspells a column, or needs a migration you forgot to write. A real one rejects it. In the animation, Orders inserts an order and the database answers. These tests take about three minutes, so run them on every pull request, not every keystroke.","idea":"Stub remote services you do not control; run the infrastructure you own, such as your database, for real.","caption":"Orders runs real SQL against a Postgres container; the database enforces its rules.","focus":["svc","db"],"flows":[{"from":"svc","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT order"},{"from":"db","to":"svc","kind":"response","label":"row + constraints"}],"metrics":{"tests":"1320","suite":"235"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"real Postgres","tone":"green"},{"node":"svc","text":"80 tests"},{"node":"db","text":"≈ 3 min","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FAULT","title":"Inject latency and failures","body":"Here Payments is a stub (a fake that returns canned replies) behind a fault proxy, a middleman that can delay, drop or break calls on command. The contract test from step 3 keeps the stub honest. The test makes Payments take 5 seconds. Orders must give up after its own 2 s timeout and reply 'payment pending, we will confirm shortly' instead of hanging. A second test retries with the same idempotency key (an ID that stops duplicate charges) and checks the customer is charged once. Untested timeout code is probably wrong. Say 15 fault tests add about 30 seconds. Other faults to try: connection reset, HTTP 503, duplicate delivery.","idea":"Failure-handling code only works if you have watched it run against a failure.","caption":"The proxy stalls Payments for 5 s; Orders must stop waiting at 2 s.","focus":["svc","proxy","pay"],"flows":[{"from":"svc","to":"proxy","kind":"request","label":"POST /charges"},{"from":"proxy","to":"pay","kind":"request","label":"+5 s delay"},{"from":"svc","to":"proxy","kind":"fail","label":"gave up at 2 s"}],"metrics":{"tests":"1335","suite":"265"},"chips":[{"node":"proxy","text":"+5 s delay","tone":"red"},{"node":"svc","text":"gives up at 2 s"},{"node":"svc","text":"charged once","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"LOAD","title":"Load test the baseline","body":"A load test shows how the system behaves when many users arrive at once. The load generator replays realistic traffic, say 500 requests per second for 10 minutes, against a production-like copy of the system. It reports latency percentiles, not averages. p99 means 99 percent of requests were faster, and the slowest 1 percent is what users complain about. The healthy result here is p99 near 180 ms with errors below 0.1 percent. Save it as the baseline to compare against after every release.","idea":"Load tests produce a baseline; their value is comparing against it later.","caption":"500 req/s flow through Orders and Postgres; p99 is 180 ms and errors are tiny.","focus":["load","svc","db"],"flows":[{"from":"load","to":"svc","kind":"request","label":"500 req/s"},{"from":"svc","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"queries"},{"from":"svc","to":"load","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"p99":"180","errors":"0.05"},"chips":[{"node":"load","text":"500 req/s"},{"node":"svc","text":"p99 180 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"svc","text":"errors < 0.1%","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Push past the limit","body":"Now raise the rate to 1,500 requests per second. Assume each request holds a database connection for about 20 ms and the service keeps a pool of 20 (a fixed set of reusable connections). Each connection serves about 50 requests per second, so the pool handles about 1,000 req/s. The extra third waits in line until the 2.5 s client timeout. So p99 reaches about 2.5 seconds and about 33 percent of requests fail. No unit test shows this, because the limit comes from many requests competing at once. Fixes: faster queries, a pooler such as PgBouncer, a cache, or shedding load early. A bigger pool alone often just moves the queue to the database.","idea":"Load tests find the knee in the curve where queues build and latency explodes.","caption":"At 1,500 req/s the pool runs out: p99 is about 2.5 s and a third of requests fail.","focus":["load","svc","db"],"flows":[{"from":"load","to":"svc","kind":"request","label":"1,500 req/s"},{"from":"svc","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"pool exhausted"}],"metrics":{"p99":"2500","errors":"33"},"chips":[{"node":"load","text":"1,500 req/s"},{"node":"svc","text":"pool exhausted","tone":"red"},{"node":"svc","text":"p99 2.5 s","tone":"red","at":"right"},{"node":"svc","text":"33% fail","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Cost and flakiness","body":"Broad tests are expensive and can fail for reasons unrelated to your change, such as a slow CI machine, a shared environment, or a timing race. A suite that fails randomly teaches people to rerun it and ignore red results. So keep the upper layers few and give each a clear purpose. Make tests deterministic (same result every run) with fixed random seeds and no real sleeps, and quarantine flaky tests quickly. Production monitoring and gradual rollouts, covered in other lessons, catch what testing cannot.","idea":"A test suite is only useful if people trust a red result.","caption":"Each layer adds coverage and also cost; flaky tests wear down trust.","focus":["runner","svc","db","pay"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"tests":"1335","suite":"265"},"chips":[{"node":"runner","text":"flaky: quarantine","tone":"red"},{"node":"runner","text":"fixed seeds","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Which layer catches what","body":"Five kinds of test, each aimed at a different risk. Unit tests guard logic. Contract tests guard API agreements between teams. Integration tests with real dependencies guard SQL and wiring. Fault injection guards timeouts and fallbacks. Load tests guard capacity. Keep many cheap tests at the bottom and a few expensive ones on top. Each layer answers a different question, so skipping one leaves that question unanswered.","idea":"Match each risk to the cheapest test that can reveal it.","caption":"Five kinds of test, five different risks covered.","focus":["load","runner","svc","broker","proxy","db","pay"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"tests":"1335","suite":"265","p99":"180","errors":"0.05"},"chips":[{"node":"runner","text":"1,335 tests · 265 s"},{"node":"load","text":"p99 180 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"load","text":"errors 0.05%","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"The Payments team renames a response field. Which test would fail in the Payments build before release?","options":["A unit test inside Orders","A load test against Orders","A provider verification of Orders' published contract","A snapshot of the Orders database"],"answer":2,"why":"The broker holds what Orders relies on. Payments verifies its real code against that contract, so the rename fails their build."},{"q":"Why run integration tests against a real Postgres container instead of a mocked repository?","options":["A real database enforces constraints, types and SQL syntax that a mock silently accepts","Mocks cannot be written for databases","Containers make tests run faster than unit tests","It removes the need for unit tests"],"answer":0,"why":"A mock returns whatever you told it to, so bugs in the SQL or schema stay hidden. A real database rejects them."},{"q":"A load test reports an average latency of 90 ms, yet users complain about slow checkouts. What should you check?","options":["Only the maximum single request","The number of unit tests","Whether the load generator is a container","p95 and p99 percentiles, which show the slow tail"],"answer":3,"why":"Averages hide the slow tail. A few percent of very slow requests barely move the mean but affect many real users."},{"q":"A fault-injection test makes a dependency take 5 s, and Orders also hangs for 5 s. What does this reveal?","options":["The dependency is healthy","The timeout is missing or misconfigured","The test pyramid is inverted","The contract is out of date"],"answer":1,"why":"A correct client would stop waiting at its own timeout. Hanging for the full delay shows that protection is not working."},{"q":"Your suite has few unit tests and thousands of browser-driven end-to-end tests, and it fails randomly. What is the best direction?","options":["Add more end-to-end tests","Rerun failures until they pass","Move checks down to unit, contract and integration tests, and keep few end-to-end ones","Delete the integration tests"],"answer":2,"why":"Lower layers are faster and more deterministic. Shifting coverage down gives quicker, more trustworthy feedback."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Test pyramid","def":"Rule of thumb: many fast unit tests, fewer integration tests, very few end-to-end tests. Cost and flakiness rise with each layer."},{"term":"Contract test","def":"Checks that a consumer's expectations of an API match what the provider actually returns, verified in the provider's build."},{"term":"Testcontainers pattern","def":"Start real dependencies such as Postgres or Kafka in disposable containers for each test run, then throw them away."},{"term":"Test double","def":"A stand-in (stub, mock, fake) for a dependency. Use for remote services you do not control or that are slow; run infrastructure you own, such as your database, for real."},{"term":"Load vs stress test","def":"Load test: expected traffic, to confirm the baseline. Stress test: push beyond it to find the breaking point."},{"term":"Percentile latency","def":"p99 means 99 percent of requests were faster. Report percentiles, not averages, because users feel the tail."},{"term":"Fault injection","def":"Deliberately add delay, errors, drops or duplicates to a dependency to prove timeouts, retries and fallbacks behave."},{"term":"Flaky test","def":"A test that passes and fails without code changes. Fix or quarantine it fast; it erodes trust in the whole suite."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"cicd","title":"CI/CD Pipelines","subtitle":"Follow one small commit through build, test, artifact and deploy stages, then watch a bad release get rolled back.","minutes":11,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["What each pipeline stage (build, test, artifact, deploy) is for and why the order matters","Why trunk-based development pairs with small, frequent merges","Why you build one artifact once and promote that same artifact to every environment","How pipeline-as-code makes delivery reviewable and repeatable","How rollback works and why it should be the fastest thing your pipeline does"],"nodes":[{"id":"dev","label":"Developer","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"repo","label":"Git trunk (main)","kind":"service","x":25,"y":50},{"id":"ci","label":"CI runner","kind":"worker","x":42,"y":22},{"id":"registry","label":"Artifact registry","kind":"storage","x":62,"y":22},{"id":"staging","label":"Staging","kind":"service","x":62,"y":78},{"id":"prod","label":"Production","kind":"server","x":88,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"dev","to":"repo","label":"push"},{"from":"repo","to":"ci","label":"webhook"},{"from":"ci","to":"registry","label":"publish"},{"from":"registry","to":"staging","label":"deploy"},{"from":"registry","to":"prod","label":"deploy"},{"from":"staging","to":"prod","label":"promote"}],"metrics":[{"id":"pipeline","label":"Pipeline time","unit":"min"},{"id":"tests","label":"Tests passed","unit":"count"},{"id":"errors","label":"Prod errors","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"How does code reach users?","body":"A developer has just finished a 40-line change. How does it reach real users? Old-style teams saved up changes for weeks, handed a big release to an ops team, and hoped. A CI/CD pipeline replaces hope with a fixed, automated path from commit to production. CI (continuous integration) means merging small changes often and automatically checking every merge. CD (continuous delivery) means every change that passes is ready to ship.","idea":"A pipeline turns 'ship it' from a risky event into a boring, repeatable routine.","caption":"One small change is ready to leave the developer's laptop.","focus":["dev"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"pipeline":"0","tests":"0","errors":"0.2"},"chips":[{"node":"dev","text":"40-line change"}]},{"tag":"TRUNK","title":"Merge small, merge often","body":"Trunk-based development means everyone merges into one shared main branch (the trunk) at least once a day. Branches live for hours, not weeks, so they rarely drift apart and merge conflicts stay tiny. Half-finished work hides behind a feature flag (an on/off switch in the code) instead of a long-lived branch. In the animation the developer pushes to main, and the repo sends a webhook to CI: an automatic HTTP call that says 'something changed, start the pipeline'. Many teams also run a quick set of checks on the pull request first.","idea":"Small changes are easier to review, to test and to undo, so trunk-based development makes everything downstream cheaper.","caption":"The push lands on main, and a webhook wakes the CI runner.","focus":["dev","repo","ci"],"flows":[{"from":"dev","to":"repo","kind":"request","label":"git push"},{"from":"repo","to":"ci","kind":"async","label":"webhook"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"dev","text":"branch lives hours"},{"node":"repo","text":"trunk: main"},{"node":"ci","text":"webhook trigger"}]},{"tag":"BUILD","title":"Build in a clean runner","body":"The CI runner is a build machine. It starts fresh, checks out the exact commit, restores cached dependencies, compiles the code and packages it as a candidate container image tagged with the commit SHA (the commit's unique hash): shop-api:a1b2c3d. A clean machine exposes 'works on my laptop' bugs. A linter (a tool that spots style slips and obvious mistakes) fails in seconds, so a typo never waits behind a long test suite. In this example, with caching, the build takes about 2 minutes.","idea":"Build in a clean, pinned environment (exact dependency and tool versions, not 'latest') so the result depends only on the commit.","caption":"A clean runner builds commit a1b2c3d into an image in about 2 min.","focus":["ci"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"pipeline":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"ci","text":"shop-api:a1b2c3d"},{"node":"ci","text":"clean runner","tone":"green"},{"node":"ci","text":"build ≈ 2 min","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TEST","title":"Run the gates","body":"Now the pipeline tests that exact candidate image. Quick checks go first: lint, unit tests, and fast security and dependency scans. Slower integration tests follow. If anything fails, the pipeline stops and nothing ships. A red (failing) main branch means stop the line: fix or revert right away. Say 1,240 tests pass in about 6 minutes on parallel runners (many machines sharing the work), so the pipeline clock reads 8 minutes. Keep this stage under roughly 10 minutes. The testing lesson covers test design.","idea":"Order stages cheapest-first and fail fast, so feedback arrives in minutes.","caption":"All 1,240 tests pass on parallel runners; the clock reads 8 min.","focus":["ci"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"pipeline":"8","tests":"1240"},"chips":[{"node":"ci","text":"1,240 tests pass","tone":"green"},{"node":"ci","text":"≈ 6 min","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"ARTIFACT","title":"Publish the tested artifact","body":"CI pushes the image that passed the gates to the artifact registry, a store for built images. It is the very same image, so nothing is rebuilt after testing. A tag can be moved to point at another image, so deployments should reference the content digest (a hash of the image) or the registry should enforce immutable tags. Every environment runs this one image; settings and secrets are added at deploy time, not baked in. Rebuilding could pull different dependencies than the ones you tested. Pushing and verifying adds about 2 minutes, so the clock reads 10.","idea":"Build once, test that artifact, then promote it everywhere; only injected configuration differs between environments.","caption":"Image shop-api:a1b2c3d is stored in the registry; the clock reads 10 min.","focus":["ci","registry"],"flows":[{"from":"ci","to":"registry","kind":"request","label":"push image"},{"from":"registry","to":"ci","kind":"response","label":"stored, sha256"}],"metrics":{"pipeline":"10"},"chips":[{"node":"registry","text":"shop-api:a1b2c3d"},{"node":"registry","text":"sha256 digest"},{"node":"registry","text":"+2 min","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"DEPLOY","title":"Staging, then production","body":"The pipeline first deploys the image to staging, a rehearsal copy of production, and runs smoke tests: log in, load a page, place a test order. If they pass, the same image is promoted to production, automatically (continuous deployment) or after a human approves (continuous delivery). The pipeline is itself a YAML file in the repo (pipeline as code), so changes get pull request review, can be diffed and reverted, and secrets are injected at run time, never committed. Canary rollouts are in the deploys lesson. Staging, smoke tests and the production rollout add about 4 minutes: about 14 in total.","idea":"Pipeline as code makes the delivery process reviewable, versioned and identical on every run.","caption":"The same image runs in staging, then in production.","focus":["registry","staging","prod"],"flows":[{"from":"registry","to":"staging","kind":"request","label":"deploy a1b2c3d"},{"from":"staging","to":"prod","kind":"request","label":"promote"},{"from":"registry","to":"prod","kind":"response","label":"pull image"}],"metrics":{"pipeline":"14","errors":"0.2"},"chips":[{"node":"staging","text":"smoke tests"},{"node":"prod","text":"same image"},{"node":"prod","text":"total ≈ 14 min","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The release is bad","body":"Suppose the change passed every test but has a bug that only real traffic exposes. On the diagram, production turns red: the error rate climbs from 0.2% to 6%. Tests cannot catch everything, which is why watching production is part of the pipeline's feedback loop. A fix-forward patch (a new fix instead of undoing the release) needs time to write and review, then another full pipeline run of about 14 minutes, while customers keep failing. Observability is its own lesson; here the point is that something must trigger the next move.","idea":"Passing tests means 'probably fine'; production signals decide whether it really is.","caption":"Errors in production jump from 0.2% to 6%.","focus":["prod"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"errors":"6"},"chips":[{"node":"prod","text":"errors 6%","tone":"red"},{"node":"prod","text":"was 0.2%","at":"bottom"},{"node":"ci","text":"fix-forward 14 min","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"ROLLBACK","title":"Redeploy the last good image","body":"The registry still holds the previous image, so a rollback is just a deploy of shop-api:9f8e7d6, with no rebuild and no new tests. In the animation production pulls that old image, and the error rate drops back to 0.2% in about a minute. Roll back configuration changes too, and watch health checks and the error rate after the redeploy. This works only if old images are kept and the release did not change data in a way the old code cannot read. A one-way database change makes rollback unsafe, which is why expand/contract migrations exist. Roll back first, then debug calmly.","idea":"Rollback should be a one-step redeploy of a stored artifact, usually much faster than fixing forward.","caption":"Production pulls the previous image and is healthy again; errors back to 0.2%.","focus":["registry","prod"],"flows":[{"from":"registry","to":"prod","kind":"response","label":"pull 9f8e7d6"}],"metrics":{"pipeline":"1","errors":"0.2"},"chips":[{"node":"registry","text":"shop-api:9f8e7d6","tone":"green"},{"node":"prod","text":"rollback ≈ 1 min","tone":"green"},{"node":"prod","text":"errors 0.2%","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"The path from commit to users","body":"Merge small changes to trunk often. The pipeline builds one candidate in a clean runner, tests it cheapest-first, publishes that same image, and promotes it through staging to production. The pipeline itself is code in the repo. When production signals go bad, redeploy the previous image first and investigate second.","idea":"Small changes, one artifact, automated gates, and a fast way back.","caption":"Commit to production in about 14 minutes, with a rollback in about 1.","focus":["dev","repo","ci","registry","staging","prod"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"pipeline":"14","tests":"1240","errors":"0.2"},"chips":[{"node":"prod","text":"commit to users 14 min"},{"node":"ci","text":"1,240 tests"},{"node":"prod","text":"errors 0.2%","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Staging passed all smoke tests, but the pipeline then rebuilds the code from source for production. What is the main risk?","options":["Production will be slower to start","The production build may differ from what was tested, for example through newer dependency versions","Staging data will leak into production","The registry will run out of space"],"answer":1,"why":"Rebuilding creates a new artifact that was never tested. Building once and promoting the same image guarantees production runs exactly what staging validated."},{"q":"Your pipeline runs a 25-minute end-to-end suite before the 30-second lint check. What is the best change?","options":["Remove the lint check","Run the end-to-end suite only after deploying to production","Skip tests on small changes","Run lint and unit tests first to fail fast, and parallelise or trim the slow suite"],"answer":3,"why":"Cheapest-first ordering gives quick failure feedback. Keep slow suites in the pipeline, but shard them in parallel or trim them, instead of removing the per-change gate."},{"q":"A team keeps feature branches open for three weeks, then merges. Which problem does trunk-based development mainly address?","options":["Slow network speed to the registry","Missing database indexes","Large, conflict-prone merges and big risky releases","Too many environments"],"answer":2,"why":"Long-lived branches diverge, causing painful merges and large untested batches. Merging small changes daily keeps integration cheap and each release small."},{"q":"A release raises production errors to 6%. The previous image is still in the registry. What is usually the best first move?","options":["Write and ship a fix through the full pipeline","Redeploy the previous artifact, then investigate","Delete the failing tests","Rebuild the old commit from source"],"answer":1,"why":"Redeploying a stored, known-good artifact restores service in about a minute. Fix-forward takes a full pipeline run while users keep failing."},{"q":"When might rolling back the application image NOT be enough to undo a release?","options":["When the image was tagged with a commit SHA","When staging is down","When tests ran in parallel","When the release also ran a one-way database migration that the old code cannot work with"],"answer":3,"why":"Code rolls back easily, but data changes do not. Backward-compatible, expand/contract migrations keep the old version working against the new schema."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Continuous integration","def":"Merge to a shared main branch often, and automatically build and test every merge."},{"term":"Delivery vs deployment","def":"Delivery: every passing change is ready to ship, release is a button. Deployment: it ships to production automatically."},{"term":"Trunk-based development","def":"Everyone merges to main at least daily; unfinished work hides behind feature flags, not long branches."},{"term":"Immutable artifact","def":"A versioned build output (image, package) tagged by commit SHA, built once and promoted unchanged through environments."},{"term":"Pipeline as code","def":"The pipeline definition (YAML) lives in the repo, so it is reviewed, versioned and reproducible."},{"term":"Fail fast","def":"Order stages cheapest-first (lint, unit, integration, e2e) so most failures show up within minutes."},{"term":"Rollback","def":"Redeploy the previous known-good artifact. Needs retained artifacts and backward-compatible data changes."},{"term":"Speed targets (rules of thumb)","def":"Aim for commit to test result in under 10 minutes and a rollback in a few minutes at most; these are guidelines, not standards."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"deploy-strategies","title":"Deploys: Canary, Blue-Green, Flags","subtitle":"Watch one new version ship by rolling, blue-green, canary, flags and shadow traffic, and see how far a bad build can spread in each.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How rolling, blue-green, and canary deploys differ in cost, speed, and blast radius","Why a canary only limits damage if automatic metric checks compare it with v1","How feature flags separate deploying code from releasing a feature","What a dark launch (shadow traffic) tests that staging cannot","How expand/contract migrations keep the database safe while two versions run at once"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"Users","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"router","label":"Traffic router","kind":"lb","x":30,"y":50},{"id":"old","label":"Old version (v1)","kind":"service","x":58,"y":18},{"id":"new","label":"New version (v2)","kind":"service","x":58,"y":82},{"id":"flags","label":"Flag service","kind":"external","x":30,"y":86},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":88,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"router"},{"from":"router","to":"old"},{"from":"router","to":"new"},{"from":"old","to":"db"},{"from":"new","to":"db"},{"from":"flags","to":"new"}],"metrics":[{"id":"share","label":"Traffic on v2","unit":"%"},{"id":"err2","label":"v2 errors","unit":"%"},{"id":"err1","label":"v1 errors","unit":"%"},{"id":"hit","label":"Users on v2","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Shipping without breaking things","body":"Your team has a new build, v2, and 40 servers running v1 for millions of users. Every deploy is a bet that v2 behaves in production like it did in tests, and sometimes the bet loses. So the real question is not how to deploy. It is how many users a bad change can hurt (the blast radius) and how fast you can undo it. On the diagram, all traffic still goes to v1 while v2 waits.","idea":"Deploy strategy is risk management: limit how many users a bad build reaches and how fast you can undo it.","caption":"All traffic is on v1. v2 is built and waiting.","focus":["users","router","old","new"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"router","kind":"request","label":"100% traffic"},{"from":"router","to":"old","kind":"request","label":"to v1"}],"metrics":{"share":"0","err2":"0","err1":"0.2","hit":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"old","text":"40 servers on v1"},{"node":"router","text":"100% to v1","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"ROLLING","title":"Rolling: swap servers in batches","body":"A rolling deploy swaps v1 servers for v2 a few at a time, for example 4 of 40 per batch. Between batches it waits for health checks (automatic probes that ask a server 'are you ready?'). It needs little or no spare capacity and is the default in most orchestrators. The catch: while it runs, both versions serve users, so a bad v2 keeps reaching more users batch after batch unless a check stops it. The two versions also share APIs, queues and caches, so keep those backward compatible. Rolling back means rolling forward again with the old build, which is slow.","idea":"Rolling is cheap and simple, but old and new run together and rollback is slow.","caption":"First batch done: 4 of 40 servers run v2, so 10% of traffic hits v2.","focus":["router","old","new"],"flows":[{"from":"router","to":"old","kind":"request","label":"36 of 40"},{"from":"router","to":"new","kind":"request","label":"4 of 40"}],"metrics":{"share":"10","err2":"0.2","err1":"0.2","hit":"10"},"chips":[{"node":"new","text":"4 of 40 on v2"},{"node":"old","text":"36 of 40 on v1"},{"node":"new","kind":"progress","value":10,"text":"10%"}]},{"tag":"BLUE-GREEN","title":"Blue-green: flip a whole environment","body":"Blue-green keeps two full environments. Blue (v1) serves users while green (v2) is started and smoke-tested with no real traffic. Then the router flips 100% of traffic to green in one step, as the animation shows. Rolling back is just flipping back, often within seconds, because blue is still running. The price is paying for roughly double capacity during the switch, and the flip exposes everyone at once.","idea":"Blue-green gives instant rollback at the cost of double capacity and an all-at-once switch.","caption":"The router flips every request from v1 to v2 in one move.","focus":["router","old","new"],"flows":[{"from":"router","to":"new","kind":"request","label":"flip: 100%"},{"from":"new","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"share":"100","err2":"0.2","hit":"100","err1":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"router","text":"flip to 100%"},{"node":"new","text":"smoke-tested","tone":"green"},{"node":"old","text":"kept for rollback","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CANARY","title":"Canary: a small slice first","body":"A canary sends a small slice of real traffic, say 5%, to v2 while 95% stays on v1, with each user kept on one version. (The name comes from the canary once carried into coal mines as an early warning.) Automation compares v2 with v1 on the same signals: error rate, p99 latency (99% of requests finish faster than this, so it shows the slow tail), and saturation (how close servers are to their limits). Give it enough requests to be meaningful. If v2 stays within a set margin of v1 (for example errors within 0.1 points) for 10 minutes, the share steps up to 25%, 50%, then 100%.","idea":"A canary tests v2 on real traffic while capping the blast radius, but only if the metric comparison is automatic.","caption":"5% of requests reach v2, and both versions are measured side by side.","focus":["router","old","new"],"flows":[{"from":"router","to":"old","kind":"request","label":"95%"},{"from":"router","to":"new","kind":"request","label":"5%"}],"metrics":{"share":"5","err2":"0.2","err1":"0.2","hit":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"new","kind":"progress","value":5,"text":"5%"},{"node":"old","text":"95% on v1"},{"node":"router","text":"hold 10 min","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The canary catches a bad build","body":"Suppose v2 has a bug that breaks 5% of its own requests. At 5% traffic the fleet-wide error rate only creeps from 0.2% to about 0.44%, which is easy to miss on a dashboard. But v2's own error rate is 25 times v1's, so the comparison trips within two minutes and the router drops v2 back to 0%. Only about 0.25% of all requests failed (5% reached v2 and 5% of those failed), for two minutes. A blue-green flip would have sent 100% of traffic to the bad build until someone noticed, and a rolling deploy with no metric checks would have kept replacing servers.","idea":"Small blast radius plus automatic rollback turns an outage into a blip.","caption":"v2 errors spike; the router pulls all traffic back to v1.","focus":["router","old","new"],"flows":[{"from":"new","to":"router","kind":"fail","label":"5% errors"},{"from":"router","to":"old","kind":"request","label":"back to 100%"}],"metrics":{"share":"0","err2":"5","err1":"0.2","hit":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"new","text":"v2 errors 5%","tone":"red"},{"node":"old","text":"v1 errors 0.2%","tone":"green"},{"node":"router","text":"fleet 0.44%","at":"bottom"},{"node":"router","text":"v2 back to 0%","tone":"green","at":"right"}]},{"tag":"FLAGS","title":"Feature flags: deploy is not release","body":"A feature flag is an on/off switch checked in code at run time, such as if flags.enabled('new-checkout', user). Here v2 is already on every server, but the new-checkout feature is switched on for only 1% of users. You can widen it to staff, then 1%, then everyone, with no new deploy, and turning it off takes seconds, not a rebuild. The cost: flags add code paths to test, and old flags pile up, so remove each one when its rollout is done. Note the Users on v2 tile counts who runs the v2 code (everyone), not who sees the feature (1%).","idea":"Flags decouple deploying code from releasing a feature, giving per-user control and a seconds-long kill switch.","caption":"All traffic runs v2, but the new-checkout flag is on for only 1% of users.","focus":["router","flags","new"],"flows":[{"from":"flags","to":"new","kind":"response","label":"new-checkout: 1%"},{"from":"router","to":"new","kind":"request","label":"all traffic"}],"metrics":{"share":"100","err2":"0.2","err1":"0","hit":"100"},"chips":[{"node":"flags","text":"new-checkout: 1%"},{"node":"flags","text":"off in seconds","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"new","text":"v2 on all servers"}]},{"tag":"DARK LAUNCH","title":"Dark launch: run it, hide the result","body":"A dark launch (also called traffic mirroring) copies live requests to v2 and throws away its responses, so users only ever see v1's answers. In the animation the router sends each request to v1 for the real answer and sends a copy to v2. You learn how v2 handles real traffic and volume, such as holding a p99 of 180 ms, and you can compare its answers with v1's. (Some teams use the term for flag-hidden features instead.) The shadow path must not send emails, charge cards or write shared data. Keep the mirror asynchronous and sampled, so it cannot slow real requests or overload shared dependencies.","idea":"Shadow traffic tests v2 on real load without exposing users, as long as the mirror is isolated and has no side effects.","caption":"Mirrored copies go to v2, but only v1's responses return to users.","focus":["users","router","old","new"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"router","kind":"request","label":"real request"},{"from":"router","to":"old","kind":"request","label":"to v1"},{"from":"router","to":"new","kind":"async","label":"mirrored copy"},{"from":"old","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"answer used"},{"from":"router","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"v1 answer only"}],"metrics":{"share":"0","err2":"0.3","err1":"0.2","hit":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"new","text":"p99 180 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"new","text":"responses discarded","at":"bottom"},{"node":"users","text":"sees v1 only","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"MIGRATION","title":"The database must serve both versions","body":"Mid-rollout, say half the servers run v2 and half run v1, and both share one database that you cannot switch in an instant. Suppose v2 renames the name column to full_name. Use expand/contract, in separate deploys. 1) Expand: add a nullable (allowed to be empty) full_name column, which v1 ignores. 2) Deploy v2, which writes both columns but still reads name. 3) Once no v1 remains, backfill (fill the new column for existing rows) and check it is complete. 4) A later deploy switches reads to full_name. 5) Contract: drop name only when rolling back to old code is off the table.","idea":"Never ship a schema change that breaks the old version; add first, migrate, and remove in a separate later deploy.","caption":"Half the servers run each version, and both share one database.","focus":["old","new","db"],"flows":[{"from":"old","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"uses name"},{"from":"new","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"writes both"},{"from":"db","to":"new","kind":"response","label":"reads name"}],"metrics":{"share":"50","err2":"0.2","err1":"0.2","hit":"50"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"+ full_name column"},{"node":"new","text":"writes both"},{"node":"old","text":"uses name","at":"bottom"},{"node":"new","kind":"progress","value":50,"text":"50%"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick by blast radius and rollback speed","body":"Rolling is cheap but slow to undo. Blue-green undoes in seconds but costs double capacity and flips everyone at once. Canary limits exposure but needs good metrics. Flags give per-user control after the deploy, and dark launches test real load without exposing users. Underneath all of them, expand/contract keeps the database working for both versions. Mature teams combine them: canary the build, flag the feature.","idea":"Combine canary for the build, flags for the feature, and expand/contract for the data.","caption":"Each tool limits risk at a different layer.","focus":[],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"router","text":"canary + flag"},{"node":"db","text":"expand/contract"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your service is stateless, you have no spare capacity budget, and a few minutes of mixed versions is fine. Which strategy fits best?","options":["Blue-green, because it is always safest","Dark launch to production","Rolling deploy in small batches with health checks","Canary with a full duplicate fleet"],"answer":2,"why":"Rolling needs little or no extra capacity. Blue-green requires roughly double the servers during the switch, which this team has ruled out."},{"q":"A canary at 5% traffic shows v2's error rate at 4% and v1's at 0.2%, but the fleet-wide error rate looks normal. What should happen?","options":["Continue; the overall rate is fine","Roll back, because the comparison is v2 against v1, not the fleet average","Wait an hour for the numbers to average out","Raise the canary to 50% to get more data"],"answer":1,"why":"At 5% share, a broken v2 is diluted in the fleet average. The canary only works when you compare the canary group directly against the baseline."},{"q":"A bug is found in a new feature already live for 100% of users behind a flag. What is the fastest fix?","options":["Roll the entire deploy back","Start a blue-green switch","Wait for the next release train","Flip the flag off"],"answer":3,"why":"The flag decouples release from deploy, so turning it off takes seconds and needs no new build. Other unrelated changes in the deploy stay live."},{"q":"You want to rename a column from name to full_name. Which plan is safe while v1 and v2 run together?","options":["Add full_name, write both, backfill once v1 is gone, switch reads, and drop name in a later deploy","Rename it in the same deploy that ships v2","Drop name first, then deploy v2","Take the site down and rename it"],"answer":0,"why":"A direct rename breaks whichever version expects the other column name. Expand/contract keeps both versions working and delays the destructive step until rollback is no longer needed."},{"q":"What does a dark launch tell you that a canary does not?","options":["Whether users like the new feature","Whether the database schema is correct","How v2 behaves under production-shaped load with no user ever seeing its responses","Nothing; it is the same thing"],"answer":2,"why":"A dark launch mirrors traffic and discards v2's output, so users never see its responses. A canary serves real responses to a small group of users."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Rolling deploy","def":"Replace instances in batches with health checks between them. Little or no extra capacity; old and new coexist; rollback is another rollout."},{"term":"Blue-green","def":"Two full environments; flip the router to switch. Instant rollback, about 2x capacity during the switch, all users move at once."},{"term":"Canary","def":"Send a small share (1-5%) of real traffic to the new version, compare its metrics with the old, then step up. Needs automatic checks."},{"term":"Blast radius","def":"How many users or requests a bad change can affect before it is stopped. Deploy strategy is mainly about shrinking this."},{"term":"Feature flag","def":"A runtime switch that turns code paths on per user or percentage. Decouples deploy from release; remove stale flags."},{"term":"Dark launch","def":"Also called traffic mirroring. Copy live traffic to the new version, discard its responses, compare with the old. Tests load and correctness; avoid side effects."},{"term":"Expand/contract","def":"Schema change in phases: add new, dual-write and backfill, switch reads, drop old in a later deploy. Keeps v1 and v2 both working."},{"term":"Rule of thumb","def":"Canary the build, flag the feature, expand/contract the schema, and always know how to roll back before you start."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"observability","title":"Observability","subtitle":"Watch a slow database turn into a page for the on-call engineer, and see which signal answers which question.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How metrics, logs, and traces differ and what question each one answers","How the RED and USE methods pick which numbers to watch","How a trace ID ties one request together across services","Why you page on symptoms users feel, not on causes like CPU"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Mobile app","kind":"client","x":9,"y":50},{"id":"api","label":"Checkout API","kind":"service","x":27,"y":50},{"id":"pay","label":"Payments service","kind":"service","x":47,"y":18},{"id":"db","label":"Orders database","kind":"db","x":72,"y":16},{"id":"collector","label":"Telemetry collector","kind":"worker","x":47,"y":66},{"id":"tsdb","label":"Metrics store","kind":"db","x":70,"y":46},{"id":"logs","label":"Logs + traces store","kind":"storage","x":70,"y":82},{"id":"alert","label":"Alert rules","kind":"service","x":90,"y":46},{"id":"oncall","label":"On-call engineer","kind":"user","x":90,"y":82}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"api"},{"from":"api","to":"pay"},{"from":"pay","to":"db"},{"from":"api","to":"collector"},{"from":"pay","to":"collector"},{"from":"collector","to":"tsdb"},{"from":"collector","to":"logs"},{"from":"tsdb","to":"alert"},{"from":"alert","to":"oncall"},{"from":"oncall","to":"logs"},{"from":"oncall","to":"tsdb","label":"dashboard"},{"from":"oncall","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"rate","label":"Rate","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"errors","label":"Errors","unit":"%"},{"id":"p99","label":"p99 latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"pages","label":"Pages sent","unit":""}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Is checkout healthy right now?","body":"Checkout handles about 400 requests per second and works today. But at 2 a.m. something will break and nobody will be watching. You usually cannot attach a debugger to production, so the only way to know what happened is what the system recorded about itself. Observability means how well you can answer new questions about a running system just from its outputs. The animation follows one healthy checkout request, and the tiles show today's baseline.","idea":"You cannot fix what you cannot see, and in production the only thing you can see is what the system emitted.","caption":"A healthy baseline: 400 req/s, 0.1% errors, p99 of 220 ms.","focus":["client","api","pay","db"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"POST /checkout"},{"from":"api","to":"pay","kind":"request","label":"charge"},{"from":"pay","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"write order"},{"from":"db","to":"pay","kind":"response","label":"ok"},{"from":"pay","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"paid"},{"from":"api","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"200"}],"metrics":{"rate":"400","errors":"0.1","p99":"220","pages":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"400 req/s"},{"node":"api","text":"errors 0.1%","tone":"green","at":"bottom"},{"node":"api","text":"p99 220 ms","at":"right"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Three signals, three questions","body":"Each service sends its telemetry (the data it reports about itself) to a collector, a middleman that forwards it to storage. There are three kinds. Metrics are numbers over time, such as requests per second: cheap, and good for 'is something wrong?'. Logs are timestamped event records: detailed, and good for 'what exactly happened?'. Traces follow one request across services: good for 'where did the time go?'. No single signal answers all three questions.","idea":"Metrics tell you that something is wrong, traces tell you where, and logs tell you why.","caption":"Services push metrics, logs, and spans (timed steps of a trace) to a collector.","focus":["api","pay","collector"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"collector","kind":"async","label":"metrics+logs+spans"},{"from":"pay","to":"collector","kind":"async","label":"metrics+logs+spans"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"collector","text":"metrics+logs+traces"}]},{"tag":"METRICS","title":"Metrics: RED for services, USE for resources","body":"Services are instrumented (given code that reports on themselves) with a library such as OpenTelemetry. A collector forwards the numbers to the metrics store, which keeps them as time series, sampled about every 15 seconds. For anything that serves requests, watch RED: Rate (requests per second), Errors (share that fail), and Duration (latency, shown as a percentile like p99, the time that 99% of requests beat, not an average). For resources such as a database host or queue, watch USE: Utilization (how busy), Saturation (work waiting), and Errors. Keep label values few: a user_id label would create millions of separate series and make storage and queries very costly.","idea":"RED asks how users are served; USE asks how machines are coping. Avoid high-cardinality labels.","caption":"The collector forwards aggregated numbers to the metrics store.","focus":["collector","tsdb"],"flows":[{"from":"collector","to":"tsdb","kind":"async","label":"RED + USE series"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"tsdb","text":"sample every 15 s"},{"node":"tsdb","text":"RED + USE","at":"bottom"},{"node":"collector","text":"OpenTelemetry"}]},{"tag":"LOGS","title":"Logs: detailed, but expensive","body":"Each service also writes one structured JSON line per event: timestamp, level, order id, and the trace ID. Structured fields can be searched and filtered, unlike free text. The cost adds up. At about 2 KB of logs per request and 400 requests per second, that is roughly 70 GB a day. So teams log at the right level and sample noisy debug lines.","idea":"Log structured events with a trace ID, and budget for volume, since it grows with traffic.","caption":"Structured log lines flow to the logs store, about 70 GB a day.","focus":["collector","logs"],"flows":[{"from":"collector","to":"logs","kind":"async","label":"JSON log lines"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"logs","text":"≈ 70 GB / day","tone":"red"},{"node":"logs","text":"2 KB / request","at":"bottom"},{"node":"collector","text":"JSON lines"}]},{"tag":"TRACING","title":"Traces: one ID across every hop","body":"The Checkout API creates a trace ID for the incoming request and passes it on every downstream call in a header (the W3C traceparent standard). Each service records a span: its name, start time, duration and parent span. Spans that share a trace ID join into one timeline of the whole request. The ID 4bf9 is shortened here; real IDs are 32 hex characters, and an instrumented client can start the trace instead. Tracing every request is costly, so teams sample. Sampling up front (say 1%) is cheap but can miss the bad request. Sampling after the trace finishes lets you keep every slow or failed one.","idea":"A propagated trace ID turns separate service logs into one story per request.","caption":"The trace ID travels with the request, and each service sends its span to the collector.","focus":["client","api","pay","db","collector"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"POST /checkout"},{"from":"api","to":"pay","kind":"request","label":"traceparent 4bf9"},{"from":"pay","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"traceparent 4bf9"},{"from":"pay","to":"collector","kind":"async","label":"span: pay"},{"from":"api","to":"collector","kind":"async","label":"span: api"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"trace 4bf9"},{"node":"pay","text":"traceparent 4bf9"},{"node":"db","text":"traceparent 4bf9"},{"node":"collector","text":"sample 1%","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The database slows down","body":"A batch update runs in one long transaction and holds row locks in the orders database. Most writes still take about 20 ms, but writes that wait on a locked row take over 2 seconds. Payments gives up on a database write after 3 seconds, and about 5% of writes wait that long and fail. Nothing crashed, no server is down, and CPU looks fine. From the user's side, checkout is slow and 4.8% of attempts fail.","idea":"Many real outages are slowness, not crashes, so you must measure latency and errors, not just uptime.","caption":"The database answers slowly, so payments time out and checkouts fail.","focus":["api","pay","db"],"flows":[{"from":"pay","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"write order"},{"from":"db","to":"pay","kind":"fail","label":"2-3 s, timeouts"},{"from":"pay","to":"api","kind":"fail","label":"payment timeout"}],"metrics":{"errors":"4.8","p99":"3000"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"writes wait > 2 s","tone":"red"},{"node":"pay","text":"timeout 3 s"},{"node":"api","text":"errors 4.8%","tone":"red"},{"node":"api","text":"p99 3 s","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"ALERTING","title":"Page on symptoms, not causes","body":"An alert rule reads the metrics store and pages a human only for something users feel, such as checkout errors above 2% for 5 minutes. Alerting on causes like CPU above 80% fires on harmless spikes and stays silent on this lock problem. A good version uses SLO burn rate. An SLO is a reliability target; a 99.9% target leaves an error budget of 0.1% failures, and burn rate is how many times faster than budgeted you are spending it. Here 4.8% errors burns the budget about 48 times too fast. Causes mostly belong on dashboards, not pagers, unless they predict imminent user impact (for example, disk full within hours).","idea":"Every page should mean users are hurting and a human must act now; everything else is a dashboard or a ticket.","caption":"The rule fires, and one page reaches the on-call engineer.","focus":["tsdb","alert","oncall"],"flows":[{"from":"alert","to":"tsdb","kind":"request","label":"errors>2% for 5m?"},{"from":"tsdb","to":"alert","kind":"response","label":"4.8% for 5m"},{"from":"alert","to":"oncall","kind":"async","label":"PAGE: checkout"}],"metrics":{"pages":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"tsdb","text":"4.8% for 5 min","tone":"red"},{"node":"alert","text":"errors > 2% · 5 min"},{"node":"alert","text":"burn 48×","tone":"red","at":"bottom"},{"node":"oncall","text":"paged","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"DIAGNOSE","title":"From symptom to cause","body":"Paged at 2 a.m., the engineer opens the dashboard, which shows RED going bad: errors and p99 are up at the Checkout API while the rate is unchanged. That shows the symptom but not the cause. Opening a slow trace shows a 2.1 second span inside the orders database call, and the log lines with that trace ID reveal lock waits. USE on the database confirms it: saturation is high while CPU is low.","idea":"Work top-down: a metric finds the symptom, a trace finds the slow hop, a log explains it.","caption":"Metrics show the symptom; traces and logs lead to the cause.","focus":["oncall","tsdb","logs","db"],"flows":[{"from":"oncall","to":"tsdb","kind":"request","label":"open dashboard"},{"from":"tsdb","to":"oncall","kind":"response","label":"errors+p99 up"},{"from":"oncall","to":"logs","kind":"request","label":"open slow trace"},{"from":"logs","to":"oncall","kind":"response","label":"lock waits"},{"from":"db","to":"oncall","kind":"response","label":"saturation high"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"tsdb","text":"errors + p99 up","tone":"red"},{"node":"logs","text":"lock waits"},{"node":"db","text":"span 2.1 s","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"CPU low","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"See, locate, explain, wake someone","body":"Metrics show that users are hurting, using RED for services and USE for resources. Traces carrying one ID show which hop is slow, and structured logs explain why. Alerts watch user-facing symptoms, so a page means real impact. Instrument before you need it: the outage you cannot see is the one you cannot fix.","idea":"Metrics detect, traces locate, logs explain, and symptom-based alerts decide who gets woken up.","caption":"The full path from request to telemetry to a page.","focus":["client","api","pay","db","collector","tsdb","logs","alert","oncall"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"tsdb","text":"RED + USE"},{"node":"logs","text":"why: lock waits"},{"node":"alert","text":"symptoms only","tone":"green"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Checkout p99 latency tripled but traffic and error rate are normal. Which signal best tells you which downstream call is responsible?","options":["A CPU utilization graph for the API servers","A distributed trace of a slow request","A count of total requests per minute","The service's uptime percentage"],"answer":1,"why":"A trace breaks one slow request into spans per hop, so you can see which call ate the time. Rate and uptime would look normal here."},{"q":"You are monitoring a message queue. Which set matches the USE method?","options":["Rate, Errors, Duration","Uptime, Speed, Errors","Latency, Traffic, Capacity","Utilization, Saturation, Errors"],"answer":3,"why":"USE (Utilization, Saturation, Errors) is for resources like queues and disks. RED (Rate, Errors, Duration) is for request-serving services."},{"q":"Which alert is the better page for the on-call engineer?","options":["Database CPU above 80% for 1 minute","Disk usage above 60%","Checkout success rate below 98% for 5 minutes","A single pod restarted"],"answer":2,"why":"It measures what users experience and needs action. The others are possible causes that often fire harmlessly, so they belong on dashboards or tickets."},{"q":"Should you add a user_id label to every metric time series?","options":["No, because each distinct value creates a new series and cost explodes","Yes, because it lets you filter any metric by user","Yes, because metrics are free to store","No, because labels slow down the network"],"answer":0,"why":"Each unique label combination is its own time series. Unbounded values like user IDs create millions of series. Put such detail in logs or traces instead."},{"q":"Checkout requests succeed normally, but a trace shows only the Checkout API span, with no spans from the Payments service. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Payments is down","The database is too slow","The trace ID header was not passed on the call to Payments","Metrics are being sampled too often"],"answer":2,"why":"If the trace context header is dropped, Payments starts its own unconnected trace. Propagating the ID on every outbound call is what stitches spans together."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Metrics","def":"Numbers sampled over time. Cheap to store and ideal for dashboards and alerts, but they hide the detail of individual requests."},{"term":"Logs","def":"Timestamped event records. Use structured JSON with a trace ID so they can be searched and joined to traces."},{"term":"Trace, span, context","def":"A trace is one request's whole journey; a span is one timed step inside it. The trace ID travels in headers such as W3C traceparent so every service joins the same trace."},{"term":"RED method","def":"For services: Rate, Errors, Duration. Use percentiles such as p99 for duration, not averages."},{"term":"USE method","def":"For resources: Utilization, Saturation, Errors. Saturation (queued work) often shows trouble before utilization does."},{"term":"Cardinality","def":"The number of distinct label combinations on a metric. Unbounded labels like user_id blow up storage and cost."},{"term":"SLO and burn rate","def":"An SLO is a reliability target, such as 99.9% success. Its error budget is the allowed 0.1% failures; burn rate is how many times faster than budgeted you are spending it."},{"term":"Symptom-based alerting","def":"Page on user-visible impact (errors, latency, SLO burn rate). Keep cause metrics like CPU on dashboards."}],"isNew":false}]},{"id":"case-studies","title":"Whole Systems","lessons":[{"slug":"design-url-shortener","title":"Design a URL Shortener","subtitle":"Build the whole system: create a short link, redirect fast from a cache, and count clicks without slowing anyone down.","minutes":16,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why a shortener is a read-heavy system and how the 100:1 ratio shapes every choice","Three ways to generate short keys and why block allocation from a counter wins","How a cache-aside redirect path serves most traffic without touching the database","Why click analytics go through a queue instead of the redirect path","How to size traffic, storage, and cache with simple back-of-envelope numbers"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Browser","kind":"client","x":8,"y":50,"url":"https://short.example"},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":27,"y":50},{"id":"redirect","label":"Redirect service","kind":"server","x":47,"y":25},{"id":"create","label":"Create service","kind":"service","x":47,"y":75},{"id":"cache","label":"Redis cache","kind":"cache","x":68,"y":12},{"id":"db","label":"URL store","kind":"db","x":88,"y":45},{"id":"keygen","label":"Key allocator","kind":"service","x":68,"y":88},{"id":"queue","label":"Click events","kind":"queue","x":68,"y":55},{"id":"worker","label":"Analytics worker","kind":"worker","x":88,"y":78}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"lb"},{"from":"lb","to":"redirect"},{"from":"lb","to":"create"},{"from":"redirect","to":"cache"},{"from":"redirect","to":"db"},{"from":"redirect","to":"queue"},{"from":"create","to":"keygen"},{"from":"create","to":"db"},{"from":"queue","to":"worker"},{"from":"worker","to":"db"},{"from":"redirect","to":"client"},{"from":"create","to":"client"}],"metrics":[{"id":"rps","label":"Redirects/s","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"hit","label":"Cache hit","unit":"%"},{"id":"dbq","label":"DB reads/s","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"p99","label":"p99 redirect","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Two jobs, very different load","body":"A URL shortener does two jobs. Create: turn a long URL into a short one (a write). Redirect: turn a short link back into the long URL (a read). Assume 100 million new links a month, which is about 40 writes per second. If each link is clicked about 100 times, reads average about 4,000 per second and may reach 12,000 at peak. These are assumptions, but the shape is real: reads far outnumber writes. Right now there is no cache, so every read hits the database.","idea":"Design for the read path first; the write path is small and can afford to be slower.","caption":"Rare writes (40/s) and constant reads (4,000/s) share one database.","focus":["client","create","redirect","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"rps":"4000","hit":"0","dbq":"4000","p99":"40"},"chips":[{"node":"create","text":"40 writes/s"},{"node":"redirect","text":"4,000 reads/s"},{"node":"db","text":"no cache yet","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"WRITE","title":"Create a short link","body":"You submit a long URL and the client sends POST /links (a request to create a link). The load balancer, which spreads traffic across servers, passes it to the create service. That service checks the URL (valid scheme, sensible length, not on a blocklist), picks a short key, and saves one row: key to long URL, plus owner and optional expiry. It then replies 201 with the short URL. Writes are only 40 per second, so speed is easy; total storage (about 3 TB over 5 years) is the real challenge.","idea":"Writes are low volume, so correctness (unique keys, valid URLs) matters more than raw speed here.","caption":"The request passes the load balancer; the reply is drawn straight back to the client.","focus":["client","lb","create","db"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"POST /links"},{"from":"lb","to":"create","kind":"request","label":"POST /links"},{"from":"create","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT row"},{"from":"db","to":"create","kind":"response","label":"ok"},{"from":"create","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"201 short URL"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"create","text":"201 short URL","tone":"green"},{"node":"db","text":"1 row inserted"}]},{"tag":"KEYS","title":"Generating the short key","body":"Each link needs a unique short key. A 7-character key from 62 symbols (a-z, A-Z, 0-9) gives 62^7, about 3.5 trillion keys, so random guessing rarely hits a real link. Hashing the URL or picking random keys can collide (two links get the same key), so you must detect and retry. Cleaner: the create service leases a block of 1 million numbers from a key allocator, then hands them out locally as a counter. Each number is converted to base62 text and padded to 7 characters. The allocator is called once per million links, and servers keep working from their block if it is briefly down.","idea":"Allocating ID blocks from a counter avoids collisions and makes key creation a local, constant-time step.","caption":"Once per million links the create service leases a block; every other write is local.","focus":["create","keygen","db"],"flows":[{"from":"create","to":"keygen","kind":"request","label":"lease 1M ids"},{"from":"keygen","to":"create","kind":"response","label":"ids 0 to 1M"},{"from":"create","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT aZ3kQ9x"},{"from":"db","to":"create","kind":"response","label":"ok"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"keygen","text":"lease 1M ids"},{"node":"create","text":"base62 · 7 chars"},{"node":"keygen","text":"1 call / 1M links","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Sequential keys leak","body":"A plain counter gives keys in order (aaaaaab, aaaaaac...), so anyone could walk through every link. To hide the order, the create service scrambles each number locally with format-preserving encryption: a keyed, reversible shuffle that turns each number into a different, unique number of the same size. The result looks random but is still unique. This is obfuscation, not security, because keys can still be guessed. When a block runs out, the service leases the next one (shown). A block lost in a crash just leaves unused gaps.","idea":"A counter plus a reversible scramble keeps keys unique and stops people walking them in order, but short keys are still guessable, so truly private links need auth or long random tokens.","caption":"The scramble runs inside the create service; the allocator only hands out new blocks.","focus":["create","keygen"],"flows":[{"from":"create","to":"keygen","kind":"request","label":"next 1M block"},{"from":"keygen","to":"create","kind":"response","label":"1M to 2M"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"create","text":"scrambled · unique"},{"node":"keygen","text":"next 1M block"}]},{"tag":"READ","title":"A redirect, served from cache","body":"Someone opens short.example/aZ3kQ9x. The redirect service first asks Redis, an in-memory cache, for that key. For a popular link this is a hit: the long URL comes back in under a millisecond and the service replies with an HTTP redirect, which tells the browser to go to the long URL. A few links get most clicks (assume the hottest 1 to 2 percent serve about 90 percent of reads), so a small cache absorbs most traffic. This pattern is cache-aside: the app, not the cache, loads missing entries.","idea":"A small cache in front of the store absorbs almost all redirect traffic.","caption":"Cache hit at peak load: the redirect is answered without touching the database.","focus":["client","lb","redirect","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /aZ3kQ9x"},{"from":"lb","to":"redirect","kind":"request","label":"GET /aZ3kQ9x"},{"from":"redirect","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET aZ3kQ9x"},{"from":"cache","to":"redirect","kind":"response","label":"hit: long URL"},{"from":"redirect","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"302 Location"}],"metrics":{"rps":"12000","hit":"90","dbq":"1200","p99":"25"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"hit · <1 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"cache","kind":"progress","value":90,"text":"90%"},{"node":"redirect","text":"302 Location"}]},{"tag":"MISS","title":"A cache miss falls to the store","body":"A rare link is not in Redis (a miss). The redirect service reads its row from the URL store by key, one indexed lookup of a few milliseconds, then saves it in the cache with a TTL (time to live: how long before the entry expires) so the next click is a hit. Keys that do not exist are cached briefly as \"not found\" (30 to 60 seconds), so repeated bad lookups skip the database. A flood of random keys still misses, so add a Bloom filter (a compact check for \"definitely not stored\") or rate-limit clients. Edits and deletes remove the cache key, and the TTL never outlasts the link's expiry.","idea":"Misses cost one indexed read plus a cache fill; negative caching absorbs repeated bad keys, and a Bloom filter or rate limit handles random ones.","caption":"Miss: read from the store, then refill the cache so the next click is a hit.","focus":["redirect","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"redirect","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET rareKey"},{"from":"cache","to":"redirect","kind":"response","label":"miss"},{"from":"redirect","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT by key"},{"from":"db","to":"redirect","kind":"response","label":"long URL"},{"from":"redirect","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"SET, TTL 24h"}],"metrics":{"p99":"25"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"miss","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"by key · few ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"cache","text":"TTL 24h","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"301 or 302?","body":"After finding the long URL, the service must pick a redirect status code. A 301 means moved permanently: browsers remember it and skip your service next time. That saves load, but hides repeat clicks from your analytics and makes edits slow to take effect. A 302 means temporary: the browser asks you every time, so you keep counting and can change the target. Services that need stats or editable links usually send 302 (or 307). A 301 with Cache-Control: no-store also works, but you pay the 302 cost with a misleading status.","idea":"The redirect status code decides whether you keep control and visibility, or save load.","caption":"With a 302, every click comes back through the redirect service.","focus":["client","lb","redirect"],"flows":[{"from":"redirect","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"302 Location"},{"from":"client","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"next click"},{"from":"lb","to":"redirect","kind":"request","label":"next click"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"redirect","text":"302 every click","tone":"green"},{"node":"client","text":"301 hides clicks","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"ANALYTICS","title":"Count clicks off the hot path","body":"Updating a counter on every redirect would turn 12,000 reads per second into 12,000 writes per second. Instead, the redirect service drops a small event (key, time, country) on a queue, a waiting line for messages, and replies at once. A worker takes events in batches and updates one row per key, minute and country. If the queue is briefly down, the redirect still works and that click goes uncounted. Queues deliver at least once, so a crashed batch may count twice; for click stats that is usually fine, or dedupe by event ID. Counts lag by seconds.","idea":"Fire-and-forget events plus batch aggregation keep analytics from slowing redirects.","caption":"Click events flow through a queue to a worker that writes them in batches.","focus":["redirect","queue","worker","db"],"flows":[{"from":"redirect","to":"queue","kind":"async","label":"click event"},{"from":"queue","to":"worker","kind":"async","label":"batch of 500"},{"from":"worker","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"add batch totals"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"queue","text":"click event"},{"node":"worker","text":"batch of 500"},{"node":"db","text":"1 row/key/min/country","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The cache goes cold","body":"Now imagine the Redis cluster restarts and every cached key is gone (a cold cache). All 12,000 reads per second miss and land on the database, which is sized for about 1,200 reads per second, so it may fall over and latency spikes. Defences: replicate the cache, warm it with recent hot keys before taking traffic, merge simultaneous misses for the same key into one database read, add random jitter to TTLs so keys do not expire together, and shed load while it warms.","idea":"A cache that hides load also hides your real capacity, so plan for the day it is empty.","caption":"Cold cache: nearly every redirect lands on the database.","focus":["redirect","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"redirect","to":"cache","kind":"fail","label":"cache down"},{"from":"redirect","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"12k reads/s"}],"metrics":{"hit":"0","dbq":"12000","p99":"180"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"cold · 0% hits","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"12,000 reads/s","tone":"red"},{"node":"redirect","text":"p99 180 ms","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Numbers that hold it together","body":"Napkin math: 100 million links a month for 5 years is 6 billion rows. At about 500 bytes each that is roughly 3 TB, so shard by key (split data across servers) or use a store that scales out. The hot 1 to 2 percent of keys (60 to 120 million at about 200 bytes) is only 12 to 24 GB, so a small Redis cluster is enough. Summary: ID blocks make writes cheap, cache-aside makes reads cheap, and a queue keeps analytics out of the way.","idea":"Read-heavy design: cache the hot keys, allocate IDs in blocks, and push counting off the critical path.","caption":"Healthy again at average load: 90 percent of reads never reach the store.","focus":["client","lb","redirect","create","cache","db","keygen","queue","worker"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"rps":"4000","hit":"90","dbq":"400","p99":"25"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"6B rows · 3 TB"},{"node":"cache","text":"12-24 GB hot set"},{"node":"redirect","text":"p99 25 ms","tone":"green"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a block-allocation counter beat hashing the long URL to make short keys?","options":["Hashes cannot be encoded in base62 at all","Each key is unique by construction, so no collisions need retrying","Counters always produce shorter keys than any hash can","Hashing needs a separate database for every single key"],"answer":1,"why":"Handing out numbers from leased blocks guarantees uniqueness without checks. Truncated hashes can collide and need retry logic."},{"q":"After you switch redirects from 302 to 301, the click counts on your dashboard drop sharply, while the destination sites report unchanged visitor numbers. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The cache hit rate dropped","Analytics workers crashed at the same moment","Base62 keys are being truncated","Browsers cache the 301, so repeat clicks never reach your service"],"answer":3,"why":"A 301 is permanent and cacheable, so browsers skip your service on repeat visits and those clicks are never counted."},{"q":"The cache has a 90 percent hit rate at 12,000 reads/s. Roughly how many reads per second reach the database, and what does a cache restart do?","options":["About 120; nothing noticeable happens","About 10,800; load drops to 1,200 afterwards","About 1,200; it jumps toward 12,000 when cold","About 12,000 always; the cache only saves latency"],"answer":2,"why":"10 percent of 12,000 is 1,200 misses. With an empty cache nearly every read misses, so the store suddenly sees up to ten times its normal load."},{"q":"Why send click events through a queue instead of incrementing a counter in the database during each redirect?","options":["It keeps a slow write off the redirect path and lets a worker batch","Queues guarantee the exact count in real time, with no lag at all","Databases cannot store counters, so a queue must hold them instead","It removes the need for a cache in front of the store entirely"],"answer":0,"why":"Redirect latency should not depend on a write. Queueing decouples the two and batching turns thousands of tiny updates into few larger ones."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Read:write ratio","def":"Assume about 100 reads per write. It tells you to optimise redirects and cache hard, and keep creation simple."},{"term":"Key space","def":"7 base62 characters give 62^7, about 3.5 trillion keys. 6 characters give 56 billion, which is enough for many systems."},{"term":"Block allocation","def":"Servers lease ranges of IDs (for example 1M at a time) and encode them locally. Crashes only waste unused numbers."},{"term":"Cache-aside","def":"On a read, check the cache, on a miss read the store and fill the cache. Add TTLs, jitter, and negative caching."},{"term":"301 vs 302","def":"301 is permanent and browser-cached, saving load but hiding clicks. 302 is temporary, keeps analytics and edits working."},{"term":"Async analytics","def":"Emit a click event to a queue, aggregate in batches, write totals. Counts lag by seconds but redirects stay fast."},{"term":"Sizing rule","def":"100M links/month is about 40 writes/s; at 100:1 reads are about 4,000/s average, 12,000 at a 3x peak. 5 years is about 3 TB."}],"lab":{"prompt":"You are the engineer for a shortener expecting 100 million new links per month and 100 redirects per link. Produce a design: API, key generation, storage, redirect path with caching, and analytics. Defend each choice with a number.","requirements":["Compute average and peak writes per second and redirects per second, stating your peak factor","Pick a key length and show the key space is large enough for 5 years","Describe how the create path makes keys unique across many servers","Describe the redirect path including cache hit and miss behaviour and TTLs","Choose 301 or 302 and justify it against analytics and edit needs","Describe how click counts are collected without slowing redirects","Explain what happens when the cache is empty and one mitigation"],"hints":["100M per month divided by about 2.6M seconds per month is roughly 40 per second","Total rows after 5 years times bytes per row gives storage; compare with one server's disk","Think about what a lost ID block costs: almost nothing","Estimate what fraction of keys is hot, multiply by bytes per entry, and compare with one cache node's RAM","Ask whether the counts must be exact in real time or can lag a few seconds"],"checklist":["Traffic estimates are stated with assumptions","Key space is larger than total links needed","No two servers can issue the same key","Redirect reads check the cache before the database","Analytics writes are not on the redirect critical path","A cold-cache or expired-key failure case is addressed"]},"isNew":false},{"slug":"design-chat","title":"Design a Chat App","subtitle":"Send one message through persistent connections, storage, delivery receipts, group fan-out and offline sync.","minutes":18,"level":"Advanced","learn":["Why chat uses long-lived WebSocket connections and how a gateway tier scales them","How a message is stored first, then routed to the recipient's gateway","How sent, delivered and read receipts flow back as ordinary messages","How group fan-out cost grows with group size, and how presence stays affordable with batched heartbeats","How an offline user catches up using a per-conversation sequence number"],"nodes":[{"id":"alice","label":"Alice's phone","kind":"client","x":9,"y":28},{"id":"gw1","label":"WS gateway A","kind":"gateway","x":31,"y":28},{"id":"chat","label":"Chat service","kind":"service","x":54,"y":28},{"id":"store","label":"Message store","kind":"db","x":80,"y":12},{"id":"presence","label":"Presence + routes","kind":"cache","x":80,"y":44},{"id":"bus","label":"Delivery queue","kind":"queue","x":54,"y":62},{"id":"push","label":"Push (APNs/FCM)","kind":"external","x":80,"y":84},{"id":"gw2","label":"WS gateway B","kind":"gateway","x":31,"y":72},{"id":"bob","label":"Bob's phone","kind":"client","x":9,"y":72}],"edges":[{"from":"alice","to":"gw1"},{"from":"gw1","to":"chat"},{"from":"chat","to":"store"},{"from":"chat","to":"presence"},{"from":"chat","to":"bus"},{"from":"bus","to":"gw2"},{"from":"gw2","to":"bob"},{"from":"gw2","to":"chat"},{"from":"chat","to":"push"},{"from":"bus","to":"gw1"}],"metrics":[{"id":"rate","label":"Msgs/sec","unit":"/s"},{"id":"gateways","label":"Gateways","unit":""},{"id":"ack","label":"Send ack","unit":"ms"},{"id":"deliv","label":"Deliveries","unit":"per msg"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Alice texts Bob","body":"Alice types \"hi\" and presses send. Within a fraction of a second Bob's phone should buzz, Alice should see a tick, and nothing may be lost, even if Bob's phone is off. The catch: plain HTTP only lets the client start a request, so a server cannot speak first. How can it push a message to Bob the moment it arrives? Assume 100 million daily users sending about 40 messages each, which is roughly 46,000 messages per second.","idea":"Chat needs server-initiated delivery, durability, and ordering, all at once.","caption":"Two phones, and one message that must cross the whole system.","focus":["alice","bob"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"rate":"46000"}},{"tag":"CONNECT","title":"Keep a socket open","body":"Asking the server \"anything new?\" every second wastes millions of empty requests. Instead, each app opens a WebSocket: a normal HTTP request upgraded into a long-lived, two-way channel. A gateway server holds that channel. The gateway tells the chat service Alice is online, and the service saves \"Alice is reachable via gateway A\" in a fast store (the route table). With about 20 million users online and roughly 100,000 sockets per gateway, you need around 200 gateways, plus spares. If one dies, its clients reconnect to others after a random delay, and its stale routes expire on their own.","idea":"Gateways only hold connections; a route table says which gateway holds which user.","caption":"The socket opens and Alice's route (gateway A) is recorded.","focus":["alice","gw1","chat","presence"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"gw1","kind":"handshake","label":"HTTP Upgrade"},{"from":"gw1","to":"alice","kind":"handshake","label":"101 Switching"},{"from":"gw1","to":"chat","kind":"async","label":"user online"},{"from":"chat","to":"presence","kind":"request","label":"alice -> gwA"}],"metrics":{"gateways":"200"},"chips":[{"node":"gw1","text":"101 Switching","tone":"green"},{"node":"presence","text":"alice → gwA"},{"node":"gw1","text":"200 gateways","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"SEND","title":"Store first, then ack","body":"Alice's app sends the message over the socket with an ID it made itself. The chat service gives the message the next sequence number for that conversation (4021 here), writes it to durable storage, and only then replies. Alice now sees a single tick, which means \"safe on the server\". If her connection drops and the app retries, the server spots the same ID and returns the original ack instead of storing a second copy.","idea":"Persist before acknowledging, and use a message ID so retries are safe.","caption":"The message is saved first; then the sent tick travels back to Alice.","focus":["alice","gw1","chat","store"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"gw1","kind":"request","label":"msg id=m1"},{"from":"gw1","to":"chat","kind":"request","label":"msg id=m1"},{"from":"chat","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"append seq=4021"},{"from":"store","to":"chat","kind":"response","label":"committed"},{"from":"chat","to":"gw1","kind":"response","label":"ack seq=4021"},{"from":"gw1","to":"alice","kind":"response","label":"sent tick"}],"metrics":{"ack":"60"},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"seq 4021 · durable","tone":"green"},{"node":"chat","text":"ack 60 ms"},{"node":"alice","text":"single tick"}]},{"tag":"STORAGE","title":"Where messages live","body":"At 46,000 messages a second, one database server cannot keep up, so the store is sharded (split across many servers). Rows are grouped by conversation ID and ordered by sequence number, so one chat's history is a single quick range read. One owner at a time handles each conversation, and it only inserts a row if that sequence number is free, so 4021 is never issued twice. At 200-300 bytes per message (text plus IDs, timestamps and indexes) that is about 0.8-1.2 TB a day before replication. Photos and videos go to separate blob storage.","idea":"One owner per conversation allocates seq; partition by conversation, order by seq.","caption":"The store is split by conversation, with each chat's rows ordered by seq.","focus":["chat","store"],"flows":[{"from":"chat","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"write by conv id"},{"from":"store","to":"chat","kind":"response","label":"committed 4021"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"store","text":"sharded by conv id"},{"node":"store","text":"0.8-1.2 TB/day","at":"bottom"},{"node":"chat","text":"seq 4021 once"}]},{"tag":"ROUTE","title":"Deliver, then receipts","body":"The chat service looks up Bob in the route table, finds he is on gateway B, and puts the message on the delivery queue for that gateway, which pushes it down his socket. A user may have several devices, so the table holds a set of gateways per user. Delivery is at-least-once (a message can arrive twice), so Bob's app drops duplicates by message ID. His phone then sends a \"delivered\" receipt, and later a \"read\" receipt. They travel back through the same queue to Alice's gateway. One read receipt can cover everything up to seq 4021.","idea":"A route lookup plus a queue reaches any user, and receipts are small cursor messages (up to seq N) sent back the same way.","caption":"The message reaches Bob; receipts travel back and become Alice's ticks.","focus":["chat","presence","bus","gw2","bob","gw1","alice"],"flows":[{"from":"chat","to":"presence","kind":"request","label":"where is bob?"},{"from":"presence","to":"chat","kind":"response","label":"gateway B"},{"from":"chat","to":"bus","kind":"async","label":"m1 for gwB"},{"from":"bus","to":"gw2","kind":"async","label":"m1"},{"from":"gw2","to":"bob","kind":"request","label":"m1 seq=4021"},{"from":"bob","to":"gw2","kind":"response","label":"delivered 4021"},{"from":"gw2","to":"chat","kind":"async","label":"receipt"},{"from":"chat","to":"bus","kind":"async","label":"receipt for gwA"},{"from":"bus","to":"gw1","kind":"async","label":"receipt"},{"from":"gw1","to":"alice","kind":"response","label":"double tick"}],"metrics":{"deliv":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"presence","text":"gateway B"},{"node":"bob","text":"delivered 4021"},{"node":"alice","text":"double tick","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"GROUP","title":"One message, many inboxes","body":"In a group of 500 people, one message must reach 499 others. The group message is stored once. Then the service looks up all 499 routes, groups them by gateway, and queues one batch per gateway. Storage stays flat, but delivery work grows with group size, and so do receipts: big groups usually show only a total (\"read by 37\") or skip per-member receipts. That is why apps cap group size; huge broadcast channels let readers pull messages instead.","idea":"Store once, deliver many: fan-out cost scales with members, not with storage.","caption":"One stored message turns into hundreds of deliveries.","focus":["chat","store","presence","bus","gw2"],"flows":[{"from":"chat","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"1 copy"},{"from":"chat","to":"presence","kind":"request","label":"routes x499"},{"from":"chat","to":"bus","kind":"async","label":"1 batch / gateway"},{"from":"bus","to":"gw2","kind":"async","label":"batch per gateway"}],"metrics":{"deliv":"499"},"chips":[{"node":"chat","text":"499 recipients"},{"node":"store","text":"1 copy","tone":"green"},{"node":"bus","text":"batches by gateway"},{"node":"gw2","text":"push to member sockets"}]},{"tag":"OFFLINE","title":"Bob's phone is off","body":"Replay the same send, but Bob's phone is off when m1 (seq 4021) arrives. He has no socket, so the route table has no entry for him. Nothing is lost, because the message is already stored. The chat service asks the push provider (Apple's APNs or Google's FCM) to wake his phone with a notification. The push carries little or no text; it only says \"something new\". Push is best effort and can be delayed or dropped. Presence can also lag, so if no delivered receipt arrives within a few seconds, the service sends the push anyway.","idea":"The stored message is the truth; push is only a nudge to come and get it.","caption":"No route is found, so a push notification nudges Bob's phone.","focus":["chat","presence","push"],"flows":[{"from":"chat","to":"presence","kind":"request","label":"where is bob?"},{"from":"presence","to":"chat","kind":"fail","label":"not online"},{"from":"chat","to":"push","kind":"async","label":"wake bob"}],"metrics":{"deliv":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"presence","text":"no route","tone":"red"},{"node":"push","text":"wake-up push"},{"node":"bob","text":"offline","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"SYNC","title":"Catch up on reconnect","body":"Bob turns his phone on. It reconnects and tells the server the last sequence number it holds for each chat, say 4020. The server reads everything after that from the store and sends it in order. Sequence numbers make catch-up exact; timestamps would not, because clocks can disagree. The same trick repairs gaps: if Bob gets seq 4023 right after 4021, he knows 4022 is missing and asks for it. Real apps often also keep one per-user inbox counter, so reconnecting needs one question instead of one per chat.","idea":"Clients ask for everything after seq N, which makes sync idempotent and gap-free.","caption":"Bob asks for everything after seq 4020 and gets messages 4021 to 4030.","focus":["bob","gw2","chat","store"],"flows":[{"from":"bob","to":"gw2","kind":"request","label":"sync after 4020"},{"from":"gw2","to":"chat","kind":"request","label":"sync after 4020"},{"from":"chat","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"range read"},{"from":"store","to":"chat","kind":"response","label":"seq 4021-4030"},{"from":"chat","to":"gw2","kind":"response","label":"10 messages"},{"from":"gw2","to":"bob","kind":"response","label":"10 messages"}],"metrics":{"deliv":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"bob","text":"after 4020"},{"node":"store","text":"seq 4021-4030"},{"node":"gw2","text":"10 messages","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Presence without a storm","body":"The green dot means \"online\". Each app sends a heartbeat (a tiny \"I'm alive\" ping) every 30 seconds, and its route entry expires if two are missed, so crashed phones clean themselves up. Naively, 20 million users means about 670,000 refreshes a second. So each gateway collects its sockets' heartbeats and sends one batched refresh every few seconds. To cut writes too, a gateway can hold a single lease key that covers all its routes while renewed. The cost: a dot can lag by up to about 70 seconds. Presence is only fetched for chats on screen.","idea":"Treat presence as soft, expiring state and publish it only to people looking.","caption":"Gateways batch heartbeats into short-lived presence entries.","focus":["bob","gw2","chat","presence"],"flows":[{"from":"bob","to":"gw2","kind":"async","label":"ping every 30s"},{"from":"gw2","to":"chat","kind":"async","label":"batch alive"},{"from":"chat","to":"presence","kind":"request","label":"SET ttl=70s"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"gw2","text":"ping / 30 s"},{"node":"presence","text":"ttl 70 s"},{"node":"chat","text":"batched refresh"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"The whole path","body":"Here is the whole path. Persistent sockets on a gateway tier carry messages. The chat service stores each message with a sequence number, acks the sender, looks up the recipient's gateway, and delivers through a queue. Receipts flow back the same way. Groups fan out from one stored copy. Offline users get a push nudge and then sync by sequence number. Presence is soft, expiring state refreshed in batches.","idea":"Store first, route by lookup, and let sequence numbers repair every gap.","caption":"Every component and its job, in one picture.","focus":["alice","gw1","chat","store","presence","bus","push","gw2","bob"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"rate":"46000","gateways":"200","ack":"60","deliv":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"chat","text":"46,000 msgs/s"},{"node":"gw1","text":"200 gateways"},{"node":"push","text":"nudge only"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A gateway crashes while holding 100,000 sockets. What should the design guarantee about messages sent to those users?","options":["They are lost, because the sockets died with the gateway","They stay in the store; clients reconnect and sync after their last seq","They are retried forever, but only by the sender's app","They are moved into the other gateways' memory automatically"],"answer":1,"why":"Messages are persisted before being acked and delivery is only a nudge on top. After reconnecting to any gateway, each client asks for everything after its last sequence number."},{"q":"Why does the chat service write the message to the store before sending the sent tick to Alice?","options":["Writes are always faster than delivering to the recipient","So the message needs no client-generated ID at all","Because Bob must already be online to receive it","So a tick always means the message will not be lost"],"answer":3,"why":"If the tick were sent first and the server then crashed, Alice would believe it was sent while no copy existed. Persisting first makes the ack a durable promise."},{"q":"A group has 1,000 members and gets 5 messages per second. About how many deliveries per second must the system make, and what stays flat?","options":["About 5,000 deliveries/s; only 5 stored writes/s","About 5 deliveries/s; stored writes grow to 5,000/s","About 1,000 deliveries/s; only 1 stored write/s","About 5,000 deliveries/s and 5,000 stored writes/s"],"answer":0,"why":"Each message goes to roughly 1,000 recipients, so about 5,000 deliveries per second, but one stored copy per message keeps storage at 5 writes per second."},{"q":"Bob was offline and the push notification never arrived. When does he get his messages?","options":["Never, push is the only delivery route","When Alice resends them","When he next opens the app and syncs after his last seq","After the queue's retention expires"],"answer":2,"why":"Push is best effort and only a hint. The authoritative path is the sync request on reconnect, which reads the stored messages by sequence number."},{"q":"Why is presence implemented as heartbeats with a TTL instead of an online/offline event on every change?","options":["Events cannot cross gateways at all","TTLs are required by the WebSocket protocol","It guarantees that messages arrive in order","Crashes send no offline event, and fanning every flap out is costly"],"answer":3,"why":"A crashed phone cannot announce it left, so an expiring entry cleans itself up. Publishing every change to all contacts multiplies cost, so presence is fetched lazily."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"WebSocket","def":"A long-lived two-way connection started by an HTTP upgrade, letting the server push data without the client asking."},{"term":"Gateway tier","def":"Servers that only hold sockets (about 100k each as a rule of thumb) and forward messages; kept stateless apart from their connections."},{"term":"Route table","def":"A fast key-value map from user to the gateway holding their socket, with a TTL refreshed by heartbeats."},{"term":"Sequence number","def":"A per-conversation counter assigned on write. Gives ordering, gap detection, and exact catch-up with sync after N. Duplicates are dropped by message ID."},{"term":"At-least-once + idempotent","def":"Delivery may repeat, so receivers drop duplicates by message ID. Easier and safer than trying for exactly-once."},{"term":"Receipt cursor","def":"One read or delivered marker meaning everything up to seq N, instead of a receipt per message."},{"term":"Fan-out on delivery","def":"Store a group message once and queue it for each member's gateway (looked up in the route table); cost grows with member count."},{"term":"Push notification","def":"A best-effort wake-up through APNs or FCM for offline devices; never the source of truth."}],"lab":{"prompt":"Design a one-to-one and group chat service for 100 million daily users (about 20 million online at peak). Sketch the components, the message path, and the data model, then say what happens when a gateway dies.","requirements":["Messages from one sender in one conversation arrive in order","A message is never lost once the sender sees it as sent","Delivery to an online recipient in well under a second","Groups up to 500 members","Offline users receive everything when they return","Handle roughly 46,000 messages per second on average and 3x at peak"],"hints":["Estimate gateway count from concurrent connections divided by sockets per server","Choose a partition key for storage so one conversation's messages sit together, ordered by sequence number","Decide what the ack means and when it is sent","Think about how a client detects a gap or a duplicate","Keep presence as expiring soft state rather than durable data","Estimate storage per day and per year, and decide retention"],"checklist":["Persistent connection tier separated from the message-handling service","Route table from user to gateway with expiry","Storage partition key and how sequence numbers are allocated safely","Message stored with a per-conversation sequence number before the ack","Receipts defined as cursors that flow back along the same path","Group fan-out plan and a size limit","Offline path covering push and sync after last seq","Failure story for a dead gateway"]},"isNew":false},{"slug":"design-news-feed","title":"Design a News Feed","subtitle":"Watch one post reach a million timelines, and see why celebrities force a hybrid of push and pull.","minutes":14,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Fan-out on write (push) versus fan-out on read (pull), and what each costs","Why a feed is stored as a per-user list of post IDs in a cache","The celebrity problem and the hybrid that fixes it","Where the ranking stage sits and why it only scores a few hundred candidates","How to size writes per second and cache memory from a few assumptions"],"nodes":[{"id":"author","label":"Author app","kind":"client","x":8,"y":20},{"id":"post","label":"Post service","kind":"service","x":28,"y":20},{"id":"queue","label":"Fan-out queue","kind":"queue","x":48,"y":20},{"id":"worker","label":"Fan-out workers","kind":"worker","x":68,"y":20},{"id":"store","label":"Post store","kind":"db","x":28,"y":50},{"id":"cache","label":"Feed cache","kind":"cache","x":86,"y":47},{"id":"reader","label":"Reader app","kind":"client","x":8,"y":80},{"id":"feed","label":"Feed service","kind":"service","x":48,"y":80},{"id":"ranker","label":"Ranker","kind":"service","x":68,"y":80}],"edges":[{"from":"author","to":"post"},{"from":"post","to":"store"},{"from":"post","to":"queue"},{"from":"queue","to":"worker"},{"from":"worker","to":"cache"},{"from":"reader","to":"feed"},{"from":"feed","to":"cache"},{"from":"feed","to":"store"},{"from":"feed","to":"ranker"}],"metrics":[{"id":"writes","label":"Writes / post","unit":""},{"id":"read","label":"Feed load","unit":"ms"},{"id":"cache","label":"Feed cache","unit":"GB"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"What does opening the app need?","body":"You open the app and expect recent posts from the people you follow, newest and most relevant first, in under half a second. Assume 100 million daily users, about 200 followers each, and one post per user per day. That is roughly 100 million posts and 1 billion feed loads a day, so reads outnumber writes about 10 to 1. The design question is when to do the work of building each person's feed: when someone posts, or when someone opens the app?","idea":"A feed is a read-heavy join between who you follow and what they posted; the design question is when to compute it.","caption":"Many authors, many readers, and one costly job: joining them.","focus":["author","reader"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"NAIVE","title":"Fan-out on read (pull)","body":"The simplest design builds your feed when you open the app, called fan-out on read or \"pull\". The feed service gets your list of followed accounts, asks the post store for each one's recent posts, merges them, and returns the top page. Writing is trivial: one row per post. But each feed load becomes hundreds of lookups. At about 11,000 feed loads per second, the post store gets crushed and each load is slow.","idea":"Pull makes writes cheap and reads expensive, which is backwards when reads dominate.","caption":"Every feed load queries the post store once per followed account.","focus":["reader","feed","store"],"flows":[{"from":"reader","to":"feed","kind":"request","label":"GET /feed"},{"from":"feed","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"posts of 200 users"},{"from":"store","to":"feed","kind":"response","label":"merge + sort"},{"from":"feed","to":"reader","kind":"response","label":"feed page"}],"metrics":{"writes":"1","read":"800"},"chips":[{"node":"feed","text":"800 ms","tone":"red"},{"node":"store","text":"11k loads/s"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Fan-out on write (push)","body":"Flip it: do the work when a post is created. This is fan-out on write, or \"push\". The author saves a post, and a background job later copies the new post's ID into every follower's ready-made feed list. A queue (a waiting line for jobs) holds that job, so the post service answers \"201 Created\" right away and the author never waits. A 200-follower author costs 200 small writes, and none of them slow down any reader.","idea":"Push moves the cost from the many reads to the fewer writes, and does it in the background.","caption":"A new post is saved and a fan-out job is queued; the author gets 201 Created.","focus":["author","post","store","queue"],"flows":[{"from":"author","to":"post","kind":"request","label":"new post"},{"from":"post","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"save post"},{"from":"post","to":"queue","kind":"async","label":"fan-out job"},{"from":"post","to":"author","kind":"response","label":"201 Created"}],"metrics":{"writes":"200"},"chips":[{"node":"post","text":"201 Created","tone":"green"},{"node":"queue","text":"fan-out job"},{"node":"queue","text":"200 writes","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Workers fill the feed cache","body":"Workers take the job off the queue, look up the author's followers, and add the post ID to each follower's list in the feed cache (fast in-memory storage). Each list keeps only the latest 500 IDs. We store IDs, not whole posts: 500 IDs of 8 bytes is about 4 KB per user, so 100 million users need roughly 400 GB, which fits on a modest cache cluster. At 100 million posts a day and 200 followers each, that is about 230,000 cache writes per second on average.","idea":"Feeds are lists of post IDs in memory; the cap keeps memory bounded and the writes cheap.","caption":"Workers write the new post's ID into each follower's list.","focus":["queue","worker","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"queue","to":"worker","kind":"async","label":"job"},{"from":"worker","to":"cache","kind":"async","label":"push ID x200"}],"metrics":{"cache":"400"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"500 IDs / user"},{"node":"cache","text":"≈ 400 GB","at":"bottom"},{"node":"worker","text":"230k writes/s"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Reading becomes a lookup","body":"Now opening the app is cheap. The feed service reads your ID list from the cache in a millisecond or two, then fetches the post contents for the top IDs in one batched request to the post store, and returns them. The list was built ahead of time, so the cost no longer depends on how many people you follow. Posts from deleted or blocked accounts are filtered out when you read, so a delete does not require rewriting every list.","idea":"Push turns a feed load into a cache read plus a batched fetch of post bodies.","caption":"The feed service reads a ready-made ID list, then fetches the post bodies.","focus":["reader","feed","cache","store"],"flows":[{"from":"reader","to":"feed","kind":"request","label":"GET /feed"},{"from":"feed","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"my post IDs"},{"from":"cache","to":"feed","kind":"response","label":"500 IDs"},{"from":"feed","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"fetch bodies"},{"from":"store","to":"feed","kind":"response","label":"post bodies"},{"from":"feed","to":"reader","kind":"response","label":"feed page"}],"metrics":{"read":"60"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"500 IDs · 1-2 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"feed","text":"60 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"store","text":"batched fetch"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The celebrity problem","body":"Push breaks for accounts with huge audiences. One post from a user with 50 million followers means 50 million cache writes. At a few hundred thousand writes per second, that is minutes of backlog, which delays everyone else's posts. Most of those writes are wasted on followers who will not open the app soon. One account can create a write spike bigger than the whole system's normal load.","idea":"Push cost scales with follower count, so a few huge accounts can dominate total write load.","caption":"One celebrity post triggers tens of millions of writes, and the queue backs up.","focus":["author","post","queue","worker","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"author","to":"post","kind":"request","label":"celebrity post"},{"from":"post","to":"queue","kind":"async","label":"50M followers"},{"from":"queue","to":"worker","kind":"fail","label":"backlog grows"},{"from":"worker","to":"cache","kind":"fail","label":"50M writes"}],"metrics":{"writes":"50000000"},"chips":[{"node":"queue","text":"50M followers","tone":"red"},{"node":"worker","text":"backlog grows","tone":"red"},{"node":"cache","text":"50M writes","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"The hybrid","body":"Real systems mix both. Normal accounts use push. Accounts above a follower threshold, say 100,000 (a tunable rule of thumb), skip fan-out and are only saved. When you open the app, the feed service takes your pre-built list from the cache, pulls recent posts from the few celebrities you follow directly from the post store, and merges the two. Pull is cheap here because you follow only a handful of celebrities, and their latest posts are hot and easy to cache.","idea":"Push for the many small authors, pull for the few huge ones, merge at read time.","caption":"The feed service merges the cached ID list with fresh celebrity posts.","focus":["reader","feed","cache","store"],"flows":[{"from":"reader","to":"feed","kind":"request","label":"GET /feed"},{"from":"feed","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"pushed IDs"},{"from":"cache","to":"feed","kind":"response","label":"500 IDs"},{"from":"feed","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"celebrity posts"},{"from":"store","to":"feed","kind":"response","label":"recent posts"},{"from":"feed","to":"reader","kind":"response","label":"merged feed"}],"metrics":{"writes":"200","read":"70"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"pushed IDs"},{"node":"store","text":"celebrity pull"},{"node":"feed","text":"70 ms","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"The ranking stage","body":"A newest-first list is simple, but most products rank posts by how interesting they are. Scoring every post you could ever see is too slow, so ranking is a second stage. The feed service gathers a few hundred candidates (cached IDs plus celebrity posts). The ranker scores only those, using signals like recency, how often you interact with the author, and how popular the post is. It returns the top 20. Gathering candidates is cheap and wide; ranking is costly and narrow.","idea":"Candidate generation is cheap and wide; ranking is expensive and narrow, so keep them separate.","caption":"The ranker scores a few hundred candidates and returns the top page.","focus":["feed","ranker","reader"],"flows":[{"from":"feed","to":"ranker","kind":"request","label":"300 candidates"},{"from":"ranker","to":"feed","kind":"response","label":"top 20 scored"},{"from":"feed","to":"reader","kind":"response","label":"ranked feed"}],"metrics":{"read":"120"},"chips":[{"node":"ranker","text":"300 candidates"},{"node":"ranker","text":"top 20","at":"bottom"},{"node":"feed","text":"120 ms"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A cache node dies","body":"The feed cache is a copy built from other data, not the source of truth, so losing a node loses no posts. For affected users the feed service finds a missing list and rebuilds it by pulling recent posts from the people they follow, then saves the result back in the cache. A rebuild is slow, so it is rate limited and often done lazily, on the user's next visit. It also gives a cheap policy for inactive users: skip pushing to them and rebuild only if they return.","idea":"Treat feeds as rebuildable caches: pull is the fallback that makes push safe to lose.","caption":"A missing list is rebuilt with a slower pull, then cached again.","focus":["feed","cache","store"],"flows":[{"from":"feed","to":"cache","kind":"fail","label":"miss"},{"from":"feed","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"rebuild by pull"},{"from":"store","to":"feed","kind":"response","label":"recent posts"},{"from":"feed","to":"cache","kind":"replicate","label":"refill list"}],"metrics":{"read":"400"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"node lost","tone":"red"},{"node":"feed","text":"rebuild by pull"},{"node":"feed","text":"400 ms","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"When to do the work","body":"Pull builds feeds when you read: cheap writes, slow reads. Push builds them when someone posts, into a per-user list of IDs in cache: fast reads, but write cost grows with the number of followers. Celebrities break push, so the hybrid pushes for normal accounts and pulls for huge ones. A ranker then scores a few hundred candidates, and the cache can always be rebuilt from the post store.","idea":"Move work from the hot read path to the write path, except where the write cost explodes.","caption":"Push for most accounts, pull for celebrities, rank a shortlist.","focus":["author","post","store","queue","worker","cache","reader","feed","ranker"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"writes":"200","read":"120","cache":"400"},"chips":[{"node":"cache","text":"400 GB"},{"node":"feed","text":"120 ms"},{"node":"worker","text":"200 writes / post"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Reads outnumber writes about 10 to 1 and most users follow around 200 accounts. Why is fan-out on write usually preferred for these users?","options":["It uses less total storage than pulling","It does the merge work once per post, instead of once per feed load","It guarantees every follower sees the post instantly","It removes the need for a post store"],"answer":1,"why":"With many more reads than writes, doing the work once at write time (200 small inserts) beats redoing a 200-way merge on each of the 10 reads."},{"q":"An account with 30 million followers posts. Pure fan-out on write would do what?","options":["Make 30 million cache writes for one post, creating a large backlog","Make one write, because the cache is shared","Fail the post until followers come online","Have no effect on other users' posts"],"answer":0,"why":"Push cost equals follower count, so one post means about 30 million writes, delaying fan-out for everyone queued behind it."},{"q":"In the hybrid design, how does a user's feed include a celebrity's post?","options":["The celebrity's post is pushed to the user's list as usual","The feed service pulls recent celebrity posts at read time and merges them with the cached list","The user's app polls the celebrity's profile directly","Celebrity posts are shown only if the user refreshes twice"],"answer":1,"why":"Celebrities are skipped during fan-out. At read time the feed service fetches their latest posts and merges them with the pushed list."},{"q":"Why does the ranker score only a few hundred candidates instead of every post from followed accounts?","options":["Ranking models cannot handle more than 300 inputs","The cache can only hold 300 IDs per user","Users never scroll past 300 posts","Scoring is expensive per item, so a cheap first stage narrows the set within the latency budget"],"answer":3,"why":"Candidate generation is cheap and wide, ranking is costly per post. Limiting the scored set keeps the feed load within its latency budget."},{"q":"A feed cache node is lost. What is the right recovery approach?","options":["Restore from the ranker's output","Declare the affected users' posts lost and notify them","Rebuild each affected list by pulling from the post store, ideally lazily and rate limited","Stop all new posts until the node returns"],"answer":2,"why":"The cache is derived data. The post store is the source of truth, so lists can be rebuilt by pull, throttled so a rebuild storm does not overload the store."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Fan-out on write","def":"On each new post, insert its ID into every follower's feed list ahead of time. Fast reads, write cost grows with follower count."},{"term":"Fan-out on read","def":"Build the feed at request time by querying and merging the posts of everyone followed. Cheap writes, slow and costly reads."},{"term":"Celebrity problem","def":"Accounts with millions of followers make push write millions of entries per post. Handle them with pull instead."},{"term":"Hybrid feed","def":"Push for normal accounts, pull for accounts above a follower threshold, merged by the feed service at read time."},{"term":"Feed cache","def":"Per-user capped list of recent post IDs (not bodies) in memory, about 4 KB for 500 IDs. Derived data that can be rebuilt."},{"term":"Candidate generation","def":"The cheap first stage that gathers a few hundred possible posts before the ranker scores them."},{"term":"Ranking stage","def":"A model scoring candidates on recency, affinity and engagement, returning the top page. Costly per item, so run on a shortlist."},{"term":"Back-of-envelope","def":"100M posts/day is about 1,160 posts/s; times 200 followers is about 230K cache writes/s. Divide per-day numbers by 86,400."}],"lab":{"prompt":"Design the news feed for a social app with 100 million daily users. Users follow about 200 accounts on average, a small number of accounts have over 10 million followers, and a feed must load in under 500 ms at p99. Sketch the write path, the read path, and what is stored where.","requirements":["Posting returns quickly and does not wait for followers' feeds to update","Feed loads meet a 500 ms p99 target at roughly 12K reads per second average","Accounts with tens of millions of followers must not stall fan-out for everyone else","Feeds can be ranked, not only sorted by time","Losing a feed cache node must not lose any posts","State your assumptions and compute writes per second and cache memory"],"hints":["Convert per day to per second by dividing by about 86,400, then multiply posts per second by average followers.","Store post IDs, not post bodies, in the feed list; keep bodies in the post store and batch the lookup.","Pick a follower threshold for pull accounts, and say what happens to a user who follows both kinds.","Split feed building into candidate generation and ranking so each has its own latency budget.","Ask what to do for inactive users: do you push to them at all?"],"checklist":["Post path: save to post store first, then enqueue fan-out asynchronously","Feed list stored as a capped per-user list of post IDs in cache with a size estimate","Hybrid rule: push for normal accounts, pull and merge for celebrity accounts","Ranking stage scores a few hundred candidates and returns a top page","Recovery plan: rebuild lists from the post store, throttled","Numbers shown: about 1.2K posts/s, about 230K push writes/s, about 400 GB of feed cache"]},"isNew":false},{"slug":"design-typeahead","title":"Design Search Autocomplete","subtitle":"Watch each keystroke become a sub-100 ms suggestion list, and see how the index behind it is built and refreshed.","minutes":14,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why a trie (prefix tree) fits prefix lookups, and why walking it per request is still too slow","How storing the top-k suggestions on every prefix node turns a query into one lookup","How search logs flow through a queue and batch job to rebuild the index","Why edge caching works so well for short prefixes, and what it costs in freshness","How to split a ~100 ms latency budget across client, network, edge and server"],"nodes":[{"id":"user","label":"Search box","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"edge","label":"Edge cache","kind":"cdn","x":28,"y":50},{"id":"svc","label":"Suggest service","kind":"service","x":48,"y":50},{"id":"index","label":"Top-k prefix index","kind":"cache","x":72,"y":20},{"id":"queue","label":"Search log stream","kind":"queue","x":48,"y":86},{"id":"builder","label":"Aggregate + build","kind":"worker","x":72,"y":86},{"id":"counts","label":"Query counts","kind":"storage","x":92,"y":55}],"edges":[{"from":"user","to":"edge"},{"from":"edge","to":"svc"},{"from":"svc","to":"index"},{"from":"svc","to":"queue"},{"from":"queue","to":"builder"},{"from":"builder","to":"counts"},{"from":"builder","to":"index"}],"metrics":[{"id":"latency","label":"Latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"hit","label":"Edge hit rate","unit":"%"},{"id":"qps","label":"Peak req/s","unit":"k"},{"id":"fresh","label":"Data age","unit":"min"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Suggest while they type","body":"A user types \"wea\" and expects \"weather\", \"weather tomorrow\" and \"wearable tech\" to appear before they type the next letter. Assume 5 billion searches a day. The app waits for a short typing pause, so each search triggers about 4 suggestion requests. That is roughly 20 billion requests a day: about 230,000 per second on average and perhaps 700,000 at peak. Each answer must feel instant, so we aim for under 100 ms from the request being sent to the list appearing.","idea":"Autocomplete is a read-heavy, latency-critical lookup: huge request volume, tiny responses, strict deadline.","caption":"Each pause in typing sends a prefix (the letters typed so far) to the system.","focus":["user"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"qps":"700"},"chips":[{"node":"user","text":"typed: wea"},{"node":"user","text":"100 ms target","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"A trie finds prefixes","body":"A trie (say \"try\") is a tree that stores words one letter per level, so every word starting with \"wea\" hangs below the same path w-e-a. Finding the spot for a prefix takes time proportional to the prefix length, not to the number of stored searches. That is exactly our question. But the spot only tells us where matches start. Collecting every completion below \"w\" would mean scanning millions of entries.","idea":"A trie makes 'find everything starting with X' cheap to locate, but expensive to enumerate and rank.","caption":"The index is a trie, but we cannot scan a whole subtree for every request.","focus":["index"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"index","text":"node for 'wea'"},{"node":"index","text":"subtree: millions","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Precompute top-k per prefix","body":"The fix is to do the ranking ahead of time. Every trie node stores its top k completions (k is about 10), already sorted by popularity. A request walks a few dozen letters at most and returns the list sitting on that node: no scanning, no sorting. The price is memory and build time, because each popular search is copied onto all of its prefixes. As a rule of thumb that is only a few GB per shard (one slice of the index), so it fits in RAM.","idea":"Trade space and offline work for speed: store the answer on each prefix so the read path is one lookup.","caption":"Each prefix node already holds its ranked suggestions.","focus":["index"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"index","text":"top 10 per node","tone":"green"},{"node":"index","text":"few GB / shard","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Most requests end at the edge","body":"The app sends a prefix like \"wea\" after a 100 to 150 ms typing pause (a \"debounce\"), cancelling any older request still in flight. The request goes to an edge cache, a cache server near the user. Short prefixes are typed by millions of people, so the same request (like /suggest?q=wea) arrives again and again. With a TTL (expiry time) of a few minutes, a hit rate around 90% is a fair rule of thumb, and this lesson uses it. A hit costs one short network hop.","idea":"Because short prefixes are asked by everyone, caching the response at the edge absorbs most of the load.","caption":"The edge cache answers a popular prefix directly.","focus":["user","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"user","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"GET q=wea"},{"from":"edge","to":"user","kind":"response","label":"10 suggestions"}],"metrics":{"latency":"35","hit":"90"},"chips":[{"node":"edge","text":"hit · 35 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"edge","kind":"progress","value":90,"text":"90%"},{"node":"user","text":"debounce 100-150 ms"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"A miss goes to the index","body":"Long or rare prefixes like \"weather in lisb\" are probably not in the edge cache, so the edge forwards them to the suggest service. The service looks the prefix up in the in-memory index and returns the stored list. There is no scan, so server time is about 1 to 5 ms. The edge saves the answer for next time. Only the remaining 10% of traffic, about 70,000 requests per second of the 700,000 peak, reaches this tier.","idea":"The origin sees only misses, and each miss is a single in-memory lookup.","caption":"On a miss, the edge asks the suggest service, which reads the index.","focus":["edge","svc","index"],"flows":[{"from":"edge","to":"svc","kind":"request","label":"miss: long prefix"},{"from":"svc","to":"index","kind":"request","label":"lookup prefix"},{"from":"index","to":"svc","kind":"response","label":"top-10 list"},{"from":"svc","to":"edge","kind":"response","label":"suggestions"}],"metrics":{"latency":"60","qps":"70"},"chips":[{"node":"edge","text":"miss"},{"node":"svc","text":"1-5 ms"},{"node":"svc","text":"70k req/s","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"PIPELINE","title":"Collect what people search","body":"Suggestions are only as good as the popularity data behind them. When a user actually submits a search, an event is appended to a log stream. This happens in the background, so a slow log never slows down suggestions. (The drawing shows it leaving the suggest service to keep the picture small.) We log completed searches, not every keystroke, which means less noise and less volume. Later, the logs must be cleaned so bots, abuse and private queries never become suggestions.","idea":"Feed the index from completed searches, written asynchronously so reads stay fast.","caption":"Each submitted search is logged to a stream, off the fast path.","focus":["svc","queue"],"flows":[{"from":"svc","to":"queue","kind":"async","label":"search submitted"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"queue","text":"completed searches"},{"node":"svc","text":"off critical path"}]},{"tag":"PIPELINE","title":"Aggregate and rebuild","body":"A batch job reads the log stream, filters spam and sensitive terms, and drops queries searched by only a handful of users so rare private strings never surface. It counts each query over a time window, often weighting recent searches higher. It saves the counts, then builds a new trie with top-k lists on every node, and ships it to the serving machines as a whole new version. A rebuild about every hour is common, done offline on separate machines.","idea":"Ranking is computed offline in batches, so the serving path never does aggregation.","caption":"A worker counts queries and builds a fresh index.","focus":["queue","builder","counts","index"],"flows":[{"from":"queue","to":"builder","kind":"async","label":"batch of events"},{"from":"builder","to":"counts","kind":"request","label":"write counts"},{"from":"builder","to":"index","kind":"replicate","label":"new index version"}],"metrics":{"fresh":"60"},"chips":[{"node":"builder","text":"hourly rebuild"},{"node":"counts","text":"counts written"},{"node":"index","text":"new version"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Freshness versus speed","body":"A breaking-news term can surge in minutes, yet an hourly rebuild plus a few minutes of edge caching can delay it by an hour or more. The worst case is what users notice. Teams close the gap with a small \"trending\" list, updated about every minute from a stream counter and merged into results at request time. That brings the age of spikes down to about 5 minutes while the big index stays hourly. A new index is loaded beside the old one and switched atomically, so nobody sees a half-built trie.","idea":"Batch rebuilds give stable, cheap ranking; a small fast side-channel handles breaking trends.","caption":"Fresher data costs more work, so a small fast list handles trends.","focus":["builder","index","svc"],"flows":[{"from":"builder","to":"index","kind":"async","label":"trending list"},{"from":"builder","to":"index","kind":"replicate","label":"swap version"},{"from":"svc","to":"index","kind":"request","label":"merge trending"}],"metrics":{"fresh":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"builder","text":"trending · 1 min"},{"node":"index","text":"atomic swap"},{"node":"svc","text":"age ≈ 5 min","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"BUDGET","title":"Spend the 100 ms","body":"Here is a budget for a cache miss, measured from request sent to list drawn. The typing pause comes on top, so many apps use a short pause (100 to 150 ms) or skip it for the first letters. The path costs roughly 30 ms round trip to the edge, 20 ms round trip from edge to origin, 2 to 5 ms for the lookup, and a few ms to draw the list, about 60 ms in all. An edge hit skips the origin trip and takes about 35 ms. Network distance is the biggest cost, so the edge cache matters more than a faster trie.","idea":"Latency is dominated by distance; the index lookup is the cheap part.","caption":"Edge hit takes about 35 ms; a full miss takes about 60 ms.","focus":["user","edge","svc","index"],"flows":[{"from":"user","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"30 ms"},{"from":"edge","to":"svc","kind":"request","label":"20 ms"},{"from":"svc","to":"index","kind":"request","label":"3 ms"}],"metrics":{"latency":"60"},"chips":[{"node":"edge","text":"30 ms RTT"},{"node":"svc","text":"20 ms RTT"},{"node":"index","text":"3 ms"},{"node":"user","text":"≈ 60 ms miss"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"The whole design","body":"Reading: the app waits for a typing pause, the edge answers if it can, and otherwise the service does one lookup of a precomputed top-k list in a memory-resident trie. Writing: log completed searches, aggregate and filter them in batches, build a new index, and swap it in atomically, with a small trending list merged in for breaking queries. To scale, shard the index by prefix range and replicate each shard. The key move is shifting expensive work from request time to build time.","idea":"Make reads trivial by precomputing answers, and refresh them on a schedule you can afford.","caption":"A fast read path on top, a slower build path underneath.","focus":["user","edge","svc","index","queue","builder","counts"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"latency":"35","hit":"90","qps":"70","fresh":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"edge","kind":"progress","value":90,"text":"90%"},{"node":"index","text":"top-10 per prefix"},{"node":"user","text":"35 ms hit","tone":"green"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why store the top-k list on every trie node instead of computing it when a request arrives?","options":["It lets the trie use less memory","Finding all completions under a short prefix means scanning a huge subtree, which is far too slow per request","Tries cannot be traversed at request time","It removes the need to log searches"],"answer":1,"why":"A prefix like 'w' has millions of descendants. Precomputing the ranked list trades memory and build time for a constant-time read."},{"q":"Edge hit rate for short prefixes is much higher than for long ones. What is the best explanation?","options":["Edge servers have more memory for short strings","Long prefixes are blocked by CDNs","Short prefixes are never updated","Short prefixes are shared by many users, so the same request repeats often"],"answer":3,"why":"Few distinct short prefixes exist and everyone types them, so the same cache key is requested constantly. Long prefixes are far more varied."},{"q":"A new celebrity name starts trending, but suggestions miss it for over an hour. Which change best targets this without rebuilding the whole index more often?","options":["Add more edge locations","Raise k from 10 to 100","Add a small, frequently updated trending list merged at request time","Store the trie on disk instead of memory"],"answer":2,"why":"The delay comes from batch rebuild interval and cache TTL. A small fast-updating side list covers spikes cheaply while the big index stays batch-built."},{"q":"Why is the search event written to the log stream asynchronously?","options":["So the suggestion response does not wait on logging, and a slow or failing log cannot hurt latency","Because queues guarantee ranking order","Because the trie requires a write on every read","To reduce the edge hit rate"],"answer":0,"why":"Logging is not needed to answer the user. Keeping it off the critical path protects the latency budget."},{"q":"The p99 latency is 300 ms for users far from your region, while lookup time is 3 ms. What is the most effective fix?","options":["Replace the trie with a faster data structure","Increase k so fewer requests are needed","Rebuild the index more often","Serve cached suggestions from edges near those users"],"answer":3,"why":"Time is dominated by network distance, not the lookup. Moving answers closer to users removes the biggest term."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Trie","def":"A tree where each edge is a character, so strings sharing a prefix share a path. Finding a prefix costs time proportional to its length."},{"term":"Top-k per node","def":"Each trie node stores its k best completions (k around 10), ranked in advance, so a request needs no subtree scan or sort."},{"term":"Debounce","def":"The client waits a short pause (about 100 to 150 ms) after the last keystroke before sending, and cancels stale requests, cutting traffic a lot."},{"term":"Edge caching","def":"Short prefixes repeat across users, so caching responses near users with a TTL of minutes absorbs most traffic. Cost: results lag the index."},{"term":"Offline build","def":"Logs, aggregation, filtering and trie construction run in batches off the serving path, then ship as a new version swapped in atomically."},{"term":"Sharding by prefix","def":"Split the index by prefix range (for example a-c, d-f) across machines, with replicas for load and failover. Watch for skewed popular ranges."},{"term":"Freshness gap","def":"Index age plus cache TTL is how stale suggestions can be. Fix spikes with a small fast trending list rather than constant full rebuilds."},{"term":"Latency budget","def":"Split a target (about 100 ms) across stages. Network distance usually dominates; the in-memory lookup is only a few ms."}],"lab":{"prompt":"Design autocomplete for a search engine with 5 billion searches a day. Suggestions must appear within 100 ms of the request being sent (after a short client-side debounce), rank by popularity, and reflect new trends reasonably quickly. Sketch the read path, the build path, and how you would shard and refresh the index.","requirements":["Return up to 10 ranked suggestions for any prefix","End-to-end p95 latency under 100 ms at about 700k requests per second peak","Rank by popularity with some recency weighting","Keep offensive, private and bot-generated queries out of suggestions","Surface a new trending term within roughly 15 minutes","Survive the loss of a serving machine or a failed index build"],"hints":["Start from the read path and ask what answer could be precomputed so a request is one lookup.","Estimate cache hit rate for short prefixes first; it decides how big the origin tier must be.","Treat the build as a separate batch pipeline fed by completed searches, not keystrokes.","Think about how you would roll out a new index version safely and roll it back.","Shard by prefix range, but check how uneven the traffic across ranges would be."],"checklist":["Client debounces and cancels stale requests","Edge cache with sensible TTL for popular prefixes","Trie with precomputed top-k per node, held in memory","Async log stream plus batch aggregation and filtering","Atomic index swap and a plan for stale data","Sharding and replication plan, with failure behavior","Latency budget that adds up to under 100 ms"]},"isNew":true},{"slug":"design-notifications","title":"Design a Notification System","subtitle":"Send one event out as push, email, and SMS, and watch preferences, dedup, queues, retries, and rate limits keep it safe.","minutes":16,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why notifications are sent through per-channel queues instead of directly from the caller","How a preference service and quiet hours decide who gets what, before anything is queued","How idempotency keys stop duplicate sends, and why delivery is at-least-once","How retries with backoff, dead-letter queues, and rate limits survive flaky providers","How to size the system from daily volume to peak sends per second"],"nodes":[{"id":"events","label":"Upstream services","kind":"service","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"api","label":"Notification API","kind":"gateway","x":28,"y":50},{"id":"pref","label":"Preference service","kind":"service","x":28,"y":14},{"id":"redis","label":"Dedup + rate limits","kind":"cache","x":28,"y":86},{"id":"pushq","label":"Push queue","kind":"queue","x":52,"y":22},{"id":"emailq","label":"Email queue","kind":"queue","x":52,"y":50},{"id":"smsq","label":"SMS queue","kind":"queue","x":52,"y":78},{"id":"workers","label":"Channel workers","kind":"worker","x":74,"y":50},{"id":"provider","label":"APNs/FCM/SES/SMS","kind":"external","x":92,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"events","to":"api"},{"from":"api","to":"pref"},{"from":"api","to":"redis"},{"from":"api","to":"pushq"},{"from":"api","to":"emailq"},{"from":"api","to":"smsq"},{"from":"pushq","to":"workers"},{"from":"emailq","to":"workers"},{"from":"smsq","to":"workers"},{"from":"workers","to":"redis"},{"from":"workers","to":"provider"}],"metrics":[{"id":"ingest","label":"Accepted","unit":"events/s"},{"id":"depth","label":"Queue backlog","unit":"msgs"},{"id":"sent","label":"Provider OK","unit":"%"},{"id":"dupes","label":"Duplicates","unit":"sent"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One event, three channels","body":"A user's order ships. The shipping service wants to tell them: a push notification to their phone, an email with the tracking link, maybe an SMS. Assume 10 million users and about 50 million notifications a day, which is roughly 600 a second on average and about 10 times that in a burst. How do you deliver all of it once, on the right channel, without overloading a provider or spamming anyone?","idea":"A notification system is a pipeline that decides whether, where, and when to send, then sends reliably.","caption":"An upstream service has something to tell a user.","focus":["events"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"ingest":"600"},"chips":[{"node":"events","text":"600 / s avg"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Accept fast, send later","body":"The shipping service calls the Notification API with an event: a user id, a template, and some data. The API validates it, runs the checks in the next steps (preferences and duplicates), saves the messages safely in queues (waiting lines for messages), and only then answers 202 Accepted, meaning \"received, not yet sent\". This takes tens of milliseconds. The API never waits for the provider, because sending is slow and flaky (100 ms to several seconds) and must not hold up the caller.","idea":"Decouple the caller from delivery: acknowledge once the event is durably stored, never after the slow send.","caption":"The event reaches the API, which validates it before doing anything else.","focus":["events","api"],"flows":[{"from":"events","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"order_shipped"}],"metrics":{"ingest":"600"},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"queued first"},{"node":"api","text":"tens of ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Ask the preference service","body":"Before queuing anything, the API asks the preference service what this user wants. Is push allowed for shipping updates? Did she unsubscribe from email? Is it 3 a.m. in her time zone? If so, non-urgent sends go to a delay queue until quiet hours end. Opt-outs and legal consent live here too. Checking first avoids queuing what we may not send. For marketing messages, workers re-check the opt-out flag just before sending, so an unsubscribe or an SMS \"STOP\" takes effect at once.","idea":"Preferences are a gate in front of the queues: opt-outs, channel choice, and quiet hours are decided before any work is queued.","caption":"The preference service answers: push and email yes, SMS no.","focus":["api","pref"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"pref","kind":"request","label":"prefs for u42?"},{"from":"pref","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"push, email"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"pref","text":"push + email ok"},{"node":"pref","text":"quiet hours: delay","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Dedup with an idempotency key","body":"Upstream services retry, and message brokers redeliver, so the same event can arrive twice. To catch repeats, the API builds a key from user, template and event id, then runs one atomic Redis command, SET ... NX (set only if the key does not exist). It stores \"in progress\" with a short expiry. Once the message is queued, the key becomes \"done\" for 24 hours. A retry that finds \"done\" is dropped. If the API crashed midway, the short expiry lapses and the retry resumes. Without this, one retried order event means two emails.","idea":"A two-state idempotency key makes a repeated request harmless without ever losing the first one.","caption":"The key has not been seen before, so the event continues.","focus":["api","redis"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"redis","kind":"request","label":"SET NX EX"},{"from":"redis","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"new, go"}],"metrics":{"dupes":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"redis","text":"new · go","tone":"green"},{"node":"redis","text":"SET NX · short TTL"},{"node":"redis","text":"done · 24 h","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"One queue per channel","body":"The API renders a small message for each allowed channel and puts it on that channel's own queue: push and email here. Once they are safely queued, it marks the dedup key done and answers the caller with 202 Accepted. Separate queues matter because channels behave differently: push is fast and bulk, email is slower, and SMS is costly and tightly limited. If the SMS provider stalls, only its queue grows while push and email keep flowing.","idea":"Per-channel queues isolate failures and let each channel scale and throttle on its own.","caption":"Messages split into one queue per channel, then the caller gets its 202.","focus":["events","api","pushq","emailq","redis"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"pushq","kind":"async","label":"push msg"},{"from":"api","to":"emailq","kind":"async","label":"email msg"},{"from":"api","to":"redis","kind":"request","label":"mark done, 24h"},{"from":"api","to":"events","kind":"response","label":"202 Accepted"}],"metrics":{"depth":"300"},"chips":[{"node":"pushq","text":"push msg"},{"node":"emailq","text":"email msg"},{"node":"redis","text":"done · 24 h"},{"node":"api","text":"202 Accepted","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Workers send, within limits","body":"Channel workers take messages from a queue and call the provider: APNs and FCM (Apple's and Google's push services), an email service, or an SMS gateway. Before each call, a worker takes a token from a token bucket in Redis. The bucket refills at a steady rate and each send spends one token. If it is empty, the worker waits. This keeps us under provider limits and SMS costs. Per-user caps (for example 3 marketing pushes a day) stop one person being flooded. The provider then replies that it accepted the message.","idea":"Rate limits protect the provider relationship, the budget, and each user; workers pull at the speed the limits allow.","caption":"Workers take a token to stay within limits, then call the provider.","focus":["emailq","workers","redis","provider"],"flows":[{"from":"emailq","to":"workers","kind":"async","label":"pull batch"},{"from":"workers","to":"redis","kind":"request","label":"take token"},{"from":"redis","to":"workers","kind":"response","label":"token granted"},{"from":"workers","to":"provider","kind":"request","label":"send email"},{"from":"provider","to":"workers","kind":"response","label":"250 OK"}],"metrics":{"depth":"40","sent":"99"},"chips":[{"node":"redis","text":"token granted","tone":"green"},{"node":"provider","text":"250 OK","tone":"green"},{"node":"provider","kind":"progress","value":99,"text":"99%","tone":"green"},{"node":"emailq","text":"backlog 40"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The provider fails","body":"Now the email provider returns 503 errors. Only temporary errors are retried (5xx, 429 with Retry-After, timeouts); permanent 4xx errors like a bad address are not. The worker puts the message back on the queue with a delay that doubles from 1 s up to about 5 minutes, plus random jitter so thousands of retries do not arrive at once. A circuit breaker pauses workers while the provider is down. Messages expire by type: an OTP (one-time code) is stale in minutes, a shipping notice can wait hours. Past expiry or the attempt cap, a message goes to a dead-letter queue, a holding area to replay after recovery, so nothing is silently lost.","idea":"Retry transient errors with capped backoff and jitter, expire stale messages, and park the rest in a dead-letter queue.","caption":"Failed sends go back on the queue with a growing delay.","focus":["workers","provider","emailq"],"flows":[{"from":"workers","to":"provider","kind":"request","label":"send email"},{"from":"provider","to":"workers","kind":"fail","label":"503"},{"from":"workers","to":"emailq","kind":"async","label":"retry in 4s"}],"metrics":{"depth":"9000","sent":"71"},"chips":[{"node":"provider","text":"503","tone":"red"},{"node":"provider","kind":"progress","value":71,"text":"71%","tone":"red"},{"node":"emailq","text":"backlog 9,000","tone":"red"},{"node":"workers","text":"retry in 4 s"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"At-least-once means duplicates","body":"A worker sends the email, then crashes before telling the queue it finished. The queue redelivers the message, so a second email could go out. Exactly-once delivery over a network is not achievable here, so we choose at-least-once and shrink duplicates. Before sending, the worker marks the notification id \"sending\" in Redis with a short lease, and \"sent\" once the provider accepts. A redelivery that finds \"sent\" skips. One that finds only an expired \"sending\" tries again, using the provider's idempotency key if it has one. A rare duplicate beats a lost message.","idea":"Pick at-least-once delivery and make sending idempotent; a rare duplicate beats a lost notification.","caption":"The redelivered message finds \"sent\" in Redis and is skipped.","focus":["workers","redis","emailq"],"flows":[{"from":"emailq","to":"workers","kind":"async","label":"redelivered"},{"from":"workers","to":"redis","kind":"request","label":"already sent?"},{"from":"redis","to":"workers","kind":"response","label":"yes, skip"}],"metrics":{"dupes":"0","depth":"2500","sent":"93"},"chips":[{"node":"emailq","text":"redelivered"},{"node":"redis","text":"sent → skip","tone":"green"},{"node":"workers","text":"duplicates: 0","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A flash sale spike","body":"Marketing launches a promo to 5 million users. A campaign job turns it into per-user events at a controlled rate, about 8,000 pushes per second against a 600 per second average. The queues absorb the burst, so the API stays fast. Workers drain the push queue at their rate limit, and you add workers when the oldest message gets too old. Priority matters too: password resets and payment alerts go on a high-priority queue, so a promo never delays them.","idea":"Queues turn a spike into a backlog; separate priorities keep urgent messages ahead of bulk ones.","caption":"A backlog builds in the queue, then drains at the allowed rate.","focus":["events","api","pushq","workers"],"flows":[{"from":"events","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"campaign batch"},{"from":"api","to":"pushq","kind":"async","label":"8k/s burst"},{"from":"pushq","to":"workers","kind":"async","label":"drain"}],"metrics":{"ingest":"8000","depth":"120000","sent":"97"},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"8,000 push / s"},{"node":"pushq","text":"backlog 120,000","tone":"red"},{"node":"workers","text":"drain at limit"},{"node":"provider","kind":"progress","value":97,"text":"97%"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"The whole pipeline","body":"Accept the event fast, check preferences, dedup with an idempotency key, then queue per channel. Workers send within rate limits, retry with backoff, park failures in a dead-letter queue, and guard against duplicates at send time. Watch queue age, delivery rate and provider errors per channel to see which stage is hurting. Provider feedback (bounces, complaints, dead push tokens) flows back into preferences so bad addresses stop being targeted.","idea":"Gate, dedup, queue per channel, send within limits, retry safely: each stage removes one failure mode.","caption":"Each stage guards against one kind of failure.","focus":["events","api","pref","redis","pushq","emailq","smsq","workers","provider"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"ingest":"600","depth":"40","sent":"99","dupes":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"provider","kind":"progress","value":99,"text":"99%","tone":"green"},{"node":"redis","text":"duplicates: 0","tone":"green"},{"node":"events","text":"600 / s avg"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the Notification API return 202 Accepted and put messages on queues instead of calling the email provider directly?","options":["Queues make emails arrive faster than direct calls","Provider calls are slow and flaky, so the caller should not wait on them, and a queue lets us buffer and retry","Providers refuse direct calls from application servers","It lets us skip the preference check"],"answer":1,"why":"Sending is slow and can fail. A queue decouples the caller, absorbs bursts, and gives a place to retry. It does not make a single email faster."},{"q":"The email provider has an outage for 10 minutes. Which design keeps push notifications flowing normally?","options":["A single shared queue with FIFO order","Sending push and email in the same worker call","Retrying email immediately in a tight loop","One queue and worker pool per channel"],"answer":3,"why":"Per-channel queues isolate failures: the email backlog grows while push workers keep draining their own queue."},{"q":"A worker sends an email, then crashes before acknowledging the queue message. What does an at-least-once system do, and how do we limit the harm?","options":["The message is lost; add more workers","It sends exactly once by design, so nothing is needed","It redelivers the message; a send marker keyed by notification id lets the next worker skip the duplicate","It moves the message to the dead-letter queue immediately"],"answer":2,"why":"Queues redeliver unacknowledged messages. A two-state send marker (sending, then sent) lets the next worker skip a finished send, while an expired 'sending' is retried, favoring a rare duplicate over a loss."},{"q":"Retries after a provider outage all fire at the same moment and knock the provider over again. What is the best fix?","options":["Retry as fast as possible to finish sooner","Exponential backoff with random jitter and a maximum attempt count","Never retry; drop the messages","Retry every message exactly every 10 seconds"],"answer":1,"why":"Backoff spreads load over time, jitter prevents synchronized retry waves, and a cap plus dead-letter queue stops endless retrying."},{"q":"A marketing blast of 5 million messages is queued. Which step best protects password-reset notifications from long delays?","options":["Use a separate high-priority queue that workers drain first","Give each user a bigger rate limit","Add more workers to the shared bulk queue only","Increase the retry count"],"answer":0,"why":"Priority queues keep urgent, time-sensitive messages from waiting behind bulk traffic, even when the bulk backlog is large."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Idempotency key","def":"A unique id for an event or send. Stored in two states (in progress, then done) with an expiry; a second request with a done key is ignored, and a crashed first attempt can resume."},{"term":"At-least-once delivery","def":"Messages are redelivered until acknowledged, so none are lost but duplicates are possible. Handle duplicates with idempotent sends."},{"term":"Exponential backoff + jitter","def":"Wait longer after each failure (1s, 2s, 4s...) plus a random offset so many retries do not synchronize."},{"term":"Dead-letter queue (DLQ)","def":"Holds messages that exceeded their retry cap, so they can be inspected or replayed instead of vanishing."},{"term":"Token bucket","def":"Rate limiter: tokens refill at a steady rate, each send takes one, and an empty bucket means wait. Allows small bursts."},{"term":"Quiet hours","def":"A user setting that delays non-urgent notifications during certain local hours. Urgent types such as security alerts may bypass it."},{"term":"Queue age","def":"Often a better alert signal than depth, because it shows user-visible delay."},{"term":"Sizing rule of thumb","def":"50M a day is about 580 per second average; plan for 10x peaks and size workers from provider throughput and latency."}],"lab":{"prompt":"Design the notification system for a marketplace with 20M users sending 100M notifications a day across push, email, and SMS. Sketch the components, data flow, and failure handling on paper.","requirements":["Accept events from many internal services and respond in under 50 ms","Respect per-user channel preferences, opt-outs, and quiet hours","Never send the same notification twice in normal operation, even when callers retry","Survive a provider outage without losing messages","Deliver urgent notifications such as security codes within 10 seconds, even during a promo blast","Stay within each provider's rate limits and the SMS budget"],"hints":["Compute average and peak sends per second first: 100M a day is about 1,160 per second, so plan for 10x or more at peak.","Put the preference check and idempotency check before queuing, so we avoid queuing work we cannot send.","Use one queue per channel, and a separate high-priority queue for urgent types.","Retry with exponential backoff and jitter, cap attempts, and add a dead-letter queue.","Rate limit per provider, and consider per-user limits so one user is not flooded with ten messages in a minute.","Decide what happens if Redis is unavailable: fail open and risk duplicates, or fail closed and delay."],"checklist":["Is the caller decoupled from delivery by a queue, with preferences and opt-outs checked before anything is queued?","Do both an idempotency key at the API and a send marker at the worker exist?","Does each channel have its own queue and worker pool?","Are retries bounded, with backoff, jitter, and a dead-letter queue?","Is there a priority path for urgent notifications?","Do you monitor queue age, delivery rate, and provider error rate per channel?","Are there per-user frequency caps?","Is there a fallback provider or channel when one provider is down?"]},"isNew":true},{"slug":"design-video-streaming","title":"Design Video Streaming","subtitle":"Follow one uploaded video through transcoding and segmenting, then watch a player pull it from a CDN and adapt to bandwidth.","minutes":16,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why uploads go straight to object storage and are processed asynchronously","How a transcoding pipeline turns one source file into a ladder of renditions","How HLS and DASH split video into short segments listed in a manifest","How a player switches quality mid-stream without the server being involved","Why the CDN carries almost all the bytes and the metadata service carries almost none"],"nodes":[{"id":"creator","label":"Creator app","kind":"client","x":8,"y":18},{"id":"upload","label":"Upload service","kind":"service","x":30,"y":18},{"id":"queue","label":"Transcode queue","kind":"queue","x":52,"y":18},{"id":"workers","label":"Transcode workers","kind":"worker","x":74,"y":18},{"id":"raw","label":"Raw video store","kind":"storage","x":30,"y":50},{"id":"meta","label":"Metadata service","kind":"db","x":52,"y":50},{"id":"origin","label":"Segment store","kind":"storage","x":74,"y":50},{"id":"viewer","label":"Viewer player","kind":"client","x":10,"y":82},{"id":"cdn","label":"CDN edge","kind":"cdn","x":52,"y":82}],"edges":[{"from":"creator","to":"upload"},{"from":"creator","to":"raw"},{"from":"upload","to":"queue"},{"from":"upload","to":"meta"},{"from":"queue","to":"workers"},{"from":"raw","to":"workers"},{"from":"workers","to":"origin"},{"from":"workers","to":"meta"},{"from":"viewer","to":"meta"},{"from":"viewer","to":"cdn"},{"from":"cdn","to":"origin"}],"metrics":[{"id":"source","label":"Source size","unit":"MB"},{"id":"renditions","label":"Renditions","unit":"versions"},{"id":"startup","label":"Start delay","unit":"ms"},{"id":"hit","label":"Edge hit rate","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One file in, thousands of ways out","body":"A creator uploads one 10-minute 1080p video file. Viewers will watch it on a phone over weak mobile data, on a laptop with fast fibre, and on a TV, all at the same time, from every continent. No single file or server fits all of them. This lesson builds the system that turns one upload into something every viewer can play smoothly. It has two halves: a slow, heavy \"prepare the video\" path and a huge, cheap \"deliver the video\" path.","idea":"Streaming is two problems: a slow, heavy write path (upload and process) and a huge, cheap read path (deliver bytes).","caption":"One source video, many different viewers.","focus":["creator","viewer"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"UPLOAD","title":"Upload straight to storage","body":"The creator app asks the upload service to start. The service checks who is uploading and creates a video record marked \"processing\". It returns a presigned URL: a temporary signed link that lets the app write straight into object storage. The app then sends the file (about 600 MB here) in chunks of roughly 8 MB, and can resume if the connection drops. The big bytes never pass through our app servers.","idea":"Presigned, chunked, resumable uploads keep large files off your app servers and survive flaky networks.","caption":"A record is created, then chunks flow straight into the raw video store.","focus":["creator","upload","raw","meta"],"flows":[{"from":"creator","to":"upload","kind":"request","label":"init upload"},{"from":"upload","to":"meta","kind":"request","label":"status: processing"},{"from":"upload","to":"creator","kind":"response","label":"presigned URL"},{"from":"creator","to":"raw","kind":"request","label":"8 MB chunks"}],"metrics":{"source":"600"},"chips":[{"node":"upload","text":"presigned URL"},{"node":"raw","text":"8 MB chunks"},{"node":"meta","text":"processing"}]},{"tag":"PIPELINE","title":"Queue the work","body":"When the last chunk lands, the upload is marked complete and a job message goes onto a queue (a waiting line for tasks). Transcoding (re-encoding the video) takes minutes, so the creator is not kept waiting. Workers pull jobs when they have capacity, and a failed job is simply retried. A long video is split into pieces a few seconds long, so many workers can encode different pieces in parallel.","idea":"A queue decouples slow processing from the upload request and lets you scale workers by backlog.","caption":"A job is queued and a worker picks it up.","focus":["upload","queue","workers"],"flows":[{"from":"upload","to":"queue","kind":"async","label":"job: video 81f"},{"from":"queue","to":"workers","kind":"async","label":"pull job"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"queue","text":"job: video 81f"},{"node":"workers","text":"parallel pieces"}]},{"tag":"TRANSCODE","title":"Encode a bitrate ladder","body":"Workers read the source and re-encode it several times. The result is a bitrate ladder: the same video at several qualities, for example 240p at 0.4 Mbps, 360p at 0.8, 480p at 1.4, 720p at 3 and 1080p at 5 Mbps. Together that is about 10.6 Mbps, so a 10-minute video becomes roughly 800 MB across all versions (renditions). This is CPU-heavy work, often done on GPUs or dedicated encoder chips. Audio is encoded once or twice and shared.","idea":"Each rendition is a separate encode of the same content at a different resolution and bitrate.","caption":"Workers read the raw file and produce 5 renditions of it.","focus":["raw","workers"],"flows":[{"from":"raw","to":"workers","kind":"response","label":"source chunks"}],"metrics":{"renditions":"5"},"chips":[{"node":"workers","text":"5 renditions"},{"node":"workers","text":"10.6 Mbps total"},{"node":"raw","text":"source ≈ 600 MB"},{"node":"origin","text":"≈ 800 MB out"}]},{"tag":"SEGMENT","title":"Cut into short segments","body":"Each rendition is cut into segments of about 4 seconds, saved as ordinary files, and listed in order in a playlist. The two main formats are HLS (Apple's, .m3u8 playlists) and DASH (an open standard, .mpd manifests); both can share the same segment files (CMAF). Every segment starts on a keyframe (a full picture that needs no earlier frames), and the cut points are identical in every rendition. A 600-second video gives 150 segments per rendition, so about 750 files.","idea":"Segments are plain static files, so any HTTP server or CDN can serve them. Aligned cuts make switching quality safe.","caption":"Segments and playlists land in the segment store.","focus":["workers","origin"],"flows":[{"from":"workers","to":"origin","kind":"async","label":"4 s segments"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"origin","text":"150 segs / rendition"},{"node":"origin","text":"≈ 750 files","at":"bottom"},{"node":"workers","text":"4 s segments"}]},{"tag":"PUBLISH","title":"Mark it ready","body":"When every rendition is done, a worker updates the metadata service: the status becomes \"ready\" and the record stores the playlist location, the duration, the available renditions and thumbnails. The metadata service holds titles, owners, permissions and counters, usually in a database with a cache in front. It stores kilobytes per video, never the video itself. Search and recommendations read from it too.","idea":"Metadata is small and queried constantly; media is huge and fetched by URL. Keep them in separate systems.","caption":"The video record flips from processing to ready.","focus":["workers","meta"],"flows":[{"from":"workers","to":"meta","kind":"request","label":"status: ready"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"meta","text":"ready","tone":"green"},{"node":"meta","text":"manifest saved","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"PLAYBACK","title":"Player asks what to play","body":"A viewer opens the video. The player asks the metadata service, which checks the viewer may watch it and returns the title plus a signed playlist URL. The player then downloads the master playlist from the CDN (a network of cache servers near viewers). It is a small file listing every rendition. No video bytes have moved yet, and the metadata service is now out of the picture for the rest of the session.","idea":"The metadata call happens once per video; everything after it is plain file fetching.","caption":"Metadata returns a playlist URL, and the player fetches the master playlist.","focus":["viewer","meta","cdn"],"flows":[{"from":"viewer","to":"meta","kind":"request","label":"GET video 81f"},{"from":"meta","to":"viewer","kind":"response","label":"manifest URL"},{"from":"viewer","to":"cdn","kind":"request","label":"master playlist"},{"from":"cdn","to":"viewer","kind":"response","label":"rendition list"}],"metrics":{"startup":"300"},"chips":[{"node":"meta","text":"manifest URL"},{"node":"cdn","text":"master playlist"},{"node":"viewer","text":"no video bytes yet"}]},{"tag":"CDN","title":"Segments come from the edge","body":"The player requests the first segment at a middle quality. The request goes to the nearest CDN edge. On the first view the edge does not have it, so it fetches the segment from the segment store (a cache miss) and keeps a copy. Every later viewer in that region gets it straight from the edge. Segments never change, so they can be cached for a very long time. Startup takes about 1.2 seconds.","idea":"Only the first viewer per region pays the trip to the origin; the CDN serves everyone after that.","caption":"Cache miss: the edge fetches from the origin, keeps a copy, and serves it.","focus":["viewer","cdn","origin"],"flows":[{"from":"viewer","to":"cdn","kind":"request","label":"seg 1 @ 480p"},{"from":"cdn","to":"origin","kind":"request","label":"miss: fetch"},{"from":"origin","to":"cdn","kind":"response","label":"segment"},{"from":"cdn","to":"viewer","kind":"response","label":"segment"}],"metrics":{"startup":"1200","hit":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"cdn","text":"miss · fetch"},{"node":"origin","text":"segment kept at edge"},{"node":"viewer","text":"start ≈ 1.2 s"}]},{"tag":"ADAPT","title":"Bandwidth drops, quality follows","body":"The player times each segment download and watches how much video it has buffered. When a train enters a tunnel, speed drops and the buffer shrinks, so the player asks for the next segment at 360p instead of 480p. This is adaptive bitrate. The server does nothing special; it serves whichever file is requested. Because every rendition is cut at the same points, the switch is invisible apart from softer pixels. Popular segments are now cached, so the edge hit rate climbs.","idea":"Adaptive bitrate is decided by the player, using ordinary file requests. This keeps the servers stateless and cacheable.","caption":"The player drops to 360p, and the edge serves it from cache.","focus":["viewer","cdn"],"flows":[{"from":"viewer","to":"cdn","kind":"fail","label":"slow download"},{"from":"viewer","to":"cdn","kind":"request","label":"seg 40 @ 360p"},{"from":"cdn","to":"viewer","kind":"response","label":"cache hit"}],"metrics":{"hit":"96"},"chips":[{"node":"viewer","text":"480p → 360p"},{"node":"cdn","kind":"progress","value":96,"text":"96%","tone":"green"},{"node":"cdn","text":"cache hit","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Heavy write, cheap read","body":"Upload goes straight to storage, a queue feeds workers that encode the ladder and cut segments, and the metadata service records when the video is ready. Playback is one metadata call, then segment files from a CDN, with the player choosing the quality. The write path is slow and compute-heavy but runs once per video. The read path runs millions of times, so it is built from cacheable static files.","idea":"Do the expensive work once, ahead of time, so serving is just fetching cached files.","caption":"The whole path, from upload to playback.","focus":["creator","upload","queue","workers","raw","meta","origin","viewer","cdn"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"source":"600","renditions":"5","startup":"1200","hit":"96"},"chips":[{"node":"viewer","text":"start ≈ 1.2 s"},{"node":"cdn","kind":"progress","value":96,"text":"96%","tone":"green"},{"node":"workers","text":"5 renditions"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the creator app send file chunks directly to object storage with a presigned URL instead of through the upload service?","options":["Object storage encrypts data and the upload service cannot","Presigned URLs make the upload faster by using a better protocol","Large file bytes would otherwise tie up app servers and bandwidth, while the service still controls who may upload","The upload service cannot accept files over 1 MB"],"answer":2,"why":"The upload service only authorizes and records the upload. Moving gigabytes through it would waste its capacity, and object storage already handles large, resumable writes well."},{"q":"Segments for every rendition are cut at the same timestamps, each starting on a keyframe. What does that make possible?","options":["The CDN can merge renditions into one file","The player can switch quality at a segment boundary without a glitch","Transcoding runs without needing a queue","The metadata service can skip storing the manifest URL"],"answer":1,"why":"A segment that begins on a keyframe can be decoded on its own. Aligned boundaries mean segment 40 at 360p picks up exactly where segment 39 at 480p ended."},{"q":"A video goes viral and 2 million people start watching within an hour. Which part of the design protects the origin segment store?","options":["The transcode queue absorbs the viewers","The metadata service rate-limits playback","More transcoding workers are added automatically","Segments are immutable static files, so CDN edges cache them and serve nearly every request"],"answer":3,"why":"Each segment is fetched from the origin about once per region, then served from cache. Viewers hitting the edge never touch the origin."},{"q":"A 2-hour movie takes 6 hours to transcode on one machine. What is the most effective fix within this design?","options":["Split the source into chunks of a few seconds and encode them in parallel on many workers","Encode only one rendition and let the player upscale","Store the source in a faster database","Have the CDN transcode on the first request"],"answer":0,"why":"Video chunks can be encoded independently, so parallelism cuts wall-clock time almost linearly. The segment playlist already treats the output as separate files, so no heavy re-joining is needed."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Bitrate ladder","def":"The set of renditions of one video, from low resolution and bitrate to high, e.g. 240p at 0.4 Mbps up to 1080p at 5 Mbps."},{"term":"Transcoding","def":"Decoding a video and re-encoding it at a different resolution, bitrate, or codec. CPU/GPU heavy, so run it asynchronously on workers."},{"term":"Segment","def":"A short file (about 2 to 6 seconds) of one rendition. Starts on a keyframe so it decodes independently."},{"term":"HLS / DASH","def":"Two adaptive streaming standards that deliver video as segments listed in a playlist or manifest (.m3u8 / .mpd) over plain HTTP."},{"term":"Adaptive bitrate (ABR)","def":"The player picks the next segment's rendition from measured throughput and buffer level. The server stays unaware."},{"term":"Presigned URL","def":"A time-limited signed link that lets a client read or write one object in storage without holding credentials."},{"term":"Origin vs edge","def":"Origin holds the full set of segments. Edges cache the popular ones near viewers; only misses travel back to origin."},{"term":"Time to first frame","def":"Delay from pressing play to the first picture. Driven by the metadata call, manifest fetch, and first segment download."}],"lab":{"prompt":"Design the video platform for 5 million daily active viewers watching an average of 40 minutes each, with 50,000 new uploads per day averaging 8 minutes. Sketch the upload path, the processing pipeline, the delivery path, and estimate storage and peak egress.","requirements":["Uploads must be resumable and must not pass large files through the application servers.","Each video is playable at several quality levels and adapts to changing bandwidth.","Playback starts within about 2 seconds for most viewers worldwide.","A failed or slow transcode must not lose the upload or block other videos.","Estimate daily new storage for all renditions and peak delivery bandwidth, stating your assumptions."],"hints":["Use a ladder of about 10 Mbps total. 8 minutes is 480 s, so one video is roughly 600 MB across renditions. Multiply by 50,000 uploads.","Watch time: 5 million viewers times 40 minutes. Convert to bytes using an average delivered bitrate of about 3 Mbps, then think about peak versus average (a rule of thumb is 2 to 3 times).","Ask which pieces are idempotent. Re-running a transcode job for the same video should overwrite the same output paths.","Decide where the metadata lives and what the playback request returns. Keep its size and cost far below the media path.","Consider long-tail content: most videos get few views, so edge caches hold the popular head while the tail comes from origin."],"checklist":["Presigned, chunked upload direct to object storage, with a record created in processing state.","Queue plus parallel transcode workers, with retries and a dead-letter path for bad files.","Multiple renditions cut into aligned segments with HLS or DASH manifests.","Metadata service separate from media storage, returning a signed manifest URL.","CDN in front of the segment store, with long TTLs for immutable segments.","Back-of-envelope numbers for storage per day and peak egress, with assumptions written down."]},"isNew":true},{"slug":"design-ride-sharing","title":"Design Ride Sharing","subtitle":"Watch a driver's GPS ping become a nearby-car search, a match, a trip, and a price.","minutes":16,"level":"Advanced","learn":["Why driver location updates are a huge write stream that belongs in memory, not in the main database","How a grid of cells (geohash or H3) turns 'who is nearby?' into a few cheap lookups","How matching ranks candidates by ETA and avoids offering one driver to two riders","How a trip state machine with guarded transitions survives retries, timeouts and cancellations","How surge pricing is computed per cell from demand and supply, and why it is smoothed"],"nodes":[{"id":"rider","label":"Rider app","kind":"client","x":9,"y":20},{"id":"driver","label":"Driver app","kind":"client","x":9,"y":80},{"id":"gw","label":"API gateway","kind":"gateway","x":28,"y":50},{"id":"loc","label":"Location service","kind":"service","x":48,"y":82},{"id":"geo","label":"Geo index (memory)","kind":"cache","x":74,"y":82},{"id":"match","label":"Matching service","kind":"service","x":50,"y":50},{"id":"surge","label":"Surge pricing","kind":"service","x":50,"y":17},{"id":"trip","label":"Trip service","kind":"service","x":74,"y":50},{"id":"tripdb","label":"Trip database","kind":"db","x":91,"y":22}],"edges":[{"from":"rider","to":"gw"},{"from":"driver","to":"gw"},{"from":"gw","to":"loc"},{"from":"loc","to":"geo"},{"from":"gw","to":"match"},{"from":"match","to":"geo"},{"from":"match","to":"surge"},{"from":"surge","to":"geo"},{"from":"match","to":"trip"},{"from":"gw","to":"trip"},{"from":"trip","to":"tripdb"},{"from":"trip","to":"geo"}],"metrics":[{"id":"pings","label":"Pings/s","unit":"k"},{"id":"cands","label":"Candidates","unit":""},{"id":"wait","label":"Request age","unit":"s"},{"id":"surge","label":"Surge","unit":"x"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Where is everyone, right now?","body":"A rider taps Request. Within a few seconds the system must find a nearby free driver, get that driver to say yes, and agree on a price. The hard part is that every driver keeps moving, so \"nearby\" changes every few seconds. We will build four pieces: a stream of driver locations, a map index that answers \"who is close?\", a matcher with a trip state machine, and surge pricing.","idea":"Ride sharing is a real-time search over things that will not stand still.","caption":"A rider wants a car; drivers are scattered and always moving.","focus":["rider","driver"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"INGEST","title":"Drivers report their position","body":"Assume 1 million drivers are online at peak, and each app sends its GPS position every 4 seconds. That is about 250,000 updates (\"pings\") per second, each roughly 100 bytes (driver id, latitude, longitude, heading, time). Pings travel over a persistent connection to the gateway, then to the location service. Losing one ping is harmless because another comes in 4 seconds, so there is no heavy durable write per ping. A sampled copy goes to a log for trip routes and analytics, away from matching.","idea":"Location updates are a high-volume stream where only the newest value matters for matching.","caption":"GPS pings stream in from driver phones.","focus":["driver","gw","loc"],"flows":[{"from":"driver","to":"gw","kind":"async","label":"lat,lng every 4s"},{"from":"gw","to":"loc","kind":"async","label":"location update"}],"metrics":{"pings":"250"},"chips":[{"node":"gw","text":"250k pings/s"},{"node":"driver","text":"ping / 4 s"},{"node":"loc","text":"≈ 100 B each"}]},{"tag":"INDEX","title":"Index drivers by map cell","body":"The location service files each driver into an in-memory index, grouped by map cell. Geohash and H3 are schemes that give each map region a short id (geohash uses rectangles, H3 hexagons); a cell is about a kilometre across. A driver who crosses into a new cell moves to that cell's set. Each entry has a TTL (time to live): a countdown after which it is deleted unless the next ping refreshes it, so a dead app vanishes in about 30 s. The index is sharded (split across machines by cell) to handle 250,000 writes per second. The data is only about 100 MB, so sharding is for speed and availability, not space. A lost shard refills from new pings within one TTL.","idea":"Cutting the map into cells turns 'find nearby' into 'read a few small sets'.","caption":"Each driver is filed under their current cell and expires if not refreshed.","focus":["loc","geo"],"flows":[{"from":"loc","to":"geo","kind":"request","label":"move to new cell"},{"from":"geo","to":"loc","kind":"response","label":"ok"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"geo","text":"≈ 100 MB"},{"node":"geo","text":"TTL ≈ 30 s","at":"bottom"},{"node":"loc","text":"250k writes/s"}]},{"tag":"REQUEST","title":"A rider asks for a car","body":"The rider sends a pickup and a destination. The gateway checks who they are and forwards the request to the matching service, which creates a ride request. Ride requests are rare compared with location pings: even a big service sees only hundreds to a few thousand per second. That is why the matcher can afford to be careful while the location path must stay cheap.","idea":"Ride requests are rare compared with location writes, so the matcher can afford to be careful.","caption":"The ride request reaches the matching service.","focus":["rider","gw","match"],"flows":[{"from":"rider","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"request ride"},{"from":"gw","to":"match","kind":"request","label":"pickup + dropoff"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"gw","text":"authenticated","tone":"green"},{"node":"match","text":"ride request"}]},{"tag":"SEARCH","title":"Find nearby drivers","body":"The matcher works out the rider's cell, then asks the geo index for free drivers in that cell and the ring of cells around it. Neighbours matter because the rider may stand at a cell's edge, where the closest car is in the next cell. If too few drivers come back, it widens to the next ring. This usually returns a few dozen candidates from a handful of in-memory reads, in single-digit milliseconds.","idea":"Search the rider's cell plus neighbours, and widen the ring only if you must.","caption":"Candidate drivers come back from the cells around the rider.","focus":["match","geo"],"flows":[{"from":"match","to":"geo","kind":"request","label":"nearby free cars"},{"from":"geo","to":"match","kind":"response","label":"42 drivers"}],"metrics":{"cands":"42"},"chips":[{"node":"geo","text":"42 drivers"},{"node":"match","text":"cell + neighbours"},{"node":"match","text":"few ms","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"OFFER","title":"Rank, reserve, and offer","body":"The matcher keeps the closest 10 or so candidates by straight-line distance, then ranks them by ETA (estimated arrival time) from a routing service, since a car across a river may be ten minutes away. It asks the trip service to reserve the best driver. That is a conditional write on the driver's record: \"set current_offer to this trip only if the driver is AVAILABLE\", in the same transaction that creates the trip as REQUESTED. The reservation expires after 15 s. So no driver is offered two rides. Larger platforms often batch requests for a couple of seconds and match them together; this one-at-a-time version is the simple form.","idea":"Rank by ETA, then reserve one driver with a compare-and-set so nobody is offered two rides.","caption":"The driver is reserved, a trip row is created, and the offer goes out.","focus":["match","trip","tripdb","gw","driver"],"flows":[{"from":"match","to":"trip","kind":"request","label":"reserve driver"},{"from":"trip","to":"tripdb","kind":"request","label":"reserve + insert"},{"from":"trip","to":"gw","kind":"async","label":"offer"},{"from":"gw","to":"driver","kind":"async","label":"offer, 15s"}],"metrics":{"wait":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"match","text":"closest ~10 → ETA"},{"node":"tripdb","text":"REQUESTED"},{"node":"driver","text":"offer · 15 s"}]},{"tag":"STATE","title":"The trip state machine","body":"Drivers usually take 5 to 10 s to tap accept, so the request is about 8 s old. A trip moves through REQUESTED, ACCEPTED, ARRIVED, IN_PROGRESS and COMPLETED, and can be CANCELLED from the early states. Every change is a guarded update such as \"set ACCEPTED only if state is REQUESTED and this driver holds the offer\", so retries fail harmlessly. The database is the source of truth, and the trip service then marks the driver busy in the geo index. If every attempt is declined or times out, the trip becomes EXPIRED and the rider is told no cars are available.","idea":"Guarded transitions make retries and races harmless.","caption":"The accept becomes a guarded state change in the trip database.","focus":["driver","gw","trip","tripdb","geo"],"flows":[{"from":"driver","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"accept"},{"from":"gw","to":"trip","kind":"request","label":"accept trip"},{"from":"trip","to":"tripdb","kind":"request","label":"to ACCEPTED"},{"from":"tripdb","to":"trip","kind":"response","label":"1 row changed"},{"from":"trip","to":"geo","kind":"async","label":"mark busy"}],"metrics":{"wait":"8"},"chips":[{"node":"tripdb","text":"ACCEPTED","tone":"green"},{"node":"geo","text":"driver busy"},{"node":"driver","text":"request ≈ 8 s old"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The driver does not answer","body":"Now suppose the driver is in a tunnel and the 15 second timer runs out. The trip service releases the reservation and tells the matcher, which about 1 s later offers the ride to the next candidate. The request is now about 17 s old (1 s before the first offer, a 15 s timeout, 1 s to re-offer). If the first driver's accept arrives late, the guarded update sees the trip is no longer waiting for that driver and rejects it. Each decline adds delay, so many systems cap attempts and then widen the search.","idea":"Timeouts plus guarded updates keep a slow driver from corrupting a trip that moved on.","caption":"The offer expires, the matcher moves on, and a late accept is rejected.","focus":["match","trip","gw","driver"],"flows":[{"from":"gw","to":"driver","kind":"async","label":"offer, 15s"},{"from":"trip","to":"gw","kind":"fail","label":"offer expired"},{"from":"trip","to":"match","kind":"response","label":"try next driver"},{"from":"match","to":"trip","kind":"request","label":"reserve driver 2"},{"from":"trip","to":"gw","kind":"async","label":"offer driver 2"},{"from":"driver","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"late accept"},{"from":"gw","to":"trip","kind":"request","label":"accept trip"},{"from":"trip","to":"gw","kind":"fail","label":"rejected: moved on"},{"from":"gw","to":"driver","kind":"fail","label":"offer gone"}],"metrics":{"wait":"17","cands":"41"},"chips":[{"node":"driver","text":"no answer · 15 s","tone":"red"},{"node":"trip","text":"late accept rejected"},{"node":"match","text":"request 17 s old","tone":"red"},{"node":"match","text":"next candidate","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"SURGE","title":"Price from supply and demand","body":"When requests far outnumber free drivers in an area, surge raises the price. This attracts more drivers and cools demand. A pricing job counts free drivers per cell from the geo index every few seconds and averages them over a one to two minute window. It also counts open requests from the matcher. It turns the ratio into a multiplier, smooths it and caps it. When the matcher quotes a fare, it asks the pricing service for that cell's current multiplier. The quote is locked for a few minutes, so the price does not change between confirming and matching.","idea":"Surge is a per-cell ratio of demand to supply, smoothed and capped.","caption":"Supply and demand counts per cell become a multiplier on the fare.","focus":["surge","geo","match"],"flows":[{"from":"surge","to":"geo","kind":"request","label":"count free drivers"},{"from":"geo","to":"surge","kind":"response","label":"supply per cell"},{"from":"match","to":"surge","kind":"async","label":"open requests"},{"from":"match","to":"surge","kind":"request","label":"price for cell?"},{"from":"surge","to":"match","kind":"response","label":"1.4x"}],"metrics":{"surge":"1.4"},"chips":[{"node":"surge","text":"1.4x"},{"node":"surge","text":"smoothed + capped","at":"bottom"},{"node":"match","text":"quote locked"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"The whole picture","body":"Pings flow into a sharded, in-memory cell index with short TTLs. A ride request searches the rider's cell and its neighbours, ranks by ETA, reserves one driver, and offers the ride with a timeout. A guarded state machine in the trip database keeps every change safe under retries. Surge turns per-cell supply and demand into a price. Only the few decisions that matter touch the durable database.","idea":"Keep the hot stream in memory, and put strong guarantees only on the small set of decisions that need them.","caption":"Ingest, index, match, trip, price.","focus":["rider","driver","gw","loc","geo","match","surge","trip","tripdb"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"pings":"250","surge":"1.4"},"chips":[{"node":"loc","text":"250k pings/s"},{"node":"geo","text":"TTL ≈ 30 s"},{"node":"surge","text":"1.4x"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the live driver location store usually an in-memory index with a TTL instead of rows in the main trip database?","options":["Relational databases cannot index latitude and longitude at all","Each position is overwritten within seconds, so durability is wasted and the write rate is huge","Location data is too sensitive to ever be written to disk","In-memory stores guarantee no two riders get the same driver"],"answer":1,"why":"Only the latest position matters and a new one arrives every few seconds. Durable storage would pay a high cost to keep data that is stale for matching almost immediately. A TTL also removes drivers whose apps went silent."},{"q":"A rider stands right on the boundary between two grid cells. What must the nearby-driver search do?","options":["Search only the rider's own cell, since it contains the rider","Search every cell in the city","Ask drivers to report which rider is closest","Search the rider's cell and its neighbouring cells"],"answer":3,"why":"The nearest car may sit just across the cell edge. Checking the cell plus its neighbours fixes that cheaply, and the ring widens only if too few drivers are found."},{"q":"Two riders are matched to the same best driver at the same moment. Which design stops both from getting the trip?","options":["Send both offers and let the driver pick","Make the geo index fully consistent across regions","Reserve the driver with a conditional update that succeeds for only one request","Cache the driver list for longer"],"answer":2,"why":"A compare-and-set on the driver's record (set current_offer only if the driver is AVAILABLE) lets exactly one request win. The loser sees the condition fail and tries the next candidate. The index is only a hint, so the guarantee must live in the store holding that record."},{"q":"A driver's accept message arrives 20 seconds after the 15-second offer window closed and the trip was given to another driver. What should happen?","options":["Accept it and reassign the trip","Reject it because the guarded update finds the trip is no longer in the expected state for that driver","Accept it and cancel the other driver","Ignore it silently and leave the driver waiting"],"answer":1,"why":"State transitions are conditional on current state and owner, so a stale message cannot change a trip that moved on. The driver should be told the offer is gone so their app returns to idle."},{"q":"Why is surge computed per cell over a window and then smoothed, instead of using the raw request count each second?","options":["Averaging over a window is needed because the index only stores counts per day","Raw counts cannot be compared between cells of different sizes","Per-second ratios swing wildly and would make prices flicker","It lets the index skip TTLs"],"answer":2,"why":"Short windows are noisy: one burst of requests would spike prices and then drop them seconds later. A one to two minute window with smoothing and a cap gives stable, explainable prices."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Geohash / H3","def":"Ways to cut the map into cells with ids. Nearby places share a cell or neighbouring cells, so a spatial search becomes a lookup by id."},{"term":"Cell ring search","def":"Query the rider's cell, then its neighbours, then wider rings only if too few drivers are found."},{"term":"Location TTL (time to live)","def":"Each driver entry expires after about 30 seconds, so silent or crashed apps drop out of the index on their own."},{"term":"Ping rate maths","def":"1M online drivers pinging every 4 s is about 250k writes/s at roughly 100 bytes each, around 25 MB/s. Rule of thumb, not a measured figure."},{"term":"ETA ranking","def":"Rank candidates by estimated drive time on roads, not straight-line distance, since rivers and one-way streets change the answer."},{"term":"Trip state machine","def":"Fixed states (REQUESTED, ACCEPTED, ARRIVED, IN_PROGRESS, COMPLETED, CANCELLED, EXPIRED) with conditional updates so only valid moves succeed."},{"term":"Driver reservation","def":"A compare-and-set on the driver's availability that holds one driver for one request for a short lease, preventing double offers."},{"term":"Surge multiplier","def":"Fare factor from demand versus supply per cell over a sliding window, smoothed and capped to avoid flicker."}],"lab":{"prompt":"Design the backend for a ride-sharing service in one city of 200,000 online drivers at peak. Cover how location updates are ingested, how nearby drivers are found, how a driver is matched and a trip tracked, and how surge is priced. State your numbers and where each piece of data lives. Assume each driver pings every 4 seconds and the city sees about 100 to 300 ride requests per second at peak.","requirements":["Ingest driver location updates and make them searchable within a few seconds.","Return nearby free drivers for a pickup point in under 100 ms.","Never assign one driver to two active trips.","Handle driver decline, timeout, and late messages without corrupting trip state.","Define the trip states and the allowed transitions.","Compute a per-area surge multiplier that does not flicker.","Estimate write rate, memory for the index, and ride requests per second."],"hints":["Work out pings per second first: drivers divided by the ping interval. Then size the index by entries times bytes per entry.","Pick a cell size so a typical cell holds a handful of drivers, and decide how many rings you will search.","Put the location index in memory with a TTL, and the trip record in a durable database with a version column.","Write the reservation and each transition as a conditional update, and think about what happens if the same request is sent twice.","For surge, count requests and free drivers per cell over a window, take the ratio, then smooth and cap it."],"checklist":["Location writes go to an in-memory, sharded index with a TTL, not the trip database.","Nearby search checks the rider's cell plus neighbouring cells.","Candidates are ranked by ETA and offered one at a time with a timeout.","Reservation is a compare-and-set on driver availability, and state changes use conditional updates, so a second assignment fails.","States and transitions are listed, including cancellation.","Surge is per cell, windowed, smoothed, and capped.","Numbers are stated: pings per second, index size, requests per second."]},"isNew":true},{"slug":"design-web-crawler","title":"Design a Web Crawler","subtitle":"Watch a crawler pull URLs from a frontier, fetch pages politely, skip duplicates, and feed new links back in.","minutes":16,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How a URL frontier separates priority from per-host politeness","Why a crawler must rate-limit each host, not just itself","How to dedup URLs (normalise + Bloom filter) and content (hash or SimHash)","Why DNS lookups become a bottleneck and how a local cache fixes it","How to shard workers by host so politeness and dedup stay simple"],"nodes":[{"id":"seeds","label":"Seed URLs","kind":"client","x":8,"y":22},{"id":"frontier","label":"URL frontier","kind":"queue","x":28,"y":52},{"id":"fetcher","label":"Fetcher workers","kind":"worker","x":50,"y":52},{"id":"dns","label":"DNS cache","kind":"dns","x":68,"y":12},{"id":"web","label":"Websites","kind":"external","x":90,"y":28},{"id":"parser","label":"Parser + extractor","kind":"service","x":72,"y":62},{"id":"dedup","label":"Dedup filters","kind":"cache","x":50,"y":88},{"id":"store","label":"Content store","kind":"storage","x":90,"y":82}],"edges":[{"from":"seeds","to":"frontier"},{"from":"frontier","to":"fetcher"},{"from":"fetcher","to":"dns"},{"from":"fetcher","to":"web"},{"from":"fetcher","to":"parser"},{"from":"parser","to":"dedup"},{"from":"dedup","to":"frontier"},{"from":"parser","to":"store"}],"metrics":[{"id":"rate","label":"Pages/sec","unit":"pg/s"},{"id":"dns","label":"DNS lookup","unit":"ms"},{"id":"dupes","label":"Dup links","unit":"%"},{"id":"workers","label":"In flight","unit":"fetches"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Fetch a billion pages","body":"We want to download about 1 billion web pages a month to build a search index. That is roughly 400 pages per second, and at 100 KB per page about 100 TB of raw HTML a month. A crawler is a loop: pick a URL, fetch the page, find new links on it, repeat. The hard part is not downloading one page. It is deciding what to fetch next, never hammering one website, and not wasting work on pages we already have.","idea":"A crawler is a loop: pick a URL, fetch it, find new URLs, repeat. Every box we add exists to keep that loop fast, polite, and non-redundant.","caption":"Seed URLs start the loop; the frontier decides what comes next.","focus":["seeds","frontier"],"flows":[{"from":"seeds","to":"frontier","kind":"request","label":"100 seed URLs"}],"metrics":{"rate":"0","workers":"0","dupes":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"seeds","text":"100 seed URLs"},{"node":"frontier","text":"≈ 400 pages/s"}]},{"tag":"FRONTIER","title":"The URL frontier","body":"The frontier is the crawler's to-do list of URLs, and it is more than a simple queue. Front queues sort URLs by priority (how important or how stale they are). Back queues keep one queue per website host, plus the earliest time that host may be visited again, and only hand out a URL when its host's time has come. Billions of URLs do not fit in memory, so most live on disk with small in-memory buffers at the front.","idea":"Two jobs, two layers: front queues pick WHAT is valuable, back queues enforce WHEN each host may be hit.","caption":"The frontier hands a URL to a free fetcher.","focus":["frontier","fetcher"],"flows":[{"from":"frontier","to":"fetcher","kind":"request","label":"next URL"}],"metrics":{"workers":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"frontier","text":"1 queue / host"},{"node":"frontier","text":"bulk on disk","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"DNS","title":"Resolve the host name","body":"Before connecting, the fetcher needs the site's IP address, which DNS (the internet's phone book) provides. A normal lookup blocks a thread and costs 10 to 100 ms or more when not cached. At crawl scale that wastes workers and overloads the resolver. So workers use a local caching resolver with async lookups (many at once, without waiting). Most URLs belong to hosts we have already seen, so cache hits are common and a lookup takes about 1 ms.","idea":"DNS is a hidden bottleneck at crawl scale; cache answers locally and resolve asynchronously.","caption":"A cached DNS lookup returns the IP in about 1 ms.","focus":["fetcher","dns"],"flows":[{"from":"fetcher","to":"dns","kind":"request","label":"A news.example?"},{"from":"dns","to":"fetcher","kind":"response","label":"198.51.100.4"}],"metrics":{"dns":"1","workers":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"dns","text":"198.51.100.4"},{"node":"dns","text":"1 ms · cached","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"POLITE","title":"Fetch, but politely","body":"The fetcher first reads the site's robots.txt (cached for a day), a file saying which paths bots may not visit and sometimes a Crawl-delay hint. Then it downloads the page, with a timeout so a slow server cannot tie up a worker. Rule of thumb: one request at a time per host, a few seconds apart. Fetching takes about 0.5 s, so 60 fetches in flight give about 120 pages per second (Little's law: in flight divided by time per fetch). Good crawlers also honour 429 and 503 Retry-After replies and send a User-Agent with a contact URL.","idea":"Politeness is per host: the crawler can be huge overall while each website sees a trickle.","caption":"The fetcher checks robots.txt, then requests the page politely.","focus":["fetcher","web"],"flows":[{"from":"fetcher","to":"web","kind":"request","label":"GET /robots.txt"},{"from":"web","to":"fetcher","kind":"response","label":"rules + delay"},{"from":"fetcher","to":"web","kind":"request","label":"GET /article"},{"from":"web","to":"fetcher","kind":"response","label":"200 + HTML"}],"metrics":{"rate":"120","workers":"60"},"chips":[{"node":"web","text":"200 + HTML","tone":"green"},{"node":"fetcher","text":"60 in flight"},{"node":"fetcher","text":"≈ 120 pages/s","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"PARSE","title":"Extract links and content","body":"The parser reads the HTML, pulls out the text, and extracts every link. Relative links (like /about) are turned into full URLs, fragments like #top are dropped, and URLs are normalised: lowercase host, remove default ports, sort query parameters. Without this, the same page appears under many spellings and we crawl it many times.","idea":"Normalise URLs before comparing them, or dedup will miss obvious repeats.","caption":"The parser produces text plus a list of candidate URLs.","focus":["fetcher","parser"],"flows":[{"from":"fetcher","to":"parser","kind":"request","label":"raw HTML"}],"metrics":{"rate":"120"},"chips":[{"node":"parser","text":"URLs normalised"},{"node":"parser","text":"fragments dropped","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"DEDUP","title":"Skip what we have seen","body":"Each candidate URL is checked against a set of URLs already seen. Storing 10 billion URLs exactly takes about 1 TB, or about 80 GB even as 8-byte fingerprints. A Bloom filter (a compact set that can say \"definitely not seen\" or \"probably seen\") needs only about 12 GB, so it stays in memory. A \"no\" means the URL is certainly new. A \"yes\" is treated as seen, which wrongly skips about 1% of new URLs; confirm against an exact store if that is too costly. The page body is hashed too: an identical hash, or a similar SimHash (a fingerprint where similar pages get similar values), marks a copy, so we skip storing it.","idea":"Dedup happens twice: by URL before fetching (saves bandwidth) and by content after fetching (saves storage and index noise).","caption":"Of 40 extracted links only 9 are new, so about 78% are duplicates.","focus":["parser","dedup"],"flows":[{"from":"parser","to":"dedup","kind":"request","label":"40 links"},{"from":"dedup","to":"parser","kind":"response","label":"9 are new"}],"metrics":{"dupes":"78"},"chips":[{"node":"parser","text":"40 links"},{"node":"dedup","text":"9 are new","tone":"green"},{"node":"dedup","text":"78% already seen"},{"node":"dedup","text":"Bloom ≈ 12 GB","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"LOOP","title":"New URLs return to the frontier","body":"Only genuinely new URLs go back into the frontier, with a priority, in their host's queue. A page is saved (compressed, in object storage, keyed by URL and fetch time) only if its content hash is new. Pages that change often get a short recrawl interval, static ones a long one. Recrawls are re-queued by a scheduler that skips the seen-URL filter, and use conditional GETs: if the server supports ETag or Last-Modified, an unchanged page costs a tiny 304 reply instead of a full download.","idea":"The frontier is both the input and the output of the loop; recrawl scheduling lives there too.","caption":"New links feed the frontier, and the new page is saved.","focus":["dedup","frontier","parser","store"],"flows":[{"from":"dedup","to":"frontier","kind":"async","label":"9 new URLs"},{"from":"parser","to":"dedup","kind":"request","label":"content hash?"},{"from":"parser","to":"store","kind":"async","label":"new page + hash"}],"metrics":{"rate":"120","workers":"60"},"chips":[{"node":"frontier","text":"9 new URLs"},{"node":"dedup","text":"content hash?"},{"node":"store","text":"new page + hash"}]},{"tag":"SCALE","title":"Distribute the workers","body":"One tuned machine could handle 400 pages/s, but we spread out for headroom and safety: peaks several times the 320 Mbps average, parsing and dedup CPU and memory, 100 TB a month of storage, and no single point of failure. At 0.5 s per fetch, 400 pages/s means about 200 fetches in flight. We shard (split) work by host: in practice by registered domain, since many hostnames share one server. Links are sent to the shard that owns their host, which dedups them and enqueues them. Consistent hashing means adding a worker moves few hosts. Workers lease each URL: the frontier hides it until a deadline and removes it only when the worker acknowledges (next steps).","idea":"Shard by host, not by URL: politeness, DNS, and robots.txt all become local decisions.","caption":"Links go to the shard that owns their host, which dedups and enqueues them.","focus":["frontier","fetcher","dedup"],"flows":[{"from":"frontier","to":"fetcher","kind":"request","label":"assign URL"},{"from":"dedup","to":"frontier","kind":"async","label":"forward to owner"},{"from":"fetcher","to":"frontier","kind":"async","label":"lease + ack"}],"metrics":{"rate":"400","workers":"200"},"chips":[{"node":"fetcher","text":"200 in flight"},{"node":"frontier","text":"400 pages/s"},{"node":"frontier","text":"shard by host hash","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A spider trap and a dead worker","body":"Some sites generate endless URLs, like a calendar with a \"next month\" link forever (a spider trap). Defences: cap URL length, depth and pages per host. Leases handle dead workers. A lease is a temporary claim: the URL stays in the frontier, hidden until a deadline, and is removed only when finished. If a worker dies, its leases expire and the URLs are tried again. Each shard saves its queues and seen-URL log, so a new owner can reload them and rebuild the Bloom filter; a few repeat fetches are fine. A host that keeps timing out gets its whole queue delayed.","idea":"Assume the web is hostile and machines fail: use limits on crawl depth and per-host budgets, and lease URLs instead of deleting them.","caption":"Traps are capped; a dead worker's lease expires and the URL is retried.","focus":["fetcher","web","frontier"],"flows":[{"from":"fetcher","to":"web","kind":"fail","label":"timeout"},{"from":"fetcher","to":"frontier","kind":"async","label":"retry later"},{"from":"frontier","to":"fetcher","kind":"request","label":"reassign: expired"},{"from":"fetcher","to":"frontier","kind":"async","label":"cap depth/host"}],"metrics":{"rate":"390"},"chips":[{"node":"fetcher","text":"timeout","tone":"red"},{"node":"frontier","text":"lease expired · retry"},{"node":"frontier","text":"cap depth + host","at":"bottom"},{"node":"fetcher","text":"≈ 390 pages/s"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"The whole crawl loop","body":"Seeds fill the frontier. Workers take a URL, look up the host in a DNS cache, fetch politely, and parse the page. Dedup drops repeated URLs and repeated content, new URLs return to the frontier, and pages go to storage. Sharding by host keeps politeness a local decision. Limits and leases stop traps and failures from stalling the crawl.","idea":"Fast overall, gentle per host, and never doing the same work twice.","caption":"A closed loop: fast overall, gentle on each host.","focus":["seeds","frontier","fetcher","dns","web","parser","dedup","store"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"rate":"400","dns":"1","dupes":"78","workers":"200"},"chips":[{"node":"fetcher","text":"≈ 400 pages/s"},{"node":"dns","text":"1 ms"},{"node":"dedup","text":"78% seen"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your crawler is fast and healthy overall, but one small blog owner emails that your bot took their site down. Which design flaw does this most likely point to?","options":["No Bloom filter on the seen-URL set","No per-host politeness limit in the frontier or fetchers","Too few DNS cache entries","The content store is too slow"],"answer":1,"why":"Total crawl rate can be high while a single host must still see only a slow trickle. The per-host back queues and delays enforce that."},{"q":"Why shard the crawl by hash of the host name instead of hashing the full URL?","options":["It makes the Bloom filter smaller","It spreads load more evenly than hashing URLs","One worker then owns all URLs of a host, so it can enforce politeness and cache DNS and robots.txt locally","It removes the need for a frontier"],"answer":2,"why":"Hashing the full URL would scatter one host across all workers, forcing them to coordinate delays. Host-based sharding keeps those decisions local."},{"q":"A Bloom filter says a URL is NOT in the set. What can you conclude?","options":["It is definitely new, so fetch it","It is probably new but might be a duplicate","It was seen but the filter lost it","Nothing; you must check the database"],"answer":0,"why":"Bloom filters have no false negatives. A 'no' is certain; only a 'yes' might be wrong, which just costs us skipping a rare new URL."},{"q":"Two different URLs return pages that are 99% identical (a mirror site). Which mechanism catches this?","options":["URL normalisation","robots.txt","Content fingerprinting such as SimHash","DNS caching"],"answer":2,"why":"The URLs differ, so URL dedup cannot see it. Hashing page content, with SimHash for near duplicates, detects the repeat after fetching."},{"q":"A worker crashes after taking a URL from the frontier but before finishing the fetch. How do we avoid losing that URL?","options":["Mark the URL done as soon as a worker takes it","Never remove URLs from the frontier","Ask the website to resend it","Keep the URL leased and delete it only after success"],"answer":3,"why":"Leasing gives at-least-once processing: the URL stays recoverable until the worker acknowledges completion, so a crash just delays it."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"URL frontier","def":"The prioritised to-do list of URLs, with front queues for importance and back queues per host for politeness."},{"term":"Politeness","def":"Limiting requests per host (often one at a time with a delay) and obeying robots.txt so a site is never overloaded."},{"term":"URL normalisation","def":"Canonicalising URLs (lowercase host, drop fragments and default ports, sort query params) so duplicates compare equal."},{"term":"Bloom filter","def":"A compact probabilistic set with no false negatives but some false positives; ideal for 'have we seen this URL?'."},{"term":"SimHash","def":"A fingerprint where similar documents get similar hashes, used to spot near-duplicate pages."},{"term":"Shard by host","def":"Route all URLs of one host to one worker so politeness, DNS, and robots.txt caching need no coordination."},{"term":"Spider trap","def":"A site that generates endless URLs; counter it with depth, length, and per-host page caps."},{"term":"Back-of-envelope","def":"1B pages/month is about 400 pages/s; at 100 KB each that is roughly 100 TB of HTML per month."}],"lab":{"prompt":"Design a crawler that fetches 1 billion pages per month, refreshes news sites hourly, and never overloads any single website. Sketch the frontier, workers, and dedup, and say what you store.","requirements":["Estimate pages per second, bandwidth, and storage for one month","Define the frontier with priority ordering and per-host queues","Enforce a per-host politeness delay and honour robots.txt","Dedup URLs before fetching and content after fetching","Cache DNS results locally and resolve asynchronously","Shard workers so one host maps to one worker","Schedule recrawls from observed change rate so news sites refresh hourly (conditional GET with If-Modified-Since / ETag)","Handle worker crashes and spider traps"],"hints":["1B pages / 30 days / 86,400 s is about 385 pages/s; multiply by average page size for bandwidth","Use Little's law: in-flight fetches = rate x average fetch time","A Bloom filter at 10 bits per URL holds 10B URLs in about 12 GB with roughly 1% false positives","Think about how recrawl frequency is derived from how often a page changed last time","Lease URLs from the frontier and acknowledge on completion"],"checklist":["Numbers shown for rate, bandwidth, and storage with stated assumptions","Frontier has both a priority layer and a per-host layer","Politeness is enforced in one place per host","URL dedup and content dedup are both present and distinguished","DNS and robots.txt caching are mentioned","Recrawl policy ties interval to change frequency and priority","Failure handling covers worker crash and infinite URL traps"]},"isNew":true},{"slug":"design-kv-store","title":"Design a Key-Value Store","subtitle":"Build a Dynamo-style store that stays writable when nodes die, and watch a key travel through the ring, quorums, gossip and hinted handoff.","minutes":18,"level":"Advanced","learn":["How consistent hashing with virtual nodes spreads keys and limits data movement","How N, R and W set the trade-off between latency, durability and freshness","How gossip lets nodes agree on who is alive without a central master","How hinted handoff keeps writes accepted while a replica is down","How vector clocks detect concurrent writes that must be reconciled"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Client","kind":"client","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"coord","label":"Coordinator node","kind":"service","x":30,"y":50},{"id":"a","label":"Node A (replica)","kind":"db","x":56,"y":14},{"id":"b","label":"Node B (replica)","kind":"db","x":56,"y":50},{"id":"c","label":"Node C (replica)","kind":"db","x":56,"y":86},{"id":"d","label":"Node D (next on ring)","kind":"db","x":87,"y":68}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"coord"},{"from":"coord","to":"a"},{"from":"coord","to":"b"},{"from":"coord","to":"c"},{"from":"a","to":"b"},{"from":"b","to":"c"},{"from":"coord","to":"d"},{"from":"d","to":"c"}],"metrics":[{"id":"acks","label":"Replica acks","unit":"of 3"},{"id":"latency","label":"Op latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"versions","label":"Versions","unit":"stored"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"A store that never says no","body":"Design a key-value store (think \"look up a value by its name\") for a shopping cart. It must handle 200,000 operations per second and 50 TB of data, with values under 10 KB. It must also accept writes when machines fail or a data centre link breaks. One database server is a poor fit: it would be a single point of failure and has no headroom for that load. So the data must be split across many nodes (servers). The hard part is that any node can fail at any time, and we still want fast answers.","idea":"Choose availability and low latency up front, and accept that replicas may briefly disagree.","caption":"A client talks to a cluster of nodes, not to one server.","focus":["client","coord"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"coord","text":"200,000 ops/s"},{"node":"coord","text":"50 TB data","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"PARTITION","title":"Place the key on a ring","body":"First, which node owns which key? Hash the key (say cart:42) to a point on a ring of hash values. Each node owns many points on the ring, called virtual nodes (often 16 to 256 per machine). The key belongs to the first node clockwise from its point. When a node is added, it takes small slices from many neighbours, so only about 1/N of keys move, instead of nearly all of them. Any node can act as the coordinator, the node that handles a request and finds the owners.","idea":"Consistent hashing with virtual nodes spreads load evenly and keeps rebalancing cheap.","caption":"The client sends PUT cart:42; the coordinator hashes the key onto the ring.","focus":["client","coord"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"coord","kind":"request","label":"PUT cart:42"}],"metrics":{"acks":"0","latency":"0","versions":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"coord","text":"hash(cart:42)"},{"node":"coord","text":"1/N of keys move","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"REPLICATE","title":"Copy it to N nodes","body":"One copy would be lost with its machine. So the coordinator writes to the key's owner plus the next distinct nodes on the ring, giving N = 3 replicas (copies), ideally in different racks or zones. Every node has the full ring map, so the coordinator sends the write straight to all three, with no lookup service in the middle. N = 3 is a rule of thumb: no data is lost unless all three copies die, at three times the storage.","idea":"Replication is what makes failure survivable; the ring tells you which nodes hold a key.","caption":"The coordinator sends the write to three replicas.","focus":["coord","a","b","c"],"flows":[{"from":"coord","to":"a","kind":"replicate","label":"write v1"},{"from":"coord","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"write v1"},{"from":"coord","to":"c","kind":"replicate","label":"write v1"}],"metrics":{"versions":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"coord","text":"N = 3 replicas"},{"node":"a","text":"v1"},{"node":"b","text":"v1"},{"node":"c","text":"v1"}]},{"tag":"QUORUM","title":"Wait for W acknowledgements","body":"Waiting for all three replicas would make every write as slow as the slowest node. Instead, the coordinator answers the client after W = 2 acknowledgements (acks). Assume 1 ms network time, replicas that need 2 to 3 ms to save, and one replica frozen for 40 ms by a garbage-collection pause (the runtime briefly stops to clean memory). The write finishes in about 4 ms, not 40. The slow third replica still gets the data.","idea":"A write quorum W hides slow replicas: latency is set by the W-th fastest, not the slowest.","caption":"Two acks arrive, so the client is answered while C is still stuck.","focus":["coord","a","b","client"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"b","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"coord","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"OK"}],"metrics":{"acks":"2","latency":"4"},"chips":[{"node":"coord","text":"2 of 3 acks","tone":"green"},{"node":"client","text":"OK · 4 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"c","text":"GC pause 40 ms","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"QUORUM","title":"Read R, overlap with W","body":"A read asks the replicas and waits for R = 2 answers. Because R + W = 4 is greater than N = 3, any two replicas that answer must include at least one that acknowledged the latest write (when writes went to the key's home replicas). The coordinator returns the newest value (judged by a vector clock, explained later) in about 3 ms, and quietly fixes stale replicas in the background (read repair). Setting R = 1 reads faster but may return old data.","idea":"When R + W > N and writes go to the key's home replicas, the read set overlaps the write set, so reads normally see the latest acknowledged write.","caption":"Two replicas answer, and the overlap with the write set carries the newest value.","focus":["client","coord","a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"coord","kind":"request","label":"GET cart:42"},{"from":"coord","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"coord","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"a","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v1"},{"from":"b","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v1"},{"from":"coord","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"v1"}],"metrics":{"latency":"3"},"chips":[{"node":"coord","text":"R + W = 4 > 3"},{"node":"client","text":"v1 · 3 ms","tone":"green"},{"node":"b","text":"v1"}]},{"tag":"GOSSIP","title":"Detecting failure without a master","body":"Nodes need to know who is alive and who owns which ring slices. Every second, each node picks a random peer and swaps what it knows: the member list, heartbeat counters (numbers each node keeps raising to prove it is alive) and ring changes. News reaches everyone in roughly log2(cluster size) rounds, so a 1,000-node cluster agrees in about 10 to 15 seconds. A node marks a peer \"suspect\" when its heartbeat has not advanced for a while. No central master exists to fail or become a bottleneck.","idea":"Gossip spreads membership and failure information epidemically, with no single point of failure.","caption":"Peers swap heartbeat tables with random neighbours.","focus":["a","b","c"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"async","label":"gossip"},{"from":"b","to":"c","kind":"async","label":"gossip"},{"from":"c","to":"b","kind":"async","label":"gossip"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"swap views / 1 s"},{"node":"b","text":"heartbeat counters"},{"node":"c","text":"suspect if stale"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A replica dies mid-write","body":"Node C crashes. The write for cart:42 still reaches A and B, which both ack, so W = 2 is met and the client sees success in about 4 ms. If a second replica were also down, a strict quorum would reject the write. Dynamo-style stores relax this with a sloppy quorum: a healthy stand-in node may take the write instead. That keeps writes available but weakens the \"reads overlap writes\" guarantee until the missed data is replayed.","idea":"With N = 3 and W = 2 the system tolerates one failed replica with no client-visible effect.","caption":"C is unreachable, but A and B still acknowledge.","focus":["coord","a","b","c"],"flows":[{"from":"coord","to":"c","kind":"fail","label":"timeout"},{"from":"coord","to":"a","kind":"replicate","label":"write v2"},{"from":"coord","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"write v2"},{"from":"a","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"b","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"ack"}],"metrics":{"acks":"2","latency":"4","versions":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"c","text":"crashed","tone":"red"},{"node":"a","text":"v2"},{"node":"b","text":"v2"},{"node":"coord","text":"W = 2 met","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"HANDOFF","title":"Hinted handoff","body":"To keep three copies alive, the coordinator also sends C's copy to node D, the next healthy node on the ring, with a hint: \"this belongs to C\". D's ack means three copies exist (A, B and D), but C still has none. D keeps hints in a separate area. When gossip reports that C is back, D sends the hinted writes to C and deletes them. Hints are kept for a limited outage window, for example 3 hours. A slower background repair, anti-entropy using Merkle trees (hash trees that find which key ranges differ), fixes any remaining gap.","idea":"Hinted handoff catches a returning node up quickly; anti-entropy repair is the safety net.","caption":"D holds C's write with a hint, then forwards it when C returns.","focus":["coord","d","c"],"flows":[{"from":"coord","to":"d","kind":"replicate","label":"write + hint(C)"},{"from":"d","to":"c","kind":"replicate","label":"replay hint"}],"metrics":{"acks":"3","versions":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"d","text":"hint for C"},{"node":"d","text":"kept up to 3 h","at":"bottom"},{"node":"c","text":"no copy yet","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"CONFLICT","title":"Concurrent writes and vector clocks","body":"Two phones update the same cart at the same moment, through different coordinators (in the diagram, A and B each play coordinator), so neither sees the other's write. Each coordinator stamps its write with a vector clock: a list of (node, counter) pairs, here [A:1] and [B:1]. Neither clock includes the other, so the writes are concurrent, and the store keeps both as siblings. Replicas swap versions so each holds both. The next read returns both, the app merges them (for example, the union of the two carts), and it writes the result back with a clock covering both.","idea":"Vector clocks tell causal order from true concurrency; concurrent versions are kept and merged, not silently dropped.","caption":"Two concurrent versions are stored side by side as siblings.","focus":["client","coord","a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"coord","kind":"request","label":"PUT cart:42"},{"from":"client","to":"coord","kind":"request","label":"PUT cart:42"},{"from":"coord","to":"a","kind":"replicate","label":"v [A:1]"},{"from":"coord","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"v [B:1]"},{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"v [A:1]"},{"from":"b","to":"a","kind":"replicate","label":"v [B:1]"}],"metrics":{"acks":"2","versions":"2"},"chips":[{"node":"a","text":"v [A:1]"},{"node":"b","text":"v [B:1]"},{"node":"coord","text":"siblings: 2"},{"node":"a","text":"keeps both","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"The whole design","body":"A hash ring places each key on N = 3 replicas. Quorums W = 2 and R = 2 give fast reads and writes that overlap. Gossip finds failures, hinted handoff and Merkle-tree repair heal them, and vector clocks expose conflicts for the application to merge. The price is eventual consistency (replicas may briefly disagree, then converge). If you need strict single-copy ordering, use a consensus-based store instead.","idea":"Dynamo-style systems trade strict consistency for availability, using tunable quorums to choose the balance.","caption":"Every mechanism serves one goal: stay writable.","focus":["client","coord","a","b","c","d"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"acks":"2","latency":"4","versions":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"coord","text":"N=3 · W=2 · R=2","tone":"green"},{"node":"a","text":"eventual consistency"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A cluster has 10 nodes with virtual nodes and you add an 11th. Roughly what share of keys must move?","options":["About 100%, because the hash modulus changed","About 50%","About 1/11 of the keys, taken in small slices from many nodes","None; new nodes only take new keys"],"answer":2,"why":"Consistent hashing only reassigns the slices the new node takes over, roughly 1/N of the data, and virtual nodes spread that load across many donors."},{"q":"With N = 3 you choose W = 1 and R = 1. What is the main risk?","options":["Writes become very slow","The ring cannot be built","Vector clocks stop working","A read can hit a replica that has not received the latest write"],"answer":3,"why":"R + W = 2 is not greater than N = 3, so the read set and write set may not overlap, and stale reads are possible."},{"q":"Replica C is down and a write uses N = 3, W = 2. Which statement is correct?","options":["It succeeds with A and B; a hint can later bring C up to date","The write must fail until C returns","The coordinator must elect a leader first","Both A and B must be rolled back"],"answer":0,"why":"Two acks meet W, so the write succeeds. A stand-in node can hold a hint and replay it to C when gossip shows C is back."},{"q":"Two clients write the same key at the same time via different coordinators. Their vector clocks are [A:1] and [B:1]. In a Dynamo-style store that never silently drops concurrent updates, what should the store do?","options":["Keep the one with the later wall-clock timestamp and discard the other","Reject the second write","Keep both as siblings and let a later read reconcile them","Pick the lower node id"],"answer":2,"why":"Neither clock descends from the other, so the writes are concurrent. Dropping one by timestamp can lose data; keeping siblings lets the application merge."},{"q":"Why do nodes use gossip rather than one health-check master?","options":["Gossip is always faster than a master","A master is a single point of failure and bottleneck; gossip spreads information through random peer exchanges","Gossip guarantees strong consistency","A master makes strong consistency impossible"],"answer":1,"why":"Gossip needs no special node, scales with cluster size, and still converges in about log N rounds, though a view may be briefly stale."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Consistent hashing","def":"Maps keys and nodes onto a ring; a key belongs to the next node clockwise. Adding or removing a node moves only a small fraction of keys."},{"term":"Virtual nodes","def":"Each machine owns many small ring slices (commonly 16 to 256 depending on the system), giving even load and letting bigger machines take more slices."},{"term":"N, R, W","def":"N replicas per key; reads wait for R answers, writes wait for W acks. R + W > N makes reads overlap the latest write."},{"term":"Sloppy quorum","def":"When a home replica is down, a write may be accepted by another healthy node so the write still reaches W nodes."},{"term":"Hinted handoff","def":"A stand-in node stores a write meant for a down node, with a hint, and replays it once that node is back."},{"term":"Vector clock","def":"Per-node counters attached to a value. If one clock includes another, it is newer; if neither does, the writes are concurrent."},{"term":"Read repair / anti-entropy","def":"Read repair fixes stale replicas seen during reads. Anti-entropy compares Merkle trees in the background to fix the rest."},{"term":"Gossip protocol","def":"Nodes periodically swap state with random peers, so membership and failure news spreads to everyone in about log N rounds."}],"lab":{"prompt":"Design the key-value store for a cart service: 200,000 operations per second at peak (70% reads), 20 billion keys, average value 2 KB, always writable, and surviving the loss of one availability zone out of three. Choose partitioning, replication, quorum settings, failure handling and conflict handling, and justify each with numbers.","requirements":["Estimate raw data size and total size with replication, then the node count if one node holds 4 TB","State N, R and W and explain which failures and staleness they allow","Explain how keys map to nodes and how adding a node limits data movement","Describe how a write behaves when one replica is down, including hinted handoff","Describe how failure detection and membership spread without a master","Explain how concurrent writes are detected and who resolves them","Describe a repair path for data a hint did not cover"],"hints":["20 billion keys times 2 KB is about 40 TB before replication; multiply by N","Spread the three replicas across the three zones so a zone loss removes one copy","If R + W > N and writes reach the home replicas, a quorum read normally sees the latest acked write; sloppy quorums and hints weaken this","Carts merge by union (deleted items may reappear unless removals are tracked); counters can use CRDTs; a balance needs conditional or consensus-backed writes","Keep disks under about 50-60% full (compaction, repair need headroom), round nodes to a multiple of 3 for zone balance, and check survivors carry about 1.5x load after losing a zone","Hints expire, so something slower such as Merkle tree comparison must cover long outages"],"checklist":["Storage and node count are computed with replication included","Node count includes headroom and zone balance, and throughput (writes x N, reads x R) is sanity-checked","R + W vs N is stated and its consequence explained","Replicas are placed in different failure domains","A single node failure does not make writes fail","Conflict detection method and merge owner are named","Both short-term (hints) and long-term (anti-entropy) repair are covered"]},"isNew":true},{"slug":"design-payments","title":"Design a Payment System","subtitle":"Watch one card payment survive a timeout, a retry, a duplicate webhook and a bank mismatch without charging twice.","minutes":16,"level":"Advanced","learn":["How an idempotency key turns a retried payment request into a safe replay","Why a payment timeout means 'unknown', not 'failed'","How a double-entry ledger makes money movements auditable and self-checking","Why webhooks need dedup and why you cannot trust them alone","How reconciliation catches the errors every other layer missed","Why 'exactly-once' is really at-least-once plus idempotency"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Merchant app","kind":"client","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"api","label":"Payment API","kind":"service","x":30,"y":50},{"id":"idem","label":"Idempotency store","kind":"db","x":30,"y":16},{"id":"ledger","label":"Payments + ledger DB","kind":"db","x":56,"y":16},{"id":"psp","label":"PSP / card network","kind":"external","x":82,"y":50},{"id":"queue","label":"Webhook intake + queue","kind":"queue","x":82,"y":84},{"id":"worker","label":"Settlement worker","kind":"worker","x":56,"y":84}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"api"},{"from":"api","to":"idem"},{"from":"api","to":"ledger"},{"from":"api","to":"psp"},{"from":"psp","to":"queue"},{"from":"queue","to":"worker"},{"from":"worker","to":"ledger"},{"from":"worker","to":"psp"}],"metrics":[{"id":"charges","label":"Real charges","unit":"count"},{"id":"entries","label":"Ledger rows","unit":"rows"},{"id":"imbalance","label":"Debits - credits","unit":"cents"},{"id":"gap","label":"Recon gap","unit":"cents"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Charge once, no matter what","body":"A customer taps Pay on a 50.00 order. The request leaves the phone, but the response gets lost in a tunnel. The app cannot tell whether the charge happened, so it sends the request again. Networks fail all the time, so retries are certain. The design question is how to make a retry harmless, because charging someone twice is the one bug a payment system must never ship.","idea":"In payments, retries are guaranteed, so the system must be safe under repetition by design.","caption":"The merchant app sends a payment, and may have to send it again.","focus":["client","api"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"POST /payments"}],"metrics":{"charges":"0","entries":"0","imbalance":"0","gap":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"client","text":"response lost","tone":"red"},{"node":"client","text":"retry certain"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"The idempotency key","body":"The client makes a random key (a UUID) once per purchase and sends it in a header on every attempt. This is the idempotency key: \"idempotent\" means doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once. The API first inserts the key into a store with a unique constraint, saving a hash of the request body. Whoever inserts first owns the work. Later requests with the same key are not processed again; they get the saved outcome. The same key with a different body is rejected as a client bug. If the first attempt is still running, the duplicate waits or gets a 409 Conflict.","idea":"A unique-keyed insert before any side effect decides, atomically, who is allowed to do the work.","caption":"The API claims the key in a store before touching any money.","focus":["client","api","idem"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"Key: 7f3a..."},{"from":"api","to":"idem","kind":"request","label":"INSERT key"},{"from":"idem","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"claimed"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"client","text":"Key: 7f3a..."},{"node":"idem","text":"claimed","tone":"green"},{"node":"idem","text":"unique constraint"},{"node":"api","text":"409 if running","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Authorize with the PSP","body":"A payment service provider (PSP), such as a card processor, talks to the card networks and banks for us. The API records the payment as PENDING in the payments database, then calls the PSP. (The diagram draws the key store and payments database separately; if they share one database, the key claim and this record can be one transaction.) It also sends its own payment id as the PSP's idempotency key, so the PSP protects us the same way we protect the merchant. The PSP approves and returns an authorization id. We assume a one-step charge, with funds reaching the merchant days later.","idea":"Idempotency must hold at every hop: our API, and again on the call to the provider.","caption":"Happy path: the payment is recorded as pending, then approved by the PSP.","focus":["api","ledger","psp"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"ledger","kind":"request","label":"payment PENDING"},{"from":"api","to":"psp","kind":"request","label":"charge 50.00"},{"from":"psp","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"approved"}],"metrics":{"charges":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"ledger","text":"PENDING"},{"node":"psp","text":"approved","tone":"green"},{"node":"psp","text":"charges: 1","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The call times out","body":"Now replay it with the reply lost: the PSP charged the card, but its answer never reached us. After a 10 second timeout, the API cannot tell what happened. A timeout is not a failure; the money may have moved. So the API marks the payment UNKNOWN, keeps the key claimed, and does not ship the order or refund yet. Guessing \"failed\" here is how double charges and lost sales are born.","idea":"A timeout means 'unknown'. Treat unknown as its own state and resolve it by asking, never by guessing.","caption":"The PSP charged the card, but its reply is lost.","focus":["api","psp","ledger"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"psp","kind":"request","label":"charge 50.00"},{"from":"psp","to":"api","kind":"fail","label":"timeout"},{"from":"api","to":"ledger","kind":"request","label":"state=UNKNOWN"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"timeout 10 s","tone":"red"},{"node":"psp","text":"charged · reply lost","tone":"red"},{"node":"ledger","text":"UNKNOWN"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"The retry replays safely","body":"The client retries with the same key. The API finds the key already claimed and the payment UNKNOWN, so it does not make a second charge. It calls the PSP again with the same PSP idempotency key, and the PSP returns the original approval instead of charging twice. The API saves that result against the key, and every later retry gets it back instantly. The real charge count stays at one.","idea":"Retry with the same key is a question ('what happened?'), not a second command.","caption":"The same key at every step lets the PSP replay its first result.","focus":["client","api","idem","psp"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"retry, same key"},{"from":"api","to":"idem","kind":"request","label":"lookup key"},{"from":"idem","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"state: UNKNOWN"},{"from":"api","to":"psp","kind":"request","label":"same PSP key"},{"from":"psp","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"original result"},{"from":"api","to":"idem","kind":"request","label":"store result"},{"from":"api","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"200 approved"}],"metrics":{"charges":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"idem","text":"state: UNKNOWN"},{"node":"psp","text":"original result","tone":"green"},{"node":"client","text":"200 approved","tone":"green"},{"node":"psp","text":"charges: 1","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Double-entry ledger","body":"Once approved, the API writes the money movement to a ledger, a permanent record of every transaction. It is double-entry: each transaction has at least two rows that add up to zero. Here we debit the PSP receivable (money the PSP owes us) 50.00 and credit the merchant's payable 50.00, less a fee row if there is one. Ledger rows are never changed or deleted. A mistake is fixed by a new, opposite transaction. Because every transaction balances, total debits minus total credits must always be zero.","idea":"Double entry makes money conserved by construction, and any imbalance is instantly visible.","caption":"Two balanced rows are added in one database transaction.","focus":["api","ledger"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"ledger","kind":"request","label":"debit + credit"},{"from":"ledger","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"committed"}],"metrics":{"entries":"2","imbalance":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"ledger","text":"debit 50.00"},{"node":"ledger","text":"credit 50.00"},{"node":"ledger","text":"sum = 0","tone":"green","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Webhooks report later outcomes","body":"The PSP also sends webhooks (messages it posts to our server) such as payment.succeeded, because some outcomes arrive later and responses can be lost. Our endpoint checks the PSP's signature, puts each event on a queue, and replies fast. A worker marks the payment CAPTURED. Delivery is at-least-once, so an event can arrive three times or out of order. So we save each event id under a unique constraint and apply the state change in the same database transaction.","idea":"Webhooks are at-least-once and unordered, so consume them with dedup by event id and state checks.","caption":"A duplicate webhook is absorbed, so the ledger changes only once.","focus":["psp","queue","worker","ledger"],"flows":[{"from":"psp","to":"queue","kind":"async","label":"payment.succeeded"},{"from":"psp","to":"queue","kind":"async","label":"same event, again"},{"from":"queue","to":"worker","kind":"async","label":"event evt_91"},{"from":"worker","to":"ledger","kind":"request","label":"mark CAPTURED"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"queue","text":"evt_91 ×2 (dup)"},{"node":"worker","text":"dedup by event id"},{"node":"ledger","text":"CAPTURED","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"RECONCILE","title":"Reconciliation finds what slipped","body":"Even good code loses events: a webhook dropped after retries, a bug, a manual refund at the PSP. So each day the worker downloads the PSP's settlement report and compares it line by line with our ledger. Here the PSP lists a 50.00 refund our ledger never saw, leaving a gap of 5,000 cents. Bank statements get the same check. The worker raises a \"break\" for people to investigate instead of silently fixing it.","idea":"Reconciliation is the independent audit that catches errors the real-time path cannot see.","caption":"The daily report versus the ledger exposes a 50.00 mismatch.","focus":["worker","psp","ledger"],"flows":[{"from":"worker","to":"psp","kind":"request","label":"settlement report"},{"from":"psp","to":"worker","kind":"response","label":"report rows"},{"from":"worker","to":"ledger","kind":"request","label":"compare"},{"from":"ledger","to":"worker","kind":"fail","label":"50.00 missing"}],"metrics":{"gap":"5000"},"chips":[{"node":"psp","text":"refund 50.00"},{"node":"ledger","text":"50.00 missing","tone":"red"},{"node":"worker","text":"gap 5,000 cents","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Exactly-once is a promise we build","body":"No network gives true exactly-once delivery. What we build is at-least-once delivery plus idempotent handlers, which has the same effect. The remaining gap is closed with a compensating ledger entry that books the missing refund, never by editing history. Rules of thumb: keep idempotency keys at least 24 hours (longer than clients retry, and resolve UNKNOWN payments before the PSP's own key expires); keep ledger rows for the legal retention period, often years. Store money as whole numbers in cents, never as floats.","idea":"Effectively-once = retries + idempotency + append-only corrections. Never edit the past; add to it.","caption":"A compensating entry books the refund and the gap closes.","focus":["worker","ledger"],"flows":[{"from":"worker","to":"ledger","kind":"request","label":"adjust: refund"},{"from":"ledger","to":"worker","kind":"response","label":"balanced"}],"metrics":{"entries":"4","imbalance":"0","gap":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"ledger","text":"adjust: refund"},{"node":"ledger","text":"balanced · 4 rows","tone":"green"},{"node":"worker","text":"gap 0","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Four layers of safety","body":"Four layers of safety. Idempotency keys make client retries safe. A PENDING, UNKNOWN, CAPTURED state machine makes timeouts safe. The double-entry, append-only ledger makes the books check themselves. Deduplicated webhooks and daily reconciliation catch whatever still slips through. Each layer assumes the one before it will sometimes fail, and that mindset is the whole design.","idea":"Assume every step can repeat or fail; build each layer so repeating it changes nothing.","caption":"Safe retries, balanced books, and an independent audit.","focus":["client","api","idem","ledger","psp","queue","worker"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"idem","text":"same key, same result"},{"node":"ledger","text":"append-only · sum 0","tone":"green"},{"node":"worker","text":"daily recon"},{"node":"queue","text":"webhooks deduped"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A client sends POST /payments with key K and the connection drops before any response. It retries with key K. What should the API do if the first attempt already succeeded?","options":["Process it again, because the first response was never seen","Return the stored result of the first attempt without charging again","Reject the retry with an error because the key is already used","Delete the key and start fresh so the data is clean"],"answer":1,"why":"The key exists so a retry is a replay. The API returns the saved outcome, so the card is charged once and the client still learns the result."},{"q":"The PSP call times out after 10 seconds. What is the correct payment state?","options":["FAILED, so the customer can try again immediately","SUCCEEDED, since most calls succeed","UNKNOWN until resolved by a retry with the same key, a status lookup or a webhook","CANCELLED and refunded automatically"],"answer":2,"why":"A timeout does not tell you whether the card was charged. Marking it failed risks a double charge on retry; marking it success risks shipping unpaid goods. Resolve it by asking."},{"q":"Why is a payment ledger append-only with balanced debit and credit rows?","options":["It uses less disk space than updating rows","It lets the database skip transactions","It removes the need for reconciliation","Corrections are new opposite entries, so history is auditable and total debits always equal credits"],"answer":3,"why":"Never editing rows preserves the audit trail, and balanced transactions make any bug show up as a nonzero sum. It does not replace reconciliation, which compares against outside parties."},{"q":"The same payment.succeeded webhook arrives three times. Which design handles it correctly?","options":["Store the event id with a unique constraint and ignore repeats, also checking the payment's current state","Trust the PSP to send each event exactly once, so no extra checks are needed","Apply the event every time it arrives so that no retry is ever lost","Drop all webhooks and rely only on the synchronous API response instead"],"answer":0,"why":"Webhook delivery is at-least-once and can be reordered. Dedup by event id plus state checks makes repeated delivery harmless, and you still keep webhooks because they report later changes."},{"q":"Every webhook we received was processed and every ledger transaction balanced, yet the daily PSP report shows a refund our ledger lacks. What does this show?","options":["Reconciliation duplicates the webhook checks and can be removed to save cost","The ledger must be edited in place so that it matches the PSP report again","The idempotency store is corrupted and every stored key must be purged","Internal consistency does not prove agreement with the outside world, so you need the independent comparison"],"answer":3,"why":"A ledger can balance perfectly and still be wrong about reality, for example a missed event. Reconciliation against the PSP and bank is the check that finds it, fixed with a compensating entry."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Idempotency key","def":"Client-chosen unique id per purchase intent, sent on every retry. The server claims it with a unique insert and replays the saved result."},{"term":"Request hash","def":"Fingerprint of the body saved with the key. Same key with a different body is a client error, not a replay."},{"term":"UNKNOWN state","def":"Result of a timeout after a side effect may have happened. Resolve by retry with the same key, status query or webhook; never guess."},{"term":"Double-entry ledger","def":"Append-only rows where every transaction has debits equal to credits. Total debits minus total credits is always zero; fix errors with new entries."},{"term":"Minor units","def":"Store money as integers (cents) with a currency code. Floats cause rounding drift."},{"term":"Webhook dedup","def":"Unique constraint on the provider event id, stored in the same transaction as the state change, plus state checks, so duplicates and reordering are harmless."},{"term":"Reconciliation","def":"Daily comparison of ledger with PSP and bank reports. Differences become breaks that are investigated; true ledger gaps are fixed with new adjusting entries."},{"term":"Effectively-once","def":"At-least-once delivery plus idempotent processing. True exactly-once delivery is not available over a network."}],"lab":{"prompt":"Design the backend for a payments product handling 10 million card payments per day. Cover the idempotent payment API, the state machine, the ledger schema, PSP integration, webhook handling and reconciliation. Show how a double charge is prevented when the client retries after a timeout.","requirements":["Compute average and peak payments per second, stating your peak factor","Specify the idempotency key flow: where it is stored, uniqueness, request hash, and retention period","Define the payment states and the transitions allowed, including UNKNOWN","Sketch the ledger tables and show one payment and one refund as balanced entries in integer minor units","Explain how you call the PSP safely, including its idempotency key and timeouts","Describe webhook ingestion: ack quickly, dedup by event id, handle out-of-order events","Describe the reconciliation job, what a break is, and how you correct it without editing history"],"hints":["10M per day divided by 86,400 seconds is about 115 per second; with a peak factor of 10 that is roughly 1,200 per second. One well-provisioned primary can handle that; shard by merchant only for headroom.","Use a relational database with ACID transactions for the ledger and payments; write the payment row and ledger rows in one transaction.","A UNIQUE(merchant_id, idempotency_key) constraint is the simplest correct claim mechanism; a Redis lock alone can lose state.","Derive the PSP idempotency key from your payment id so every retry of the same payment hits the same provider key.","Keep a stuck-payment sweeper that queries the PSP for payments in UNKNOWN for longer than a few minutes.","Reconciliation should compare three sources: PSP report, bank settlement and your ledger, matching on the provider reference."],"checklist":["Retrying with the same key can never create a second charge","A timeout after the PSP call leaves the payment in UNKNOWN, not FAILED","Every ledger transaction sums to zero and no row is updated or deleted","Duplicate and out-of-order webhooks are harmless","Money is stored as integers with a currency","Reconciliation runs daily and corrections are compensating entries"]},"isNew":true},{"slug":"design-file-sync","title":"Design File Sync (Dropbox-like)","subtitle":"Edit one spot in a 100 MiB file and watch only 4 MiB travel, then see how your other laptop learns about it.","minutes":14,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How files are split into content-hashed blocks, and why identical blocks are stored once","Why file metadata and file bytes live in two different stores","How a device finds out what changed, using a cursor instead of trusting notifications","How delta sync moves only changed blocks in both directions","How to detect a conflict with base versions and resolve it without losing data"],"nodes":[{"id":"laptop","label":"Laptop A","kind":"client","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"api","label":"Sync API","kind":"service","x":36,"y":50},{"id":"meta","label":"Metadata DB","kind":"db","x":63,"y":20},{"id":"blocks","label":"Block store","kind":"storage","x":63,"y":80},{"id":"notify","label":"Notify service","kind":"queue","x":63,"y":50},{"id":"laptopb","label":"Laptop B","kind":"client","x":90,"y":25}],"edges":[{"from":"laptop","to":"api"},{"from":"laptop","to":"blocks"},{"from":"api","to":"meta"},{"from":"api","to":"notify"},{"from":"notify","to":"laptopb"},{"from":"laptopb","to":"api"},{"from":"laptopb","to":"blocks"}],"metrics":[{"id":"blocks","label":"Blocks to send","unit":"blocks"},{"id":"sent","label":"Data moved","unit":"MiB"},{"id":"saved","label":"Saved","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One small edit, one big file","body":"You fix a typo in a 100 MiB slide deck on Laptop A. Laptop B should show the fix within seconds. The naive plan is to upload all 100 MiB and make B download all 100 MiB. Multiply that by millions of users saving files all day and the bandwidth bill is absurd. We want to move only what changed. (MiB means mebibyte, about 1.05 MB; think of it as \"a megabyte\". Pretend the deck is stored uncompressed.)","idea":"Sync is a bandwidth problem first: send the change, not the file.","caption":"A 100 MiB file has just changed on Laptop A.","focus":["laptop"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"laptop","text":"100 MiB deck"}]},{"tag":"CHUNKING","title":"Cut the file into blocks","body":"The sync client cuts the file into fixed 4 MiB blocks, so 100 MiB becomes 25 blocks. It computes a SHA-256 hash of each block, a short fingerprint of its bytes. The hash becomes the block's name, because identical bytes always give the same hash. The file is now just an ordered list of 25 hashes. All this happens on the laptop, before any network call. Assume the app edits in place without changing the length (like a database file or disk image). Zipped formats like .pptx can shift bytes; the trade-off step covers that.","idea":"A file becomes an ordered list of content hashes, and a block's name is its content.","caption":"Laptop A hashes its 25 blocks locally.","focus":["laptop"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"blocks":"25","saved":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"laptop","text":"25 blocks × 4 MiB"},{"node":"laptop","text":"SHA-256 per block","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"DEDUP","title":"Ask which blocks are missing","body":"Laptop A sends the 25 hashes to the Sync API. The API asks the metadata database which hashes the block store already holds. Only one is unknown, because the edit changed just one block; the other 24 are the same as in the previous version. So the server answers \"send me 1 block\", a saving of 96%. The same check also avoids duplicates across files and users: a block the system already has is never uploaded again.","idea":"Because blocks are named by content, the server can say exactly which ones it still needs.","caption":"The server replies: I need 1 of your 25 blocks.","focus":["laptop","api","meta"],"flows":[{"from":"laptop","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"25 hashes"},{"from":"api","to":"meta","kind":"request","label":"which exist?"},{"from":"meta","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"24 known"},{"from":"api","to":"laptop","kind":"response","label":"send 1 block"}],"metrics":{"blocks":"1","sent":"0","saved":"96"},"chips":[{"node":"meta","text":"24 known","tone":"green"},{"node":"api","text":"send 1 block"},{"node":"laptop","kind":"progress","value":96,"text":"96%","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"UPLOAD","title":"Upload only the changed block","body":"Laptop A uploads the single 4 MiB block straight to the block store, usually through a short-lived signed URL the API handed out, so file bytes never pass through the API servers. Blocks are immutable: written once under their hash and never modified. That makes retries safe, because uploading the same block again writes identical bytes. The store (or a checker) recomputes the SHA-256 of what arrives and rejects a mismatch, so a block's name can be trusted.","idea":"Immutable, content-addressed blocks make uploads idempotent (doing it twice has the same effect as once) and cacheable.","caption":"One 4 MiB block reaches the block store.","focus":["laptop","blocks"],"flows":[{"from":"laptop","to":"blocks","kind":"request","label":"block 7f3a (4 MiB)"},{"from":"blocks","to":"laptop","kind":"response","label":"stored"}],"metrics":{"blocks":"0","sent":"4","saved":"96"},"chips":[{"node":"blocks","text":"block 7f3a · 4 MiB"},{"node":"blocks","text":"SHA-256 verified","at":"bottom"},{"node":"blocks","text":"stored","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"METADATA","title":"Commit the new version","body":"Now the client tells the API: \"/deck.pptx version 8 is this list of 25 hashes, built on top of version 7.\" In one database transaction, the metadata database checks that the current version is still 7, checks that every listed block exists, adds version 8, and appends an entry to the user's change log. Metadata is small and needs strong consistency; blocks are huge and need cheap, durable storage. Committing only after all blocks exist means no version ever points at a missing block.","idea":"Keep tiny, consistent metadata apart from huge, immutable bytes, and commit metadata last.","caption":"The metadata database records version 8.","focus":["laptop","api","meta"],"flows":[{"from":"laptop","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"commit v8 on v7"},{"from":"api","to":"meta","kind":"request","label":"write v8"},{"from":"meta","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"ok"},{"from":"api","to":"laptop","kind":"response","label":"v8 saved"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"meta","text":"v8 on top of v7"},{"node":"meta","text":"one transaction","at":"bottom"},{"node":"laptop","text":"v8 saved","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"NOTIFY","title":"Tell the other devices","body":"After the commit, the API sends a tiny event to the notify service: \"something changed in this user's folder\". Laptop B keeps a long-lived connection open to that service (long poll or WebSocket) and receives a ping. The ping carries no file data, and it can be lost, delayed or duplicated. It is only a nudge telling B to go and ask for changes.","idea":"Notifications are a cheap hint to wake a device up, not the source of truth.","caption":"A small ping reaches Laptop B.","focus":["api","notify","laptopb"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"notify","kind":"async","label":"folder changed"},{"from":"notify","to":"laptopb","kind":"async","label":"ping"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"notify","text":"folder changed"},{"node":"laptopb","text":"ping · no data"}]},{"tag":"DELTA SYNC","title":"Laptop B pulls the delta","body":"Laptop B asks the API for changes since its cursor, a position number it saved after its last sync (here 981). The API answers that deck.pptx is now version 8 with a new hash list. B compares that list with the one it has, finds one hash it lacks, downloads just that block, and rebuilds the file locally. If the ping had been lost, B would still catch up at its next poll or reconnect, because asking \"what is new after N?\" is safe to repeat.","idea":"A cursor turns sync into 'give me everything after N', which is repeatable and loss-tolerant.","caption":"B downloads one block, not 100 MiB.","focus":["laptopb","api","blocks"],"flows":[{"from":"laptopb","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"changes after 981"},{"from":"api","to":"laptopb","kind":"response","label":"v8 hash list"},{"from":"laptopb","to":"blocks","kind":"request","label":"block 7f3a"},{"from":"blocks","to":"laptopb","kind":"response","label":"4 MiB"}],"metrics":{"sent":"4","saved":"96"},"chips":[{"node":"laptopb","text":"cursor 981"},{"node":"api","text":"v8 hash list"},{"node":"blocks","text":"1 block · 4 MiB"},{"node":"laptopb","kind":"progress","value":96,"text":"96%","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Both laptops edited offline","body":"Suppose B was offline and edited the same file starting from version 7, while A already saved version 8. When B reconnects and commits \"new version on top of 7\", the API sees the current version is 8, not 7, and rejects it as a conflict. Picking the edit with the later timestamp (last-writer-wins) would silently destroy someone's work, and device clocks are unreliable. Instead the server keeps v8 and saves B's edit as a separate \"conflicted copy\" for a person to merge.","idea":"Detect conflicts by comparing base version to head; resolve by keeping both rather than guessing.","caption":"B's commit is built on the old version 7, so it is rejected.","focus":["laptopb","api","meta"],"flows":[{"from":"laptopb","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"commit on v7"},{"from":"api","to":"meta","kind":"request","label":"head is v8?"},{"from":"meta","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"head is v8"},{"from":"api","to":"laptopb","kind":"fail","label":"409 conflict"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"meta","text":"head is v8"},{"node":"api","text":"409 conflict","tone":"red"},{"node":"laptopb","text":"conflicted copy","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"How big should a block be?","body":"Small blocks dedup and sync deltas better, but mean more hashes, more metadata rows and more requests. Big blocks are cheap to track but resend more on every edit. About 4 MiB is a common middle ground. One weakness: fixed boundaries break when bytes are inserted near the start, because every later block shifts. Content-defined chunking (cut points chosen from the data itself) fixes that, at the cost of more client CPU.","idea":"Block size trades metadata overhead against bytes resent; fixed cuts are simple, content-defined cuts survive insertions.","caption":"Block size balances metadata overhead against bandwidth.","focus":["meta","blocks"],"flows":[],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"blocks","text":"≈ 4 MiB blocks"},{"node":"meta","text":"small = more rows"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Only the change travels","body":"The client chunks and hashes the file. The API checks which hashes are already known, and only the missing blocks are uploaded to the block store. A final atomic metadata commit publishes the new version. A small notification wakes the other devices, which pull changes after their cursor and fetch only the new blocks. Stale commits are caught by the version check and kept as separate copies.","idea":"Hash blocks, store each once, commit metadata last, sync by cursor, and never silently overwrite.","caption":"A 100 MiB edit cost about 4 MiB each way.","focus":["laptop","api","meta","blocks","notify","laptopb"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"blocks":"0","sent":"4","saved":"96"},"chips":[{"node":"blocks","text":"1 of 25 uploaded","tone":"green"},{"node":"meta","text":"v8 committed"},{"node":"notify","text":"nudge only"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A 1 GiB file is stored as fixed 4 MiB blocks. You overwrite some bytes in the middle without changing the file length, touching one block. About how much must be uploaded?","options":["The whole 1 GiB, since the file changed","About 1 MiB, the size of the edit","About 4 MiB, the one affected block","About 256 MiB, a quarter of the blocks"],"answer":2,"why":"Only the block containing the edit gets a new hash. The other 255 blocks hash the same and are already stored, so one 4 MiB block moves."},{"q":"Why does the client commit the new file version to the metadata database only after the blocks are uploaded?","options":["So no committed version can reference a block that does not exist yet","Because the metadata database is faster than the block store","To make the upload bytes pass through the API","To avoid needing hashes at all"],"answer":0,"why":"If metadata were committed first, another device could read a version whose blocks are missing. Blocks first, pointer last makes the commit the visible switch."},{"q":"The notification service drops the ping for Laptop B during a brief outage. What keeps B from staying stale forever?","options":["Laptop B stays stale until the user manually re-uploads the file","The Sync API pushes the whole file to B on every commit","B's clock detects the newer file timestamp","B asks for changes after its saved cursor when it reconnects or polls"],"answer":3,"why":"Notifications are only hints. The cursor query returns every change after the last position B saw, so a missed ping delays sync but cannot lose a change."},{"q":"Two devices edit the same file offline from version 7. Which handling best avoids losing work?","options":["Keep whichever edit has the later device timestamp and drop the other","Reject the stale commit and keep both versions as a conflicted copy","Lock the file for every user at all times so edits never overlap","Merge the two files byte by byte automatically"],"answer":1,"why":"Device clocks drift, so timestamp wins can destroy data, and generic byte merges corrupt binary formats. Detecting a stale base and keeping both copies is safe and simple."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Block / chunk","def":"A fixed-size slice of a file (about 4 MiB here). The unit of upload, storage, and dedup."},{"term":"Content-addressed storage","def":"Blocks are stored under the hash of their bytes, so equal content has one name and is stored once."},{"term":"Dedup","def":"Skip storing or uploading a block already held. Caveat: cross-user dedup can reveal that someone has a file; dedup per account or require proof of possession."},{"term":"Metadata store","def":"Small, consistent records: paths, versions, ordered block-hash lists, sharing. Needs transactions and sharding by account."},{"term":"Block store","def":"Cheap, durable object storage for immutable blocks, with replication or erasure coding (splitting data with parity so lost pieces can be rebuilt). Clients reach it through signed URLs."},{"term":"Block garbage collection","def":"Delete blocks no version references, found by reference counts or mark-and-sweep, after a grace period so an in-flight commit cannot lose a block it just deduped against."},{"term":"Cursor","def":"A per-device position in the change log. 'Changes after cursor N' is safe to repeat and survives lost notifications."},{"term":"Conflict handling","def":"Commit carries its base version; if head moved on, reject and keep both copies instead of last-writer-wins."}],"lab":{"prompt":"Design a Dropbox-like file sync service for 100 million registered users, about 20 million active per day, averaging 20 file changes per day each. Sketch the client, the services, the two stores, and the data model, then explain what happens when two devices edit the same file offline.","requirements":["Edits to large files upload only changed data, not the whole file","A new version becomes visible to other devices only when all of its blocks are durably stored","Other devices learn of changes within a few seconds when online and catch up after being offline","Never silently lose a user's edit, even in a conflict","Identical content is stored once","Handle roughly 400 million file changes per day, about 4,600 commits per second on average and several times that at peak"],"hints":["Divide daily commits by 86,400 seconds, then multiply by a peak factor of 3 or more","Decide what the metadata row for a file version holds: version number, base version, ordered hash list","Think about what the per-user change log and its cursor look like, and what shards it","Let clients upload to the block store with signed URLs so the API tier stays light","Ask what a client must send with a commit so the server can detect a stale base","Blocks are shared. Decide how you know one is unreferenced (reference counts or mark-and-sweep), and wait a grace period before deleting so an in-flight commit cannot lose a block it just deduped against","Consider the privacy risk of cross-user dedup and what you would do about it"],"checklist":["Client chunking and hashing step with a stated block size","Hash-existence check that returns only the missing blocks","Separate metadata store and block store with reasons","Blocks-first, metadata-commit-last ordering","Per-user change log with a cursor for delta sync","Notification path treated as a hint, with a polling fallback","Base-version conflict check and a keep-both resolution","Block garbage collection plan for blocks no version references"]},"isNew":true},{"slug":"design-ticketing","title":"Design Ticket Booking","subtitle":"Watch 200,000 fans fight for 20,000 seats, and see how holds, expiry and a waiting room prevent double booking.","minutes":14,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why seat inventory is a contention problem, not a throughput problem","How a temporary hold with an expiry claims a seat atomically","Why the database, not the app server, must be the final judge of who owns a seat","How a virtual waiting room turns a stampede into a steady trickle","How payment, hold expiry and confirmation fit into one seat state machine"],"nodes":[{"id":"fans","label":"Fans (browsers)","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"room","label":"Waiting room","kind":"queue","x":28,"y":22},{"id":"api","label":"Booking API","kind":"service","x":30,"y":76},{"id":"hold","label":"Hold store (Redis)","kind":"cache","x":55,"y":22},{"id":"db","label":"Seat database","kind":"db","x":62,"y":76},{"id":"pay","label":"Payment provider","kind":"external","x":90,"y":50},{"id":"sweeper","label":"Expiry sweeper","kind":"worker","x":88,"y":84}],"edges":[{"from":"fans","to":"room"},{"from":"room","to":"api"},{"from":"fans","to":"api"},{"from":"api","to":"hold"},{"from":"api","to":"db"},{"from":"api","to":"pay"},{"from":"sweeper","to":"db"},{"from":"hold","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"wait","label":"In waiting room","unit":"people"},{"id":"active","label":"Shopping now","unit":"people"},{"id":"held","label":"Seats held","unit":"seats"},{"id":"sold","label":"Seats sold","unit":"seats"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"20,000 seats, 200,000 fans","body":"Tickets for a stadium show go on sale at 10:00. The venue has 20,000 seats, and about 200,000 fans arrive in the first minute. Most of the traffic is not buying yet; it is refreshing and picking seats. Speed alone is not the hard part. Two people must never end up owning the same seat, and a seat in someone's cart must not be lost forever if they walk away.","idea":"Ticketing is a contention problem: many users, a small fixed inventory, and zero tolerance for double sales.","caption":"A huge crowd aims at a small, fixed number of seats.","focus":["fans","api","db"],"flows":[{"from":"fans","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"200k arrive"}],"metrics":{"wait":"0","active":"200000","held":"0","sold":"0"},"chips":[{"node":"fans","text":"200,000 arrive"},{"node":"db","text":"20,000 seats"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The naive version breaks","body":"A simple design reads the seat, checks that it is free, then writes the sale. But two requests can both read \"free\" before either one writes, so both succeed. This is a race condition: the check and the write are separate steps, so another request can slip in between. With thousands of people clicking seat A12 at once, double sales become very likely, and adding more servers does not fix it.","idea":"Check-then-write is unsafe; the check and the claim must be one atomic operation.","caption":"Two requests both see A12 as free, and both write.","focus":["api","db"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"read A12: free"},{"from":"api","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"both write A12"}],"metrics":{},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"race condition","tone":"red"},{"node":"db","text":"A12: both write","tone":"red"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Claim the seat atomically","body":"Let the database decide. A conditional update, \"set owner = me WHERE seat = A12 AND status = available\", succeeds for exactly one caller, because the database handles writes to the same row one at a time. The loser gets \"0 rows changed\" and is asked to pick another seat. A version column that must still match when you write, or a unique constraint on (event_id, seat_id) in a tickets table, gives the same guarantee.","idea":"Let a single authority with an atomic conditional write decide; losers just retry elsewhere.","caption":"One conditional UPDATE wins; the others get zero rows.","focus":["api","db"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"UPDATE if free"},{"from":"db","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"1 row: yours"},{"from":"db","to":"api","kind":"fail","label":"0 rows: taken"}],"metrics":{"held":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"UPDATE if free"},{"node":"db","text":"1 row: yours","tone":"green"},{"node":"db","text":"0 rows: taken","tone":"red","at":"bottom"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Holds with an expiry","body":"Buying takes minutes: choose seats, enter card details, maybe pass a bank check. So a click moves the seat from available to held until a deadline, typically 5 to 10 minutes (a rule of thumb). The seat row stores status, held_by and held_until, claimed with the same conditional UPDATE, which may also take a seat whose hold has expired. Many systems also set a Redis key with SET NX EX (set only if absent, with a time-to-live) as a fast first gate. The database row is what survives a Redis restart.","idea":"A hold is a lease: it reserves the seat now and releases itself if the buyer goes quiet.","caption":"A12 and three other seats are held for 8 minutes.","focus":["api","hold","db"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"hold","kind":"request","label":"SET A12 NX EX 480"},{"from":"hold","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"OK: held"},{"from":"api","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"row: held_until"}],"metrics":{"held":"4"},"chips":[{"node":"hold","text":"A12 held · 8 min"},{"node":"hold","text":"SET NX EX 480","at":"bottom"},{"node":"db","text":"held_until set"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Pay, then confirm","body":"The user pays while the hold is ticking. When payment starts, mark the seat payment_pending and extend the hold past the payment timeout, so it cannot expire mid-checkout. The API calls the payment provider with an idempotency key, so a retry cannot charge twice (payments have their own lesson). On success, one database transaction flips the seat to sold, but only if it is still held by this buyer. If the hold was somehow lost, refund the payment; this should be rare.","idea":"Seat state changes to sold only inside a transaction that re-checks ownership after payment succeeds.","caption":"Payment succeeds, and the seat becomes sold.","focus":["api","pay","db"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"pay","kind":"request","label":"charge + idem key"},{"from":"pay","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"paid"},{"from":"api","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"pending -> SOLD"}],"metrics":{"held":"3","sold":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"api","text":"idempotency key"},{"node":"pay","text":"paid","tone":"green"},{"node":"db","text":"pending → SOLD","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The buyer walks away","body":"Most holds are abandoned: people close the tab or change their mind. When the TTL passes, the Redis key disappears, but that only frees the fast gate; Redis does not tell the database. The database row is freed either by a sweeper (a background job that resets seats whose held_until has passed) or by the next conditional UPDATE, which may take an expired seat directly. This also covers a Redis restart that forgot every hold. A late payment on a lost hold must be refunded, never double sold.","idea":"Expiry returns abandoned inventory to the pool, and the database row remains the source of truth.","caption":"Expired holds release their seats back to sale.","focus":["sweeper","db"],"flows":[{"from":"sweeper","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"release expired"}],"metrics":{"held":"1"},"chips":[{"node":"hold","text":"TTL passed"},{"node":"sweeper","text":"release expired"},{"node":"db","text":"seat → available","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"The stampede problem","body":"Atomic claims keep data correct, but not the system healthy. If 200,000 users hammer the seat map, the database melts and everyone gets errors, even people who would have succeeded. Seat maps are mostly reads, so serve them from a cache that can be a second or two out of date (the Redis node doubles as the seat-map cache). A clicked seat may turn out to be taken, and that is fine: the claim, not the display, enforces correctness.","idea":"Protect the write path: cache reads loosely, and let only the claim be strict.","caption":"Too many shoppers overload the database, so the seat map comes from cache.","focus":["fans","api","hold","db"],"flows":[{"from":"fans","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"200k clicks"},{"from":"api","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"overloaded"},{"from":"api","to":"hold","kind":"request","label":"seat map?"},{"from":"hold","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"cached seat map"},{"from":"api","to":"fans","kind":"response","label":"seat map"}],"metrics":{"active":"200000"},"chips":[{"node":"fans","text":"200,000 clicks"},{"node":"db","text":"overloaded","tone":"red"},{"node":"hold","text":"seat map · 1-2 s stale","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Virtual waiting room","body":"Put a queue in front: a virtual waiting room. Everyone who arrives in the first few minutes gets a random position, so refreshing early earns nothing; later arrivals queue behind them. They wait on a light page served from a CDN (a network of edge caches). The room admits a limited number at a time, say 2,000 (a rule of thumb: tune it to what the booking path can handle), and lets in more as others finish or time out. Admitted users carry a signed token the API checks. The database now sees thousands of users, not hundreds of thousands.","idea":"A waiting room converts a spike into a steady, admission-controlled flow the backend can handle.","caption":"Only 2,000 shoppers are admitted at a time.","focus":["fans","room","api"],"flows":[{"from":"fans","to":"room","kind":"request","label":"join queue"},{"from":"room","to":"fans","kind":"response","label":"signed token"},{"from":"room","to":"api","kind":"async","label":"admit 2,000"},{"from":"fans","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"buy, with token"}],"metrics":{"wait":"198000","active":"2000"},"chips":[{"node":"room","text":"198,000 waiting"},{"node":"api","text":"2,000 shopping"},{"node":"fans","text":"signed token","tone":"green"}]},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Fairness and sellouts","body":"Random order is fairer than first-come-first-served, which rewards fast connections and bots. Limit tickets per account (say 4). Claim multi-seat requests in one all-or-nothing transaction, locking seats in a fixed order (for example by seat id) to avoid deadlocks, where two buyers each wait on the other. Require login before admission to slow scalpers. If the event sells out in minutes, show \"sold out\" to everyone still waiting. The cost is complexity: the waiting room is another system that must never fall over.","idea":"Admission control is a product decision as well as an engineering one: fairness, anti-bot, and honesty.","caption":"Admitted shoppers hold and buy while the rest wait.","focus":["room","api","hold"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"hold","kind":"request","label":"hold up to 4"},{"from":"api","to":"room","kind":"async","label":"slot freed"}],"metrics":{"wait":"190000","active":"2000","held":"800","sold":"6000"},"chips":[{"node":"room","text":"190,000 waiting"},{"node":"hold","text":"held: 800"},{"node":"db","text":"sold: 6,000"},{"node":"api","text":"max 4 / account"}]},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Safe under pressure","body":"Throttle the crowd with a waiting room, hold seats with expiring leases, take payment, and let the database's atomic write decide the final owner. The cache and hold store absorb pressure; the database guarantees no seat is sold twice. Each seat moves through available, held, payment_pending and sold, and goes back to available if a hold expires or payment fails.","idea":"Correctness lives in one atomic claim; everything else exists to keep that claim fast and fair.","caption":"Every seat has exactly one owner at a time.","focus":["fans","room","api","hold","db","pay","sweeper"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"wait":"0","active":"0","held":"0","sold":"20000"},"chips":[{"node":"db","text":"20,000 sold","tone":"green"},{"node":"hold","text":"expiring holds"},{"node":"api","text":"one owner / seat"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two buyers click seat A12 in the same millisecond. Which design reliably prevents a double sale?","options":["Check the seat is free in app code, then write the sale","Use more app servers so each request is faster","A conditional database write that only succeeds if the seat is still available","Show a warning in the UI when someone else is looking at the seat"],"answer":2,"why":"Only an atomic claim by one authority guarantees a single winner. Check-then-write has a race window, faster servers just shrink it, and UI warnings are advisory."},{"q":"Why give a seat hold a TTL instead of waiting for the user to cancel?","options":["TTLs make payments faster","Abandoned carts are common, and expiry returns those seats to sale automatically","It removes the need for a database","It lets two users hold the same seat"],"answer":1,"why":"Many users never finish. A TTL makes the hold a lease that frees itself, so inventory is not stranded by people who left."},{"q":"The seat map is cached for 2 seconds and shows A12 free, but it was just taken. What happens when a user clicks it?","options":["They buy it twice","The cache is corrupted and must be flushed","The hold store grants it anyway","The atomic claim fails and they are asked to pick another seat"],"answer":3,"why":"Displays may be stale, but correctness is enforced at claim time, so the loser simply gets a 'taken' response."},{"q":"What is the main reason to add a virtual waiting room?","options":["To limit concurrent shoppers so the booking path stays healthy","To make the atomic update unnecessary","To make payments cheaper","To sort buyers by internet speed"],"answer":0,"why":"The waiting room admits a controlled number of users. It protects capacity and does not replace atomic claims."},{"q":"Despite hold extension, a payment succeeds at 8 minutes 5 seconds, but the hold expired at 8 minutes and another user holds the seat. What should the system do?","options":["Give the seat to both users","Confirm anyway and ignore the other hold","Fail the confirmation and refund, because the hold is no longer theirs","Delete the other user's hold silently"],"answer":2,"why":"Confirmation must re-check ownership inside the transaction. If the lease is lost, refund rather than breaking the one-owner rule."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Atomic claim","def":"A single conditional write (UPDATE ... WHERE status = available) that only one concurrent caller can win."},{"term":"Hold (lease)","def":"A temporary reservation with a deadline, typically 5 to 10 minutes, that frees itself if unused."},{"term":"SET NX EX","def":"Redis command: set a key only if absent, with an expiry. A fast one-winner gate in front of the database row, which still carries the hold."},{"term":"Seat state machine","def":"available -> held -> payment_pending -> sold, with held/payment_pending -> available on expiry, failure or cancel. Only legal transitions are allowed."},{"term":"Waiting room","def":"Admission-controlled queue that lets in a fixed number of shoppers; often randomised for fairness."},{"term":"Source of truth","def":"The seat row (status, held_by, held_until) records the hold and final ownership. Caches and Redis are fast helpers, never the last word."},{"term":"Idempotency key","def":"A unique ID on the payment call so retries never charge twice."}],"lab":{"prompt":"Design ticket sales for a 50,000-seat stadium where 500,000 people show up at on-sale time. Describe the seat model, how holds work, how double booking is prevented, how the waiting room protects the backend, and how failures like abandoned carts and late payments are handled.","requirements":["Define seat states and the allowed transitions between them","Choose a hold duration and explain how expiry releases seats","Show the exact write that makes a claim atomic","Describe how the waiting room admits users and what token the API checks","Estimate peak requests per second to the booking path with and without the room","Explain what happens if payment succeeds after the hold expired","Say how the seat map stays fast despite heavy reads"],"hints":["If 500,000 users each make 1 request per second, that is 500,000 requests per second; the room might cap shoppers at 5,000","A unique constraint on (event_id, seat_id) can enforce one owner even if app code has a bug","Ask what is lost if the hold store restarts or fails over and forgets all holds; the database row must still say who owns what","Claim multi-seat requests in one transaction, all or nothing, locking seats in a consistent order (e.g. by seat id) to avoid deadlocks","Seat maps can be a cached snapshot refreshed every second or two","Think about per-account ticket limits and bots when picking admission order"],"checklist":["Seat states and transitions are explicit","Claim is atomic and enforced by the database","Holds expire without relying on the user","Seat rows store held_by and held_until so a hold-store restart cannot cause double holds","Backend load is capped by admission control","Late payment on an expired hold is handled with a refund or re-check","Stale seat map views cannot cause double booking"]},"isNew":true}]}]};
